<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Start here</span>
      <span class="tag">Orientation · ~15 minutes</span>
      <span class="tag">Read once, before everything else</span>
    </div>
    <h1>How This Academy Works</h1>
    <p class="sub">Welcome. This course was written by a practising Royal College CASC examiner — someone who sits on the other side of the table you are preparing for. It will not teach you psychiatry; you already know psychiatry. It will teach you the thing candidates cannot see from their side of the table: <b style="color:var(--lgold)">how examiners think</b>. Fifteen minutes here will change how you use everything that follows.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK — MEMORISE THIS ONE SENTENCE</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar — someone I could hand a patient to, and trust to manage safely?&rdquo;</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="rail"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">Orientation progress</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div>
  <span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<!-- ============ 1 THE LENS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The one sentence</span>
    <h2>Every mark comes from the same question</h2>
    <p class="intro">Examiners do not sit with a list of your questions, ticking them off. They watch you work, and behind everything they are answering one question: <b>could I trust this person as my registrar?</b> Structure, warmth, safety, judgement — every mark is a piece of evidence for or against that answer. Once you see the exam this way, every station becomes the same station: seven minutes of showing one experienced clinician that you are safe to trust.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this matters for how you prepare:</b> memorised scripts answer the wrong question. A script proves you prepared; it does not prove you can be trusted with a patient. This Academy trains the second thing — which is the thing being marked.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 TASTE IT ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Feel the difference — right now</span>
    <h2>Twenty seconds, seen through an examiner''s eyes</h2>
    <p class="intro">Here is one small moment — a safety question near the end of a low-mood history — done two ways. Tap the gold circles to see what the examiner is thinking at that exact second. This is how the whole Academy teaches.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Demo</b> · one moment, two ways</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="w">The usual way</button>
          <button data-t="s">The examiner''s way</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="script" id="d-w">
        <p class="takenote"><b>NOTICE:</b> nothing here is &ldquo;wrong.&rdquo; Watch what it costs anyway.</p>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">And any thoughts of harming yourself? No? Good. Now, about your sleep…</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Three faults in one breath. The question is closed, the answer was supplied for the patient (&ldquo;No? Good&rdquo;), and the candidate has already moved on. This is a tick on a checklist, not a safety assessment — and safety is exactly what I am watching for.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Patient</span><span class="txt">…No. Nothing like that.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Notice what the patient just did: gave the answer the question asked for. If there was something to tell, this question made it easy to hide. The candidate will finish this station believing it went well.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="script" id="d-s" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE SAME MOMENT,</b> asked the way an examiner hopes to hear it.</p>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Thank you for telling me all of this. I''d like to ask something I ask everyone, because it matters. When things have been at their worst — have you ever felt that life wasn''t worth living, or had thoughts of harming yourself?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Prepared, made normal, and asked as a genuinely open question — in about ten seconds. This candidate is not ticking a box; they are opening a door and standing quietly beside it. That is a safety net I can see working.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Patient</span><span class="txt"><em>(pause)</em> …Sometimes I''ve thought people would manage fine without me.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The disclosure the closed question could never reach. Same patient, same seven minutes, one different sentence — and now a real assessment can begin. This ten-second difference is often the difference between the two candidates'' marks.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>Your first decision</h3>
      <p class="stem">Earlier in that same station, the patient says: &ldquo;It''s been a strange few months, really&rdquo; — and looks down at his hands. What does the examiner hope you do next?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again — that is how every decision in this Academy works.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reassure him warmly that strange patches happen to everyone, and continue with your planned questions.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="1">Make a note of the comment so you can return to it later, and carry on through the symptom checklist.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="2">Pick the comment up gently — &ldquo;strange in what way?&rdquo; — and follow where he takes you before anything else.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Kindness that closes a door. Reassurance told him the strange months don''t need explaining — and they were probably the whole story. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-1"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Later&rdquo; rarely comes in a seven-minute station — and he offered it <em>now</em>. A noted cue is usually a lost cue. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>That comment was an offered thread — patients hand you the real story in half-sentences like this one. Following it is what curiosity looks like from where I sit, and curiosity is marked. You have just made your first examiner''s-eye decision. The whole Academy works like this.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 HOW IT''S BUILT ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">How the Academy is built</span>
    <h2>Domains, not diagnoses</h2>
    <p class="intro">Most courses organise by diagnosis: a depression station, a lithium station, an OCD station. Examiners do not think that way. They mark <b>competencies</b> — the same small set of skills, tested behind many different faces. The Academy is organised the way examiners think: eight Domains, each with the stations that test it.</p>
    <ul class="doms">
      <li><b>Communication &amp; Rapport</b> — the grammar of every consultation</li>
      <li><b>Information Giving</b> — medication and diagnosis conversations</li>
      <li><b>Risk Assessment</b> — one skill behind many disguises</li>
      <li><b>Mental State Examination</b> — eliciting, and using what you find</li>
      <li><b>Capacity, Consent &amp; the Law</b> — the statutory backbone</li>
      <li><b>Management &amp; Emergencies</b> — safe decisions under pressure</li>
      <li><b>Difficult Conversations</b> — the stations that push back</li>
      <li><b>Physical Examination</b> &mdash; the examinations psychiatry cannot delegate</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="reframe">&ldquo;This isn''t really a lithium station. It''s a medication-explanation station.&rdquo; &mdash; when you start saying sentences like that to yourself, the Academy is working. Unfamiliar stations stop being frightening, because you recognise what they are really testing.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>You can still find everything the normal way.</b> Search &ldquo;lithium&rdquo;, browse by specialty, or follow the Domains in order — every route leads to the same stations. The Domain structure is not a filing system; it is the first lesson.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE STATION RHYTHM + LADDER ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Inside every station</span>
    <h2>One rhythm, two modes, an honest ladder</h2>
    <p class="intro">Every station follows the same rhythm, so your attention goes to the clinical work, not to finding your way around: the task → the examiner''s lens → the station performed in full, with the examiner''s thinking one tap away → your decisions, with feedback in the examiner''s voice → the hidden trap the station was built around → the red flag and the examiner''s smile → take-home points → a practice pack for you and your colleagues.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="card"><b>Learn Mode</b>
        Guided and generous: every reveal open to you, every decision retriable until you find the move that works, the trap explained after you have felt its pull. This is where understanding is built. Fall into the traps here on purpose — this is the safe place to fail.</div>
      <div class="card gold"><b>Exam Mode</b>
        The same skill on a patient you have <b>not</b> met — timed, no hints, no second tries, the full analysis unlocked only when you finish. Certain decisions are marked <b>critical</b>: miss one, and the station is not passed whatever your total. That is how examiners think, so it is how the Academy scores.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder">
      <div class="rung"><div class="st"><span>Step 1</span>Explored</div><div>You have worked through the station in Learn Mode. You understand it — which is not yet the same as being able to do it.</div></div>
      <div class="rung"><div class="st"><span>Step 2</span>Recognised</div><div>You passed Exam Mode: you recognised the skill on an unfamiliar patient, under time, without help.</div></div>
      <div class="rung"><div class="st"><span>Step 3</span>Demonstrated</div><div>You produced it yourself — your own words, spoken or written, checked against the principles. Choosing is not performing; this step is the difference.</div></div>
      <div class="rung"><div class="st"><span>Step 4</span>Validated</div><div>A colleague watched you do it in a practice station and marked you against the examiner''s own domains.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>The ladder is deliberately honest.</b> Many courses call a finished video &ldquo;completed.&rdquo; Completion is not competence, and we will not pretend it is. Your progress here tells you what you can actually do — because that is what walks into the exam with you.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 PRACTICE SYSTEM ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The part no screen can do</span>
    <h2>The practice system — and why you need two colleagues</h2>
    <p class="intro">CASC is a spoken exam, and speaking is trained by speaking. Every major station ships with a <b>practice pack</b>: a candidate card, a role-player card, and an observer card. The scenarios are ones you have not seen on screen, and the role-player card carries <b>hidden information</b> — a disclosure the &ldquo;patient&rdquo; only reveals if your technique earns it, exactly as in the real exam. The observer marks you by domain, the way examiners actually mark, then gives an overall judgement: pass, borderline, or fail — and one sentence of evidence.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>Recruit two study partners this week.</b> Fifteen minutes per person per station: seven for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. Then rotate. Playing the patient and the examiner will teach you almost as much as the candidate''s chair — you will feel, from the other side, exactly what earns trust and what loses it.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 HOW TO USE IT ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Getting the most from it</span>
    <h2>The rhythm that makes it stick</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><b>Work in Domains, not in panic.</b> Follow the Domain order, or start with the Domain you fear most. Finish stations properly rather than sampling many at once.</li>
      <li><b>Learn Mode first — slowly.</b> Open every reveal, take every decision, and let yourself be tempted by the traps. The mistakes you feel here are the ones you will not make in the exam.</li>
      <li><b>Space the Exam Mode.</b> Return after a day or two, not immediately. The gap is the point: it tests what stayed with you, not what you just read.</li>
      <li><b>Practise aloud every week.</b> The packs with your two colleagues, rubric read aloud, roles rotated. Nothing on this screen replaces it.</li>
      <li><b>In the final fortnight, switch to Exam Modes only</b> — a timed circuit across the Domains. Your ladder will show you exactly where the gaps are.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="note"><b>One promise from the author:</b> everything in this Academy is written from the examiner''s chair — what is actually marked, what actually fails, and what makes an examiner quietly note that a candidate is safe. Where something is an instant fail, it is called an instant fail, because from where I sit, those are real.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 7 CLOSING DECISION ============ -->
<section id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">07</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Before you begin</span>
    <h2>One last decision — did the orientation land?</h2>
    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>The question behind every mark</h3>
      <p class="stem">You are mid-station, unsure what to ask next. What is the examiner actually deciding as they watch you?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Whether you have covered every question on their checklist for this particular clinical presentation.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Whether they would trust you as their registrar — safe to hand a patient to, whatever you ask next.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Whether your factual knowledge of this condition has reached the level expected of a consultant.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>There is no checklist being ticked behind the desk — there is a clinician forming a judgement. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Knowledge is assumed by the time you reach this exam — it is the entry ticket, not the prize. Something else is being weighed. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>That is the sentence to carry into every station: <em>would they trust me as their registrar?</em> When you don''t know what to ask next, ask what a trustworthy registrar would ask. You are ready to begin.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

