<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Domain 1 of 8</span>
      <span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">10 constructs · the foundation domain</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Communication &amp; Rapport</h1>
    <p class="sub">This is the grammar of the exam. Every history you take, every risk you assess, every difficult conversation you hold is delivered <b style="color:var(--lgold)">through</b> this domain — which is why it is marked in almost every station you will ever sit, and why the Academy begins here. Master this, and every other domain gets easier to show.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE LENS, APPLIED TO THIS DOMAIN</b>
      &ldquo;Would I trust this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; — In this domain the evidence is simple: could my patients talk to this doctor? Would they tell them the truth?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ============ 1 WHY FIRST ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this domain comes first</span>
    <h2>Marked everywhere, practised nowhere</h2>
    <p class="intro">Most candidates prepare content — symptoms, criteria, drug facts — and assume the talking will take care of itself. Examiners watch the opposite happen: candidates who know everything and cannot make a frightened person feel safe enough to tell them the truth. Communication is not the soft part of the exam. It is the delivery system for every mark you hope to earn — and it is trainable, skill by skill, which is what this domain does.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>How this domain works differently:</b> it has its own stations below, but its ten constructs are also woven through every station in the Academy. You will keep meeting them — in risk assessments, medication talks, angry-relative stations — because that is exactly how the exam tests them: never alone, always under load.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THE CONSTRUCTS ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Your mastery spine for this domain</span>
    <h2>The ten skills examiners are marking</h2>
    <p class="intro">These are the domain''s constructs — the specific, checkable skills behind the communication marks. As you work through the Academy, each one climbs the ladder you met in the orientation: Explored → Recognised → Demonstrated → Validated. This spine fills as you work, and it is the honest answer to &ldquo;am I ready?&rdquo;</p>
    <div class="spine">
      <div class="spine-head"><b>Communication &amp; Rapport</b><span>0 of 10 recognised — your spine starts here</span></div>
      <div class="cons">
        <div class="con"><span class="n">1</span><span>The structured opening — orientation, agenda, permission</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">2</span><span>Open questions before closed ones — letting the story arrive</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">3</span><span>Picking up cues instead of walking past them</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">4</span><span>Empathy that lands — felt by the patient, not performed for the examiner</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">5</span><span>Signposting — telling the patient where the conversation goes next</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">6</span><span>Being easy to follow — pace, plain words, one thread at a time</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">7</span><span>Checking understanding without testing the patient</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">8</span><span>The difficult start — the reluctant, minimising, or guarded patient</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">9</span><span>Working through an interpreter — and carrying the language load</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">10</span><span>Closing well — summary, plan, and a safety-net the patient can repeat</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="legend">
        <span><i></i> Explored</span><span><i></i> Recognised</span><span><i></i> Demonstrated</span><span><i></i> Validated</span>
        <span class="f"><i></i> = achieved</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 STATIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The stations in this domain</span>
    <h2>Walk them in order</h2>
    <div class="stn">
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">1.1</span>
        <span class="t"><b>The &ldquo;Simple&rdquo; Station</b><span>A low-mood history everyone can do — which is exactly why the marks hide in <em>how</em>. Constructs 1–4, 6 and 10, plus the safety question that changes everything.</span></span>
        <span class="chip go">Begin here</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">1.2</span>
        <span class="t"><b>The Patient Who Says Nothing Is Wrong</b><span>A guarded young man brought in by his family. Rapport built from a cold start — construct 8 under real load, with constructs 2 and 3 doing the quiet work.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 2</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">1.3</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Working Through an Interpreter</b><span>Three people in the room, one conversation. Construct 9 — the station IMG candidates fear and, with the right technique, often do best.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 3</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">1.4</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Medically Unexplained Symptoms</b><span>Tamsin, eighteen months of collapses and four normal investigations — where &ldquo;nothing is wrong&rdquo; is not an explanation, and the diagnosis has to be ruled in, named and believed before anything else can happen.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 4</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="also"><b>And then it follows you everywhere.</b> These ten constructs are marked again inside the overdose assessment, the lithium conversation, the angry father, the patient who won''t take no — every station in the Academy carries communication marks, because every station in the exam does. This domain is never finished; it is compounding.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 ENTRY DECISION ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Before you enter</span>
    <h2>One decision at the door</h2>
    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>Where do communication marks actually come from?</h3>
      <p class="stem">Two candidates take the same history and gather the same facts in the same seven minutes. One scores clearly higher on communication. What most likely made the difference?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">They asked more questions in the time available, showing the examiner broader clinical coverage.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="1">They used more precise professional terminology, showing the examiner a higher level of knowledge.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="2">The patient visibly relaxed and offered things unasked — the examiner watched trust being built.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>More questions usually means less listening. Coverage without connection reads as a checklist being emptied — and it earns knowledge marks at best, not communication marks. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-1"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Precise terminology impresses colleagues and loses patients. In this domain, plain words done warmly beat technical words done fluently. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Communication marks are read off the <em>patient</em>, not the candidate. When the patient relaxes, opens up, and offers what was never asked — the examiner is watching your skill work in real time. Everything in this domain aims at that moment.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Domain open</span>
    <h2>Could your patients talk to you? Prove it.</h2>
    <p>Station 1.1 is a history every candidate in the exam hall can take. The marks are in how — and after it, you will never call a station &ldquo;easy&rdquo; again.</p>
    <a class="btn mark" href="02_Station_1.1_The_Simple_Station.html">Begin Station 1.1 · The &ldquo;Simple&rdquo; Station</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

