  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '02_Station_1.1_The_Simple_Station'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 1.1 · Domain 1</span>
      <span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span><span class="tag">Risk Assessment</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult</span><span class="tag">History taking</span>
    </div>
    <h1>The &ldquo;Simple&rdquo; Station</h1>
    <p class="sub">Tom, 35, has been referred by his GP with three months of low mood. Nothing unusual, nothing dramatic — a history you have taken a hundred times. That is exactly why this station opens the Academy: when every candidate can do the task, the examiner is free to watch <em>how you work</em>. This station finds your defaults — and your defaults are what walk into every station after it.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar — someone I could hand a patient to, and trust to manage safely?&rdquo; On the simple station, add: if this is how they work when it''s easy, what will they drop when it''s hard?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">A routine low-mood history seems a strange choice for a high-stakes exam — until you sit on the marking side. When the task is familiar, candidates stop performing and start being themselves, and the examiner gets an honest look at their <b>defaults</b>: how they open, whether they truly listen, how they ask about safety when nothing about the case makes them nervous. The ordinary history is the daily bread of psychiatry. This station asks a simple question with a career behind it: <b>are your basics excellent when nobody seems to be testing them?</b></p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> familiarity. As you work through the decisions below, notice the pull toward doing this on autopilot — the urge to be quick, or the urge to simply be nice. Notice it. We will come back to it.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">This station has two opposite failure modes — and each one feels safe from the inside. Read all three versions. Tap the gold circles for the examiner''s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 1.1</b> · Low-mood history · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The checklist</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The drift</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE CHECKLIST.</b> Efficient, complete, and empty. Nothing here is wrong. That is the problem.</p>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Hi Tom, I''m one of the doctors. So — you''ve been feeling low? And how''s your sleep? Appetite okay? Energy?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>No introduction worth the name, no plan shared, and three questions fired in one breath. Tom has not finished describing what &ldquo;low&rdquo; means for him, and the candidate is already working down a list. I can see the list from here.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tom</span><span class="txt">Sleep''s not great. I wake early. Food''s… fine, mostly. <em>(pause)</em> Work''s been the hard bit, really.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Okay. And any thoughts of harming yourself? No? Good. Do you drink at all?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Two doors just closed in one line. &ldquo;Work''s been the hard bit&rdquo; was the real history offering itself — walked past. Then the safety question arrived closed, with the answer supplied. On a mild case, the safety question is precisely what I am watching. This one was a tick, not a question.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tom</span><span class="txt">Not really. The odd beer. <em>(he looks down)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Right — that all sounds like mild depression. I think we''ve covered everything. <em>(ninety seconds remain)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Finished early, satisfied, with the work untouched: no exploration of work, no impact on his life, nothing about what &ldquo;fine, mostly&rdquo; was hiding. Everything was adequate. Nothing was good. On the station everyone can do, that is how good candidates fail.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE DRIFT.</b> This candidate is warm, kind, and genuinely listening. Watch the station dissolve anyway.</p>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Hello Tom, it''s really good to meet you. There''s no rush at all — just tell me everything, from wherever feels right.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Lovely warmth — and no shape. &ldquo;Tell me everything&rdquo; hands the seven minutes to the patient with no map of where they need to go. I cannot yet see a doctor with a plan; I can see a kind person hoping the story lands somewhere useful.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tom</span><span class="txt">Well… it started around the restructure at work. My manager left, and then the new team… <em>(he talks; minutes pass)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">That sounds so difficult. And what happened after the second meeting with HR? <em>(more minutes pass)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Following every thread is not the same as curiosity — at this point it is surrendering the wheel. Four minutes in: no mood, no sleep, no appetite, no safety, a detailed map of one office. Empathy is being spent where steering was needed.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tom</span><span class="txt">…so that''s mostly the work side. Sorry — I''ve been going on.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Not at all. <em>(glances at the clock — 6:20)</em> I should quickly ask — no thoughts of hurting yourself or anything? And sleep''s been okay-ish?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The safety question arrives at the bell — rushed, closed, and apologised for. A risk question asked as an afterthought tells the patient it is an afterthought, and he answers it that way. Kindness is not a structure. This candidate was warmer than Fail 1 and will fail for the opposite reason: seven minutes of feeling, no assessment inside it.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Structured and warm at the same time. Watch how both are held — and what each one earns.</p>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Hello Tom, I''m Dr Ali, one of the psychiatry doctors. I''d like to hear how you''ve been feeling, ask how it''s affecting your life, and I''ll also ask some important questions about your safety. Is that alright?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Ten seconds, and the whole consultation has a visible shape — including safety, announced in advance, so it will arrive later as an expected part of care rather than an ambush. Tom relaxes. So do I.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tom</span><span class="txt">Sleep''s not great. I wake early. Food''s… fine, mostly. <em>(pause)</em> Work''s been the hard bit, really.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Mostly? <em>(Tom explains he''s been skipping meals)</em> Thank you. And work — tell me what''s changed there. <em>(follows it for a focused minute, then signposts:)</em> That helps me understand a lot. Now the important question I mentioned — at your worst moments, have you ever felt life wasn''t worth living?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Three skills in one stretch: the one-word cue picked up (&ldquo;Mostly?&rdquo;), the offered thread followed with purpose and then closed, and the safety question delivered exactly as promised — open, prepared, unhurried. This is what structure and warmth look like when they work together instead of taking turns.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tom</span><span class="txt">…Sometimes I''ve thought everyone would manage fine without me. Not plans. Just — tired.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I''m glad you told me — let''s stay with that. <em>(explores how often, how strong, what holds him — then, with time in hand:)</em> Can I ask what you make of all this yourself, and how things have been at home?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The disclosure was treated as a doorway, not a destination — depth, protective factors, what keeps him going. And the &ldquo;spare&rdquo; minutes became the second layer: his own understanding, the picture at home. The station ends full, with nothing left that I wanted asked. Same task as the other two takes. A different doctor entirely.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you''re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner''s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · The first thirty seconds</h3>
      <p class="stem">Tom sits down, polite and a little flat. How do you open?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Keep it light and get moving — &ldquo;Hi Tom, I''m one of the doctors. So — you''ve been feeling low, then?&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="1">Spend the first minute in friendly small talk — his journey in, his work — so he relaxes before questions.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="2">Introduce yourself, explain what you''d like to cover — including his safety — and ask if that''s alright.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Fine, and forgettable — it shows me no shape and buys you nothing. The ten seconds that could set up the whole station were given away. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-1"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Warmth without direction. A minute passes and nothing is built that the station can use — rapport comes from purpose the patient can feel, not from chat. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Orientation, agenda, permission — and safety announced in advance, so the risk question later arrives as promised care, not an ambush. Ten seconds that buy the whole station.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · &ldquo;Fine, mostly.&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Asked about appetite, Tom says &ldquo;fine, mostly&rdquo; — and glances away. What do you do?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Record it as a normal appetite and move along to the next symptom area on your mental checklist.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Hand the word gently back to him — &ldquo;Mostly?&rdquo; — and then wait quietly to see where he takes it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Challenge him directly on it — &ldquo;You don''t sound completely sure about that answer to me.&rdquo;</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Mostly&rdquo; was doing a lot of work in that sentence. Moving on records the answer and misses the person. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A direct challenge can open the door, but it spends warmth where one soft word would do the same job without pressure. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>One word, said kindly, then silence. It tells him you are listening to <em>him</em> rather than your list — and it is where the skipped meals, and the story behind them, were waiting.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · The offered thread</h3>
      <p class="stem">Mid-way through the biological symptoms, Tom adds: &ldquo;Work''s been the hard bit, really.&rdquo; What now?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Finish the remaining biological symptoms first, and return to the work comment if time allows later.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Leave the checklist where it is and follow him — &ldquo;Tell me about work. What''s changed there for you?&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Reassure him that work stress is very common alongside low mood, and continue with your questions.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Later&rdquo; rarely comes in a seven-minute station — and he offered it <em>now</em>. The checklist will still be there in a minute; his openness may not be. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Reassurance closes the door he just opened. &ldquo;Common&rdquo; is not the same as &ldquo;unimportant&rdquo; — and to him, it is the whole story. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The thread is where the real history lives — and the symptoms you paused will surface inside the story anyway, in his own words. Follow with purpose, then steer back. That is curiosity with a steering wheel.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · The safety question</h3>
      <p class="stem">You reach the point of asking about his safety. Which version does the examiner hope to hear?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">&ldquo;And no thoughts of harming yourself or anything like that? No? Good — that''s reassuring to hear.&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="1">&ldquo;I have to ask this one for completeness — you''re not feeling suicidal, are you? Just routine.&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="2">&ldquo;I ask everyone this, because it matters — at your worst, have you felt life wasn''t worth living?&rdquo;</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The question supplied its own answer and then celebrated it. Whatever he says now tells me nothing — and you have shown me your safety net has holes. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-1"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;You''re not… are you?&rdquo; leads him to no, and &ldquo;just routine&rdquo; apologises for the most important question in the station. Both tell him not to answer honestly. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Prepared, made normal, genuinely open, and pointed at the worst moments — where the truth lives. This is the sentence that separates the two candidates on this station. It found the disclosure the others never reached.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · The disclosure</h3>
      <p class="stem">Tom says: &ldquo;Sometimes I think everyone would manage fine without me. Not plans — just tired.&rdquo; What next?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reassure him warmly that many people feel this way, and that these feelings usually pass with treatment.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Thank him for telling you and stay there — how often, how strong, any plans, and what keeps him going?</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Acknowledge it briefly, note down that risk has now been covered, and complete the rest of the history.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Reassurance ends the conversation at exactly the point it needed to deepen. He did not need to be told it will pass; he needed you to want to understand it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Covered&rdquo; is the checklist talking. A disclosure is a doorway, not a tick — walking on from it is the biggest miss available in this station. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Frequency, intensity, plans, and the protective side — what holds him here. Now the &ldquo;passive thoughts&rdquo; have a shape, and your plan at the end will have evidence under it. This is the moment the station was built around.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · Ninety seconds left</h3>
      <p class="stem">You have genuinely covered the task, and ninety seconds remain. What now?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Thank him and finish early — a station completed with time to spare shows the examiner efficiency.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="1">Go back over the sleep, appetite and energy questions once more, to be certain nothing was missed.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="2">Go one level deeper — the impact on his life, his own view of what''s happening, who supports him.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>An early finish tells me you ran out of ideas, not material. The silence costs you in a station where the second layer was sitting there waiting. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-1"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Repeating what you already asked is padding — and an examiner tells padding from purpose in about four seconds. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Impact, his own understanding, his support — the second layer turns an early finish into a full station, and it is almost always where the richest material was waiting.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The assessment is done. What happens next?</h3>
      <p class="stem">Three months of low mood, passive thoughts without plans, work stress, a supportive wife. Your closing plan?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reassure him this is a mild episode that usually settles by itself, and leave the follow-up open-ended.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Agree a plan together — treatment options, follow-up, and exactly what to do if the thoughts worsen.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Refer him to the crisis team today, since thoughts of death have now featured in the history you took.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Passive thoughts need a safety-net, not a wave goodbye. &ldquo;It usually settles&rdquo; leaves him alone with the thing he just trusted you with. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The crisis team for passive thoughts with protective factors overshoots today''s picture — and teaches him that honesty triggers alarms. The response must match the risk you actually found. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>A plan agreed <em>with</em> him: options explained, follow-up arranged, and a clear safety-net — what to do, and who to call, if the thoughts grow. The plan matches the findings. That is the registrar I would trust.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you''ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Familiarity is the trap, and it pulls two ways</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt the pull in the decisions above — toward being quick, or toward simply being nice. That pull is the station''s design. Familiarity invites autopilot in two opposite directions: <b>efficiency that stops listening</b> (the checklist — everything covered, nothing heard) and <b>warmth that stops steering</b> (the drift — everything felt, nothing assessed). Each feels safe from the inside; each fails from the marking side. The pass is the third path the autopilot hides: <b>structure and warmth at the same time</b> — a visible plan, delivered by someone genuinely listening, for seven full minutes.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        The safety question that supplies its own answer: &ldquo;No thoughts of harming yourself? No? Good.&rdquo; A leading, closed risk question on a low-mood history is not a small style point — from the examiner''s chair it is an instant fail, however smooth the rest of the station was. It is the safety net with a hole in the middle.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner''s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: <em>&ldquo;Mostly?&rdquo;</em> — a whole cue picked up in one soft word — and the safety question that arrives exactly as the opening promised, open and unhurried, so the patient answers it honestly. When a patient says something he did not plan to say, the examiner just watched your skill work.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more reason this station opens the Academy: the ordinary history is the commonest station type in the exam — and the defaults it exposes are the ones you will carry into every risk assessment, medication talk and difficult conversation that follows. Fix them here, and you have fixed them everywhere.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every history</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Open with an audible plan</b> — orientation, agenda, permission — and announce the safety question inside it, so it later arrives as promised care.</li>
      <li><b>Half-sentences are the history.</b> &ldquo;Fine, mostly&rdquo;, &ldquo;work''s been the hard bit&rdquo; — patients hand you the real story in fragments. Pick them up; the checklist can wait a minute.</li>
      <li><b>Ask about safety so it can be answered.</b> Prepared, normalised, open, pointed at the worst moments. Never supply the answer; never apologise for the question.</li>
      <li><b>A disclosure is a doorway.</b> Stay: frequency, strength, plans, and what keeps them here. Then your plan has evidence under it.</li>
      <li><b>Spare time is the second layer</b> — impact, their own view, their support. Never finish early; never pad.</li>
      <li><b>Structure and warmth are not rivals.</b> The pass is both at once — and if you can hold both here, you can hold them anywhere.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Same skill, different disguise — so it''s a real test, not a memory test. Three people: candidate, role-player, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud from the checklist. The role-player card contains hidden information; candidates, don''t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Layla, 38, has come to her GP with months of tiredness and poor sleep. Take a focused history. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Open with a clear plan — and announce the safety question inside it.</li>
            <li>Follow what she gives you; her half-sentences are the history.</li>
            <li>Ask about her mood and her safety openly — never as quick yes/no questions.</li>
            <li>Use the whole station: impact, her own view, her support. No early finish.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Layla — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Layla, 38, a school administrator. You are exhausted, sleeping badly, and low underneath it — but you present it all as a sleep problem: &ldquo;I just need something to help me sleep.&rdquo; Drop small cues: &ldquo;the evenings are the worst&rdquo;, &ldquo;I''m not myself lately.&rdquo; Answer warm, open questions honestly; give short answers to rapid checklist questions.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          For months you have been drinking two or three glasses of wine every night to get to sleep, and it is creeping up. You are ashamed and have told no one. Only reveal it if the candidate asks openly and without judgement how you have been coping or winding down at night. If they ask a quick closed question (&ldquo;do you drink much?&rdquo;), say &ldquo;socially, you know.&rdquo; If asked about your safety warmly, admit that lately you sometimes feel &ldquo;what''s the point&rdquo; — no plans. If asked with a leading question, deny everything.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner''s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Would you trust them with this patient? Say one sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains and see if they agree with you.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="comm"><td colspan="2"><b>Communication skills</b><span class="dc" data-dc="comm">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="comm"></td><td>Clear opening — introduction, plan and permission, with safety announced inside it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="comm"></td><td>Open questions before closed ones; picked up her cues (&ldquo;the evenings are the worst&rdquo;, &ldquo;not myself&rdquo;).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="comm"></td><td>Empathy that landed; summarised or checked understanding along the way.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="prof"><td colspan="2"><b>Professionalism</b><span class="dc" data-dc="prof">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="prof"></td><td>Warm and unhurried throughout — no rapid-fire questioning, no judgement at any point.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="prof"></td><td>Managed the seven minutes: steered without rushing, no early finish, no padding.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="core"><td colspan="2"><b>Core symptoms</b><span class="dc" data-dc="core">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="core"></td><td>Looked beneath the &ldquo;sleep problem&rdquo; — mood, enjoyment and energy explored properly.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="core"></td><td>Biological symptoms covered naturally inside the conversation, not read as a list.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="other"><td colspan="2"><b>Other relevant areas</b><span class="dc" data-dc="other">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="other"></td><td>Asked openly how she copes or winds down at night — and earned the wine disclosure.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="other"></td><td>Explored the drinking without judgement once revealed — amount, pattern, her own worry about it.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="risk"><td colspan="2"><b>Risk</b><span class="dc" data-dc="risk">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Safety asked openly and warmly — prepared, normalised, never a supplied answer.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Stayed with the &ldquo;what''s the point&rdquo; feeling — how often, any plans, what keeps her going.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="wider"><td colspan="2"><b>The wider picture</b><span class="dc" data-dc="wider">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="wider"></td><td>Impact on work and home life; who is around her and who knows how she is.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="wider"></td><td>Her own view of what is happening — and a close with a plan she understood.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Layla hides a mood problem inside a sleep complaint, exactly as Tom hid his inside &ldquo;fine, mostly.&rdquo; The disclosures — the wine, the &ldquo;what''s the point&rdquo; — are earned by the same two skills this station trains: cues followed, and safety asked so it can be answered. If neither came out, that is the most useful feedback the session can give.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Tom proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: can you recognise the <b>same station wearing a different face</b>, and hold structure and warmth together with a patient you have never met — timed, with no feedback and no second tries? This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Kwame, 61</b>, a recently retired teacher, referred by his GP. He arrives with a written list of his memory failures: &ldquo;I forget names. I lose the thread of things. I think we both know what this is, doctor.&rdquo; Precise, courteous, and worried. Take a focused history.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type the exact safety question you would ask Kwame — the full sentence, as you would speak it in the room.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Kwame, I ask everyone this…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It is prepared or signposted — she can hear an important question coming.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It is made normal — &ldquo;I ask everyone this&rdquo; — never apologised for.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It is fully open — no answer supplied, nothing like &ldquo;you''re not… are you?&rdquo;</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It reaches the worst moments — &ldquo;at your worst, have you felt life wasn''t worth living?&rdquo;</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the open safety question, in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Structure and warmth — at the same time.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take the Layla pack to your colleagues — peer practice is where it becomes demonstrated. Next: the patient who says nothing is wrong.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="03_Station_1.2_Nothing_Is_Wrong.html">Continue · Station 1.2: The Patient Who Says Nothing Is Wrong</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

<footer><div class="wrap">
  <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
  <span>Station 1.1 · all patients are fictional composites</span>
</div></footer>


