  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '03_Station_1.2_Nothing_Is_Wrong'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 1.2 · Domain 1</span>
      <span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span><span class="tag">Risk Assessment</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult</span><span class="tag">The guarded start</span>
    </div>
    <h1>The Patient Who Says Nothing Is Wrong</h1>
    <p class="sub">Daniel, 24, has been brought to the clinic by his mother. For months he has barely left his room, stopped seeing friends, and lost weight — according to her. According to Daniel, sitting in front of you with his arms folded: &ldquo;There''s nothing wrong with me. My mum worries too much.&rdquo; He did not choose to be here, he does not want to talk to you, and you have seven minutes.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; In this station the evidence is specific: can they reach a patient who starts behind a wall — because half of psychiatry starts exactly there.</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Psychiatry''s patients often did not choose the room. They are brought by families, persuaded by employers, sent by courts — ashamed, wary, or simply certain that nothing is wrong. A candidate who can only assess the willing patient can only do half the job. So the examiners built a station where the barrier <b>is</b> the task: seven minutes with someone who has decided, before you speak, that there is nothing to discuss. The question behind it: <b>can you build a working alliance with a person who starts behind a wall?</b></p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> his guard will make you feel you must choose — press through the wall, or respect it and back away. As you work through the decisions below, notice that pull in yourself. We will come back to it.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">This station has two opposite failure modes — and each one arrives dressed as a virtue: thoroughness, or respect. Read all three versions. Tap the gold circles for the examiner''s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 1.2</b> · The guarded patient · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The interrogation</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The retreat</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE INTERROGATION.</b> This candidate is thorough, organised, and pressing. Watch the wall grow.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Daniel</span><span class="txt"><em>(arms folded)</em> This was my mum''s idea. There''s nothing wrong with me.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Well, your mum told us you''ve barely left your room for months, you''ve stopped seeing your friends, and you''ve lost weight. That doesn''t sound like nothing, does it?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Her evidence, read out against him, in the first thirty seconds. The candidate has just announced whose side the room is on — and Daniel heard it. Everything he says from here will be a defence, not a disclosure.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Daniel</span><span class="txt">She exaggerates. I see people. I''m fine.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Which people, when did you last see them, and how is your sleep? I also need to ask about your mood, your appetite, and whether you''ve had any thoughts of harming yourself.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Pressure meeting resistance with more pressure. The questions are all legitimate — and all wasted, because they are being fired at a closed door. The candidate is assessing the wall and calling it the patient. Even the safety question just went past him at speed.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Daniel</span><span class="txt">Fine. Dunno. No. <em>(he looks at the door)</em> Are we done?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Not yet — I still have several important areas to cover, and this is for your own benefit, Daniel. <em>(the answers get shorter; the wall gets higher)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;For your own benefit&rdquo; — the sentence of someone doing an assessment <em>to</em> a person rather than with them. Seven minutes of thorough questions will end with nothing true in the notes. Thoroughness was never the problem to solve here; trust was.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE RETREAT.</b> This candidate is respectful, gentle, and completely defeated. Watch the station end politely.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Daniel</span><span class="txt"><em>(arms folded)</em> This was my mum''s idea. There''s nothing wrong with me.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">That''s completely fair, and I don''t want to force anything. You know yourself better than anyone. Is there anything at all you''d like to talk about?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Gentle — and already retreating. &ldquo;Anything you''d like to talk about?&rdquo; hands the agenda to someone whose stated agenda is to leave. Respecting his autonomy is right; abandoning the assessment in its name is not. There is a difference, and this station exists to find it.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Daniel</span><span class="txt">Not really. Like I said, I''m fine. Mum worries.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Mums do worry! Well — I''m glad things are okay from your side. It''s really good that you came in anyway. <em>(a friendly silence settles)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The candidate has just co-signed &ldquo;I''m fine&rdquo; — the one claim the whole referral disputes. Months in his room, lost weight, vanished friends: somebody in this building needs to be curious about that, and it was supposed to be this doctor. The guard did not have to defeat anyone; it was joined.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Daniel</span><span class="txt">So… can I go?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Almost — let me just quickly ask, no thoughts of harming yourself or anything like that? No? Great. I''ll let your mum know everything''s alright. <em>(four minutes remain, unused)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A token safety question at the door, and a reassurance to his mother that nothing in this room actually earned. This candidate was kinder than Fail 1 and ends in the same place: no alliance, no assessment — just a politer version of not finding out.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Patient and persistent at the same time. Watch the wall come down from his side.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Daniel</span><span class="txt"><em>(arms folded)</em> This was my mum''s idea. There''s nothing wrong with me.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">You didn''t ask to be here, did you? That''s a strange position to be in — so thanks for coming in at all. Can I ask what <b>you</b> make of it? Why do you think she''s worried?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The alliance offered before the assessment: his position acknowledged, his effort credited, and the first question asked from <em>inside his frame</em> — her worry, through his eyes. He is being treated as the expert on his own situation. Watch his shoulders.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Daniel</span><span class="txt"><em>(shrugs, slightly less folded)</em> She counts how many times I leave my room. It''s… I''ve just been on my Xbox a lot. Jake''s basically the only one I talk to now, anyway.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Now? <em>(a pause; Daniel looks up)</em> Was that different before?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>One word, his own word, handed back softly — the cue technique working under guard conditions, where it matters most. No clinical vocabulary, no pounce. The door he left ajar is being opened at his pace, and he is the one opening it.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Daniel</span><span class="txt"><em>(long pause)</em> Look — things aren''t great, alright? But if I tell you stuff, you''ll just tell my mum.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">That''s a fair question, so let me be straight with you. What you tell me stays between us — unless I''m worried about your safety or someone else''s. And if that ever happened, you''d hear it from me first, not find out later. <em>(Daniel studies him, then nods — and begins to talk)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The station''s hinge, handled exactly right: no blanket promise, the safety limit named plainly, and the dignity of hearing it first. He trusts this doctor <em>because</em> the doctor promised less. What follows — the university he left months ago, the mornings spent pretending, the &ldquo;what''s the point&rdquo; moments — can now be assessed properly, warmly, through an open door. The wall never had to be defeated. It was retired.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you''re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner''s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · The folded arms</h3>
      <p class="stem">Daniel sits down: &ldquo;This was my mum''s idea. There''s nothing wrong with me.&rdquo; How do you open?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reassure him that this will be quick and painless — a few routine questions and he can be on his way.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Acknowledge that he didn''t choose this, credit him for coming, and ask what he makes of it himself.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Begin the standard questions gently anyway — mood, sleep, appetite — so the assessment gets moving.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Quick and painless&rdquo; frames this as an ordeal to be endured — something done <em>to</em> him. He will wait you out politely, and you have promised him it will be over soon. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Questions fired at a closed door bounce. Until there is some alliance, you are assessing the wall and recording its answers. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The alliance before the assessment: his position named, his effort credited, and the first question asked from his side of the table. The first minute of this station decides whether the other six exist.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · &ldquo;Mum worries too much&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">He shrugs: &ldquo;Mum worries too much. That''s honestly all this is.&rdquo; What do you do with it?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Gently put her side to him — the months in his room, the missed meals — and ask him to respond to it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="1">Agree that mothers do tend to worry, and steer the conversation toward your own questions instead.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="2">Stay inside his frame and get curious with him — &ldquo;What is it she worries about, do you think?&rdquo;</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Her evidence, used against him, tells him whose side you are on — and the wall gains a second row of bricks. Her account matters; quoted as a charge sheet, it costs the station. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-1"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Agreeing-and-steering dismisses the one topic he has raised. Her worry is the only door he has opened; walking past it leaves you both in the corridor. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>His frame is the open door. Exploring <em>her</em> worry through <em>his</em> eyes keeps him the expert, keeps him talking — and quietly starts the history: whatever he thinks she has noticed is usually what is really happening. </template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · One-word answers</h3>
      <p class="stem">Your open questions get &ldquo;Fine.&rdquo; &ldquo;Dunno.&rdquo; &ldquo;Not really.&rdquo; What is most likely to work?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Name it directly but kindly — &ldquo;I notice you''re giving me short answers&rdquo; — and ask him why that is.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Step off the clinical path onto neutral ground — his day, his interests — and let him warm up there.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Keep the questions coming so no silence settles — steady momentum will loosen him up eventually.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Naming the resistance makes the resistance the topic — and him the problem. That is heat applied to someone already guarded. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>More questions, more wall. Volume is not progress; to a guarded patient it is evidence that this really is an interrogation. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Neutral ground is not wasted time — it is where the alliance gets built. People talk from safe ground first; the gaming, the music, the ordinary day is the bridge the assessment will later cross.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · The fragment</h3>
      <p class="stem">Talking about gaming, he lets slip: &ldquo;Jake''s basically the only one I talk to now, anyway.&rdquo; What do you do?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Bank it — keep the easy topic rolling to protect the warmth, and return to friendships a little later.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Pick it up softly, inside his own words — &ldquo;Now? Was that different before?&rdquo; — and follow gently.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Ask directly about social withdrawal — how long he has been isolating, and what he thinks drives it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Under guard, cues arrive once. &ldquo;Later&rdquo; is where they go to disappear — and warmth that never becomes assessment is Fail 2 in slow motion. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Isolating&rdquo; is your word, not his — clinical vocabulary yanks him off his safe ground onto yours, and you can watch the shutters come down as it lands. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>His word, handed back softly, at his pace. The guard barely notices the door opening — because he is the one opening it. This is the cue skill from Station 1.1, working in much harder weather.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · &ldquo;You''ll just tell my mum&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">He stops: &ldquo;Look — things aren''t great, alright? But if I tell you stuff, you''ll just tell my mum.&rdquo;</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Promise him complete confidentiality — whatever he tells you stays inside this room, no exceptions.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="1">Explain that his mum is genuinely worried about him, and that she has a right to understand things.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="2">Be honest — it stays private unless safety is at risk, and he would be the first to hear about it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A promise you may have to break within the next five minutes — and when you break it, you take the last adult he trusted down with you. This is the station''s instant fail. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-1"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Every word true — and it confirms exactly what he feared: you are her agent. The disclosure he was about to make dies in this sentence. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Honest limits build more trust than total promises. The safety exception named plainly, the dignity of hearing it first — and watch: he opens up <em>because</em> you promised less. That paradox is the whole skill.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The door opens</h3>
      <p class="stem">It comes out: he left university months ago, leaves each morning pretending to go, feels he has &ldquo;ruined everything.&rdquo; What next?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reassure him — plenty of people step away from university, and there are always routes back into it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Stay with him inside it — how has he been in himself through this, and how heavy do the days get?</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Move to the practical question — how, and when, he is going to tell his parents what has happened.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Reassurance answers the fact and abandons the feeling. &ldquo;Ruined everything&rdquo; was the point of the sentence — and it is doing more work than the university is. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Logistics can wait; the disclosure was about weight, not planning. Solving the parents now skips the assessment the whole alliance was built to reach. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Now the assessment rides through the open door — his mood through these months, and from there his safety, asked warmly. This is what the alliance was <em>for</em>: everything before this moment was building the road; this is the journey.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · &ldquo;You won''t make a big thing of this?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The picture: months of concealment, low mood, &ldquo;what''s the point&rdquo; moments, no plans. He asks you not to make it big. Your close?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Agree to keep it small — no follow-up unless he decides he wants one, and he knows where you are.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Agree a plan with him — follow-up he consents to, support for telling his family, a clear safety-net.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Explain kindly that his mum needs to be told today, so that the people at home can support him properly.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Undershoot. Passive thoughts plus total concealment need a net, not an open door and a wave. &ldquo;Small&rdquo; cannot mean &ldquo;unsupported.&rdquo; Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Overshoot — and it breaks the confidentiality position you took five minutes ago, on today''s risk picture. His consent is the foundation this plan stands on. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Consented follow-up, help with telling his family on his timetable, and a safety-net he can repeat back. The plan honours both the findings <em>and</em> the alliance — which is why he will actually come back. That is the registrar I would trust.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you''ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The wall pulls you two ways — and both wear a virtue</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt it in the decisions above: the urge to <b>press</b> — put her evidence to him, keep the questions coming, get the assessment done — and the urge to <b>retreat</b> — respect his &ldquo;I''m fine&rdquo;, keep it friendly, let him go. Each arrives dressed as something admirable: the first as thoroughness, the second as respect for his autonomy. Both end the same way: no alliance, no assessment, nothing true in the notes. The pass is the third path: <b>patient and persistent at the same time</b> — you neither push through the wall nor walk away from it. You sit down beside it, and let it come down from his side.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        The rented disclosure: &ldquo;everything you say stays between us — I promise.&rdquo; A blanket confidentiality promise, made to win the patient''s trust, is a promise medicine cannot keep — and this station is built to make you want to make it. From the examiner''s chair it is an instant fail: you have traded the patient''s future safety for this minute''s rapport.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner''s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: <em>&ldquo;You didn''t ask to be here, did you?&rdquo;</em> — the alliance offered inside the first minute — and the honest confidentiality answer, safety limit and all, that wins his trust <em>because</em> it promised less. When a guarded patient starts talking freely, the examiner just watched the skill this station exists to find.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more reason this scenario was chosen: the brought-in, guarded patient is everywhere — in the exam and in the job. Families bring sons; daughters bring mothers; courts and employers send people who insist they are fine. The alliance skills this station trains are the opening move of half the stations in this Academy — and of half the consultations of your career.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every history</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Side with the person before the task.</b> &ldquo;You didn''t choose to be here&rdquo; costs ten seconds and buys the station. The alliance is not a warm-up for the assessment; it is the road the assessment travels on.</li>
      <li><b>Their frame is the door.</b> &ldquo;What do <em>you</em> make of it? What does she worry about, do you think?&rdquo; — the guarded patient will discuss other people''s worry long before their own trouble.</li>
      <li><b>Neutral ground is not wasted time.</b> The gaming, the garden, the ordinary day — people talk from safe ground first, and the cues fall there.</li>
      <li><b>Under guard, cues come once, in fragments.</b> Pick them up softly, in the patient''s own words — never in clinical vocabulary.</li>
      <li><b>Never promise blanket confidentiality.</b> Honest limits — &ldquo;unless safety is at risk, and you''d hear it first&rdquo; — build more trust than total promises, and they are the only promise you can keep.</li>
      <li><b>When the door opens, the assessment rides through.</b> Mood, then safety, warmly — and a close that honours the consent the whole station was built on.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Same skill, different disguise — so it''s a real test, not a memory test. Three people: candidate, role-player, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud from the checklist. The role-player card contains hidden information; candidates, don''t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Adam, 19, has been brought in by his father after months of staying out late, skipping college, and going quiet at home. Adam''s position: &ldquo;Dad''s overreacting.&rdquo; Engage him and assess. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Side with him first — he didn''t choose to be here.</li>
            <li>His frame is the door: what does he think his dad is worried about?</li>
            <li>Use neutral ground; pick up his fragments softly, in his own words.</li>
            <li>If he asks about confidentiality, answer honestly — no blanket promises.</li>
            <li>When the door opens: his mood, and his safety, asked warmly.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Adam — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Adam, 19. Arms folded, monosyllabic: &ldquo;Dad''s overreacting.&rdquo; Give one-word answers to clinical questions. Warm up only on neutral ground — gaming, music, football. If the candidate quotes your dad''s worries against you, go colder. Drop small cues once warmed: &ldquo;college is whatever&rdquo;, &ldquo;I''m out most evenings, just walking, thinking.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          You are failing college badly and hiding it at home; most nights you smoke cannabis to switch your head off. You are ashamed, and afraid of your dad finding out. Before revealing anything real, test the candidate: &ldquo;If I tell you stuff, does my dad get told?&rdquo; If they promise total secrecy, get suspicious — &ldquo;doctors can''t actually promise that&rdquo; — and give nothing more. If they answer honestly (private unless safety is at risk, and you''d hear first), open up. If asked warmly about your safety once the door is open, admit you sometimes think &ldquo;what''s the point&rdquo; — no plans, and Jake would notice if you disappeared.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner''s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Would you trust them with this patient? Say one sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains and see if they agree with you.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="alli"><td colspan="2"><b>The alliance (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="alli">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="alli"></td><td>Sided with him first — acknowledged he didn''t choose this, and credited him for coming.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="alli"></td><td>Explored his frame — what he thinks his dad is worried about — before any agenda of their own.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="alli"></td><td>Gave him control of pace and topics; never argued with &ldquo;I''m fine&rdquo;.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="comm"><td colspan="2"><b>Communication skills</b><span class="dc" data-dc="comm">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="comm"></td><td>Used neutral ground (gaming, music, his day) to build before assessing.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="comm"></td><td>Picked up his fragments softly, in his own words — never in clinical vocabulary.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="prof"><td colspan="2"><b>Professionalism</b><span class="dc" data-dc="prof">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="prof"></td><td>Never quoted the father''s account against him; no pressure applied at any point.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="prof"></td><td>Answered the confidentiality question honestly — limits named, no blanket promise.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="core"><td colspan="2"><b>The story reached</b><span class="dc" data-dc="core">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="core"></td><td>Earned the real story — the college trouble and the nightly cannabis.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="core"></td><td>Explored his mood through these months once the door was open.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="risk"><td colspan="2"><b>Risk</b><span class="dc" data-dc="risk">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Asked about his safety warmly, through the open door — never as a token question.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Stayed with the &ldquo;what''s the point&rdquo; answer — depth, plans, what keeps him going.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>Plan and close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Agreed a consented plan — follow-up he accepted, support for telling his dad on his timetable.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Closed with a safety-net he could repeat, and the door left open — no forced disclosure.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Adam guards with silence where Daniel guarded with denial — but the skill is identical: alliance before assessment, his frame as the door, honest confidentiality when tested. The role-player card is built to punish the blanket promise: if the candidate promises total secrecy, Adam trusts them <em>less</em>. If the real story never came out, start the feedback at the alliance domain — that is almost always where it was lost.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Daniel proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: can you recognise the <b>same wall wearing a different face</b>, and build the alliance with a patient you have never met — timed, with no feedback and no second tries? This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Raj, 55</b>, a taxi driver, brought in by his wife, who told the GP he has been short-tempered, secretive, and &ldquo;not himself&rdquo; for months. Raj, arms crossed: &ldquo;My wife panics. I work nights, I get tired. That''s the whole story.&rdquo; He has diabetes, which he mentions he &ldquo;keeps an eye on.&rdquo; Engage him and assess.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Raj asks: &ldquo;This stays medical, yes? My wife doesn''t hear about it?&rdquo; Type your exact answer — one or two sentences, as you would say them in the room.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Raj, let me be straight with you…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It makes no blanket promise — nothing that says &ldquo;whatever it is, it stays here.&rdquo;</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The safety exception is named plainly — not hidden, not apologised for.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> He hears it first — the dignity of never finding out from someone else.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It is warm — the door to talking stays open in the same breath.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): honest confidentiality, in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Patient and persistent — at the same time.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take the Adam pack to your colleagues — peer practice is where it becomes demonstrated. Next: working through an interpreter.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="04_Station_1.3_Working_Through_an_Interpreter.html">Continue · Station 1.3: Working Through an Interpreter</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

