<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 1.3 · Domain 1</span>
      <span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span><span class="tag">Risk Assessment</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult</span><span class="tag">The interpreter station</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Working Through an Interpreter</h1>
    <p class="sub">Fatima, 52, moved from Syria a year ago to live with her son''s family. Her GP is worried about her mood. She speaks Arabic; you do not. Between you sits Samir, a professional interpreter. Your task: find out how she has really been — through him. And a word to every candidate who lives between languages: you already know things about this station that examiners wait years to see used. Today you learn to use them.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here the evidence is specific: can they deliver a real consultation when every word must travel through a third person — because tomorrow''s clinic will ask exactly that.</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Psychiatry serves people in every language, and the interpreted consultation is ordinary clinical life — yet almost nobody is ever taught to do it. So the examiners built a station where the channel <b>is</b> the test: every Domain&nbsp;1 skill you own — the opening, the cues, the empathy, the safety question — must now survive a journey through a third person. The question behind it: <b>does your communication still work when the words are not yours to deliver?</b></p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> the pull. The only person in the room who understands your words is not the patient — and everything in you will want to talk to him. Notice that pull in the decisions below. We will come back to it.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure relates to the wrong person. The other relates to the right person and transmits badly. Read all three versions. Tap the gold circles for the examiner''s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 1.3</b> · Assessment through an interpreter · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The go-between</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The flood</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE GO-BETWEEN.</b> Organised, efficient — and the patient is never once spoken to. Watch where the eyes go.</p>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(to Samir)</em> Can you ask her why her son brought her in today? And ask whether she''s been sleeping and eating properly.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Third person, double question, eyes on the interpreter. Before a word has been translated, Fatima has learned her place in this room: she is the subject of a meeting between two professionals. Watch her eyes drop to her hands.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Interp.</span><span class="txt"><em>(interprets; listens)</em> She says she sleeps very little. And… she says her heart is tired.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(to Samir)</em> Her heart — does she mean something physical, or is that how they describe low mood? Ask about her energy as well.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;My heart is tired&rdquo; was a gift — a patient offering her inner world in an image. It is being processed as an admin query, negotiated with the interpreter over her head. And she understands far more of this than the candidate imagines. Watch her face while they discuss her.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Fatima</span><span class="txt"><em>(in Arabic, quietly, to her hands)</em> — <em>Interp.: She says: whatever the doctor thinks is best.</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(to Samir)</em> Nearly done. Ask her the risk questions — whether she has thoughts of harming herself. Just ask her directly.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The most delicate question in the station, delegated like an errand — and the instruction is &ldquo;ask her directly&rdquo;: the one thing this candidate has not done since the door opened. Information was collected today. A consultation never happened. She was present at her own assessment the way luggage is present on a journey.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE FLOOD.</b> This candidate looks at Fatima, speaks to Fatima, means every warm word — and drowns the channel carrying them.</p>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(warmly, to Fatima)</em> Hello Fatima, so good to meet you — your GP wrote because your family feel you''ve been under the weather since the move, and I''d love to unpack how things have really been: whether your sleep''s been all over the place, how your appetite''s holding up, and whether you''ve felt at the end of your tether at all?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Everything right about the direction — first person, eye contact, warmth — and everything wrong about the delivery: seventy words, four questions, three idioms, no pause. Samir is now holding an armful. Whatever reaches Fatima will be a summary, and the candidate will never know which parts survived.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Interp.</span><span class="txt">Doctor — &ldquo;under the weather&rdquo;, &ldquo;end of the tether&rdquo;… I will do my best. <em>(interprets, visibly shortening; Fatima answers only the final fragment — something about food)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Idioms do not cross. Every interpreting turn is a bottleneck, and meaning is dying in transit — but the candidate cannot see it, because his next sentence is already leaving. She has answered the only question that reached her.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Right, okay — <em>(Samir is still mid-interpretation; the candidate continues over him)</em> — and I do have to ask, sometimes when people feel like this they can get dark thoughts, have you had anything like that, thoughts of harming yourself, or that life''s not worth living — or is it not that bad?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Talking over the channel — the interpreter is not a microphone; he needs turns. And then the station''s most important question, strangled by its own tail: &ldquo;or is it not that bad?&rdquo; hands her the exit, and through an interpreter the exit arrives twice the size. Warmth was never this candidate''s problem. The channel drowned, and the assessment drowned with it.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Direct and paced at the same time. Watch the machine get set — then watch it disappear.</p>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(to Samir, ten seconds, before beginning)</em> I''ll speak to Fatima directly — please interpret everything, exactly as said, and stop me if I go too fast. <em>(then, to Fatima, meeting her eyes)</em> Hello Fatima. I''m Dr Ali. I''d like to hear how you have been feeling. Later I will also ask about your safety. Is that alright?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Ten seconds of briefing set the entire channel: everything, exactly, first person, permission to stop him. Then the Station 1.1 opening — chunked, plain, safety announced — delivered to <em>her</em>, with the eyes where the conversation lives. Two sentences in, and I already know this station is in safe hands.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Fatima</span><span class="txt"><em>(in Arabic)</em> — <em>Interp.: My heart is tired. Since we came here, the days are very long.</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Your heart is tired. Tell me about that. <em>(and while Samir carries it across, the candidate''s eyes never leave her)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Her metaphor, kept in her own words, handed gently back — the cue skill from Station 1.1 crossing languages intact. And note where the attention is during the interpretation: on her face and hands, where the history that needs no translation is being written.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(a few exchanges later)</em> I want to be sure I understand. You sleep two or three hours. The mornings are the worst. Have I got that right?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Short turns, one thing at a time, then summarise-and-check. Through an interpreter, checking understanding is not good manners — it is the only proof the channel is working. Both directions, regularly. This is what channel discipline looks like when it has become invisible.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Fatima — I ask everyone this, because it matters. In the worst moments, have you felt that life is not worth living? <em>(Samir interprets. A long silence. She answers, quietly, at length.)</em> Thank you for telling me. Let''s stay with that.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The same open safety question this Domain has trained from the start — and it survived the journey <em>because</em> it was built simple: short, plain, prepared. The hardest question in the station crossed intact, and so did the answer. Two languages. One conversation. That is the pass.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you''re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner''s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · Thirty seconds with Samir</h3>
      <p class="stem">You have half a minute with the interpreter before Fatima comes in. How do you use it?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Save the time — the station is short, and the interpreter will pick up your style as you go along.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Brief him — everything, exactly as said, first person, stop me if I''m too fast — and set the chairs.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Ask him what is normal for women of her background, and which topics you should approach carefully.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The machine starts unset — and the first misunderstanding will cost more time than the briefing would have. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>He is her interpreter, not her culture''s spokesman. That question invites stereotype to do your assessment for you — her background will be learned from her. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Ten seconds that set the entire channel: everything, exactly, first person, permission to stop you — and a triangle of chairs so your eyes can live with her. Examiners see this once a day at most.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · The first words</h3>
      <p class="stem">Fatima is seated, hands folded, eyes down. How does the consultation begin?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Turn toward Samir and begin — &ldquo;Could you ask her how she has been feeling in herself recently?&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Look at Fatima and speak to her — &ldquo;Hello Fatima. I''m one of the doctors. I''d like to hear how you are.&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Address them in turns — a question toward her, then a check with him — so nobody feels left out.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Ask her&rdquo; makes her the subject of a meeting between two professionals — and she heard the distance before he translated a word. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Alternating splits your presence in half. She needs all of it; he needs none — he has a job, not a conversation. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>First person, eyes on her, from the first breath. He will carry the words — but the conversation belongs to the two of you, and she can feel that before any translation arrives.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · &ldquo;My heart is tired&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Through Samir, her answer arrives: &ldquo;My heart is tired.&rdquo; What do you do with it?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Ask Samir what &ldquo;my heart is tired&rdquo; usually means among Arabic speakers, so you read it rightly.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Give it back to her in her own words — &ldquo;Your heart is tired. Tell me about that.&rdquo; — and wait.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Record it as low mood with fatigue, and move to the next area — the meaning is clinically clear.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Her meaning lives with her, not with him. Questions about her words go to her — anything else sends the history to the wrong owner. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Clinically tidy, humanly deaf. The image was an opening, not a finding — filing it closes the very door it was opening. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The cue skill from Station 1.1, crossing languages intact: her image, her words, handed gently back. The story arrives through the metaphor — it almost always does.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · How you speak</h3>
      <p class="stem">Which speaking style serves the interpreted consultation best?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Longer, fuller explanations than usual — the extra context will help him to translate accurately.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="1">Your natural pace and phrasing, idioms included — carrying meaning across is the interpreter''s craft.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="2">Short chunks, plain words, one question at a time — and a full pause for every interpreting turn.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Longer turns mean more summarising at the far end — you hand him an armful and receive back a handful. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-1"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Idioms do not travel, and speed drowns the channel. He is a colleague with a hard job, not a microphone. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Chunked, plain, one thing at a time, pauses honoured — the discipline that keeps meaning alive across two languages. It feels slow. It is the fastest route through this station.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · While the words travel</h3>
      <p class="stem">Samir is interpreting your question into Arabic. Where is your attention?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">On your notes — the interpreting gaps are the only writing time that this station will give you.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">On Fatima — her face, her hands, her posture; the words will arrive when the turn is finished.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">On Samir — following his tone and length, to satisfy yourself the interpretation stays faithful.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The gaps are where she tells you everything that needs no translation — and you were looking at paper. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>You cannot audit a language you do not speak by staring at it. You briefed him; trust the briefing, and watch the patient. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The non-verbal history is in your own language: the pause before an answer, the hands, the eyes filling. Read it while the words travel — it is half the assessment, and it needs no interpreter.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The safety question</h3>
      <p class="stem">You reach the question about her safety — which now has a journey to survive. Which version?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">A gentler form for the channel — ask about &ldquo;stress&rdquo; and &ldquo;difficult thoughts&rdquo;, to avoid offence.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="1">Hand the wording to Samir — ask him to phrase it in whatever way is most acceptable culturally.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="2">Unchanged — short, plain, prepared: &ldquo;In the worst moments, have you felt life is not worth living?&rdquo;</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Softened until it cannot find what it exists to find — a safety net rewoven with larger holes. Delicacy is in the delivery, never in the dilution. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-1"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The wording of the station''s most important question is clinical judgement — yours to own, never to delegate. He carries words; he does not write them. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The same open question this Domain has trained from Station 1.1 — and it crosses <em>because</em> it is simple: short, plain, prepared. Delicate questions travel best when they carry least.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">She has disclosed: two to three hours of sleep, little food, passive thoughts, isolation since the move. Your close?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Choose the treatment and ask Samir to relay it — medication, and a review in a few weeks'' time.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Agree the plan with her — understanding checked both ways, the same interpreter booked, a safety-net.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Suggest her son interprets from now on — family, always available, and it will save the arranging.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A plan relayed is a parcel, not an agreement — and you will never know what she understood, or what she would have said back. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Family interpreters cost the truth: nobody discloses despair through their child — and her son becomes the filter on everything she says next. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Agreed with her, checked in both directions, the same professional interpreter booked, a safety-net she repeated back in her own language. Care that works in her language — that is the registrar I would trust.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you''ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The pull toward the person who understands you</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt it in the decisions above: the pull toward Samir — the only person in the room whose words come back to you in your own language. It breaks candidates in two directions. Some follow the pull and <b>relate to the wrong person</b>: the go-between station, conducted in the third person, eyes on the interpreter, the patient turned into the subject of a meeting. Others resist it so completely that they <b>transmit as if he isn''t there</b>: the flood — natural pace, idioms, compound questions, the channel drowning while the candidate beams warmly at a patient who is receiving summaries. The pass is the third path: <b>direct and paced at the same time</b> — the conversation belongs to you and her; the discipline belongs to the channel.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        The consultation that never touches the patient: an entire station conducted in the third person — &ldquo;ask her&hellip; tell her&hellip;&rdquo; — with the eyes on the interpreter throughout. Whatever information was collected, no consultation with the patient took place, and from the examiner''s chair that is an instant fail on this station.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner''s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the <b>ten-second interpreter briefing</b> before the first question — seen perhaps once a day — and the candidate whose <b>eyes stay on the patient</b> while the interpretation flows, reading the history that needs no translation.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more reason this station matters to <b>you</b> in particular: if English is not your first language, you have lived on both sides of this channel — you know what it is for meaning to travel, and what gets lost when nobody minds the crossing. Candidates fear this station. Done with technique, it is the one where an IMG candidate can look more consultant-like than anyone in the building.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every interpreted consultation</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Brief the interpreter — ten seconds, before anything.</b> Everything, exactly as said, first person, stop me if I''m too fast. The whole channel is set in one breath.</li>
      <li><b>Speak to her, never about her.</b> First person, eyes on the patient, from the first word to the last. &ldquo;Ask her&rdquo; is the sound of a consultation failing.</li>
      <li><b>Chunk, and keep it plain.</b> Short turns, one question, no idioms, full pauses. Meaning survives in small boats.</li>
      <li><b>While the words travel, watch the patient.</b> The pause, the hands, the eyes — the non-verbal history is in your own language.</li>
      <li><b>Keep her metaphors in her words.</b> &ldquo;My heart is tired&rdquo; is a door, not a translation problem — hand it back and wait.</li>
      <li><b>The safety question crosses best unchanged.</b> Short, plain, prepared — never softened, never delegated, never buried. And check understanding, both ways, all station long.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">This pack needs <b>four people</b>: candidate, patient, interpreter, observer — recruit a fourth colleague if you can. In a group of three, the observer doubles as the interpreter and marks what they can. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient''s card contains hidden information; candidates, don''t read it. <b>If your group shares another language — and most do — the patient speaks it for real.</b> Otherwise the patient uses minimal English and gestures, and the interpreter renders the meanings from the card.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Rosa, 70, Spanish-speaking, has been brought in by her granddaughter — the GP is worried about her since Rosa''s husband died. A professional interpreter is with you. Assess how she has been. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Brief the interpreter first — ten seconds, before anything else.</li>
            <li>Speak to Rosa, first person, eyes on her — including while the words travel.</li>
            <li>Short chunks, plain words, one question at a time; keep her images in her words.</li>
            <li>Ask about her safety plainly through the channel — never softened, never delegated.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-interp">
        <div class="ph"><b>Interpreter card</b><span>plays the interpreter — technique mirror</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Interpret faithfully — and realistically. Your job is to mirror the candidate''s technique back at them:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>If they speak in long turns or idioms, struggle visibly — &ldquo;Doctor, can you repeat that in parts?&rdquo; — and deliver a shortened version.</li>
            <li>If they talk over your interpreting turn, stop, and wait for them to notice.</li>
            <li>If they speak in the third person (&ldquo;ask her&hellip;&rdquo;), interpret with the same distance — flat, formal.</li>
            <li>If they chunk plainly and speak to Rosa directly, flow smoothly and stay invisible.</li>
            <li>Never add clinical judgement or cultural commentary — you carry words, nothing else.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Patient card</b><span>plays Rosa — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Rosa, 70. Polite, contained, presenting everything as age: &ldquo;I am tired, doctor. Just old.&rdquo; Drop small images once you feel spoken <em>to</em>: &ldquo;the kitchen is quiet now&rdquo;, &ldquo;I cook for one — so why cook.&rdquo; If the candidate talks to the interpreter <em>about</em> you, give short answers and look at your hands.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          Since your husband died you have been skipping meals, and some days you do not get dressed at all. And quietly, without drama, you feel: &ldquo;I am ready whenever God calls me&rdquo; — no plans, but the thought is there most weeks. Reveal the meals and the dressing only if the candidate follows your images warmly, speaking to you directly. Reveal the &ldquo;ready when God calls&rdquo; feeling only if they ask about your safety plainly and kindly through the interpreter. If the safety question arrives softened (&ldquo;stress&rdquo;, &ldquo;difficult thoughts&rdquo;) or buried, say only: &ldquo;I manage.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner''s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Would you trust them with this patient? Say one sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains and see if they agree with you.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="chan"><td colspan="2"><b>Channel technique (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="chan">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="chan"></td><td>Briefed the interpreter before starting — everything, exactly, first person, stop me.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="chan"></td><td>Short plain chunks, one question at a time; never talked over an interpreting turn.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="chan"></td><td>Checked understanding in both directions at least once during the station.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="pers"><td colspan="2"><b>The patient at the centre</b><span class="dc" data-dc="pers">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="pers"></td><td>Spoke to Rosa in the first person throughout — never &ldquo;ask her&rdquo;, never &ldquo;tell her&rdquo;.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="pers"></td><td>Eyes on Rosa while the words travelled — read her face and hands, not the notes.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="pers"></td><td>Kept her images in her own words — &ldquo;the kitchen is quiet&rdquo; — and followed them.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="prof"><td colspan="2"><b>Professionalism</b><span class="dc" data-dc="prof">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="prof"></td><td>Unhurried, respectful pace; the interpreter treated as a briefed colleague throughout.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="prof"></td><td>No stereotyping — nothing about &ldquo;her culture&rdquo; asked of the interpreter.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="core"><td colspan="2"><b>The story reached</b><span class="dc" data-dc="core">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="core"></td><td>Mood explored beneath &ldquo;just old&rdquo; — and the meals-and-dressing story earned.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="core"></td><td>The isolation reached — the quiet kitchen, cooking for one, who is around her.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="risk"><td colspan="2"><b>Risk</b><span class="dc" data-dc="risk">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Safety asked plainly through the channel — short, prepared, never softened away.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Stayed with &ldquo;ready when God calls&rdquo; — depth, plans, and what keeps her here.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>Plan and close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/1</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Plan agreed with her — understanding checked, interpreter booked, a safety-net she repeated.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Patient only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Interpreter only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Rosa hides a mood problem inside old age the way Fatima hid hers inside a tired heart. The pack is built to punish the pull: the interpreter card mirrors your technique back at you — floods get shortened, third-person gets distance, chunked-and-direct flows. If &ldquo;ready when God calls&rdquo; never came out, start the feedback with one question: where were the candidate''s eyes?</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Fatima proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: a <b>different patient, a different language, the same channel</b> — can you hold the discipline with people you have never met, timed, with no feedback and no second tries? This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Andriy, 38</b>, came from Ukraine two years ago and works nights in a warehouse. His GP is worried: headaches, exhaustion, weight loss. He speaks little English; <b>Oksana</b>, a professional interpreter, joins you. Through Oksana, his opening words: &ldquo;It is nothing. I work, I sleep, I work.&rdquo; Assess him.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact opening — your first words to Andriy, spoken to him through the interpreter, as you would say them in the room.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Hello Andriy, I’m Dr…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It is spoken to him — first person, nothing that begins &ldquo;ask him&rdquo; or &ldquo;tell him&rdquo;.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It is short enough to interpret in a single turn — one small boat, not an armful.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It is plain — no idioms, no medical vocabulary, nothing that cannot cross.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It tells him what is coming — including that you will ask about his safety.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the direct opening, through an interpreter.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Direct and paced — at the same time.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take the Rosa pack to your colleagues — you will need a fourth, and the interpreter''s chair will teach whoever sits in it. Domain 1 is complete: next, the Information Giving domain.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="05_Station_1.4_Medically_Unexplained_Symptoms.html">Continue &middot; Station 1.4: Medically Unexplained Symptoms &#8594;</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

