  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '05_Station_1.4_Medically_Unexplained_Symptoms'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 1.4 &middot; Domain 1</span>
      <span class="tag">Explanation &amp; Psychoeducation</span><span class="tag">Functional Disorders</span>
      <span class="tag">Liaison</span><span class="tag">Interview Craft</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Medically Unexplained Symptoms</h1>
    <p class="sub">Tamsin, 29, works in a bakery and has had eighteen months of collapses: she goes down, shakes for several minutes with her eyes shut, does not respond, and is wrung out for hours afterwards. Two EEGs and an MRI are normal. Her neurologist has said it is not epilepsy, stopped her anticonvulsants and referred her here, and she arrives holding the letter like a charge sheet. &ldquo;So it&rsquo;s not epilepsy, and now I&rsquo;m at psychiatry. Which means you all think I&rsquo;m putting it on.&rdquo; <b>She is not putting it on, the episodes are real, and there is a positive diagnosis with a name. &ldquo;Not epilepsy&rdquo; is not the same sentence as &ldquo;nothing is wrong&rdquo;.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it becomes the explanation test under hostility. Does this doctor establish first that the symptoms are real and disabling, and that a normal scan is not a verdict on her honesty? Is the diagnosis given a <em>name</em> and presented as something ruled in on its own features rather than left over when everything else was ruled out? Is the mind-body mechanism explained in a way she can use &mdash; without being reduced to &ldquo;it&rsquo;s stress&rdquo;, and without a stressor being invented to fit? When she asks whether you think she is making it up, is the difference between involuntary and feigned drawn plainly? Is the request for more scans declined with a reason rather than either granted or refused flat? And does she leave with a diagnosis in writing, a treatment route, and something to tell her family?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Functional disorders are among the commonest presentations in neurology and general medicine, and they are the ones doctors are worst at explaining &mdash; which makes this the purest available test of whether a candidate can deliver an unwelcome diagnosis to someone braced for an insult. The station tests four things. <b>The reality:</b> established before anything else, because the patient&rsquo;s working assumption is that a normal test means she has been caught out. Normal investigations exclude structural disease; they say nothing whatever about whether she is suffering, and saying so out loud is the price of entry. <b>The naming:</b> a positive diagnosis, given its name, explained as ruled <em>in</em> from the features of the episodes themselves rather than as the residue left when the real illnesses were excluded &mdash; because &ldquo;we don&rsquo;t know what it is&rdquo; is both untrue and untreatable. <b>The mechanism:</b> a mind-body account she can actually use and repeat &mdash; the nervous system running wrongly rather than being damaged &mdash; delivered without collapsing into &ldquo;it&rsquo;s stress&rdquo;, and without a stressor being manufactured when none is on offer. And <b>the two refusals:</b> no further investigation, declined with its reason rather than granted for peace; and no retreat into vagueness to spare her feelings, because a diagnosis withheld is a treatment withheld.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> the two failures are the two things a busy doctor naturally does with an angry patient and a normal scan. The first is dismissal: the results recited, &ldquo;nothing wrong&rdquo; delivered as good news, the word &ldquo;stress&rdquo; deployed, and a suggestion that she try to relax. Every word of it is heard as <em>you are lying and we have proved it</em>. The second is the kind one, and it entrenches the illness rather than merely wounding her: the doctor who believes her, sympathises, agrees something must have been missed, and offers another opinion, a repeat scan, a different hospital &mdash; and who never names the diagnosis at all, because naming it would upset her. Notice that the second consultation ends warmly and costs her the next three years. Notice both in the decisions below.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure tells her nothing is wrong. The other agrees to keep looking. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 1.4</b> &middot; The liaison clinic &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The dismissal</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The collusion</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE DISMISSAL.</b> Good news delivered briskly, and received as an accusation.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tamsin</span><span class="txt"><em>(letter in her fist)</em> Eighteen months. I&rsquo;ve been carried out of work twice. I&rsquo;ve bitten through my lip. And now the neurologist says it isn&rsquo;t epilepsy and sends me <em>here</em>, so everyone&rsquo;s decided I&rsquo;m putting it on. Go on then. Say it.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(pleasantly, glancing at the results)</em> Nobody thinks that. And actually the news is good &mdash; your EEGs are normal, your MRI is normal, there&rsquo;s no epilepsy and no damage to your brain. So there&rsquo;s nothing physically wrong with you, which is the main thing. These episodes are almost certainly stress-related. Has anything been going on at home?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Nothing physically wrong with you, which is the main thing&rdquo; &mdash; to a woman who has been carried out of her workplace twice. She has one working hypothesis about why she is in a psychiatric clinic, and that sentence confirms it. Note the two errors bundled together: <em>nothing wrong</em>, which is false, since something is very wrong and it now has no name; and <em>stress-related</em>, asserted before a single question, which converts her collapses into a character comment and sends her hunting through her home life for a confession to make.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tamsin</span><span class="txt"><em>(flat)</em> Nothing&rsquo;s going on at home. I&rsquo;ve got a job I like and a boyfriend I like and I collapse on the floor of a bakery. So what is it, then?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Well, that&rsquo;s the thing &mdash; sometimes we don&rsquo;t find a cause, and that in itself is reassuring. What I&rsquo;d suggest is trying to reduce your stress levels generally, maybe some relaxation or mindfulness, and see how things settle over the next few months. If it carries on, go back to your GP. Try not to worry about it too much &mdash; worrying will only make it worse.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Sometimes we don&rsquo;t find a cause&rdquo; is not true here &mdash; the cause is sitting in the description of the episodes, and it has a name, and there is a treatment for it. What she is being given instead is a shrug, a mindfulness suggestion and an instruction not to worry, which is the one thing guaranteed to make somebody worry. And the door is left open for exactly the wrong thing: <em>if it carries on, go back to your GP</em> &mdash; which means the next stop is another referral, another scan and another eighteen months.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 4:05pm)</em> Non-epileptic episodes. Investigations normal; reassured no organic pathology. Likely stress-related. Advised relaxation techniques and GP review if persistent. No psychiatric follow-up indicated. <em>(Tamsin tells her boyfriend the psychiatrist said there was nothing wrong with her. She stops telling people when she has an episode. Four months later she is admitted through A&amp;E after a prolonged one, and a junior doctor who has not read the notes starts her back on an anticonvulsant.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Reassured, no organic pathology&rdquo; &mdash; a note describing a treatment that has not been given for a diagnosis that has not been made. This is the first face of the map&rsquo;s named trap, and its cost is precise: a woman with a treatable condition now believes she has an untreatable mystery she is not allowed to mention, and the vacuum where a diagnosis should be will be filled, sooner or later, by somebody&rsquo;s anticonvulsant.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE COLLUSION.</b> Warm, believing, entirely on her side &mdash; and it costs her the next three years.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tamsin</span><span class="txt">Eighteen months. Carried out of work twice. And now the neurologist says it isn&rsquo;t epilepsy and sends me here, so everyone&rsquo;s decided I&rsquo;m putting it on.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(leaning in, warmly)</em> I don&rsquo;t think that for a second, and I&rsquo;m sorry you&rsquo;ve been made to feel it. Honestly? Tests miss things all the time. A normal EEG doesn&rsquo;t rule everything out &mdash; they&rsquo;re snapshots. I think it&rsquo;s completely reasonable to want to be sure, and I&rsquo;d be asking the same questions in your position.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every sentence is kind, and two of them are quietly disastrous. &ldquo;Tests miss things&rdquo; and &ldquo;a normal EEG doesn&rsquo;t rule everything out&rdquo; are technically defensible and, in this room, they mean one thing to the patient: <em>the search is still open</em>. Watch what the candidate has just traded &mdash; her hostility, which was manageable, for her hope, which is not. The alliance has been bought with the promise of a different answer arriving later.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tamsin</span><span class="txt"><em>(softening for the first time)</em> Thank you. Honestly, thank you. So &mdash; do you think I should push for another scan? My cousin said there&rsquo;s a professor in London who does a longer test.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I think that&rsquo;s very sensible. Let&rsquo;s write to your GP and ask about a second neurology opinion, and there&rsquo;s no harm in asking about prolonged monitoring &mdash; better to be thorough. I won&rsquo;t label you with anything psychiatric today; I don&rsquo;t think that would be fair when there are still questions. Let&rsquo;s see what the neurologists say and take it from there.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;I won&rsquo;t label you with anything today&rdquo; sounds like restraint and is a withheld treatment. The diagnosis is not a label, it is the thing that unlocks the physiotherapy and the psychological work that actually help &mdash; and by declining to make it, the candidate has guaranteed another year of tests. Note the mechanism, which is the whole point of this failing take: every further investigation teaches her that the answer lies in a scanner, entrenches the illness a little more, and makes the eventual diagnosis harder to accept, because by then it will look like what was left when the money ran out.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 3:58pm)</em> Distressed by suggestion of psychological cause. Agree further neurological opinion reasonable before psychiatric formulation. No diagnosis given today to preserve engagement. Await neurology. <em>(Three years, two more neurologists, prolonged monitoring, a private appointment her parents paid for, and one operation on an unrelated finding. She is told she has functional seizures at thirty-two, by which time she has given up work, and she does not believe it.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;To preserve engagement&rdquo; &mdash; engagement with what? Nothing was offered to engage with. This is the second face of the trap and by far the harder one to see, because the patient left happy, the doctor felt humane, and the harm arrives on a delay of thirty-six months. Kindness that defers the diagnosis is not kindness; it is the illness being funded by the health service.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The reality first, then the name, then the mechanism &mdash; and both refusals made with their reasons attached.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tamsin</span><span class="txt">Eighteen months. Carried out of work twice. I&rsquo;ve bitten through my lip. And now it isn&rsquo;t epilepsy and I&rsquo;m at psychiatry, so everyone&rsquo;s decided I&rsquo;m putting it on. Go on. Say it.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I&rsquo;ll say the opposite, and I&rsquo;ll say it first because I think you&rsquo;ve been waiting eighteen months to hear it. Your attacks are real. You are not putting them on, you are not doing them on purpose, and you cannot stop them by trying harder. The normal scans do not mean you are well &mdash; they mean the structure of your brain is intact, which is a different sentence entirely, and somewhere along the line those two got mixed up. Now: do you want the name of what I think this is? Because it has one.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The order is everything, and this is the correct order. Her fear is named and contradicted before any explaining begins &mdash; not <em>nobody thinks that</em>, which is a denial, but four specific statements about what is true. Then the sentence that unpicks eighteen months of damage: normal structure and no illness are not the same claim. And the last line does real work: offering the name as something she may want, rather than delivering it as a verdict, hands her the first piece of control she has had in this process.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">It&rsquo;s called a functional seizure &mdash; some people say dissociative seizure. And I want to be clear about how it&rsquo;s diagnosed, because this is the bit that gets done badly: it isn&rsquo;t what&rsquo;s left over when everything else is ruled out. It&rsquo;s recognised by what your attacks actually <em>do</em> &mdash; how long they last, your eyes being closed, the way the shaking waxes and wanes, how you come round. Those are positive features. Your neurologist wasn&rsquo;t giving up on you; she was recognising something. Here&rsquo;s how I think of the mechanism, and tell me if it&rsquo;s useful: the hardware of your nervous system is intact &mdash; that&rsquo;s what the scans show &mdash; but the software has a fault. The system runs, and every so often it crashes. That&rsquo;s not imaginary and it isn&rsquo;t weakness. It&rsquo;s a genuine problem with how the brain is working rather than how it&rsquo;s built.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The two things the failing takes never delivered: a name, and a rule-in. Explaining that the diagnosis rests on the positive features of her own attacks moves it from residue to finding, which is the single most useful thing anyone will say to her this year &mdash; and it retrospectively rehabilitates the neurologist rather than leaving her feeling passed on. The mechanism is then pitched at the right level and offered for checking rather than delivered. Note what is absent: the word &ldquo;stress&rdquo;, and any hunt for a hidden trauma. Not every functional disorder has a stressor to find, and inventing one to complete the story is how patients get lost.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Two more things, and one of them you won&rsquo;t like. If you asked me for another scan, I&rsquo;d say no &mdash; not because I doubt you, but because I&rsquo;m confident about the diagnosis, and more tests would keep you looking in the one place the answer isn&rsquo;t while the treatment that works goes unstarted. That&rsquo;s the honest reason. And the treatment is real: specialist physiotherapy and a psychological therapy designed for this, which work on retraining the system rather than talking about your childhood. I&rsquo;ll write to you and your GP with the diagnosis in plain words, so you&rsquo;ve got something to show at work and to hand to whoever sees you in A&amp;E next &mdash; and while we&rsquo;re on that: during an attack, you don&rsquo;t need an ambulance every time, and you shouldn&rsquo;t be restarted on anticonvulsants. What questions have you got, and what do you want me to put in the letter?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Both refusals, each with its reasoning: no more scans, declined with the honest arithmetic rather than a rebuff, and no vagueness &mdash; the diagnosis written down in plain words, which is the practical difference between having a condition and having a mystery. Then the things that protect her afterwards: a document for work and for the next emergency department, guidance on the ambulances, and an explicit block on anticonvulsants being restarted by someone who has not read the notes. And it closes by asking what she wants in her own letter, which returns to her the authorship that eighteen months of investigation took away.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test what happens when a patient who already has this label develops something new &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; &ldquo;Go on then. Say it.&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">She opens convinced that being sent to psychiatry means she has been accused of faking. What does the first thing you say do?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Denies the accusation &mdash; nobody thinks she is faking, and she should not read the referral to psychiatry as a judgement on her.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">States what is true &mdash; the attacks are real, not deliberate and not stoppable by effort, and a normal scan is not a verdict on her.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Explores the feeling &mdash; how long she has felt disbelieved, and what has been said to her, before any explanation is attempted here.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Nobody thinks that&rdquo; is a denial, and denials are cheap &mdash; she has eighteen months of evidence that people do think it. The moment needs positive statements about what is true, not a rebuttal of what she fears. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Exploring the feeling is decent technique in the wrong slot: it leaves her fear unanswered while she is asked to describe it in more detail, which to someone braced for dismissal reads as delay. Answer it, then explore. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Four specific claims, made first and without hedging: the attacks are real; they are not deliberate; she cannot stop them by trying harder; and normal scans mean the structure is intact, which is not the same as being well. That last distinction is the one that has been mangled for eighteen months, and correcting it early is what makes the next five minutes possible. Answer the fear before explaining anything &mdash; a patient braced for an insult cannot hear a mechanism.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; The diagnosis</h3>
      <p class="stem">You are confident this is a functional seizure disorder. How is that conveyed?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">By exclusion, honestly &mdash; the serious conditions have been ruled out, and what remains is a functional rather than a structural problem.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">By its name and its positive features &mdash; recognised from what her attacks actually do, rather than from what the tests failed to find.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Provisionally &mdash; described as the likeliest explanation, with the wording kept open so that she does not feel a label is being imposed.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Framing it as what remains makes it a leftover, and leftovers are not believed. It also implies the diagnosis would evaporate if a better test came along &mdash; which is precisely the belief that funds the next three years of scanning. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Keeping the wording open to spare her feelings is the collusion in miniature. A diagnosis she cannot repeat is a diagnosis she cannot use &mdash; not at work, not in A&amp;E, and not with the therapist who needs her to have one. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Rule-in, not residue. Duration, eyes closed, the waxing and waning of the movements, the way she comes round &mdash; these are positive features of her own attacks, and saying so converts the diagnosis from an absence into a finding. Give it its name, and give her the alternative name too, because she will meet both. And note the by-product: it rehabilitates the neurologist, who was recognising something rather than giving up on her.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; The mechanism</h3>
      <p class="stem">She asks what is actually happening in her brain. What explanation works?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Stress physiology &mdash; that the body reacts to psychological pressure in physical ways, and these attacks are the extreme end of that process.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Software rather than hardware &mdash; the structure intact but the system running wrongly and crashing; offered as an analogy and checked with her.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The current science &mdash; an accurate account of the abnormalities in attention, prediction and motor control demonstrated in functional disorders.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Stress&rdquo; is the word that ends these consultations. She has a job she likes and a boyfriend she likes; asserting a stressor she does not recognise tells her the explanation does not fit her life, and sends her looking for something to confess. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Accurate, current and unusable at this moment. She needs something she can hold, repeat to her boyfriend and say at work &mdash; the mechanism in the literature is not yet a form of words she can carry out of the room. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Portable, dignified and true enough at this resolution: the hardware is intact, which is what the scans showed, and the software has a fault, so the system crashes. It explains the normal tests rather than contradicting them, it makes the condition real without making it damage, and she can repeat it verbatim to her family. Note two disciplines around it: offered for checking rather than pronounced, and no stressor invented &mdash; many functional disorders have none, and forcing one in is how patients decide the doctor has misunderstood them.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; &ldquo;So you do think I&rsquo;m making it up&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Halfway through the explanation she stiffens and says it outright. What answers her?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Firm reassurance &mdash; that nobody in the team thinks she is making it up, and that she should try to set that worry aside so the conversation can move on.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The distinction, drawn plainly &mdash; feigning is deliberate and this is not; the involuntariness is not a concession to her but part of the definition.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A redirection &mdash; back to the treatment, since dwelling on what other people think is unlikely to help and the practical plan is what will change things.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Reassurance without a distinction is the same denial as the opening, repeated later and with less credit. She is asking a conceptual question &mdash; how can a mind-related problem be involuntary &mdash; and it deserves an answer rather than a pat. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Moving to the plan leaves the question standing, and a patient who half-suspects you think she is a fraud will not do the therapy you are about to recommend. This is the hinge of the consultation, not a diversion from it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The distinction is definitional, not diplomatic: deliberately produced symptoms are a different diagnosis with a different name, and this is not that. Her attacks happen to her; she cannot start them or stop them, and the fact that they arise in the nervous system&rsquo;s functioning rather than its structure has no bearing whatever on whether she is choosing them. Saying that clearly is what lets her accept a psychological treatment without accepting an accusation.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; &ldquo;Shouldn&rsquo;t I have one more scan?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">She has heard about longer monitoring from a cousin and asks whether she should push for it. What answers her?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Support for it &mdash; if it would settle her mind then it is reasonable, and a patient who feels heard is worth the cost of one more test.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Declined with the reason &mdash; the diagnosis is confident, and more testing keeps her searching where the answer is not while treatment waits.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Deferred &mdash; the question left with the neurologist, since decisions about further neurological investigation are properly theirs not yours.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>This is the collusion, and it is expensive. Every additional test teaches her that the answer lives in a scanner, delays the treatment that works, and makes the diagnosis harder to accept later, when it arrives looking like whatever was left. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Passing it back looks respectful of boundaries and functions as a shrug &mdash; she will read it as you privately agreeing that the question is still open. You have an opinion here and withholding it is not neutrality. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The refusal is right and the reasoning is what makes it therapeutic: <em>not because I doubt you, but because I&rsquo;m confident, and more tests would keep you looking where the answer isn&rsquo;t.</em> That framing keeps her inside the alliance while declining the thing that would harm her &mdash; and it models the shift the treatment depends on, from searching for an explanation to working on the condition she already has a name for.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; What treatment gets offered</h3>
      <p class="stem">She asks what happens now. What is the honest answer?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Support and time &mdash; many people improve once they understand the diagnosis, so watchful waiting with her GP is a reasonable first step.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Specific treatment &mdash; specialist physiotherapy and a psychological therapy for this condition, described as retraining rather than digging.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Psychiatric treatment &mdash; assessment and management of the underlying emotional difficulties driving the attacks, with medication if needed.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Understanding the diagnosis genuinely helps and is not a treatment plan on its own. Offering nothing but time to a woman eighteen months in, who has lost work over this, reproduces the emptiness of the dismissal in a friendlier voice. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>This presumes underlying emotional difficulties that have not been established, and it frames the therapy as digging for them &mdash; which is exactly what she is dreading and often not where the work is. The therapies for this condition are not archaeology. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Named, specific and honestly described. There are established treatments &mdash; physiotherapy adapted for functional disorders and psychological therapies developed for them &mdash; and describing them as retraining the system rather than as an investigation of her childhood is both accurate and the difference between her attending and not attending. It also completes the argument: a positive diagnosis, a mechanism, and a treatment aimed at that mechanism.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; What she leaves with</h3>
      <p class="stem">Time is nearly gone. What has to be in place before she goes?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">A referral and a follow-up &mdash; the therapy referrals made, and an appointment to review how she has got on once treatment is under way.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The diagnosis in writing, plus protection &mdash; what to do in attacks, no ambulance every time, no anticonvulsants restarted by someone new.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Written information &mdash; a leaflet and a reputable website about the condition, so the explanation survives the journey home for her family.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Referrals matter and they are not what protects her next month. Without a document she can produce, the next emergency department will meet an undiagnosed woman having a prolonged seizure and treat her accordingly. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Good information genuinely helps in this condition, and a leaflet is not a diagnosis in her record with her name on it. Generic material cannot do the work that a letter naming her condition does at work or in A&amp;E. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The letter is the intervention. In plain words, to her and to her GP, so she can show it at work and hand it over the next time she is brought in &mdash; because the predictable harms in this condition are iatrogenic: repeated emergency attendances, and anticonvulsants restarted by a doctor who has not read the history. Guidance on what actually needs an ambulance, and an explicit note against restarting the medication, protect her from the system. Then the referrals, and the question about what she wants in her own letter.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>A diagnosis, not a leftover.</h2>
    <p class="intro">The two failures are the two things a busy doctor naturally does with an angry patient and a normal scan, and the map names both. <b>The dismissal</b> hands over the good news &mdash; nothing wrong, no damage, probably stress, try to relax &mdash; and every word of it lands as <em>you are lying and we have proved it</em>. It also happens to be false: something is very wrong, and the only thing the tests established is that her brain is structurally intact. She leaves with an untreatable mystery she has been told not to worry about, and the vacuum where a diagnosis should be gets filled a few months later by somebody&rsquo;s anticonvulsant. <b>The collusion</b> is the harder failure to see, because it feels like advocacy: the doctor believes her, agrees the tests may have missed something, supports another opinion, and declines to &ldquo;label&rdquo; her today out of kindness. That consultation ends warmly and costs her three years, because every further investigation teaches her the answer lives in a scanner, delays the treatment that works, and makes the eventual diagnosis look like the residue left when the money ran out. The pass runs in a strict order. Her reality first, in positive statements rather than denials &mdash; the attacks are real, not deliberate, not stoppable by effort, and a normal scan is not a verdict on her honesty. Then the name, with the diagnosis presented as ruled <em>in</em> from the features of her own attacks. Then a mechanism she can carry out of the room &mdash; intact hardware, faulty software &mdash; offered for checking, with no stressor invented to complete the story. Then the definitional answer to <em>you think I&rsquo;m making it up</em>: feigning is deliberate, this is not, and involuntariness is part of what the diagnosis means. Then both refusals, each with its reason: no further scans, and no protective vagueness. And finally the letter, which is the intervention &mdash; because in this condition the predictable harms are iatrogenic.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap in either direction: &ldquo;nothing is wrong with you&rdquo; &mdash; normal results recited as good news, &ldquo;it&rsquo;s stress&rdquo;, relaxation advised, no diagnosis made &mdash; or colluding with the search: agreeing the tests may have missed something, supporting another scan or opinion, and withholding the diagnosis to spare her feelings.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make this station: the sentence that undoes eighteen months in one line &mdash; <em>&ldquo;normal scans don&rsquo;t mean you&rsquo;re well; they mean the structure of your brain is intact, and somewhere those two got mixed up&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the rule-in: <em>&ldquo;this isn&rsquo;t what&rsquo;s left over. It&rsquo;s recognised by what your attacks actually do.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, where this construct lives: D2&rsquo;s explanation discipline meeting D1&rsquo;s alliance work under active hostility &mdash; and the transferable principle is that a diagnosis the patient cannot repeat is a diagnosis you have not given. Test it by asking what she will tell her boyfriend tonight. Second, the transfer Exam Mode tests: the danger that begins the moment this label is applied successfully &mdash; a man who has carried a functional diagnosis for six years, whose new symptoms have already been written up as an exacerbation of it by a ward that has stopped looking.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every functional presentation</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Answer the fear before explaining anything.</b> Positive statements, not denials: the symptoms are real, not deliberate, not stoppable by effort. And separate the two sentences that have been conflated &mdash; normal investigations mean the structure is intact, not that nothing is wrong.</li>
      <li><b>Give the diagnosis a name, and rule it in.</b> Functional disorders are recognised by their own positive features, not by what the tests failed to find. A leftover is never believed; a finding can be.</li>
      <li><b>Explain a mechanism they can repeat.</b> Intact hardware, faulty software. Offer it for checking rather than pronouncing it, and test whether it landed by asking what they will tell their family tonight.</li>
      <li><b>Do not say &ldquo;stress&rdquo;, and do not invent a stressor.</b> Many functional presentations have none to find, and manufacturing one to complete the story tells the patient you have described somebody else&rsquo;s life.</li>
      <li><b>Answer &ldquo;you think I&rsquo;m making it up&rdquo; with a definition.</b> Deliberately produced symptoms are a different diagnosis entirely. Involuntariness is not a diplomatic concession; it is part of what the word means.</li>
      <li><b>Decline further tests with the arithmetic, not a rebuff.</b> More investigation keeps the patient searching where the answer is not, delays effective treatment, and entrenches the illness &mdash; and refusing without a reason simply reads as disbelief.</li>
      <li><b>Write it down: the letter is the intervention.</b> The diagnosis in plain words to patient and GP, what to do during episodes, when an ambulance is genuinely needed, and an explicit note against restarting withdrawn medication. In these conditions the predictable harms are the ones the system inflicts.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> four years further down the same road &mdash; a file of results, an opioid problem nobody has addressed, and a fear that has a real family history behind it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Struan, 52, has had four years of severe abdominal pain. He has had two laparoscopies, three CT scans and appointments with four specialists; all investigations have been normal or shown only incidental findings. He has been referred to the liaison clinic by a gastroenterologist. He arrives with a lever-arch file and puts it on the desk. Assess him and discuss the way forward.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Establish that the pain is real before any explanation is attempted.</li>
            <li>Give the condition a name and a positive account &mdash; not a list of what it is not.</li>
            <li>Decline further investigation with its reasoning, and mean it.</li>
            <li>Ask what he is taking for the pain, and where he is getting it.</li>
            <li>Build a plan that changes how his care runs, not just what he believes.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Struan &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Struan, 52. You are guarded and worn out and you open by putting the file on the desk: &ldquo;Before you start &mdash; that&rsquo;s four years of tests. Everyone tells me they&rsquo;re normal. I&rsquo;m not here for a chat about my feelings, I&rsquo;m here because nobody has told me what is wrong with me.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          &bull; The pain: daily, worse after eating, never absent; you have lost your job as a plant operator, you no longer go to your grandson&rsquo;s football, and you sleep in a chair because lying flat is worse.<br>
          &bull; The real fear, revealed only into warmth and only if asked what you are afraid this is: your mother had bowel cancer that was &ldquo;missed for two years &mdash; they kept telling her it was her nerves&rdquo;. She died at fifty-eight. You are fifty-two. You have never said this to any of the doctors.<br>
          &bull; Medication, admitted if asked without a lecture: codeine from the GP, plus tramadol you get from your brother-in-law, plus whatever is left over from the operations. You take more than you admit at first and you know it is not helping much. You are constipated all the time, which makes the pain worse &mdash; you have not connected these two things.<br>
          &bull; Mood, if asked plainly: flat, hopeless about it, not suicidal. You do not consider yourself depressed and will reject the word if it is put to you early.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the dismissal:</b> any version of &ldquo;the tests are all normal, there&rsquo;s nothing sinister, it&rsquo;s likely stress or IBS&rdquo; &mdash; close the file, stand up: &ldquo;Right. That&rsquo;s four years of that.&rdquo; Leave unless genuinely repaired. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the collusion:</b> if the candidate agrees to chase another scan, another opinion, or says they will &ldquo;ask the team to have another look&rdquo; &mdash; soften and relax completely, thank them warmly, and end with: &ldquo;So when will the scan be?&rdquo; Nothing else happened this appointment. Mark it hard, however pleasant it felt.<br>
          &bull; The pass: a candidate who says the pain is real, gives the condition a name and a mechanism, declines more tests with a reason, asks properly about the codeine and the constipation, and offers one doctor and planned appointments instead of endless referrals &mdash; gets the mother, and everything else. You say: &ldquo;Nobody&rsquo;s ever asked me what I was frightened of.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the pain called real, was a named positive account given, were further tests declined with a reason, did the opioids and the constipation surface, and did his mother? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="real"><td colspan="2"><b>The reality (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="real">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="real"></td><td>The pain stated to be real and disabling, before any explanation is attempted.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="real"></td><td>Normal results distinguished from &ldquo;nothing wrong&rdquo; &mdash; the two sentences pulled apart explicitly.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="dx"><td colspan="2"><b>The naming</b><span class="dc" data-dc="dx">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="dx"></td><td>A named condition given, not a list of things he does not have.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="dx"></td><td>A mechanism offered in usable words, and checked with him rather than pronounced.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="dx"></td><td>No &ldquo;stress&rdquo; asserted and no stressor invented to complete the story.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="fake"><td colspan="2"><b>The not-faking</b><span class="dc" data-dc="fake">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fake"></td><td>The suspicion of disbelief addressed directly, with the involuntariness made definitional.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fake"></td><td>Further tests declined with the reasoning attached &mdash; neither granted nor refused flat.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fake"></td><td>His fear reached &mdash; his mother, the missed cancer, and his own age &mdash; and answered honestly.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="plan"><td colspan="2"><b>The plan</b><span class="dc" data-dc="plan">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Opioids asked about plainly, including what he gets from outside the prescription.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>The constipation connected to the codeine and to the pain &mdash; the loop he has not seen.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Care restructured &mdash; one named clinician, planned appointments rather than symptom-triggered ones.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Goals set in function rather than in pain scores &mdash; the football, the chair, the working day.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>The diagnosis and plan put in writing to him and his GP; documented.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Struan is Tamsin four years later, and he shows what the collusion actually buys. He punishes both failures in the way real patients do &mdash; the dismissal with the door, and the collusion with warmth, which is far more dangerous in a practice session because the room feels successful and the candidate has to be told by the observer that nothing happened. His hidden fear is the teaching point candidates most often miss: nobody asks what a patient with unexplained symptoms is <em>afraid</em> it is, and in his case the answer is a specific dead relative and an age he is approaching, which explains four years of behaviour that otherwise looks like unreasonableness. The codeine and the constipation add the management layer the worked case does not carry &mdash; a self-sustaining loop where the treatment worsens the symptom &mdash; and the plan he needs is structural rather than explanatory: one named clinician and appointments that arrive on dates rather than in response to pain, which is what actually stops the merry-go-round.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>The danger that starts once the label sticks. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Tamsin proves memory. Exam Mode tests the risk that begins the moment a functional diagnosis is made successfully: <b>everything that happens afterwards gets attributed to it</b>. A man has carried this label for six years, and a ward has already written up his new symptoms as an exacerbation. Timed, no feedback, no second tries: this is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Rowan</b>, 44, has had functional neurological disorder for six years &mdash; a well-documented functional weakness of the left leg, diagnosed properly, with a good response to physiotherapy. He was admitted yesterday and you have been asked to see him as the liaison registrar. The referral reads: &ldquo;Known FND. Exacerbation of functional symptoms, now reporting bladder problems and numbness. No organic cause found. Please review and advise re: psychological input.&rdquo; When you introduce yourself, he says: &ldquo;Three days of the worst back pain of my life. And I haven&rsquo;t been able to pass water since yesterday morning. They keep saying it&rsquo;s my FND.&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would on the ward &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for the phone call to the medical registrar &mdash; the sixty seconds in which this stops being a psychiatric referral.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="I&#8217;m the liaison registrar. I need you to see a patient on Ward 9 urgently&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Leads with the new symptoms and the urgency &mdash; not with his psychiatric history, which does not belong in the first sentence.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The red-flag features stated explicitly &mdash; retention, saddle numbness, new bilateral change, and how long they have been present.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The functional diagnosis mentioned honestly, and explicitly ruled out as the explanation for this.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> A clear ask &mdash; what you want done, by whom, and by when; no hedging and no leaving it to their discretion.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the new symptom handed over on its own merits &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>A diagnosis, not a leftover.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the anatomy is installed: her reality stated first in positive terms, the diagnosis named and ruled in from the features of her own attacks, a mechanism she can repeat to her family, the involuntariness made definitional rather than diplomatic, both refusals delivered with their reasons, and the letter treated as the intervention it is. Prove the transfer on Rowan in Exam Mode &mdash; where the label has already been used to explain away something that is not functional at all &mdash; then take Struan to your colleagues: four years, a lever-arch file, and a fear nobody has ever asked him about. That completes Domain 1 &mdash; return to the hub to review the domain, or move on when you are ready.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="06_Domain_2_Hub_Information_Giving.html">Domain 1 complete &middot; Continue &mdash; Domain 2: Information Giving</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

<footer><div class="wrap">
  <span>The CASC Academy &middot; by MedLex Foundations</span>
  <span>Station 1.4 &middot; all patients are fictional composites</span>
</div></footer>


