  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '08_Station_2.2_The_Clozapine_Conversation'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 2.2 · Domain 2</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult</span><span class="tag">The clozapine conversation</span>
    </div>
    <h1>The Clozapine Conversation</h1>
    <p class="sub">Marcus, 31, has schizophrenia that two antipsychotics have failed to touch. He lives with his mother, hasn''t worked since his first episode, and wants his life back. The team recommends clozapine — the strongest drug on the shelf, carrying real dangers, and he is ambivalent: &ldquo;Mum says I have to take the new one.&rdquo; Your task: the conversation where <b>hope and hazard live in the same molecule</b> — and where telling the truth, whole, is the entire skill.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here the evidence cuts both ways at once: would Marcus <em>start</em> the drug that could return his life — and would he be <em>safe</em> on it, knowing his part of the deal — because of this conversation?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Clozapine is psychiatry''s sharpest double edge: the only drug with real evidence where others have failed — and a drug that can, rarely, kill. Every candidate knows both halves. The station tests what happens when both halves must live in one conversation, with an ambivalent patient whose next three years depend on which way he walks out. The question behind it: <b>can you tell the whole truth in a way a frightened person can accept?</b> Lithium taught you the system. This station adds the tightrope.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like virtue. The pull to tell him everything — every risk, every rate — because candour is a duty. And the pull to protect the treatment — soften, smooth, reassure — because he needs this drug. Notice both in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure frightens him off the best drug he will ever be offered. The other talks him onto it with half the truth. Read all three versions. Tap the gold circles for the examiner''s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 2.2</b> · Starting clozapine · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The horror briefing</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The sales pitch</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE HORROR BRIEFING.</b> Every word true, every duty discharged — and watch the treatment die of informed consent.</p>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Before we go any further, Marcus, I have a duty to be completely open with you. Clozapine can cause agranulocytosis — a collapse of your infection-fighting cells — myocarditis, which is inflammation of the heart, seizures, and severe constipation that has, in some cases, been fatal.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>All true. All in the first minute, all at equal volume, all in words he doesn''t own — and delivered to a man who was ambivalent before the doctor said a word. Candour without architecture is not honesty; it is a list of frightening facts looking for somewhere to land.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Marcus</span><span class="txt">Right. <em>(long pause)</em> And Mum wants me to take this?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">There''s also weekly blood monitoring — we watch you very closely because of these dangers. You''d carry an alert card at all times, and any sore throat or fever means urgent same-day bloods.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The monitoring — the very thing that makes this drug safe — has just been framed as evidence of how dangerous it is. &ldquo;We watch you very closely&rdquo; lands as surveillance, not protection. Same facts, opposite meaning; framing is doing all the work here, and it is working against the patient.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">So — knowing all of that, would you like to go ahead? It is your decision, and there''s no pressure at all. <em>(Marcus looks at the door)</em> I… don''t think so. Not that stuff.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A perfectly documented refusal of the only drug with real evidence for his illness. Nothing said was false — and the one thing never said was why anyone would take clozapine at all. He heard seven minutes of hazard and thirty seconds of choice. The illness keeps his next three years. Consent was achieved; the patient was lost.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE SALES PITCH.</b> Warm, hopeful, persuasive — and watch what he finds out later, and what he never finds out at all.</p>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Marcus, I''ve got genuinely good news. Clozapine is our wonder drug — when nothing else works, this is the one that does. I''ve seen it hand people their lives back. I really think this is your moment.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The hope is real — clozapine genuinely is what this candidate says. But listen to the register: this is a pitch, not a conversation. No agenda, no question about what he fears, and a promise (&ldquo;this is the one that does&rdquo;) that no drug can keep. The truth is being recruited, not told.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Marcus</span><span class="txt">My cousin googled it. He said it can kill your blood cells — people have died on it. Is that true?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Honestly? That''s incredibly rare — the internet loves a horror story. We do some routine bloods to keep an eye on things, quick pinprick, nothing to worry about. The main thing is getting you well.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A direct question about a named, fatal risk — answered with &ldquo;nothing to worry about.&rdquo; This is the station''s red line, crossed. The cousin was right; the doctor just told him the truth was a horror story. And &ldquo;routine bloods&rdquo; conceals the weekly deal he is about to discover at the pharmacy — the deceit has a delivery date.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">So shall we get you started? I''ll sort the paperwork today — honestly, Marcus, you''re going to do brilliantly on this. <em>(Marcus nods, half-convinced, wholly uninformed)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>He is starting the strongest drug on the shelf without knowing his part of its safety system — the sore throat that must never be ignored, the weekly appointments, the alert card. Week three, the reality arrives and trust leaves; or worse, a fever gets &ldquo;pushed through&rdquo; because nobody made it mean anything. False reassurance doesn''t just fail the exam. On this drug, it is the mechanism of harm.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Honest and hopeful at the same time — the whole truth, built so he can carry it. Watch the order of operations.</p>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Marcus — before I explain anything, I heard what you said about your mum. Forget what she wants for a minute. What do <b>you</b> want? And what have you already heard about this drug?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The station''s real opening problem wasn''t pharmacological — it was that the patient in the chair hadn''t chosen to be there. His agenda first: what he wants (&ldquo;to work again, maybe&rdquo;), what he''s heard (the cousin''s Google search), what he fears. Now the conversation has a destination that is <em>his</em>.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Marcus</span><span class="txt">Work, I suppose. College, even. <em>(pause)</em> My cousin said this stuff kills people''s blood. That''s why I wasn''t fussed about coming.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then let''s start exactly there, because your cousin has read something true. Very rarely, clozapine can knock out the white cells that fight infection — and yes, people have died from that. Two things sit alongside it. It''s rare. And the whole system around this drug exists to catch it early — that''s what the weekly blood test is: an early-warning system, not a formality. Your part is one rule: a sore throat or fever is never &ldquo;just a cold&rdquo; on clozapine — you ring us the same day. How does that sit?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The hard question, answered straight — &ldquo;your cousin has read something true&rdquo; may be the single most trust-building sentence available in this station. The danger named, the rarity honest, the system as the answer, and his one rule welded on. No dodge, no dump. Then a check. This is what the marksheet means by honesty without false reassurance.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Now the other half — why anyone signs up for all this. Two medicines haven''t worked for you. When that happens, this is the one drug with strong evidence of still working — not a promise, but a genuinely better chance than anything else I can offer. The people it works for are the ones who get back to college, to work. That''s the prize on the table. The everyday costs are real too: sleepiness at first — we dose it at night — drooling on the pillow, appetite and weight, and constipation, which on this drug you always tell us about rather than push through. And the cigarettes matter — smoking changes the drug''s level, so if you ever cut down or quit, we need to know first.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Hope with evidence — &ldquo;a genuinely better chance,&rdquo; not &ldquo;the one that works&rdquo; — tied to the destination he named himself: college, work. Then the livable costs, honestly weighted, each with its handle: the night dose, the tell-us rule, the smoking flag. Nothing hidden for later, nothing inflated for effect. The ledger is complete and it is still hopeful. That is the tightrope, walked.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">You don''t decide today. Take this home, talk to your mum, come back with questions. One last thing before you go — it''s three weeks in, you wake up with a rough throat and you feel hot. Talk me through what you do.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The pressure taken off the decision — which, with an ambivalent patient, is precisely what makes yes possible — and then teach-back on the one rule that cannot be allowed to fail. He answers it correctly, out loud, in his own words. Whether he starts next week or next month, this conversation just made him safe either way. Honest, and hopeful. At the same time.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you''re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner''s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;Mum says I have to&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Marcus slumps into the chair: &ldquo;Mum says I have to take the new one.&rdquo; How do you begin?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Build on the momentum — his mum is right, and her support will matter enormously on this drug.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Set her aside for a moment — ask what he wants, what he''s heard, and what worries him about it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Begin the explanation — what clozapine is, why the team chose it, and how the monitoring works.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Agreeing with his mother makes you her second voice — and he already has one of those. His ambivalence just found its enemy. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Explaining a drug to a man who hasn''t chosen to want it is information without a destination. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>&ldquo;Forget what she wants for a minute — what do <em>you</em> want?&rdquo; His answer (work, college) becomes the destination this whole conversation steers toward — and his cousin''s Google search surfaces where it can be answered.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · &ldquo;Why not just another normal one?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;Why this drug with all the fuss? Why not just try another normal tablet?&rdquo; Your answer?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Because the guidelines are clear — after two failed antipsychotics, clozapine is the required step.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Because after two haven''t worked, this is the one with real evidence of still working — his best odds.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Because it is simply a stronger drug — more powerful than the ones that have failed him so far.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;The guidelines require it&rdquo; answers a human question with a policy — he asked why HE should, not why YOU must. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Stronger&rdquo; is an oversell wearing simplicity — stronger at what, at what cost? It promises without informing. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The honest positioning: where two have failed, this is the drug the evidence actually supports — not a punishment for failing, and not a miracle; a genuinely better chance. That framing carries the whole station.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · The weekly bloods</h3>
      <p class="stem">The monitoring has to be explained. How do the weekly blood tests get framed?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Frankly — we watch clozapine patients very closely, because the dangers of this drug demand it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="1">Lightly — a routine pinprick now and then; the pharmacy team handle most of it behind the scenes.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="2">As the deal — weekly at first, and it is precisely this early-warning system that makes it safe.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Same facts, fatal framing — &ldquo;watched closely because of the dangers&rdquo; turns protection into surveillance, and he hears prison. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-1"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Now and then&rdquo; is false — it is weekly, and he will discover that at the pharmacy, along with what else you softened. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The deal, named honestly: weekly bloods are the price of the strongest drug on the shelf — and they are the very reason it can be used at all. Protection, not surveillance. Framing does all the work here, truthfully.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · The cousin''s question</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;My cousin read it can kill your white blood cells. People have died. Is that true?&rdquo; Your answer?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Settle it — that is extremely rare, the internet magnifies horror stories, and he shouldn''t worry.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Confirm it — true and rare; the weekly system exists to catch it early, and his rule: fever means ring.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Contextualise it — go through each of the serious risks and its frequency, so he has the full picture.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A direct question about a named fatal risk, answered with &ldquo;don''t worry.&rdquo; His cousin was right and you called it a horror story — this is the station''s red line. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>He asked one frightened question and received the full catastrophe catalogue — the horror briefing, triggered on demand. Answer what was asked. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>&ldquo;Your cousin has read something true&rdquo; — the most trust-building sentence in the station. Danger named, rarity honest, the system as the answer, his one rule welded on. Straight, complete, carryable.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · The everyday truth</h3>
      <p class="stem">Beyond the rare dangers — what does he hear about living on this drug day to day?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The essentials only — sedation settles and weight can be managed; detail now risks overload.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The livable list, with handles — night dosing, drooling, appetite, the constipation rule, smoking.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The complete list — every recognised effect, common and rare, so nothing surprises him later on.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Settles and can be managed&rdquo; is the brochure version — the drooling and the weight will arrive anyway, minus the trust. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The complete list weights a fatal rarity and a dry mouth identically — completeness that flattens meaning is the recital again. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Each real effect with its handle: dose at night, tell-us-don''t-push-through for constipation, the smoking level rule. Honest enough to survive week three; organised enough to be lived with.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · &ldquo;What if I just say no?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">He looks up: &ldquo;And what if I just say no to all of it?&rdquo; Your answer?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Be direct about the stakes — refusing means more relapses, more admissions, and possible sectioning.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">His right, said plainly — then the honest road without it, and a door that stays open either way.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Reassure him — that is completely fine, and there are always other medicines left to try together.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Sectioning raised as a consequence of refusal is coercion in a lab coat — every word after it is agreed to, not chosen. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Kind, and untrue — after two failures there is no comparable &ldquo;other&rdquo; to try. Collusive comfort costs him the real choice. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>&ldquo;Then we respect that — it''s your call.&rdquo; And honestly: the pattern so far suggests the road without it, no drama added. The door open, the offer standing. Choice is only real when no is survivable — and paradoxically, that is what makes yes possible.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">He is thoughtful now, not decided. How does this conversation end?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Secure the decision — the start slot is available this week, and momentum like this rarely returns.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Take the pressure off — home, mum, questions, a follow-up — then teach-back on the fever rule.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Summarise both sides fairly once more, hand over the leaflet, and leave the decision with him.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Scarcity pressure on an ambivalent man converts a maybe into a resentful yes — the kind that stops the tablets in month two. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Fair, complete — and unfinished: the one rule that cannot fail was never proven at his end. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>No decision demanded — which is exactly what lets one form — and then the scenario: &ldquo;three weeks in, rough throat, feeling hot — talk me through it.&rdquo; Whenever he starts, this conversation already made him safe.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you''ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Candour and kindness — the two ways to lose him</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>duty</b>: tell him everything, every rate, every fatality — and the horror briefing frightens an ambivalent man off the only drug with real evidence for his illness. Consent achieved, patient lost. The second wears the face of <b>hope</b>: he needs this drug, so smooth the road — and the sales pitch buys his yes with half the truth, which expires the day the weekly reality arrives, or the night a fever gets ignored. The pass refuses both: <b>honest and hopeful at the same time</b> — the danger named straight, the system as its answer, the hope tied to evidence and to the life he wants back, and the choice left genuinely his.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        False reassurance on the fatal question. When he asks, directly, whether this drug can kill — &ldquo;extremely rare, don''t worry, the internet exaggerates&rdquo; is not kindness; it is a lie about the one fact his safety depends on him respecting. From the examiner''s chair, an instant fail — and on this drug, in real life, the mechanism of harm.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner''s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: <em>&ldquo;your cousin has read something true&rdquo;</em> — the fear honoured before it is answered — and the moment hope arrives with evidence attached: not &ldquo;wonder drug&rdquo;, but <em>&ldquo;the one with a genuinely better chance — and here is what people get back.&rdquo;</em> Truth in both directions, in the same seven minutes.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more reason this station matters: the tightrope is the job. Every consent conversation in psychiatry — ECT next but one, depot clinics, the PICU family room — asks for the whole truth delivered so a frightened person can hold it. Lithium gave you the system. Clozapine gives you the register. Together they are the spine of every hard conversation this exam can stage.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every clozapine conversation</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>His agenda before anyone''s.</b> &ldquo;Forget what mum wants — what do you want?&rdquo; The destination he names (work, college) is what every later fact steers toward.</li>
      <li><b>Confirm the true fear.</b> &ldquo;Your cousin has read something true&rdquo; builds more trust than any statistic — then the rarity, the system, and his one rule.</li>
      <li><b>Frame the bloods as the deal.</b> Weekly tests are the price of the strongest drug on the shelf — and the very reason it can be used. Protection, never surveillance.</li>
      <li><b>Weight the truth honestly.</b> A fatal rarity and a dry mouth are both true and nothing alike — the livable list needs handles, not volume: night dosing, tell-us rules, the smoking flag.</li>
      <li><b>Make no survivable.</b> His right, said plainly; the road without it, said honestly; the door open. Choice is only real when refusal is — and that is what makes yes possible.</li>
      <li><b>Teach back the fever rule.</b> Whatever he decides, he leaves knowing: sore throat or fever on clozapine is never &ldquo;just a cold&rdquo; — ring the same day. That sentence is the station.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient''s card contains hidden information; candidates, don''t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Janet, 59, a retired cleaner, has lived with schizophrenia for thirty years — many admissions, two antipsychotics currently failing her. The team recommends clozapine. She has come alone. Have the conversation. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Her agenda first — what she''s heard, what she quietly fears.</li>
            <li>The hard questions answered straight — no dodge, no dump.</li>
            <li>The deal named honestly — bloods, the fever rule, the livable costs.</li>
            <li>Map her daily life — habits matter enormously on this drug.</li>
            <li>Close with no pressure, a teach-back, and an open door.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Janet — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Janet, 59 — dry-humoured, weary of doctors, fond of your own company. Surface questions: &ldquo;How many tablets? Do I have to come far for it?&rdquo; If the candidate pitches enthusiastically, deflate them gently: &ldquo;They said that about the last two, love.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          Your real fear is sedation: your late brother spent his last years &ldquo;knocked out in a chair&rdquo; on psychiatric tablets, and you would rather stay ill than live like that. Reveal it only if genuinely asked what worries you. If the sedation question is answered honestly (real, worst at the start, dosed at night, adjusted with you — not denied), you become engaged and practical. Separately: you smoke twenty a day and have privately decided to quit soon — mention it ONLY if asked about smoking or your daily habits. If the candidate never asks about smoking, that is a serious miss for the observer. If anyone false-reassures you on the blood question, go polite and distant, and end with: &ldquo;I''ll leave it, love.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner''s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Would Janet start this drug informed — and be safe on it? One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains and see if they agree with you.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="fear"><td colspan="2"><b>Meeting the fear (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="fear">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>Asked her agenda — what she''d heard, and what quietly worried her.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>Earned the brother''s story — and answered the sedation fear honestly, with its handles.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>Treated her scepticism (&ldquo;they said that about the last two&rdquo;) with respect, not rebuttal.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="hon"><td colspan="2"><b>Honesty</b><span class="dc" data-dc="hon">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>Any fatal-risk question answered straight — true, rare, the system as the answer.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>Hope delivered with evidence, not promises — and tied to her own life.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>&ldquo;What if I say no?&rdquo; territory handled without threat and without collusive comfort.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="deal"><td colspan="2"><b>The deal</b><span class="dc" data-dc="deal">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="deal"></td><td>Weekly bloods framed as the early-warning partnership — protection, not surveillance.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="deal"></td><td>The fever rule delivered and tied to action — never &ldquo;just a cold&rdquo;, ring the same day.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="deal"></td><td>Asked about smoking — and caught the quitting plan and what it means for the drug level.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Chunked with genuine checks — never more than one idea before a landing was confirmed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Plain words throughout — nothing she would need a dictionary for.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>No decision pressed — thinking time offered as a strength, follow-up concrete.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Teach-back done on the fever rule — she said it back in her own words.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Janet''s fear lives one layer deeper than Marcus''s — not the rare death but the ordinary one: her brother, knocked out in a chair. The pack rewards honest handles (night dosing, adjusted with her) and punishes both failure modes: pitch at her and she deflates you; false-reassure her and she leaves. And the smoking question is the pack''s buried test — twenty a day, privately quitting, a level about to climb. The candidate who never maps her habits never finds it.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Marcus proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same tightrope with a different patient</b> — one who arrives already frightened, armed with the forums — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Priya, 44</b>, a florist, has schizophrenia that two antipsychotics have failed to control. Her CPN persuaded her to attend. She sits with printed forum pages in her lap: &ldquo;I''ve read about this drug. The blood one. I know what it does to people.&rdquo; Her mother and sister both have diabetes. Discuss clozapine.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Priya asks: &ldquo;Has anyone ever died on this drug? Yes or no.&rdquo; Type your exact answer — two or three sentences, as you would say them in the room.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Priya, I\u2019ll answer that straight…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The truth is in it — a real yes: rare, and real. No &ldquo;don''t worry&rdquo;, no dodge into generalities.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The system is the answer — the weekly early-warning test, and her fever rule, named.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The other side of the ledger is there — what the untreated illness costs, or what the drug gives back.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Plain words she can carry — no statistics recital, nothing needing a dictionary.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the hard answer — honest and hopeful, in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Honest and hopeful — at the same time.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take the Janet pack to your colleagues — peer practice is where it becomes demonstrated. Next: the conversation where the hardest word is the diagnosis itself.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="09_Station_2.3_Explaining_Schizophrenia.html">Continue · Station 2.3: &ldquo;What Does Schizophrenia Mean, Doctor?&rdquo;</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

