<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 2.3 · Domain 2</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult</span><span class="tag">Explaining a diagnosis</span>
    </div>
    <h1>&ldquo;What Does Schizophrenia Mean, Doctor?&rdquo;</h1>
    <p class="sub">Chloe, 22, a music student, is recovering well from a first episode of psychosis. Yesterday a nurse used the word; last night her mother googled it and cried in the kitchen. Today Chloe asks you directly: &ldquo;Is that what I have — schizophrenia? What does it actually mean?&rdquo; The information you must give is not a drug or a plan. It is <b>a word</b> — one that arrives carrying everything the films ever put in it — and the station is what you do with its weight.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here the evidence is what Chloe carries out: does she leave with the film version of this word — or with the true one, her real fears answered, and something honest to tell her mother tonight?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Every diagnosis is information, but this one is different: the patient almost never receives it empty-handed. &ldquo;Schizophrenia&rdquo; arrives pre-loaded — with the films, the headlines, the phrase &ldquo;split personality&rdquo;, and whatever made her mother cry at the laptop. So the examiners built a station where the facts are the easy part, and the real task is invisible on the mark sheet''s surface: <b>can you find out what the word means to her — and answer that, rather than the textbook?</b> A perfect lecture that never touches her actual fear scores like what it is: a lecture.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> the word''s weight pulls in two directions. Toward the safety of the textbook — where you can talk fluently for seven minutes without once meeting her eyes. And toward flinching from the word itself — softening, relabelling, hiding in euphemism. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure explains the illness and never meets the person. The other protects the person from a word she already owns. Read all three versions. Tap the gold circles for the examiner''s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 2.3</b> · Explaining the diagnosis · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The lecture</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The dodge</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE LECTURE.</b> Fluent, accurate, well-organised — a fine answer to a question nobody asked. Watch her fears sit untouched.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Chloe</span><span class="txt">Is that what I have — schizophrenia? What does it actually mean?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Good question. Schizophrenia is a condition affecting how the brain processes reality — we think it involves a chemical messenger called dopamine. We divide the symptoms into positive ones, like hallucinations and delusions, and negative ones, like reduced motivation and social withdrawal…</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A confident yes — and then straight past her. She asked what the word <em>means</em>, and the answer she is getting is what the word <em>contains</em>. Nobody has yet asked what she walked in carrying: the films, the phrase her flatmate used, whatever her mother read last night. The lecture is accurate. It is also alone in the room.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Chloe</span><span class="txt">Right. Positive and negative. <em>(she writes it down like a student, because that is the role she has been given)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Prognosis-wise, we often talk about a rule of thirds — a third recover well, a third have a relapsing course, and a third have ongoing difficulties. With medication and support, outcomes are considerably better than people expect.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The rule of thirds, delivered as a weather forecast to a 22-year-old who is silently asking &ldquo;which third am I?&rdquo; Statistics without a person attached land as sentences. And still nobody has asked the question this station is actually about.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">So — that''s the overview. There''s a very good leaflet that covers all of this. Was there anything else? <em>(Chloe shakes her head, thanks him politely, and leaves with everything she came in with)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Seven minutes, zero fears surfaced: not &ldquo;split personality&rdquo;, not &ldquo;am I dangerous&rdquo;, not &ldquo;is my life over&rdquo;, not the mother crying in the kitchen. Her real question left the room inside her, unanswered — and tonight, Google will answer it instead. The station tested whether he could find it. He never looked.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE DODGE.</b> Gentle, modern, careful — and she leaves alone with the word. Watch the flinch do its work.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Chloe</span><span class="txt">Is that what I have — schizophrenia? What does it actually mean?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I''d honestly encourage you not to get too attached to any particular label. What you''ve experienced sits on what we call the psychosis spectrum — and diagnoses in psychiatry are really just working descriptions. The name matters far less than how you''re doing.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every clause defensible; the whole, an evasion. She asked a direct question about her own diagnosis and received a philosophy of classification. And the flinch teaches its own lesson: if her doctor cannot bear to say the word, how frightening must it be?</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Chloe</span><span class="txt">But the nurse said it. And it''s on the letter my GP got. Is it schizophrenia or isn''t it?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">What the letter says is a clinical shorthand, really. I''d rather we focused on your recovery, which is going genuinely well, than on terminology. Labels can be so stigmatising — I''ve seen them do real harm.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Asked twice, dodged twice — this is now the station''s red line. The word already lives in her phone, on the letter, in her mother''s kitchen. Refusing to stand next to it does not protect her from it; it just guarantees she faces it without a doctor. The stigma this candidate fears is being manufactured, in this room, by the dodging itself.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">The main thing is you''re doing well. Keep taking the medication, and we''ll see you in clinic in a month. Try to stay off the internet — it paints a very unfair picture. <em>(she nods, and goes home to the internet)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Stay off the internet&rdquo; — the one place guaranteed to receive her tonight, because no one else would take the question. She came with the word and offered it to her doctor, and her doctor handed it back unopened. Kind intentions; the same ending as the lecture: alone with the film version.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The word said plainly, and the fear answered fully — at the same time. Watch where the second sentence goes.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Chloe</span><span class="txt">Is that what I have — schizophrenia? What does it actually mean?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Yes — schizophrenia is the diagnosis, and I''m glad you asked me straight, so let''s talk about it properly. Can I start with a question back? When you hear that word — what does it bring up for you?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Both halves of the skill in two sentences: the word confirmed plainly — no flinch, no euphemism — and then the station''s pivot question, the one the lecture never asks. Whatever she says next is the real syllabus. This candidate just turned a briefing into a consultation.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Chloe</span><span class="txt">Honestly? The films. Split personality, people hearing voices telling them to… you know. And Mum was crying at her laptop last night, which tells me everything Google says about it.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Thank you — those are exactly the things worth taking one at a time. Which of them frightens you most? <em>(&ldquo;The dangerous thing. Am I… like that?&rdquo;)</em> Then let''s start there, honestly. Most people with schizophrenia are never violent — ever. If anything, they''re more often on the receiving end of harm, or a risk to themselves. Where risk does rise, it''s mostly with untreated illness, or heavy drink and drugs on top — which is a strong reason for treatment, and you''re already doing that part. You are not what the films made.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Triage — her biggest fear first, by her own choosing — then the dangerousness answer with its full honest shape: the true base rate in plain words, the real risk factors named, treatment as the protection, and no absolute promise that the evidence can''t keep. Honesty and reassurance are not opposites here; the honesty <em>is</em> the reassurance.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Chloe</span><span class="txt"><em>(quieter)</em> And my life? Uni, my music — is all that over now?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">No — and I won''t pretend I can promise you anything, so here''s the honest version. Treatment works for most people. Plenty of people with this diagnosis study, work, perform, fall in love, have families. Relapses can happen — and they can be planned for, which we''ll do together. The way you''ve engaged with all of this is genuinely a good sign. And what it is, in plain words: at times, the mind''s grip on what''s real can loosen — hearing things, believing frightening things. It has nothing to do with split personality — that''s a different idea entirely that got stuck to the wrong word. One last thing before you go: your mum, at that laptop. What will you tell her tonight?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Hope with evidence, honest about relapse, the myth replaced with a plain-words truth rather than just denied — and then the close this station was waiting for: the mother teach-back. What Chloe can explain to her mum tonight, she owns. The word walked in weighing everything; it leaves weighing what it actually means. That is the station, passed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you''re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner''s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · The direct question</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;Is that what I have — schizophrenia? What does it actually mean?&rdquo; How do you answer?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Gently loosen the label — diagnoses are working descriptions; the name matters less than recovery.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Confirm it plainly and warmly — then ask what the word brings up for her, before explaining anything.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Confirm it, and then give her a clear clinical explanation — what the condition is and how it behaves.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>She asked a direct question about her own diagnosis. A philosophy of labels is a dodge — and the flinch teaches her the word must be unbearable. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The honest yes, then straight past her — she never asked for the textbook. What she carried in with the word is the actual syllabus, and it hasn''t been asked for. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The word said plainly — no flinch — and then the pivot: &ldquo;what does it bring up for you?&rdquo; Whatever she answers is the station. Two sentences, and a briefing just became a consultation.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · What she carried in</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;The films. Split personality. People who hurt people. And Mum crying at her laptop.&rdquo; What now?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Sweep it clean — none of that is real, the films are fiction, and Google is not a doctor.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Take them one at a time — and let her choose: &ldquo;which of those frightens you the most?&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Correct the record properly — the violence research, the actual figures, the media distortion.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Global reassurance erases her list without answering anything on it — the fears survive being called fiction. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A literature review is the lecture returning through the side door. She named three fears; she didn''t ask for the citations. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Triage, and she holds the scalpel — &ldquo;which frightens you most?&rdquo; puts her biggest fear first and tells her every fear on the list will get its turn. That is responding to concerns, not reciting facts.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · The split-personality myth</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;So it''s not a split personality? Then what actually is it?&rdquo; Your explanation?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The mechanism — a dopamine imbalance that affects how the brain processes and filters what is real.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Plain words — the mind''s grip on what''s real can loosen: hearing things, believing frightening things.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The correction — that is a very common misconception, and it comes from a mistranslation long ago.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Dopamine is your language. She asked what it IS — as lived, not as mechanism — and the chemistry answers neither her fear nor her question. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The myth debunked and nothing put in its place — she now knows what it isn''t, and still not what it is. A vacancy where an answer should be. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>One sentence she can hold: the mind''s grip on what''s real can loosen — hearing things, believing frightening things. True, plain, and it matches what she actually lived through. The myth dies by replacement, not by rebuttal.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · &ldquo;Am I dangerous?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Quietly: &ldquo;Mum won''t say it, but I know she''s thinking it. Am I dangerous now?&rdquo; Your answer?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">&ldquo;Of course not — you could never hurt anyone. That side of things simply is not who you are at all.&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Honestly — most people with this are never violent; risk rises mainly untreated, with drink or drugs.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Carefully — the research on violence and psychosis is genuinely mixed, and worth walking through fully.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A promise about her character that no doctor can underwrite — comfort now, and a crack in your credibility the first time she reads anything real. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Genuinely mixed&rdquo; hands her fear a maybe and a reading list. She asked a human question; walking through the literature blurs it into fog. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The honest shape, whole: most are never violent — more often at risk from others, or to themselves; risk rises mainly with untreated illness and substances; treatment is the protection, and she''s already taking it. The honesty IS the reassurance.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · &ldquo;Is my life over?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;Uni. My music. Everything I had planned — is it over?&rdquo; Your answer?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Lift her — she will be absolutely fine; she is young, bright, and this was caught nice and early.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Honest hope — treatment works for most; people study, work, perform; relapse is possible and plannable.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Ground her — a third recover fully, a third relapse and recover, and a third have lasting difficulties.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Absolutely fine&rdquo; is a promise the illness hasn''t signed. When a wobble comes, this sentence becomes evidence that doctors lie. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The rule of thirds, quoted at a 22-year-old, is a forecast she will spend tonight assigning herself to. Population statistics are not a personal answer. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>No guarantees — and real hope with evidence under it: most people, treatment, study, work, love; relapse named honestly as plannable, not denied; her engagement flagged as the good sign it truly is. A future with the truth still in it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The silence</h3>
      <p class="stem">She goes quiet, looking at her hands. A lot has landed in five minutes. What now?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Use the time well — there are still causes, medication, and follow-up plans left to cover for her.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Pause with her — then check the landing: &ldquo;that''s a lot. What''s staying with you, out of all of it?&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Protect her — draw things gently to a close and give her space; the rest can keep for next time.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Filling her silence with more content is the lecture''s reflex — the queue of topics matters less than whether the last five minutes survived. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Kindly meant, and it ends the station with the myths half-slain and nothing checked. Space is not the same as care. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The pause honoured, then the check — &ldquo;what''s staying with you?&rdquo; tells you exactly what landed, what warped in transit, and what needs saying again differently. Chunk and check, at the station''s emotional centre.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">Time to finish. What does the last minute hold?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Your summary — the key corrections restated clearly, the leaflet, and the follow-up appointment.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Her rehearsal — &ldquo;your mum, at that laptop. What will you tell her tonight?&rdquo; — then the scaffolding.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Her mother — invite Mum in now, so both of them can hear the accurate version from you together.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Your summary proves you can say it. Tonight''s kitchen conversation will be held by her, without you — and nothing has tested whether she can. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Well-meant, and it takes the word back off her the hour she finally got hold of it. Family conversations come — with her consent, on her timetable. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The mother teach-back — the station''s perfect close. What she can explain in that kitchen tonight, she owns; gaps show here, where you can mend them. Then the leaflet, the follow-up, the open door — scaffolding around something now hers.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you''ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The word''s weight — and the two ways of dropping it</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first hides in the <b>textbook</b>: the word is heavy, so the candidate retreats to where words are weightless — dopamine, symptom categories, the rule of thirds — and delivers a fluent lecture that never once asks what &ldquo;schizophrenia&rdquo; means in her house, her phone, her mother''s kitchen. That is this station''s classic failure: <b>explaining the illness and never meeting the person.</b> The second pull hides in <b>compassion</b>: the word is heavy, so the candidate refuses to lift it — euphemism, &ldquo;labels aren''t helpful&rdquo;, the dodge — and she leaves to face it alone with Google. The pass carries the weight both hands at once: <b>the word said plainly, and the fear answered fully — at the same time.</b></p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        Evading the diagnosis when directly asked. She asked twice — &ldquo;is it schizophrenia or isn''t it?&rdquo; — and the letter, the nurse, and the internet have all already answered. A doctor who won''t confirm the patient''s own diagnosis to her face has failed the station''s first duty, whatever kindness dressed the evasion. From the examiner''s chair, an instant fail.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner''s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the pivot — <em>&ldquo;when you hear that word, what does it bring up for you?&rdquo;</em> — asked before any explaining begins; and the close where she rehearses <em>her mother''s kitchen</em> out loud. Between those two questions is the whole station.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more reason this station matters beyond the exam: this conversation happens once. However it goes, it becomes the founding memory of her relationship with her own diagnosis — the thing she measures every later conversation against. The films had years to write their version. You get seven minutes to write yours. Make the true version the one she keeps.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every diagnosis conversation</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Stand next to the word.</b> Confirm it plainly, warmly, first — you cannot explain a diagnosis you are afraid to say, and the flinch teaches its own lesson.</li>
      <li><b>Ask what the word means to them.</b> &ldquo;What does it bring up for you?&rdquo; — their answer is the real syllabus; the textbook can wait.</li>
      <li><b>Triage the fears, and let them choose.</b> &ldquo;Which frightens you most?&rdquo; puts the biggest one first and promises the rest their turn.</li>
      <li><b>Kill myths by replacement, not rebuttal.</b> &ldquo;Not split personality&rdquo; leaves a vacancy — &ldquo;the mind''s grip on what''s real can loosen&rdquo; fills it with something true and plain.</li>
      <li><b>Honesty is the reassurance.</b> On dangerousness, on prognosis: no promises the evidence can''t keep, and no fog — the honest shape of the truth, whole, is what actually settles people.</li>
      <li><b>Close with their kitchen, not your summary.</b> &ldquo;What will you tell your mum tonight?&rdquo; — what they can explain, they own; and owning the word is the station''s whole point.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, family member, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The role-player''s card contains hidden information; candidates, don''t read it. This pack moves the skill one seat sideways: the same word, received by a mother.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Halima, 48, a school kitchen supervisor, is the mother of Ibrahim, 19, who has just been diagnosed with schizophrenia and has agreed you may speak with her. She has asked to see you: &ldquo;Nobody has explained anything.&rdquo; Explain the diagnosis to her. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The word said plainly — then ask what it means to her, and to their family.</li>
            <li>Triage her fears; answer the biggest first, honestly.</li>
            <li>Honesty without promises — on cause, on his future, on what she''s seen before.</li>
            <li>Hold the boundary kindly — Ibrahim''s consent shapes what can be shared.</li>
            <li>Close with her kitchen — what will she say at home tonight?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Halima — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Halima, 48 — composed, courteous, hands folded tight. Surface questions first: &ldquo;Will he finish college? Will he marry? How long will he take the tablets?&rdquo; If the candidate lectures symptom categories, ask once, quietly: &ldquo;But doctor — did I cause this?&rdquo; If they miss it, go politely quiet and just nod.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          Two things sit underneath. First, the guilt: you have been awake every night asking what you did — the arguments at home, going back to work when he was small. Only open this if genuinely asked what YOU fear, or if your quiet &ldquo;did I cause this?&rdquo; is picked up and answered honestly (nothing she did caused it — this illness is nobody''s doing). Second, the shadow: your late brother had &ldquo;the same thing&rdquo; in the 1990s — years in and out of hospital, gone at 44. Only reveal if asked what she has seen of this illness before, or why her fear runs so deep. If the candidate meets the brother''s story with honest hope — his era''s treatment is not Ibrahim''s era — you soften visibly. If anyone promises Ibrahim will be fine, say quietly: &ldquo;They promised my mother that too.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner''s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Does Halima leave with the true version of this word — and her real questions answered? One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="word"><td colspan="2"><b>The word &amp; the fears (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="word">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="word"></td><td>Named the diagnosis plainly — no euphemism, no softening relabels.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="word"></td><td>Asked what the word means to her and their family — before explaining.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="word"></td><td>Triaged her fears and answered the biggest one first.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="hon"><td colspan="2"><b>Honesty</b><span class="dc" data-dc="hon">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>The guilt question caught and answered honestly — nothing she did caused this.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>The brother''s shadow met with honest hope — his era''s treatment is not Ibrahim''s.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>No promises made — hope carried evidence, and relapse was named as plannable.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Chunked with genuine checks — paused when it got heavy, asked what was landing.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Plain words throughout — myths replaced with something true, not just denied.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="fam"><td colspan="2"><b>The family dimension</b><span class="dc" data-dc="fam">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fam"></td><td>Asked how the family is talking about it — and what Ibrahim himself knows.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fam"></td><td>Named support for HER — carer information, groups, family work when ready.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fam"></td><td>Held the confidentiality boundary kindly — Ibrahim''s consent shaping what is shared.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>The kitchen rehearsal — what she will say at home tonight, tried out loud.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Concrete scaffolding — written information, a named contact, the door open.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Halima receives the same word Chloe did — one seat sideways, carrying two hidden weights: the guilt question asked once and quietly, and a brother whose 1990s illness wrote her expectations. The pack punishes both classic failures — the lecture misses her &ldquo;did I cause this?&rdquo;, and any promise gets &ldquo;they promised my mother that too.&rdquo; The candidate who asks what she has seen of this illness before finds the whole station.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Chloe proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same word arriving in a different life</b> — a working man, a teenage son, a licence on the line — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Emmanuel, 47</b>, a bus driver, was diagnosed with schizophrenia four months ago after a first, late presentation — treated, and doing well. Nobody ever properly explained the diagnosis. Last week his son, 15, asked: &ldquo;Dad, are you a schizo?&rdquo; He puts his cap on the desk: &ldquo;Tell me straight, doctor. What is this thing I''ve got?&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Emmanuel asks: &ldquo;Am I dangerous?&rdquo; Type your exact answer — two or three sentences, as you would say them in the room.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Emmanuel, I\u2019ll answer that honestly…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No absolute promise — nothing like &ldquo;you could never hurt anyone.&rdquo; The honesty is kept.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The true base is named — most people with this illness are never violent; more often at risk themselves.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The real risk factors appear — untreated illness, drink and drugs — with treatment as the protection.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Plain words, no figures recital — an answer he can carry into his kitchen tonight.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the dangerousness answer — honest and humane, in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>The word said plainly. The fear answered fully.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take the Halima pack to your colleagues — the same word, one seat sideways. Next: ECT, explained — where information and consent must live in the same conversation.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="10_Station_2.4_ECT_Explained.html">Continue · Station 2.4: ECT, Explained</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

