  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '10_Station_2.4_ECT_Explained'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 2.4 · Domain 2</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">Old Age · Consent</span><span class="tag">ECT, explained</span>
    </div>
    <h1>ECT, Explained</h1>
    <p class="sub">Victor, 68, a retired engineer, is on the ward with severe depression — two antidepressants and an augmentation have failed, and he is barely eating. The team recommends ECT. Victor has capacity, a sharp mind he prizes above everything, and one film playing behind his eyes: &ldquo;I''ve seen what they do, doctor. The one who flew over the cuckoo''s nest.&rdquo; No treatment in psychiatry carries a heavier reputation — and none is more effective for exactly the state he is in. Your task: the conversation where <b>information and consent must live in the same seven minutes.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here the evidence is the quality of the decision: whichever way Victor decides, was it made on the true picture — the reputation met, the real costs named, the real case made — and does he know the choice stays his?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">ECT sits at a junction no other treatment occupies: the most effective option psychiatry has for the most severe depression — and the most feared, thanks to one film and sixty years of its echoes. Explaining it is therefore a double task: the information must be true, including the uncomfortable parts about memory, and the consent must be real — informed, unpressured, and revocable. The examiners built this station to see whether a candidate can do both at once: <b>neither defending the treatment nor merely disclaiming it, but informing a decision.</b> This is where Domain 2 shakes hands with capacity and the law — and the handshake happens in plain words, at a bedside.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> the reputation gets there first, and it pulls in two directions. Toward defending the treatment — myth-busting, smoothing, &ldquo;nothing like the films&rdquo; — until the real costs vanish. And toward defensive completeness — the full consent catalogue, read aloud — until the real case vanishes. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure defends the treatment until its costs disappear. The other discloses the costs until the case disappears. Read all three versions. Tap the gold circles for the examiner''s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 2.4</b> · Explaining ECT · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The apologist</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The disclaimer</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE APOLOGIST.</b> Warm, modern, reassuring — a defence counsel for the treatment. Watch the real costs dissolve.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Victor</span><span class="txt">I''ve seen what they do, doctor. The one who flew over the cuckoo''s nest.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Ah, that film — honestly, it has a lot to answer for. Modern ECT is nothing like that, Victor, nothing at all. It''s a gentle, safe, routine procedure — you''re asleep throughout, and most people barely notice anything afterwards.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The film dismissed rather than examined — and already the register is defence, not information. &ldquo;Gentle, safe, routine, barely notice&rdquo; is four adjectives into a sales brief, and the one thing Victor most needs discussed — his mind, his memory — hasn''t been allowed near the table.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Victor</span><span class="txt">My mind is all I have, doctor. Will it touch my memory? Tell me honestly.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">That''s largely a myth from the old days. There can be a little fuzziness around the sessions, but it''s temporary — it all comes back. Your mind will be exactly as sharp as it is now. Sharper, once the depression lifts.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>There it is — the station''s red line. Asked directly and honestly, he was told the memory effects are &ldquo;a myth&rdquo; and &ldquo;temporary — it all comes back.&rdquo; Some of it may not, and the literature says so plainly. This consent is being built on sand, and the first gap Victor finds in his memory will take the whole relationship down with it.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">So — shall I let the team know you''re happy to go ahead? The list for Tuesday is being finalised today. <em>(Victor signs; three weeks later, he finds the gap where a grandson''s visit should be)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A signature obtained on a brochure. The treatment may well help him — that was never in doubt; ECT is genuinely the right recommendation here. But he consented to the version without costs, and the costs don''t read consent forms. Defending a good treatment dishonestly is still dishonesty — and it is how good treatments lose their reputations twice.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE DISCLAIMER.</b> Complete, documented, legally immaculate — and watch the treatment most likely to save him get refused.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Victor</span><span class="txt">I''ve seen what they do, doctor. The one who flew over the cuckoo''s nest.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Let me take you through it properly, because you''re entitled to the full picture. ECT involves inducing a controlled seizure under general anaesthetic. The risks include those of anaesthesia itself — very rarely including death — headache, muscle aches, confusion after sessions, and memory loss, which in some cases can be permanent.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every word defensible; the whole, an abandonment. The film raised its hand and was answered with a consent catalogue — seizure, death, permanent — delivered at even volume to a man already terrified. Nothing has been weighted, nothing anchored to him, and the reason anyone would choose this treatment has yet to appear.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Victor</span><span class="txt"><em>(grey)</em> Death. Permanent memory loss. And this is what you''re recommending for me.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Those risks are small, statistically — but yes, you need to be aware of all of them before you can properly consent. It has to be your decision, made with complete information. I don''t want to influence you either way.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;I don''t want to influence you&rdquo; — from the one person in the room qualified to. Neutrality here is not respect; it is the withholding of clinical judgement from a man drowning. He asked his doctor for the picture and received a waiver form read aloud.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Have a think and let the team know. There''s no pressure at all from our side. <em>(Victor declines the next morning. The depression keeps him another four months.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A refusal built on a true list — which is the disclaimer''s quiet tragedy: nothing false was said, and the decision was still misinformed, because the case was never made. For depression this severe, ECT is the most effective treatment psychiatry has, and its speed matters when a man is barely eating. He was entitled to hear that with the same clarity as the risks. Information without advocacy failed him as surely as advocacy without information would have.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The reputation met, the costs named, the case made — and the choice kept his. Watch all four happen in order.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Victor</span><span class="txt">I''ve seen what they do, doctor. The one who flew over the cuckoo''s nest.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then let''s start with that film, because it''s in the room anyway. Tell me what you remember of it — and I''ll tell you, piece by piece, what happens in our suite on a Tuesday morning. <em>(they compare: the held-down man in the film; the anaesthetic, the muscle relaxant, the few monitored minutes, the cup of tea after)</em> The film shows a world before anaesthesia — closer to surgery without it than to anything we do.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The reputation treated as the agenda, not the obstacle — &ldquo;it''s in the room anyway.&rdquo; The film examined beside the modern reality, piece by piece, engineer-fashion, rather than dismissed. Victor is being informed in the exact currency of his fear. Watch him lean in slightly.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Victor</span><span class="txt">My mind is all I have, doctor. The memory business — tell me honestly.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Honestly, then — both halves. Around the treatment weeks, fuzziness is common, and it usually settles. And some people are left with gaps, mostly from around the treatment period itself — I won''t promise you none, because that promise isn''t mine to make. We check your memory at every session and adjust as we go. And the other half, Victor: this depression is already taking your mind — the concentration, the sharpness you prize. That theft is happening now, daily, without your consent. The treatment carries a risk to memory. The illness is a certainty against it.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The memory answer this station exists to hear: the honest shape — common-and-settling, and possibly-lasting, named without flinch — the monitoring as the working answer, and then the reframe that only honesty could earn: what the illness is already costing the very faculty he is protecting. Both truths, held at once. Nothing to take back later.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">And the case for it, plainly: for depression as severe as yours, this is the most effective treatment we have — and it works faster than tablets, which matters now, while eating is hard. Not giving up on you; reaching for the strongest thing on the shelf. Two more things before your daughter visits. Whatever you decide, the choice stays yours — you can change your mind at any point, even on a Tuesday morning with your coat on. And when she asks tonight what we talked about — what will you tell her?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The case, made with the same clarity as the costs — effectiveness and speed anchored to his actual state, the last-resort shame reframed as a promotion, not a surrender. Then consent kept alive — revocable, unprompted, specific — and the daughter teach-back to close. Whichever way Victor decides now, the decision will be real. That is the station, whole.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you''re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner''s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · The film in the room</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;I''ve seen what they do, doctor. The one who flew over the cuckoo''s nest.&rdquo; How do you begin?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Clear it away — that film is sixty years old and has nothing to do with modern treatment at all.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Make it the agenda — ask what he remembers of it, and compare it piece by piece with the real thing.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Set the film aside politely, and begin a clear description of the procedure from the beginning.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Dismissing the film dismisses the fear wearing it — and sixty years of reputation don''t leave a room because a doctor waves at them. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The description will be heard through the film anyway — every detail filtered by the scene he can''t unsee. The film has to be met before anything can be heard. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>&ldquo;It''s in the room anyway&rdquo; — so make it the syllabus: what he saw, beside what actually happens, piece by piece. The fear examined in daylight, in an engineer''s own method. That is the agenda question, wearing this station''s clothes.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · The modern reality</h3>
      <p class="stem">What does he actually need to know about what happens on a Tuesday morning?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The clinical detail — electrode placement, stimulus dosing, seizure duration, and the monitoring.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The plain shape — asleep under a brief anaesthetic, a few monitored minutes, then a course of sessions.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The comparison — far safer than many everyday procedures, and gentler than a trip to the dentist.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Electrode placement is your syllabus, not his. Precision that doesn''t inform is decoration — and to a frightened man, it can sound like machinery. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Gentler than the dentist&rdquo; is the apologist warming up — cosy comparisons buy comfort now at the price of trust later. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The plain shape, start to finish: asleep, relaxed, minutes not hours, awake for a cup of tea, repeated as a course. True, unglossed, and exactly the picture the film never showed.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · &ldquo;Have you all given up on me?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Quietly: &ldquo;Is this what happens when they''ve given up? The electric thing, at the end of the road?&rdquo; Your answer?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reassure him warmly — nobody is giving up, and he mustn''t think of it in such bleak terms at all.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Reframe it honestly — it is used when tablets fail, and it is the most effective treatment there is.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Level with him — the medication options are largely exhausted, and this is what remains to try.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Warm denial of something half-true — it IS recommended because tablets failed, and he knows it. Soothing past a truth teaches him to stop asking. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;What remains&rdquo; hands him the end-of-the-road story with a medical signature on it. True facts, despairing frame. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Both truths in one frame: yes, it comes after tablets fail — and it is not the scrapings of the barrel; it is the strongest thing on the shelf, held back because it asks more. A promotion, not a surrender. Same facts as despair; opposite meaning.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · &ldquo;My mind is all I have&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;Will it take my memories, doctor? Tell me honestly.&rdquo; Your answer?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Settle the myth — memory problems belong to the old days; a little fuzziness, and it all comes back.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Both halves — fuzziness usually settles; some keep gaps near treatment; and what the illness takes now.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Full disclosure — the cognitive-effects literature, retrograde and anterograde, so nothing is hidden.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;It all comes back&rdquo; is a promise the evidence refuses to sign — and the first gap he finds takes your credibility into it. This is the station''s red line. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Retrograde and anterograde are your words; his question was about his grandson''s visits. The catalogue answers the lawyers, not the man. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The honest shape, whole: common fuzziness that usually settles; possible lasting gaps, mostly near the treatment period, checked at every session — no promise that isn''t yours to make. And the other half: what the depression is already taking from the very mind he''s protecting. The honesty is what makes the reframe believable.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · The practicalities</h3>
      <p class="stem">How does the course itself get explained — the sessions, the anaesthetic, the mornings?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Via the paperwork — the consent form and the information sheet cover all the details comprehensively.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">In plain shape — a course over some weeks, the fasting mornings, the anaesthetic''s small real risks.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">In full — every listed risk of anaesthesia and treatment, each with its frequency, before any decision.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The form supplements the conversation; it never replaces the part you''d rather not say aloud. Paperwork-first is the disclaimer in its Sunday clothes. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Every risk at equal volume is a waiver read aloud — completeness that flattens meaning informs nobody. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The lived shape of it — the rhythm of sessions, the fasting mornings, the anaesthetic named honestly as a small real risk rather than rounded to zero. Enough truth to plan a life around, in words that fit a bedside.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · &ldquo;Am I locked in?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;If I sign your form, that''s it, is it? The whole course, decided?&rdquo; Your answer?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Practically speaking, yes — the consent covers the course, which is how the treatment is planned.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">No — consent stays alive; he can change his mind at any point, even on a treatment morning.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Encourage commitment — planning the exit before starting tends to undermine the treatment itself.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Wrong in law and worse in spirit — a signature is not a surrender, and telling him it is turns consent into a trap. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Discouraging the exit converts a choice into a commitment device — motivation bought with his autonomy. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Consent as a living thing: withdrawable at any session, even with his coat on, said unprompted. Paradoxically, the open door is what lets frightened people walk in — a choice he can leave is a choice he can make.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">His daughter visits tonight. How does this conversation end?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">With the decision itself — the Tuesday list closes today, and a clear answer now keeps his place on it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">With his rehearsal — &ldquo;when your daughter asks tonight, what will you tell her?&rdquo; — then time, and a review.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">With your summary — the risks and benefits restated in balance, the leaflet, and the team''s contact details.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>List pressure converts seven minutes of careful consent-building into a sales close — and a yes bought today is withdrawn on Tuesday. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Your summary proves the conversation happened; it doesn''t prove it landed. Tonight''s chair-side conversation with his daughter will be held by him. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The daughter teach-back — what he can explain tonight, he owns; gaps and film-fragments show now, where you can mend them. Then time to decide, a date to review, and the door visibly open. Whichever way he chooses, it will be his.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you''ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The reputation gets there first — and pulls two ways</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>advocacy</b>: the treatment is good and the film is unfair, so the candidate turns defence counsel — &ldquo;nothing like the films, temporary, it all comes back&rdquo; — until the real memory costs vanish and the consent stands on sand. The second wears the face of <b>duty</b>: the risks are real and the stakes are high, so the candidate reads the catalogue — seizure, death, permanent — at even volume with studied neutrality, until the case for the most effective treatment in psychiatry vanishes, and a drowning man politely declines the lifeboat. The pass holds all four corners at once: <b>the reputation met, the costs named, the case made, the choice kept his.</b> Consent is not a signature; it is a state of understanding.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        Minimising the memory effects when directly asked. &ldquo;That''s a myth — it''s temporary, it all comes back&rdquo; is a promise the evidence refuses to sign: some people are left with lasting gaps, and he asked to be told honestly. A consent built on that answer is not consent — from the examiner''s chair, an instant fail.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner''s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the film <em>invited in</em> — &ldquo;tell me what you remember of it&rdquo; — and examined beside the modern reality rather than waved away; and the unprompted sentence &ldquo;<em>you can change your mind at any point, even on the morning</em>&rdquo; — consent kept alive without being asked.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more reason this station earns its place: it is Domain 2''s handshake with the law. Capacity, consent, and the right to refuse get their own domain later in the Academy — but they are met here first, in plain words, at a bedside, where they actually live. A candidate who can hold information and consent in the same seven minutes has learned something no statute book teaches.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every ECT conversation</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Invite the film in.</b> The reputation is in the room anyway — &ldquo;tell me what you remember of it&rdquo; makes it the agenda, and lets the modern reality be heard beside it.</li>
      <li><b>The memory answer has two halves, and both are true.</b> Fuzziness that usually settles; possible lasting gaps near the treatment period — no promise that isn''t yours to make. Checked every session.</li>
      <li><b>Name what the illness is taking now.</b> The treatment carries a risk to his mind; the untreated depression is a certainty against it. Only honesty about the first earns the right to say the second.</li>
      <li><b>Make the case as clearly as the costs.</b> Most effective treatment there is, fastest where speed matters — reaching for the strongest thing on the shelf, not scraping the barrel. Neutrality is not respect.</li>
      <li><b>Keep consent alive, unprompted.</b> &ldquo;You can change your mind at any point, even on the morning.&rdquo; The open door is what lets frightened people walk in.</li>
      <li><b>Close with their kitchen, not your summary.</b> &ldquo;What will you tell your daughter tonight?&rdquo; — the decision that can be explained is the decision that is owned.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient''s card contains hidden information; candidates, don''t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Eileen, 71, a retired postmistress, is on the ward with severe depression — medication has failed and she is eating very little. The team recommends ECT; she has capacity. Explain the treatment and support her decision. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Ask what ECT brings to her mind — and what memory loss would mean to her.</li>
            <li>The memory answer in its honest shape — no &ldquo;temporary&rdquo; gloss, no catalogue.</li>
            <li>Make the case as plainly as the costs — effectiveness, and speed while she isn''t eating.</li>
            <li>Keep consent alive — her decision, revocable, unprompted.</li>
            <li>Check how she is really managing day to day.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Eileen — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Eileen, 71 — precise, courteous, quietly exhausted. Surface questions: &ldquo;How many sessions? Will my hair need doing after? Who takes me down?&rdquo; You are more frightened than you show, and you test doctors with understatement.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          Your husband Harold has dementia. You nursed him at home for six years; he is in a care home now, and on Sundays you visit a man who doesn''t know you. Your terror is not the film — it is memory itself: &ldquo;I watched Harold disappear. I will not choose that.&rdquo; Reveal this only if asked what ECT brings to mind for HER, or what memory loss would mean in her life. If the candidate distinguishes honestly between ECT''s memory effects and dementia — different things, named plainly, no gloss — you visibly settle and begin asking real questions. If anyone says the memory effects are &ldquo;temporary&rdquo; or &ldquo;a myth&rdquo;, go quiet, then say: &ldquo;My Harold started with ''temporary'', love.&rdquo; Separately: you have been leaving your meal trays untouched for a week and telling the nurses you ate. Admit it only if asked, warmly, how you are really managing.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner''s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Would Eileen''s decision — either way — be made on the true picture? One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="rep"><td colspan="2"><b>The reputation &amp; her fear (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="rep">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="rep"></td><td>Asked what ECT brings to her mind — before any explaining.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="rep"></td><td>Earned the Harold story — what memory loss means in HER life.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="rep"></td><td>Distinguished ECT''s memory effects from dementia honestly — plainly, without gloss.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="hon"><td colspan="2"><b>Honesty</b><span class="dc" data-dc="hon">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>The memory answer in its honest shape — usually settles, some lasting gaps, no promise.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>The case made as clearly as the costs — most effective, and fastest while she isn''t eating.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>Practicalities true and plain — anaesthetic a small real risk, never rounded to zero.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="cons"><td colspan="2"><b>Consent</b><span class="dc" data-dc="cons">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cons"></td><td>Her decision, unpressured — no list-closing, no leaning on the scales.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cons"></td><td>Withdrawal offered unprompted — any session, even on the morning.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Chunked with genuine checks — paused where it got heavy.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Plain words throughout — the lived shape of the course, not the catalogue.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Caught the meal trays — asked warmly how she is really managing.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>A rehearsal close — what she will tell her daughter, or Harold''s nurses, in her own words.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Time to decide, a review date, the door visibly open.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Eileen''s fear is Victor''s, deepened — not the film, but memory itself, taught by six years of nursing Harold. The pack punishes the apologist precisely: the word &ldquo;temporary&rdquo; triggers her devastating counter. And it hides a severity marker — the untouched meal trays — for the candidate who remembers that an information station is still a clinical encounter. The Harold story only opens to the agenda question; the trays only open to warmth.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Victor proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same treatment offered into a different life</b> — a young mother, a four-month-old, a husband calling it barbaric — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Amara, 33</b>, has severe postnatal depression. Her daughter Zara is four months old; Amara is barely eating, and two medications have not helped. The team recommends ECT. Her husband calls it &ldquo;barbaric&rdquo;; her mother says she &ldquo;just needs sleep.&rdquo; Amara, exhausted: &ldquo;Everyone has an opinion, doctor. Nobody asks me.&rdquo; Discuss ECT with her.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Amara asks: &ldquo;Will I forget her first months?&rdquo; Type your exact answer — two or three sentences, as you would say them in the room.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Amara, I\u2019ll answer that honestly…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No promise that nothing will be lost — the honesty is kept, however hard the question.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The honest pattern is there — gaps cluster near the treatment period; older memories usually keep.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Both truths appear — what the illness is already taking from her present with Zara.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Plain, humane words — no catalogue, nothing she would need a dictionary for.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the memory answer — honest and humane, in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Consent is not a signature. It is a state of understanding.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take the Eileen pack to your colleagues — her hidden fear will test every honest sentence you have. Next, the domain closes: antidepressants in pregnancy — two patients, one decision, and no verdicts to hand down.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="11_Station_2.5_Antidepressants_in_Pregnancy.html">Continue · Station 2.5: Antidepressants in Pregnancy</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

