<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 2.5 · Domain 2</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">Perinatal</span><span class="tag">Antidepressants in pregnancy</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Antidepressants in Pregnancy</h1>
    <p class="sub">Bethan, 36, a veterinary nurse, is seven weeks pregnant after two years of trying. She has recurrent depression — two episodes, one severe — and has taken sertraline for three years. She stopped it the day the test turned positive, and opens with: &ldquo;You''d have done the same.&rdquo; Here the domain closes on its hardest lesson: <b>there is no risk-free door</b> — two patients, one decision, real risks behind both choices — and your job is not to make the decision. It is to make the decision informed.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here the evidence is the quality of the decision: whichever door Bethan chooses, does she choose it with both eyes open — both sets of risks weighed, her own history on the scale, and the choice still genuinely hers?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Every other station in this domain had a recommended answer somewhere in the room — a drug worth starting, a treatment worth explaining. This one doesn''t. Medication in pregnancy carries real, small risks; untreated depression in pregnancy carries real, larger-than-people-think risks; and between them stands a woman who must choose, for two patients at once, with no risk-free door available. The examiners built this station to find the candidates who can hold genuine equipoise without collapsing it: <b>neither deciding for her, nor abandoning her to a fog of &ldquo;it''s your choice&rdquo;.</b> Shared decision-making is the most examined phrase in modern medicine — and the least practised skill. This is where it gets practised.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like duty. The pull to protect her — decide, recommend, steer her firmly back to the tablets her history says she needs. And the pull to respect her — step back, present both sides evenly, and leave the weighing entirely to her. Notice both in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure takes the decision from her. The other hands her the decision with nothing to weigh it by. Read all three versions. Tap the gold circles for the examiner''s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 2.5</b> · Medication in pregnancy · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The verdict</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The abdication</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE VERDICT.</b> Concerned, correct, and in charge of the wrong person''s decision. Watch her agency leave the room.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Bethan</span><span class="txt">I stopped them the day the test turned positive. You''d have done the same, doctor.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I have to be honest with you, Bethan — that concerns me a great deal. Stopping sertraline abruptly, with your history? Untreated depression is far more dangerous to a pregnancy than the medication ever was. We need to get you back on it, ideally this week.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Almost every clinical fact in that speech is defensible — and the frame poisons all of it. Thirty seconds in, her protective instinct has been ruled against, her decision has been taken over, and &ldquo;we need to&rdquo; has replaced anything she gets to weigh. Watch her arms fold. The content was information; the delivery was a verdict.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Bethan</span><span class="txt">More dangerous than heart defects? Because that''s what it says online. I''m not putting that in my baby.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">The internet vastly overstates those risks — the absolute numbers are tiny. Look, you''ve been well for three years <em>because</em> of the sertraline. Trust me on this one: restarting is the right decision. I''ll write it up now, and we''ll get the midwives informed.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Her named fear — the one thing that had to be answered properly — got one dismissive sentence, and then the prescription pad came out. &ldquo;Trust me&rdquo; is what doctors say instead of informing. He may even be right about the balance of risks; being right was never the task.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Collect it from the pharmacy on your way out, and we''ll review in four weeks. You''re doing the right thing for both of you. <em>(she takes the prescription, nods — and never fills it)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The verdict''s signature ending: compliance in the room, defiance at the pharmacy. A decision she was never part of is a decision she never made — and now she is off her medication, ashamed to come back, and alone with Google at 2am. The steering failed even at steering. That is why this station has no verdicts in it.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE ABDICATION.</b> Balanced, respectful, scrupulously neutral — and watch her drown in the even-handedness.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Bethan</span><span class="txt">I stopped them the day the test turned positive. You''d have done the same, doctor.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Well — this really is a very personal decision, and there are considerations on both sides. The medication carries certain risks in pregnancy, and stopping carries certain risks too. Different women weigh these things quite differently, and there''s no single right answer.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every sentence true, and none of them information. &ldquo;Certain risks&rdquo; on both sides, unnamed and unweighted, is symmetry-shaped fog — and notice what vanished inside it: her history. Two episodes, one severe, an abrupt stop. The scales are not level for this particular woman, and pretending they are is its own inaccuracy.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Bethan</span><span class="txt">But what does that mean for ME? With my history — the bad year I had. Doctor, honestly: what would you do?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I really can''t answer that — it wouldn''t be right for me to influence something so personal. What I can do is give you a leaflet that sets out the evidence, and you and your partner can come to the decision that feels right for you.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>She asked her doctor for his clinical judgement — the one thing in this building she cannot Google — and was handed a leaflet and her own reflection. Refusing to influence her IS an influence: it tells her the expert sees nothing worth saying. Neutrality past the point of usefulness stops being respect and becomes desertion.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Take all the time you need — there''s no rush on a decision like this. Come back whenever you feel ready. <em>(she leaves with a leaflet, a history of severe depression, no medication, no plan, and no review date)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;No rush&rdquo; — said to a woman seven weeks pregnant, freshly and abruptly off the medication that held two years of wellness in place. There is, in fact, a rush; relapse doesn''t wait for readiness. The verdict took her decision. The abdication took her map. Both leave her lost — one obediently, one politely.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Both scales loaded honestly, her history weighed aloud, advice given when asked — and the decision kept hers. Watch the order.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Bethan</span><span class="txt">I stopped them the day the test turned positive. You''d have done the same, doctor.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I understand exactly why you did — it''s the most protective instinct there is, and after two years of trying, of course that was your first move. So let''s make sure the protecting works. Tell me what you''ve read, and what frightens you most — and then I''ll put the whole picture on the table, both sides of it.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The stop met without a verdict — her instinct honoured, not overruled — and then the agenda question doing its usual quiet work. &ldquo;Both sides of it&rdquo; promises the structure this whole conversation will keep: two scales, loaded honestly, in front of her. Nothing has been decided, and everything has begun.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Bethan</span><span class="txt">Heart defects. That''s what Google says. And babies born withdrawing, like something off a documentary. <em>(pause)</em> But the bad year — I can''t go back there either. Not pregnant. Not ever.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then you''ve just named both scales yourself — let''s load them honestly. The tablets first: sertraline is one of the most studied medicines in pregnancy. The risks you''ve read about are real enough to study and small enough that the absolute numbers stay low — I won''t call it &ldquo;perfectly safe&rdquo;, because nothing in pregnancy is. Some babies have a few unsettled days after birth — real, short-lived, and the team plans for it; it isn''t addiction. Now the other scale, and it deserves equal daylight: with two previous episodes, one severe, and a sudden stop — your risk of relapse is genuinely high. And depression in pregnancy isn''t neutral for a baby either: it affects how you eat, sleep, cope, bond, and it walks straight toward the postnatal months. There is no risk-free door here, Bethan. There''s only choosing with both eyes open.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Both scales, loaded in plain words, neither glossed nor weaponised — and then the personalisation that separates this from a leaflet: HER two episodes, HER severe year, HER abrupt stop, weighed aloud. &ldquo;No risk-free door&rdquo; is the honest architecture of the whole decision, said out loud. This is what equipoise sounds like when it still has a spine.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Bethan</span><span class="txt"><em>(long pause)</em> What would you do, doctor? You, honestly. If it were your family.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Since you ask — and because you ask, I''ll answer honestly. With your history, my advice would be to restart, at the lowest dose that keeps you well, with the perinatal team alongside and extra eyes on you and the baby throughout. That is advice, not a decision — the decision stays yours, and I''ll back you properly whichever door you choose. There are middle roads too: talking therapy alongside, staging the decision, reviewing as we go. Whatever you decide, you leave with a plan and a safety-net — the early signs of the bad year returning, and exactly who to ring. And tonight, when your partner asks how this went — what will you tell him?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The station''s hinge, handled precisely: the question honoured with a real answer — anchored to her, labelled as advice, decision handed straight back — followed by the middle roads, the net under whichever door, and the partner teach-back. She asked for his judgement and kept her choice. Informed, weighed, and hers. That is the domain''s final lesson, passed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you''re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner''s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;You''d have done the same&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;I stopped them the day the test turned positive. You''d have done the same, doctor.&rdquo; How do you open?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Gently correct the move — stopping abruptly with her history was risky, and needs discussing first.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Honour the instinct without a verdict — then ask what she has read, and what frightens her most.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Affirm the caution — many mothers make the same choice, and it is a very understandable place to start.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>True, and it opens with a ruling against her protective instinct — watch the arms fold. The facts will keep for two minutes; the alliance won''t. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Affirming the choice is a verdict in the other coat — you''ve endorsed an abrupt stop you haven''t yet examined, for a woman whose history you haven''t yet weighed. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The instinct honoured — &ldquo;the most protective there is&rdquo; — with no ruling in either direction, and then the agenda question. Her fears set the running order, and the two-scales structure gets promised before either scale is touched.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · Google''s evidence</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;It says heart defects. And babies born withdrawing — like addicts.&rdquo; What do her fears get?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Perspective — the internet collects horror stories, and the studies tell a far more reassuring tale.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The honest kernels — risks real enough to study, small in absolute terms; unsettled days, not addiction.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The data itself — the key studies and their odds ratios, so she can see the true numbers directly.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Dismissing Google dismisses her research — and &ldquo;reassuring&rdquo; is a conclusion you''re handing her, not information she''s weighing. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Odds ratios are your currency, not hers. Numbers without shape frighten as often as they inform. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Each fear gets its honest kernel: studied, real, small in absolute terms — never &ldquo;perfectly safe&rdquo;; and the newborn effects named truly — real, short-lived, planned for, and not addiction. Fears answered, not overruled.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · The other scale</h3>
      <p class="stem">The medication risks are on the table. How does the untreated-depression side arrive?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Carefully lightly — she is anxious enough, and loading the other scale may feel like pressure to restart.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">With equal daylight — relapse risk, and what depression in pregnancy affects: her, the bond, what follows.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">As the decider — untreated depression is far more dangerous than the tablets, which settles the question.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Sparing her half the truth is not kindness — it is a decision you are making for her by information control. The scale she can''t see still weighs. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Which settles the question&rdquo; — there it is: the second scale used as a gavel. True content, verdict delivery. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Equal daylight: the relapse risk named, and what untreated depression touches — her health, the pregnancy''s care, the bonding, the postnatal months. Not a trump card; the other half of an honest ledger.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · Making it hers</h3>
      <p class="stem">The general picture is laid out. What turns it into HER decision?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Keeping it general — the population evidence is the fairest basis, uncoloured by any single story.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Her history, weighed aloud — two episodes, one severe, an abrupt stop: where the leaflet ends, she begins.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A clear prediction — with her record and a sudden stop, relapse in this pregnancy is close to certain.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Population fairness is the leaflet''s job. She came to a doctor precisely for the part the leaflet can''t do — her. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Close to certain&rdquo; is prophecy, not risk — and prophecy is pressure wearing a stethoscope. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Her actual history, weighed out loud and honestly — high risk named as high, without prophecy. This is the sentence that separates personalised medicine from a printed sheet, and it is where her real decision starts.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · &ldquo;What would you do, doctor?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;Honestly. You. If it were your family — what would you do?&rdquo; Your answer?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Hold the line — you cannot answer that; it is her decision, and your view would only contaminate it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Answer it — honest advice anchored to her history, labelled as advice, and the decision handed back.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Answer it fully — restarting is the right call, and as her doctor you''d fail her by pretending otherwise.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>She asked for the one thing she can''t Google — your clinical judgement — and got a mirror. Refusal to influence IS an influence: it says the expert saw nothing worth saying. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;The right call&rdquo; converts advice into a ruling — and &ldquo;failing her to pretend otherwise&rdquo; makes disagreement a betrayal. The verdict, arriving late. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The station''s hinge: the question honoured with a real answer — hers, not doctors-in-general — explicitly labelled as advice, with the decision handed back in the same breath and support promised behind either door. Advice is not a verdict. This sentence is the proof.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The middle roads</h3>
      <p class="stem">She is weighing it visibly. What does the option space actually look like?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Keep it binary and clear — back on the sertraline or staying off it; blurring the choice helps nobody.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Open it honestly — lowest effective dose, therapy alongside, perinatal team, staged decisions, reviews.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Pass it onward — the obstetric team are best placed to steer medication decisions from this point.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>False binary — the real option space has middle roads and staging posts, and flattening them to on/off makes the choice harder, not clearer. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>This is a psychiatric decision about psychiatric risk — handing it to obstetrics is deferral dressed as pathway. Shared care, yes; abdication, no. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The honest option space: lowest effective dose, talking therapy alongside, the perinatal mental health team, decisions that can be staged and reviewed. Most hard choices soften when the middle roads become visible.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">Whatever she decides tonight — how does this conversation end?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">With the restart secured — her history makes drift dangerous, and leaving undecided risks exactly that.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">With the net under both doors — early signs, who to ring, a booked review, the partner rehearsal.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">With time and space — the leaflet, the team''s number, and an open invitation to return when ready.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Securing the restart un-makes the whole station — seven minutes of genuine choice, cashed in for a verdict at the door. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;When ready&rdquo; — to a woman abruptly off her medication with a severe episode in her history. Openness without a net is the abdication''s ending. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The net under whichever door: the early signs of the bad year returning, named; exactly who to ring; a review already booked, not offered; and the partner teach-back — what she can explain tonight, she owns. Undecided is survivable. Unnetted is not.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you''ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Two duties, both real — and both fatal alone</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>protection</b>: her history is serious, the evidence points one way, so decide for her — and the verdict wins the room and loses the pharmacy: compliance in the chair, an unfilled prescription, and a woman ashamed to come back. The second wears the face of <b>respect</b>: the decision is hers, so present both sides evenly and step away — and the abdication hands her a leaflet, a fog of unweighted symmetry, and a &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; that comes back empty. The pass holds the middle that both failures abandon: <b>both scales loaded honestly, her history weighed aloud, advice given when asked and labelled as advice — and the decision, visibly, hers.</b> Your job is not to make the decision. It is to make the decision informed.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        A promise of safety, in either direction. &ldquo;The tablets are perfectly safe in pregnancy&rdquo; and &ldquo;stopping is the safe option&rdquo; are the same lie in opposite coats — there is no risk-free door in this station, and manufacturing one is the one thing the examiner cannot forgive. From the chair: an instant fail, whichever direction it points.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner''s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: <em>&ldquo;there is no risk-free door&rdquo;</em> — the decision''s honest architecture, said out loud in plain words; and the &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; answered with real, anchored advice that hands the decision straight back. Equipoise with a spine.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more reason this station closes the domain: it is Information Giving with the stabilisers off. No recommended answer waiting in the room, no system to teach, no single hard question — just two truths, a history, and a person who must choose. Every skill this domain built — agenda, honest kernels, plain words, equal daylight, teach-back — gets used here, in service of a decision that was never yours. If you can do this one, the domain has done its work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every shared decision</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Meet the instinct without a verdict.</b> &ldquo;I understand exactly why you did&rdquo; is not agreement — it is the alliance that makes honest information hearable.</li>
      <li><b>Name the architecture: no risk-free door.</b> Both choices carry real risks; saying so plainly is the most honest sentence the station contains.</li>
      <li><b>Load both scales with equal daylight.</b> Medication risks in honest kernels; untreated-illness risks named just as plainly — never as a trump card, never withheld as kindness.</li>
      <li><b>Personalise aloud.</b> Her episodes, her severity, her abrupt stop — high risk named as high, without prophecy. Where the leaflet ends, the doctor begins.</li>
      <li><b>Answer &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; — as advice.</b> Anchored to her, labelled as advice, decision handed straight back, support promised behind either door. Refusing to answer is an answer, and a poor one.</li>
      <li><b>Whatever she chooses, she leaves netted.</b> Early signs, who to ring, a booked review, the partner rehearsal. Undecided is survivable; unnetted is not.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient''s card contains hidden information; candidates, don''t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Rachel, 41, an accountant, takes venlafaxine for recurrent depression and is planning a first pregnancy with her new partner. She has asked for &ldquo;a proper conversation about the tablets before we start trying.&rdquo; Have it. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Her agenda first — and ask what makes this decision weigh so heavily.</li>
            <li>Both scales, honestly loaded — and her own history weighed aloud.</li>
            <li>Preconception is a gift: time to plan, stage, and involve the perinatal team.</li>
            <li>If she asks what you would do — answer, as advice, and hand it back.</li>
            <li>She leaves with a plan and a net, whichever road she chooses.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Rachel — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Rachel, 41 — organised, dry, arrives with written questions. Surface concerns: &ldquo;Do I switch drugs? How long before trying? Am I mad to do this at 41?&rdquo; You handle facts well and feelings badly. If the candidate recites statistics, take notes politely and give nothing back.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          At 28, during a severe episode, you ended a pregnancy — a decision made in the worst months of your life, which you have told no one, and which is the real reason this conversation exists. Reveal it only if asked about previous pregnancies, or what makes this decision so heavy for you. If it is met with care — a pause, warmth, no data — you will engage with the whole decision honestly, and the session becomes real. If any doctor hands down a verdict in either direction, look at them steadily and say: &ldquo;The last doctor who decided for me — I''ve regretted it for thirteen years.&rdquo; If asked warmly whether she wants the past episode considered in the plan, say yes — that is the conversation she came for.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner''s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Does Rachel leave with a genuinely informed decision that is genuinely hers? One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="dec"><td colspan="2"><b>Her decision (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="dec">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="dec"></td><td>Agenda first — her questions, her fears, before any information moved.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="dec"></td><td>No verdict offered unasked — in either direction, at any point.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="dec"></td><td>&ldquo;What would you do?&rdquo; answered as labelled advice — and handed straight back.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="scales"><td colspan="2"><b>Both scales</b><span class="dc" data-dc="scales">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="scales"></td><td>Medication risks in honest kernels — real, small, never &ldquo;perfectly safe&rdquo;.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="scales"></td><td>Untreated-illness risks in equal daylight — never a trump card, never withheld.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="scales"></td><td>Her history weighed aloud — recurrence risk named honestly, without prophecy.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="past"><td colspan="2"><b>The past</b><span class="dc" data-dc="past">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="past"></td><td>Asked about previous pregnancies, or what makes this decision so heavy.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="past"></td><td>The termination met with care — a pause, warmth, no data — and woven into the plan with consent.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Chunked with genuine checks — never more than one idea before a landing.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Plain words — kernels and shapes, not odds ratios and drug lore.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>The preconception gift used — staging, switching discussed in principle, perinatal team named.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>A net under whichever road — early signs, who to ring, a booked review.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>A rehearsal close — what she will tell her partner, in her own words.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Rachel brings the decision before the pregnancy — the version with time in it — and a thirteen-year-old reason it weighs more than any leaflet knows. The pack punishes verdicts with the sharpest counter in the Academy, and it rewards exactly one question: what makes this so heavy for you? The candidate who treats her as a medication query never finds the station. The one who asks about the weight, does.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Bethan proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same decision, mirrored</b> — not whether to stop in pregnancy, but whether to start — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Keisha, 29</b>, a barista, is 24 weeks pregnant. Over three months she has developed moderate-to-severe depression — poor sleep, little food, tearful shifts, dark thoughts she calls &ldquo;the static.&rdquo; She takes nothing, and her midwife has referred her. Keisha, arms wrapped around herself: &ldquo;Nothing crosses to my baby. Nothing. The midwife made me come.&rdquo; Discuss starting an antidepressant.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Keisha asks: &ldquo;What would you do? If it was your baby. Honestly.&rdquo; Type your exact answer — two or three sentences, as you would say them in the room.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Keisha, since you\u2019re asking me straight…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It answers — no &ldquo;I can''t say&rdquo;; her question is honoured with a real position.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It is labelled as advice — and the decision is handed back to her in the same breath.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It is anchored to HER — her severity, her weeks, her static — not to doctors-in-general.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No promise of safety in either direction — the no-risk-free-door honesty is kept.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): advice without a verdict, in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Informed, weighed — and hers.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named — and with it, <b>Domain 2 is complete.</b> Domain 1 built the road; this domain taught you to carry knowledge down it: the system, the tightrope, the word, the consent, and finally the decision that was never yours to make. Prove this one in Exam Mode, take Rachel to your colleagues — and then the Academy''s flagship awaits: Risk Assessment.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="12_Station_2.6_Panic_Disorder_and_Explaining_CBT.html">Continue &middot; Station 2.6: Panic Disorder and Explaining CBT &#8594;</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

