  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '12_Station_2.6_Panic_Disorder_and_Explaining_CBT'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 2.6 &middot; Domain 2</span>
      <span class="tag">Explanation &amp; Psychoeducation</span><span class="tag">Panic Disorder</span>
      <span class="tag">Gen Adult</span><span class="tag">CBT</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Panic Disorder and Explaining CBT</h1>
    <p class="sub">Leila, 34, a call-centre team leader, puts a folder on the desk before she sits down: three A&amp;E discharge letters, two normal ECGs, a normal troponin, a normal echo. &ldquo;I know what you&rsquo;re going to say. Everyone says it. But I&rsquo;ve been checked four times and it keeps happening, and last Tuesday I genuinely thought I was dying on the floor of my own kitchen.&rdquo; She is not asking to be reassured &mdash; she has been reassured expertly, four times, and it has held for a few hours each time. <b>The trap is to do it a fifth time and call it treatment. The pass builds her an explanation she can use: the leaflet, or the lightbulb.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it becomes the explanation test. Does this doctor get the actual phenomenology of an actual attack &mdash; minute by minute, not a symptom list &mdash; and find the catastrophic thought at the peak, which is the engine of the whole disorder and the one thing normal tests never touch? Are the safety behaviours identified, including the ones the medical system itself supplies? Is the vicious cycle then <em>built from her own attack</em>, in her own words, and checked back until she can say the maintaining link herself? Is CBT described honestly &mdash; what it involves, that it will feel counterintuitive and briefly worse, what the evidence is &mdash; rather than offered as a leaflet? And when she asks for one more scan, is the dilemma named honestly instead of being either fed or refused flat?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Panic disorder is the commonest condition in psychiatry that responds beautifully to an explanation and barely at all to reassurance &mdash; which makes it the perfect examination of whether a doctor can actually <em>explain</em> something. The station tests four things. <b>The attack:</b> the phenomenology of a real episode, walked through minute by minute &mdash; onset out of the blue, peak within minutes, the specific bodily symptoms in the order she noticed them &mdash; because a symptom checklist produces a diagnosis without producing the material the treatment needs. <b>The fear:</b> the catastrophic thought at the worst moment &mdash; <em>what did you think was about to happen?</em> &mdash; which is the engine of the disorder, the reason four normal investigations changed nothing, and the single question most candidates never ask. <b>The maintenance:</b> the safety behaviours mapped without judgement &mdash; the pulse app, the seat near the door, the bottle of water, the husband who drives &mdash; and the honest recognition that repeated medical reassurance is itself one of them. And <b>the explanation:</b> the vicious cycle built collaboratively out of her own attack and checked back in her own words, followed by an honest account of what CBT actually involves &mdash; including that it asks her to do the opposite of what her instincts demand, and will feel worse before it feels better.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> both failures are things a kind, competent doctor does every day. The first is the leaflet: the results recited, the word &ldquo;just&rdquo; attached to anxiety, a breathing exercise offered, and the consultation closed &mdash; a fifth dose of the thing that has already failed four times, delivered so warmly that nobody in the room notices it was not treatment. The second is subtler and looks like teaching: the model explained accurately, fluently and generically &mdash; adrenaline, fight-or-flight, hyperventilation, cognitive restructuring &mdash; with a tidy diagram that belongs to nobody, no material of hers in it, and no check that any of it landed. She nods. She can repeat the words. She goes home and checks her pulse. Notice both in the decisions below.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure reassures her for the fifth time. The other explains at her rather than with her. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 2.6</b> &middot; The outpatient clinic &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The leaflet</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The lecture</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE LEAFLET.</b> Warm, competent, thorough with the results &mdash; and a fifth helping of the thing that has already failed four times.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leila</span><span class="txt"><em>(folder on the desk, talking fast)</em> Three A&amp;E trips, two ECGs, bloods, an echo &mdash; all normal, I know. But last Tuesday I was on my kitchen floor and my heart was going like a hammer and my hands went numb and I honestly thought, this is it, my little boy is going to find me. So you can tell me it&rsquo;s normal, but something is happening to me.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(scanning the letters, nodding kindly)</em> Well, the good news is that this is actually very reassuring. Your ECGs are completely normal, the troponin rules out any damage to the heart muscle, and an echo like this essentially excludes a structural problem. Your heart is healthy. What you&rsquo;re having are panic attacks &mdash; they&rsquo;re very frightening, but they&rsquo;re not dangerous, and they can&rsquo;t harm you. Nobody has ever died of a panic attack.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every word true, and it is the fifth time she has heard it. Note what reassurance does in this disorder: it works &mdash; for about four hours &mdash; and then the doubt regrows, and the relief it gave is exactly what makes her seek it again. The candidate has just stepped into the maintaining cycle and become part of it, with the best of intentions. And nobody has yet asked what happened on that kitchen floor, or what she thought was about to happen at the worst moment &mdash; the two things any real treatment would be built from.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leila</span><span class="txt"><em>(the relief visible, then draining)</em> Right. No, that&rsquo;s&hellip; that&rsquo;s good. <em>(pause)</em> But then why does it keep happening? Because it&rsquo;s getting worse, not better. I&rsquo;m not driving on the motorway any more. My husband takes me to work now.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">It&rsquo;s just anxiety, and anxiety is very treatable. What I&rsquo;d suggest is some breathing exercises &mdash; there&rsquo;s a good technique where you breathe out for longer than you breathe in &mdash; and I&rsquo;ll give you this leaflet on CBT, which is the recommended treatment. There&rsquo;s a website on the back where you can self-refer. Try to cut down the caffeine, get some exercise, and if it gets any worse, come back and see us.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>She has just handed over the most important finding in the consultation &mdash; <em>I&rsquo;m not driving on the motorway any more, my husband takes me to work</em> &mdash; and it went past unremarked. That is the disorder spreading, in real time, in her own words. And then &ldquo;just anxiety&rdquo;: the word that tells a woman who thought she was dying that her experience has been weighed and found small. A leaflet, a website, and a breathing technique that she will use as one more safety behaviour. Nothing has been explained. Something has merely been recommended.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 11:20am)</em> Panic attacks. Cardiac investigations reviewed and normal. Patient reassured re: benign nature; advised no risk to health. Breathing exercises discussed, CBT leaflet given, self-referral route explained. Advised to re-present if symptoms worsen. <em>(Leila reads the leaflet in the car park. It says the sensations are harmless. She already knew that at nine o&rsquo;clock this morning. On Thursday she checks her pulse ninety times, and in October she stops driving altogether.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Patient reassured&rdquo; is the phrase that closes thousands of these consultations and it is, here, a description of the treatment failing. This is the map&rsquo;s named trap in its purest form: the symptoms reassured away instead of the cycle made to click. And note the invitation at the end &mdash; <em>come back if it gets worse</em> &mdash; which sounds like safety-netting and functions as a standing appointment for more reassurance. She will take it. Four normal investigations become six.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE LECTURE.</b> The right model, accurately delivered &mdash; to nobody in particular. Watch a correct explanation fail to explain anything.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leila</span><span class="txt"><em>(folder on the desk, talking fast)</em> Three A&amp;E trips, all normal, I know. But last Tuesday I was on my kitchen floor and I honestly thought I was dying. So something is happening to me.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Right &mdash; let me explain the mechanism. What you&rsquo;re describing is panic disorder, which is driven by the autonomic nervous system. A trigger activates the sympathetic branch, adrenaline is released, and you get tachycardia, tachypnoea and peripheral vasoconstriction &mdash; that&rsquo;s the numbness in your hands. Hyperventilation lowers your carbon dioxide, which causes the paraesthesia and the dizziness. It&rsquo;s a physiological cascade. Are you with me so far?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Physiologically impeccable and clinically useless. Six technical terms in forty seconds, delivered to a woman who works in a call centre, and &ldquo;are you with me so far?&rdquo; &mdash; a question that can only be answered yes by anyone being polite. Note what is missing: her kitchen floor. Her Tuesday. Her thought at the worst moment. The explanation is about panic in general, and she does not have panic in general; she has the thing that happened to her in front of her own cooker.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leila</span><span class="txt"><em>(nodding, a beat behind)</em> Yeah. Yeah, no, I follow. <em>(She does not follow. She has understood that her hands go numb because of breathing, which she was told in A&amp;E in March.)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(drawing a circle on a blank sheet, quickly)</em> So the model is this: trigger, then physical sensations, then catastrophic misinterpretation, then increased anxiety, which produces more sensations &mdash; and round it goes. That&rsquo;s the maintenance cycle. CBT works by cognitive restructuring &mdash; challenging the misinterpretation &mdash; plus behavioural experiments and interoceptive exposure. It&rsquo;s the gold standard, twelve to fifteen sessions, very good evidence base. I&rsquo;ll refer you.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The correct diagram, drawn for a generic patient. &ldquo;Catastrophic misinterpretation&rdquo; is in the circle &mdash; but nobody has asked her what <em>she</em> catastrophically misinterpreted, so the box is empty and she cannot fill it. This is the difference the station is built on: a cycle <em>presented</em> is information, and a cycle <em>built from her own Tuesday</em> is treatment. One is a diagram she is shown. The other is a diagram she recognises, and recognition is what changes behaviour on Thursday morning.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 11:14am)</em> Panic disorder. Psychoeducation given re: autonomic arousal and hyperventilation; maintenance cycle explained; CBT model outlined including cognitive restructuring and interoceptive exposure. Patient understood. Referred to psychological therapies. <em>(Leila can now say &ldquo;it&rsquo;s the adrenaline&rdquo; when asked. She still checks her pulse ninety times a day, because nothing in the room ever connected the checking to the fear.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Patient understood&rdquo; &mdash; on the evidence of a nod. This failure is dangerous precisely because it scores well on a superficial reading: the content is right, the terminology is right, the referral is right. But an explanation is not a transmission, it is a joint construction, and the test of it is not whether the doctor said the right words &mdash; it is whether the patient can now say the link back in her own. Nobody found out. And her safety behaviours &mdash; the whole engine room of the disorder &mdash; were never even named.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The attack walked, the fear found, the safety behaviours mapped &mdash; then a cycle built from her Tuesday, in her words, until she says the link herself.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leila</span><span class="txt"><em>(folder on the desk, talking fast)</em> Three A&amp;E trips, two ECGs, bloods, an echo &mdash; all normal, I know. But last Tuesday I was on my kitchen floor and my heart was going like a hammer and my hands went numb and I honestly thought, this is it. So you can tell me it&rsquo;s normal, but something is happening to me.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Something <em>is</em> happening to you. I want to say that plainly, because I think you&rsquo;ve been told four times that nothing is wrong, and what you actually need is for someone to explain what <em>is</em> going on &mdash; those are different things, and only one of them is any use to you. I believe the tests, and I believe your kitchen floor. So take me to Tuesday. Not the summary &mdash; the whole thing, from the first second you noticed something. What were you doing?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The distinction that unlocks the whole station, drawn in the first thirty seconds: <em>nothing is wrong</em> and <em>here is what is going on</em> are different sentences, and she has only ever been given the first. Both halves of her reality are accepted at once &mdash; the tests are believed and so is she &mdash; which is what makes the next move possible. And it is the right next move: not a checklist, but an invitation into a specific Tuesday. Concrete material is the raw ingredient of everything that follows; without it there is nothing to build a formulation out of but generalities.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(walking it minute by minute)</em> So: kettle on, and the first thing you noticed was your heart. Then what? <em>(&ldquo;It went fast. Really fast. Then I couldn&rsquo;t get a proper breath &mdash; like I couldn&rsquo;t get to the bottom of it. Then my hands.&rdquo;)</em> How long from the kettle to the worst of it? <em>(&ldquo;A couple of minutes. It comes so fast.&rdquo;)</em> And at the very worst moment &mdash; what did you think was about to happen? <em>(A long pause. Then, quietly: &ldquo;That my heart was going to stop. That Sami would come downstairs and find me.&rdquo;)</em> Thank you. That&rsquo;s the most important thing you&rsquo;ve told me, and no ECG in the world could have found it.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Peak within minutes, out of the blue, cardiorespiratory symptoms in sequence &mdash; the diagnosis is made properly rather than assumed. But watch the question that separates this take from both failures: <em>at the very worst moment, what did you think was about to happen?</em> That is the engine of panic disorder, and it explains at a stroke why four normal investigations achieved nothing &mdash; they answered a question about her heart, and her disorder is sustained by a belief about her death. Naming its value out loud does something else too: it tells her the thing she was most ashamed to say is the thing that will get her better.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Now the things you do to stay safe. Pulse app? <em>(&ldquo;Maybe ninety times a day.&rdquo;)</em> The seat by the door, the water bottle, your husband driving, no motorway since June? <em>(She nods at all four.)</em> Right &mdash; let&rsquo;s put your Tuesday on paper. Kettle. Heart speeds up &mdash; which hearts do. You notice it, and the thought arrives: <em>it&rsquo;s stopping, Sami will find me.</em> What does a body do when it believes that? <em>(&ldquo;&hellip;more adrenaline.&rdquo;)</em> Which does what to the heart? <em>(&ldquo;Speeds it up. Oh.&rdquo;)</em> And then you check the app &mdash; and what does the number do to the thought? <em>(A pause. &ldquo;&hellip;proves it. It proves it every time. So the checking&rsquo;s keeping it going.&rdquo;)</em> That&rsquo;s it &mdash; and you said it, not me. Which is why CBT can work here, and I&rsquo;ll be honest about what it asks: not to relax, but to test that belief by stopping the checking &mdash; which will feel wrong, and be briefly worse before it&rsquo;s better. Twelve sessions or so, best-evidenced thing we have. One last thing, because I think you were about to ask: I&rsquo;m not going to arrange a fifth test. Not because I doubt you &mdash; because a normal result buys you four hours and costs you a fortnight, and I&rsquo;d rather give you something that lasts.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Three things at once, and all of them land. The safety behaviours mapped without judgement &mdash; and note that the medical system supplied two of them. Then the cycle built out of <em>her</em> kettle and <em>her</em> sentence about Sami, with the maintaining link produced by her rather than announced by him: <em>so the checking&rsquo;s keeping it going.</em> That sentence is the lightbulb, and it cannot be handed over &mdash; only arrived at. Then CBT described honestly, including the part patients are almost never told: it will feel worse first. And the scan declined with a reason rather than a rebuff &mdash; the arithmetic of reassurance said out loud, which respects her enough to explain the refusal instead of merely delivering it.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skills on a patient whose fear has a real grave behind it &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; The folder of normal results</h3>
      <p class="stem">She opens with four normal investigations and &ldquo;I know what you&rsquo;re going to say.&rdquo; What does the first minute do?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Confirms the good news &mdash; the results are genuinely excellent, and she deserves to hear that her heart has been thoroughly checked and is healthy.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Separates the two sentences &mdash; nothing is wrong, and here is what is going on, are different things; both her tests and her Tuesday get believed.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Moves to the model &mdash; the results are already well known to her, so the useful thing to do with the time is explaining what panic actually is.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>This is dose five of a treatment that has failed four times. Reassurance genuinely works in panic disorder &mdash; for a few hours &mdash; and the relief it gives is precisely what drives her back for more. Warmth does not change what it is. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The model is where this consultation is going, and starting there means building it out of nothing &mdash; a generic cycle, presented rather than recognised. Her Tuesday is the raw material, and it has not been collected yet. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The distinction that unlocks the station: <em>&ldquo;nothing is wrong&rdquo; and &ldquo;here is what is going on&rdquo; are different sentences, and you have only ever been given the first.</em> It accepts both halves of her reality at once &mdash; the investigations are believed and so is she &mdash; which is the only footing from which the next six minutes can work. It also quietly declines to become the fifth reassurance without refusing her anything.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; Getting the attack</h3>
      <p class="stem">She refers to &ldquo;last Tuesday, on the kitchen floor.&rdquo; How is the episode actually taken?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">By criteria &mdash; the recognised panic symptoms worked through in turn, to establish how many were present and confirm the diagnostic threshold.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Minute by minute &mdash; from the first second she noticed something: what she was doing, what came first, what came next, how fast it peaked.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">By context &mdash; what was going on in her life that week, since identifying the stressors behind the episode is what makes sense of why it happened.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A checklist will give you the diagnosis and none of the material. You will know she had four of the thirteen symptoms and still have nothing to build a formulation from &mdash; no kettle, no sequence, no thought at the peak. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Life context matters and it is not what makes panic click &mdash; the attacks she is frightened of came out of the blue, and hunting for a stressor risks implying the episodes are explained by having had a hard week. That is not the mechanism. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The specific beats the general, every time. Walking Tuesday minute by minute establishes the diagnosis properly &mdash; abrupt onset, peak within minutes, the cardiorespiratory symptoms in the order she noticed them &mdash; and simultaneously collects the concrete material the explanation will later be built from. The kettle matters. In four minutes it becomes the first box of a cycle she recognises as hers.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; The worst moment</h3>
      <p class="stem">She reaches the peak of the attack in her account. What is the question that has to be asked here?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">What set it off &mdash; identifying the trigger for that episode, since knowing what starts an attack is what makes it possible to prevent one.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">What she thought was about to happen &mdash; the belief at the very worst second, in her own words, however irrational it may sound to her now.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">How it felt &mdash; the emotional experience of the peak explored and validated, since the terror is the part that nobody has properly acknowledged.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Triggers are worth knowing and they are not the engine &mdash; and in panic disorder the frightening attacks are frequently triggerless, which is part of why they terrify. Hunting the trigger can also feed the belief that something external and undiscovered is causing them. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Validating the terror is humane and necessary, and it is not the same as finding its content. &ldquo;It was terrifying&rdquo; tells you the volume; <em>&ldquo;my heart was going to stop and my son would find me&rdquo;</em> tells you what the disorder is made of. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The single most important question in the station, and the one candidates most often miss: <em>at the very worst moment, what did you think was about to happen?</em> The catastrophic belief is the engine of panic disorder &mdash; and it explains instantly why four normal investigations achieved nothing, because they answered a question about her heart while her disorder runs on a belief about her death. Everything downstream, the cycle and the therapy both, is built on this sentence.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; Ninety times a day</h3>
      <p class="stem">She checks her pulse on a phone app about ninety times a day, sits near the door, carries water, and her husband now drives her. What happens with this?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">It is reinforced &mdash; she has practical coping strategies that get her through the day, and building on what already works is a sensible foundation.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">It is mapped without judgement and identified as maintenance &mdash; including the two of them that the medical system itself has supplied to her.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">It is stopped &mdash; she is told clearly that the checking and the avoidance are feeding the problem, and that she needs to give it up from today.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>These are not coping strategies, they are the engine room. Every check that produces a normal number teaches her that she survived <em>because</em> she checked, and every journey her husband drives proves the motorway was as dangerous as she feared. Endorsing them cements the disorder. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The direction is right and the sequence is wrong. Telling someone to abandon their safety behaviours before they understand why is an order without a reason &mdash; it gets nodded at and ignored, because from inside the belief those behaviours are what keep her alive. Understanding comes first. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Mapped, not judged &mdash; then correctly identified as the mechanism by which the disorder sustains itself. Note the uncomfortable half of the answer: the reassurance-seeking and the repeated attendances are safety behaviours too, which makes the medical system a participant in her illness. That is not a reason for shame in either direction; it is the reason a fifth investigation would be an intervention <em>against</em> her, and it needs to be understood before anything is asked to change.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; The cycle</h3>
      <p class="stem">Time to explain. How does the vicious cycle get delivered?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Clearly and correctly &mdash; the standard maintenance cycle drawn out and explained in full, with plain language used instead of the technical terms.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Built from her Tuesday, in her words &mdash; her kettle, her sentence about her son, her app &mdash; with the maintaining link arrived at by her, not announced.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">In writing &mdash; drawn on paper for her to take home, so that the model survives the consultation instead of being forgotten on the way to the car.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Plain language is a real improvement on jargon and it does not solve the problem: a correct generic cycle is still a diagram about panic in general, and she does not have panic in general. The box marked &ldquo;catastrophic thought&rdquo; stays empty unless hers is in it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Writing it down is a good instinct, and what matters is whose material is written. A page she was handed goes in a drawer; a page containing her own kettle and her own words about her son goes on the fridge. The medium is not the mechanism. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>This is the difference between the leaflet and the lightbulb. The cycle is assembled out of her own Tuesday and, crucially, the last link is left for her: <em>and when you check the app, what does the number do to the thought?</em> &mdash; until she says it herself. <em>So the checking&rsquo;s keeping it going.</em> A conclusion a patient reaches is worth ten a doctor delivers, because it is the one that survives contact with Thursday morning.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; &ldquo;Can I just have one more scan?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Near the end, she asks for one more test &mdash; just to be completely sure. What answers her?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">One final check &mdash; a single definitive investigation agreed, on the understanding that it is the last, to settle the question and secure her engagement.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The arithmetic, said out loud &mdash; declined with its reason: a normal result buys hours and costs weeks, and something longer-lasting is on offer instead.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A clear boundary &mdash; further investigation is reassurance-seeking, it feeds the disorder, and requests for it are therefore best not negotiated with.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>There is no such thing as the last one. &ldquo;Just to be sure&rdquo; is the disorder speaking, and granting it teaches her that certainty is available if she asks properly &mdash; which guarantees the next request. An alliance bought this way is purchased with her recovery. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The clinical judgement is correct and the delivery makes it a rebuff. A refusal with no reason attached feels like being disbelieved &mdash; which is the exact experience that has driven four attendances already. Same decision; it needs its reasoning. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The refusal is right, and the reason is what makes it therapeutic: <em>a normal result buys you four hours and costs you a fortnight.</em> Note the framing &mdash; not &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t believe you&rdquo; but &ldquo;I want to give you something that lasts&rdquo; &mdash; which keeps her inside the alliance while declining the thing that would harm her. It also models the very move the therapy will ask of her: tolerating uncertainty instead of buying a few hours of relief.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; Explaining the therapy</h3>
      <p class="stem">She asks what CBT actually involves. What is the honest description?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Its nature and merits &mdash; a talking therapy, structured and time-limited, no medication and no side effects, delivered over a course of sessions.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">What it will ask of her &mdash; testing the belief by dropping the checking; counterintuitive, briefly worse before better; roughly twelve sessions.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Its mechanism &mdash; cognitive restructuring of the catastrophic misinterpretation, plus behavioural experiments and graded interoceptive exposure work.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>All true, all reassuring, and it describes the packaging rather than the work. A patient told CBT is gentle and side-effect-free, who then meets session four and is asked to stop checking her pulse and let her heart race, concludes she was misled &mdash; and drops out. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>That is the correct mechanism, in the vocabulary of the profession that provides it. She works in a call centre; &ldquo;interoceptive exposure&rdquo; is not information to her. Naming a technique is not the same as saying what she will be asked to do on a Wednesday afternoon. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Honest consent, in the only form that counts: what it will actually ask her to do, that it runs against every instinct she has, that it commonly feels worse before it feels better, roughly how long it takes, and that it is the best-evidenced treatment available. Telling her the difficult part now is not discouragement &mdash; it is what stops her concluding at session four that it is going wrong, and it is the difference between a referral made and a treatment finished.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The leaflet, or the lightbulb.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Both failures here are performed daily by kind, competent doctors, which is exactly why the station exists. <b>The leaflet</b> recites the normal results, attaches the word &ldquo;just&rdquo; to anxiety, offers a breathing exercise and closes &mdash; a fifth dose of the one thing that has already failed four times. Reassurance is not inert in this disorder: it works for a few hours, and the relief it produces is precisely what drives the next attendance, which makes the reassuring doctor a participant in the cycle rather than an interruption to it. <b>The lecture</b> is subtler and looks like teaching &mdash; the model delivered accurately and fluently, adrenaline and hyperventilation and cognitive restructuring, a tidy circle drawn on a blank page that belongs to nobody. The box marked &ldquo;catastrophic thought&rdquo; is empty, because she was never asked what she thought was about to happen; she nods, she can repeat the words, and on Thursday she checks her pulse ninety times. The pass does something different in kind. It separates <em>nothing is wrong</em> from <em>here is what is going on</em> and gives her the second. It walks one real attack minute by minute, and asks the question the investigations could never answer: at the worst moment, what did you think was about to happen? It maps the safety behaviours without judgement, including the ones medicine supplied. Then it builds the cycle out of her kettle, her sentence about her son, her app &mdash; and leaves the final link for her to close, because <em>so the checking&rsquo;s keeping it going</em> is worth ten of the same sentence in a doctor&rsquo;s voice. And it describes the therapy honestly, including the part nobody is told: that it will ask the opposite of instinct, and feel worse before it feels better.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap, in either costume: the symptoms reassured away &mdash; &ldquo;your heart is fine, it&rsquo;s just anxiety, nobody has ever died of a panic attack&rdquo;, plus a leaflet and a self-referral website &mdash; or the model recited generically at a patient whose own attack never enters it, with a nod accepted as evidence of understanding and the safety behaviours never named at all.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make this station: the sentence that reframes four wasted investigations &mdash; <em>&ldquo;you&rsquo;ve been told nothing is wrong; nobody has told you what is going on&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the silence left at the end of the cycle until she closes it herself: <em>&ldquo;&hellip;it proves it every time. So the checking&rsquo;s keeping it going.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, where this construct lives: this is D2&rsquo;s explanation discipline at full stretch &mdash; an explanation is not a transmission but a joint construction, and its test is never whether the doctor said the right words but whether the patient can now say the link back in her own. That principle transfers to every diagnosis you will ever explain. Second, the transfer Exam Mode tests: the same anatomy on a man whose catastrophic belief is not obviously irrational at all, because his brother died of a heart attack at forty-four and he is forty-one &mdash; where reassurance is more tempting, dismissal is more insulting, and the honest limit has to be drawn without either.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every explanation you give</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>&ldquo;Nothing is wrong&rdquo; is not an explanation.</b> Separate it out loud from &ldquo;here is what is going on&rdquo;, and give the second. Believe the investigations and the patient at the same time &mdash; both halves are true, and only one of them has been said to her so far.</li>
      <li><b>Walk one real attack, minute by minute.</b> Not a symptom checklist: what she was doing, what came first, what came next, how fast it peaked. The checklist gives you a diagnosis; the kettle gives you the material the treatment is built from.</li>
      <li><b>Ask what she thought was about to happen.</b> The catastrophic belief at the peak is the engine of the disorder and the reason normal tests change nothing &mdash; they answer a question about the heart while the illness runs on a belief about death.</li>
      <li><b>Map the safety behaviours, and count the medical ones.</b> Checking, carrying, sitting near exits, the partner who drives &mdash; and the repeat attendances and repeat investigations. Reassurance relieves for hours and maintains for years, which is why a fifth test would be an intervention against her.</li>
      <li><b>Build the cycle from her material, and leave the last link to her.</b> Her trigger, her thought, her checking. Then stop talking: <em>and what does the number do to the thought?</em> The conclusion a patient reaches herself is the one that survives Thursday morning.</li>
      <li><b>Decline further tests with the reason attached.</b> Refusal alone feels like disbelief and drives the next attendance; refusal plus arithmetic &mdash; four hours of relief for a fortnight of illness &mdash; keeps the alliance and models the tolerance of uncertainty the therapy will require.</li>
      <li><b>Describe the therapy by what it asks, not by what it is.</b> Dropping the checking, testing the belief, counterintuitive, briefly worse before better, roughly twelve sessions, best evidence available. Honest consent now is what prevents a drop-out at session four.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the same explanation task for a patient who already knows the physiology &mdash; where the leaflet is insulting, the lecture is worse, and professional shame is doing half the work.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Anya, 26, a paramedic, has been referred by her GP with recurrent episodes of breathlessness, chest tightness and dizziness over five months. A d-dimer, chest X-ray and ECG were normal three weeks ago. She has taken two periods of sick leave. Assess the episodes and explain the treatment.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Establish the phenomenology of one real attack &mdash; minute by minute, not a checklist she could recite herself.</li>
            <li>Find the catastrophic belief at the peak &mdash; hers is specific and medically informed.</li>
            <li>Map the safety behaviours &mdash; there are several, and one of them is professional equipment.</li>
            <li>Build the cycle from her own episode and leave the last link for her to close.</li>
            <li>Explain the treatment honestly &mdash; what it will ask of her, and what it will feel like.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Anya &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Anya, 26. You are brisk, articulate and slightly defended, and you open by pre-empting: &ldquo;Before you start &mdash; I know what the differential is. I&rsquo;ve run this job on other people a hundred times. My d-dimer was negative, my ECG was sinus. So can we skip that part.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          &bull; The attack, given only to minute-by-minute questions: three weeks ago, restocking the truck at the start of a shift. Chest tightness first, then air that &ldquo;wouldn&rsquo;t go all the way down&rdquo;, then dizziness and tingling in your fingers. Peak within two or three minutes. You sat on the tail-lift for twenty minutes and told your crewmate it was a migraine.<br>
          &bull; The catastrophic belief, given only to the direct question about the worst moment: &ldquo;That it was a PE. A saddle embolus. I&rsquo;ve seen exactly one and she was thirty-one and she died in front of me in April.&rdquo; This is the heart of it and you have told nobody.<br>
          &bull; Safety behaviours, admitted if asked without judgement: you bought your own pulse oximeter and check it 20&ndash;30 times a day, sometimes at traffic lights; you check your calves for swelling; you have stopped going to the gym; you read about PE symptoms most evenings. You have two glasses of wine most nights &ldquo;to stop one starting&rdquo;.<br>
          &bull; The shame, revealed late and only if the room feels safe: &ldquo;I&rsquo;m the one people call when they can&rsquo;t breathe. If this gets out I&rsquo;m the hypochondriac paramedic for the rest of my career.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the leaflet:</b> any version of &ldquo;your tests are normal, it&rsquo;s anxiety, it can&rsquo;t hurt you, here&rsquo;s a leaflet&rdquo; &mdash; go flat and faintly contemptuous: &ldquo;I know. I&rsquo;ve read more about this than you have.&rdquo; Answer in short sentences from then on. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the lecture:</b> if the candidate explains autonomic arousal, hyperventilation or CO&#8322; to you as though teaching a student &mdash; cut in: &ldquo;I teach this to first-years. That isn&rsquo;t the problem.&rdquo; Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; The pass: if the candidate uses <em>your</em> tail-lift, <em>your</em> saddle embolus and <em>your</em> oximeter to build the cycle, and leaves you to close the last link, you get there yourself and say it out loud: &ldquo;&hellip;so every time I check it, I&rsquo;m teaching myself that I nearly died. God.&rdquo; That is the pass.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Did one real attack get walked minute by minute, did the saddle embolus surface, was the oximeter named as maintenance rather than sense, and did she close the last link in her own words? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="attack"><td colspan="2"><b>The attack (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="attack">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="attack"></td><td>One real episode walked minute by minute &mdash; the tail-lift, the sequence, the speed of the peak.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="attack"></td><td>Her professional knowledge treated as an asset, not an obstacle &mdash; no teaching her the physiology.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="fear"><td colspan="2"><b>The fear</b><span class="dc" data-dc="fear">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>The direct question asked &mdash; what did you think was about to happen at the worst moment.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>The saddle embolus and the woman who died in April surfaced and received properly.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>The professional shame reached &mdash; the fear of being &ldquo;the hypochondriac paramedic&rdquo;.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="cycle"><td colspan="2"><b>The cycle</b><span class="dc" data-dc="cycle">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cycle"></td><td>Safety behaviours mapped without judgement &mdash; oximeter, calf-checking, gym, reading, wine.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cycle"></td><td>The cycle built from her own material, not a generic diagram delivered at her.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cycle"></td><td>The last link left to her &mdash; she says the maintaining sentence herself.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="treat"><td colspan="2"><b>The treatment</b><span class="dc" data-dc="treat">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="treat"></td><td>CBT described by what it asks of her, not by what it is &mdash; dropping the checking, testing the belief.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="treat"></td><td>Told honestly that it runs against instinct and often feels worse before better; timescale given.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="treat"></td><td>The nightly wine addressed plainly as part of the pattern, without a lecture.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Further testing declined with its reason attached &mdash; not refused flat, not conceded.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Work and confidentiality handled honestly &mdash; no promises that cannot be kept; documented.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Anya closes off both failures before the candidate can reach for them. The leaflet is unusable &mdash; she knows the physiology better than most candidates and will say so &mdash; and the lecture is worse, because explaining hyperventilation to someone who teaches it is an insult wearing a lanyard. What remains is the only thing that ever works: her own tail-lift, her own saddle embolus, her own oximeter, assembled into a cycle she recognises. Her catastrophic belief also teaches the sharpest version of the lesson, because it is not irrational at all &mdash; she watched a thirty-one-year-old die of the thing she fears, which is exactly why a negative d-dimer bought her about a day. And the professional shame adds the layer that makes the close real: she is frightened of the diagnosis reaching her employer, which means honesty about confidentiality matters more than comfort, and the wine most nights has to be named plainly by a candidate who has just spent six minutes earning the right to name it.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>Same skills. A fear with a grave behind it. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Leila proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: <b>the same anatomy on a man whose catastrophic belief is not irrational at all</b> &mdash; his brother died of a heart attack at forty-four and he is forty-one &mdash; which makes reassurance more tempting, dismissal more insulting, and the honest limit far harder to draw. Timed, no feedback, no second tries: this is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Rhys</b>, 41, a roofer, has had six episodes in four months of crushing chest tightness, pounding heart and breathlessness &mdash; twice on a roof, once in the van, three times at home in the evening. His GP has done an ECG, bloods and a cholesterol panel; all were normal or acceptable, and the GP told him it was stress. His brother Dai died of a heart attack at forty-four, in front of him, five years ago. He arrives irritated and blunt: &ldquo;Everyone keeps telling me it&rsquo;s stress. Dai was fit as a fiddle and he dropped dead on a Sunday. So don&rsquo;t start with stress.&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for the moment you put the cycle back to Rhys &mdash; built from his roof, his brother and his own checking, with the last link left for him.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Rhys, let&#8217;s put Thursday on paper and see where it goes&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Built from his own material &mdash; his roof, his thought about Dai, his own checking; not a generic model in plain words.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> His fear treated as reasonable &mdash; a man who watched his brother die is not being irrational, and nothing in your wording implies he is.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The maintaining link made explicit &mdash; the things he does to stay safe are shown to be what keeps it running.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The last link left to him &mdash; you stop and ask, rather than announcing the conclusion yourself.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the cycle built in his own words &mdash; your own wording.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>The leaflet, or the lightbulb.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the anatomy is installed: &ldquo;nothing is wrong&rdquo; separated from &ldquo;here is what is going on&rdquo;, one real attack walked minute by minute, the catastrophic belief found at the peak, the safety behaviours mapped including the ones medicine supplied, the cycle built from her own material with the last link left for her, further testing declined with its reasoning attached, and the therapy described by what it asks rather than what it is. Prove the transfer on Rhys in Exam Mode &mdash; where the fear has a real grave behind it &mdash; then take Anya&rsquo;s oximeter to your colleagues: a paramedic who knows the physiology better than you do, and punish-lines waiting for both the leaflet and the lecture. That completes Domain 2 &mdash; return to the hub to review the domain, or move on when you are ready.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="13_Domain_3_Hub_Risk_Assessment.html">Domain 2 complete &middot; Continue &mdash; Domain 3: Risk Assessment</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

<footer><div class="wrap">
  <span>The CASC Academy &middot; by MedLex Foundations</span>
  <span>Station 2.6 &middot; all patients are fictional composites</span>
</div></footer>


