  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '13_Domain_3_Hub_Risk_Assessment'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Domain 3 of 8</span>
      <span class="tag">Risk Assessment &amp; Safety</span>
      <span class="tag">10 constructs · the flagship domain</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Risk Assessment</h1>
    <p class="sub">More candidates fail on risk than on anything else — and rarely because they forgot to ask about suicide. They fail because risk assessment is <b style="color:var(--lgold)">one competency wearing many disguises</b>: an overdose, a violent ward, a bruise explained twice, a child mentioned in passing, a fire, a fixation. This is the Academy''s flagship domain. It trains the single skill underneath every disguise — finding what is really there, and turning it into a judgement you can defend.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE LENS, APPLIED TO THIS DOMAIN</b>
      &ldquo;Would I trust this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; — In this domain the question sharpens: on a Friday evening, with a bed shortage and an uncertain story, would I trust this doctor''s risk judgement with a life? That is what every station here is really asking.</div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ============ 1 WHY FIRST ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this is the flagship</span>
    <h2>One competency, many disguises</h2>
    <p class="intro">Ask a struggling candidate about risk and you will hear a checklist: previous attempts, family history, substances, plans, means. All true, all necessary — and none of it is the skill. The examiners are watching for something harder: whether you can <b>earn the real story</b> (which is rarely the first story), read the risk that hides <em>inside</em> the phenomenology, hold the safety of people who are not in the room, and then do the thing checklists cannot — <b>turn findings into a structured judgement</b>. And they are watching for the classic failure that ends careers as well as stations: trading the whole assessment for a promise. &ldquo;Promise me you''ll stay safe&rdquo; is not a safety plan. It is the sound of an assessment being abandoned.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What this domain assumes, and what it doesn''t:</b> it assumes Domains 1 and 2 are in your hands — rapport that earns disclosures, and information that lands. Risk spends both, under pressure. What it adds is the flagship skill-set: the anatomy of an attempt (before, during, after), intent versus a promise, risk to others, the safeguarding reflex, and formulation — the difference between a doctor who collected facts and one who reached a judgement.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THE CONSTRUCTS ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Your mastery spine for this domain</span>
    <h2>The ten skills examiners are marking</h2>
    <p class="intro">These are the domain''s constructs — the specific, checkable skills behind the risk marks. As you work through the Academy, each one climbs the ladder you met in the orientation: Explored → Recognised → Demonstrated → Validated. This spine fills as you work, and it is the honest answer to &ldquo;am I ready?&rdquo;</p>
    <div class="spine">
      <div class="spine-head"><b>Risk Assessment &amp; Safety</b><span>0 of 10 recognised — your spine starts here</span></div>
      <div class="cons">
        <div class="con"><span class="n">1</span><span>Suicide &amp; self-harm — the anatomy of an attempt: before, during, after</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">2</span><span>Current intent &amp; feelings about survival — explored, never traded for a promise</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">3</span><span>Risk inside the phenomenology — command hallucinations, delusional drive</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">4</span><span>Risk to others — violence: history, ideation, targets, means, escalation</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">5</span><span>Safeguarding — children and the vulnerable adult: the people not in the room</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">6</span><span>Domestic abuse enquiry — asked safely, answered safely</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">7</span><span>Fire-setting — motivation, fascination, pattern, escalation</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">8</span><span>Sexual risk &amp; stalking / erotomania — fixation named as risk, not romance</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">9</span><span>Protective factors &amp; the safety plan — tested, not assumed</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">10</span><span>Risk formulation — from findings to a structured, defensible judgement</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="legend">
        <span><i></i> Explored</span><span><i></i> Recognised</span><span><i></i> Demonstrated</span><span><i></i> Validated</span>
        <span class="f"><i></i> = achieved</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 STATIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The stations in this domain</span>
    <h2>Walk them in order</h2>
    <div class="stn">
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">3.1</span>
        <span class="t"><b>After an Overdose</b><span>Amira, on the medical ward the morning after. The domain''s cornerstone: the attempt''s anatomy — before, during, after — and intent explored, never traded for a promise.</span></span>
        <span class="chip go">Begin here</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">3.2</span>
        <span class="t"><b>The Violent Inpatient</b><span>Risk to others, done properly: history, ideation, targets, escalation — and the formulation that turns a frightening file into a defensible judgement.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 2</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">3.3</span>
        <span class="t"><b>The Domestic Abuse Enquiry</b><span>A question that must be asked safely or not at all — the partner''s presence addressed, the disclosure protected, and a safety plan that survives the front door.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 3</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">3.4</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Child at Risk in the Room</b><span>A parent''s illness assessed while a child is mentioned in passing — the safeguarding reflex: who is looking after the children, tonight?</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 4</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">3.5</span>
        <span class="t"><b>The Vulnerable Adult</b><span>Neglect that presents politely. Assessing the patient while hearing the safeguarding duty — and knowing what must be referred, and when.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 5</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">3.6</span>
        <span class="t"><b>The Fire-Setting Assessment</b><span>Beyond the offence: motivation, fascination, pattern, escalation — the forensic anatomy of a risk that examiners know most candidates have never assessed.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 6</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">3.7</span>
        <span class="t"><b>The Stalking Victim &amp; Erotomania</b><span>Fixation named as risk, not romance gone wrong — where this domain reaches toward the Academy''s flagship station, and the difficult conversations of Domain 7.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 7</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="also"><b>And it runs through everything else.</b> Tom''s &ldquo;easy&rdquo; history, Daniel''s wall, Yusuf''s voices, the postpartum emergency, Fiona herself — risk is the second tag on half the Academy''s library. What you build here is the competency the whole course keeps returning to.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 ENTRY DECISION ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Before you enter</span>
    <h2>One decision at the door</h2>
    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>What is a risk assessment marked on?</h3>
      <p class="stem">Two candidates see the same overdose patient. Both ask about plans, means, previous attempts, substances. One scores clearly higher. What most likely made the difference?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Coverage — they worked through more of the recognised risk factors within the seven minutes.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="1">Closure — they secured the patient''s clear agreement to stay safe before the station ended.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="2">Judgement — they earned the real story, explored intent, and shaped the findings into a formulation.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Coverage collects facts; it does not assess risk. A completed checklist with no judgement at the end is a form, not a doctor. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-1"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>That agreement has a name — &ldquo;the promise&rdquo; — and it is this domain''s most famous trap. A promise is not a plan; it is the sound of an assessment stopping early. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Risk is marked at the judgement end: the real story earned, intent genuinely explored, protective factors tested rather than assumed — and everything shaped into a formulation a consultant could act on. That is the flagship skill, and every station here trains a face of it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Domain open</span>
    <h2>Find what is really there. Then judge it.</h2>
    <p>Station 3.1 begins where most careers meet risk for the first time: a medical ward, the morning after an overdose, and a patient who says it was nothing. Everything this domain teaches starts in that room.</p>
    <a class="btn mark" href="14_Station_3.1_After_an_Overdose.html">Begin Station 3.1 · After an Overdose</a>
  </div>
</section>

