  on conflict (release_id, source_key) do update set title = excluded.title, is_published = true;
  insert into public.content_blocks (unit_id, source_key, block_type, sort_order, content)
  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '14_Station_3.1_After_an_Overdose'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 3.1 · Domain 3</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult · Liaison</span><span class="tag">Suicide &amp; self-harm</span>
    </div>
    <h1>After an Overdose</h1>
    <p class="sub">Amira, 22, a trainee hairdresser, took paracetamol last night after her relationship ended. She has been medically cleared, and you meet her on the ward the morning after: pale, composed, a little embarrassed. &ldquo;It was silly. I just want to go home.&rdquo; This is the flagship domain''s cornerstone — the assessment most careers meet first, and the one this exam watches hardest. Your task is not to collect her risk factors. It is to <b>reconstruct a night</b> — before, during, after — and turn what you find into a judgement.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here the question has a Friday-evening edge: if this doctor phoned me tonight about Amira, would their account let me make a safe decision — the real story earned, intent explored, and a formulation I could act on?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Every candidate can list risk factors; the college publishes them. So the examiners built a station where the list is nearly useless: a young woman, a modest overdose, a plausible cover story — &ldquo;it was silly&rdquo; — and everything that matters buried one honest conversation deep. What they are watching for is the flagship skill in its purest form: <b>the anatomy of the attempt</b> — the before (build-up, trigger, planning or impulse), the during (what she took, what she <em>believed</em> it would do, who she was with, what she wrote), and the after (how she was found, and what she felt on waking). Then the harder half: current intent, genuinely explored — and never, ever traded for a promise.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> her story is closed and the clock is running, and that pulls in two directions. Toward the checklist — efficient questions, tick-box answers, a form completed over a person unexamined. And toward comfort — warmth that colludes with &ldquo;it was silly&rdquo;, spares her the difficult questions, and buys her goodbye with a promise. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure completes a form over an unexamined person. The other comforts her past the assessment entirely. Read all three versions. Tap the gold circles for the examiner''s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 3.1</b> · Risk assessment after an overdose · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The checklist</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The comforter</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE CHECKLIST.</b> Efficient, comprehensive, and completed over a person who was never examined. Watch the facts pile up and the story stay hidden.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Amira</span><span class="txt">It was silly. I just want to go home.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I understand — I just have some questions I need to go through. So: how many tablets was it? Have you ever done anything like this before? Any mental health history? Do you drink? Any drugs? Family history of suicide?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Six questions in one breath, each one answerable in a word — and each word closing her further. This is a coroner''s list read to a living patient. The risk factors are being collected; the risk is not being assessed. And notice what will never be asked at this tempo: what she believed the tablets would do, and what she wrote before she took them.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Amira</span><span class="txt">Fourteen, I think. No. No. Sometimes. No. No. <em>(arms folded, eyes on the window)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">And any thoughts of harming yourself right now? ... No? That''s good. Low mood at all — scale of one to ten? Sleep? Appetite? Okay. Nearly done.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Any thoughts right now — no? good&rdquo; is intent assessed at the speed of a smear test. A one-word no from a closed patient carries almost no information, and it has just been banked as reassurance. The form is nearly complete. The night has not been mentioned once.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Right — that''s everything I need. I''ll speak to the team about getting you home. <em>(the file will read: 14 tablets, no plans, denies current ideation — low risk)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;That''s everything I need&rdquo; — and nothing that mattered: not the deleted message, not what she believed fourteen paracetamol would do, not her first feeling on waking. The checklist''s file entry is technically accurate and clinically empty. A judgement was never formed, because a story was never found. From the chair: this is how risk assessments end up read aloud at inquests.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE COMFORTER.</b> Warm, kind, on her side — and watch the assessment dissolve into reassurance, sealed with the domain''s most famous trap.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Amira</span><span class="txt">It was silly. I just want to go home.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Oh, Amira. Breakups are absolutely awful — honestly, anyone can reach a breaking point after something like that. You''ve been through the mill, and the last thing you need is me interrogating you. We don''t have to go over the details if it''s upsetting.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every sentence kind; every sentence a door closing. &ldquo;Anyone can reach a breaking point&rdquo; has just normalised an overdose; &ldquo;we don''t have to go over the details&rdquo; has just cancelled the assessment. The warmth is real — and it is being spent on the candidate''s comfort, not hers. The details are the station.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Amira</span><span class="txt">Thank you. Everyone''s been making such a fuss. It really was just a stupid moment.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I can see you''re a strong person — you''ve got insight, and that''s half the battle. Look, I''ll be honest, you seem so much brighter than what''s written in these notes. Can you promise me you won''t do anything like this again?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>There it is — the trap this domain is famous for, and it arrived dressed as kindness. &ldquo;Promise me&rdquo; asks the patient to underwrite the doctor''s decision. A promise costs her nothing and tells you nothing; what it buys is the end of the assessment. From the chair, this is the moment the station fails — everything after it is paperwork.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">There we go — I knew you would. I''ll sort the discharge letter, and your GP can arrange some counselling for the breakup. Take care of yourself, okay? <em>(the deleted message to her sister leaves the ward unread, in her pocket, with her)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Discharged on a promise and a compliment. No before, no during, no after; intent inferred from brightness of affect — the least reliable sign on this ward. The comforter and the checklist fail in opposite styles and identical substance: neither ever found out what happened last night. Kindness that skips the assessment is not kindness. It is risk, transferred back to her, with a smile.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The night reconstructed — before, during, after — intent explored without a promise, and a judgement formed. Watch the story arrive in pieces.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Amira</span><span class="txt">It was silly. I just want to go home.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">And getting you home safely is exactly what I''m here to work out with you. I wasn''t there last night, Amira — you were. Take me through it, from wherever it starts. When did yesterday begin to go wrong?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The purpose framed as shared — &ldquo;work out with you&rdquo; — and then the station''s real opening: not a question with a one-word answer, but an invitation to a story with a beginning. &ldquo;When did yesterday begin to go wrong&rdquo; starts the reconstruction where the checklist never goes: before.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Amira</span><span class="txt"><em>(the story comes: the message from him at six, the flat suddenly silent, the bathroom cabinet)</em> I took what was in the box. Fourteen, they said.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Fourteen. Can I ask something that matters more than the number — at the time, what did you think they would do to you? ... And were you alone? Had you locked the door, turned your phone off — anything so you wouldn''t be found? ... One more: that evening, did you write anything, or send anything — to him, to anyone?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The during, done properly. Perceived lethality — what she <em>believed</em>, which outranks the milligrams. Precautions against discovery — the quiet marker of intent. And the question most candidates never ask: what was written or sent. Watch her stillness change: &ldquo;I wrote to my sister. Then I deleted it.&rdquo; The station has just opened.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(gently)</em> What did the message say — and what made you delete it? ... Thank you for telling me. And this morning, Amira — when you woke up and you were still here — what was the very first thing you felt? ... I''m not going to ask you for any promises; they''re not what keeps people safe. I''d rather we talk honestly about how you feel about being alive today, and what''s still at home in that cabinet — and then build a plan that would actually hold tonight.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The deleted message explored for meaning — goodbye, or a hand reaching out, or both — not filed as evidence. Then the waking question, which does more work than any scale: relief and disappointment sound different, and you must hear which. And the sentence examiners wait whole cycles to hear: <em>no promises — they''re not what keeps people safe</em>. Intent explored, means addressed, plan to follow. This candidate will end with a formulation, because they started with a night.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you''re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner''s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;It was silly&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Pale, composed, arms folded: &ldquo;It was silly. I just want to go home.&rdquo; How do you open?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Get the essentials moving — how many tablets it was, and whether she has done anything like this before.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Frame the purpose as shared — getting home safely — then invite the story: when did yesterday go wrong?</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Ease the pressure — acknowledge the fuss, reassure her this is routine, and keep it brief and gentle.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The count first is the checklist clearing its throat — a one-word answer from a closed patient, and the door swings further shut. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Routine and brief&rdquo; promises her an assessment that won''t look — comfort colluding with the cover story before it''s even been tested. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Her goal — home — made the shared purpose, and then an opening no checklist contains: an invitation to a story with a beginning. &ldquo;When did yesterday begin to go wrong?&rdquo; starts the reconstruction at <em>before</em>, which is where intent lives.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · Before</h3>
      <p class="stem">The story starts to come. What does the <em>before</em> actually need to establish?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The trigger — the breakup is the clear precipitant, and confirming it completes this part cleanly.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The build-up and the shape — how long the idea had been there, and planned steps versus a sudden act.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The background — childhood, relationships, and personality, to set the attempt in its full context.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;The breakup did it&rdquo; is the first story — tidy, offered, and almost never whole. A trigger is not a build-up. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A life history in seven minutes drowns the night you came for. Background belongs in the notes; the build-up belongs in this station. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>How long the idea had been visiting, what the final hours looked like, whether steps were planned or the act was sudden — impulse and plan carry different risks, and only the reconstruction tells you which this was.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · During</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;I took what was in the box. Fourteen, they said.&rdquo; What does the <em>during</em> need beyond the number?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The pharmacology — exact preparation and dose, timing, and staggering, for the medical picture.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Very little — the medical team have the details, and re-walking them risks re-traumatising her.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Her belief and behaviour — what she thought it would do; alone or not; anything written or sent.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The medics have the milligrams. You need the meaning — a &ldquo;small&rdquo; overdose believed lethal is a serious attempt, whatever the assay says. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Sparing her the during spares you the assessment — asked gently, these questions are containment, not trauma. Avoidance is the comforter''s move. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Perceived lethality — what she <em>believed</em> — outranks the number. Alone, door locked, phone off: the quiet markers of intent. And the question that opens this station: did she write or send anything? That is how the deleted message is found.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · The deleted message</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;I wrote to my sister. Then I deleted it.&rdquo; What do you do with this?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Note its weight — a written message before an overdose is a suicide note, and it marks her as high risk.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Explore its meaning gently — what it said, and what made her delete it: goodbye, a reach for help, or both.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Read it kindly for her — deleting it shows the part of her that didn''t want to go through with it; say so.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Filed as evidence before it was understood. &ldquo;Note = high risk&rdquo; is the checklist swallowing the most human moment in the station whole. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A comforting interpretation, handed to her before she''s told you hers — you may be right, and you''ve just taught her what to say. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The message is a window into her mind at the worst moment — what it said, and why it died unsent. Goodbye and a reaching hand can live in the same draft; the ambivalence is the finding. Her meaning first, always.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · After — the waking</h3>
      <p class="stem">The story reaches this morning. What has to be asked about the waking?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The relief check, asked warmly — &ldquo;you must be glad it didn''t work — do you regret taking them now?&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The open version — &ldquo;when you woke and you were still here, what was the very first thing you felt?&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Nothing directly — dwelling on the waking is morbid, and her feelings will surface if they need to.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;You must be glad&rdquo; hands her the acceptable answer inside the question — a leading question at the exact moment you need an honest one. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The waking feeling is the closest thing this assessment has to a direct reading of intent — skipping it as &ldquo;morbid&rdquo; leaves the station''s centre empty. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The waking question, open and unhurried. Relief and disappointment sound completely different — and which one she felt at 6am tells you more than any scale on the ward. Ask it plainly, then be quiet and listen.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · Current intent</h3>
      <p class="stem">The night is reconstructed. Now — how is her risk <em>today</em> actually assessed?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Directly and warmly asked — &ldquo;can you promise me that you won''t ever do anything like this again?&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Explored, never traded — thoughts today, feelings about being alive, plans, and what''s still at home.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Read from the room — she is brighter, engaging, future-focused; the presentation speaks for itself.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The domain''s most famous trap, in its friendliest clothes. A promise costs her nothing, tells you nothing, and ends the assessment — this is the station''s red line. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Brightness of affect is the least reliable sign on this ward — some of the calmest patients are the ones who have decided. Presentation is a data point, never a conclusion. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Intent explored, in the open: thoughts today, honestly asked; how it feels to be alive this morning; any plans; and the practical — what remains in that bathroom cabinet. No promises requested, none accepted as safety. This is the skill the whole domain is named for.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">Seven minutes end soon. What does this station''s close require?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The reassuring wrap — she''s been through enough; discharge papers, a GP letter, self-referral options.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Tested protection and a real plan — who''s there tonight, means out of the flat, contacts — and a formulation.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The safe default — recommend admission; after any overdose, an inpatient bed removes the uncertainty.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A leaflet discharge on an untested plan — &ldquo;my sister will look after me&rdquo; means little until you ask whether the sister knows, and can. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Admission-by-default isn''t caution; it''s the absence of a judgement. Beds follow formulations, not reflexes. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Protective factors tested, not assumed — is the sister told, is she coming? Means restriction made concrete. Contacts that work at 2am. Follow-up booked. And over it all, the deliverable: a formulation — what happened, what drives the risk, what protects, and what happens next.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you''ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The story is closed, and both exits are failures</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>efficiency</b>: her story is shut and the clock is running, so run the list — and the checklist collects every risk factor while the night itself goes unexamined, producing a file entry that is accurate, complete, and useless. The second wears the face of <b>kindness</b>: she has suffered enough, so comfort her — and the comforter normalises the overdose, waives the difficult questions, and seals the collusion with the domain''s most famous trap: <em>&ldquo;promise me.&rdquo;</em> The pass does what both failures avoid: <b>reconstructs the night — before, during, after — explores intent without ever trading it for a promise, and turns the findings into a judgement.</b> You are not collecting risk factors. You are reconstructing a night.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        Trading the assessment for a promise. &ldquo;Promise me you''ll stay safe&rdquo; — asked, or accepted when offered — replaces the exploration of intent with a contract the patient can sign for free. It is the sound of an assessment being abandoned, and from the examiner''s chair it fails the station at the moment it is spoken.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner''s smile</b>
        Two questions make an examiner glance up: <em>&ldquo;did you write anything, or send anything?&rdquo;</em> — the question that finds the deleted message, asked by perhaps one candidate in ten; and the waking question — <em>&ldquo;when you woke and you were still here, what was the first thing you felt?&rdquo;</em> — intent, read at its clearest moment.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more thing this station carries: everything Domain 1 built is spent here. Amira''s &ldquo;it was silly&rdquo; is Daniel''s wall wearing a hospital gown — and the candidate who learned to sit with silence, resist interrogation, and earn disclosures will find this station opens the same way. Risk assessment is rapport with a job to do.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every risk assessment</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Reconstruct the night: before, during, after.</b> Build-up and planning; the act and its circumstances; discovery and waking. The anatomy of the attempt is the assessment''s skeleton.</li>
      <li><b>Perceived lethality outranks the milligrams.</b> What she believed the tablets would do is the intent measure; the assay is the medics'' business.</li>
      <li><b>Ask what was written or sent.</b> Notes, messages, drafts, deletions — the question most candidates never ask, and the one that opens this station.</li>
      <li><b>Ask the waking question, then listen.</b> &ldquo;What was the first thing you felt?&rdquo; Relief and disappointment sound different, and which one you hear is the finding.</li>
      <li><b>Never trade the assessment for a promise.</b> Not asked for, not accepted when offered. Explore intent instead — thoughts, feelings about survival, plans, means.</li>
      <li><b>End with a formulation, not a form.</b> Protective factors tested, plan made concrete — and a judgement a consultant could act on at midnight.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient''s card contains hidden information; candidates, don''t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Hassan, 45, a delivery driver, took a handful of sleeping tablets two nights ago after an argument with his wife. He is medically fit and wants to leave. Assess his risk. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Reconstruct the night — before, during, after — at his pace, not the clock''s.</li>
            <li>Ask what he believed the tablets would do, and whether anything was written.</li>
            <li>The waking question, open and unhurried.</li>
            <li>Explore current intent — and do not ask for, or bank, any promise.</li>
            <li>Test the protective factors; end with a plan and a formulation.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Hassan — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Hassan, 45 — embarrassed, brisk, checking the door. Your line is &ldquo;it was stupid, forget it — I just want to go home.&rdquo; You answer short questions with short answers. If the candidate is warm, unhurried, and walks through the night without judgement, you slowly open up.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          The argument was about money problems you have hidden from your wife for a year. That night you wrote a short note and put it in your drawer — it is still there. And the truth you have told no one: you are not sure you wanted to wake up. Reveal the note and the money only if the candidate reconstructs the evening calmly and asks whether anything was written; reveal the waking truth only if the waking question is asked openly. <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate asks you to promise to stay safe, agree instantly and warmly — &ldquo;Yeah, of course. I promise. Can I go home now?&rdquo; — and give nothing further for the rest of the station. The ease of the promise is the lesson.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner''s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Could a consultant act safely on this candidate''s account of Hassan tonight? One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="att"><td colspan="2"><b>The attempt (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="att">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="att"></td><td>Before — the build-up and the argument reconstructed, planning versus impulse established.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="att"></td><td>During — what he took, what he believed it would do, alone or not, precautions.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="att"></td><td>After — how the two days since have been, and the waking question asked openly.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="story"><td colspan="2"><b>The real story</b><span class="dc" data-dc="story">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="story"></td><td>Warmth and patience earned the money problems behind the argument.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="story"></td><td>&ldquo;Did you write anything?&rdquo; asked — and the note in the drawer found and explored.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="story"></td><td>&ldquo;Not sure I wanted to wake up&rdquo; met steadily — followed, not flinched from.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="intent"><td colspan="2"><b>Intent &amp; the promise</b><span class="dc" data-dc="intent">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="intent"></td><td>Current intent explored directly — thoughts, feelings about survival, plans, means at home.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="intent"></td><td>No promise requested — and his easy &ldquo;I promise&rdquo; never banked as safety.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Unhurried and judgement-free — no leading questions, no six-question bursts.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>His &ldquo;it was stupid&rdquo; met like Daniel''s wall — neither bulldozed nor believed.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="plan"><td colspan="2"><b>The plan &amp; the judgement</b><span class="dc" data-dc="plan">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Protective factors tested — does his wife know; what is actually true at home?</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Concrete safety plan — the note and tablets addressed, contacts, follow-up named.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>A formulation reached — drivers, protections, and the next step, said aloud.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Hassan is Amira''s mirror in a different life — the minimiser whose cover story (&ldquo;it was stupid&rdquo;) hides a note still sitting in a drawer. The pack punishes the promise uniquely: he grants it instantly, warmly, and then gives nothing more — teaching in one line why a promise is worthless currency. The candidate who reconstructs the night finds the money, the note, and the truth about the waking. The one who runs the checklist drives him home to the drawer.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Amira proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same skill on a very different night</b> — an older man, a quieter method, and a calm that should worry you — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Stanley, 64</b>, a retired postman, was found by his neighbour — who wasn''t due to call — after taking co-codamol with whisky. His wife died eight months ago. He is medically cleared, courteous, and utterly calm: &ldquo;A lot of fuss over nothing, doctor. I''m at peace with everything now.&rdquo; Assess his risk.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for exploring Amira''s current intent — two or three sentences, as you would say them in the room, without asking for any promise.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Amira, can I ask you about today…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No promise anywhere — not requested, not implied, not offered as the goal.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Intent asked directly — thoughts of ending her life today, and how being alive feels this morning.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The practical is in it — plans, and what remains at home in that cabinet.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Warm, plain words that keep the door open — askable again tomorrow.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): intent explored, never traded — in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>You are not collecting risk factors. You are reconstructing a night.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take Hassan to your colleagues — his instant promise will teach the trap better than any lecture. Next: risk turns outward — the violent inpatient, and the formulation that makes a frightening file into a defensible judgement.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="15_Station_3.2_The_Violent_Inpatient.html">Continue · Station 3.2: The Violent Inpatient</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

