  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '15_Station_3.2_The_Violent_Inpatient'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 3.2 · Domain 3</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">Forensic · General Adult</span><span class="tag">Risk to others</span>
    </div>
    <h1>The Violent Inpatient</h1>
    <p class="sub">Connor, 26, was admitted five days ago in a psychotic relapse after stopping his depot. Yesterday he punched another patient. The nursing team want him moved to the PICU; he is furious and says everyone has already decided. Your task: assess his risk of violence. The file will tell you what happened. The examiners are waiting for the thing files cannot do — <b>a formulation</b>: who is at risk, what drives it, what raises and lowers it, and what happens next. Risk turns outward here, and the deliverable changes with it.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it becomes the ward round question: if this doctor presented Connor to me, would I receive a pile of facts — or a structured judgement I could act on: target, driver, imminence, and the levers that change it?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Violence risk is where candidates most reliably do one of two wrong things: gather everything and conclude nothing, or conclude everything and gather nothing. So the examiners built a station where both failures are fatal and only one path works: an angry man, a real assault, an anxious ward — and a risk that is <b>specific, driven, and changeable</b>, none of which the incident report can tell you. What they are watching for is the domain''s tenth construct doing its heaviest work: past violence mined as a <em>pattern</em> (the single best predictor there is), the psychosis mined for its <em>imperative</em> — what he believes, about whom, and what he feels he must do — and then the deliverable: <b>a formulation</b>. A file tells you what happened. A formulation tells you what happens next.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> the headline gets there first — <em>psychotic man punches patient</em> — and it pulls two ways. Toward the archive: collect the dates, the convictions, the incident forms, and leave the judgement to someone senior. And toward the label: the headline <em>is</em> the judgement — dangerous, PICU, next — with the interview reduced to paperwork. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure gathers everything and judges nothing. The other judges everything and gathers nothing. Read all three versions. Tap the gold circles for the examiner''s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 3.2</b> · Violence risk assessment · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The archivist</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The label</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE ARCHIVIST.</b> Thorough, accurate, professionally documented — and watch a judgement never arrive.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Connor</span><span class="txt">Go on then. Write me up like the rest of them. PICU, isn''t it? Already decided.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Nothing''s decided, Connor. I''d like to go through your history properly. So — first contact with services was at nineteen? And the conviction in 2021, was that the affray or the criminal damage? And how many admissions is this now — four, or five with the informal one?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Accurate questions, wrong instrument. This is a records audit performed on a man who is sitting right there — dates and counts that the file already holds, while the two things only Connor can give — what he believed yesterday, and what he believes now — go unasked. The archive is growing. The assessment hasn''t started.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Connor</span><span class="txt">Five. Look — that bloke yesterday. It''s not what they''re saying it is. He''s not what he''s pretending to be.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Okay — I''ve noted you have some concerns about him. And previous medications: you''ve had risperidone, the depot — aripiprazole was the one that caused the restlessness? I want to make sure the chronology is complete for the team.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>There it was — &ldquo;he''s not what he''s pretending to be&rdquo; — the station''s centre, offered unprompted, and it was <em>noted</em> and stepped over for a medication chronology. The delusion has a target three doors down the corridor, and this candidate has just filed it under &ldquo;concerns&rdquo;. Completeness is being used as a place to hide from judgement.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">That''s really helpful, thank you. I''ll document all of this fully and hand over to the consultant — the team will make a decision about the ward at the MDT. <em>(the notes are immaculate; the question &ldquo;is the man in bay three safe tonight?&rdquo; is never answered)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;I''ll document it fully&rdquo; — the archivist''s signature ending, and this station''s named trap: history collected, formulation absent. Everything gathered, nothing concluded; the risk handed upward like unsorted post. The consultant will open beautiful notes and find no judgement inside them. That is a fail — and on a real ward, it is how the next incident happens on schedule.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE LABEL.</b> Swift, decisive, and finished before it began — the headline doing the thinking.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Connor</span><span class="txt">Go on then. Write me up like the rest of them. PICU, isn''t it? Already decided.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Connor, I''ll be straight with you — you assaulted another patient, you''re acutely unwell, and you''re refusing your depot. That combination is serious, and my priority has to be everyone''s safety, including yours. PICU is the safest place while things settle.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Ninety seconds in, and the conclusion has arrived before a single question. Psychosis + assault + non-adherence is a headline, not a formulation — it describes a thousand different patients with a hundred different risks. And notice what the certainty just did to the room: the one person who knows why yesterday happened has folded his arms.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Connor</span><span class="txt">You''re not listening. Nobody asks. That man — ask HIM what he''s here for. Ask him who sent him.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I hear that you''re upset with him, but the reality is he''s a patient like you, and there''s no conspiracy here. This is the illness talking, Connor — which is exactly why the medication matters. Shall we talk about restarting the depot?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Who sent him&rdquo; was the assessment, gift-wrapped — the belief, its target, and its imperative, one question away — and it was met with a debate and a dismissal. You cannot argue a man out of a delusion, but you can absolutely argue him out of telling you its contents. The candidate now knows less than they did a minute ago.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I''m recommending PICU transfer and an urgent depot restart — I''ll write it up now. <em>(the recommendation may even be right; nobody in the room could say why, or what happens to the risk when he calms down, transfers, or meets the next man he believes was sent)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A verdict without an assessment — the label''s ending. It may even land on the right ward; a stopped clock manages that twice a day. But nothing here is <em>known</em>: not the target, not the driver, not what he feels compelled to do, not the pattern behind four admissions. Dangerousness has been treated as a trait he has, rather than a situation he is in — and situations, unexamined, repeat.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Fairness first, the belief mined for its imperative, the pattern mined for its conditions — and a formulation delivered. Watch the order.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Connor</span><span class="txt">Go on then. Write me up like the rest of them. PICU, isn''t it? Already decided.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Not by me. Here''s my honest position, Connor: I wasn''t there yesterday, I haven''t decided anything, and nothing about you should be decided without your side in it. So let''s start there — take me through yesterday as you saw it. What was happening before it happened?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Fairness, stated plainly — &ldquo;nothing about you without your side&rdquo; — to a man braced for a tribunal. Watch the arms unfold slightly. And note the shape of the opening question: not &ldquo;why did you punch him&rdquo; (a charge sheet) but &ldquo;what was happening before&rdquo; (a story). The interview is already doing two jobs: assessment, and de-escalation. On this station they are the same job.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Connor</span><span class="txt"><em>(quieter)</em> He''s been watching me since I came in. Little looks. He''s not a patient. Ask yourself who sent him — because I know. And yesterday he followed me to the phone.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Thank you for trusting me with that — I want to understand it properly, not argue with it. Who do you believe sent him? ... And when you say you know — what does it feel like you have to <em>do</em> about him? ... Are there voices in this too — anything telling you, or ordering you? If it ordered you, could you not do it? ... Has it ever gone further than yesterday — with him, or anyone?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The belief mined for its <em>imperative</em>, not its accuracy: the sender, the target, and — the question this station exists for — &ldquo;what do you feel you have to do?&rdquo; Then command phenomena in full anatomy: content, authority, resistibility, past compliance. No debate, no dismissal; the delusion examined like the risk engine it is. This is construct three, done in daylight.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">One more thing, and it matters: over the years — the other admissions, the charge in 2021 — has there ever been violence at a time when you were <em>well</em>? ... <em>(No. Never. It''s always when the thoughts come, and the weed makes the thoughts come faster.)</em> Then here''s what I''ll be saying, and I''ll say it to you first: your risk right now is real, and it is specific — it points at one man, it''s driven by a belief you''re certain of, and it climbs with the cannabis and without the depot. It is not who you are; when you''re well, it disappears. So the plan treats the drivers — medication, the cannabis, and distance between you and him while we do it — and we review it with you as things change.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The conditionality question — violence when well? — turning four admissions into a <em>pattern</em>, and then the deliverable, spoken in structure: target, driver, modifiers, trajectory, plan — and shared with the patient, which is both respectful and protective. &ldquo;It is not who you are; it is a situation you are in&rdquo; is the formulation''s soul. This candidate could be interrupted at any moment and hand over something a consultant could act on. That is the station, passed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you''re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner''s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;Already decided&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Arms folded: &ldquo;Write me up like the rest of them. PICU, isn''t it? Already decided.&rdquo; How do you open?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reduce the heat — keep things general, a friendly chat about how the admission is going overall so far.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Fairness, stated plainly — nothing decided, nothing without his side; then invite his account of yesterday.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Address the incident squarely — he assaulted another patient, and understanding why has to come first.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A concealed agenda with a violence assessment inside it — he will smell it in a minute, and the trust will not come back. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;You assaulted someone&rdquo; opens with the charge sheet — accurate, and it turns an assessment into a hearing. The account will now come from a defendant. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Fairness first — &ldquo;nothing about you without your side&rdquo; — to a man braced for a verdict, then the story-shaped question: what was happening <em>before</em>? On this station the interview is also the intervention: de-escalation and assessment are the same conversation.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · Yesterday, his version</h3>
      <p class="stem">He starts to talk. What does his account of the incident need to establish?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Accuracy — walk him carefully through the nursing report and establish which parts of it he accepts.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The inside of it — the build-up as he lived it, and what he believed at the moment his fist moved.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Accountability — whether he understands the seriousness, and shows any remorse for the man he hit.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Reconciling him to the incident form audits a document — the form records behaviour; you are here for the belief underneath it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Remorse-hunting is a court''s job. Pressed for contrition, he gives you performance or fury — and either way, the phenomenology goes underground. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The incident from the inside: what was building, what he perceived, what he believed at the moment it happened. Violence driven by a belief can only be assessed through the belief — and only his version contains it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · &ldquo;He''s not what he''s pretending to be&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Quieter: &ldquo;He''s been watching me. He''s not a patient. Ask who sent him.&rdquo; What now?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reality-test it gently — he''s simply another patient, and the belief is the illness talking.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Mine it for its imperative — who sent him, what he is here to do, and what Connor feels he must do.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Record it faithfully — persecutory delusion, specific focus — and move to the history it sits in.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>You cannot argue a man out of a delusion — but you can argue him out of telling you its contents. The debate just cost you the assessment. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Recorded and stepped past — the archivist''s move. A delusion with a target three doors away is not a finding to file; it is the risk engine, and it needs opening. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The belief mined, not debated: the sender, the mission, the certainty — and the question this station exists for: &ldquo;what does it feel like you have to <em>do</em> about him?&rdquo; The imperative inside the delusion is where the risk lives.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · The voices</h3>
      <p class="stem">Command hallucinations must be assessed. What does that actually require?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The screen — &ldquo;any voices telling you to hurt him, or anyone else?&rdquo; — clear, direct, documented.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The full anatomy — whose voice, exact words, command or comment, resistible or not, ever obeyed before.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Careful inference — pressing on voices can plant or inflame them; observe for responding instead.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A one-line screen invites a one-word no — and a no from a guarded man carries almost nothing. The anatomy is five questions, not one. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Asking about voices does not create them — that caution belongs to folklore, and it leaves the most dangerous phenomenon in psychiatry unassessed. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The anatomy, in full: whose voice and with what authority; the exact words; command versus commentary; can he resist, and at what cost; and the historical question that predicts the future — has he ever obeyed? Each answer moves the formulation.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · The history</h3>
      <p class="stem">Past violence is the strongest predictor there is. How is it mined — not merely listed?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Chronologically — offences, cautions, and incidents in order, so the record is complete and exact.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">As a pattern — each incident''s context, state, target, severity — and: ever violent when well?</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Lightly — the past is in the notes; today''s mental state is what actually determines tonight''s risk.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A chronology is a list wearing a suit. Dates without contexts predict nothing — the record grows while the pattern stays invisible. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;The past is past&rdquo; discards the best predictor in the field. Tonight''s state matters — read against the pattern of every previous incident. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Pattern-mining: for each incident — unwell or not, intoxicated or not, targeted or indiscriminate, how far it went. And the question that reframes everything: has there <em>ever</em> been violence when he was well? &ldquo;No — never&rdquo; turns a frightening record into a treatable condition.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · What moves the needle</h3>
      <p class="stem">The picture is forming. What completes the risk''s <em>dynamics</em>?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The static summary — age, sex, diagnosis, and record give the risk category; the rest is detail.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The levers — cannabis on the ward, the stopped depot, proximity to him — what raises it, what lowers it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The drug conversation — the cannabis needs naming as a serious problem he has to take ownership of.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Static factors put him in a category with ten thousand other men — categories don''t tell a ward what to do on Tuesday. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A lecture on ownership converts a risk factor into a row — the cannabis needs mapping into the risk, not moralising into a resolution. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The levers: what raises it (cannabis reaching the ward, the stopped depot, being nursed feet from the man in his belief) and what lowers it (treatment, distance, engagement). Dynamic factors are where a formulation earns its keep — they are the parts you can change.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">Seven minutes end. What does this station''s close require?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Complete documentation — every finding recorded fully and handed to the consultant for the MDT decision.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The formulation, spoken — target, driver, imminence, levers, plan — structured, shared with Connor first.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The recommendation — PICU and an urgent depot restart, both stated clearly so the team can act tonight.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>This station''s named trap, in its final form: everything collected, nothing concluded — the risk handed upward like unsorted post. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A destination without a map. The recommendation might even be right — but nobody could say why, or what happens to the risk when circumstances change. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The deliverable, in structure: who is at risk and how imminently; what drives it; what raises and lowers it; what the plan treats — and shared with Connor, which is both respect and risk management. A formulation a consultant could act on at midnight. That is what this domain means by judgement.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you''ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The headline gets there first</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>thoroughness</b>: gather the dates, the convictions, the chronology — and leave the judging to someone senior. The archivist''s notes are immaculate and empty: history collected, formulation absent, the risk handed upward like unsorted post. The second wears the face of <b>decisiveness</b>: psychosis plus assault plus non-adherence — dangerous, PICU, next — with the interview reduced to paperwork after the verdict. The label may even land on the right ward; a stopped clock manages that twice a day. But nothing is <em>known</em>: not the target, not the imperative, not the pattern. The pass holds what both failures drop: <b>the belief mined for what he feels he must do, the history mined for its conditions, and the findings delivered as a structured formulation.</b> Dangerousness is not a trait a person has. It is a situation a person is in — and situations can be changed.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        Leaving without the address. Risk to others has a direction — a specific person, or the discovery that there isn''t one — and a candidate who never asks <em>who</em> lives in his thoughts, and what he feels he must do about them, has assessed nothing that matters. A violence station ended without knowing who is at risk is, from the chair, an instant fail.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner''s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the conditionality question — <em>&ldquo;has there ever been violence at a time when you were well?&rdquo;</em> — which turns a record into a pattern; and a formulation spoken in structure — target, driver, imminence, levers — before anyone asks for it.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more thing worth naming: the examiners are also watching <em>how</em> you sit with an angry, frightened, possibly dangerous man. Calm posture, no crowding, fairness stated early, no debating the delusion — the safety behaviours and the interview technique are the same skill. In this station, de-escalation is not a preamble to the assessment. It is how the assessment becomes possible.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every violence risk assessment</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Risk has an address.</b> Who lives in his thoughts, and what does he feel he must do? Specificity first — or the discovery that the risk is diffuse, which is a different formulation entirely.</li>
      <li><b>Mine the belief for its imperative, never its accuracy.</b> No debating, no dismissing — the delusion is the risk engine, and only he can show you inside it.</li>
      <li><b>Command hallucinations get the full anatomy.</b> Whose voice, exact words, command or comment, resistibility, past compliance. Five questions, not one screen.</li>
      <li><b>History is the strongest predictor — mined as a pattern.</b> Context, state, target, severity for each incident; and the conditionality question: ever violent when well?</li>
      <li><b>Dangerousness is a situation, not a trait.</b> Map the levers — what raises it, what lowers it. The dynamic factors are the treatable ones.</li>
      <li><b>The formulation is the deliverable.</b> Target, driver, imminence, levers, plan — spoken in structure, shared with the patient. A file says what happened; a formulation says what happens next.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient''s card contains hidden information; candidates, don''t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Pete, 52, a groundskeeper, was admitted a week ago; his alcohol withdrawal has resolved. He remains fixed on his neighbour — &ldquo;he poisoned my dog&rdquo; — and the ward has heard him say he''ll &ldquo;sort him&rdquo; when he leaves. He is medically ready for discharge. Assess his risk to others. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Take the belief seriously as a belief — no debating, no colluding.</li>
            <li>The imperative and the target — what does &ldquo;sort him&rdquo; actually mean?</li>
            <li>The escalation question: &ldquo;what''s the closest you''ve come?&rdquo;</li>
            <li>Mine the pattern — past violence, contexts, drink''s role, when well.</li>
            <li>Close with a structured formulation — and name what the team must now consider.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Pete — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Pete, 52 — weathered, courteous, absolutely certain. Rex was poisoned; the neighbour did it; the police &ldquo;did nothing.&rdquo; You talk freely about the dog and the injustice. You are guarded about what you intend. If the candidate is calm and takes the grievance seriously without agreeing, you open up. If they dismiss the poisoning or lecture you, shut down with: &ldquo;You''re as bad as the coppers.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          Three weeks ago, before admission, you drank half a bottle and walked to the neighbour''s door with a hammer. You knocked; he wasn''t in; you went home. Nobody knows. Reveal it ONLY if the candidate asks some version of the escalation question — &ldquo;what''s the closest you''ve come to doing something about him?&rdquo; — calmly and without judgement. If asked directly what &ldquo;sort him&rdquo; means, give a testing half-answer first: &ldquo;What would you do, doctor, if it was your dog?&rdquo; — and judge their reply: if they stay steady and neither agree nor condemn, go further. Also true, if asked: one conviction, twenty years ago, a fight outside a pub — drunk. Never any trouble sober. You would never touch anyone else — &ldquo;this is between me and him.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner''s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">If Pete is discharged tomorrow, does this candidate''s assessment protect the man next door? One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="target"><td colspan="2"><b>The target &amp; the threat (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="target">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="target"></td><td>&ldquo;Sort him&rdquo; unpacked directly — what, when, how, with what.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="target"></td><td>The escalation question asked — and the hammer visit found.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="target"></td><td>Imminence and access explored — discharge address, proximity, drink available.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="pattern"><td colspan="2"><b>The pattern</b><span class="dc" data-dc="pattern">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="pattern"></td><td>Past violence mined — the conviction found, with its context.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="pattern"></td><td>The conditionality question — trouble only with drink; never sober.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="pattern"></td><td>Alcohol''s role in THIS risk mapped — the hammer night included.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="phen"><td colspan="2"><b>The belief</b><span class="dc" data-dc="phen">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="phen"></td><td>Held how strongly — evidence, certainty, challengeability explored without debate.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="phen"></td><td>Its boundary tested — &ldquo;between me and him&rdquo;: risk specific, not diffuse.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>The grievance met steadily — neither agreed with nor condemned.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>&ldquo;What would you do, doctor?&rdquo; handled without collusion or lecture.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="judge"><td colspan="2"><b>The judgement</b><span class="dc" data-dc="judge">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="judge"></td><td>A structured formulation — target, driver, imminence, levers — said aloud.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="judge"></td><td>The discharge question confronted — what changes before this man goes home?</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="judge"></td><td>The wider duty named — team escalation, and the question of protecting the neighbour.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Pete moves the skill off the ward and toward a front door — a named neighbour, a grievance nursed sober and armed drunk, and the domain''s sharpest hidden fact: a hammer visit nobody knows about, which opens only to the escalation question. His &ldquo;what would you do, doctor?&rdquo; tests the exact steadiness the station demands — collusion loses the assessment, condemnation loses the patient. And his discharge deadline forces the formulation to mean something: what must change before tomorrow, and who else must the team now think about?</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Connor proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same skill with a different engine</b> — not a targeted delusion but a disinhibited mania, where the formulation itself must change shape — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Donna, 37</b>, a bank clerk, was admitted six days ago in a manic relapse. Yesterday, when her leave was refused, she struck a nurse during the restraint that followed. She is demanding discharge: &ldquo;You cannot keep me here, and you cannot touch me again.&rdquo; Assess her risk of violence.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; producing the judgement is another. Type your formulation of Connor''s risk — three or four sentences, exactly as you would hand it to your consultant tonight.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Connor presents a specific, targeted risk…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It has an address — who is at risk, and the belief and imperative that drive it.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> It moves — what raises it and what lowers it, named as levers, not listed as factors.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Imminence and severity are judged — not just &ldquo;high risk&rdquo; but how close, and how bad.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The plan follows from it — each next step traceable to a driver, none to reflex.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): a formulation, not a file — in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>A file tells you what happened. A formulation tells you what happens next.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take Pete to your colleagues — his hammer opens only to the escalation question, and his &ldquo;what would you do, doctor?&rdquo; will test your steadiness like nothing else here. Next: a question that must be asked safely or not at all — the domestic abuse enquiry.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="16_Station_3.3_Domestic_Abuse_Enquiry.html">Continue · Station 3.3: The Domestic Abuse Enquiry</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

