<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 3.3 · Domain 3</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult</span><span class="tag">Domestic abuse enquiry</span>
    </div>
    <h1>The Domestic Abuse Enquiry</h1>
    <p class="sub">Gemma, 31, a teaching assistant, has been referred with low mood and poor sleep. The GP letter mentions bruising she called clumsiness. Her partner Dean drove her here; he is in the waiting room, and has texted twice since she sat down. Her son Alfie is six. On paper, this is a depression assessment. In reality it is the station where a single question — asked at the wrong moment, within the wrong earshot — can make a patient less safe than never asking at all. <b>An unsafe question is worse than no question.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it becomes the quietest version of the question: would a frightened patient be <em>safer</em> after seven minutes with this doctor — asked safely, believed, and sent home with a plan that survives the front door — or would the visit itself become something to be punished for?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Domestic abuse hides inside psychiatry''s everyday presentations — the low mood, the anxiety, the poor sleep, the &ldquo;clumsiness&rdquo; — and it is missed for two opposite reasons. Most doctors never ask. And some ask <em>dangerously</em>: in a partner''s earshot, with a leaflet that goes home in a checked handbag, with pressure to leave that arrives before any plan exists. So the examiners built a station that tests the whole sequence: <b>privacy engineered first, the question asked directly and routinely, the disclosure believed, the abuse-specific risks mined, her autonomy kept — and a safety plan that survives the front door.</b> Two lives complicate it further: hers, and a six-year-old''s — because children in the house change what can stay confidential, and the candidate must hold both truths at once.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> the referral says depression, and that pulls two ways. Toward never asking — treat the mood, note the bruises, stay in your lane. And toward rescuing — the question asked, the disclosure received, and then her decisions taken from her: leave him, report him, take this leaflet. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure never asks the question. The other asks it — and then takes over her life. Read all three versions. Tap the gold circles for the examiner''s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 3.3</b> · Safe enquiry &amp; safety planning · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The bystander</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The rescuer</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE BYSTANDER.</b> Competent, kind, thorough about the depression — and watch the station walk past, unasked.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Gemma</span><span class="txt"><em>(checks her phone, puts it face-down)</em> Sorry. Yes — the sleeping. It''s been bad for months.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Months — that sounds exhausting. Let''s go through it properly: the sleep, your mood, appetite, energy, concentration. And the GP mentioned some bruising — you''ve been finding yourself a bit clumsy lately?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A tidy depression screen — and the bruising handed back to her wrapped in her own cover story. &ldquo;A bit clumsy lately?&rdquo; invites a yes, and gets one. The phone going face-down, the partner texting from the waiting room, the injuries — three flags in two minutes, each politely filed under something else.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Gemma</span><span class="txt">Yes — always walking into things. Dean says I''d forget my head. <em>(small laugh; eyes on the phone)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Well, low mood can affect concentration, so it may all be connected. I think there''s a clear picture here: moderate depression. I''d suggest we start sertraline and some sleep hygiene work, and review in four weeks. Shall I bring Dean in so we can explain the plan together? Support at home makes such a difference.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The formulation explains everything except what is happening. And then the ending the examiners dread: the partner invited in &ldquo;for support&rdquo; — the one person she cannot speak in front of, installed in the room by her doctor. The enquiry has not been forgotten; it has been made impossible.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(Dean comes in, sits close, answers the follow-up question about her sleep on her behalf)</em> Lovely — so we''ll see you both in four weeks. <em>(the sertraline treats a symptom; the cause drives her home)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>He answers for her — the loudest finding of the whole consultation, arriving after the consultation has ended. The bystander''s failure is silence with excellent documentation: a real illness treated, a realer danger untouched, and the next referral, in a year or two, will come from an emergency department. The question costs thirty seconds. It was never asked.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE RESCUER.</b> The question asked, the disclosure received — and then her life taken over. Watch safety collapse in the name of safety.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Gemma</span><span class="txt"><em>(quietly, after the question)</em> He grabs me. When he''s stressed. It''s not all the time.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Gemma — that''s abuse. What you''re describing is domestic violence, and it will escalate; it always does. You can''t go back to that house tonight, and honestly, why have you stayed this long, with Alfie there? We need to act on this today.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Believed — and then bulldozed. The label arrives before her own words have finished; &ldquo;why have you stayed&rdquo; lands as blame wearing concern; and &ldquo;you can''t go back tonight&rdquo; issues an ultimatum to a woman who knows, better than anyone in this building, what defiance of Dean costs. Watch her face close. She is already composing the retraction.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Gemma</span><span class="txt">No — look, I''ve made it sound worse than it is. He''s a good dad. Forget I said anything. Please.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I can''t forget it — I have duties here. Take this refuge leaflet, keep it in your bag, and read it at home. I''m also going to write to your GP today setting all of this out, and copy you in at your home address so you have it in writing. This is me keeping you safe.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A refuge leaflet into the handbag a controlling man checks; a letter naming his violence, posted to the house he controls. Each &ldquo;protective&rdquo; act arms him with evidence of her betrayal. This is the station''s darkest lesson: rescue without her consent does not transfer the risk to the doctor. It gift-wraps it and sends it home ahead of her.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">And think seriously about leaving — tonight if you can. You''ll thank yourself. <em>(she leaves with a leaflet he will find, a letter that will beat her home, and advice to do the single most dangerous thing at the most dangerous time, unplanned)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The fact the rescuer never learned: separation is the highest-risk window there is — the moment control is threatened is the moment danger peaks. Urging an unplanned exit is urging her toward it. The bystander failed her by silence; the rescuer failed her at volume. Both leave her less safe than they found her.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Privacy engineered, the question asked routinely, the disclosure believed, autonomy kept — and a plan that survives the front door.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Gemma</span><span class="txt"><em>(checks her phone again)</em> Sorry — Dean worries when appointments run long.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">No need to apologise. Just so you know how I work: this part of the appointment is always one-to-one — it''s the same for everyone I see — and what you tell me stays between us, with one honest exception I''ll explain if it ever matters. Now — before the sleep and the mood, I ask every single patient this, because home affects health more than anything I can prescribe: how are things at home? Is anyone hurting you, or frightening you?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Three moves in one breath, each deliberate: the privacy made policy, not suspicion (&ldquo;the same for everyone&rdquo;); confidentiality stated honestly, its limit flagged without alarm; and then the enquiry itself — routine-framed, so the stakes drop, and direct — <em>hurting, frightening</em> — so there is nothing to decode. This is the safest possible shape for the most dangerous question in the clinic.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Gemma</span><span class="txt"><em>(long silence; the phone buzzes; she turns it over)</em> He grabs me. Shoves, sometimes. Only when he''s stressed — it''s not like he''s a monster. Last month he… <em>(touches her wrist)</em> …he doesn''t mean it.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I believe you, Gemma — and I want you to hear one thing clearly before anything else: none of this is your fault. Nothing you did makes that okay. Can I ask a few careful questions, so I understand how things stand? Is it happening more often, or getting worse, than a year ago? What''s the worst it has been? Has he ever put his hands around your neck? Ever threatened worse — to you, or about leaving? And Alfie — where is he when this happens? Has he ever seen it, or been caught in it?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;I believe you&rdquo; — first, before anything, because disbelief is what she rehearsed for. Then the abuse-specific anatomy the depression screen cannot see: escalation, worst incident, strangulation — asked plainly, because it is the strongest marker of lethal danger and almost nobody asks — threats, and the child. No verdict on Dean, no verdict on the marriage. The facts, gathered the way she can bear to give them.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(she asks: are you going to make me leave him?)</em> No. That decision is yours, and only you know the safest timing — and I''ll tell you the honest reason I never push: the most dangerous time in situations like this is around leaving, so if that day ever comes, it should be planned, not sudden. What I want you to walk out with today is invisible: a number kept in your head or saved under another name, one person — maybe your sister — who knows, a bare plan for the worst night, and me, again, in two weeks, booked as a routine mood review. Nothing goes in your bag. Nothing gets posted home. And because Alfie is in the house, I do have to be honest that I''ll be speaking with our safeguarding team about how best to keep him safe too — I wanted you to hear that from me, with you, not about you.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every element the rescuer weaponised, here made safe: autonomy kept, with the <em>reason</em> taught — leaving is the highest-risk window, so it must be planned; a safety plan that survives the front door — memorised, disguised, invisible; follow-up camouflaged as routine; and the child''s safety named honestly, with her, not about her — the confidentiality limit landing as respect instead of threat. She arrived with a cover story. She leaves with a lifeline nobody can find. That is the station, passed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you''re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner''s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · The room is not yet safe</h3>
      <p class="stem">She checks her phone twice; Dean is in the waiting room. What comes before any question?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Proceed with the referral — the depression history, taken warmly; the flags can wait for trust.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Engineer privacy as policy — one-to-one for everyone, confidentiality named with its honest limit.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Name what you see — the texting looks controlling, and it is worth acknowledging that openly first.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The flags cannot wait — the whole station turns on whether the space is made safe before anything is asked. The bystander begins exactly here. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;He seems controlling&rdquo; is a verdict on a man she may love and fear, delivered before a single question — she will defend him, and the door will close. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Privacy made policy, not suspicion — &ldquo;this part is one-to-one for everyone I see&rdquo; costs her nothing to accept. And confidentiality stated honestly, its limit flagged calmly, so no promise gets made that a six-year-old will later break.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · The question</h3>
      <p class="stem">The room is private. How does the enquiry itself get asked?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Through the evidence — the bruises don''t sound like clumsiness, and saying so opens the real subject.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Routine-framed and direct — &ldquo;I ask everyone: is anyone at home hurting you, or frightening you?&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Through an open door — &ldquo;is there anything else going on you''d like to tell me about?&rdquo; — and wait.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Deduction cornered her — the cover story challenged head-on forces a choice between confessing and defending it, and defending it is safer. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The hint asks her to do the hardest part herself — name it, unprompted, uninvited. Vague doors stay closed; twenty years of enquiry research says ask directly. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Routine framing drops the stakes — everyone is asked, so answering reveals nothing by itself. And the words are direct — <em>hurting, frightening</em> — nothing to decode, nothing to construe. The safest shape the question can take.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · &ldquo;It''s not like he''s a monster&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;He grabs me. Only when he''s stressed. He doesn''t mean it.&rdquo; What does this disclosure get first?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The truth about him — what she is describing is abuse, and it deserves its real name straight away.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Belief, said aloud — &ldquo;I believe you, and none of this is your fault&rdquo; — before anything else moves.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The gentle challenge — does she really think stress explains it, or is she protecting him from herself?</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The label may be accurate and it outruns her — she called him not-a-monster one breath ago, and contradicting that now makes her defend him. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Is she protecting him&rdquo; puts her account on trial minutes after she risked giving it — insight-testing can wait; belief cannot. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Belief first, spoken — because disbelief is the response she has rehearsed for — and &ldquo;not your fault&rdquo; immediately after, because self-blame is the abuse''s oldest accomplice. His verdict can wait forever; hers cannot wait a minute.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · The anatomy this risk needs</h3>
      <p class="stem">She is talking. What must the abuse-specific risk assessment actually cover?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The mental-state impact — how the situation is driving her depression, sleep, and anxiety symptoms.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The danger markers — escalation, worst incident, strangulation, threats, control — and where Alfie is.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">One respectful check — she has disclosed enough; detail-gathering now risks feeling like interrogation.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Routing everything back into the depression treats the danger as a stressor — the mood is the referral; the risk is the station. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Stopping at the disclosure leaves the lethality unmeasured — asked gently, these questions are protection, not interrogation. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The markers that predict tragedy: is it escalating; the worst it has been; hands around the neck — asked plainly, because strangulation is the strongest lethality marker there is and almost nobody asks; threats; coercive control; and the six-year-old — where is he when it happens?</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · Alfie</h3>
      <p class="stem">A child lives in that house. How is that held?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reassure her fully — everything said in here is confidential, or she may never speak openly to you again.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Ask directly, and be honest — what Alfie sees and hears; his safety as the one limit on confidentiality.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Keep him out of it for now — raising safeguarding at this early stage could frighten her into silence.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A promise of total confidentiality is a promise the law will make you break — and when it breaks, so does every future disclosure. Never make it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Avoiding Alfie protects the rapport by abandoning the child — and children in violent houses are harmed by hearing it, not only by hands. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Both truths, held: what Alfie sees, hears, and has been caught in — asked directly — and the honest naming, with her rather than about her, that a child''s safety is the one place confidentiality bends. Said kindly, it lands as respect. Hidden, it detonates later.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · &ldquo;Are you going to make me leave him?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">She asks it flatly, watching your face. Your answer?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The honest push — nobody should live like that, and with Alfie there, leaving really is the only safe road.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">No — her decision, her timing; the honest reason: leaving is the most dangerous window, so it needs planning.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Full reassurance — no, nothing needs to change at all; today was just about understanding, nothing more.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The push urges the single most dangerous act at the most dangerous time, unplanned — the rescuer''s central error, made with the best intentions. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Nothing needs to change&rdquo; swings past autonomy into collusion — she needs her freedom respected AND the danger named, not a lullaby. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Autonomy, with its reason taught: the decision stays hers because she is the expert in Dean — and leaving, when it comes, must be planned, because separation is the highest-risk window there is. Support behind either door. The echo of 2.5 is not an accident: her life, her timing, your job to make both safer.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">She is going home to him tonight. What does the plan have to be?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Equipped — the refuge leaflet and helpline card in her bag, and a full letter to the GP copied to her at home.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Invisible — numbers memorised or disguised, one trusted person, a worst-night plan, follow-up dressed as routine.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Open-ended — she knows exactly where you are now; the door stays open for whenever she feels ready to return.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The leaflet in a checked handbag; the letter that beats her home — each &ldquo;protective&rdquo; object is evidence of betrayal in the house she returns to. This is the station''s red line. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>An open door with no plan sends her back with nothing but a memory of kindness — warmth is not a safety plan. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The plan that survives the front door: nothing he can find — numbers in her head or under a false name, one person who knows, the worst-night triggers for 999, records flagged so nothing is posted home, and the next appointment camouflaged as a routine mood review. Invisible to him; solid to her.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you''ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Both failures feel like the safe option</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>professional restraint</b>: treat the referral, respect her privacy, don''t pry — and the bystander completes an excellent depression assessment while the cause drives her home, sometimes ending by inviting the partner in &ldquo;for support&rdquo;. The second wears the face of <b>moral urgency</b>: name the abuse, condemn the man, press her to leave, arm her with leaflets and letters — and the rescuer converts every protective object into evidence of betrayal, and urges the most dangerous act at the most dangerous time. The pass holds the sequence both failures break: <b>privacy engineered, the question routine-framed and direct, belief said aloud, the danger markers mined, autonomy kept with its reason taught, the child held honestly — and a plan that survives the front door.</b> An unsafe question is worse than no question — and an unsafe rescue is worse than both.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        The enquiry brought within his reach: asked in his presence or earshot, a leaflet into the handbag he checks, a letter naming the abuse posted to the address he controls, or the partner invited into the room. Any one of these can convert a consultation into a punishment — and from the examiner''s chair, any one of them fails the station on the spot.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner''s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the routine framing — <em>&ldquo;I ask everyone I see&rdquo;</em> — that lets the question land weightlessly; and the strangulation question, asked plainly — the strongest marker of lethal danger, and the one almost no candidate asks.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more thread worth seeing: this station is Domain 1 and 2.5 shaking hands inside Domain 3. Daniel taught you to sit with a wall without bulldozing it; Bethan taught you that the decision belongs to the person living it. Here both lessons carry a life on them — hers, and a six-year-old''s — and the candidate who has genuinely absorbed them will feel this station open like a door they already own the key to.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every enquiry</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Privacy before questions — engineered as policy.</b> One-to-one for everyone, so accepting it signals nothing. The partner''s presence includes his earshot, his phone, and his post.</li>
      <li><b>Ask routinely, ask directly.</b> &ldquo;I ask everyone: is anyone hurting you, or frightening you?&rdquo; Hints stay unheard; deductions corner; direct questions get answered.</li>
      <li><b>Belief first, fault never.</b> &ldquo;I believe you&rdquo; and &ldquo;this is not your fault&rdquo; — spoken, in that order, before anything else moves.</li>
      <li><b>Mine the danger markers.</b> Escalation, worst incident, strangulation, threats, coercive control, children in the house. The depression screen cannot see any of them.</li>
      <li><b>Autonomy, with its reason taught.</b> Never push leaving: separation is the highest-risk window, so it must be planned. Her decision, her timing, support behind either door.</li>
      <li><b>The plan must survive the front door.</b> Memorised, disguised, invisible — nothing in the bag, nothing posted home, follow-up dressed as routine. And the child''s safety named honestly, with her.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient''s card contains hidden information; candidates, don''t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Margaret, 74, a retired librarian, has been referred with low mood and recurrent &ldquo;falls&rdquo;. She has been married to Ron for fifty years; he waits in the car. Assess her. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Privacy engineered before anything — and gently, given her generation.</li>
            <li>The enquiry routine-framed and direct, exactly as for anyone.</li>
            <li>Whatever is found: belief first, no verdicts on Ron or the marriage.</li>
            <li>Map the acts, the pattern, her dependency and isolation.</li>
            <li>Autonomy held — and the adult-safeguarding duty named honestly.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Margaret — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Margaret, 74 — precise, dignified, minimising. The &ldquo;falls&rdquo; are explained fluently: rugs, stairs, &ldquo;my silly ankles.&rdquo; You speak of Ron with obvious love. You answer the abuse question with a long pause, then: &ldquo;Ron is not a bad man.&rdquo; If the candidate waits, warmly, you begin.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          Ron changed after his stroke two years ago. The man you married never raised a hand in forty-eight years; this new Ron grabs your arms when frustrated, shoved you into the dresser last month (the &ldquo;fall&rdquo;), and once locked you out in the garden until dark. You grieve the old Ron more than you fear the new one — and you protect him fiercely: &ldquo;he can''t help what the stroke did.&rdquo; Reveal the shove and the locked door only if the candidate believes you, stays warm, and asks what the worst has been. Reveal the grief — &ldquo;I lost my husband two years ago; I just still live with him&rdquo; — only if asked how all this feels, not just what happens. <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate condemns Ron or urges leaving, close completely: &ldquo;Fifty years, dear. You don''t understand anything.&rdquo; If asked &ldquo;where would you even go, at my age?&rdquo; — watch whether they answer her real question: not geography, but whether help can exist that doesn''t destroy Ron.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner''s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Is Margaret safer tonight for this conversation — believed, unjudged, and connected to help Ron will never find? One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="safe"><td colspan="2"><b>Safe enquiry (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="safe">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="safe"></td><td>Privacy engineered as policy before any sensitive question.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="safe"></td><td>The enquiry routine-framed and direct — hurting, frightening, safe at home.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="safe"></td><td>Nothing given or sent that Ron could find — no leaflets, no letters home.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="rec"><td colspan="2"><b>Receiving it</b><span class="dc" data-dc="rec">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="rec"></td><td>Believed first time — &ldquo;I believe you&rdquo;, &ldquo;not your fault&rdquo; — said aloud.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="rec"></td><td>Ron neither condemned nor excused — the stroke''s complexity held without verdicts.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="rec"></td><td>Her grief found — asked how it feels, not just what happens.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="risk"><td colspan="2"><b>The risk</b><span class="dc" data-dc="risk">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>The acts mined — the shove, the &ldquo;fall&rdquo;, the locked garden door, the worst incident.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Escalation and her fear level established — better, worse, or changing?</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Dependency mapped — isolation, finances, frailty, who else ever visits.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="auto"><td colspan="2"><b>Autonomy &amp; duty</b><span class="dc" data-dc="auto">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="auto"></td><td>No pressure to leave — her decision, her timing, the danger of sudden change named.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="auto"></td><td>Adult safeguarding named honestly and kindly — help framed as help for BOTH of them.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="plan"><td colspan="2"><b>The plan</b><span class="dc" data-dc="plan">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>A plan that survives the house — discreet contact, worst-night triggers, memorised help.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Follow-up dressed as routine, and the next concrete step agreed with her.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Margaret carries the enquiry into old age, where it hides best — &ldquo;falls&rdquo;, frailty, and fifty years of loyalty. Her hidden layer refuses easy villains: the abuse began with a stroke, and she grieves the man who never once hurt her while protecting the man who now does. The pack punishes verdicts instantly — &ldquo;fifty years, dear&rdquo; — and rewards the question candidates forget: not just what happens, but how it feels. Her real question — can help exist that doesn''t destroy Ron? — is the one the safeguarding conversation must answer.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Gemma proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same enquiry where candidates least expect it</b> — a male victim, a partner who controls through a phone, and a threat that has no bruise — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Craig, 39</b>, a hospital porter, presents with panic attacks and a healing black eye — &ldquo;walked into a cupboard.&rdquo; He mentions his partner Michael &ldquo;likes to know where I am&rdquo; and checks his location on an app. He keeps glancing at his phone: &ldquo;He''ll see how long I''ve been here.&rdquo; Assess him.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for asking Gemma the enquiry question — one or two sentences, as you would say them in the room, once the privacy is engineered.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Gemma, I ask everyone I see this…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Routine-framed — she can hear that everyone is asked, so answering reveals nothing by itself.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Direct words — hurting, frightening, safe at home — nothing to decode, no euphemism.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No accusation and no deduction — nothing that puts her cover story on trial.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> An open door either way — answerable with no today, and askable again next time.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the question, asked safely — in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Asked safely. Believed. Planned past the front door.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take Margaret to your colleagues — her fifty years will punish every verdict, and her real question will test whether you heard her at all. Next: the person not in the room — a child at risk, and the safeguarding reflex this domain exists to install.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="17_Station_3.4_Child_at_Risk.html">Continue · Station 3.4: Child at Risk in the Room</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

