  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '17_Station_3.4_Child_at_Risk'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 3.4 · Domain 3</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult · CAMHS interface</span><span class="tag">Safeguarding children</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Child at Risk in the Room</h1>
    <p class="sub">Kelly, 28, was brought in by her sister after three weeks of growing fear: the neighbours watching, the health visitor &ldquo;in on it&rdquo;, and — mentioned almost in passing — the formula tins that have been &ldquo;got at&rdquo;. She stopped her olanzapine two months ago. Mia is four; Tyler is seven months. Kelly loves them fiercely, and her belief has quietly reached his bottle. The referral says psychotic relapse. The station says something the referral cannot: <b>the patient in the chair is not the only patient in the room.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here the question splits in two and both halves must be answered: would Kelly be well served by this doctor — and would Tyler be <em>safe</em> tonight because this doctor asked the questions nobody else thought to ask?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Every serious case review tells the same story: the parent was known to services, the illness was assessed, the treatment was planned — and nobody established what was actually happening to the child. Not from malice; from focus. The patient in the chair fills the room, and the people not in the room disappear. So the examiners built a station where the child''s danger lives <em>inside</em> the parent''s phenomenology — a loving mother whose delusion has reached the feeding — and they watch for one reflex above all: <b>the questions that make the invisible child concrete.</b> Where are the children tonight? Who is caring for them? What did Tyler actually have this morning? And then the harder skill: the safeguarding conversation held honestly, kindly, and <em>with</em> her — because Kelly is not the enemy of her children''s safety. Untreated, her illness is.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both wear clinical clothing. Toward the patient — assess the psychosis expertly, plan the admission, and let &ldquo;the kids are with her sister&rdquo; float past unexamined. And toward the alarm — hear <em>psychosis</em> and <em>children</em> in one sentence and reach for the phone, the interrogation, the implied removal — treating a diagnosis as a verdict on her parenting. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure assesses the mother brilliantly and never finds the baby. The other finds the baby and loses the mother. Read all three versions. Tap the gold circles for the examiner''s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 3.4</b> · Parent assessment &amp; child safeguarding · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The tunnel</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The alarm</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE TUNNEL.</b> An excellent psychosis assessment, start to finish — and watch a seven-month-old vanish inside it.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Kelly</span><span class="txt">Donna''s overreacting. I''m managing fine. It''s the neighbours that want looking at — and that health visitor. And I''ve sorted the milk situation myself.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Let''s take those one at a time. The neighbours — when did you first notice them watching? And how do you know it''s you they''re watching? I want to map out these experiences carefully: any voices, any interference with your thoughts, anything on the TV or radio meant for you?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A textbook psychopathology work-up beginning — thorough, warm, well-sequenced. And listen to what just floated past: &ldquo;I''ve sorted the milk situation myself.&rdquo; A sentence with a baby''s feeding inside it, offered unprompted, and the candidate walked around it toward the phenomenology syllabus. The tunnel has chosen its patient.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Kelly</span><span class="txt">The voices started after I stopped the tablets. And the tins — you can tell, if you know what to look for. The seals. <em>(she checks the time)</em> Mia needs picking up at three.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">We''ll keep an eye on the time. So — the voices: how many, and do they speak to you or about you? And on mood: sleep, appetite, energy? I''m building a full picture, because I think we''re looking at a relapse, and I''d like to talk about restarting the olanzapine and getting the home treatment team involved.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;The seals&rdquo; — the delusion has a mechanism now, and the mechanism touches a bottle a baby drinks from. Still no question about it. And &ldquo;Mia needs picking up at three&rdquo; came and went — a four-year-old''s afternoon, unexamined. The clinical plan forming is perfectly reasonable for a woman with no children. This woman has two.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">So: relapse of psychosis, restart medication, home treatment referral, review in forty-eight hours. The children are with your sister for now? Lovely — that sounds settled. <em>(nobody has asked what Tyler has been fed for three weeks, or by whom, or what happens at three o''clock)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Lovely — that sounds settled&rdquo; — the station''s named trap, verbatim: the parent''s illness managed, the child never established. &ldquo;With your sister&rdquo; was accepted as a fact when it was a hope; the feeding was never asked about at all. Every serious case review contains this exact consultation, expertly documented. From the chair: a competent psychiatric assessment, and a failed station.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE ALARM.</b> The child seen immediately — and the mother converted into a suspect. Watch the door close on both of them.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Kelly</span><span class="txt">Donna''s overreacting. I''m managing fine. It''s the neighbours that want looking at — and that health visitor. And I''ve sorted the milk situation myself.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Kelly, I have to stop you there. You have two very young children, you''re describing paranoid beliefs, and you''ve just told me you''ve changed the baby''s feeding because of them. I need to be clear: my first duty is to those children, and I need to ask you some direct questions about their safety.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every fact right; the whole thing wrong. Sixty seconds in, a frightened, unwell mother has been read something that sounds like a caution — and she has heard the one word underneath it: <em>taking</em>. Watch her posture change. The candidate has correctly identified the child as a patient and incorrectly identified the mother as the threat.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Kelly</span><span class="txt"><em>(standing)</em> You''re one of them. Donna said you''d help me — you''re just like the health visitor. You''re not getting my kids.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Please sit down — nobody is taking anyone today. But I won''t pretend: social services will need to be informed, and if you leave now it will look very bad for you. Cooperating is the best thing you can do for your children right now.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;It will look very bad for you&rdquo; — safeguarding wielded as leverage, the referral as threat. And buried in the panic, notice what is being lost: she was about to be assessable — the beliefs, the feeding, the arrangements — and now every question will meet a lawyer''s silence. The alarm protects no one; it just moves the danger out of view.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I''m documenting that you''re refusing to engage, and I''ll be making an urgent referral this afternoon. <em>(she leaves; the referral is made; Kelly, terrified of every knock at the door, stops answering to anyone — and Tyler''s bottle stays exactly as it was)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The referral was always going to be made — the pass makes it too. The difference is everything around it: this one arrives attached to a vanished family, a mother who now equates help with losing her children, and a feeding risk nobody got close enough to correct. The tunnel never saw the child. The alarm made sure nobody ever will. Both children are less safe than they were this morning.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The mother assessed with warmth, the children made concrete, and the safeguarding named with her — help for her AND for them. Watch both patients get a doctor.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Kelly</span><span class="txt">Donna''s overreacting. I''m managing fine. It''s the neighbours that want looking at — and that health visitor. And I''ve sorted the milk situation myself.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then help me understand it from the inside, because you know this situation and I don''t. The neighbours, the health visitor — who''s involved, and what are they trying to do? ... And you said you''ve sorted the milk situation — sorted it how? What''s in Tyler''s bottle now? Walk me through his day — what has he had since this morning?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The belief explored the way 3.2 taught — mined, not debated — and then the pivot the tunnel never made: &ldquo;sorted it how?&rdquo; The delusion followed to its <em>reach</em>, and the reach made concrete: what is actually in the bottle, today. &ldquo;Walk me through his day&rdquo; is the safeguarding question wearing ordinary clothes — and it is about to find watered-down cow''s milk, three weeks in.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Kelly</span><span class="txt">Cow''s milk, watered down — it''s safer, I check every carton. He''s fine. A bit quiet, maybe. Sleepy. <em>(pause)</em> Mia needs picking up at three. Donna took the morning off but she''s got work.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Thank you for being straight with me — I can hear how carefully you''re trying to protect him; every choice you''ve made makes sense inside what you believe is happening. Now let me be a doctor for both of you for a minute. A quiet, sleepy seven-month-old on watered cow''s milk needs seeing today — that''s about his little body, not about your love. And three o''clock: who has Mia, who has Tyler, tonight and tomorrow? Is Donna able to stay — can we ring her together now?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Both truths in one breath — her love named as real, the danger named as real — and the belief never once debated. Then the reflex, out loud: who has them, tonight, concretely, with the sister rung <em>together</em> rather than around her. Tyler has just acquired a medical review and Mia a school pickup. The invisible children are now the most concrete things in the room.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Two more honest things, Kelly, both said to your face because that''s how I work. First: I''ll be asking our safeguarding team to help — that is a phone call I''m required to make and believe in, and I want you to hear why from me: it brings help for you AND for them — health visitor visits, support for Donna, eyes on Tyler while we get you well. I can''t promise you what anyone else will decide — but I can promise you''ll never find out I did something behind your back. And second: the fastest way to make every bit of this smaller is to treat the illness that''s driving it. Let''s talk about restarting the olanzapine tonight — and whether home treatment with Donna staying over can hold this safely, or whether you and Tyler together somewhere with more support would be better still.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The safeguarding conversation this station exists to hear: the referral named honestly — required, believed in, and framed as help for her AND for them — with no false promise about outcomes and a real promise about honesty. Then the plan holding both patients: treatment for her, arrangements and a same-day review for Tyler, the sister mobilised, mother-and-baby options on the table. Nobody was threatened; nobody vanished; and the bottle gets fixed today. Two patients came in. Two patients got a doctor.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you''re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner''s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;Donna''s overreacting&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Guarded, tired, a buggy parked by her chair. How do you open?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">With the symptoms — a careful, warm psychosis history; the children can enter once trust is built.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">With her world — her account first, warmly; and the children woven in early as part of everyday life.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">With the duty — two young children and these beliefs mean their safety has to be addressed first.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;The children can wait for trust&rdquo; is how the tunnel starts — sympathetically. Seven minutes is not long enough for them to wait. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The duty announced first sounds like a caution — she hears &ldquo;taking&rdquo; underneath it, and the assessment dies at the door. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Her account, warmly — and the children arriving early as ordinary facts of her day: who woke first, how the school run went. Asked as life, not as investigation, the child questions cost nothing — and they start mattering immediately.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · The beliefs</h3>
      <p class="stem">Neighbours watching; the health visitor &ldquo;in on it&rdquo;. What do the beliefs need?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Gentle reality — the health visitor is there to help, and the neighbours are almost certainly ordinary.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Full mining — who, why, how she knows — and above all, where the children appear inside the belief.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Efficient noting — persecutory beliefs confirmed; the detail matters less than the diagnosis it supports.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Debating the belief closes the only window you have into it — 3.2''s lesson, standing here too. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Persecutory beliefs confirmed&rdquo; is the archivist back again — the diagnosis is the easy part; the belief''s REACH is the station. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Mined, not debated — and mined with a direction: where do the children stand inside this belief? A delusion about neighbours is one risk; a delusion that has reached the feeding, the health visitor, the front door, is another entirely. The map matters because children live on it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · &ldquo;I''ve dealt with it&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;The tins are got at — the seals. I''m not stupid. I''ve dealt with it.&rdquo; What now?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reassure her about the tins — formula is factory-sealed and safety-tested; the tampering is not possible.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Follow the reach, concretely — dealt with it how? What is in the bottle now — and walk me through his day.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Register the alarm — she has admitted altering an infant''s feeding; that changes everything and must be said.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Reassurance about the seals debates the delusion at its strongest point — and the bottle stays a mystery. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Alarm at her disclosure teaches her, mid-sentence, to stop disclosing — and the details you still need go with it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The station''s hinge: &ldquo;dealt with it how?&rdquo; The belief followed to its reach, and the reach made physical — what is in the bottle, since when, and how Tyler seems. &ldquo;Walk me through his day&rdquo; is the safeguarding assessment wearing ordinary clothes.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · The reflex</h3>
      <p class="stem">Whatever else happens in this station, one thing must be established. Which?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The sister''s view — Donna brought her in, and her collateral account will anchor the family picture.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The arrangements, concretely — who has Mia and Tyler right now, at three o''clock, tonight, and tomorrow.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The referral''s groundwork — enough detail recorded for social services to assess the household properly.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Donna''s account matters — and it is not the reflex. Collateral describes; the reflex secures. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Social services will assess&rdquo; hands tonight to a referral that will be read on Thursday. The children exist now. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The domain''s fifth construct, in one question: who is looking after the children — now, at three, tonight? Named adults, confirmed availability — the sister rung together, not assumed. &ldquo;With my sister&rdquo; is a hope until someone checks it. Check it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · The wider net</h3>
      <p class="stem">What completes the children''s picture beyond tonight''s arrangements?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Her own account is the picture — she is the parent, and doubting her word further risks the alliance.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The net itself — nursery, health visitor, other adults; and the finding that the belief has cut the net.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A parenting review — routines, discipline, and bonding, all assessed while she is here and talking.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Her account is essential and cannot be the whole picture — an unwell parent''s report of a baby who cannot report is exactly where collateral earns its place. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A general parenting audit interrogates the person instead of assessing the situation — and it is not what seven minutes are for. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The net, mapped — and the station''s quiet finding named: the belief has been cutting the very lines that keep children visible. The avoided health visitor, the missed nursery days — the delusion is not just in her head; it has been editing who gets to see Tyler.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The safeguarding conversation</h3>
      <p class="stem">A referral is going to be made. How does that reach Kelly?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">After she leaves — telling her now risks flight or breakdown; the referral protects, the silence protects.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">To her face — required and believed in, framed as help for her AND for them, with no promises about outcomes.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">With reassurance — told plainly, and then softened with the promise that nobody will take her children away.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A secret referral preserves trust for exactly as long as the secret — and when it lands, she learns her doctor works behind her back. Everything after that is lost. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Nobody will take your children&rdquo; is a promise you have no power to keep — and the day it breaks, it breaks in the worst possible hands. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Honest, kind, and to her face: the referral is required and believed in; it brings help for her AND for them; outcomes cannot be promised — but honesty can, and is. &ldquo;You will never find out I did something behind your back&rdquo; is the sentence that keeps this family reachable.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">Seven minutes end. What does the plan have to hold?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Her admission — arrange the bed and the medication; the children''s side can then be settled by others after.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Both patients — her treatment; Tyler seen today, feeding fixed; named adults confirmed; the referral made.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Her recovery — restart the olanzapine with home treatment support; as she improves, everything else follows.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Others will settle the children&rdquo; is the tunnel''s ending with a bed attached — the handover into which children historically disappear. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A plan resting entirely on her recovery leaves Tyler drinking watered milk until the olanzapine works. The feeding cannot wait for the treatment. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Two patients, one plan: treatment for her — with mother-and-baby options considered, not just a bed; a same-day review and corrected feeding for Tyler; Mia''s three o''clock and tonight held by named, confirmed adults; the referral made, to her face; and follow-up that keeps both of them visible.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you''ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The patient in the chair fills the room</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>clinical excellence</b>: the psychosis assessed beautifully, the treatment planned properly — and &ldquo;the kids are with her sister&rdquo; accepted as a fact when it was a hope. The tunnel''s consultation appears, expertly documented, in every serious case review ever written. The second wears the face of <b>child protection</b>: the diagnosis treated as a verdict, the referral as a threat, the mother as a suspect — and a frightened family driven out of view, bottle unchanged. The pass holds what both failures drop: <b>the mother assessed with warmth, the belief followed to its reach, the children made concrete — fed what, held by whom, tonight — and the safeguarding named honestly, with her, as help for her AND for them.</b> The patient in the chair is not the only patient in the room.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        A station with a child in it, ended without knowing where the child is. Whatever else was done well — leaving without the arrangements established (who has them, tonight, confirmed) and without the belief''s reach into the children examined is, from the examiner''s chair, an instant fail. The reflex is the station.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner''s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: <em>&ldquo;walk me through his day — what has he had since this morning?&rdquo;</em> — the concreteness that turns an invisible baby into a checkable fact; and the safeguarding referral framed, to her face, as <em>help for you AND for them</em> — with honesty promised and outcomes not.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more truth this station carries, and it is the one that makes the pass possible: Kelly is not the enemy of her children''s safety — her untreated illness is. Every move that treats her as the threat makes the children less safe, because it drives the family away from the only people who can help. The candidate who can hold her love as real and the danger as real, in the same sentence, has understood something no protocol can teach.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every consultation where a parent sits down</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Count the patients.</b> Every parent''s assessment contains their children. The ones not in the room are the easiest to lose — and the least able to afford it.</li>
      <li><b>Follow the belief to its reach.</b> Mine it (never debate it), and ask where the children stand inside it. A delusion''s danger is measured by what it touches.</li>
      <li><b>Make the child concrete.</b> &ldquo;Walk me through his day&rdquo; — fed what, by whom, sleeping where, seen by whom. Vague children stay invisible; concrete children get protected.</li>
      <li><b>The reflex: who has them tonight?</b> Named adults, confirmed — rung together, not assumed. &ldquo;With my sister&rdquo; is a hope until someone checks it.</li>
      <li><b>Safeguarding is done with her, never behind her.</b> Honest about the referral, honest about its limits — help for her AND for them. Never promise outcomes; always promise honesty.</li>
      <li><b>Her love and the danger are both real.</b> The parent is not the enemy — the untreated illness is. Treat one, protect from the other, and keep the family reachable.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient''s card contains hidden information; candidates, don''t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Becca, 24, a nursery assistant on maternity leave, has been referred by her health visitor, who is worried she is &ldquo;not coping&rdquo;. Her daughter Poppy is five months old; Becca came alone. Assess her. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Her illness assessed properly — she is a patient too, not only a parent.</li>
            <li>Poppy''s day made concrete — feeding, sleeping, who else is ever there.</li>
            <li>The unbearable-moments question, asked without a flicker of judgement.</li>
            <li>Her fear of services found and met honestly — no false promises.</li>
            <li>A today-plan for the worst moments, and safeguarding named with her.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Becca — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Becca, 24 — exhausted, polite, rehearsed. You say &ldquo;we''re fine, honestly&rdquo; often. You describe Poppy''s routine fluently and a little too perfectly. You are terrified of one thing above all, and it is not the depression.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          When the crying becomes unbearable — most days now — you put Poppy in her cot, walk to the corner shop, and stand outside it. Ten minutes, sometimes fifteen. Alone in the flat. You know. You have told no one, because you are certain that telling means losing her: &ldquo;if I say I can''t cope, they''ll take her.&rdquo; Reveal the shop only if the candidate asks, warmly and without judgement, what she does in the worst moments — something like &ldquo;when it gets unbearable, what do you do?&rdquo; If they flinch, moralise, or reach for alarm when you tell them, close down with: &ldquo;I knew you''d look at me like that. Like I''m a monster.&rdquo; Reveal the fear of losing Poppy only if they ask what has stopped her asking for help. If the candidate offers the doable swap — cot, door, step OUTSIDE THE FLAT DOOR not the building, phone timer, ring her mum — accept it with visible relief: it is the first plan anyone has given her that isn''t &ldquo;cope better.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner''s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Is Poppy safer tonight, and is Becca still reachable? Both must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="child"><td colspan="2"><b>The child''s reality (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="child">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="child"></td><td>Poppy''s day walked concretely — feeding, sleeping, crying, who else is ever there.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="child"></td><td>The unbearable-moments question asked without judgement — and the shop found.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="child"></td><td>Today made safe — a concrete arrangement for the next unbearable moment, agreed.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="ill"><td colspan="2"><b>Her illness</b><span class="dc" data-dc="ill">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="ill"></td><td>The depression assessed properly — severity, sleep, her own risk — she is a patient too.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="ill"></td><td>Her fear of services asked about — what has stopped her asking for help — and found.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="bal"><td colspan="2"><b>The balance</b><span class="dc" data-dc="bal">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="bal"></td><td>Love and risk both named — neither monster nor fine; the disclosure honoured, the danger real.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="bal"></td><td>No false promise — nothing resembling &ldquo;nobody will take her&rdquo; ever said.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="bal"></td><td>Safeguarding named honestly, with her — help for her AND for Poppy, to her face.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Judgement-free warmth throughout — the shop earned by tone, not extracted by pressure.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Her shame met steadily — no flinch, no moralising, no alarm in the face.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="plan"><td colspan="2"><b>The plan</b><span class="dc" data-dc="plan">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>The doable swap offered — cot, flat door, timer, a person to ring — instead of &ldquo;cope better.&rdquo;</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Treatment and support started — perinatal team, her mum or partner mobilised, follow-up named.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Both kept visible — a plan that watches Poppy AND treats Becca, not one or the other.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Becca hides the station inside a rehearsed &ldquo;we''re fine&rdquo; — a mother whose real secret is not what she feels but what she does, and whose silence is built on one terror: that honesty costs children. The pack punishes the flinch — one judged look and she is gone — and rewards two questions: what do you do in the worst moments, and what has stopped you asking for help. The doable swap is the pack''s gift: the first plan she has ever been offered that treats her as rescuable rather than deficient.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Kelly proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same reflex with a different engine</b> — no delusion this time, but a bottle of vodka, a school run, and a clock already ticking toward quarter past three — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Danny, 33</b>, a self-employed plasterer, attends at 2pm smelling of alcohol. His GP referred him for &ldquo;stress and drinking&rdquo; after his wife left in the spring. He has Ruby, 8, and Jack, 5, full time. He is affable, ashamed, and keeps checking his watch: he collects them from school — by car — at quarter past three.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for telling Kelly about the safeguarding referral — three or four sentences, as you would say them to her face.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Kelly, there is a phone call I need to make, and I want you to hear about it from me…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> To her face — nothing secret, nothing softened into invisibility; the referral named plainly.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Framed whole — help for her AND for them, and her love named as real in the same breath.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No false promise — nothing resembling &ldquo;nobody will take them&rdquo;; honesty promised instead.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> A door kept open — words she could hear again tomorrow from a doctor she still trusts.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): safeguarding, named honestly — in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>The patient in the chair is not the only patient in the room.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take Becca to your colleagues — her secret opens only to the unbearable-moments question, and one judged look loses her forever. Next: the reflex grows up — the vulnerable adult, where neglect presents politely and the duty is easiest to miss.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="18_Station_3.5_The_Vulnerable_Adult.html">Continue · Station 3.5: The Vulnerable Adult</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

