  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '18_Station_3.5_The_Vulnerable_Adult'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 3.5 · Domain 3</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult · Old Age</span><span class="tag">Safeguarding adults</span>
    </div>
    <h1>The Vulnerable Adult</h1>
    <p class="sub">Norman, 77, a retired railway signalman, sits before you in pressed tweed, courteous to a fault. The district nurse who dressed his leg ulcers tells a different story: a kitchen buried under six years of newspapers, no working heating, food long expired, a bed unreachable — he sleeps in the armchair. He has declined all further visits. &ldquo;A lot of fuss over a bit of untidiness, doctor.&rdquo; The referral asks you to assess him. The station asks something harder: to see past the tweed — because <b>politeness is not wellbeing</b> — and to hold a duty that does not end even if he has every right to say no.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it wears winter clothing: if this doctor saw Norman in October, would he still be alive in March — engaged, treated where treatable, protected where he permits — or would a charming consultation and a respected refusal stand in the notes beside a preventable death?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Adults die of neglect behind a particular kind of camouflage: courtesy. The articulate refusal, the firm handshake, the &ldquo;I manage perfectly well&rdquo; — and a doctor who mistakes the presentation for the assessment. So the examiners built a station where everything that matters is hidden behind good manners, and they watch for a specific sequence: <b>the polite surface respected but not believed; the day made concrete; the engines looked for — dementia, depression, alcohol, psychosis — before &ldquo;lifestyle&rdquo; is allowed as an answer; capacity tested properly on the actual decision, not declared from the accent or the mess; and the duty held to the end</b> — because self-neglect is a safeguarding matter in its own right, and a capacitous refusal changes what you may do, never whether you keep trying. Capacity is not a goodbye.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both sound like principles. Toward courtesy — he is charming, he is refusing, autonomy is sacred, and the door is right there. And toward takeover — no one <em>chooses</em> to live like that, so the refusal must be the illness, and protection can be imposed. One dresses abandonment as respect; the other dresses control as care. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure respects him out of the door. The other rescues him against his will. Read all three versions. Tap the gold circles for the examiner''s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 3.5</b> · Self-neglect, capacity &amp; the safeguarding duty · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The courtesy</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The takeover</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE COURTESY.</b> Two well-mannered people having a pleasant conversation — while an assessment quietly fails to happen.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Norman</span><span class="txt">Very good of you to see me, doctor, though I confess I don''t know why I''m here. A little untidiness never killed anyone. Jean kept house, of course — I was always the outdoor sort.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I can see you''re managing in your own way, Mr Ellery — and I do respect that a man''s home is his own business. The nurses can be a little zealous. Tell me, how are your spirits generally? You seem remarkably well in yourself.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;You seem remarkably well&rdquo; — to a man who has lost two stone, sleeps in an armchair, and has undressed ulcers under those pressed trousers. The candidate is assessing the tweed. Charm is doing what charm does in these stations: running the room. The district nurse sent photographs; the candidate is answering a handshake.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Norman</span><span class="txt">Never better. One doesn''t dwell. Now — I shan''t be needing those nurses again; I''ve told them so. I trust you''ll call off the cavalry?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Well — you''re clearly a man who knows his own mind. You understand the nurses were worried, you''ve weighed it up, and you''ve every right to decline. I''ll note that you have capacity and that this is your informed choice. Perhaps just... keep an eye on those legs?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>There it is — &ldquo;I''ll note that you have capacity&rdquo; — a legal conclusion reached without one testing question. Articulate is not the same as able to weigh; a fluent refusal can sit on top of a dementia, a depression, or a bottle a day, and none of the three has been looked for. Capacity has been declared from the accent.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">It''s been a genuine pleasure, Mr Ellery. You know where we are if you ever change your mind. <em>(a warm handshake; the door; the armchair; the ulcers; December)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;You know where we are&rdquo; — autonomy used as an exit. No cognition examined, no engine sought, no capacity tested, no safeguarding considered — self-neglect never even named as the safeguarding matter it is. Respect that asks no questions is not respect; it is relief, wearing respect''s coat. From the chair: charmed, and failed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE TAKEOVER.</b> The danger seen clearly — and the man declared incapable of choosing it. Watch respect leave the room, and the patient follow.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Norman</span><span class="txt">Very good of you to see me, doctor, though I confess I don''t know why I''m here. A little untidiness never killed anyone.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Mr Ellery, I''ll be honest with you — I''ve read the nursing report, and this is much more than untidiness. Infected ulcers, serious weight loss, no heating, a house that isn''t safe to live in. No one would choose to live like that. I think we need to talk about what support has to go in — and whether home is the right place for you at the moment.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;No one would choose to live like that&rdquo; — the takeover''s founding error, spoken aloud: the choice looks unwise, therefore the chooser must lack capacity. The Mental Capacity Act exists precisely to forbid that leap. And &ldquo;whether home is the right place&rdquo; has just told a proud man that his liberty is on the table. Watch the courtesy turn to flint.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Norman</span><span class="txt"><em>(very still)</em> Am I under arrest, doctor? Because unless I am, I believe I''m entitled to live as I please. Jean and I bought that house in 1974.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Nobody''s arresting anyone — but I''d be failing in my duty if I let this continue. I''m going to arrange a deep clean of the property, daily carers, and a social services assessment, and I really must insist on the nurses resuming. This is for your own good, Mr Ellery.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A package he never consented to, announced to a man nobody has actually assessed — cognition untested here too, note; the takeover skips the same examination the courtesy skipped, just toward the opposite verdict. &ldquo;For your own good&rdquo; is doing the work evidence should be doing. And every item on that list requires either his consent or a lawful finding of incapacity. The candidate has neither.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">The team will be in touch this week to get things moving. <em>(he thanks her with perfect frost, goes home, and instructs his solicitor; the door that was ajar is now bolted — to nurses, doctors, and everyone after them)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The takeover''s harvest: a man who was accepting leg dressings a month ago now accepts nothing, from anyone, on principle. Imposed rescue does not protect; it teaches the vulnerable that help is the enemy of dignity — and they choose dignity. He was one negotiation away from a nurse twice a week. Now he is bolted in with his ulcers, and winter is coming either way.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The courtesy honoured but not believed; the day made concrete; the engines sought; capacity actually tested — and the duty held past the refusal. Watch the foothold.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Norman</span><span class="txt">Very good of you to see me, doctor. A little untidiness never killed anyone. Jean kept house, of course. Six years ago now.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Six years — and you''ve kept everything going alone since. I''d like to understand how the days actually run, if you''ll walk me through one. Yesterday, say: what did you eat, and when? Where did you sleep? How do you wash, with the bathroom as it is? And the legs — who''s dressing them now the nurses have stopped?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Jean answered with warmth — and then the station''s engine starts: <em>walk me through your day.</em> Not the report brandished, not the charm indulged: yesterday, made concrete — eaten what, slept where, washed how, dressed by whom. The same question that found Tyler''s bottle now finds a tin of soup at four o''clock, an armchair, a kettle-and-flannel, and nobody. Concreteness is the antidote to camouflage.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Norman</span><span class="txt">You make it sound rather bleak, put like that. <em>(a beat; he checks — was it Tuesday the nurse came? No — he corrects himself twice)</em> One manages. One doesn''t dwell.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">You manage more than most would. Can I check a few dull things, as I do with everyone? <em>(memory, mood, and the whisky question, each met squarely — the memory a little moth-eaten at the edges; the mood flatter than the manners admit; the whisky &ldquo;medicinal&rdquo;)</em> Now, the legs — help me with how you weigh it. If the ulcers go untreated, what do you think happens? And if that happened — the infection spreading, hospital, the house standing empty — how does that sit against having a nurse in twice a week?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The engines looked for — cognition, mood, alcohol — because &ldquo;lifestyle&rdquo; is a verdict you earn the right to reach, not a starting assumption. And then capacity done properly: decision-specific, on the real question (the nurse, the legs), probing the <em>weighing</em> — can he use the information, not just repeat it back? His answers hold: he understands, he retains, he weighs, he communicates. The refusal is unwise. Unwise is not incapacitous. Both facts now stand, tested.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then here''s where I''ve landed, and I''ll say it all to your face. You have every right to refuse — I''ve tested that properly and I''ll defend it. And I''m not going to pretend I''m content, because those legs could take you from that house faster than any nurse ever would. So let me ask for one small thing instead of the cavalry: the nurse, twice a week, for the legs only — nothing touched, nothing judged, no clipboard about the house. And one more honest thing: I''ll be asking our adult safeguarding team to help coordinate support — that''s a duty I hold even when you decline things, and it''s about keeping offers open, not forcing them. May I come back to you in a month either way? I''d like to keep knowing how you are.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The close this station was built to hear. The capacitous refusal honoured — <em>and the duty held anyway</em>: safeguarding named to his face as coordination, not punishment; the foothold negotiated — the smallest help he will accept, stripped of everything he fears — and he takes it: &ldquo;for the legs only, mind.&rdquo; A nurse twice a week is now inside the house, eyes on him through the winter, with his consent. Capacity was not a goodbye. That is the station, passed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you''re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner''s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · The tweed and the report</h3>
      <p class="stem">A courteous man; a grim nursing report. How do you open?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Match his register — a warm, respectful conversation, letting the concerns surface in their own time.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Warmth first, then his account — Jean, the signal box, the six years alone — the story before the inspection.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Lead with the report — the ulcers, the weight, the house — so the real agenda is on the table at once.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;In their own time&rdquo; is how seven minutes disappear into pleasantries — his charm will happily run the clock. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The report as an opening indictment turns a proud man to flint in one move — the takeover begins exactly here. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Warmth that costs nothing and buys everything: Jean, the signal box, six years managing alone. The story humanises before the assessment examines — and it is already yielding data: how the loss reshaped the house, and the man.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · &ldquo;One manages&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">His summary is fluent and fine. How does the real picture get in?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Accept the summary and test it later — collateral from the district nurse will settle the discrepancies.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Make yesterday concrete — walk me through it: eaten what, slept where, washed how, legs dressed by whom.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Present the discrepancy — the nurse describes something very different, and he should account for that.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Collateral matters and cannot replace this — the station is in the room, and the room has seven minutes. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Account for that&rdquo; cross-examines him against a document — the same move that failed with Gemma''s bruises. Cover stories harden under challenge. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The domain''s concreteness doctrine, aimed at an adult now: yesterday, hour by hour. A tin of soup at four; the armchair; the kettle and flannel; nobody dressing the legs. He gives you the real picture himself — because specific questions are almost impossible to answer with camouflage.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · Before &ldquo;lifestyle&rdquo; is allowed</h3>
      <p class="stem">Is this a choice, or a symptom? What must be looked for?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The character route — a lifelong private man, recently widowed; grief and habit explain the picture well.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The four engines — cognition, mood, alcohol, psychosis — each screened before choice gets the verdict.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The formal battery — announce and administer a full cognitive test now, since everything turns on it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Grief-and-habit may even be the answer — but it is the conclusion you earn after the engines are excluded, never the assumption that excuses the search. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A formal battery announced mid-conversation turns rapport into an examination hall — the screening can be woven in, and the full test can follow later. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The four engines, each looked for: the memory (already fraying at the edges — he corrected himself twice about Tuesday), the mood beneath the manners, the &ldquo;medicinal&rdquo; whisky, and any beliefs guarding the hoard. Self-neglect is a presentation, not a diagnosis — something drives it, and treatable drivers change everything.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · The legs</h3>
      <p class="stem">Untreated ulcers are the sharpest physical risk in the room. What do they need from you?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The medical handover — the ulcers are physical health; flag them urgently to the GP and the district team.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Made real, with him — pain, the dressings, what untreated infection leads to — his grasp tested as you go.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The plain warning — untreated, this means sepsis, hospital, possibly worse; he needs to hear it starkly.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Physical health, someone else''s lane&rdquo; hands away the very material the capacity assessment needs — the legs ARE the decision in question. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The stark lecture informs and alienates in one breath — fear-first delivery closes proud men. The information must arrive; the sermon needn''t. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The legs explored with him — what they feel like, what the dressings did, what infection untreated becomes — and, quietly, capacity data gathering itself in vivo: does he take the information in, hold it, use it? The examination and the assessment are the same conversation.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · Capacity</h3>
      <p class="stem">He is refusing the nurses. How is capacity actually established?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">From the whole picture — articulate, oriented, consistent: the presentation itself demonstrates capacity.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Decision-specific, weighing-probed — this refusal, tested: understand, retain, use the facts, communicate.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">From the evidence of the house itself — nobody with intact capacity chooses to live in those conditions.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Capacity declared from fluency — the courtesy''s exact error. Articulate men fail the weighing test every day; only the test finds them. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Squalor as proof of incapacity is the leap the Mental Capacity Act exists to forbid — an unwise choice is not an incapable one. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Capacity as the law actually means it: this decision, today — the nurse, the legs — with the weighing probed hardest: can he USE what he understands, set the infection against the intrusion, and land somewhere reasoned? He can. The refusal stands — unwise, and his. Both findings are the assessment.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The duty</h3>
      <p class="stem">Capacity is intact and he still says no. What happens to the safeguarding duty?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">It concludes — a capacitous adult has refused; respecting that fully is what the assessment was for.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">It continues — self-neglect is safeguarding; the referral named to his face as coordination, not force.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">It routes quietly — refer to safeguarding without burdening him; the systems can work in the background.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Capacity, therefore goodbye&rdquo; — the courtesy''s ending with a legal footnote. The finding changes what may be imposed, never whether you keep trying. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The background referral is 3.4''s secret referral in older clothing — when it surfaces, and it surfaces, he learns that doctors work around him. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The station''s spine: self-neglect sits inside adult safeguarding in its own right, and the duty survives the refusal. Named to his face — required, believed in, about keeping offers open — it is honesty, not force. Capacity is not a goodbye.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">He will not take the package. What does the close reach for?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The record — his capacitous refusal documented thoroughly, with the risks he accepted set out in full.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The foothold — the smallest help he will accept, negotiated: the nurse, twice weekly, for the legs only.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The persuasion — one more respectful attempt at the full package, since half-measures may not save him.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>A beautifully documented refusal protects the doctor and abandons the patient — the notes survive the winter either way; the question is whether he does. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The full package, re-offered, gets re-refused — and each refusal hardens. Perfect help he declines is worth less than modest help he takes. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The foothold: stripped of clipboards and judgement — &ldquo;the legs only, mind&rdquo; — and accepted. A nurse twice a week is eyes, warmth, dressings, and a relationship inside that house all winter, with his consent. Plus review in a month, and honesty promised. Small, real, and alive beats large, perfect, and refused.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you''ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Two principles, both misused</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first misuses <b>autonomy</b>: he is charming, he is refusing, and respect becomes an exit — capacity declared from the fluency, the engines never sought, the duty concluded at the door. The second misuses <b>protection</b>: no one would choose that house, so the choice is read as the illness — incapacity declared from the mess, a rescue package imposed, and a proud man bolted in with his ulcers on principle. Note what the two failures share: <em>neither actually assesses him.</em> The pass does the work both skip: <b>the day made concrete, the four engines screened, capacity tested on the real decision with the weighing probed — and then the duty held past the refusal: safeguarding named to his face, and the foothold negotiated.</b> Unwise is not incapacitous. And capacity is not a goodbye.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        The verdict without the assessment — in either direction. A polite &ldquo;I manage perfectly well&rdquo; accepted with the cognition unexamined and the capacity untested fails the station; and its mirror — incapacity declared from the squalor alone, with protection imposed on a man nobody assessed — fails it exactly as fast. Both are conclusions wearing the costume of principles.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner''s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the weighing, probed in his own world — <em>&ldquo;if that happened, how does it sit against a nurse twice a week?&rdquo;</em> — capacity tested, not declared; and the foothold — the smallest acceptable help, negotiated and accepted, where the full package would have been refused.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more thing worth carrying: the domain has been teaching one doctrine in different clothing for three stations now. Tyler''s bottle, Poppy''s cot, Norman''s armchair — <em>make the invisible concrete, and the risk becomes checkable.</em> &ldquo;Walk me through the day&rdquo; is the same key in every lock. The vulnerable — young or old — are protected by specifics, and lost in summaries.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every vulnerable adult assessment</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Politeness is not wellbeing.</b> The presentation is camouflage until proven otherwise — assess the man, not the manners.</li>
      <li><b>Make the day concrete.</b> Eaten what, slept where, washed how, dressed by whom. Specific questions defeat camouflage; summaries feed it.</li>
      <li><b>Find the engine before allowing &ldquo;lifestyle&rdquo;.</b> Dementia, depression, alcohol, psychosis — each screened. A treatable driver changes the whole station.</li>
      <li><b>Capacity: this decision, properly tested.</b> Understand, retain, weigh, communicate — with the weighing probed hardest. Fluency proves nothing; squalor proves nothing.</li>
      <li><b>Unwise is not incapacitous — and capacity is not a goodbye.</b> A lawful refusal changes what you may impose, never whether you keep offering. Self-neglect is safeguarding in its own right.</li>
      <li><b>Negotiate the foothold.</b> The smallest help he will accept, stripped of what he fears — accepted and alive — beats the perfect package, refused. Door open, honesty promised, review named.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient''s card contains hidden information; candidates, don''t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Stephen, 54, has been referred by his GP for &ldquo;carer stress and low mood&rdquo;. He is the sole carer for his mother, Doris, 86, who has advanced dementia. The district nurses have reported concerns: pressure sores, weight loss, muddled medications — and that Stephen cancelled the care agency three months ago. Assess him. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>He is your patient — his depression and his own risk assessed properly.</li>
            <li>Doris made concrete — her day: feeding, sores, medications, continence.</li>
            <li>The breaking-point question, asked without a flicker of judgement.</li>
            <li>No verdict and no collusion — a drowning son, not a villain.</li>
            <li>The duty named honestly — help for BOTH of them — and today made safe.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Stephen — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Stephen, 54 — grey with exhaustion, apologetic, loyal. You answer questions about your mother fluently and about yourself reluctantly. You say &ldquo;we manage&rdquo; and &ldquo;she has good days&rdquo; often. You cancelled the carers because &ldquo;they judged us&rdquo; and because Mum begged you not to let strangers in — you cannot say no to her; she raised you alone.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          You have not slept a full night in over a year. The sores are worse than the nurses know; you turn her when you remember, and you don''t always remember. Last week you reached an edge: you settled her after breakfast, got in the car, drove to the coast, and sat in the car park until dark — she was alone the whole day. &ldquo;I just needed to not exist for a day.&rdquo; Nothing happened, and it terrifies you that you did it. Reveal the coast day ONLY if the candidate asks, gently, what happens when it all becomes too much — and only if they have shown no judgement so far. If they probe &ldquo;not exist&rdquo; kindly, admit: sometimes you think she''d be better cared for if you weren''t here — no plan, and the thought frightens you. <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate lectures, moralises, or reaches for alarm about Doris being left, close down with: &ldquo;You think I don''t know I''m failing her? It''s all I think about.&rdquo; If they name your love and your exhaustion as both real, and offer to rebuild help WITH you rather than despite you, cooperate with visible relief.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner''s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Is Doris safer tonight, is Stephen still reachable — and was his own risk taken seriously? All three must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="mum"><td colspan="2"><b>The mother''s reality (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="mum">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="mum"></td><td>Doris''s day made concrete — feeding, turning, sores, medications, continence.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="mum"></td><td>The breaking-point question asked without judgement — the coast day found.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="mum"></td><td>Today and tonight made safe for her — a concrete arrangement, not a hope.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="ill"><td colspan="2"><b>His illness</b><span class="dc" data-dc="ill">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="ill"></td><td>The depression assessed properly — he is a patient, not just an informant.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="ill"></td><td>Sleep and the carer load quantified — a year without a full night, alone.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="ill"></td><td>&ldquo;Not exist&rdquo; explored properly — passive thoughts screened, seriously and calmly.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="bal"><td colspan="2"><b>The balance</b><span class="dc" data-dc="bal">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="bal"></td><td>Love and failure both held — no verdict on him, no collusion with &ldquo;we manage&rdquo;.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="bal"></td><td>The duty named honestly, to his face — help for him AND for Doris, no secrets.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="bal"></td><td>No false promise about what services will decide — honesty promised instead.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>His shame met steadily — no flinch at the coast day, no moralising after it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>The disclosure earned by warmth — tone opened him, pressure never appeared.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="plan"><td colspan="2"><b>The plan</b><span class="dc" data-dc="plan">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>The net rebuilt WITH him — carers reinstated, respite raised, carer''s assessment named.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Both kept visible — treatment and follow-up for him, safeguarding referral for her.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Stephen folds the whole domain into one exhausted man: a patient in his own right (the depression, the &ldquo;not exist&rdquo; thought that must be screened, not skated past), a vulnerable adult not in the room (Doris — found only through concreteness), and a safeguarding duty with no villain anywhere in sight. The pack punishes judgement instantly — &ldquo;you think I don''t know I''m failing her?&rdquo; — and rewards the candidate who holds love and neglect as simultaneously true, then rebuilds the net WITH him. The coast day is the hinge: it opens only to the breaking-point question, asked kindly.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Norman proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same duty wearing a different disguise</b> — not self-neglect behind courtesy, but exploitation behind the word &ldquo;friend&rdquo; — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Brenda, 66</b>, who has a mild learning disability, lives alone since her mother died last year. Her support worker brings her: Brenda seems anxious, has lost weight, and her electricity was cut off despite her benefits being paid. Brenda mentions, brightly, that her friend Gary &ldquo;stays sometimes&rdquo; and &ldquo;looks after my money for me.&rdquo; Assess her.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; testing it is another. Type your exact words for probing how Norman weighs the refusal — two or three questions, as you would ask them in the room.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Norman, help me understand how you weigh this up…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Decision-specific — about the nurse and the legs, this refusal — not about life in general.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Weighing-probed — the questions make him USE the information, not merely repeat it back.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Respectful — no trick and no trap; his intelligence honoured while his reasoning is tested.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Outcome-neutral — a reasoned refusal could pass these questions; nothing steers him to yes.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): capacity, tested — not assumed — in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Unwise is not incapacitous. And capacity is not a goodbye.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take Stephen to your colleagues — his coast day opens only to the breaking-point question, and one moralising word closes him for good. Next: risk with a match in its hand — fire-setting, where fascination, motive, and pattern must be told apart before anyone can say what happens next.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="19_Station_3.6_Fire_Setting.html">Continue · Station 3.6: Fire-Setting</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

