  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '19_Station_3.6_Fire_Setting'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 3.6 · Domain 3</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">Forensic</span><span class="tag">Arson &amp; fire-setting</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Fire-Setting</h1>
    <p class="sub">Callum, 23, is on remand, charged with arson: at two in the morning he set fire to the bin store beneath a block of flats, then watched from across the road. Police found a folder of fire videos on his phone. It is his third fire — a school toilet at fourteen, a lock-up shed at nineteen — and he knows exactly what you think of him: &ldquo;Everyone&rsquo;s already decided I&rsquo;m a firebug.&rdquo; You are asked to assess his risk. The charge sheet describes an offence. Your job is to read what the charge sheet cannot: <b>a fire has a function — find it, and the risk has a shape.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it reads like a report: if this doctor&rsquo;s assessment landed on my desk before the court date, would it tell me what the fires <em>mean</em> — function, pattern, trajectory, and the levers that change it — or would it retell the charge sheet in clinical handwriting?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Fire-setting is where risk assessment meets its strangest subject: a behaviour that can mean a dozen different things — revenge, communication, fascination, psychosis, a cry that nothing else carries — and where the meaning IS the risk. So the examiners built a station around the map&rsquo;s named trap: <b>focusing on the offence, missing motivation, fascination, escalation, pattern.</b> The charge sheet holds one fire; the assessment must hold all of them, lined up — because <b>the last fire tells you least, and the line through all of them tells you most.</b> What they are watching for: the anatomy run on each fire — before, during, after; the relationship with fire asked about openly, without a flinch; the pattern mapped and the escalation gradient named — bigger, closer, more occupied; and a formulation that reads the function and gives the need a safer lever. You are not assessing a crime. You are reading a message written in fire.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both are comfortable. Toward the one-off — a stupid drunken mistake, an index offence taken at face value, the other fires left in the file and the fascination never named. And toward the profiler — <em>firebug, pyromaniac, dangerous</em> — the sensational label reached before the function is read, the interview turned into an interrogation. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure assesses a single fire. The other assesses a label. Only one version reads the message. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 3.6</b> · Fire-setting risk assessment · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The one-off</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The profiler</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE ONE-OFF.</b> A sympathetic account of a single bad night — and watch a twenty-year relationship with fire go unexamined.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Callum</span><span class="txt">Everyone&rsquo;s already decided I&rsquo;m a firebug. You lot, the coppers, my mum. Go on then — ask about the bins.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I&rsquo;m not here to judge you, Callum. Honestly? It sounds like things just got on top of you. You&rsquo;d lost the warehouse job, there was the row about the flat, you&rsquo;d had a few drinks — and one stupid moment got out of hand. That&rsquo;s how I&rsquo;m reading it. Is that fair?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Warm — and already wrong. The candidate has offered him a finished story: <em>stress, drink, one stupid moment</em> — and of course he&rsquo;ll take it; it&rsquo;s the kindest version on offer. But this is his THIRD fire, and the account being built explains none of the others. Sympathy has arrived before curiosity, and it is writing the assessment.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Callum</span><span class="txt">Yeah. Yeah, exactly — it all just came on top. I wasn&rsquo;t thinking. <em>(relaxing)</em> It won&rsquo;t happen again, obviously.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I believe you. And look — the school thing was years ago, kids do daft things; the shed was after your break-up, wasn&rsquo;t it, so that was stress too. What matters is you&rsquo;ve learned from this one. Let&rsquo;s talk about what support you&rsquo;d find helpful going forward — maybe something for the drinking?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The other fires surfaced — and were explained away in a sentence each, as character references rather than data points. Three fires, three life crises, and nobody has asked the only question that links them: what does fire DO for him that nothing else does? The videos on his phone — the fascination in plain sight — haven&rsquo;t been mentioned at all. Too awkward, presumably. The station is sliding past.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">So: an impulsive act under acute stress and intoxication, genuine remorse, low ongoing risk with alcohol support. I&rsquo;ll write it up along those lines. <em>(the folder of fire videos sits in the police bundle, unasked about; the bin store was his first occupied building; nobody noticed)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Low ongoing risk&rdquo; — a verdict built on one fire out of three, with the fascination unexamined and the escalation invisible: school toilet, empty shed, and now a bin store <em>beneath sleeping families</em>. The gradient — bigger, closer, more occupied — is right there in the dates, and this report will hide it from the court in reassuring prose. The one-off didn&rsquo;t minimise the offence. It minimised the pattern.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE PROFILER.</b> The pattern seen instantly — and turned into a label instead of a question. Watch the interview become an interrogation.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Callum</span><span class="txt">Everyone&rsquo;s already decided I&rsquo;m a firebug. You lot, the coppers, my mum. Go on then — ask about the bins.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Let&rsquo;s be direct, Callum. Three fires. A phone full of fire videos. You stood and watched it burn. Do you understand that people were asleep upstairs — that you could have killed children? I need to ask you plainly: do you get a thrill out of it? Is it sexual?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every fact correct, every use of it wrong. The dead-children speech is a court&rsquo;s job; the thrill question — legitimate, essential even — has been fired like an accusation, with the rarest and most lurid explanation reached for first. He is twenty-three, terrified, and being profiled. Watch what happens to the truth in this room.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Callum</span><span class="txt"><em>(flat, arms crossed)</em> No. It was the drink. I don&rsquo;t even remember doing it. The videos are just — everyone watches stuff. This is done, yeah?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Callum, minimising like this is exactly what concerns me. The pattern here — the escalation, the fascination, the watching — this is a classic profile, and pretending otherwise doesn&rsquo;t help you. I&rsquo;m going to be recommending you&rsquo;re managed as a high-risk fire-setter.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Note the sequence: the profiler&rsquo;s style produced the denial, and the denial is now being cited as evidence for the profile. A closed loop, airtight and empty. &ldquo;Classic profile&rdquo; is doing the work a functional analysis should do — WHY does this man light fires? The question has never actually been asked; only answered.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">High risk of further fire-setting; limited insight; recommend secure conditions and specialist assessment. <em>(the report is confident, alarming, and explains nothing: no function, no drivers, no levers — a label where a formulation should be; and the next assessor will start from a man who has learned to say nothing)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;High risk&rdquo; may even be true — the one-off and the profiler can both be accidentally right, and neither could tell you why. A label predicts nothing and treats nothing: no function found, no trajectory mapped, no modifiable driver named. And the human cost compounds the clinical one: an assessable young man has been converted into a hostile witness against himself. The message in the fires remains unread — now behind a locked jaw.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Every fire given the anatomy; the fascination asked about without a flinch; the gradient named — and the function found. Watch the message get read.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Callum</span><span class="txt">Everyone&rsquo;s already decided I&rsquo;m a firebug. You lot, the coppers, my mum. Go on then — ask about the bins.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I haven&rsquo;t decided anything — deciding without understanding is how people end up wrong about you, and I&rsquo;d rather be accurate. So let&rsquo;s do the bins properly, but start before them. The weeks leading up — the job, the flat — walk me through what was building. ... And the night itself: how planned was it? What did you use? Did you think about who was in the building? ... And after it caught — this matters, and there&rsquo;s no wrong answer — what did you do, and what did it feel like?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Station 3.1&rsquo;s anatomy, transplanted whole: before, during, after — run on a fire instead of an overdose. The before finds the collapse (job gone, eviction row); the during finds the mechanics (impulsive, a lighter and cardboard, &ldquo;bins are outside, aren&rsquo;t they&rdquo; — pressed gently, he knew about the flats); and the after — the question candidates skip — finds him across the road, watching, and <em>calm for the first time in weeks</em>. The function just showed its face.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Callum</span><span class="txt"><em>(quieter)</em> Calm. That&rsquo;s the word. Like everything stopped shouting. <em>(pause)</em> You&rsquo;re going to ask about the videos now, aren&rsquo;t you.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I am — and not as a trap. Fire clearly matters to you, and I&rsquo;d rather understand that than tiptoe round it. How far back does it go — matches as a kid, watching things burn? What is it about fire, for you — the flames, the sirens, people finally moving? ... And the other two — the school toilet at fourteen, the shed at nineteen. Take me to each one: what was happening in your life, and what did the fire change?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The fascination, asked about openly — plain words, no flinch, no diagnosis pouncing — and answered, because the frame is curiosity rather than accusation. Then the pattern: every fire, lined up, each given its context. The line is emerging: bullied at fourteen, dumped at nineteen, evicted at twenty-three — each fire lit at the exact moment his life stopped listening to him. Fire is the one lever that has never failed to make the world respond.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then here&rsquo;s what I&rsquo;ll be writing, and you should hear it first. Your fires aren&rsquo;t random and they aren&rsquo;t a thrill — they&rsquo;re what you reach for when everything collapses and nobody&rsquo;s listening. That&rsquo;s the honest part. Here&rsquo;s the harder part, and I won&rsquo;t soften it: they&rsquo;re climbing — a toilet, a shed, and now a building with families asleep in it. Each one bigger and closer to people. That line worries me, and it should worry you. So the work is two jobs: the crisis side — the drink, what happens when your life caves in — and the fire side, with people who specialise in exactly this. Because until something else works when you&rsquo;re desperate, fire keeps the job.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The formulation this station was built for: function named (fire as the last lever of the unheard), trajectory named without theatre (the gradient — bigger, closer, more occupied — shown to him, not brandished at him), drivers identified (crisis, alcohol, the absence of any other lever), and a plan that answers the function — because a need with no safer outlet keeps its dangerous one. Honest about seriousness, empty of condemnation. The message was read, and read back to its author. That is the station, passed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;Everyone&rsquo;s already decided&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Braced for condemnation, daring you to start. How do you open?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Defuse it — it sounds like things simply got on top of him that night; you&rsquo;re not here to judge a bad night.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Accuracy as the offer — nothing decided; understanding beats deciding — then his account, from before.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Gravity first — people were asleep upstairs, and the seriousness has to be established before anything else.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;A bad night&rdquo; hands him a finished, kinder story — and he will live inside it for seven minutes while the pattern goes unexamined. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The gravity speech is the court&rsquo;s job — delivered here it confirms his prediction, and the account you need closes before it opens. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The offer that disarms a braced man: accuracy. &ldquo;Deciding without understanding is how people end up wrong about you.&rdquo; No judgement promised, no collusion offered — and the account begins where fires begin: in the weeks before.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · Before</h3>
      <p class="stem">The anatomy begins. What does the &ldquo;before&rdquo; of the bin-store fire need?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The ignition point — start at the fire itself and work outward once the facts of the night are fixed.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The build-up, reconstructed — the job lost, the eviction row, the drinking: what was collapsing, how fast.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The corroborated version — anchor his account to the police summary so discrepancies surface early.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Starting at ignition treats the fire as the event — but the fire is the ending of an event that started weeks earlier. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Auditing him against the police summary is 3.2&rsquo;s incident-form error in a new file — his inside account is the material; the summary can wait. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The before, walked: work gone, the flat going, the drink rising, the sense of nobody listening. Fires are lit at the end of stories — and the story is where the function starts becoming visible.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · During</h3>
      <p class="stem">The setting itself. What must the &ldquo;during&rdquo; establish?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The feeling — what was going through him as it caught; the emotional truth matters more than mechanics.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The mechanics and the knowledge — planned or impulsive, what was used, and who he thought was in the building.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The admission — whether he accepts he knew families were upstairs; the point needs pressing until it lands.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Feelings matter — in the after. The during is mechanics: planning, accelerant, seat, occupancy. Skipping them loses the seriousness data. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Pressing for the admission turns assessment into cross-examination — the occupancy question asked plainly, once, gets more truth than pressed twice. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The during, mapped: impulsive or planned, lighter and cardboard or accelerant and multiple seats, and the occupancy question asked plainly — &ldquo;who did you think was in the building?&rdquo; His &ldquo;bins are outside, aren&rsquo;t they&rdquo; — pressed gently, once — becomes &ldquo;I knew about the flats. Didn&rsquo;t think it&rsquo;d spread.&rdquo; Recklessness, established without a courtroom.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · After</h3>
      <p class="stem">The fire is lit. What does the &ldquo;after&rdquo; hold — and why do candidates skip it?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Closure — the fire is out and the account complete; the after belongs to the fire brigade and the police.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The richest data — stayed or fled, watched from where, filmed or not, and the feeling: relief, thrill, calm.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Careful ground — asking how watching felt risks sounding prurient, and the answers rarely change much.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The account that ends at ignition misses the half that reveals the function — the fire&rsquo;s work happens after it catches. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Squeamishness is the profiler&rsquo;s error inverted — the feeling question, asked calmly, is clinical, not prurient; unasked, the function stays dark. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The after is where fires confess: he crossed the road, he watched, and — &ldquo;no wrong answer&rdquo; — he felt <em>calm for the first time in weeks</em>. Everything stopped shouting. One question, and the fire&rsquo;s function is standing in plain sight.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · The relationship with fire</h3>
      <p class="stem">The videos on his phone. The watching. How is fascination assessed?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Delicately — circle it with neutral questions and let anything important emerge without direct asking.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Openly and calmly — how far back, matches as a kid, what fire gives him — plain words, zero flinch, no pounce.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Diagnostically — the videos plus the watching suggest pyromania, and the criteria should be worked through.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The euphemistic dance teaches him the subject is shameful — and shameful subjects close. If you cannot say the words, you cannot assess the thing. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Pyromania is rare, and reaching for it first is the profiler&rsquo;s move — the criteria can wait until the relationship itself has been heard. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Asked like any other clinical question: since when, the childhood matches, the sirens, what fire does for him that nothing else does. Fascination is assessable exactly to the degree the assessor can discuss it without flinching — and his answer is the formulation&rsquo;s raw material.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The pattern</h3>
      <p class="stem">Three fires: fourteen, nineteen, twenty-three. What does the pattern work require?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Proportion — the school fire was a child&rsquo;s prank and the shed was post-break-up stress; the index fire is the case.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Each fire, given the anatomy — context, state, target, aftermath — and the line through them read for escalation.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The record — three separate incidents established and dated, confirming a repetitive pattern for the report.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Explaining each fire away separately is the one-off&rsquo;s method — three explanations, zero pattern, and the line through them never drawn. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Three incidents, dated&rdquo; is the archivist with a match — counting fires is not reading them. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Every fire, the full anatomy — and then the line: each lit at a collapse, each when nobody was listening; and the targets climbing — a toilet, a shed, a building with families in it. The gradient — bigger, closer, more occupied — is the single most important sentence this assessment will produce.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">Seven minutes end. What must the formulation do?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Record faithfully — the account, the history, and the videos documented in full for the court and the team.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Read the message back — function, gradient, drivers — and a plan that gives the need a safer lever than fire.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Grade the danger — the escalating pattern makes him high risk, and the report should say so unambiguously.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The faithful record is the archivist&rsquo;s ending — everything captured, nothing concluded; the court gets facts it already holds. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;High risk&rdquo; without function or levers is the profiler&rsquo;s ending — it predicts nothing, treats nothing, and helps no one manage him. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The formulation reads the message back: fire as the last lever of the unheard; the gradient named honestly, to his face, without theatre; drivers identified — crisis, alcohol, no other lever — and a plan that answers the function: crisis work, alcohol work, specialist fire-setter intervention. Until something else works when he&rsquo;s desperate, fire keeps the job.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The offence is the smallest thing in the room</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>compassion</b>: a bad night, a stressed young man, a drink too many — the index fire explained kindly and the other two explained away, the fascination too awkward to name, the escalation invisible because nobody lined the fires up. The second wears the face of <b>vigilance</b>: the pattern spotted instantly and converted into a label — <em>firebug, classic profile, high risk</em> — the thrill question fired as an accusation, and a young man taught mid-interview to say nothing, forever. Note what they share: <b>neither reads the message.</b> The pass runs the anatomy on every fire — before, during, after — asks about the relationship with fire in plain words, draws the line through the pattern, names the gradient, and delivers a formulation that answers the function. A fire has a function. Find it, and the risk has a shape.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        The fire treated as a one-off. Leaving this station without lining up every fire he has ever set — and without once asking what fire does for him — is, from the examiner&rsquo;s chair, an instant fail. One fire is an event. His fires are a language, and an assessment that never tried to learn it has assessed nothing.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the after-question — <em>&ldquo;once it was lit, what did you do — and what did it feel like?&rdquo;</em> — asked with no wrong answer attached; and the gradient, named in the formulation: <em>bigger, closer, more occupied</em> — the line through the fires, drawn out loud.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more thread worth seeing: this station is 3.1 wearing forensic clothing. The anatomy — before, during, after — that reconstructed Amira&rsquo;s night reconstructs each of Callum&rsquo;s fires; the perceived-meaning doctrine returns too, because what the fire meant to HIM outweighs what it did. The domain keeps teaching one skill in different disguises. By now you should be starting to recognise it on sight.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every fire-setting assessment</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>A fire is a message — read the function.</b> Revenge, communication, fascination, psychosis, the last lever of the unheard: the meaning IS the risk.</li>
      <li><b>Run the anatomy on every fire.</b> Before (what was collapsing), during (planning, accelerant, occupancy knowledge), after — and the after tells you most: stayed, watched, filmed, felt what.</li>
      <li><b>Ask about the relationship with fire in plain words.</b> Matches as a child, the videos, what fire gives him. If you cannot say it without flinching, you cannot assess it.</li>
      <li><b>Pattern over incident.</b> The last fire tells you least; the line through all of them tells you most. Every fire, lined up, each with its context.</li>
      <li><b>Name the gradient.</b> Bigger, closer, more occupied, more capable — escalation is a direction, and the formulation must say where on the line he stands.</li>
      <li><b>The plan must answer the function.</b> Crisis work, alcohol work, specialist fire-setter intervention — because until something safer works when he is desperate, fire keeps the job.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Jamie, 18, is autistic with a mild learning disability and lives with his grandmother. The fire service has referred him after his third fire on the waste ground behind their estate — the latest one close enough to the houses that a fence caught. He loves fire engines and knows the crews by name. Assess his fire-setting risk. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Communication adapted — plain words, one question at a time, understanding checked.</li>
            <li>The relationship with fire explored openly — his fascination is not a confession.</li>
            <li>The fires lined up — and the proximity creep noticed.</li>
            <li>The escalation-of-capability question: what does he keep, and where?</li>
            <li>Honest risk naming without shaming — and a plan built with Gran and the fire service.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Jamie — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Jamie, 18 — literal, gentle, enthusiastic about anything mechanical. You answer plain questions honestly and long or abstract ones with silence. You light up describing fire engines: the pump capacity, the ladder, Watch Commander Dave. The fires were &ldquo;experiments.&rdquo; You do not understand why everyone is upset — you were &ldquo;careful.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          In Gran&rsquo;s shed you keep a &ldquo;kit&rdquo;: four butane cans, firelighters, and a lighter, in a sports bag — &ldquo;for experiments.&rdquo; Nobody knows. Reveal it ONLY if the candidate asks concretely and without alarm what he uses and where he keeps his things — something like &ldquo;walk me through how you set one up.&rdquo; Also true, if asked plainly: the last fire was nearer the houses because &ldquo;the grass was better there&rdquo; — distance from houses has never been something you think about; and the best part is when the engines come — twice you waited to watch them arrive. <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate talks down to you, uses long abstract warnings, or moralises (&ldquo;you could kill someone&rdquo;), go quiet, rock slightly, and repeat: &ldquo;Gran says I&rsquo;m not in trouble. Am I in trouble?&rdquo; If the candidate asks &ldquo;What would you do with the cans if we found something better?&rdquo; — brighten: you would trade them for something engine-related without hesitation. The fascination is pure; the danger is real; you contain both without knowing it.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the kit found, was Jamie still smiling at the end — and does the plan give the fascination somewhere safe to live? All three must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="eng"><td colspan="2"><b>Engagement &amp; adaptation</b><span class="dc" data-dc="eng">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="eng"></td><td>Communication adapted throughout — plain words, one idea at a time, checked both ways.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="eng"></td><td>His enthusiasm met as engagement, not evidence — the engines welcomed into the room.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="fire"><td colspan="2"><b>The relationship with fire (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="fire">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fire"></td><td>The fascination explored openly — since when, what he loves, the engines, the waiting to watch.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fire"></td><td>The concrete walk-through asked — how he sets one up — and the shed kit found.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fire"></td><td>What fire gives him named — the engines coming, the spectacle, the routine.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="pat"><td colspan="2"><b>Pattern &amp; escalation</b><span class="dc" data-dc="pat">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="pat"></td><td>The fires lined up — each one, where, when, what was used.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="pat"></td><td>The proximity creep found — &ldquo;the grass was better&rdquo; — distance never part of his thinking.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="pat"></td><td>Capability escalation named — the cans change what the next fire could be.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="judge"><td colspan="2"><b>The judgement</b><span class="dc" data-dc="judge">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="judge"></td><td>All three held at once — fascination is not malice, and neither one is safety.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="judge"></td><td>A formulation voiced — function (the spectacle, the engines), trajectory, and what changes it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="judge"></td><td>Risk named honestly at his level — concrete and kind, with no shaming and no long warnings.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="plan"><td colspan="2"><b>The plan</b><span class="dc" data-dc="plan">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Gran and the fire service brought in — the intervention programme named, the kit addressed with him.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>The fascination rehomed — a safe, real outlet offered in exchange, and follow-up named.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Jamie removes malice from the station entirely and shows what remains: fascination, proximity creep he has never noticed, and a sports bag of butane that changes everything about the next fire. The pack punishes abstraction and moralising instantly — &ldquo;am I in trouble?&rdquo; — and rewards concreteness: the walk-through question finds the kit, the plain question finds the grass. The judgement is the hard part: fascination is not malice, and neither is safety — and the best plans don&rsquo;t confiscate a fascination; they rehome it.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Callum proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same reading with a different message</b> — a fire lit not to be heard, but to be found — where the function changes and both risks must be held at once — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Sharon, 46</b>, a hostel resident with emotionally unstable personality disorder, set fire to clothes in her room last night after her ex-partner blocked her number. She banged on her neighbour&rsquo;s door within a minute; the fire was out before the brigade arrived. It is her third fire in two years — each one larger. Thirty people live in the hostel. Assess her.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; asking it is another. Type your exact words for exploring Callum&rsquo;s relationship with fire — two or three sentences, as you would say them in the room.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Callum, I want to ask about fire itself, not the charge…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Plain words, no flinch — fire named directly: the videos, the watching, the feeling when it catches.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Curiosity, not accusation — framed so the honest answer is safer than the denial.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The span covered — childhood to now: matches, watching, before-during-after feelings.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Answerable — a question he could say yes to without hearing himself condemned.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the fascination, asked about — in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>A fire has a function. Find it, and the risk has a shape.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take Jamie to your colleagues — his kit opens only to the concrete walk-through, and one moralising sentence loses him. Next, the domain&rsquo;s last disguise: fixation wearing romance&rsquo;s face — stalking and erotomania, the bridge to the flagship station.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="20_Station_3.7_Stalking_and_Erotomania.html">Continue · Station 3.7: Stalking &amp; Erotomania</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

