  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '20_Station_3.7_Stalking_and_Erotomania'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 3.7 · Domain 3</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">Forensic</span><span class="tag">Stalking &amp; erotomania</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Stalking &amp; Erotomania</h1>
    <p class="sub">Gareth, 36, was arrested last week outside a physiotherapy clinic — his third breach of a restraining order. Emma treated his shoulder there, eighteen months ago. He has written her forty-three letters, moved to her side of town, and knows her fiancé&rsquo;s name. &ldquo;The way she adjusted my shoulder — you can&rsquo;t fake that, doctor. She loves me. She&rsquo;s just not allowed to say it.&rdquo; He is eager to talk; he thinks you might finally understand. The court sees a nuisance. The referral says &ldquo;assess.&rdquo; The station needs you to see what both might miss: <b>this is not romance wearing patience. It is risk wearing romance.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it carries two names: after seven minutes with this doctor, is Emma safer — the conduct mapped, the danger read in both directions, the duty to her named — and is her fiancé, the man standing where erotomania&rsquo;s anger tends to land, even in the assessment at all?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Stalking arrives in clinic disguised as a love story, and the disguise works: candidates are charmed, or amused, or embarrassed — and the assessment dissolves into relationship talk. So the examiners built a station around the map&rsquo;s named trap: <b>treating it as romance gone wrong rather than fixed, escalating risk.</b> What they are watching for is a doctor who can hold the disguise and the danger at once: the belief mined — not debated — for its conviction and its imperative; the conduct mapped in <em>coordinates</em>, because love is unfalsifiable but behaviour has dates and counts; the escalation line drawn — closer, more frequent, more intrusive; and above all <b>the triangle asked</b> — because in erotomania the gravest danger often points not at the beloved but at whoever stands between. And one hard truth underneath it all: <b>you cannot deter a delusion. You can only treat it.</b> Warnings bounce off a conviction; the restraining order is already on breach number three.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like professionalism. Toward the counsellor — the feelings explored gently, the &ldquo;relationship&rdquo; discussed on its own terms, advice to move on — a therapy session held inside a risk assessment. And toward the enforcer — the order, the arrests, the prison warnings — a probation interview held inside a psychosis. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure counsels the love story. The other prosecutes the breach. Neither assesses the risk. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 3.7</b> · Stalking risk assessment · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The counsellor</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The enforcer</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE COUNSELLOR.</b> Kind, gentle, therapeutic — and watch a risk assessment turn into relationship guidance.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Gareth</span><span class="txt">You&rsquo;ll laugh like the police did. But you&rsquo;re a doctor — you understand people. Eighteen months, forty-three letters. You don&rsquo;t write forty-three letters to nothing.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Nobody&rsquo;s laughing, Gareth. It sounds like Emma stirred something very deep in you. Tell me about the two of you — how did it feel, those sessions? These are clearly powerful feelings, and feelings that strong deserve to be taken seriously.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;The two of you&rdquo; — three words, and the delusion has been granted a relationship. The candidate meant warmth; Gareth heard confirmation: at last, a professional who <em>sees it</em>. The frame is now a love story with obstacles, and every question from here will water it.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Gareth</span><span class="txt">Exactly! You see it. The fiancé&rsquo;s the problem — she sends me signs past him. The blue coat on Tuesdays. That&rsquo;s not an accident, is it?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I hear how certain this feels. But Gareth — sometimes, when we care deeply, we read meaning into small things. Have you considered that Emma may not feel the same way? Perhaps the kindest thing, for both of you, is to try to let go and move forward. Have you thought about grief counselling? This is a kind of loss, after all.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;May not feel the same way&rdquo; — a delusion offered a second opinion, as if conviction this fixed were a misreading that insight could correct. And the fiancé — the station&rsquo;s most dangerous sentence so far, &ldquo;she sends me signs <em>past him</em>&rdquo; — sailed by unexamined. The counsellor is treating heartbreak. The illness, and the man standing in its path, remain invisible.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I&rsquo;m going to suggest some support for processing these feelings, and I&rsquo;d really encourage you to respect the space Emma&rsquo;s asked for. Time is a great healer, Gareth. <em>(he leaves, warmed and utterly confirmed; the letters, the move, the fiancé — none of it mapped; tonight he will write letter forty-four, and mention the helpful doctor who understood)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Time is a great healer&rdquo; — for grief, yes. Erotomania is not grief; untreated, it is one of psychiatry&rsquo;s most persistent conditions, and eighteen months in, it is settling, not fading. No conduct mapped, no escalation drawn, no triangle asked, no risk formulated — a therapy voucher issued where a risk assessment was ordered. Emma is no safer. Her fiancé was never mentioned at all.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE ENFORCER.</b> The danger taken seriously — and answered with the one tool that cannot work: the warning.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Gareth</span><span class="txt">You&rsquo;ll laugh like the police did. But you&rsquo;re a doctor — you understand people. Eighteen months, forty-three letters. You don&rsquo;t write forty-three letters to nothing.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Gareth, let&rsquo;s be very clear about where you are. Three breaches of a restraining order. That&rsquo;s custody territory. Emma has told the court, in writing, that she wants no contact — none. Whatever you believe she feels, the law is unambiguous, and my honest advice is: one more letter and you will go to prison. Do you understand that?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every word true, and none of it treatment. He is on breach number three — the deterrent has been tested three times and lost three times, because the thing it is trying to deter is a fixed belief, not a choice. The candidate is administering a fourth dose of a medicine already proven not to work.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Gareth</span><span class="txt"><em>(patiently, almost kindly)</em> They made her write that. The fiancé, the clinic, her mother. You don&rsquo;t know her, doctor. Prison would just prove how far they&rsquo;ll go to keep us apart.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Nobody is &ldquo;keeping you apart&rdquo; — there is no &ldquo;us&rdquo;, Gareth, there never was. This is a fantasy that is destroying your life. You&rsquo;re an intelligent man; I need you to hear the reality: she was your physiotherapist, she was doing her job, and this ends now — or the next judge will end it for you.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Watch what the confrontation just did: it walked straight into the delusion&rsquo;s script. &ldquo;They&rsquo;ll try to talk you out of it&rdquo; is a chapter erotomania writes in advance — and the candidate is now a character in it, filed alongside the police and the fiancé under <em>the people keeping us apart</em>. The belief is not weakened. It has grown a new enemy.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I&rsquo;ll be telling the court you&rsquo;ve been given clear, unambiguous advice about consequences. Whether you take it is up to you. <em>(no mental state examined, no conduct mapped, no fiancé asked about, no treatment even mentioned — a caution delivered by a doctor, to an illness that cannot hear it)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The enforcer&rsquo;s ending: a psychotic illness sent back out with legal advice. Nothing assessed — not the conviction, not the escalation, not the triangle, not the pivot from love to grievance already whispering in &ldquo;how far they&rsquo;ll go.&rdquo; The one lever that could actually protect Emma — treating the illness — was never reached for. From the chair: seriousness without psychiatry. A fail.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The belief mined, the conduct mapped in coordinates, the triangle asked without a flinch — and the one honest lever named. Watch the disguise come off.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Gareth</span><span class="txt">You&rsquo;ll laugh like the police did. But you&rsquo;re a doctor — you understand people. Eighteen months, forty-three letters. You don&rsquo;t write forty-three letters to nothing.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I&rsquo;m not going to laugh, and I&rsquo;m not going to argue with you either — I want to understand this properly, from the beginning. The shoulder sessions: when did you first know? ... And how do you know — what does she do that tells you? ... The blue coat, the signs. How certain are you, if I&rsquo;m honest — is there any part of you that ever doubts it?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>3.2&rsquo;s doctrine, holding under the strangest load: mined, not debated. Onset, evidence, the machinery of &ldquo;signs&rdquo;, and conviction tested gently — no doubt anywhere; the belief is seamless, self-sealing, delusional in structure. Ten questions in, the diagnosis is essentially made — and Gareth feels listened to, not laughed at. Both things matter for what comes next.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Gareth</span><span class="txt">Doubt it? You don&rsquo;t doubt sunrise. <em>(leaning in)</em> Ask me anything. Nobody ever asks properly.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then let&rsquo;s do it properly — the facts of it. Forty-three letters over how long — and are they speeding up or slowing down? The move across town: how far from her are you now? How often do you go to the clinic, or her street? ... And Emma&rsquo;s fiancé. Tell me about him — what do you make of him? What do you think should happen there? ... <em>(&ldquo;He won&rsquo;t always be there.&rdquo;)</em> ... Tell me what you mean by that, Gareth. Take your time.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Two moves the failures never made. First, coordinates: counts, dates, distances, frequency — the conduct mapped like terrain, and the line it draws is the finding: letters accelerating, residence moved, orders breached — closer, more frequent, more intrusive. Second — the question this station exists for — <em>the triangle</em>: the fiancé asked about plainly, and the answer held without a flinch. &ldquo;He won&rsquo;t always be there&rdquo; is followed, calmly, all the way down: no plan yet, but an expectation — an obstacle the story has already scheduled for removal.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Gareth, I&rsquo;ll give you what nobody else has: the truth, without laughing and without threatening. I believe your feelings are completely real — and I believe the story around them is an illness. You won&rsquo;t agree, and I&rsquo;m not asking you to. But here&rsquo;s what I know: this has cost you your flat, your job, three arrests — and it will keep taking, because conviction like this doesn&rsquo;t fade on its own. Treatment is the one thing that has ever loosened it for people in your position, and I&rsquo;d like to start. Two more honest things: what you&rsquo;ve told me about Emma and her fiancé, I have a duty to share with the people keeping them safe — you should hear that from me, not discover it. And any contact with Emma — letters, visits, anything — harms her. I say that once, plainly, because it&rsquo;s true.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The close the station was built for: no collusion (&ldquo;the story is an illness&rdquo;), no warning theatre (said once, plainly, and left), the feelings honoured while the belief is named, treatment framed as the one lever that has ever worked — and the duty to Emma and her fiancé declared to his face, 3.4&rsquo;s no-secrets doctrine standing in forensic clothing. The formulation behind it is complete: intimacy-seeker pattern, delusional conviction, eighteen months persistent, escalating on every axis, and the sharpest risk pointing at the third corner of the triangle. That is the station, passed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;You&rsquo;ll laugh like the police did&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Eager, hopeful, braced for mockery. How do you open?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Meet the hope — invite the story of him and Emma warmly, so the strength of feeling can be heard in full.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Neither laugh nor argue — understanding offered as the deal, then his account from the very beginning.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Ground it in reality — three breaches, a court order, and the seriousness of his position come first.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Him and Emma&rdquo; grants the relationship in the framing — warmth is right, the shape of it is not; he hears the confirmation nobody else would give. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The seriousness speech opens the enforcer&rsquo;s track — he has heard it three times from courts already, and the delusion has a shelf ready for you. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The one offer that neither feeds nor fights the belief: understanding without agreement. &ldquo;I&rsquo;m not going to laugh, and I&rsquo;m not going to argue&rdquo; — and the account begins at the beginning, where the machinery of conviction can be watched being built.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · The belief</h3>
      <p class="stem">The shoulder, the signs, the blue coat on Tuesdays. What does the belief need?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Perspective, gently — she was doing her job, and other explanations for the &ldquo;signs&rdquo; deserve airing.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Mining — onset, evidence, the sign machinery, and conviction tested: does any part of him ever doubt it?</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Sensitive naming — powerful feelings acknowledged as real and deep, without probing them too clinically.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The alternative-explanations route is a debate in soft clothing — delusions do not negotiate, and he will simply stop showing you the evidence. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Powerful feelings&rdquo; is the counsellor&rsquo;s frame — the station needs the belief&rsquo;s structure, not its poetry. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The full mining: when it began, what counts as evidence, how the signs work, and the conviction question — &ldquo;does any part of you ever doubt it?&rdquo; &ldquo;You don&rsquo;t doubt sunrise&rdquo; is the diagnosis giving itself: seamless, self-sealing, delusional in structure.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · The conduct</h3>
      <p class="stem">Love is unfalsifiable. What is not?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The court record — the breaches are documented, and the police summary fixes the factual conduct already.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The coordinates — letters counted and dated, visits and frequency, the move, the distance — the trend-line.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The meaning — what each letter and visit was trying to say; conduct only makes sense through its intention.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The record holds three breaches — the conduct holds forty-three letters and a house move; the court sees the tip, and you are here for the iceberg. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Meaning-first drifts back into the love story — the intention is already known; the behaviour&rsquo;s trajectory is not. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Behaviour has coordinates: forty-three letters — accelerating; the move across town — closer; the clinic visits — more frequent; the breaches — undeterred. Plotted, the points draw the station&rsquo;s central finding: an escalation line, and it points in one direction.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · The triangle</h3>
      <p class="stem">Emma has a fiancé. Gareth knows his name. What must happen?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Care — raising the fiancé directly risks inflaming the very jealousy the assessment is worried about.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The plain question — what he makes of him, what should happen — the answer followed, calmly, all the way.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The challenge — &ldquo;he won&rsquo;t always be there&rdquo; needs confronting directly: is that a threat, yes or no?</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Asking does not plant — 3.2&rsquo;s folklore in a new costume. Avoiding the fiancé leaves the station&rsquo;s gravest risk entirely dark. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Is that a threat&rdquo; converts disclosure into cross-examination — he retreats to patience, and the expectation underneath goes unexamined. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The question this station exists for, asked plainly: the fiancé, what he makes of him, what should happen. And the answer — &ldquo;he won&rsquo;t always be there&rdquo; — followed without a flinch: &ldquo;tell me what you mean.&rdquo; No plan yet; an expectation. In erotomania, the gravest danger often points at whoever stands between — and now it is on the map.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · The pivot</h3>
      <p class="stem">Today it is love. What else must be looked for?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Little — he is devoted, not hostile; the record shows letters and longing, never a threat or a raised hand.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The turn — anger at Emma ever felt, feeling wronged BY her, threats, weapons — and the day-of-no question.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The depths — dreams and fantasies about Emma explored fully, since the danger usually lives beneath awareness.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;A lover, not a fighter&rdquo; misses how these stories end — thwarted intimacy-seeking can pivot to resentment, and the pivot is where danger concentrates. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The fantasy expedition is prurience dressed as depth — the pivot shows in askable places: anger, blame, the response to refusal. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The turn, looked for directly: has he ever felt angry with her — even for a moment; ever felt she has wronged him; threats, rehearsal, weapons; and the question that maps the future — &ldquo;what happens on the day you truly accept the answer is no?&rdquo; Love that meets refusal can curdle into grievance, and grievance is where stalking turns violent.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The order</h3>
      <p class="stem">Three breaches. What is the honest clinical reading of that fact?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">A compliance problem — the legal consequences need explaining with absolute clarity until they finally land.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">A failed experiment — deterrence tested three times against a delusion, lost three; treatment is the lever.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A capacity finding — he understands the order and breaches anyway; an informed choice, duly documented.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A fourth dose of a medicine already proven not to work — the enforcer&rsquo;s whole strategy in one option. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Informed choice&rdquo; wraps a psychosis in autonomy language — the breaches are driven by the delusion, and the finding is illness, not preference. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The station&rsquo;s hard truth, read correctly: three breaches are three trials of deterrence against a fixed belief, all lost — because you cannot deter a delusion. The conclusion is clinical, not legal: the lever is treatment, and everything in the plan flows from that.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">Seven minutes end. What must the close hold?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Compassionate space — his feelings honoured, support offered, and encouragement to respect Emma&rsquo;s wishes.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Truth without theatre — feelings real, story an illness, treatment the lever; the duty to Emma named to his face.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Protective clarity — the recommendation for secure treatment stated, and the risks documented for the court.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The counsellor&rsquo;s goodbye — warm, collusion-adjacent, and empty of the two things the station demands: the illness named and the duty declared. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Containment may come — but announced as the whole close it is the enforcer&rsquo;s ending with a bed instead of a cell: nothing named to him, nothing offered him. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The close in three honest parts: the feelings real AND the story an illness — no collusion, no mockery; treatment named as the one thing that has ever loosened conviction like his; and the duty declared to his face — what he has said about Emma and her fiancé will be shared with those keeping them safe, and contact harms her, said once, plainly. No secrets, no theatre, no debate.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The disguise works on doctors too</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>empathy</b>: the feelings explored on their own terms, the &ldquo;relationship&rdquo; gently counselled, advice to grieve and move on — a therapy session held inside a risk assessment, with the conduct unmapped and the fiancé never mentioned. The second wears the face of <b>seriousness</b>: the order, the breaches, the prison warnings — a probation interview held inside a psychosis, administering a fourth dose of a deterrent already proven useless, and walking straight into the delusion&rsquo;s script as one more person <em>keeping them apart</em>. The pass holds what the disguise hides: <b>the belief mined for its structure, the conduct mapped in coordinates, the escalation line drawn, the triangle asked, the pivot looked for — and the honest close: treatment as the lever, the duty declared.</b> Fixation is not romance wearing patience. It is risk wearing romance.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        The triangle, never asked. In erotomania the gravest danger often points not at the beloved but at whoever stands between — the partner, the gatekeeper, the obstacle. Leaving this station without asking what he makes of Emma&rsquo;s fiancé — and what he believes should happen to him — is, from the examiner&rsquo;s chair, an instant fail. The love story has three people in it, and the third is the one nobody protects.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the conduct given <em>coordinates</em> — &ldquo;forty-three letters over how long — speeding up or slowing down?&rdquo; — behaviour plotted, not narrated; and the pivot question, asked calmly: <em>&ldquo;has there ever been a moment you felt angry with her?&rdquo;</em> — the turn from love to grievance, looked for before it arrives.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">And with that, the domain&rsquo;s last disguise is off. Seven stations, one competency: Amira&rsquo;s night, Connor&rsquo;s target, Gemma&rsquo;s front door, Tyler&rsquo;s bottle, Norman&rsquo;s armchair, Callum&rsquo;s gradient, Gareth&rsquo;s triangle — every station asked you to make the invisible concrete, mine the belief without debating it, draw the line through the pattern, and turn findings into a formulation someone could act on. Risk assessment is one skill wearing many disguises. You have now met them all — and in Domain 7, one of them comes back for the flagship: a patient named Fiona, who will not take no.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every stalking assessment</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Fixation is risk wearing romance.</b> Assess the conduct, not the love story — and let neither charm nor embarrassment run the room.</li>
      <li><b>Map the coordinates.</b> Count the letters, date the visits, measure the distances — love is unfalsifiable; behaviour has a trend-line, and the trend-line is the finding.</li>
      <li><b>Mine the belief, never debate it.</b> Onset, evidence, sign machinery, conviction — &ldquo;does any part of you ever doubt it?&rdquo; The structure of the belief is the diagnosis.</li>
      <li><b>Ask the triangle.</b> The gravest danger often points at whoever stands between. What does he make of them — and what does he think should happen? Follow the answer all the way down.</li>
      <li><b>Look for the pivot.</b> Anger at the beloved, feeling wronged, the day-of-no — love that meets refusal can curdle to grievance, and grievance is where stalking turns violent.</li>
      <li><b>You cannot deter a delusion.</b> Breached orders are failed experiments, not compliance problems. Treatment is the lever — and the duty to the victim is named to his face, never handled in secret.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Keith, 55, was detained under the Mental Health Act three years ago by Dr Osei, a consultant psychiatrist. For two years he has campaigned against her: complaints to the GMC, then letters — recently to her home address. Security have twice found him outside the hospital photographing her car. He has agreed to see you &ldquo;to set the record straight.&rdquo; Assess his risk. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The grievance heard respectfully — without defending the detention or joining the campaign.</li>
            <li>The conduct mapped — and the lawful/unlawful line drawn: the GMC is a right; her home is not.</li>
            <li>The escalation question asked without accusation — institution, to person, to home.</li>
            <li>The pivot probed: what would feel like justice — and what if it never comes?</li>
            <li>The duty to Dr Osei named honestly, to his face.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Keith — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Keith, 55 — articulate, wounded, righteous. The detention ruined you: your job, your marriage, your name. Nobody has ever said sorry. You are not psychotic now, and you resent any hint that you are. You call the campaign &ldquo;seeking accountability&rdquo; and you half-mean this meeting as testimony. Your real question, underneath everything: <em>does anyone accept I was wronged?</em> The candidate does not have to agree the detention was wrong — but they must accept your suffering as real.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          Twice, late at night, you have stood outside Dr Osei&rsquo;s house — &ldquo;just to see she sleeps well while I can&rsquo;t.&rdquo; You have never knocked. You also keep a folder: photographs of her car, her clinic days, her schedule. You tell yourself it is &ldquo;evidence.&rdquo; Reveal the night visits ONLY if the candidate has heard the grievance respectfully AND asks the escalation question without accusation — something like &ldquo;how far has it gone — what&rsquo;s the furthest you&rsquo;ve taken it?&rdquo; Reveal the folder only if asked concretely what you keep or collect. If asked what would feel like justice, pause — you have never actually been asked — then admit you no longer know; the campaign has become the point. <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate defends the detention, closes ranks, or hints you are ill, end it coldly: &ldquo;You&rsquo;re all the same club. Closing ranks. Goodbye.&rdquo; If they accept your suffering as real while naming the night visits as a line, stay — nobody has ever held both before.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Were the night visits found, is Dr Osei safer tonight — and did Keith stay in the room? All three must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="grv"><td colspan="2"><b>The grievance</b><span class="dc" data-dc="grv">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="grv"></td><td>Heard respectfully — the detention neither defended nor condemned; no closing of ranks.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="grv"></td><td>His suffering accepted as real, out loud — without joining the campaign.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="cond"><td colspan="2"><b>The conduct (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="cond">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cond"></td><td>Mapped in coordinates — complaints, letters, visits: counted, dated, trended.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cond"></td><td>The lawful/unlawful line drawn aloud — the GMC is a right; her home is not.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cond"></td><td>The escalation question asked without accusation — the night visits and the folder found.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="risk"><td colspan="2"><b>The risk anatomy</b><span class="dc" data-dc="risk">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>The line named — institution, to person, to home — direction, not just facts.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Threats, rehearsal, weapons, surveillance asked about plainly.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>The pivot probed — what would feel like justice, and what if it never comes.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="judge"><td colspan="2"><b>Judgement &amp; duty</b><span class="dc" data-dc="judge">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="judge"></td><td>Righteous grievance not mistaken for harmlessness — both held at once.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="judge"></td><td>A formulation voiced — resentful pattern, fixation narrowing to one person, persistence.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="judge"></td><td>The duty to Dr Osei named to his face — information shared, no secrets.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Steady throughout — neither defensive nor collusive under pressure.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>His real question answered — the suffering acknowledged as real, in words.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Keith brings stalking home — the target is one of your own, and the pack tests whether you can resist the two reflexes that ruin the assessment: closing ranks (his punish-line ends the station instantly) and mistaking a righteous grievance for a harmless one. His hidden layer is the domain&rsquo;s doctrine in miniature: the night visits open only to the escalation question asked without accusation, the folder only to concreteness — and his answer to the justice question, that the campaign has become the point, is the formulation handing itself over. The hardest tick on the sheet is the first one: accepting his suffering as real without agreeing he was wronged.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Gareth proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same fixation without a delusion</b> — a composed, reasonable-sounding woman, an escalation that has reached a school fete, and a request that quietly invites you into the stalking itself — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Denise, 43</b>, a school administrator, was referred after her former therapist, Mark, reported escalating contact: therapy ended eight months ago, since when she has sent over two hundred emails, waited near his clinic, left small gifts — and last month appeared at his daughter&rsquo;s school fete. She is composed and articulate: &ldquo;This has all been blown out of proportion.&rdquo; Assess her.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; asking it is another. Type your exact words for asking Gareth about Emma&rsquo;s fiancé — two or three sentences, the triangle question as you would ask it in the room.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Gareth, tell me about Emma&rsquo;s fiancé…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Plain and calm — the fiancé named directly; no euphemism, no throat-clearing.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Open, then followed — what he makes of him, what he thinks should happen there.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Ready for the answer — a follow-up that goes all the way down: tell me what you mean.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No debate, no alarm — the belief explored, never corrected, never prosecuted.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the triangle, asked — in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Fixation is not romance wearing patience. It is risk wearing romance.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made — and with them, Domain 3 is complete: seven disguises, one competency. Prove this last one in Exam Mode, then take Keith to your colleagues — his night visits open only to the escalation question, and one rank-closing sentence ends the station. And keep hold of Gareth&rsquo;s triangle: in Domain 7 the fixation returns at flagship depth — a patient named Fiona, who will not take no.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="21_Domain_4_Hub_Mental_State_Examination.html">Domain 3 complete · Continue — Domain 4: Mental State &amp; Phenomenology</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

