  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '21_Domain_4_Hub_Mental_State_Examination'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Domain 4 of 8</span>
      <span class="tag">Mental State Examination &amp; Phenomenology</span>
      <span class="tag">7 constructs · the craft domain</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Mental State &amp; Phenomenology</h1>
    <p class="sub">The mental state examination is where psychiatry stops being conversation and becomes craft — and where a specific failure hides in plain sight: candidates who <b style="color:var(--lgold)">record phenomena without ever opening them</b>. &ldquo;Hears voices&rdquo; goes in the notes, and the only question that matters goes unasked: <em>what does the voice say?</em> This domain trains the three verbs the marks are really built on — <b>eliciting</b> what is there, <b>naming</b> it precisely, and <b>using</b> it: feeding every finding straight into diagnosis and risk. An MSE is not a form. It is an examination.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE LENS, APPLIED TO THIS DOMAIN</b>
      &ldquo;Would I trust this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; — In this domain the question becomes the 3am test: if this doctor examined a patient overnight, would their documented mental state let me make a decision without re-interviewing the patient myself? An MSE that has to be redone was never an examination. That is what every station here is really asking.</div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ============ 1 WHY FIRST ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this domain matters</span>
    <h2>Elicit it. Name it. Use it.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Every candidate can name the headings — appearance, behaviour, speech, mood, thought, perception, cognition, insight. The headings are not the skill. The examiners are watching for three harder things. <b>Eliciting:</b> the questions that actually open a phenomenon — content first, because &ldquo;what does the voice say?&rdquo; carries more diagnosis and more risk than an hour of screening. <b>Naming:</b> precision — a delusion told apart from an overvalued idea, an obsession from a rumination, delirium&rsquo;s fluctuation from dementia&rsquo;s decline — because in this exam the right word is the finding. And <b>using:</b> the verb candidates forget — every phenomenon fed forward into diagnosis and risk, because a hallucination recorded but never opened is Domain 3&rsquo;s instant fail wearing Domain 4&rsquo;s clothing. The MSE is not the paperwork of the interview. It IS the interview, examined.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What this domain assumes, and what it doesn''t:</b> it assumes Domains 1–3 are in your hands — rapport that opens people, information that lands, and the risk reflexes now trained. What it adds is the craft layer: the phenomenology itself — how to reach it under time pressure, how to name it so a colleague could act on your words alone, and how to make every finding earn its place in the formulation.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THE CONSTRUCTS ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Your mastery spine for this domain</span>
    <h2>The seven skills examiners are marking</h2>
    <p class="intro">These are the domain''s constructs — the specific, checkable skills behind the mental state marks. As you work through the Academy, each one climbs the ladder you met in the orientation: Explored → Recognised → Demonstrated → Validated. This spine fills as you work, and it is the honest answer to &ldquo;am I ready?&rdquo;</p>
    <div class="spine">
      <div class="spine-head"><b>Mental State Examination &amp; Phenomenology</b><span>0 of 7 recognised — your spine starts here</span></div>
      <div class="cons">
        <div class="con"><span class="n">1</span><span>The systematic MSE under time pressure — complete, ordered, and finished inside seven minutes</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">2</span><span>Hallucinations, elicited properly — content first: whose voice, and what does it say?</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">3</span><span>Delusions — conviction, content, and what he might <em>do</em> about it</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">4</span><span>Mood inside other presentations — the depression screen everywhere it hides</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">5</span><span>Cognitive assessment — delirium versus dementia, and bedside testing done properly</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">6</span><span>Insight — assessed as a finding, never assumed from fluency or agreement</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
        <div class="con"><span class="n">7</span><span>The bridge — every MSE finding fed straight into risk and diagnosis</span><span class="dots"><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i><i class="dot"></i></span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 STATIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The stations in this domain</span>
    <h2>Walk them in order</h2>
    <div class="stn">
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">4.1</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Hearing Voices</b><span>Yusuf, first episode. The domain''s cornerstone: the hallucination opened, not recorded — whose voice, what does it say, can he resist it — and the answer fed straight into risk.</span></span>
        <span class="chip go">Begin here</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">4.2</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Elated and Spending</b><span>The mania MSE — where the phenomenology is a pleasure to elicit and the marks live in what it is quietly creating: the spending, the disinhibition, the exhaustion underneath the shine.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 2</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">4.3</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Confused on the Ward</b><span>Delirium — cognitive testing that hunts the medical cause while it tests: attention first, fluctuation found, and a mental state that sends the ward looking in the right place.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 3</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">4.4</span>
        <span class="t"><b>The Memory Clinic</b><span>Dementia or depression — the memory tested AND the mood screened, because pseudodementia is the diagnosis candidates lose by examining only the thing the referral named.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 4</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">4.5</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Obsessions and Rituals</b><span>OCD — not the ritual list but the anatomy that makes it OCD: the intrusion, the distress, the resistance, and the hours a day the illness is charging.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 5</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">4.6</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Low Weight</b><span>The eating disorder assessment — the psychological story earned AND the physical risk examined, because the seam between this domain and Domain 3 is exactly where this station is marked.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 6</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">4.7</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Adult ADHD Assessment</b><span>Jess arrives carrying her own diagnosis — neither the rubber stamp nor the gatekeeper, with the childhood thread hunted by scene rather than by checklist.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 7</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">4.8</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Autism Assessment in an Adult</b><span>Bryony has been masking since she was nine — where coping gets read as absence, and the interview itself has to be adapted before it can measure anything.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 8</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">4.9</span>
        <span class="t"><b>PTSD Assessment</b><span>Erin asks not to be taken through it again — the whole diagnosis reached around the event rather than through it, and distress steadied instead of mined.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 9</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">4.10</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Alcohol Dependence Assessment</b><span>Duncan intends to stop tonight — the dependence syndrome rather than the units, and the counterintuitive instruction that keeps him safe until it can be done properly.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 10</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">4.11</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Behavioural Change in Learning Disability</b><span>Alec has been hitting the side of his head for six weeks — behaviour as the message, and the body hunted before a single behavioural formulation is written.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 11</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 ENTRY DECISION ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Before you enter</span>
    <h2>One decision at the door</h2>
    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>What is a mental state examination marked on?</h3>
      <p class="stem">Two candidates both establish that the patient hears voices. Both document it. One scores clearly higher. What most likely made the difference?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it''s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Coverage — they screened every modality: second person, third person, commands, visions, smells.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="1">Rapport — they normalised the experience so skilfully that the patient spoke about it freely.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="2">Opening — they asked what the voice actually says, and fed the answer into diagnosis and risk.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>The modality sweep is coverage, not examination — every box ticked, and the one question that carries the diagnosis and the danger still unasked. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-1"><b>THE EXAMINER''S VIEW</b>Rapport opens the door — Domain 1 taught that — but a door opened onto an unexamined phenomenon still scores as an unexamined phenomenon. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Content first. &ldquo;What does the voice say?&rdquo; is where the diagnosis lives, and where the risk lives — a running commentary points one way, a command to act points somewhere far more urgent. Elicited, named, used: that is the whole domain in one question, and every station here trains a face of it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Domain open</span>
    <h2>Elicit it. Name it. Use it.</h2>
    <p>Station 4.1 begins with the phenomenon that defines the specialty: a young man, a first episode, and voices nobody has yet asked the right question about. Everything this domain teaches starts with what the voice says.</p>
    <a class="btn mark" href="22_Station_4.1_Hearing_Voices.html">Begin Station 4.1 · Hearing Voices</a>
  </div>
</section>

