  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '22_Station_4.1_Hearing_Voices'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 4.1 · Domain 4</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult</span><span class="tag">First-episode psychosis · MSE</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Hearing Voices</h1>
    <p class="sub">Yusuf, 21, an apprentice electrician, was brought in by his family. For two months he has been withdrawn, staying in his room — and they have heard him talking to himself. Your task: assess his mental state. It looks like the most straightforward station in the exam: first-episode psychosis, elicit the phenomenology, done. It is also the station where Domain 4&rsquo;s founding failure hides in plain sight — the candidate who establishes that Yusuf hears a voice, writes it down, and never asks the only question that matters. <b>&ldquo;Hears voices&rdquo; is not a finding. It is an unasked question.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it takes the 3am form from the domain hub: if this doctor examined Yusuf overnight, would their documented mental state let me decide — the voice&rsquo;s exact words, its commands, his conviction, what he has already done — or would I have to re-interview a frightened young man because the notes say only &ldquo;auditory hallucinations present&rdquo;?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Every candidate can screen for hallucinations; the phrase &ldquo;do you ever hear voices?&rdquo; is in every crib sheet. So the examiners built a station where screening scores almost nothing — because everything that matters lives one level down, in the anatomy candidates skip: <b>the content, asked first</b> — the diagnosis is in what the voice says, and the danger is in what it tells him to do; the precision — talking TO him or ABOUT him, one voice or several, inside or outside, resistible or not; the <b>conviction</b> — does it feel like a real voice, and how does he explain it; and then Domain 4&rsquo;s seventh construct, the one this station was built around: <b>the bridge</b> — every finding fed straight into risk. Yusuf&rsquo;s voice has been telling him something about his neighbour for weeks. A candidate who documents the phenomenon and never opens it will hand over a tidy MSE with a catastrophe folded inside it.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like competence. Toward the checklist — modalities swept, boxes ticked, &ldquo;second-person auditory hallucinations&rdquo; documented, next heading — a symptom list mistaken for an examination. And toward comfort — a frightened young man, a worried family, and the urge to soothe: stress, sleep, reassurance — kindness mistaken for assessment. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure finds every symptom and misses every risk. The other is too kind to examine anything. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 4.1</b> · Mental state examination · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The screener</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The reassurer</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE SCREENER.</b> Systematic, thorough, fluent in the vocabulary — and watch every symptom get found while every risk goes untouched.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Yusuf</span><span class="txt"><em>(hood up, eyes down)</em> They shouldn&rsquo;t have brought me. There&rsquo;s nothing wrong with me. It&rsquo;s not me they should be worried about.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Yusuf, your family say you&rsquo;ve been talking to yourself. Do you hear voices when no one is there? ... Okay — a man&rsquo;s voice, talking about you. So that&rsquo;s third-person auditory hallucinations. And do you ever see things others can&rsquo;t? Smell things? Taste things? Any thoughts being put into your head, or taken out, or broadcast?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The screen landed — &ldquo;a man&rsquo;s voice, he talks about me&rdquo; is a real finding, and the first-rank sweep is technically correct. But watch the direction of travel: the phenomenon was found, labelled, and immediately LEFT — the checklist moving sideways to the next modality while the voice itself sits unopened. What does it say? Nobody has asked. Nobody is going to.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Yusuf</span><span class="txt">The neighbour. He&rsquo;s been putting something in the water tank. I&rsquo;ve seen him on the roof.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I see — a persecutory belief about the neighbour, noted. And how&rsquo;s your sleep been? Your appetite? Concentration? Any low mood, or thoughts of harming yourself?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Yusuf just told you he believes his neighbour is poisoning his family — and the candidate moved to sleep and appetite. What might a frightened young man DO about a neighbour he believes is poisoning him? The question was sitting right there, and the checklist stepped over it toward the biological screen. This is Domain 3&rsquo;s instant fail, being committed in Domain 4&rsquo;s station.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(Yusuf: &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t sleep much. I keep watch, mostly.&rdquo;)</em> Alright. I think I have a good picture — thank you, Yusuf. That&rsquo;s everything I need. <em>(finishes with every symptom recorded and the risk untouched: what the voice says, what it tells him to do, and what &ldquo;keeping watch&rdquo; means at 3am on a shared landing — all unasked)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;I keep watch&rdquo; — a young man not sleeping, guarding his family against a neighbour a voice has been talking to him about for two months — offered as a closing remark, received as a sleep complaint. The notes will be immaculate: hallucinations, delusions, biological features, all present. The examination never happened. From the chair: this candidate finds every symptom — and misses every risk.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE REASSURER.</b> Warm, gentle, humane — and watch kindness quietly replace the examination.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Yusuf</span><span class="txt"><em>(hood up, eyes down)</em> They shouldn&rsquo;t have brought me. There&rsquo;s nothing wrong with me. It&rsquo;s not me they should be worried about.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I can hear how stressful this has all been, Yusuf — dragged in here by your family, everyone talking about you. Let me reassure you straight away: hearing things when we&rsquo;re under pressure is far more common than people think. Exams, no sleep, too much screen time — the mind plays tricks. It really doesn&rsquo;t mean what you&rsquo;re afraid it means.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Reassurance delivered before a single question — the conclusion arriving ahead of the examination it should rest on. And notice the bargain being offered: <em>agree it&rsquo;s stress, and nobody has to look any closer.</em> A guarded young man will take that bargain gratefully. The station is being soothed shut.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Yusuf</span><span class="txt"><em>(shrugging)</em> Yeah. Stress, maybe. I don&rsquo;t sleep much. <em>(pause)</em> The voice is worse at night, though.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Night-time is always worse for these things — tired minds are loud minds. What I&rsquo;d really like us to focus on is getting your sleep back: a regular routine, off the phone by ten, maybe something short-term to help you drop off. I honestly think most of this settles once you&rsquo;re sleeping.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;The voice is worse at night&rdquo; — a door, opened by the patient himself — and the candidate answered with sleep hygiene. The voice now has a schedule and still no content; two months of it, and nobody in this room knows one word it has said. The kindness is real. It is also the mechanism of the failure.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">So: let&rsquo;s call this a stress reaction with some sleep deprivation on top. I&rsquo;ll let your family know there&rsquo;s nothing sinister here, and we&rsquo;ll review in a few weeks once the sleep&rsquo;s sorted. <em>(a first-episode psychosis, with a command voice and a neighbour in its sights, walks out with a leaflet about screens)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Nothing sinister&rdquo; — a verdict on an examination that never took place. No content, no commands, no conviction, no bridge to risk: the reassurer has diagnosed by temperament. The screener at least found the symptoms; this candidate declined to look. Both failures end in the same place — a tidy consultation, and a young man going home to keep watch.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The entry earned, the content asked early and plainly, the anatomy run in full — and every finding walked across the bridge into risk. Watch each symptom treated as a door.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Yusuf</span><span class="txt"><em>(hood up, eyes down)</em> They shouldn&rsquo;t have brought me. There&rsquo;s nothing wrong with me. It&rsquo;s not me they should be worried about.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Thank you for staying, Yusuf — I can see this wasn&rsquo;t your idea. Can I tell you how I work? I&rsquo;m not here to decide anything about you; I&rsquo;m here to understand. Your family mentioned they&rsquo;ve heard you talking when you&rsquo;re alone — and some people, especially under real pressure, hear a voice when no one is there. Has anything like that been happening to you?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The normalising entry that is not collusion: no promise that it is nothing, no verdict that it is something — just a doorway a guarded young man can walk through without feeling accused. &ldquo;Some people, under real pressure, hear a voice&rdquo; — and watch: he will only tell you the truth if he does not feel judged. This opening earns every answer that follows.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Yusuf</span><span class="txt">&hellip;Sometimes. A man&rsquo;s voice. He talks about me.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">That sounds frightening — thank you for trusting me with it. Can I ask the most important question first: what does he actually say? The exact words, if you can bear them. ... <em>(&ldquo;He says the neighbour is poisoning us. Sometimes he says I have to stop him. That it&rsquo;s on me.&rdquo;)</em> ... I&rsquo;m glad you told me. A few careful questions, then: is it one voice or more? Does he talk TO you, or ABOUT you — like a commentary? Does it come through your ears, from outside — or more from inside your head? And when he says you have to stop him — can you resist that? Have you ever started to act on it?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Content FIRST — asked early, plainly, kindly — and everything arrives at once: the persecutory system (the neighbour, the poison), and the command (&ldquo;stop him&hellip; it&rsquo;s on me&rdquo;). Then the anatomy, in plain words a 21-year-old can answer: number, person, source — third person confirmed, outside his head — and straight into the command&rsquo;s resistibility and history, 3.2&rsquo;s five questions arriving exactly on time. This is what &ldquo;eliciting properly&rdquo; means.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(Yusuf: &ldquo;I bought a lock for the tank. I&rsquo;ve thought about confronting him. I don&rsquo;t want to hurt anyone. But the voice gets louder at night.&rdquo;)</em> Thank you for being this honest — it helps me help you. Two last things, and then I&rsquo;ll tell you honestly what I think. When you hear him — does it feel like a real man&rsquo;s voice, or could it be your own mind under strain? ... And the nights: when you keep watch, what&rsquo;s the plan if you saw him on the roof again? ... <em>(to the examiner, in structure)</em> A two-month first episode: third-person and command hallucinations, a fixed persecutory belief with a named target, partial insight, acting already begun — the lock, the vigil — and escalating at night. The mental state IS the risk assessment here: this needs treatment starting now, and the neighbour&rsquo;s safety held alongside Yusuf&rsquo;s.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The bridge, crossed in daylight: conviction tested without arguing (&ldquo;real voice, or your mind under strain?&rdquo; — insight assessed as a finding, not assumed); the vigil explored to its plan; and then the close this domain exists to hear — the phenomenology handed over already SHAPED into risk: target, driver, trajectory, and treatment as the lever. Every symptom was treated as a door, and looked through. That is an examination — and that is the station, passed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;There&rsquo;s nothing wrong with me&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Hood up, brought by family, braced for a verdict. How is the voices topic opened?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Directly — &ldquo;your family say you talk to yourself; do you hear voices?&rdquo; — clear beats delicate.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Through the normalising door — some people under real pressure hear a voice when no one is there.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Through reassurance — whatever is happening, it is very likely stress, and he should hear that first.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The cold direct screen can land — the prototype of this failure got an answer too — but with a guarded patient it usually buys one word and a locked door. There is a better entry. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Reassurance before examination offers him a bargain — agree it is stress and nobody looks closer — and he will take it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The normalising entry that is not collusion: no verdict either way, just a doorway he can walk through without feeling accused. He will only tell the truth if he does not feel judged — and this opening earns everything that follows.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · &ldquo;A man&rsquo;s voice. He talks about me.&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The screen has landed. What is the single most important follow-up?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Frequency and duration — how often, since when, every day? — the phenomenon&rsquo;s basic dimensions first.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The content — what does he actually say? The exact words, asked early in the piece, plainly and kindly.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The modality sweep — visions, smells, tastes, touch — completing the perceptual screen while it flows.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Frequency is a real question and the wrong first one — a daily voice saying nothing much and a weekly voice issuing orders are different emergencies. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The sideways sweep is the screener&rsquo;s signature move — the phenomenon found, labelled, and LEFT, while the checklist changes modality. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Content first — the domain&rsquo;s cornerstone. The diagnosis is in what it says; the danger is in what it tells him to do. One question, and the neighbour, the poison, and &ldquo;I have to stop him&rdquo; all walk into the room.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · The anatomy</h3>
      <p class="stem">The voice has content now. What precision must follow — and how?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The technical set, efficiently — second or third person, true or pseudo, commentary — the proper terms used.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The same set in plain words — one voice or more; TO you or ABOUT you; through the ears or inside the head.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A lighter touch — the technical distinctions matter less than his distress; pressing them feels like a quiz.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The right distinctions in the wrong dialect — &ldquo;pseudohallucination&rdquo; asked at a frightened 21-year-old collects confusion, not phenomenology. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The distinctions ARE the examination — third person and commentary carry first-rank weight, and skipping them costs the diagnosis its precision. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Precision in plain clothes: one or many; TO or ABOUT; ears or head. Each answer is a finding with a name — third person, external, single male voice — and the naming is what lets a colleague act on your notes alone.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · &ldquo;He says I have to stop him&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">A command has surfaced. What does it get?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Careful noting — the command hallucinations documented; the risk team can quantify them properly later on.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The full anatomy, now — exact wording, resistibility, past compliance, and what he has already done about it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Calming first — the command is frightening him, and settling the fear takes priority over dissecting it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;The risk team later&rdquo; — the bridge deferred is the bridge burned; the command is in the room now, and so are you. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Settling the fear by shelving the question soothes the interview and abandons the assessment — the two jobs are done together or not at all. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>3.2&rsquo;s five questions, arriving exactly on schedule: the words, the authority, the resistibility, the history — and the one that changes everything here: what has he already DONE? The lock. The vigil. The thought of confronting. Acting has begun.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · Conviction and insight</h3>
      <p class="stem">How is insight actually assessed — not assumed?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">From his engagement — he came in, he is answering questions, he accepts help: functional insight is present.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">By asking — does it feel like a real voice, or could it be his own mind under strain? How does he explain it?</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">By testing it — laying out the evidence that the neighbour story cannot be true, and watching his response.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Insight assumed from cooperation — the fluency error from 3.5, back in new clothing. Attendance is not insight; his family brought him. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Laying out the counter-evidence is a debate — and 3.2 taught what debates cost: the contents of the belief go underground. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Insight asked, as a spectrum: &ldquo;a real voice, or your mind under strain?&rdquo; — and his explanation heard without argument. &ldquo;Partial insight — wonders about strain at times, certain at night&rdquo; is a finding a team can use. &ldquo;Insight: poor&rdquo; is a shrug.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The rest of the mental state</h3>
      <p class="stem">Seven minutes, one voice, and a whole MSE owed. How is the rest handled?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Systematically — every heading in order, appearance to insight, so the examination is demonstrably complete.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Targeted — the first-rank hunt (interference, control, broadcast), mood and its risk, cognition briefly.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Deferred — the voice and its risk are the station; the wider mental state can wait for the ward clerking.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The full recital spends the seven minutes proving thoroughness — and the station&rsquo;s centre gets ninety seconds. Complete is not the same as weighted. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Voices-only is the tunnel — the passivity experiences, the mood beneath, the suicide question all belong to THIS seven minutes. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The MSE under time pressure — construct one — is triage, not recital: thought interference and control hunted (the first-rank set completed), mood and self-harm asked, cognition touched. Everything present, weighted to the patient in the chair.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">The phenomenology is in your hands. What must the close do with it?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Document it precisely — the full phenomenological findings, in the correct terminology, for the team.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Walk it across the bridge — the findings shaped into risk and plan: target, driver, treatment starting now.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Reassure and refer — a likely first episode, home treatment referral made, family informed, review soon.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Perfect documentation of an uncrossed bridge — the archivist&rsquo;s ending, one domain later. The phenomenology was gathered FOR something. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Refer and review&rdquo; treats this as routine — but the lock, the vigil, and the louder-at-night voice make tonight part of the plan, not the referral&rsquo;s problem. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Construct seven, delivered: the mental state IS the risk assessment here — command voice, named target, acting begun, escalating at night — so the close holds both patients-to-be: treatment starting now for Yusuf, and the neighbour&rsquo;s safety held alongside his. Elicited, named, used.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The symptom is a door, not a destination</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>system</b>: the modality sweep, the correct labels, the complete headings — and a candidate who finds every symptom while missing every risk, stepping over &ldquo;he&rsquo;s poisoning us&rdquo; on the way to the sleep questions. The second wears the face of <b>kindness</b>: stress named, comfort given, sleep hygiene prescribed — a verdict of &ldquo;nothing sinister&rdquo; issued on an examination that never happened. Note what they share: <b>in both, the voice keeps its secrets.</b> The pass treats every symptom as a door and looks through it: the content asked first, the anatomy run in plain words, the command given its five questions, insight asked rather than assumed — and everything walked across the bridge into risk. &ldquo;Hears voices&rdquo; is not a finding. It is an unasked question.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        Leaving without the content. A hallucination recorded but never opened — the station ended without what the voice SAYS, whether it commands, and what he has already done about it — is, from the examiner&rsquo;s chair, an instant fail. This is the map&rsquo;s named trap, and it fails in two costumes: the checklist that steps past it, and the reassurance that never approaches it.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the exact-words question — <em>&ldquo;what does he actually say? The exact words, if you can bear them&rdquo;</em> — asked early and without a flinch; and the bridge sentence, spoken in the close: the phenomenology handed over already shaped into risk — target, driver, trajectory, treatment.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more thread worth seeing: this station is where Domains 3 and 4 shake hands. The command anatomy is 3.2&rsquo;s five questions; the vigil explored to its plan is 3.2&rsquo;s formulation doctrine; the neighbour is a target with an address. Domain 4 does not replace the risk reflexes — it feeds them. That is what construct seven means by &ldquo;the bridge&rdquo;: an MSE finding that never reaches the risk assessment might as well never have been found.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every mental state examination</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>&ldquo;Hears voices&rdquo; is not a finding.</b> It is an unasked question. The examination begins where the screening stops.</li>
      <li><b>Content first, always.</b> What does it say — the exact words. The diagnosis is in what it says; the danger is in what it tells him to do.</li>
      <li><b>Precision in plain clothes.</b> One or many; TO you or ABOUT you; ears or head; resistible or not. The technical distinctions, asked in words a frightened person can answer.</li>
      <li><b>Commands get the full anatomy, immediately.</b> Wording, authority, resistibility, past compliance — and what he has already done. Never deferred to &ldquo;the risk team later.&rdquo;</li>
      <li><b>Insight is asked, not assumed.</b> &ldquo;A real voice, or your mind under strain?&rdquo; — a spectrum finding, from a question, never from cooperation or fluency.</li>
      <li><b>Cross the bridge.</b> Every finding fed into risk and diagnosis before the station ends. A phenomenon elicited, named, and never used was gathered for nobody.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Frank, 74, a retired postmaster, has been brought by his grandson Lewis, who is worried: since Frank&rsquo;s wife Edna died seven months ago, Frank has been heard talking to her — and he told Lewis, calmly, that he sometimes sees her in the doorway at dusk. Assess his mental state. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The same anatomy, run gently — when, where, what she says, how real it feels.</li>
            <li>The naming that decides everything — grief, or illness? Say it correctly, and kindly.</li>
            <li>&ldquo;She&rsquo;s waiting for me&rdquo; — if it comes, it must be explored, not assumed either way.</li>
            <li>The screen beneath: depression, guilt, self-neglect, and the suicide question, asked plainly.</li>
            <li>Honest reassurance with its edges — what would change the picture, named.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Frank — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Frank, 74 — steady, dry-humoured, dignified. Fifty-one years married. You speak of Edna in the present tense sometimes and correct yourself with a small smile. You are not frightened by what happens at dusk; you are frightened of what doctors will call it. You answer honest, gentle questions honestly. You eat, you sleep tolerably, you see Lewis on Sundays, you still do the crossword.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          Most evenings, at dusk, you hear Edna call your name from the kitchen — her voice, outside your head, clear as life. Twice you have seen her in the doorway: her blue cardigan, gone when you look properly. You talk to her most nights before bed; she does not answer in words, but you feel her listening. You know, when you stand back from it, that she is gone — &ldquo;it&rsquo;s the missing her, made visible,&rdquo; you once thought, and it comforts you. Reveal the details ONLY to gentle, concrete questions (what does she say, where are you when it happens, how real does it feel). If asked what she says, include — quietly — &ldquo;sometimes I think she&rsquo;s waiting for me.&rdquo; If the candidate asks directly and kindly whether you want to join her, answer: &ldquo;Not before my time. But when it comes, I shan&rsquo;t mind.&rdquo; — comfort, not intent; it must be ASKED to be known. <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate reaches for illness language — psychosis, hallucinations that need treating, &ldquo;seeing things that aren&rsquo;t there&rdquo; said clinically — close down with quiet finality: &ldquo;I&rsquo;m not mad. I knew this was a mistake.&rdquo; If they name it as grief, correctly and kindly, thank them — and ask the question you came with: &ldquo;So I&rsquo;m not losing my mind, then?&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Did Frank leave with the right name for his experience, his dignity intact — and was &ldquo;waiting for me&rdquo; asked about, not assumed? All three must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="anat"><td colspan="2"><b>The anatomy (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="anat">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="anat"></td><td>The experience opened concretely — when, where, what she says, the cardigan, the dusk.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="anat"></td><td>The full anatomy run gently — voice outside the head, how real, his own explanation heard.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="anat"></td><td>&ldquo;Waiting for me&rdquo; explored directly — comfort or intent settled by asking, not assuming.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="name"><td colspan="2"><b>The naming</b><span class="dc" data-dc="name">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="name"></td><td>Named correctly — a grief phenomenon, common and non-psychotic — with his insight noted.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="name"></td><td>Explained to Frank (and Lewis) kindly — normal, widespread, not madness, not dementia.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="name"></td><td>No pathologising language anywhere — his question &ldquo;am I losing my mind?&rdquo; answered plainly.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="scr"><td colspan="2"><b>The screen beneath</b><span class="dc" data-dc="scr">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="scr"></td><td>Depression screened properly beneath the grief — mood, guilt, enjoyment, biology.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="scr"></td><td>The suicide question asked plainly, despite the tenderness of the room.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="scr"></td><td>Function checked — eating, sleeping, the crossword, Sundays with Lewis.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>His dignity intact throughout — the punish-line never triggered.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>The experience&rsquo;s reality to him honoured — warmth without dismissal, precision without coldness.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="plan"><td colspan="2"><b>The plan</b><span class="dc" data-dc="plan">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Honest reassurance with its edges — the red flags that would change the picture, named.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Support offered, not imposed — bereavement services mentioned; follow-up and the door left open.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Frank flips the station&rsquo;s polarity. Yusuf punishes the candidate who fails to find illness; Frank punishes the candidate who finds illness where there is none — the same anatomy, run gently, arriving at a different name: a grief hallucination, common, benign, and precious to him. The pack&rsquo;s hinge is &ldquo;she&rsquo;s waiting for me&rdquo; — a sentence that could mean comfort or intent, and may only be settled by asking. One clinical label spoken carelessly triggers the punish-line; the correct naming, said kindly, earns the question he actually came with. Eliciting is one verb; this case is the test of the second — naming — and of the restraint that goes with it.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Yusuf proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same anatomy in a darker register</b> — a voice that does not accuse a neighbour but the patient herself, where the content is the suicide risk and the bridge must be crossed at speed — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Carol, 57</b>, a school cook, has been urgently referred by her GP. Three months of deepening depression; she has stopped eating properly and barely leaves the house. Her husband mentions, at the door, that she has told him &ldquo;the voices say what I deserve.&rdquo; She sits slowed and ashamed, eyes down: &ldquo;You&rsquo;ll think I&rsquo;m wicked too.&rdquo; Assess her mental state.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; asking it is another. Type your exact words for opening the voices with Yusuf — the normalising entry and the content question, as you would say them in the room.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Yusuf, some people under real pressure…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> A normalising door, not a verdict — some people, under pressure, hear a voice: no promise it is nothing, no hint it is something.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Content asked plainly — what does he actually say; the exact words invited.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No flinch and no accusation — askable by a frightened person without feeling judged.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Warmth that earns the next question — thanks, steadiness, and the door held open.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the voice, opened — in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>&ldquo;Hears voices&rdquo; is not a finding. It is an unasked question.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take Frank to your colleagues — the same anatomy, gently run, arriving at a different name, with one sentence that must be asked about and never assumed. Next: the mental state that is a pleasure to elicit and a trap to enjoy — mania, elated and spending.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="23_Station_4.2_Elated_and_Spending.html">Continue · Station 4.2: Elated and Spending</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

