<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 4.2 · Domain 4</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult</span><span class="tag">Mania · MSE</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Elated and Spending</h1>
    <p class="sub">Dominic, 32, a car salesman, is brought in by his sister after five days of barely sleeping. He has spent nine thousand pounds this week — a watch, a drone, flights to Dubai booked at three in the morning — and he is starting three businesses. He stopped his lithium six weeks ago: &ldquo;it dulled my edge.&rdquo; He is, without question, the most entertaining patient you will meet in this exam — quick, charming, generous, funny. That is precisely the trap. <b>Mania is the only illness that interviews you back</b> — and while you enjoy the show, it is quietly running up bills its owner will pay for years. The phenomenology is the show. The risks are the station.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it has a ledger attached: after seven minutes with this doctor, do we know what mania has already spent and committed — the money, the road, the strangers, the five empty nights — and how close the elation sits to the flip? A mental state that reads &ldquo;elated, grandiose, pressured&rdquo; and nothing else has admired the illness and examined nothing.</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
