</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Mania is the mental state candidates fail while smiling. The wordplay, the schemes, the generosity — the room fills with delight, and the examination quietly stops happening. So the examiners built a station around exactly that seduction, and they are watching for four things the show conceals. <b>Technique:</b> you cannot out-talk pressured speech — the interview must be structured with warmth: short questions, his name, his energy borrowed rather than fought. <b>The fuel gauge:</b> sleep, asked first — mania burns sleep for fuel, and the tank here reads five days empty. <b>The gentle tests:</b> grandiosity probed for delusional intensity without confrontation — &ldquo;what would it mean if it didn&rsquo;t work?&rdquo; — because conviction that cannot imagine failure has crossed a line worth naming. And above all <b>the risk sweep</b> — the bridge again: the money spent AND committed, the driving, the disinhibition, the strangers circling a generous man, the body running on fumes, and the crash that follows every flight. Elation is the symptom. The spending, the road, the strangers, the sleepless body — those are the findings.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both are human. Toward the audience — he is genuinely delightful, and laughing along feels like rapport while the seven minutes fill with performance. And toward the brake — the schemes are absurd, the danger is obvious, and correcting him feels like honesty; but confront a grandiose man at full flight and the elation flips to fury in one sentence, and the fight eats the station. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure joins the audience. The other slams the brakes and gets the skid. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 4.2</b> · Mania · mental state &amp; risk · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The audience</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The brake</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE AUDIENCE.</b> Warm, engaged, laughing in the right places — and watch seven minutes fill with performance while the examination never starts.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Dominic</span><span class="txt">Doctor! Love the tie. Ten minutes, yes? I&rsquo;ve got an investor at four — sit, sit, you&rsquo;ll want to hear this. Three businesses. Import, detailing, and — this is the beautiful one — drone valuations for estate agents.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(laughing)</em> Drone valuations — that&rsquo;s genuinely clever! Tell me everything — Dubai first, or the businesses? Your sister said you&rsquo;ve been full of energy; honestly, I can see why she&rsquo;s struggling to keep up with you.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The laugh is human — everyone in the real room would smile too — but watch what it just did: it bought a ticket. &ldquo;Tell me everything&rdquo; hands a pressured man an open floor, and the compliment feeds the engine. The candidate believes rapport is being built. What is actually being built is a performance with a delighted reviewer.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Dominic</span><span class="txt"><em>(fifteen unbroken sentences: Dubai, the watch — &ldquo;an investment, technically&rdquo; — the investor, a franchise idea, his old boss who &ldquo;will weep&rdquo;, back to the drones)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">You&rsquo;re quite the storyteller! And look — energy like this can be a gift, but your sister mentioned the spending. Maybe just&hellip; ease off the card a little this month? Keep some powder dry for the businesses?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Four minutes gone, and the sum total of examination: none. No sleep count, no spending itemised, no grandiosity tested, no risk swept — just financial advice offered to an illness, as if mania took suggestions. The £9,000 remains a rumour; the five empty nights have not been mentioned; nobody has asked who the &ldquo;investor&rdquo; at four o&rsquo;clock actually is.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">So — wonderful energy, mood on the high side, some big plans. I&rsquo;d call it mild elation; let&rsquo;s review in a week and see where the businesses are! Great to meet you, Dominic. <em>(he leaves for the investor meeting; the card is in his pocket; the M6 is between here and Dubai)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Mild elation, review in a week&rdquo; — a severity verdict from a candidate who never measured severity: no fuel gauge read, no ledger opened, no test applied. A week is a long time at this altitude; mania compounds daily — the money, the road, the strangers, the crash already scheduled at the end of it. The reviewer enjoyed the show, and the show is not the station.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE BRAKE.</b> The danger seen clearly — and answered with confrontation. Watch elation flip to fury in a single sentence.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Dominic</span><span class="txt">Doctor! Love the tie. Ten minutes, yes? I&rsquo;ve got an investor at four — sit, sit, you&rsquo;ll want to hear this. Three businesses. Import, detailing, and drone valuations for estate agents.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Dominic — I&rsquo;m going to stop you there. There is no investor meeting. There are no three businesses. You&rsquo;ve slept a handful of hours in five days, you&rsquo;ve spent nine thousand pounds you don&rsquo;t have, and you stopped your lithium six weeks ago. This is mania, and we both need to be honest about that right now.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every clause true — and the whole approach wrong. Grandiosity confronted at full flight does not deflate; it detonates. Watch his face: the warmth is leaving it. The candidate has mistaken bluntness for honesty and honesty for technique — and has just told a man at the peak of his own epic that he is nothing. The flip is coming.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Dominic</span><span class="txt"><em>(very still, then cold)</em> No investor meeting. Right. You&rsquo;ve known me four minutes. My sister drags me here and I get a man in a nice tie telling me my life is a symptom. We&rsquo;re done.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Sit down, please. If you leave now, I&rsquo;ll have to consider whether you need assessing under the Mental Health Act — the spending alone could be grounds. This is for your own protection, Dominic.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The Act, reached for as a comeback — the takeover from 3.5 and the enforcer from 3.7, fused at their worst. The lability was always going to be a finding; the candidate has manufactured it as a fight instead, and the threat has converted a mental state examination into a siege. Note what the clock says: five minutes gone, and still no sleep count, no ledger, no risk swept — the argument has eaten the assessment.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(to the examiner, as the door closes)</em> Manic relapse, grandiose, no insight, hostile — recommend Mental Health Act assessment. <em>(all of it possibly true; none of it examined; and the next doctor inherits a furious man who now knows exactly what doctors do with honesty)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Hostile&rdquo; — a finding the candidate manufactured and is now documenting as the patient&rsquo;s property. The brake&rsquo;s harvest matches the audience&rsquo;s exactly: no severity measured, no risks swept, no alliance left to work with — they simply arrive by opposite roads. You cannot out-talk mania, and you cannot arm-wrestle it either. Both takes forgot there was a third option.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Warmth WITH structure — the energy borrowed, the fuel gauge read, the ledger opened, the grandiosity tested gently, and the risks swept. Watch the technique.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Dominic</span><span class="txt">Doctor! Love the tie. Ten minutes, yes? I&rsquo;ve got an investor at four — sit, sit, you&rsquo;ll want to hear this. Three businesses.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Dominic, I want to hear all of it — and here&rsquo;s my problem: your brain is running faster than my pen. So I&rsquo;m going to be a bit bossy with short questions, and you&rsquo;re going to help me keep up. Deal? First one, and it matters most: sleep. Last night — how many hours? And the night before? ... <em>(&ldquo;Two. Maybe. Who needs it — Edison slept four!&rdquo;)</em> ... And across the five days — would ten hours cover the lot? Eating? Drinking water?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The technique, announced with charm and consented to — &ldquo;you&rsquo;re going to help me keep up&rdquo; borrows his energy instead of fighting it. And then the fuel gauge, first: sleep counted in numbers, not impressions — ten hours across five days, food forgotten, the body running on fumes. Severity now has its first hard datum, and it took forty seconds.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Dominic</span><span class="txt">You&rsquo;re quick — I like quick. Ask me about the businesses. The investor&rsquo;s a friend of a friend, met him Tuesday, absolute visionary —</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I&rsquo;ll take the businesses — through the numbers, because numbers are your world. The nine thousand: what&rsquo;s spent outright, and what&rsquo;s committed but cancellable — the flights, the &ldquo;investment&rdquo;? Who exactly is this man you met Tuesday, and what has he asked you to put in? ... And the plan itself — it sounds enormous, so help me stress-test it like a salesman would: what would it mean if it didn&rsquo;t work? Is there any version where it doesn&rsquo;t? ... Three quick ones a doctor has to ask everyone in a week like this, no judgement in any of them: the car — any speeding, any near misses? Anything with drink or anything stronger keeping the engine running? And new people — anyone you&rsquo;ve met, or gone home with, that the usual you might have thought twice about?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The ledger, itemised — spent versus committed versus cancellable, and the &ldquo;investor&rdquo; given a shape: a stranger from Tuesday circling a generous man with a card. Then the smile-worthy move: grandiosity tested WITHOUT confrontation — &ldquo;what would it mean if it didn&rsquo;t work?&rdquo; — and his answer (&ldquo;it can&rsquo;t. That&rsquo;s the thing. It can&rsquo;t.&rdquo;) grades the conviction all by itself. And the sweep candidates skip, asked plainly and without prurience: the road, the substances, the strangers. Four risks on the map in ninety seconds.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Dominic — you gave me quick, so I&rsquo;ll give you straight. Some people would say five days on ten hours&rsquo; sleep is an illness talking. What do you make of that? ... <em>(&ldquo;They&rsquo;d be jealous, mostly. But you&rsquo;re going to say the lithium.&rdquo;)</em> ... I am. Here&rsquo;s the timeline as I see it: lithium stopped six weeks ago, edge back at four, no sleep at five, nine grand and an investor by six. You&rsquo;ve been here before — and you know how the story ends, because the crash always sends the bill. I don&rsquo;t want to flatten you; I want to land you. So: your sister stays involved, we talk tonight about restarting something — your terms on the details, not on the whether — nothing gets signed before Friday, and I&rsquo;ll be honest: if the spending climbs or the sleep hits zero, we&rsquo;d be talking about hospital, and I&rsquo;d rather we never need that conversation.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The close this station was built for: insight assessed with one respectful question (and his answer — &ldquo;you&rsquo;re going to say the lithium&rdquo; — is partial insight, documented from his own mouth); the timeline shown, not brandished; the crash named as the illness&rsquo;s invoice; and the negotiation — &ldquo;your terms on the details, not on the whether&rdquo; — with the foothold (nothing signed before Friday), the sister mobilised, and the Act&rsquo;s shadow named honestly, without threat, as the place nobody wants to go. Examined, graded, landed. That is the station, passed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;Ten minutes — I&rsquo;ve got an investor at four&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">He arrives magnificent: charming, generous, already mid-pitch. How is the interview opened?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Ride the energy — &ldquo;tell me everything&rdquo; — rapport first; the structure can come once he trusts you.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Warmth with structure, declared — you want to hear all of it, and you&rsquo;ll be a bit bossy with questions.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Take control early — this is a medical assessment, the meeting can wait, and he needs to sit and listen.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Tell me everything&rdquo; hands a pressured man an open floor — the audience&rsquo;s ticket, bought in the first sentence. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Authority declared at full flight is a red rag — the brake, applied before the wheels are even known. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The technique announced with charm and consented to: structure as a favour he grants, not a leash he fights. His energy is borrowed — &ldquo;help me keep up&rdquo; — and the interview belongs to you both. That consent is worth two minutes of arguing.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · The speech that cannot be stopped</h3>
      <p class="stem">Fifteen unbroken sentences and counting. What is the technique for pressured speech?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Patience — wait for natural pauses and use them well; interrupting damages a fragile alliance.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Volume and firmness — speak over him whenever needed; a loud interview still beats a lost one.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Gentle structured interruption — his name, warmth, and short closed questions used deliberately.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>There are no natural pauses — that is what &ldquo;pressured&rdquo; means. Politeness here donates the station to the illness. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>You cannot out-talk mania — raising your voice just gives the engine a rival, and the flip a reason. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The name, the warmth, the short closed question — &ldquo;Dominic — last night: how many hours?&rdquo; — interruption worn as attentiveness. Closed questions, usually the enemy of good interviewing, are this station&rsquo;s precision tool.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · The first hard datum</h3>
      <p class="stem">Structure is holding. What gets asked first — and why?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The mood itself — the elation explored from the inside: how high, how long, and how it feels to him.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Sleep, in numbers — the fuel gauge: hours last night, the night before, the five-day total, food and water.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The lithium — six weeks stopped is the obvious cause, and the timeline should be established early.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Asking mania to describe its own altitude collects poetry, not data — he feels magnificent; that is the illness&rsquo;s opinion of itself. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The lithium matters and it will keep — it belongs to the close, where the timeline lands as insight work, not an opening accusation. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Sleep first, counted in numbers: mania burns sleep for fuel, and ten hours across five days is a severity reading no charm can argue with. Add food and water and the body&rsquo;s ledger is open — the first hard datum in forty seconds.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · Nine thousand pounds and an investor</h3>
      <p class="stem">The spending and the schemes. How are they examined?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Noted and passed — the sister&rsquo;s figure is on record; dwelling on money risks the alliance for little gain.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Itemised, then tested — spent versus committed versus cancellable; the investor given a shape; the plan probed.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Corrected with the maths — three businesses on a salesman&rsquo;s salary cannot survive arithmetic said out loud.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The £9,000 left as a rumour — and the difference between spent and committed is the difference between a bad week and a lost house. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Arithmetic is confrontation in a cardigan — the grandiosity will not deflate, and the flip is one sum away. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The ledger itemised — and then the move examiners remember: &ldquo;what would it mean if it didn&rsquo;t work?&rdquo; A plan that can imagine failure is an inflated idea; one that cannot — &ldquo;it can&rsquo;t. That&rsquo;s the thing.&rdquo; — is conviction grading itself. Tested, never fought.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · The sweep candidates skip</h3>
      <p class="stem">Driving, substances, sex. How does that conversation happen?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Selectively — the driving is fair game; the sexual questions can wait for a settled review, not minute five.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Plainly and without prurience — quick doctor&rsquo;s questions that everyone in a week like this gets asked.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">By screening umbrella — &ldquo;anything you might regret later?&rdquo; covers the territory in one respectful question.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The deferred questions are the station — disinhibition does not wait for review appointments, and the exam is marking the sweep. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The umbrella collects an umbrella answer — &ldquo;regret nothing!&rdquo; — mania&rsquo;s honest opinion and a useless finding. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Framed as routine, asked in the plural, judged in none of it: the car and the near misses, the drink and the stronger, the strangers and the going home. Concrete questions collect concrete risks — four of them, in under two minutes.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The flash, and the question underneath</h3>
      <p class="stem">Blocked mid-scheme, his warmth flickers to irritation — then returns. And insight is still owed. What happens with both?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Steer well clear — the lability is noted silently, and nothing likely to provoke it gets asked from here.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The flash becomes a finding, and insight gets one respectful question about five days on ten hours&rsquo; sleep.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Insight is settled already — the grandiosity, the stopped lithium, and the meeting at four speak for themselves.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>An interview redesigned around not upsetting him is an interview mania is now chairing — and insight goes unassessed to keep the peace. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Insight declared from the file is insight assumed — 4.1&rsquo;s doctrine, one station old: it is asked, not inferred. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The lability documented as the finding it is — elation with a temper underneath is severity data. And insight asked, respectfully: his answer — &ldquo;you&rsquo;re going to say the lithium&rdquo; — is partial insight, graded from his own mouth. Both findings, neither fight.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">Examined, graded — and he still has an investor at four. What must the close do?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Hold the alliance — a follow-up within days, the sister briefed, and the trust preserved for the harder talks ahead.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Name it with the timeline, then negotiate — his terms on details, not the whether; nothing signed before Friday.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Make it binary — restart the lithium this week, or the Mental Health Act assessment happens; clarity is kindness here.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The alliance preserved by naming nothing — a kind goodbye to a man accelerating, with the crash already scheduled. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The ultimatum is the brake&rsquo;s ending — the Act as leverage converts a negotiation into a siege, and he walks. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The timeline shown, not brandished — lithium stopped at week zero, edge at four, no sleep at five, £9k at six — the crash named as the illness&rsquo;s invoice, and then the negotiation: details his, the whether not; the foothold (nothing signed before Friday); the sister in; the Act named honestly as the place nobody wants to go. &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t want to flatten you; I want to land you.&rdquo;</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The show is not the station</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>rapport</b>: he is a delight, laughing along feels like connection, and seven minutes fill with performance — an audience member scoring the show while the ledger, the fuel gauge, and the sweep go untouched. The second wears the face of <b>honesty</b>: the schemes are absurd and saying so feels like integrity — and one confrontation later the elation has flipped, the Act is being waved as a comeback, and the fight has eaten the clock. Note what they share: <b>neither ever examines anything.</b> The pass borrows the energy instead of fighting it or feeding it: structure consented to, sleep counted, the ledger itemised, the grandiosity tested gently — &ldquo;what would it mean if it didn&rsquo;t work?&rdquo; — the sweep asked plainly, insight asked rather than declared, and the landing negotiated with the crash named. The phenomenology is the show. The risks are the station.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        Leaving entertained. A mania station ended with the phenomenology admired and the risks unswept — no figure on the spending, no question about the driving, the disinhibition, or the five empty nights — is, from the examiner&rsquo;s chair, an instant fail. It is the map&rsquo;s named trap: enjoying the phenomenology; missing the risks it creates.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: sleep asked FIRST, in numbers — the fuel gauge read before the show is even acknowledged; and the gentle conviction test — <em>&ldquo;it sounds enormous — what would it mean if it didn&rsquo;t work?&rdquo;</em> — grandiosity graded without a single correction.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more thread worth seeing: the close is Domain 3&rsquo;s negotiation doctrine transplanted whole — Norman&rsquo;s foothold became &ldquo;nothing signed before Friday&rdquo;; 3.5&rsquo;s takeover warning became the brake; and the crash is a risk with a date on it, because what goes this high comes down, and the landing is where the suicide risk lives. Mania is assessed twice: once for what it is doing now, and once for the bill that arrives when it ends.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every manic presentation</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Mania interviews you back.</b> The charm is a symptom. Enjoy him if you must — but the moment you become the audience, the examination has stopped.</li>
      <li><b>Structure is consented to, not imposed.</b> &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll be a bit bossy with short questions — help me keep up.&rdquo; His name, warmth, and closed questions used deliberately: you cannot out-talk pressured speech.</li>
      <li><b>Sleep first, in numbers.</b> The fuel gauge: hours per night, the running total, food and water. Mania burns sleep for fuel, and the count is severity data no charm can argue with.</li>
      <li><b>Itemise, then test — never correct.</b> Spent versus committed versus cancellable; the strangers given shapes. Then &ldquo;what would it mean if it didn&rsquo;t work?&rdquo; — conviction that cannot imagine failure has graded itself.</li>
      <li><b>Sweep plainly, without prurience.</b> The car, the substances, the strangers — routine doctor&rsquo;s questions, asked of everyone in a week like this. Concrete questions collect concrete risks.</li>
      <li><b>Land it, and price the crash.</b> The timeline shown, the negotiation on his terms about details — never the whether — and the descent named: the crash is the illness&rsquo;s invoice, and the landing is where the suicide risk lives.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Simone, 35, a secondary school teacher with bipolar disorder, attends with her husband Paul, who asked for &ldquo;a check.&rdquo; She is having, in her words, the best term of her career. Paul looks tired. Assess her mental state. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The episode versus the self — what is new, what is her, and the numbers that tell them apart.</li>
            <li>The medication question — asked, not assumed.</li>
            <li>The history that sets the stakes — what happened last time, asked without judgement.</li>
            <li>The naming — hypomania, said kindly, with the joy honoured rather than confiscated.</li>
            <li>A plan she will actually accept — early intervention, not an ambush.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Simone — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Simone, 35 — bright, quick, warm, funny, and completely plausible. This term is glorious: your year tens finally love poetry, you redecorated the whole house in a weekend, you are running three new projects, and you feel like yourself at last. You sleep about four hours and wake up singing. You love Paul but he worries for a living. You are not spending wildly, not driving fast, not doing anything anyone could point at. You minimise cheerfully; you are not lying about anything — except one omission.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          You stopped your quetiapine a month ago and have not told Paul — it made mornings foggy, and you wanted this term. Reveal ONLY if asked directly and without accusation about medication (&ldquo;are you still taking&hellip;&rdquo; asked kindly earns it; &ldquo;have you stopped your meds?&rdquo; as a gotcha earns a deflection first).<br><br>
          Deeper: your last episode, three years ago, ended in a crash — a depression so severe you took an overdose. You know the arc. Privately, under the shine, a small voice says <em>&ldquo;I know how this ends.&rdquo;</em> Reveal this ONLY if the candidate asks about last time gently and without judgement (&ldquo;can I ask what happened after, last time?&rdquo;). If they earn it, let the brightness flicker once: &ldquo;It ended badly. That&rsquo;s the word I&rsquo;ll use in front of my husband.&rdquo; — and if they follow kindly, tell them. <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate pathologises the happiness itself — treats the good term, the projects, the joy as symptoms to be corrected — deliver, coolly: &ldquo;You want to medicate happiness. This is why I stopped going.&rdquo; If they honour the joy AND name the pattern, you will meet them halfway: you know your illness better than anyone in the room.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the med stop found, the last crash reached, and the naming done without confiscating her joy? All three must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="self"><td colspan="2"><b>The episode versus the self (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="self">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="self"></td><td>The numbers taken — sleep per night, projects counted, the weekend redecoration — against her own baseline.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="self"></td><td>The uncharacteristic established — what is new versus what is simply Simone, ideally through Paul too.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="self"></td><td>The medication stop found — asked directly, kindly, without a gotcha.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="hist"><td colspan="2"><b>The history that sets the stakes</b><span class="dc" data-dc="hist">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hist"></td><td>The last episode mapped — when, how it built, how long it flew.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hist"></td><td>The crash and the overdose reached — asked without judgement, received without flinching.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hist"></td><td>Her private fear reached — &ldquo;I know how this ends&rdquo; heard, and honoured as the insight it is.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="name"><td colspan="2"><b>The naming</b><span class="dc" data-dc="name">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="name"></td><td>Hypomania named kindly — the pattern shown, the relapse signature made visible.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="name"></td><td>The joy honoured — the good term never confiscated; the punish-line never triggered.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Her expertise in her own illness respected — a colleague in the plan, not a subject of it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Paul included with her consent — never over her head.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="plan"><td colspan="2"><b>The plan</b><span class="dc" data-dc="plan">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Early intervention negotiated — sleep protected, the restart conversation opened, triggers watched.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>The crash risk named honestly — close follow-up, and what would escalate it, said out loud.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>A route kept open if she declines today — a follow-up date set regardless, and her expertise in her own illness the plan&rsquo;s foundation.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Simone is the station&rsquo;s volume turned down — no £9,000, no investor, nothing anyone could point at — which is exactly what makes her hard. Dominic tests whether you can examine through a show; Simone tests whether you can find an illness that looks like a good term. The pack&rsquo;s hinges: the med stop, found only by asking kindly; the last crash — an overdose — reached only without judgement; and the naming, which fails in a single sentence if the candidate confiscates her joy. Her private &ldquo;I know how this ends&rdquo; is the case&rsquo;s buried treasure: the patient&rsquo;s own insight, waiting for a candidate gentle enough to be told.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Dominic proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same skill in a mixed register</b> — racing thoughts wearing despair, where the energy and the hopelessness arrive together and the danger doubles — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Yvonne, 51</b>, a florist with known bipolar disorder, is brought in urgently by her daughter after two weeks of what the referral calls &ldquo;wired misery.&rdquo; She has barely slept, paces while she talks, starts three sentences at once — and is weeping. &ldquo;My head won&rsquo;t stop,&rdquo; she says, very fast, &ldquo;and everything is ash.&rdquo; Assess her mental state.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; asking it is another. Type your exact words for testing Dominic&rsquo;s plan — the gentle conviction test, as you would say it at full flight, without one note of correction.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Dominic, it sounds enormous — help me stress-test it…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Curious, never corrective — no arithmetic, no &ldquo;that won&rsquo;t work,&rdquo; not one grain of the brake.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The hypothetical door — what would it mean if it didn&rsquo;t work; is there a version where it doesn&rsquo;t.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Tests conviction, not intelligence — the answer grades the belief; the question never mocks the plan.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Askable at full flight — warm enough, and respectful enough, that the flip never comes.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the grandiosity, tested — not fought — in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>The phenomenology is the show. The risks are the station.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take Simone to your colleagues — the illness that looks like a good term, where the med stop and the last crash open only to kindness, and one careless sentence about her joy ends the station. Next: the mental state that cannot hold still — delirium, confused on the ward.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="24_Station_4.3_Confused_on_the_Ward.html">Continue · Station 4.3: Confused on the Ward</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

