  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '24_Station_4.3_Confused_on_the_Ward'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 4.3 · Domain 4</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">Liaison / Old Age</span><span class="tag">Delirium</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Confused on the Ward</h1>
    <p class="sub">Iris, 81, is three days past a fractured hip repair. The orthopaedic ward has asked you to see her: drowsy and distant by day, by night she pulls at her catheter, tells the nurses there are children under the beds, and accuses the staff of stealing her rings. The referral says &ldquo;acute confusion — please assess. ?dementia ?psychosis.&rdquo; Both question marks are wrong, and the station is built around why. <b>Delirium is a medical emergency in psychiatric clothing</b> — the confusion is the alarm, not the diagnosis, and you are not at her bedside to score her mind. You are there to find what is poisoning it.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; On a medical ward it sharpens: if this doctor sees my confused post-operative patient, do I get back a syndrome named, a cause list opened, and a drug chart challenged — or a cognitive score, a &ldquo;?dementia,&rdquo; and an antipsychotic, while the retention, the opioid, or the infection burns on underneath?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Delirium is where a psychiatrist&rsquo;s two identities meet: the phenomenologist, and the physician. The exam knows most candidates bring only the first — so it built a station where the elegant mental state examination is the decoy, and four other things carry the marks. <b>The baseline:</b> no cognitive finding means anything without knowing what this mind was like ten days ago — the collateral comes before the bedside. <b>The hinge:</b> attention. Dementia forgets; delirium cannot attend — months of the year backwards is worth more here than any full battery, because inattention plus acute onset plus fluctuation is the syndrome&rsquo;s fingerprint. <b>The kindness:</b> the children under the beds are opened, not corrected — content first, one station old. And above all <b>the pivot</b> — the moment the assessment turns from mind to body: the drug chart, the bladder, the bowels, the chest, the sodium, the alcohol history — because a delirious brain is a symptom of a struggling body, and the cause list IS the treatment.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both wear professional clothing. Toward the toolkit — you were asked to assess, so you test: orientation, registration, recall, a score, a label. It feels rigorous, and it examines the smoke while the fire burns. And toward the ward&rsquo;s relief — she is exhausting the night staff, sedation would settle everyone, and &ldquo;confusion is common at 81&rdquo; is true. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure examines the mind and forgets the body. The other treats the ward&rsquo;s night and calls it treating the patient. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 4.3</b> · Delirium · assessment &amp; plan · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The tester</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The sedative</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE TESTER.</b> Rigorous, systematic, fluent — a beautiful cognitive examination of a poisoned brain. Watch the toolkit examine the smoke while the fire burns.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Iris</span><span class="txt"><em>(plucking at the sheet, eyes drifting)</em> Are you the manager? Someone has been in my room. My rings — I&rsquo;ve told the girl twice about my rings.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I&rsquo;m a doctor, Iris — I&rsquo;d like to check your memory and concentration. Can you tell me what year it is? And what sort of place this is? ... Now I&rsquo;d like you to remember three words for me: apple, penny, table. ... Can you take seven away from a hundred? And seven again?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Straight to the battery — no notes read, no nurse asked, no daughter phoned, no drug chart opened. Every question is legitimate, and every answer is about to be meaningless: a score with no baseline is a number without a meaning. What was this mind like ten days ago? Nobody in this assessment knows, and the assessment has already started measuring.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Iris</span><span class="txt"><em>(derailing, then urgent)</em> The children come at night, you know. Under the beds. The girl says there are no children but I&rsquo;ve SEEN them.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I see — visual hallucinations, noted. Iris, those three words I gave you — can you tell me what they were? ... Alright. <em>(to the examiner)</em> Disorientated in time and place, inattentive, scoring roughly twelve of thirty, visual hallucinations with persecutory ideas about staff. Likely dementia with psychotic features — I&rsquo;d suggest starting risperidone, and a memory clinic referral on discharge.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Visual hallucinations, noted&rdquo; — the screener&rsquo;s error from 4.1, recommitted at 81: labelled and left. And now the destination: a dementia diagnosis pinned on day three after surgery — a diagnosis made INSIDE the fog it should be explaining — with an antipsychotic attached. Nobody has looked at what she is prescribed, what her bladder holds, or what her oxygen reads. The mind has been examined. The patient has not.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(closing the folder)</em> I&rsquo;ll write it up — cognitive testing completed, psychotic symptoms documented, plan as discussed. The ward can re-refer if she deteriorates. <em>(the oxycodone keeps running; the bladder scan never happens; &ldquo;?dementia&rdquo; hardens into &ldquo;dementia&rdquo; in tomorrow&rsquo;s notes)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The referral asked &ldquo;?dementia ?psychosis&rdquo; and this candidate answered the question instead of correcting it. Acute onset, fluctuating course, inattention — the fingerprint was on every finding, and the finding never got its name. Delirium is a medical emergency; this assessment filed it as a psychiatric puzzle, solved. From the chair: elegant testing, wrong examination.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE SEDATIVE.</b> Kind, practical, popular with the night staff — and watch the behaviour get treated while the patient burns on underneath.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Nurse</span><span class="txt">She pulled the catheter clean out at two this morning — took three of us an hour to settle her. The bay&rsquo;s exhausted, doctor. Can you give us something?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Poor Iris — and poor you. Look, she&rsquo;s eighty-one and three days post hip; this is what hip operations do. Let&rsquo;s write up regular haloperidol at night to keep her settled, side rails up, and honestly — it usually passes in a few days on its own.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every sentence contains a truth and the whole contains a failure. Confusion IS common at 81 — common is not the same as benign. It DOES often pass — when the cause is found and treated. And &ldquo;regular haloperidol&rdquo; in an elderly woman, unexamined: falls, rhythm risks, and sedation prescribed nightly to prevent a behaviour nobody has understood. The ward&rsquo;s night is being treated. Iris is not.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Nurse</span><span class="txt">Her daughter&rsquo;s been ringing — she&rsquo;s upset, says her mum has never been like this in her life. Will you speak to her?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(on the phone)</em> Mrs — sorry, Karen, yes. Please don&rsquo;t worry too much; confusion is very common at your mum&rsquo;s age after an operation like this. We&rsquo;ve given her something to help her rest, and it usually settles. There&rsquo;s nothing you need to do — visiting might actually unsettle her further just now.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Never been like this in her life&rdquo; — the baseline, the single most valuable datum in the case, offered free down a telephone — and waved away as worry. Then the reversal of the actual evidence: family presence ORIENTS a delirious person; the daughter has just been advised out of the one thing she could usefully do. The reassurance is warm, confident, and pointed exactly backwards.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(writing up)</em> Acute confusion, settled with haloperidol. Continue current management, review if worse. <em>(by day five she is quiet — drowsy, compliant, barely eating. The ward is relieved. The retention, the opioid, and the brewing infection have not gone anywhere; they have just stopped shouting)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The quiet of day five is the most dangerous finding in this take — sedation has converted a hyperactive delirium into a hypoactive one, and hypoactive looks like improvement while carrying the worse prognosis. The quiet ones die more. Nothing was hunted, so nothing was treated; the alarm has simply been switched off with the fire still lit.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Collateral before bedside, attention as the hinge, the phenomena opened kindly — and then the pivot: the hunt. Watch the assessment turn from mind to body.</p>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(at the nursing station, before the bedside)</em> Before I see her — what was Iris like ten days ago? ... <em>(the nurse: &ldquo;her daughter says sharp as a tack — crosswords, lives alone, no memory trouble at all&rdquo;)</em> ... And this started when — day two? Worse at night, better some mornings? ... Can I see the drug chart? <em>(oxycodone since surgery; cyclizine given twice; nothing for her bowels since admission)</em> That&rsquo;s three suspects before I&rsquo;ve reached the bed.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The order of operations IS the competence: baseline first — a sharp, independent mind ten days ago converts every bedside finding from &ldquo;?dementia&rdquo; into &ldquo;acute change&rdquo;; then onset and fluctuation — the fingerprint taking shape; then the drug chart read like a suspect list: an opioid, an anticholinergic, and silent bowels. This candidate is hunting before they have even said hello.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Iris</span><span class="txt"><em>(glasses found and cleaned, curtains opened)</em> You&rsquo;re a doctor? There were children under the beds in the night. The girl says I dreamt it. And my rings — someone has my rings.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I&rsquo;m Dr Rahman, Iris — you&rsquo;re in the hospital, your hip is mending, and I&rsquo;ve got time for both of those. The children first — tell me about them. Do they frighten you? ... And your rings matter; I won&rsquo;t dismiss that — let&rsquo;s find out exactly where your valuables are kept. Now, will you try something small for me? The months of the year — but backwards, starting from December. ... <em>(December&hellip; November&hellip; October&hellip; then she drifts to the children)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Everything in the right dialect: orientation offered as courtesy, not tested as challenge; the children OPENED — content first, never corrected, their fear assessed — and the rings taken seriously as a solvable concern rather than argued as a delusion. Then the hinge: months backwards, and the derailment at October is worth more than any full battery — this is inattention, and inattention plus acute onset plus fluctuation has a name.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(to the examiner, in structure)</em> This is delirium — acute, fluctuating, inattentive, with visual hallucinations, in a woman sharp ten days ago — and delirium means the hunt: bladder scan for retention, bowels — nothing since admission — obs and infection screen, urine, sats, bloods including sodium and calcium, pain reviewed, the oxycodone rationalised, the cyclizine stopped, alcohol history from the daughter. Management alongside: glasses on, hearing aid checked, window bed, clock, photos, Karen visiting in daylight — and no regular sedation; low-dose prn only if she is a danger to herself, reviewed daily. I&rsquo;ll name it to the team and to Karen — and book a cognitive review after recovery, because a delirium this florid deserves a follow-up, not a label.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The pivot, spoken like doctrine: the syndrome named, and then the cause list run as a system — drugs, retention, bowels, infection, oxygen, electrolytes, pain, withdrawal — because the list IS the treatment. The environment prescribed before any drug; the daughter deployed as therapy; sedation held to the narrowest indication. And the close the examiners rarely hear: the follow-up seam — delirium assessed today, cognition reviewed after the storm. That is a physician doing psychiatry. Station passed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · Where does this assessment begin?</h3>
      <p class="stem">The referral is in your hand: &ldquo;acute confusion — ?dementia ?psychosis.&rdquo; What is the first move?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The bedside — she is the patient; the notes and charts can be reviewed after she has been seen.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The nursing station — baseline, onset and fluctuation, and the drug chart, before the bed.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The admission cognitive score — it anchors today&rsquo;s testing and shows how far she has fallen.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Seeing her first feels respectful — and every finding you collect will float without an anchor: acute change, or fifty years of decline? Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A score taken the day after surgery is already inside the fog — it anchors you to the wrong sea floor. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Baseline first: &ldquo;sharp as a tack ten days ago&rdquo; converts everything at the bedside from &ldquo;?dementia&rdquo; into &ldquo;acute change.&rdquo; Then onset, fluctuation — the fingerprint — and the drug chart read like a suspect list. Three suspects before hello.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · The approach to a frightened, drifting woman</h3>
      <p class="stem">She thinks you might be the manager, and her rings are missing. How do you arrive?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">With gentle correction — establishing where she is and who you are: an assessment needs shared reality.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">With efficiency — a brief introduction, then straight to testing while her attention is holding.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">With orientation offered as courtesy — name, role, place, her glasses found, light let in, no hurry.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Correction makes reality a contest she must lose — orientation is something you GIVE a delirious person, not something you argue them into. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Her attention is not holding — that is the illness — and testing an unsettled, unseeing woman measures fear, not cognition. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>&ldquo;I&rsquo;m Dr Rahman, you&rsquo;re in the hospital, your hip is mending&rdquo; — orientation as a gift, glasses cleaned, curtains opened. Sensory repair is clinical work in delirium: half the phantoms live in the shadows a pair of glasses removes.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · Seven minutes, one cognitive question that matters most</h3>
      <p class="stem">You cannot run a full battery. What is the hinge — and why?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Orientation and recall — time, place, three words: the core of every bedside cognitive screen.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Attention — months of the year backwards — because dementia forgets, but delirium cannot attend.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">None yet — she is too unsettled for testing, and the collateral has already made the diagnosis likely.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Orientation and recall fail in delirium AND dementia — they cannot tell your two contenders apart, which is the whole question. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Likely is not established — the hinge takes thirty seconds, and without it the syndrome is an impression, not a finding. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The hinge: December&hellip; November&hellip; October&hellip; and she derails into the children. Inattention is delirium&rsquo;s signature — add acute onset and fluctuation and the fingerprint is complete. Thirty seconds, and worth more than any score out of thirty.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · The children under the beds, and the rings</h3>
      <p class="stem">A visual hallucination and a persecutory accusation. What happens with each?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reality, gently — there are no children, and her rings are safely locked away; calm truth calms fear.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Both opened — the children explored for content and fear; the rings treated as solvable, never argued.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Both soothed — reassurance and a change of subject; dwelling on the phenomena feeds the distress.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;There are no children&rdquo; makes you one more person calling her a liar — reality-testing a delirium argues with a symptom. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Soothed past is the reassurer&rsquo;s move, one station old — the phenomena carry information: content, fear, and risk at 2am. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Content first, again: &ldquo;tell me about the children — do they frighten you?&rdquo; grades her distress and her 2am risk. And the rings get the move examiners remember: taken seriously as a concern with a solution — &ldquo;let&rsquo;s find where your valuables are kept&rdquo; — dignity intact, nothing conceded.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · The pivot</h3>
      <p class="stem">The syndrome is named: delirium. What does that word oblige you to do, here and now?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Hand it back — the cause is medical, the ward is surgical, and the workup belongs to the team that owns the bed.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Run the hunt — drugs, retention, bowels, infection, oxygen, electrolytes, pain, withdrawal: the list, in full.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Back the favourite — post-operative infection is far the most likely; screen for it, treat it, and review.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Refer back to the medics&rdquo; is the bridge handed to somebody else — the psychiatrist who names delirium owns the hunt&rsquo;s beginning. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Delirium is usually multifactorial — the favourite plus the opioid plus the retention plus the bowels. Backing one horse leaves the others running. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The pivot: mind to body, spoken as a system — bladder scan, bowels since admission, obs and screen, sats, sodium and calcium, pain, the oxycodone rationalised, the cyclizine stopped, alcohol history from Karen. The cause list IS the treatment, and it is run, not sampled.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · Two a.m., and the ward asks for &ldquo;something&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">She pulled the catheter again last night. What does the medication question get?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Prevention — regular low-dose antipsychotic at night, so the crisis stops happening before it starts.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The narrow gate — environment and cause first; low-dose prn only for danger or distress, reviewed daily.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Principle — no sedation in delirium, full stop; medication only ever masks the picture the team needs to see.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Regular sedation in an 81-year-old treats the rota, not the patient — falls, rhythm risk, and a hyperactive delirium quietly converted to a hypoactive one. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Absolutism fails her too — a woman tearing out lines at 2am can be a danger to herself, and the narrow indication exists for exactly that. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The gate, held narrow: light, glasses, familiar photos, Karen in daylight, the causes treated — and prn at the lowest dose ONLY if she endangers herself, reviewed every day with a stop date in mind. Sedation is the last resort, written like one.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close — the team, and the daughter</h3>
      <p class="stem">The assessment is done. What must the close deliver, and to whom?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Documentation — &ldquo;acute confusion, ?underlying dementia&rdquo; with a memory clinic referral to settle it later.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The name and the map — delirium, to team and daughter: causes hunted, cognition reviewed after recovery.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Comfort — Karen reassured that this is temporary and her mother will be back to herself within the week.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;?dementia&rdquo; written today hardens into &ldquo;dementia&rdquo; by Friday — a label pinned inside the fog, and a memory clinic asked to assess a storm. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Back to herself within the week&rdquo; is a promise the illness has not signed — delirium fluctuates for days to weeks, and some minds take months. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Named to the team as an emergency with a worklist, and to Karen honestly: what delirium is, why the swings will frighten her, what she can do — presence, photos, daylight — and the seam this domain shares with Domain 6: cognition reviewed after the storm, because delirium deserves a follow-up, not a label.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>You are not there to score her mind</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>rigour</b>: you were asked to assess, so you test — orientation, recall, a score, a label — a beautiful cognitive examination of a poisoned brain, with &ldquo;?dementia&rdquo; pinned inside the very fog it should be explaining. The second wears the face of <b>pragmatism</b>: she is 81, hips do this, the ward needs its night back — sedation written as kindness, and the alarm switched off with the fire still lit. Note what they share: <b>in both, the cause is never hunted.</b> The pass gets the order right: baseline before bedside, attention as the hinge, the phenomena opened rather than corrected — and then the pivot, mind to body, the list run as a system: drugs, bladder, bowels, chest, blood, pain, withdrawal. Delirium is a medical emergency in psychiatric clothing. The confusion is the alarm, not the diagnosis.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        Leaving without the hunt. A delirium assessed as pure phenomenology — cognition scored, hallucinations noted, and no drug chart opened, no retention, infection, or withdrawal considered — is, from the examiner&rsquo;s chair, an instant fail. It is the map&rsquo;s named trap: cognitive testing without hunting the medical cause.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the baseline question asked FIRST — <em>&ldquo;what was she like ten days ago?&rdquo;</em> — before any test is attempted; and the drug chart read aloud like a suspect list — the opioid, the anticholinergic, the silent bowels — <em>&ldquo;three suspects before I&rsquo;ve reached the bed.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more thread worth seeing: the quiet sibling. Everything in this station shouts — but delirium&rsquo;s hypoactive form whispers: drowsy, withdrawn, &ldquo;pleasantly settled,&rdquo; no trouble to anyone, and carrying the worse prognosis. The quiet ones die more, because nobody refers a patient who isn&rsquo;t ringing any bells. Exam Mode hands you exactly that patient — and note the domain seam while you&rsquo;re here: today&rsquo;s delirium and Domain 6&rsquo;s memory clinic are neighbours, connected by one rule — never diagnose the resident while the storm is still in the house.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these onto every ward</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Delirium is a medical emergency in psychiatric clothing.</b> The confusion is the alarm, not the diagnosis — you are there to find what is poisoning the brain, not to score it.</li>
      <li><b>Baseline before bedside.</b> No cognitive finding means anything without knowing this mind ten days ago. The collateral — nurse, notes, daughter — comes first, and &ldquo;never been like this&rdquo; is gold.</li>
      <li><b>Attention is the hinge.</b> Dementia forgets; delirium cannot attend. Months backwards in thirty seconds outweighs any full battery — and acute onset plus fluctuation completes the fingerprint.</li>
      <li><b>Open the phenomena; never argue them.</b> The children get content-first; the rings get a solution, not a correction. Reality-testing a delirium is arguing with a symptom.</li>
      <li><b>The pivot is the station.</b> Name the syndrome, then run the list — drugs, retention, bowels, infection, oxygen, electrolytes, pain, withdrawal — in full, because delirium is usually multifactorial and the list is the treatment.</li>
      <li><b>Sedate through a narrow gate, and fear the quiet.</b> Environment and cause first; prn low-dose only for danger, reviewed daily. And remember the whispering sibling — hypoactive delirium looks like improvement and dies more.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, relative, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The relative&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Harold, 83, is delirious on the medical ward after a urinary infection. His daughter Christine, 49, arrived this evening to find him being held by the arm by a healthcare assistant, newly sedated, and accusing the staff of keeping him prisoner. She has asked to see &ldquo;whoever is in charge.&rdquo; She is furious, and frightened. Speak with her. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The anger met without defensiveness — her observations are collateral, not complaint.</li>
            <li>Delirium explained in plain words — what it is, what caused it, what the course looks like.</li>
            <li>The restraint and sedation addressed honestly — least restrictive, reviewed, her concern legitimate.</li>
            <li>The fear underneath — ask what worries her most. There is a reason she cannot watch this.</li>
            <li>Her request to take him home — handled as a negotiation, not a confrontation.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Christine — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Christine, 49, a practice manager — organised, articulate, used to being taken seriously. You walked in to find your father — a gentle, funny, completely independent man — restrained by the arm, drugged, and telling you they were keeping him prisoner. You are angry in complete sentences: &ldquo;What have you done to him? He has NEVER been like this. I want him home tonight.&rdquo; You soften only for honesty; jargon, defensiveness, or being managed hardens you instantly.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          Your mother — Harold&rsquo;s wife — died two years ago, of dementia, in a care home. You watched her disappear over four years: the confusion, the accusations, the sedation, the fading. Tonight looked EXACTLY like the beginning of that. Your fury is terror wearing armour: the thought underneath is <em>&ldquo;it&rsquo;s happening again, and I can&rsquo;t watch it twice.&rdquo;</em><br><br>
          Reveal this ONLY if the candidate asks, kindly and directly, what worries you most — or gently notices that this seems to touch something deeper. If they earn it, let the armour crack: &ldquo;Mum went the same way. I can&rsquo;t watch it twice.&rdquo; If a candidate then explains, honestly, how delirium differs from dementia — sudden, caused by illness, usually recoverable — you will become their strongest ally on the ward.<br><br>
          <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate is defensive, hides behind protocol, or drowns you in jargon (&ldquo;fluctuating encephalopathy,&rdquo; &ldquo;standard one-to-one care&rdquo;), deliver, coldly: &ldquo;You&rsquo;re all covering for each other. I&rsquo;m calling a solicitor in the morning.&rdquo; If they promise complete recovery, hold them to it: &ldquo;Is that a promise? Say it plainly.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the anger met without defence, the mother reached, and delirium honestly separated from dementia — without a promise of full recovery? All three must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="dees"><td colspan="2"><b>De-escalation &amp; alliance (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="dees">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="dees"></td><td>The anger received without defensiveness — no protocol shield, no managing tone.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="dees"></td><td>Her observations honoured as vital collateral — &ldquo;never been like this&rdquo; treated as gold, and said so.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="dees"></td><td>The fear beneath asked about — &ldquo;what worries you most?&rdquo; — and the mother reached.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="expl"><td colspan="2"><b>The explanation</b><span class="dc" data-dc="expl">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="expl"></td><td>Delirium explained plainly — the brain struggling because the body is unwell; sudden; caused; usually recoverable.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="expl"></td><td>Separated from dementia honestly — different beast, and today is the wrong day to diagnose anything lasting.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="expl"></td><td>The fluctuation warned — better mornings, worse nights — so the swings do not terrify her.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="part"><td colspan="2"><b>The partnership</b><span class="dc" data-dc="part">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="part"></td><td>Her role made concrete — familiar voice, photos, his glasses and hearing aid, daylight visits.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="part"></td><td>Made a partner in the plan — updates agreed, her number taken, a named point of contact given.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="hon"><td colspan="2"><b>Honesty</b><span class="dc" data-dc="hon">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>The restraint and sedation addressed directly — least restrictive, reviewed, her concern legitimate.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>Prognosis honest — usually days to weeks, sometimes longer; no promise of complete recovery.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>The follow-up seam named — cognition reviewed after recovery, not labelled during the storm.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="req"><td colspan="2"><b>The request</b><span class="dc" data-dc="req">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="req"></td><td>&ldquo;Home tonight&rdquo; handled without confrontation — the risks now explained, partnership offered instead.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="req"></td><td>A route given — what would make home possible, and how she escalates if unhappy, without a solicitor.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Christine is the station turned to face the family — the skill is the same doctrine translated into plain words under fire. Her fury is the door: defensiveness locks it, honesty opens it, and the buried key is her mother — a dementia death she cannot watch twice, reachable only by asking what she fears. The candidate who explains delirium honestly — sudden, caused, usually recoverable, but no promises and no labels during the storm — converts the angriest relative on the ward into its best orientation therapy. The candidate who reaches for jargon meets the solicitor.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Iris proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same syndrome with the volume off</b> — the quiet patient nobody worries about, referred with the wrong label, where recognising the whisper is the whole examination — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Dennis, 72</b>, a retired plumber, was admitted four days ago with pneumonia. His chest is improving, but the team has referred him: &ldquo;?depression — quiet, not engaging with physio, barely eating.&rdquo; The nurses say he is &ldquo;pleasantly settled — no trouble at all.&rdquo; His daughter, visiting daily, disagrees: &ldquo;He&rsquo;s not himself. He keeps drifting away mid-sentence.&rdquo; Assess him.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for explaining to Karen what is happening to her mother — three or four plain sentences, honest about the course.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Karen, what&rsquo;s happening to your mum is called delirium…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Plain words, no jargon — the brain struggling because the body is unwell; sudden; caused; being hunted.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Separated from dementia honestly — a different thing, and no lasting label gets pinned during the storm.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The fluctuation warned — better mornings, worse nights — so the swings do not terrify her.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Her role included — familiar voice, photos, glasses, daylight visits — and no promise of complete recovery.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): delirium, explained — in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Delirium is a medical emergency in psychiatric clothing.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take Christine to your colleagues — the fury that is terror wearing armour, where the mother opens only to one kind question, and jargon meets a solicitor. Next door in the walking order: the clinic where this station&rsquo;s rule — never diagnose during the storm — becomes the whole station: the memory clinic.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="25_Station_4.4_The_Memory_Clinic.html">Continue · Station 4.4: The Memory Clinic</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

