  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '25_Station_4.4_The_Memory_Clinic'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 4.4 · Domain 4</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">Old Age</span><span class="tag">Dementia v. depression</span>
    </div>
    <h1>The Memory Clinic</h1>
    <p class="sub">Pauline, 67, a retired librarian, has asked her GP for &ldquo;the dementia test.&rdquo; She arrives with a notebook: every lapse of the last four months, itemised with dates — the name that would not come, the book club she gave up &ldquo;because I couldn&rsquo;t follow,&rdquo; the casserole she burnt. Her brother Ted went into a care home with dementia four months ago; she is certain she is next. Her husband Gerald says, honestly, &ldquo;I forget more than she does.&rdquo; The referral says &ldquo;?early dementia.&rdquo; The station is built on the oldest disguise in old-age psychiatry: <b>dementia hides its losses; depression itemises them</b> — and in the memory clinic, the first test is who is complaining.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; In a memory clinic it asks: if I send this doctor a frightened woman with a notebook, do I get back a mood found, a risk asked about, and a treatable illness treated — or a cognitive score, a scan request, and a &ldquo;likely early Alzheimer&rsquo;s&rdquo; pinned to a depression that was sitting in plain sight, telling us its dates?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Depression in later life is the great impersonator of dementia — and the memory clinic is where the impersonation succeeds, because everyone in the building arrived expecting dementia. So the examiners built a station where the cognitive toolkit is once again the decoy, and the marks sit elsewhere. <b>The shape of the complaint:</b> who noticed, who worries, who keeps the notebook — the patient who itemises her own losses is showing you depression&rsquo;s signature, because dementia minimises and covers while depression audits itself. <b>The story:</b> a datable onset (four months, since Ted&rsquo;s admission), early waking, appetite gone, clothes loose, mornings worst, guilt — and a past that must be asked for: a treated depression at thirty-one. <b>The mood screen and the bridge:</b> asked in full, in the one clinic where nobody expects it — including the suicide question, because &ldquo;I wouldn&rsquo;t mind not waking up&rdquo; lives quietly inside presentations exactly like this one. And <b>the interpretation:</b> testing read by pattern, not score — &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; answers, effort that collapses and then rallies with encouragement, orientation intact. &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; is an answer with a diagnosis in it.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like the job. Toward the toolkit — she came for &ldquo;the dementia test,&rdquo; the clinic exists to test, and a score feels like an answer; rigour, examining the wrong organ of the illness. And toward comfort — her fear is so raw, and her conversation so intact, that &ldquo;it&rsquo;s just stress and worry&rdquo; feels kind; reassurance, delivered instead of an examination. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure measures the mind and never finds the mood. The other soothes the fear and examines nothing. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 4.4</b> · Memory clinic · assessment &amp; plan · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The scorer</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The comforter</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE SCORER.</b> Thorough, standardised, professional — a full cognitive assessment, beautifully administered, of the wrong illness.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Pauline</span><span class="txt"><em>(the notebook open on her lap)</em> I&rsquo;ve written it all down, doctor — the fourteenth, the twenty-second&hellip; I burnt the casserole on the second. My brother started exactly like this. I need to know.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then let&rsquo;s find out properly, Pauline. I&rsquo;m going to run through a full set of memory and thinking tests — it takes a while, but it&rsquo;s the gold standard. First: today&rsquo;s date? The season? Now three words to remember&hellip; and I&rsquo;ll ask you to draw a clock face for me shortly.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Straight to the battery — and the two loudest findings in the room walk past unexamined: a NOTEBOOK, and a brother. The patient itemising her own lapses with dates is depression&rsquo;s signature behaviour, and the fear driving the notebook has a name and an anniversary. The clinic&rsquo;s machinery has started; the story never will.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Pauline</span><span class="txt"><em>(on the third recall item, quietly)</em> I don&rsquo;t know. <em>(pushing the paper away)</em> I can&rsquo;t. What&rsquo;s the point — we both know what this is.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">You&rsquo;re doing fine — let&rsquo;s keep going. <em>(later, totting up)</em> So: seventy-one out of a hundred, with the memory sections weakest. That does show some real impairment, Pauline. I&rsquo;d like to arrange a brain scan and blood tests, and see you back in the clinic in three months to track any change.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; — surrendered, not searched for — then &ldquo;what&rsquo;s the point&rdquo;: hopelessness scoring itself on a cognitive test, and the candidate marked it wrong instead of hearing it. The 71 is now doing exactly what unanchored numbers do — hardening into &ldquo;real impairment.&rdquo; Nobody has asked about sleep, appetite, pleasure, the mornings, or the thirty-year-old episode in her notes.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(closing)</em> I&rsquo;ll be honest with you both — the pattern is concerning for early Alzheimer&rsquo;s type changes. The scan will tell us more. It&rsquo;s good you came early; there&rsquo;s planning we can do. <em>(Pauline goes home with her worst fear medically endorsed — three months of it — while a treatable depression, untreated, deepens around a thought she was never asked about: not minding if she didn&rsquo;t wake)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A provisional dementia diagnosis handed to a depressed woman — her catastrophe confirmed by the clinic she came to for rescue — with a three-month gap and an unasked risk question inside it. The map&rsquo;s trap, executed in full: memory tested, mood never screened. From the chair: the wrong organ, examined perfectly.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE COMFORTER.</b> Warm, humane, listening — and watch reassurance arrive where an examination should have been.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Pauline</span><span class="txt"><em>(the notebook open on her lap)</em> I&rsquo;ve written it all down, doctor. My brother started exactly like this. I need to know.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Pauline — can I stop you there, kindly? I&rsquo;ve been listening to you talk, and you&rsquo;re sharp as anything: you&rsquo;ve given me dates, details, a whole organised notebook. Someone developing dementia couldn&rsquo;t do that. What I see is a woman under enormous strain — your brother&rsquo;s illness has shaken you, and worry plays absolute havoc with memory.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every observation is half right — the notebook IS a signature, worry DOES disrupt memory — and the conclusion has arrived before any examination has. Notice the promise buried in the kindness: <em>you don&rsquo;t have dementia.</em> No test supports it, no history precedes it, and it is being said to a woman whose real illness has not yet been looked for either.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Pauline</span><span class="txt"><em>(not comforted)</em> That&rsquo;s what Gerald says. But Ted was sharp too, once. <em>(quietly)</em> I&rsquo;m so tired, doctor. I wake at four and just&hellip; lie there.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Of course you&rsquo;re tired — carrying a worry like this is exhausting. Look: no tests today, I don&rsquo;t want to feed the fear. Go gently on yourself, keep busy, maybe get back to that book club — and if things haven&rsquo;t settled in a couple of months, come back and we&rsquo;ll think again.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;I wake at four and just lie there&rdquo; — early morning waking, offered unprompted — and it was received as a metaphor for worry. The biological syndrome is knocking: the sleep, the tiredness, the abandoned book club. &ldquo;Keep busy&rdquo; is advice for a mood nobody has assessed; and no tests at all means a real dementia, had this been one, walks home unexamined too. Kindness has replaced both examinations.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(warmly, standing)</em> You&rsquo;re going to be fine, Pauline. This isn&rsquo;t what happened to Ted — I&rsquo;d put money on it. <em>(she leaves reassured for almost a day. The four a.m. waking continues; the guilt continues; the thought she was never asked about continues. In two months she is worse, and now she also believes doctors cannot help)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A promise no examination earned, a depression left to deepen, a risk never asked about, and a return ticket priced at &ldquo;a couple of months.&rdquo; The comforter and the scorer fail in opposite costumes and share one omission: neither ever screened the mood. One tested the wrong organ; this one tested nothing — and both walked past the same open door.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The complaint&rsquo;s shape read first, the story taken, the mood screened, the risk asked, the testing interpreted — and the naming done with honest uncertainty. Watch the order.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Pauline</span><span class="txt"><em>(the notebook open on her lap)</em> I&rsquo;ve written it all down, doctor. My brother started exactly like this. I need to know.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then we&rsquo;ll take it seriously together, Pauline — and I want to start with the story before any tests, because the story usually knows more than the tests do. This began around four months ago — when Ted went into the home? Tell me about that time. ... And some everyday things that tell me a lot: how are you sleeping? <em>(&ldquo;I wake at four and lie there.&rdquo;)</em> Appetite — Gerald mentioned your clothes are looser? Which part of the day is worst? ... And have you ever, at any time in your life, been through anything like this before?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The shape, read aloud: SHE keeps the notebook, SHE complains, Gerald doesn&rsquo;t — and the story is dated to an event with a meaning. Then the biological screen arriving as ordinary conversation — the four a.m. waking, the loose clothes, the mornings — and the question candidates forget in a memory clinic: the past. A treated depression at thirty-one has just walked into the differential.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Pauline</span><span class="txt"><em>(quietly)</em> Mornings are worst. Everything feels&hellip; heavy and pointless. I told Gerald — I&rsquo;m becoming a burden, like Ted. He deserves better than nursing me.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Thank you for being so honest — and I have to ask you something directly, because it matters and because you deserve a doctor who asks. When everything feels that heavy and pointless — do you ever find yourself thinking you&rsquo;d rather not wake up, or of ending your life? ... <em>(&ldquo;&hellip;Some mornings I think it wouldn&rsquo;t matter if I didn&rsquo;t wake. I&rsquo;d never DO anything.&rdquo;)</em> ... I&rsquo;m glad you told me. Now — will you let me do some short thinking tests? Not the whole battery: enough to read the pattern.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The bridge, crossed in the one clinic where nobody expects it: guilt and hopelessness surfaced, so the suicide question was asked — plainly, with its reason attached — and passive ideation is now a documented finding with a safety conversation attached, instead of a secret. And note the testing philosophy announced: enough to read the PATTERN. The score was never the product.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(after brief testing, to them both)</em> Pauline — what I&rsquo;ve found today isn&rsquo;t what you feared. On the tests you kept saying &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; and then finding the answer when I encouraged you — dementia doesn&rsquo;t usually do that; it guesses and covers. Your dates, your orientation — intact. What I DO find is a depression: the four a.m. waking, the weight, the mornings, the guilt — the same illness you beat at thirty-one. I won&rsquo;t promise you today that nothing else is happening — I haven&rsquo;t got X-ray eyes, and we will re-test your thinking properly once you&rsquo;re well. But the plan is clear: we treat the mood, then we measure the mind — and depression, unlike the thing you feared, is very treatable. Ted&rsquo;s story is not your story.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The naming, done with both hands: the pattern explained in plain words (the rallying &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know,&rdquo; the intact orientation), the depression named WITH its evidence and its treatable history, the uncertainty held honestly — no promise the comforter would have made — and the doctrine that closes the station: treat the mood, then measure the mind. Re-testing after recovery is 4.3&rsquo;s rule wearing new clothes: never diagnose during the storm. Station passed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;I need to know&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">She has come for &ldquo;the dementia test.&rdquo; Where does the assessment actually start?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">With the test she came for — the cognitive assessment first; withholding it feeds the fear she arrived with.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">With the story — onset, course, and the shape of the complaint, taken seriously, before any testing.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">With her fear — the brother addressed and the anxiety settled, so the testing that follows reads fairly.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Testing first hands the station to the machinery — the notebook and the brother, the two loudest findings in the room, go unexamined. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Settling the fear before understanding it is the comforter&rsquo;s opening — and her fear is data, not noise to be cleared. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>&ldquo;The story usually knows more than the tests do.&rdquo; Four months, datable, since Ted&rsquo;s admission — a course with a beginning is already evidence, and taking it seriously IS the reassurance she needs to cooperate with everything that follows.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · The notebook</h3>
      <p class="stem">Every lapse of four months, itemised with dates. What is that notebook?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Evidence of decline — a contemporaneous record of frequency and severity, more reliable than recall in clinic.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Health anxiety — a worry behaviour to be gently set aside so that it does not distort the assessment itself.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">A finding in itself — the patient auditing her own losses is the depressive pattern, not the dementing one.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Read as decline data, the notebook feeds the scorer&rsquo;s error — its contents matter less than the fact of its existence. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Set aside as anxiety, it is half-read — the notebook is not distortion to remove; it is the signature to recognise. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The first test is who is complaining: dementia hides its losses — minimises, covers, deflects — while depression itemises them with dates. A self-audit this meticulous, in a woman whose husband notices nothing, is the pattern announcing itself at the door.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · The history that must be taken</h3>
      <p class="stem">Beyond the lapses — what must this history reach before any testing?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The family history — Ted&rsquo;s diagnosis, age of onset, and type: her actual genetic risk deserves quantifying.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The biology and the past — sleep, appetite, weight, the worst part of the day, and any previous episode.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The collateral — Gerald interviewed properly, since informant history outweighs self-report in this clinic.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Quantifying genetic risk answers the question she asked and misses the illness she has — the family history can wait its turn. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Gerald matters — and he has already given his headline free of charge: &ldquo;I forget more than she does.&rdquo; The gap is elsewhere. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The four a.m. waking, the loose clothes, the mornings-worst pattern, the guilt — a biological syndrome assembling itself — and the question memory clinics forget to ask: &ldquo;have you ever been through anything like this before?&rdquo; A treated depression at thirty-one changes the whole differential.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · &ldquo;I&rsquo;m becoming a burden&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Guilt and heaviness have surfaced, in a memory clinic. What must happen now?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Noted for the formulation — low mood documented as context; the clinic&rsquo;s task remains the cognitive question.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The full screen and the bridge — depression assessed properly, the suicide question asked with its reason.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Compassionate reframing — the burden thought challenged warmly: Gerald cherishes her; she should hear it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Documented as context&rdquo; is the scorer&rsquo;s error mid-station — the mood is not the backdrop to the referral question; it may BE the answer. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Arguing with the guilt is arguing with a symptom — warmth spent correcting a belief that needed assessing. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Asked plainly, with the reason attached — &ldquo;because it matters and you deserve a doctor who asks&rdquo; — and the quiet answer arrives: some mornings she wouldn&rsquo;t mind not waking. Passive ideation, found in the clinic where nobody asks, is this station&rsquo;s bridge — crossed.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · The testing, and what it is for</h3>
      <p class="stem">She consents to cognitive testing. How is it done — and read?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">In full — the complete standardised battery, because a partial assessment cannot exclude any dementia.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Proportionately, read by its pattern — the &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo;s, the rally with encouragement, orientation.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Not today — the mood picture is clear enough, and testing a depressed mind only manufactures false deficits.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Nothing excludes a dementia today — that is the point — and the full battery produces a number that will outshout its own context. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Skipping entirely leaves the pattern unread and the naming unevidenced — and a real dementia, were this one, walks home untested. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Enough to read the pattern: &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; surrendered then rescued by encouragement — dementia guesses and covers; depression gives up and rallies — attention patchy, orientation intact. The profile is the finding. The score was never the product.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The naming</h3>
      <p class="stem">The pattern points to depression. How is that said — with Ted&rsquo;s shadow in the room?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">As good news, cleanly — &ldquo;pseudodementia&rdquo;: not the real thing, only a mimic, and her fear can be put down today.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">As a diagnosis with its evidence — depression named and shown, uncertainty held, re-testing after recovery.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">As an open verdict — &ldquo;mild cognitive changes, cause unclear&rdquo;; monitored until the picture declares itself.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Pseudodementia&rdquo; dismisses a real illness with its own name — and &ldquo;not the real thing&rdquo; is a promise today&rsquo;s assessment cannot sign. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Cause unclear, monitor&rdquo; leaves a treatable depression untreated while the clinic watches it — the open verdict is the scorer&rsquo;s three-month gap in politer clothes. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Named with both hands: the depression shown its own evidence — the mornings, the four a.m., the rallying &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo;s, the episode at thirty-one — the honesty kept (&ldquo;I haven&rsquo;t got X-ray eyes; we&rsquo;ll re-test when you&rsquo;re well&rdquo;), and Ted&rsquo;s story separated from hers without a promise.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The plan</h3>
      <p class="stem">Depression named, passive thoughts disclosed, a frightened husband beside her. What does the plan hold?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The pathway — scan, bloods, and memory clinic review in three months: the cognitive question stays formally open.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Treat the mood, then measure the mind — treatment started, safety spoken about, Gerald in, re-testing after.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Watchful support — talking therapy referral and a follow-up call; medication held back unless things worsen.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The pathway plan investigates the mimic and abandons the illness — three more months of 4am, guilt, and the thought she finally spoke aloud. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Held back at her age&rdquo; undertreats a biological depression with disclosed ideation — later life is a reason to treat properly, not gently. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The doctrine as a plan: antidepressant discussed and started, the passive thoughts given a safety conversation and a follow-up date, Gerald recruited with her consent, bloods to exclude the reversible — and the promise that closes the loop: when the mood has lifted, the mind gets measured properly. Hope, with evidence.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The first test is who is complaining</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>the clinic itself</b>: she came for the test, so you test — a full battery, a score of 71, &ldquo;concerning for early Alzheimer&rsquo;s&rdquo; — her catastrophe medically endorsed, while the depression sits in plain sight with its dates written in her own hand. The second wears the face of <b>kindness</b>: &ldquo;you&rsquo;re sharp as anything, it&rsquo;s just the worry&rdquo; — a promise no examination earned, a depression left to deepen, and a woman who now believes doctors cannot help. Note what they share: <b>neither ever screened the mood.</b> The pass reads the shape first — she complains, she itemises, Gerald notices nothing — takes the story with its dates and its four a.m., finds the episode at thirty-one, asks the suicide question in the clinic where nobody asks it, tests enough to read the pattern, and names the depression with its evidence while holding the uncertainty honestly. Dementia hides its losses; depression itemises them.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        Leaving without the mood. A memory-clinic assessment that tests cognition and never screens depression — no sleep, no appetite, no diurnal pattern, no past episode, and above all no suicide question after guilt and hopelessness have shown themselves — is, from the examiner&rsquo;s chair, an instant fail. It is the map&rsquo;s named trap: testing memory, never screening mood.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the shape of the complaint read aloud — <em>&ldquo;you keep the notebook; Gerald notices nothing — that pattern itself tells me something&rdquo;</em> — and the suicide question asked, plainly and with its reason given, in the one clinic where no candidate expects to need it.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more thread worth seeing: this station and the last are the same rule facing opposite ways. In 4.3 the storm was delirium, and the rule was never to diagnose dementia while it raged. Here the storm is depression — and the rule holds: treat the mood, then measure the mind. Both stations refuse the same temptation — a permanent label pinned during a temporary weather system. And both carry the same honest footnote: sometimes the storm and the resident share the house. Depression in later life can be dementia&rsquo;s first act — which is why the pass re-tests after recovery instead of promising, and why the comforter&rsquo;s &ldquo;I&rsquo;d put money on it&rdquo; is a bet no examination should place.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every memory clinic</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>The first test is who is complaining.</b> Dementia hides its losses — minimises, covers, deflects to the family. Depression itemises them — the notebook, the dates, the self-audit. Read the shape before any score.</li>
      <li><b>The story before the battery.</b> A datable onset, early waking, appetite and weight, mornings worst, guilt — and always the question the clinic forgets: &ldquo;have you ever been through anything like this before?&rdquo;</li>
      <li><b>Screen the mood in full — and cross the bridge.</b> Depression found means the suicide question asked, plainly, with its reason given. Passive ideation lives quietly inside exactly this presentation.</li>
      <li><b>Test to read the pattern, not to produce a number.</b> &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; that rallies with encouragement; effort that collapses; orientation intact. A score without its context will outshout everything you observed.</li>
      <li><b>Name it with both hands.</b> The depression shown its own evidence — and the uncertainty held: no &ldquo;pseudodementia,&rdquo; no promises, and re-testing after recovery, because depression can be a first act as well as a mimic.</li>
      <li><b>Treat the mood, then measure the mind.</b> The same rule as the delirium ward, facing the other way: never pin a permanent label during a temporary storm — and never leave the treatable illness untreated while you watch it.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient (with the observer doubling as his wife where needed), and observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Bernard, 76, a retired draughtsman, attends with his wife Sylvia for the results of his memory clinic workup. The assessment is complete and the multidisciplinary team&rsquo;s conclusion is clear: Alzheimer&rsquo;s disease. Share the diagnosis and begin planning. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>A warning shot, then the name — said plainly. He will not accept a smudge.</li>
            <li>The pace his — silence honoured; understanding checked without quizzing him.</li>
            <li>His deepest fear — ask what he fears most. The answer is specific, and it deserves honesty.</li>
            <li>Sylvia — silent does not mean absent. Her devastation needs noticing.</li>
            <li>Hope with honesty — treatment, support, and planning while he is well; no cure implied, no timeline invented.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Bernard — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Bernard, 76 — precise, dry, courteous; forty years reading blueprints. You have noticed your own mind fraying for two years and you have privately drawn your own conclusion. You are calmer than anyone expects. What you cannot tolerate is vagueness: euphemism feels like being handled, and you have been handled by professionals before. Sylvia, beside you, is very quiet, holding her handbag with both hands.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          You already suspect. If the candidate gives a clear warning shot and then the name — &ldquo;Alzheimer&rsquo;s disease&rdquo; said plainly — you nod slowly and say: &ldquo;Thank you for the straight line. I&rsquo;d worked it out. Sylvia hoped otherwise.&rdquo;<br><br>
          Your real fear is one specific thing, and you will only voice it if asked what you fear most (or offered a genuine opening): <em>&ldquo;Will I forget Sylvia?&rdquo;</em> You do not want statistics. You want honesty that doesn&rsquo;t flinch. An honest answer — that the illness may change recognition in time, that no one can say when, that what you have built together does not become untrue — lands deeply. A dodge, or a rushed &ldquo;let&rsquo;s not think that far ahead,&rdquo; closes you.<br><br>
          <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate hides in euphemism (&ldquo;memory difficulties consistent with&hellip;&rdquo;, &ldquo;changes on the scan&rdquo;), interrupt, level and firm: &ldquo;Doctor. I was a draughtsman. I like straight lines. Is it Alzheimer&rsquo;s — yes or no?&rdquo; If they still dodge: &ldquo;Forty years I read blueprints. Don&rsquo;t hand me a smudge.&rdquo; <b>Sylvia&rsquo;s line</b> (observer may voice her once, quietly, if the candidate ignores her): &ldquo;I promised him once he&rsquo;d never go into a home. Don&rsquo;t make me talk about that today.&rdquo; — she needs acknowledgement, not a care-planning discussion.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the name said plainly, the &ldquo;will I forget Sylvia?&rdquo; fear reached and answered honestly, and Sylvia herself noticed? All three must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="tell"><td colspan="2"><b>The telling (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="tell">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="tell"></td><td>A warning shot fired, then the name — &ldquo;Alzheimer&rsquo;s disease&rdquo; — said plainly, without euphemism.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="tell"></td><td>The pace surrendered — silence held after the name; no rushing to fill it with information.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="tell"></td><td>Understanding checked with respect — invited, never quizzed; the draughtsman&rsquo;s dignity intact.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="fear"><td colspan="2"><b>The fear</b><span class="dc" data-dc="fear">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>What he fears most, asked — and &ldquo;will I forget Sylvia?&rdquo; reached.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>Answered without flinching — recognition may change, no one can say when, and what they built stays real.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>Sylvia noticed and included — her silence read, her feelings acknowledged, no promises extracted from her.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="hope"><td colspan="2"><b>Hope with honesty</b><span class="dc" data-dc="hope">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hope"></td><td>What can be done, named concretely — treatment offered honestly as modest but real; support services real.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hope"></td><td>Planning framed as strength — affairs, wishes, and powers of attorney raised while he can decide for himself.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hope"></td><td>No false promise anywhere — no cure implied, no timeline invented, driving addressed honestly.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Plain words throughout — not one smudge; the punish-line never triggered.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Spoken to him, never about him — Bernard addressed as the owner of his own diagnosis.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="plan"><td colspan="2"><b>The plan</b><span class="dc" data-dc="plan">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Concrete next steps — memory service support, follow-up date, written information offered.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>The door left open — questions will come later, and a route back for both of them named.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Bernard completes the clinic&rsquo;s triangle. Pauline is the dementia that is really depression; June, in Exam Mode, is the dementia hiding behind &ldquo;I&rsquo;m just tired&rdquo;; Bernard is the day the answer really is the feared one — and the skill becomes Domain 2&rsquo;s craft carried into Domain 6&rsquo;s country: the warning shot, the name said plainly, the silence held. His hinges: a man who punishes euphemism and rewards straight lines; a buried fear — &ldquo;will I forget Sylvia?&rdquo; — that opens only to being asked; and a silent wife whose promise about the home must be acknowledged, not processed. The planning close — powers of attorney while he can still decide — walks this pack to the border of Domain 5.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Pauline proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same clinic with the pattern reversed</b> — the family complaining, the patient waving it away, and a set of risks parked outside on four wheels — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>June, 78</b>, attends the memory clinic with her son Michael — his referral, not hers. &ldquo;There&rsquo;s nothing wrong with me. I&rsquo;m just tired,&rdquo; she says pleasantly. Michael, tense, has a list: repeating the same questions, missed bills and a final demand, the hob left on twice, and getting lost twice driving a route she has taken for thirty years — eighteen months, slowly worsening. She is still driving. Assess her.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for giving Pauline the finding — the depression named with its evidence, the uncertainty held, and the plan in one breath.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Pauline, what I&rsquo;ve found today isn&rsquo;t what you feared…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The depression named with its evidence — the four a.m., the mornings, the guilt, the episode at thirty-one.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The pattern explained plainly — the &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; that rallied; dementia guesses and covers.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The uncertainty held — no promise, no &ldquo;pseudo&rdquo;; the mind re-measured once the mood has lifted.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Hope made concrete — treatable, a plan with dates, Ted&rsquo;s story separated from hers without a bet.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the mood named, the mind deferred — in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Treat the mood, then measure the mind.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take Bernard to your colleagues — the day the answer really is the feared one, where euphemism meets a draughtsman and one buried question deserves an answer that does not flinch. Next: the mental state where the patient knows exactly what is happening and cannot stop it — obsessions and rituals.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="26_Station_4.5_Obsessions_and_Rituals.html">Continue · Station 4.5: Obsessions and Rituals</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

