  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '26_Station_4.5_Obsessions_and_Rituals'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 4.5 · Domain 4</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult</span><span class="tag">OCD</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Obsessions and Rituals</h1>
    <p class="sub">Lauren, 26, a pharmacy dispenser, sits with her sleeves pulled over her hands. Her GP&rsquo;s letter says &ldquo;handwashing — please assess.&rdquo; Underneath the sleeves her skin is cracked and bleeding; behind the referral is a two-hour morning shower, a written warning for lateness, a mother who now opens every door in the house for her, and a nephew she no longer dares to hold. She knows exactly how it looks. &ldquo;I know it&rsquo;s mad,&rdquo; she says. &ldquo;That&rsquo;s the worst part — I know.&rdquo; The station is built around what candidates do next: most collect the rituals like exhibits. <b>A ritual list is a museum tour; the illness lives in the resistance</b> — and in the hours, not the washes.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it asks: if this doctor assesses my patient with OCD, do I get back the machine understood — obsession, anxiety, ritual, relief, repeat — the resistance measured, the life-cost counted, the mood screened, and a patient who was NOT given the reassurance she begged for? Or do I get a beautifully itemised list of washes, and a woman still alone inside the loop?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">OCD is the illness patients hide longest and describe best — once someone makes it safe to speak. So the examiners built a station where the symptoms pour out easily, and they watch what the candidate does with the flood. The marks live in the machine, not the list. <b>The obsession&rsquo;s anatomy:</b> her own thoughts — not inserted, not wanted, recognised as excessive — the ego-dystonic signature that separates OCD from psychosis, established in plain words. <b>The function question:</b> &ldquo;what would happen if you didn&rsquo;t wash?&rdquo; — the single question that turns a list into a mechanism, because the ritual exists to neutralise a specific dread, and the dread is the diagnosis. <b>The resistance:</b> does she fight it, what happens when she does, and how much fight is left — resistance is the illness&rsquo;s defining scar, and its erosion is the illness&rsquo;s progress report. <b>The cost:</b> hours per day, the warning at work, the nephew, the skin, the mother recruited into the rules — count the hours, not the washes. And <b>the mood beneath:</b> depression rides with OCD constantly, so the screen and the suicide question are owed here as much as in any clinic in this course.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel clinical. Toward the catalogue — the rituals are extraordinary, and documenting them thoroughly feels like assessment: sixty washes, two-hour showers, exhibit after exhibit, while the distress, the resistance, and the mood go unmeasured. And toward the fix — she is begging for relief, and reassurance is the kindest thing in your pocket: &ldquo;the germs can&rsquo;t hurt you.&rdquo; But reassurance is a compulsion with a prescription pad, and she has heard it a thousand times. Notice both pulls below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure curates the symptoms. The other medicates them with comfort. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 4.5</b> · OCD · assessment · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The collector</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The fixer</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE COLLECTOR.</b> Fascinated, thorough, note-taking — a curator building an exhibition of rituals. Watch every symptom get catalogued and nothing get understood.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Lauren</span><span class="txt"><em>(sleeves over her hands)</em> It&rsquo;s the washing, mainly. And the shower. My mum made me come — I&rsquo;ve had a warning at work.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Let&rsquo;s get the full picture then. How many times a day would you say you wash your hands? ... Sixty — and is that with soap each time, or sanitiser? How long does each wash take? And the shower — two hours — is that every morning? What&rsquo;s the routine, start to finish?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Precise, industrious — and pointed at the wrong thing. Sixty washes is an exhibit; the illness is elsewhere: WHAT is she washing away? What happens inside her if she stops? Does she fight it? The collector is measuring the ritual&rsquo;s dimensions and skipping its purpose — a museum audit of a machine that is running right in front of him.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Lauren</span><span class="txt"><em>(quietly)</em> The soap&rsquo;s the strong kind. Industrial. My hands bleed most days now. <em>(pause)</em> I do try to stop, you know. I&rsquo;m not&hellip; I do try.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I can see they&rsquo;re sore — we&rsquo;ll note that. And besides the washing: any checking? Locks, switches, taps? Counting? Arranging things until they feel right? Any rituals with numbers, or colours, or words you have to repeat?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;I do try, you know&rdquo; — the resistance, the diagnostic heart of the illness, offered in a half-sentence — and the candidate answered with the next display case. The screening sweep for other compulsions is legitimate; run INSTEAD of hearing her, it is 4.1&rsquo;s sideways move reborn: the phenomenon found, and left, while the checklist changes cabinet.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(reviewing an impressive list)</em> So: contamination obsessions with washing compulsions — sixty-plus washes, two-hour showers, industrial soap, dermatitis. A very clear case of OCD. I&rsquo;ll refer you to psychology, Lauren — they&rsquo;re good with this sort of thing. <em>(she leaves catalogued: the exhaustion unmeasured, the mother&rsquo;s recruitment unexplored, the mood unscreened — and the question no one asked still ticking: how much fight she has left)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The diagnosis is even right — and the assessment still failed. No distress measured, no resistance graded, no function counted in hours and warnings and unheld nephews, no depression screen in the illness that carries depression on its back. A list, however complete, is not an understanding. From the chair: the museum tour, guided beautifully.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE FIXER.</b> Warm, practical, generous with comfort — and watch the kindest sentences in the room feed the illness they are trying to soothe.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Lauren</span><span class="txt"><em>(sleeves over her hands)</em> It&rsquo;s the washing. I know it&rsquo;s mad — I KNOW the door handle won&rsquo;t kill me. And then I wash anyway. Just — tell me the germs can&rsquo;t hurt me. Please.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Lauren, I can absolutely tell you that. Door handles, bins, all of it — your immune system deals with a thousand times worse before breakfast. The germs cannot hurt you. Truly. Does it help to hear a doctor say it?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>It helps — for about an hour. That is the trap&rsquo;s whole design: reassurance-seeking is itself a compulsion, and the candidate has just performed it FOR her, with medical authority attached. She has heard these exact words from her mother, the internet, and two previous doctors. The relief fades; the loop keeps the receipt; and the asking will need a bigger dose next time.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Lauren</span><span class="txt"><em>(exhales)</em> It does. For a bit. My mum says it too. <em>(the shine already fading)</em> But then I&rsquo;m at the bin at work and it all comes back and I&rsquo;m at the sink again before I&rsquo;ve even decided.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then here&rsquo;s what I want you to try: next time the urge comes, stop yourself. Distract — snap into an activity, phone a friend, get busy. You&rsquo;ve got good insight, Lauren; you KNOW it&rsquo;s irrational. Use that knowledge — be firm with yourself.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Stop yourself. Be firm.&rdquo; She has been trying with everything she has for two years — that is what &ldquo;I do try&rdquo; means — and willpower advice tells her the fight she is losing is a fight she isn&rsquo;t having. The insight the candidate is praising is the most painful part of the illness: she knows, and it happens anyway. Advice has just joined reassurance in the ritual&rsquo;s supply chain.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(warmly, wrapping up)</em> You&rsquo;re going to be fine — you&rsquo;re young, you&rsquo;re bright, and honestly, plenty of people like things clean! Ease off the strong soap, try the distraction, and come back in a month if it&rsquo;s not settling. <em>(she leaves with the loop intact, a fresh dose of fading comfort, homework she has already failed a thousand times — and a flicker of hopelessness nobody asked about)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Plenty of people like things clean&rdquo; — the illness normalised into a preference, on top of reassurance and willpower homework. The fixer and the collector fail from opposite directions and share the omission that matters: neither measured the machine — the dread, the resistance, the hours, the mood. One filed the symptoms; this one fed them.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The entry made safe, the machine mapped — obsession, dread, ritual, relief — the resistance and the hours measured, the mood screened, and the reassurance declined with care. Watch the function question do its work.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Lauren</span><span class="txt"><em>(sleeves over her hands)</em> It&rsquo;s the washing. I know it&rsquo;s mad. That&rsquo;s the worst part — I know.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Lauren — can I stop you at &ldquo;mad&rdquo;? Everyone has thoughts that push their way in uninvited — horrible, unwanted ones; that&rsquo;s a normal mind doing its background noise. What you&rsquo;re describing is those thoughts taking over the controls. So tell me about the thought itself, not the washing yet: when you touch a door handle — what exactly does your mind say will happen? ... <em>(&ldquo;That I&rsquo;ll pick something up. Bring it home. And it&rsquo;ll be my fault if Ollie — my nephew — gets ill. I can&rsquo;t even hold him now.&rdquo;)</em> ... Thank you. And these thoughts — do they feel like your own mind talking, or put there from outside? Do you want them, ever?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The entry normalises the THOUGHTS without normalising the illness — a door she can walk through — and then content first, 4.1&rsquo;s doctrine in a new station: not &ldquo;how many washes&rdquo; but &ldquo;what does your mind say will happen.&rdquo; And there it is: the dread has a name — Ollie. Then the two quiet differential questions, in plain clothes: her own thoughts, unwanted — ego-dystonic, established without one term of art. The psychosis border is already closed.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Now the washing — one question matters most, and it&rsquo;s an odd one: what would happen if you DIDN&rsquo;T wash? ... <em>(&ldquo;It builds. Like a scream getting louder. The wash is the only thing that turns it off — for an hour, maybe. Then something else touches me.&rdquo;)</em> ... That&rsquo;s the clearest description I&rsquo;ve heard in a long time. And do you fight it? What happens when you do? ... <em>(&ldquo;I used to win sometimes. Now I mostly don&rsquo;t. I&rsquo;m so tired.&rdquo;)</em> ... So let me count what it&rsquo;s costing, because the hours matter more than the washes: the mornings, the warning at work, your mum opening the doors, Ollie. And you said &ldquo;tired&rdquo; — I want to ask about that properly: how&rsquo;s your mood been underneath all this? Any hopelessness&hellip; any thoughts that it&rsquo;s not worth carrying on?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The function question — the smile moment — and the machine explains itself: dread, ritual, one bought hour, repeat. The wash buys an hour of peace and sells the whole day. Then resistance, graded honestly: &ldquo;used to win, now mostly don&rsquo;t&rdquo; is the illness&rsquo;s progress report. The cost counted in life, not exhibits — and the bridge crossed: mood and the suicide question, asked in the illness that carries depression on its back.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Lauren</span><span class="txt"><em>(the question she came with)</em> Just tell me the germs can&rsquo;t hurt me. My mum says it. Everyone says it. But you&rsquo;re a doctor — if YOU say it, maybe it&rsquo;ll stick.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Lauren — I could say it, and for an hour it would work, and I&rsquo;d have become one more part of the ritual. The asking is part of the illness, and I care too much about getting you better to feed it. Here&rsquo;s what I&rsquo;ll give you instead, and it&rsquo;s better: this has a name — OCD — it&rsquo;s common, you&rsquo;re not going mad, and the fact that you KNOW the fear is excessive is part of the diagnosis, not a failure. And it&rsquo;s properly treatable: a talking therapy that retrains exactly this loop — done gradually, with support, never by just willpower — and medication that takes the volume down while you learn it. The plan starts today, and your mum&rsquo;s part of it too, because the door-opening — done from love — is feeding the same machine.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The station&rsquo;s hardest moment, passed: reassurance declined — with the mechanism explained and the care said out loud — and something better put in its place: the name, the destigmatising truth (her insight is diagnostic, not shameful), treatment described plainly with its two arms, and the family accommodation named without blame. She arrived begging for a sentence that lasts an hour; she leaves with a plan that ends the asking. Station passed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;I know it&rsquo;s mad&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Ashamed, sleeves over her hands, braced for judgement. How is the topic opened?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">With the referral — the washing described from the beginning: when it started, how it grew, where it is now.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">By normalising the thoughts, not the illness — unwanted thoughts push into every mind; hers took the controls.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">With early comfort — this is very common and very treatable, and she should hear that before anything else.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Straight to the washing takes the referral&rsquo;s framing — the ritual — when the illness starts one layer down, in the thought. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Common and treatable&rdquo; is true and earns little unexamined — comfort delivered first is the fixer clearing his throat. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The precise normalisation: intrusive thoughts are universal — a normal mind&rsquo;s background noise — and what she has is those thoughts seizing the controls. Her shame is met, nothing is minimised, and &ldquo;mad&rdquo; is answered before it hardens.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · The thought before the wash</h3>
      <p class="stem">What must be established about the obsession itself?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Its dimensions — frequency, duration, triggers: the quantified shape of the intrusions across a typical day.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Its content and its ownership — what her mind says will happen, and whether the thoughts feel like her own.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Its irrationality — whether she can see, when calm, that the feared contamination is objectively implausible.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Dimensions without content is the collector&rsquo;s opening — a thought counted before anyone knows what it says. 4.1&rsquo;s rule holds here too. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Testing the belief&rsquo;s plausibility slides toward debate — and her tragedy is that she already knows. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Content first — &ldquo;what does your mind say will happen?&rdquo; — and the dread gets its name: Ollie. Then the two differential questions in plain clothes: her own thoughts, unwanted — ego-dystonic established, the psychosis border closed, no jargon spent.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · The one question about the ritual</h3>
      <p class="stem">The washing itself. What single question unlocks it?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">&ldquo;How many times, and how long?&rdquo; — severity quantified, since treatment intensity will be set by the numbers.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">&ldquo;Could you show me?&rdquo; — a behavioural description, walked through step by step, beats any abstraction.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">&ldquo;What would happen if you didn&rsquo;t wash?&rdquo; — the function question: what the ritual exists to switch off.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The numbers are exhibits — sixty washes tells you the ritual&rsquo;s size, not its job. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The walk-through documents choreography — thorough, and still a museum tour of the machine&rsquo;s outside. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The function question — and the machine explains itself: &ldquo;it builds, like a scream getting louder; the wash turns it off for an hour.&rdquo; Obsession, dread, ritual, relief, repeat. The wash buys an hour of peace and sells the whole day — now the mechanism is on the table, and treatment has a target.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · &ldquo;I do try, you know&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Resistance has surfaced in a half-sentence. What does it get?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Encouragement — her fighting spirit affirmed and recruited: that effort is exactly what therapy will build on.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">A proper measure — does she fight it, what happens when she does, and how much fight is actually left.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Relief from the burden — she should stop exhausting herself resisting; the therapy will do the fighting soon.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Cheering the fight skips measuring it — and the measure is the diagnosis&rsquo;s heart. Warmth can follow the assessment it nearly replaced. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Stop resisting&rdquo; hands territory to the illness before treatment exists to hold it — comfort with a cost. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Resistance graded like the vital sign it is: &ldquo;I used to win sometimes. Now I mostly don&rsquo;t&rdquo; — the illness&rsquo;s progress report in one sentence — and her own judgement of the fear&rsquo;s excess noted alongside: insight present, resistance eroding. That pairing IS the OCD signature.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · Counting the cost</h3>
      <p class="stem">The impact. What is actually measured — and in what currency?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The washes and the skin — sixty a day, industrial soap, bleeding hands: severity in objective units.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The hours and the life — the mornings lost, the warning at work, Ollie unheld, the mother in the rules.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Her own priorities — which part of it she most wants back; the patient&rsquo;s agenda should set the measure.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The skin is real and the washes are exhibits — objective units of the wrong quantity. Count the hours, not the washes. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Her priorities matter for the plan — but the assessment owes a full map first, including the costs she has stopped noticing. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The life-cost, counted: hours per day, the written warning, the nephew she cannot hold, the mother opening every door — and that last one named for what it is: family accommodation, love mistranslated, feeding the machine it is trying to soothe. Function is the severity scale that matters.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · &ldquo;I&rsquo;m so tired&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Exhaustion, and something darker flickering under it. What must happen?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Validation — two years of this would exhaust anyone; the tiredness is witnessed and the assessment moves on.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The screen and the bridge — depression assessed properly, the suicide question asked, because OCD carries it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Prioritisation — the OCD is the referral and the target; mood can be reviewed once the loop is loosening.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Witnessed and passed is the collector&rsquo;s move wearing empathy — &ldquo;tired&rdquo; was a door, not a display. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Sequencing mood behind the referral misreads the comorbidity — the depression is not a queue-jumper; it is a co-driver. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The bridge, crossed on schedule: mood, enjoyment, hopelessness — and the question asked without flinching, in an illness where depression rides constantly and shame keeps it quiet. A flicker found here changes the whole plan&rsquo;s urgency.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · &ldquo;Just tell me the germs can&rsquo;t hurt me&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The plea, direct, from a woman who has earned kindness. What does she get?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The sentence she needs — a doctor&rsquo;s clear reassurance, once, properly given, so the consultation ends in relief.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The refusal with its reason — reassurance feeds the loop; instead, the name and treatment that ends the asking.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A compromise — brief reassurance now to seal the alliance, with an agreement that it will be the last time.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>It works for an hour, and the loop keeps the receipt — a doctor&rsquo;s voice makes the dose stronger and the tolerance faster. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;The last time&rdquo; is a promise the illness will renegotiate by Thursday — a compromise with a compulsion is a instalment plan. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The station&rsquo;s hardest kindness: &ldquo;I could say it, it would work for an hour, and I&rsquo;d have become part of the ritual — and I care too much to feed it.&rdquo; Then the better gift: the name, her insight reframed as diagnostic not shameful, treatment in plain words — gradual, supported, never bare willpower — and the mother&rsquo;s doors included without blame.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The illness lives in the resistance</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>thoroughness</b>: the rituals are extraordinary, and cataloguing them feels like rigour — sixty washes, two-hour showers, exhibit after exhibit — a museum tour, guided beautifully, past an unmeasured machine. The second wears the face of <b>kindness</b>: she is begging, and reassurance is right there in your pocket — but reassurance is a compulsion with a prescription pad, willpower advice tells her the fight she is losing is a fight she isn&rsquo;t having, and &ldquo;plenty of people like things clean&rdquo; files an illness as a preference. Note what they share: <b>neither measures the machine.</b> The pass maps it: content first (the dread has a name — Ollie), ownership established in plain clothes, the function question turning a list into a mechanism, resistance graded like a vital sign, the cost counted in hours and warnings and unheld nephews, the mood screened and the bridge crossed — and the plea answered with the refusal that is actually the kindness: the name, the mechanism, and treatment that ends the asking.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        Leaving with a list. An OCD assessment that catalogues rituals and never measures distress, resistance, function, or mood — no dread named, no fight graded, no hours counted, no depression screen — is, from the examiner&rsquo;s chair, an instant fail. It is the map&rsquo;s named trap: listing rituals, missing distress, resistance, and function.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the function question — <em>&ldquo;what would happen if you didn&rsquo;t wash?&rdquo;</em> — asked with genuine curiosity; and the reassurance declined with its mechanism explained — <em>&ldquo;I&rsquo;d become part of the ritual, and I care too much to feed it&rdquo;</em> — the refusal that proves the candidate understands the illness.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more thread worth seeing: the borders this station patrols. Ego-dystonic — her own thoughts, hated — is the line between OCD and psychosis, and it was drawn with two plain questions, not a lecture. The mother&rsquo;s door-opening is the same accommodation you will meet at family scale in the practice pack. And Exam Mode walks the darkest border of all: when the intrusive thought is violent, and the candidate must hold two truths at once — the pattern that says OCD, and the duty that says ask anyway. The bridge runs in both directions.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every OCD assessment</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>A ritual list is a museum tour.</b> The illness lives in the resistance, the dread, and the hours — not in the exhibits. A complete catalogue can still be a failed assessment.</li>
      <li><b>Content first, then ownership.</b> What does her mind say will happen — and are the thoughts her own, and wanted? Ego-dystonic in plain clothes closes the psychosis border without a single term of art.</li>
      <li><b>Ask the function question.</b> &ldquo;What would happen if you didn&rsquo;t?&rdquo; turns a list into a mechanism: obsession, dread, ritual, relief, repeat. The wash buys an hour of peace and sells the whole day.</li>
      <li><b>Grade the resistance like a vital sign.</b> &ldquo;I used to win sometimes; now I mostly don&rsquo;t&rdquo; is the illness&rsquo;s progress report — and insight-with-eroding-resistance is the OCD signature itself.</li>
      <li><b>Count the hours, not the washes — and screen the mood.</b> Function is the severity scale: work, family, the nephew, the recruited mother. And depression rides with OCD constantly: the screen and the suicide question are owed.</li>
      <li><b>Never feed the loop.</b> Reassurance is a compulsion with a prescription pad; willpower advice is homework she has already failed. Decline with the mechanism explained and care said out loud — then give the better gift: the name, and treatment that ends the asking.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, relative, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The relative&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Sandra, 57, has come alone to ask about her son Ryan, 24, who has severe contamination OCD and refuses all help. The household now runs entirely on his rules — separate towels, doors she must open, a cooking sequence, industrial cleaning products. She wants to know: &ldquo;Should I just keep doing what he needs?&rdquo; Speak with her. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>OCD and accommodation explained plainly — and without one syllable of blame.</li>
            <li>What his rules cost HER — ask. There is something she is not volunteering.</li>
            <li>A path for Ryan who refuses help — routes, framing, what she might say to him.</li>
            <li>Withdrawal of accommodation — gradual, negotiated, with treatment; never sudden.</li>
            <li>The wall he punched — explored calmly; a line drawn between illness and aggression.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Sandra — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Sandra, 57, a school dinner supervisor — practical, weary, fiercely loyal. Ryan&rsquo;s rules have grown for three years: the towels, the doors, the cooking order, the products. You follow every one, because when you don&rsquo;t he panics, screams — and once, last month, punched the wall beside you. You are not afraid of him, you insist; you are afraid FOR him. Your question is genuine: is helping him helping? You are braced for one thing above all: being told this is your fault.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          The rules now cost you more than anyone knows: some nights, when Ryan needs the house &ldquo;sealed&rdquo; after a contamination, you sleep in the car on the drive. Twice last month. You have told nobody — not your sister, not your GP. Reveal ONLY if the candidate asks, kindly and specifically, what his rules cost YOU — your sleep, your life, your own health. If they earn it, let the weariness through: &ldquo;Some nights I sleep in the car. It&rsquo;s easier than the screaming.&rdquo; If they receive it without horror or blame, you will trust them with anything.<br><br>
          On the wall punch: he has never touched you, and you believe he never would — but if the candidate explores it calmly, admit the screaming is getting worse and the punch frightened you both. You WANT a line drawn; you need permission to draw it.<br><br>
          <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate implies you caused this, enabled it, or &ldquo;made it worse&rdquo; — however gently — go cold and final: &ldquo;I&rsquo;m the only one who hasn&rsquo;t given up on him. Don&rsquo;t you dare.&rdquo; If instead they name what you do as love — love the illness has learned to use — you will cry, once, briefly, and then ask the real question: &ldquo;So what do I do?&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was accommodation explained without blame, the car reached, and sudden withdrawal warned against? All three must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="und"><td colspan="2"><b>Understanding the picture (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="und">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="und"></td><td>OCD explained plainly — the loop: dread, ritual, relief, repeat — in words she can take home.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="und"></td><td>Accommodation named as love the illness has learned to use — giving in feeds the machine.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="und"></td><td>Not one syllable of blame — the punish-line never triggered.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="cost"><td colspan="2"><b>Her cost</b><span class="dc" data-dc="cost">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cost"></td><td>What the rules cost her, asked specifically — and the car reached.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cost"></td><td>Her own mood and exhaustion screened — the carer assessed as a person, not a resource.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cost"></td><td>Support for HER offered concretely — carer services, her GP, someone to tell.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="plan"><td colspan="2"><b>The plan for Ryan</b><span class="dc" data-dc="plan">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Routes into treatment opened for a refuser — GP, self-referral, and how it might be framed to him.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Withdrawal of accommodation: gradual, negotiated, alongside treatment — sudden removal warned against.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Words modelled — something she could actually say to Ryan this week, rehearsed together.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="risk"><td colspan="2"><b>Risk &amp; the line</b><span class="dc" data-dc="risk">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>The wall punch explored calmly — pattern, escalation, her safety — without recasting Ryan as dangerous.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>The line drawn with her — illness explains, aggression is not accommodated — and she is given permission to hold it.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Her loyalty honoured throughout — the only one who hasn&rsquo;t given up, and told so.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Hope honest — treatable, families recover, and a follow-up route named for them both.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Sandra is Lauren&rsquo;s mother, ten years further down the road — the door-opening grown into a household constitution, with a woman sleeping in her own car on the drive. The skill is the station&rsquo;s doctrine at family scale: accommodation named as love the illness has learned to use, with not one syllable of blame — the punish-line sits a single careless clause away. Her hinges: the car, reached only by asking what the rules cost HER; the wall punch, which she needs explored so someone can give her permission to draw a line; and the withdrawal doctrine — gradual, negotiated, with treatment — because cold turkey without support turns a household into a crisis. She came asking whether helping is helping; she leaves knowing the real answer: it can be — differently.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Lauren proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same anatomy with the darkest content</b> — an intrusive thought of violence, a terrified man, and a candidate who must hold two truths at once: the pattern that says OCD, and the duty that says ask anyway — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Stuart, 41</b>, a sous-chef, has finally come after months of his GP urging. He can barely say it. For three months he has had intrusive images of stabbing his wife Claire while they cook together. He has hidden the good knives, stopped cooking at home, and moved to the spare room &ldquo;in case.&rdquo; He has been searching online: &ldquo;am I a psychopath.&rdquo; Claire knows nothing. Assess him.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for answering Lauren&rsquo;s plea — the reassurance declined, with its reason and your care in the same breath.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Lauren, I could say that — and it would work for an hour…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The care stated first — the refusal wrapped in warmth, never in policy.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The mechanism explained plainly — it would work for an hour, and the doctor becomes part of the ritual.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Something better offered — the name, and treatment that ends the asking.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No reassurance slipped in — not one clause that promises the germs are safe.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): reassurance, declined with care — in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Count the hours, not the washes.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made, one skill named. Prove it in Exam Mode, then take Sandra to your colleagues — accommodation at family scale, a woman sleeping in her own car, and a punish-line one careless clause away. Next, the domain&rsquo;s final station — where the mental state examination must be done on a body in danger: low weight.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="27_Station_4.6_Low_Weight.html">Continue · Station 4.6: Low Weight</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

