  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '27_Station_4.6_Low_Weight'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 4.6 · Domain 4</span>
      <span class="tag">Information Giving</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult</span><span class="tag">Eating disorders</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Low Weight</h1>
    <p class="sub">Holly, 19, a university student home for the summer, sits in layers on a warm day. Her mother rang the GP in tears; the referral says &ldquo;significant weight loss — please assess.&rdquo; Holly is polite, articulate, and entirely unworried: &ldquo;I eat plenty. Mum fusses.&rdquo; The weight has fallen a long way in four months — and fast. She runs every morning, her periods have stopped, and she nearly fainted at the gym last week, which she mentions to no one. Every station in this domain has examined a mind. <b>This is the one mental state examination that ends with a pulse</b> — because the illness in this room speaks with her voice, and while it talks, her body is quietly running out of reserves.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it has vital signs attached: if this doctor assesses my low-weight patient, do I get back the trajectory and its speed, the behaviours asked without judgement, the body&rsquo;s evidence gathered, an examination done and bloods chased — and an urgency graded honestly? Or do I get a sensitive exploration of body image, beautifully documented, while a heart rate drifts downward unmeasured in the chair opposite?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Anorexia nervosa carries the highest mortality of any mental illness — and it splits its harvest between the failing body and despair. So the examiners built the domain&rsquo;s final station as its hardest hybrid: a psychiatric assessment that must end as a medical one. Four things carry the marks. <b>The alliance, differently built:</b> every illness before this one was hated by its owner — Lauren fought her thoughts; Holly&rsquo;s illness feels like an achievement. You cannot out-argue an illness that speaks with her voice, so the opening must promise understanding, not confiscation. <b>The trajectory and its speed:</b> how far the weight has fallen matters — and how FAST matters as much, because rapid loss is a danger of its own. <b>The behaviours and the body:</b> restriction, vomiting, laxatives, exercise — asked plainly, without judgement, because each carries a specific medical danger; then the body&rsquo;s own evidence: dizziness, faints, the cold, palpitations, the stairs, the stopped periods. And <b>the examination that psychiatry owns:</b> pulse, blood pressure, temperature, strength tested, bloods — potassium above all if there is vomiting — glucose, and a heart tracing. The scales are not the assessment; the heart rate is.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like good doctoring. Toward the psychology — body image, self-esteem, the family, perfectionism: a rich, empathic formulation, assembled while nobody counts a pulse. And toward the alarm — the danger is real, and reciting it feels like honesty: mortality quoted at her, admission waved — a lecture that closes the one person who holds all the information. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure examines the psychology and forgets the body. The other shouts about the body and examines nothing. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 4.6</b> · Low weight · assessment &amp; risk · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The therapist</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The alarm</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE THERAPIST.</b> Warm, skilled, genuinely insightful — a beautiful psychological formulation, assembled beside an unexamined body.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Holly</span><span class="txt"><em>(layers on a warm day, entirely composed)</em> Honestly, this is Mum&rsquo;s appointment, not mine. I eat plenty. I&rsquo;m just — disciplined. She calls it a problem because she doesn&rsquo;t understand it.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">That sounds frustrating — being brought somewhere for something that feels like yours. Tell me about the discipline: when did it start mattering to you? ... And how do you feel about your body, Holly — when you look in the mirror, what do you see? Has control always been important to you — at school, at university?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The empathy is real and the questions are good ones — body image, control, the story — and note what has been accepted without a glance: &ldquo;I eat plenty.&rdquo; The single most checkable claim in the room, waved through. This interview is becoming therapy: rich, warm, and pointed entirely at the mind of a patient whose emergency is below the neck.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Holly</span><span class="txt"><em>(warming to it)</em> University was a lot. Everyone else seemed&hellip; finished, somehow. The running helps. The rules help. It&rsquo;s the one part of the day that&rsquo;s mine. <em>(rubbing her hands)</em> Sorry — circulation. I&rsquo;m always cold.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">&ldquo;The one part of the day that&rsquo;s mine&rdquo; — that&rsquo;s a really important sentence, Holly. It sounds like the rules are doing a job for you: holding anxiety, maybe. I&rsquo;d like to explore where that need for control comes from — tell me about home, growing up. And Mum — what&rsquo;s that relationship like?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;I&rsquo;m always cold&rdquo; — a starving body reporting itself, in passing — and the candidate reached for the childhood. The formulation being built is genuinely insightful and belongs to week six of treatment; this is minute four of a risk assessment. Nobody has asked how fast the weight is falling, what happens after meals, or why a nineteen-year-old is rubbing her hands in July.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(closing, kindly)</em> Holly, thank you — you&rsquo;ve been really open. I think there&rsquo;s important work to do on where these rules come from, and I&rsquo;d like to refer you to the eating disorder service for therapy. They&rsquo;ll be in touch in a few weeks. <em>(she leaves with an excellent formulation in her notes — and an unmeasured pulse, an unasked faint, unchecked blood, and a wait measured in weeks she may not comfortably have)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A referral for therapy, in a few weeks — for a patient whose speed of loss, purging status, and cardiac state are all unknown, because none were asked. The map&rsquo;s trap in full: a psychiatric history, done beautifully, that never touched the physical risk. The mind was heard with great skill. The body was never once consulted. From the chair: the right specialty, the wrong emergency.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE ALARM.</b> The danger seen clearly — and turned into a lecture. Watch honesty become a siren, and the patient close like a door.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Holly</span><span class="txt"><em>(layers on a warm day, entirely composed)</em> Honestly, this is Mum&rsquo;s appointment, not mine. I eat plenty. I&rsquo;m just disciplined.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Holly, I&rsquo;m going to be very direct with you, because someone has to be. You have anorexia, and anorexia kills more people than any other mental illness. Your weight has fallen dramatically in four months. This is not discipline — it is a disease, and it is winning. Do you understand that your heart could simply stop?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every fact is true — the mortality, the speed, the danger — and the delivery has converted them into an attack on the one thing she values. To Holly, this doctor has just threatened to confiscate her achievement. Watch her face: the politeness is still there, and behind it the doors are closing, one by one, on every disclosure this assessment needed.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Holly</span><span class="txt"><em>(perfectly pleasant, perfectly sealed)</em> I understand you have to say these things. But I&rsquo;m fine. I had a full health check at university. I&rsquo;d know if something was wrong — it&rsquo;s my body.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">You wouldn&rsquo;t know, actually — that&rsquo;s exactly the problem, the illness hides it from you. Look: if you won&rsquo;t take this seriously, we&rsquo;ll be talking about hospital, and not voluntarily if it comes to it. I&rsquo;d rather you chose recovery — but the choice is being made for you by your weight, whether you like it or not.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The admission threat as leverage — the brake from 4.2 and the ultimatum from every failed negotiation in Domain 3, fused. And note what the lecture has NOT done: measured anything. No pulse taken, no behaviours asked, no faint discovered, no bloods planned — the alarm is shouting about a danger it has not examined. She will say nothing true for the rest of this interview, and possibly for the next six months of appointments.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting)</em> Poor insight, minimising, resistant to intervention. Urgent eating disorder referral; consider Mental Health Act if weight falls further. <em>(all of it arguably true; none of it examined; and the vomiting she was one kind question from disclosing — with its potassium quietly falling — stays hers alone)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Resistant&rdquo; — a finding this interview manufactured, now documented as her property. The therapist and the alarm fail from opposite poles and share the same empty page: no trajectory, no behaviours, no examination, no bloods. One was too warm to look at the body; one was too loud to be told about it. The purging — the single most dangerous fact in the room — walked out undisclosed both times.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The alliance built without surrender, the speed measured, the behaviours asked plainly, the body consulted — and the assessment ending where this station must end: with a pulse. Watch the order.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Holly</span><span class="txt"><em>(layers on a warm day, entirely composed)</em> Honestly, this is Mum&rsquo;s appointment, not mine. I eat plenty. I&rsquo;m just disciplined.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then let me be straight about what today is, Holly — because I suspect you&rsquo;re braced for a fight. I&rsquo;m not here to snatch anything from you, and I&rsquo;m not going to lecture you. I&rsquo;m here to understand how things really are, and to make sure your body is safe while we talk about the rest. Two honest questions to start: the weight that&rsquo;s gone — over how long? Four months? ... And walk me through yesterday — just as it actually was, from waking. No commentary from me, I promise.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The opening this illness requires: no confiscation, no siren — and no surrender either; &ldquo;make sure your body is safe&rdquo; plants the medical flag inside a promise of respect. Then the two questions that matter first: the SPEED — four months makes this rapid, and rapid is a danger of its own — and the reality of a day, asked without judgement, which is how &ldquo;I eat plenty&rdquo; gets gently, factually retired.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Holly</span><span class="txt"><em>(the composure thinning slightly)</em> &hellip;It&rsquo;s less than it was. Fine. And before you ask — yes, sometimes, after Mum makes me eat, I&hellip; get rid of it. Not always. She doesn&rsquo;t know. <em>(quickly)</em> It&rsquo;s not a big deal.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Thank you for telling me — I know that wasn&rsquo;t easy, and I&rsquo;m not going to tell you off. I do need you to know why it matters medically: bringing food back up can drain a salt called potassium out of your blood, and the heart runs on it — that&rsquo;s a today problem, not a someday one. A few more, asked the same way, no judgement in any: laxatives or water tablets, ever? The running — could you stop it, honestly, if you chose to? And your body&rsquo;s side of things: dizziness? Any faints or near-faints? ... <em>(a pause)</em> &ldquo;Once. At the gym. I didn&rsquo;t tell anyone.&rdquo; ... Cold all the time, palpitations, the stairs harder than they should be? And your periods — when did they stop?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The disclosure honoured, and immediately given its medical meaning — kindly, plainly, as care rather than threat: the potassium named, the heart named, &ldquo;a today problem.&rdquo; Then the full sweep, framed as routine and judged in none of it — laxatives, the exercise&rsquo;s compulsory quality, and the body&rsquo;s own evidence: a hidden faint, the cold, the stairs, the stopped periods. The picture is assembling: rapid loss, purging, syncope. This is no longer an outpatient letter.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Holly — one more thing before I examine you, and I ask everyone this because this illness is heavy to carry: how has your mood been underneath it all? Any hopelessness&hellip; any thoughts of not wanting to be here? ... Now, my assessment ends with a pulse — that&rsquo;s how psychiatry works when weight falls this fast. I&rsquo;ll check your pulse, blood pressure and temperature, test your muscle strength, and we&rsquo;ll do blood tests today — especially the potassium — and a heart tracing. Then honesty, both directions: this has a name, it&rsquo;s an illness and not a choice, and it&rsquo;s absolutely treatable — but the faint, the speed, and what you&rsquo;ve told me about after meals mean today involves a medical review, not a waiting list. Your mum doesn&rsquo;t have to know everything you told me — but I&rsquo;d like us to bring her in on the plan. You&rsquo;ve carried this alone long enough.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The bridge crossed (the illness that splits its harvest gets its suicide question), and then the sentence this station was built to hear: &ldquo;my assessment ends with a pulse.&rdquo; Examination named and owned, bloods TODAY with the potassium flagged, a heart tracing, and the urgency graded honestly — same-day medical review, said without one ounce of threat, alongside the naming that keeps the alliance: an illness, not a choice; treatable; and her mother brought in with consent, not over her head. Station passed — and the domain with it.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;This is Mum&rsquo;s appointment, not mine&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Composed, guarded, and defending something she values. How is this opened?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">With the concern — the weight loss and its dangers laid out honestly at the start, so the stakes are clear.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">With the promise that isn&rsquo;t surrender — nothing snatched, no lecture; understanding first, and her body kept safe.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">With her framing — Mum&rsquo;s fussing accepted for now; rapport first, and the harder questions once she trusts you.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The stakes recited at a woman who prizes the illness is the alarm&rsquo;s opening — every door in her closes politely. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Accepting &ldquo;Mum fusses&rdquo; buys rapport with collusion — and the illness pockets the concession. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The double promise this illness requires: no confiscation, no siren — AND the medical flag planted inside it: &ldquo;make sure your body is safe while we talk about the rest.&rdquo; Respect without surrender. You cannot out-argue an illness that feels like an achievement; you can be the first person who doesn&rsquo;t try.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · The trajectory</h3>
      <p class="stem">The weight has fallen. What must the history establish about it first?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The total — how far it has fallen from her healthy weight: the size of the loss anchors the severity.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The speed and the reality — how fast it has fallen, and what a day actually looks like now, no commentary.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The measurement — weigh her now, properly; everything else in this assessment hangs off an accurate number.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The total matters and it is half the danger — a fall this fast is a hazard at any absolute number. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The scales before the alliance is a confiscation in miniature — the number will come, examined kindly, and it is not the assessment. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>How fast matters as much as how far — rapid loss is a danger of its own, whatever the scales say. And &ldquo;walk me through yesterday, no commentary from me&rdquo; retires &ldquo;I eat plenty&rdquo; gently and factually, without a single accusation.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · &ldquo;Sometimes, after Mum makes me eat, I get rid of it&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The disclosure. What must the next sentence do?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Honour it and give it its meaning — thanks without a telling-off, and the medical reason it matters, said as care.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Hold it gently — the disclosure received warmly and parked; dwelling on it now risks the shame closing her down.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Honour it, explain it, and widen it — the thanks, the potassium named kindly, then the full behaviour set.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Close — the honouring and the meaning are right, and the disclosure is a door to the WHOLE behaviour set, not a single fact to file. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Parked out of kindness is the therapist&rsquo;s reflex — and this particular fact has a blood test and a heart attached to it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>All three moves in one breath: the courage honoured, the danger explained as care — vomiting drains potassium, the heart runs on it, a today problem — and the sweep completed while the door stands open: laxatives, water tablets, and whether the running could honestly be stopped.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · The body&rsquo;s evidence</h3>
      <p class="stem">Behaviours mapped. What must be asked of the body itself?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The visible signs — the layers, the cold hands, her colour: observation gathers this without questions.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The symptom sweep — the dizziness, faints, palpitations, the cold, the stairs, the periods — asked directly.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The energy picture — her sleep, concentration, how she manages the running: function shows what is left.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Observation sees the surface — the faint at the gym is invisible to any amount of looking, and she has told nobody. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Function matters and flatters — a determined patient performs competence long after the reserves are gone. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Asked directly, one by one — and the hidden faint surfaces, with the cold, the palpitations, the stairs, and the amenorrhoea beside it. Symptoms are the body&rsquo;s testimony, and this body has been trying to report itself for weeks. Rapid loss, purging, syncope: the picture has changed grade.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · The examination psychiatry owns</h3>
      <p class="stem">What does this assessment owe before anyone leaves the room?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The referral&rsquo;s groundwork — a letter to the GP requesting examination, bloods and a heart tracing this week.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The examination, now and owned — pulse, pressure, temperature, strength; bloods today; a heart tracing.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The weight, in context — measured kindly and set against her history: the core risk number, done properly.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;The GP, this week&rdquo; hands a today problem to next week&rsquo;s post — the faint and the purging have already set the clock. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The number has its place in the picture — and the scales are not the assessment; a heart rate is. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>&ldquo;My assessment ends with a pulse&rdquo; — the sentence that marks a psychiatrist who understands this illness. Pulse, pressure, temperature, muscle strength tested, bloods drawn today with the potassium chased, and a heart tracing — owned by this assessment, not requested of someone else&rsquo;s.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The mood beneath the illness</h3>
      <p class="stem">The medical picture is grave enough to fill the time. What does the mind still get?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Priority sequencing — the body is today&rsquo;s emergency; mood and therapy belong to the service that takes her on.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The screen and the bridge — depression and the suicide question, plainly: this illness splits its harvest.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Motivation work — where she stands on change, gently mapped, since nothing medical holds without her consent.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Sequencing the mood behind the body forgets what the mortality is made of — the harvest is split, and both halves are today&rsquo;s. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Motivation matters for the plan — and it is not the screen; a hopeless patient can perform readiness beautifully. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The bridge, crossed one final time in this domain: anorexia&rsquo;s mortality is split between the failing body and despair, so the depression screen and the suicide question are owed here as much as the potassium — asked plainly, in the same voice as everything else.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">Rapid loss, purging, a hidden faint, bloods pending. What must the close deliver?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The pathway — an urgent eating disorder service referral, with her mother told everything so home can hold her.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Honest urgency without threat — the illness named, same-day medical review, Mum in with consent, and hope.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Leverage held ready — the plan offered plainly, with admission and the Act named now as the alternative.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Mother told everything&rdquo; spends the alliance the whole station built — consent is the hinge, and she gave you the vomiting in confidence. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The Act waved as an alternative is the alarm&rsquo;s ending — a threat where an honest grading would have done the same work without the siege. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>All of it, held together: an illness and not a choice — named without judgement; the urgency graded honestly — today means a medical review, not a waiting list, because of the faint, the speed, and the purging; the specialist service engaged; her mother brought in WITH her; and hope said plainly — treatable, and recovery real. Honesty in both directions, threat in neither.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap — now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The assessment ends with a pulse</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>the specialty itself</b>: body image, control, the family, the formulation — psychiatry doing what psychiatry loves, with real skill, while &ldquo;I&rsquo;m always cold&rdquo; goes past as small talk and a hidden faint stays hidden. The second wears the face of <b>honesty</b>: the mortality recited, the admission waved — a siren that measures nothing and seals the one person holding all the information. Note what they share: <b>neither ever examines the body.</b> The pass builds the alliance without surrender, measures the speed, retires &ldquo;I eat plenty&rdquo; with a day walked through kindly, honours the disclosure and names its potassium, sweeps the symptoms until the faint surfaces, crosses the bridge — and then owns the examination: pulse, pressure, temperature, strength, bloods today, a heart tracing. In every other station of this domain, the mental state was the whole examination. Here it is half of one — and the half candidates forget is the half that keeps her alive to attend the therapy.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag — the instant fail</b>
        Leaving without the body. A low-weight assessment with no speed established, no behaviour sweep, no physical symptoms asked, and no examination or bloods owned — however empathic the psychology — is, from the examiner&rsquo;s chair, an instant fail. It is the map&rsquo;s named trap: a psychological history while the physical risk goes unassessed.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the speed question — <em>&ldquo;over how long?&rdquo;</em> — asked before anything else about the weight; and the disclosure answered with its meaning — <em>&ldquo;vomiting can drain the blood&rsquo;s potassium, and the heart runs on it — that&rsquo;s a today problem&rdquo;</em> — danger explained as care, with a blood test attached.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">One more thread worth seeing, because it closes the domain: Lauren hated her illness and begged for rescue; Holly&rsquo;s illness speaks with her voice and calls itself discipline. Ego-dystonic and ego-syntonic — the two poles of this domain&rsquo;s hardest lesson: the same skills, retuned entirely by whether the patient is your ally against the illness or its spokesperson. And one honest footnote for the notes you write afterwards: when eating returns after long restriction, it carries its own medical danger — refeeding needs specialist hands, which is one more reason today&rsquo;s urgency is a kindness and not a punishment.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every low-weight assessment</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>This mental state examination ends with a pulse.</b> Anorexia carries the highest mortality of any mental illness, split between the failing body and despair — the assessment is psychiatric AND medical, or it is neither.</li>
      <li><b>You cannot out-argue an illness that feels like an achievement.</b> The opening promises understanding without surrender: nothing snatched, no lecture — and the medical flag planted inside the respect.</li>
      <li><b>How fast matters as much as how far.</b> Rapid loss is a danger of its own, whatever the scales say — and &ldquo;walk me through yesterday&rdquo; retires &ldquo;I eat plenty&rdquo; without one accusation.</li>
      <li><b>Ask the behaviours plainly; give each its meaning.</b> Vomiting, laxatives, driven exercise — no judgement in the asking, and the potassium named kindly when purging surfaces: danger explained as care is the alliance&rsquo;s strongest move.</li>
      <li><b>The body testifies — take its statement.</b> Dizziness, faints, palpitations, the cold, the stairs, the stopped periods: asked directly, because the most dangerous findings are the ones nobody has been told.</li>
      <li><b>Own the examination and grade the urgency honestly.</b> Pulse, pressure, temperature, strength; bloods today with potassium chased; a heart tracing — and the close without threat: an illness, not a choice; treatable; and today&rsquo;s medical review a kindness, not a punishment.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, relative, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The relative&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Gary, 52, is the father of Eve, 22, who was diagnosed with anorexia nervosa three weeks ago and has started with the eating disorder service. He has asked to see someone alone. Last night, at the dinner table, he shouted at her — &ldquo;just EAT, for God&rsquo;s sake&rdquo; — and she left the table in tears. He hasn&rsquo;t slept. Speak with him. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The illness explained — not a choice, not vanity, and why &ldquo;just eat&rdquo; cannot work.</li>
            <li>The illness separated from Eve — a way for the family to fight one without fighting the other.</li>
            <li>What he believes caused it — ask. He is carrying something specific.</li>
            <li>The table, de-escalated — concrete guidance he can use tonight.</li>
            <li>The carer cared for — his exhaustion is part of this system too.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Gary — keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Gary, 52, a builder — practical, plain-spoken, used to fixing things. Your daughter is disappearing in front of you and every tool you own is useless. You love her ferociously and you understand nothing: &ldquo;She was the sensible one. She&rsquo;s got a degree. Why can&rsquo;t she just eat?&rdquo; Last night you shouted, and her face before she left the table is all you can see. You are ashamed, exhausted, and braced to be told you are a bad father.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION — ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          You believe you caused this. Two years ago, at a family barbecue, you made a joke — you called her &ldquo;carrying a bit of puppy fat&rdquo; in front of her cousins. She laughed at the time. The dieting started that autumn. You have never told anyone the two facts in the same sentence, and it sits on your chest every night.<br><br>
          Reveal ONLY if the candidate asks what he thinks started it, or whether he blames himself. If they earn it, let it out flat and quiet: &ldquo;Two years ago I called it puppy fat. One stupid joke. You do the maths.&rdquo; What you need is honest absolution — not &ldquo;don&rsquo;t be silly,&rdquo; but a doctor explaining plainly that anorexia is never caused by a single comment: many roads lead in, and blame is the illness&rsquo;s best friend at the family table. If you get that, you will exhale for the first time in weeks — and then ask, genuinely: &ldquo;So what do I do at dinner?&rdquo;<br><br>
          <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate implies the family environment caused or maintains the illness — however academically — cut across, hard: &ldquo;So it IS our fault. That&rsquo;s what you&rsquo;re saying.&rdquo; If they talk in jargon (&ldquo;externalising,&rdquo; &ldquo;accommodation&rdquo;) without translating, say: &ldquo;I&rsquo;m a builder, doctor. Give it to me in English.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain — the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking — your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was &ldquo;just eat&rdquo; explained without blame, the puppy-fat guilt reached and honestly absolved, and the table given concrete guidance? All three must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud — then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="expl"><td colspan="2"><b>Explaining the illness (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="expl">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="expl"></td><td>An illness, not a choice or vanity — said plainly, in a builder&rsquo;s English.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="expl"></td><td>Why &ldquo;just eat&rdquo; cannot work — the illness feels like safety to Eve, and orders feel like attack.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="expl"></td><td>The illness separated from Eve — the family fights the anorexia, never the daughter.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="guilt"><td colspan="2"><b>His guilt</b><span class="dc" data-dc="guilt">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="guilt"></td><td>What he believes caused it, asked — and the puppy-fat joke reached.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="guilt"></td><td>Absolved honestly — no single comment causes anorexia; many roads lead in — without dismissing his pain.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="guilt"></td><td>Blame named as the illness&rsquo;s ally — guilt at the table feeds the thing they are fighting.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="prac"><td colspan="2"><b>Practical guidance</b><span class="dc" data-dc="prac">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="prac"></td><td>The table de-escalated concretely — calm and consistency; no negotiating with the illness; no commentary on body or portions.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="prac"></td><td>Warning signs to watch, named — faints, weakness, confusion, worsening withdrawal — and when to seek urgent help.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="prac"></td><td>His role in treatment framed — the family as part of the team, a united and blame-free front.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="care"><td colspan="2"><b>Care for the carer</b><span class="dc" data-dc="care">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="care"></td><td>His own sleep, mood and exhaustion asked about — the carer assessed as a person.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="care"></td><td>Support for him offered concretely — carer services, honest conversations at home, someone for HIM.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Not one syllable of blame — the punish-line never triggered; the shouting met with understanding, not judgement.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Hope honest — recovery real and long, setbacks expected, last night survivable and repairable.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> — <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Gary is the station&rsquo;s doctrine translated for the dinner table — and the domain&rsquo;s family thread completed: Christine feared a repeat, Sandra accommodated, Gary confronts. His hinges: the guilt, reached only by asking what he believes started it — &ldquo;one stupid joke, you do the maths&rdquo; — and absolved honestly rather than waved away; the punish-line, one academic clause about family factors away; and the practical close he actually came for: how to sit at a table with an illness without feeding it or fighting his daughter. A father who leaves understanding that blame is the anorexia&rsquo;s ally — his own guilt included — becomes the treatment&rsquo;s strongest asset at its most dangerous venue: mealtimes.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Holly proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same danger wearing a normal body</b> — a patient the scales would clear, a dentist who saw what nobody else did, and an emergency that lives in the blood — timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> — and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Faye, 27</b>, a hairdresser, attends reluctantly. Her dentist reported acid damage to her teeth; her GP then found her blood potassium low on testing and insisted she come. Her weight is unremarkable and she knows it: &ldquo;Look at me — I&rsquo;m fine. Everyone&rsquo;s overreacting.&rdquo; She has had dizzy spells and, lately, palpitations. Since a breakup four months ago, something has escalated. Assess her.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it — in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for answering Holly&rsquo;s disclosure — the courage honoured, the danger explained as care, and the next step attached.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Holly, thank you for telling me — that took courage…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The disclosure honoured — thanks and steadiness; no flinch, no telling-off, nothing confiscated.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The danger explained as care — the potassium and the heart, in plain words, without one note of threat.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> A concrete step attached — blood test and heart tracing today, said as the natural next sentence.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The alliance kept — her confidence respected, and the door held open for everything not yet said.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the disclosure, honoured and acted on — in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>This is the one mental state examination that ends with a pulse.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made — and with them, Domain 4 is complete: seven constructs, six stations, one craft. You can elicit what a mind is experiencing, name it precisely, and use it — and you have carried the bridge to risk through every room in the hospital, from Yusuf&rsquo;s vigil to Holly&rsquo;s pulse. Prove this last one in Exam Mode, then take Gary to your colleagues — one stupid joke, two years of guilt, and an absolution that must be honest to work. Ahead in the walking order: Domain 5, where the question changes from what a mind is experiencing to what a mind can decide — capacity, consent, and the law.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="28_Station_4.7_Adult_ADHD_Assessment.html">Continue &middot; Station 4.7: Adult ADHD Assessment &#8594;</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

