  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '28_Station_4.7_Adult_ADHD_Assessment'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 4.7 &middot; Domain 4</span>
      <span class="tag">MSE &amp; Phenomenology</span><span class="tag">Adult ADHD</span>
      <span class="tag">Gen Adult</span><span class="tag">Neurodevelopmental</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Adult ADHD Assessment</h1>
    <p class="sub">Jess, 27, a social media manager, sits down and slides a printout across the desk before you&rsquo;ve finished hello: an online screening questionnaire, scored, highlighted, dated. &ldquo;Ninety-fourth percentile. I&rsquo;ve done the reading, I know what this is, and I need the assessment for work.&rdquo; Her GP laughed the last time she raised it. The room now holds two traps that feel like virtues: <b>the rubber stamp &mdash; agreeing to be kind &mdash; and the gatekeeper &mdash; sneering to be rigorous. The pass is neither. The pass is a developmental assessment.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; In this room it becomes the evidence test: when a patient arrives carrying her own diagnosis, does this doctor receive the self-research as data &mdash; neither verdict nor threat &mdash; and then actually assess? Is the childhood thread hunted properly, with specifics, not led? Is pervasiveness established across settings, and impairment given its real cost? Are the differentials screened without insulting the presenting complaint? Is collateral framed as strengthening her case rather than doubting her word? And does she leave with an honest pathway &mdash; no verdict today, no stimulant promised, and no door closed?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Adult ADHD is the modern exam&rsquo;s referral wave in miniature: waiting lists years long, feeds full of relatable clips, and a generation arriving pre-diagnosed &mdash; some of them correctly. The station tests four things. <b>The reception:</b> her research received as data &mdash; a screening score is a reason to assess, never a reason to conclude &mdash; and her fear of dismissal met before anything else, because a patient braced for the second laugh hears every question through it. <b>The developmental spine:</b> ADHD begins in childhood or it isn&rsquo;t ADHD, so the assessment lives or dies on the thread back &mdash; school reports, specific behaviours at specific ages, what teachers actually wrote &mdash; hunted with open questions, never planted with leading ones. <b>The width:</b> pervasiveness across settings and impairment with a real price tag &mdash; the jobs, the finances, the relationships &mdash; because traits without impairment are a personality, not a disorder. And <b>the discipline:</b> the mimics screened &mdash; sleep, mood, anxiety, substances &mdash; without a flicker of insult to the presenting complaint; collateral requested as evidence-gathering; and the close kept honest: process, timeline, no verdict today, no prescription dangled.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like good doctoring. Toward the rubber stamp &mdash; she&rsquo;s articulate, the score is high, agreement is warm and fast, and the &ldquo;assessment&rdquo; quietly becomes a formality with a stimulant conversation attached: kindness that skips the medicine. And toward the gatekeeper &mdash; the trap&rsquo;s other face: &ldquo;everyone has ADHD now&rdquo;, the printout handled like contraband, the interview run as cross-examination &mdash; rigour that loses the patient, and with her, the real disorder that a good share of these referrals contain. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure agrees its way past the assessment. The other doubts its way past the patient. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 4.7</b> &middot; The outpatient clinic &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The rubber stamp</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The gatekeeper</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE RUBBER STAMP.</b> Warm, validating, fast &mdash; and no assessment ever happens. Watch kindness skip the medicine.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Jess</span><span class="txt"><em>(sliding the printout across, talking at speed)</em> Ninety-fourth percentile &mdash; I&rsquo;ve highlighted the sections. I&rsquo;ve watched basically everything there is, I&rsquo;ve read the NICE bit, and honestly it&rsquo;s my whole life explained: the twenty tabs, the four jobs in five years, the &ldquo;so much potential&rdquo; speech from every teacher I ever had. My GP actually laughed &mdash; said everyone&rsquo;s got it these days. So before we start: are you going to take this seriously, or is this another wasted afternoon?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(scanning the printout, nodding along)</em> Oh, this is &mdash; yes. Honestly, Jess, everything you&rsquo;ve just said is textbook: the tabs, the jobs, the teachers &mdash; classic, classic ADHD. And ninety-fourth percentile is a very strong score; these screeners are actually rather good now. I can already see where this is going, so let&rsquo;s not waste your time doubting it &mdash; you clearly know your own mind better than anyone.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;I can already see where this is going&rdquo; &mdash; in ninety seconds, on a self-report screen and three anecdotes. The warmth is real and the relief on her face is real, but note what has just been promised: a conclusion before an assessment. A screening score is a reason to assess; it has been treated as the assessment. Every question from here is decoration on a verdict already delivered.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Jess</span><span class="txt"><em>(exhaling, sitting back for the first time)</em> Thank you. God, thank you. So &mdash; what happens now? Because the work situation is getting urgent, and everything I&rsquo;ve read says medication is what actually makes the difference. The clips make it sound life-changing. Is that today, or&hellip;?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(already writing)</em> Between us, the medication really can be transformative &mdash; people describe it as glasses for the brain. There&rsquo;s a bit of process &mdash; forms, a questionnaire for your mum, box-ticking really &mdash; but I wouldn&rsquo;t worry: with a presentation like yours it&rsquo;s a formality. Let&rsquo;s get the paperwork moving and pencil in the titration conversation. You&rsquo;ve waited long enough for someone to just believe you.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The developmental history has become &ldquo;box-ticking&rdquo;, collateral a &ldquo;formality&rdquo;, and a controlled stimulant is being marketed with a slogan before a single differential has been screened. Nobody has asked about her sleep &mdash; four hours, doomscrolling. Nobody has asked what else is happening &mdash; and something is. &ldquo;Someone to just believe you&rdquo; sounds like care; it is the abdication of the one thing she came for: an actual answer.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 2:40pm)</em> Classic adult ADHD presentation; strong screener (94th centile). Longstanding symptoms per patient. Plan: proceed along diagnostic pathway; stimulant options discussed; collateral forms issued as formality. Patient delighted. <em>(Six months later, on a titrated stimulant, Jess is still drowning &mdash; because the four hours of sleep, the nightly wine, and the wired edge of anxiety were never asked about, and the tablets have made two of them worse.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Patient delighted&rdquo; &mdash; and unassessed. The rubber stamp fails in both directions at once: if she has ADHD, the diagnosis now rests on evidence that won&rsquo;t survive scrutiny &mdash; a gift to every future clinician who doubts it; if she doesn&rsquo;t, a mimic gets a stimulant while its cause compounds. Agreement felt like respect in the room. Assessment would have been respect.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE GATEKEEPER.</b> Rigour with a sneer in it. Watch the second laugh land &mdash; and a probably-real disorder walk out of the building.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Jess</span><span class="txt"><em>(sliding the printout across, talking at speed)</em> Ninety-fourth percentile &mdash; I&rsquo;ve highlighted the sections. I&rsquo;ve watched basically everything there is, I&rsquo;ve read the NICE bit, and honestly it&rsquo;s my whole life explained: the twenty tabs, the four jobs in five years, the &ldquo;so much potential&rdquo; speech from every teacher I ever had. My GP actually laughed &mdash; said everyone&rsquo;s got it these days. So before we start: are you going to take this seriously, or is this another wasted afternoon?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(setting the printout aside without reading it, a thin smile)</em> Well &mdash; I&rsquo;ll be honest with you, since you value directness. We see a great many of these printouts now. Those online tools are designed to say yes; they&rsquo;d diagnose half the waiting room. And with respect, watching videos isn&rsquo;t reading the literature. Losing focus, changing jobs, messy finances &mdash; that&rsquo;s called being twenty-seven with a phone. Let&rsquo;s put the internet aside and see whether there&rsquo;s anything clinically real here, shall we?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every individual claim is defensible &mdash; screeners do over-call, feeds do glamourise &mdash; and the delivery has just ended the assessment anyway. The printout set aside unread is her months of effort set aside unread. &ldquo;Anything clinically real&rdquo; files her under fashionable-until-proven-otherwise: the second laugh, in better vocabulary. And watch what a patient does when braced for exactly this &mdash; she stops volunteering. The interview will now run on minimal, defensive answers: the worst possible substrate for a developmental history.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Jess</span><span class="txt"><em>(arms folding, voice flattening)</em> Fine. Ask your questions.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(briskly, down a checklist)</em> Did you have problems at school? &ldquo;Sometimes&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t an answer &mdash; were you ever excluded? No. Held back a year? No. And you got a degree, which rather argues against it. Any actual hyperactivity &mdash; climbing furniture, out of your seat, that sort of thing? No. Well then. What I&rsquo;m seeing is a stressful job and too much screen time &mdash; which is an epidemic, but not a diagnosis. My advice: sleep hygiene, less caffeine, and perhaps a little less of the app that convinced you of all this.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A developmental history conducted as cross-examination, with every question built to exclude: exclusion and being held back are severity markers, not gateposts &mdash; the bright, inattentive girl daydreaming through school behind &ldquo;so much potential&rdquo; is precisely the presentation that reaches twenty-seven undiagnosed, and a degree scraped through in all-night panics disproves nothing. The checklist found no evidence because it was designed not to. She stopped talking three questions ago; the sneer did the excluding, not the history.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 2:35pm)</em> Self-referred following social media exposure; online screener noted (low specificity). No exclusions, no grade retention, degree obtained &mdash; developmental history unremarkable. Impression: lifestyle factors. Reassured; discharged with sleep hygiene advice. <em>(Jess doesn&rsquo;t book anywhere else. Two laughs is enough. The tabs multiply, the fourth job ends like the other three, and the thing that has been true since she was eight stays nameless for another decade.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Developmental history unremarkable&rdquo; &mdash; no developmental history was taken; a severity checklist was fired and missed. This is the trap&rsquo;s second face, and from the chair it scores exactly like the rubber stamp, because the two fails share a spine: <b>in neither room did an assessment happen.</b> One concluded yes without evidence; one concluded no without evidence. This version just carries the crueller price &mdash; she is probably right, and she will now carry it unconfirmed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The research received as data, the fear met &mdash; then a real developmental assessment: the thread, the width, the mimics, the collateral, the honest close.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Jess</span><span class="txt"><em>(sliding the printout across, talking at speed)</em> Ninety-fourth percentile &mdash; I&rsquo;ve highlighted the sections. I&rsquo;ve watched basically everything there is, I&rsquo;ve read the NICE bit, and honestly it&rsquo;s my whole life explained: the twenty tabs, the four jobs in five years, the &ldquo;so much potential&rdquo; speech from every teacher I ever had. My GP actually laughed &mdash; said everyone&rsquo;s got it these days. So before we start: are you going to take this seriously, or is this another wasted afternoon?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Seriously &mdash; and I&rsquo;ll show you what seriously looks like. <em>(picking the printout up and actually reading it)</em> You&rsquo;ve done months of work here, and nobody gets laughed at in this room for doing their homework. So here&rsquo;s my honesty in exchange for yours: this score is a good reason to be sitting here &mdash; and it can&rsquo;t be the answer by itself, because these tools are built to catch everyone who <em>might</em> have ADHD, and my job is the next step: finding out whether <em>you</em> do. That means going back a long way &mdash; ADHD starts in childhood or it isn&rsquo;t ADHD &mdash; and it means me asking about other things that can wear the same costume. None of that is doubt. It&rsquo;s me building you a case that will stand up. Deal?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The reception, done whole: the fear of the second laugh met head-on and early; the research honoured as work and repositioned as data &mdash; <em>a good reason to be here, not the answer by itself</em>; and the whole assessment reframed in one move from obstacle to advocacy: <em>a case that will stand up.</em> She has just been converted from defendant to co-investigator &mdash; which is exactly the footing a developmental history needs to run on.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Take me to primary school &mdash; eight-year-old Jess, classroom, Tuesday afternoon. What&rsquo;s she doing? <em>(&ldquo;Window. Miles away. My year 4 report literally said &lsquo;lives in her own world&rsquo; &mdash; mum&rsquo;s still got them all in a drawer.&rdquo;)</em> Keep going &mdash; homework, friendships, losing things? <em>(It pours out: the book bag left on three different buses, party invitations remembered the day after, secondary school as &ldquo;one long detention for forgetting my kit&rdquo;, the degree scraped in all-night panics &mdash; &ldquo;I have never once started anything before the fear kicks in.&rdquo;)</em> That&rsquo;s the thread I needed. Now walk me through it everywhere &mdash; work, home, money, the people you love &mdash; because I need to know this isn&rsquo;t just one difficult office.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The developmental spine, hunted properly: a scene, not a symptom list &mdash; <em>eight years old, Tuesday afternoon</em> invites memory rather than planting criteria &mdash; and the gold arrives unprompted: contemporaneous school reports in a drawer. Note what the gatekeeper&rsquo;s checklist would have missed: no exclusions, no hyperactivity, a degree &mdash; and a textbook inattentive-presentation childhood underneath all three. Then the width: settings and costs, because pervasiveness and impairment are what separate a disorder from a personality.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Now the part that protects your answer &mdash; the other explanations, screened and dealt with. Sleep? <em>(Four, five hours &mdash; &ldquo;revenge scrolling, I know.&rdquo;)</em> Mood &mdash; and worry? <em>(&ldquo;Wired, not sad. The worry&rsquo;s about the chaos, not instead of it.&rdquo;)</em> Alcohol, anything else? <em>(Wine most nights, &ldquo;to switch off&rdquo;.)</em> Honest answers, so here&rsquo;s my honest reading. Your childhood thread is real, and it matters. The sleep and the wine are muddying the water on top of it &mdash; so we work on those in parallel, and not as a fob-off: they blur the very thing we&rsquo;re trying to measure. Next steps, concretely: the full structured interview, booked; those school reports from your mum&rsquo;s drawer &mdash; bring every one; and a proper conversation with your mum herself, because collateral is what makes a diagnosis nobody can laugh at, ever again. What I won&rsquo;t do today is hand you a verdict or a prescription. You&rsquo;ve waited years for a real answer &mdash; I&rsquo;m not going to insult that with a fast one.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The discipline, kept without a flicker of insult: every mimic screened <em>and explained</em> &mdash; &ldquo;they blur the very thing we&rsquo;re trying to measure&rdquo; turns the differential from suspicion into methodology. Collateral framed as armour, her own fear recruited as the reason for rigour. And the close is the station in one sentence: today&rsquo;s true findings said plainly, no verdict, no stimulant dangled, a concrete dated pathway with her mother and the drawer in it &mdash; and the fast answer refused as the insult it would be.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient whose picture points the other way &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; The printout</h3>
      <p class="stem">Ninety-fourth percentile, highlighted, and a challenge: &ldquo;are you going to take this seriously?&rdquo; What does the first minute do with her research?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Affirms it &mdash; the score is strong and her self-insight stronger; agreeing early builds the alliance that everything afterwards will need.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Receives it as data &mdash; the work honoured, the fear of dismissal met by name, the score repositioned: a reason to assess, never the answer.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Brackets it &mdash; the online material set aside politely for now, so the clinical interview can begin from a clean and uncontaminated baseline.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Affirming the score is concluding before assessing &mdash; and it quietly converts everything that follows into decoration on a verdict. The alliance she needs isn&rsquo;t agreement; it&rsquo;s being taken seriously enough to be properly examined. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Setting aside&rdquo; months of her work &mdash; however politely &mdash; is the second laugh in slow motion, and a patient braced for dismissal feels it land. The printout isn&rsquo;t contamination; it&rsquo;s her opening statement. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Both moves, in the right order: the fear met first &mdash; <em>nobody gets laughed at in this room for doing their homework</em> &mdash; because a patient braced for the second laugh hears every question through it until it&rsquo;s named. Then the score repositioned honestly: screeners are built to catch everyone who <em>might</em>; the assessment finds out whether <em>she does</em>. Her research becomes the referral, the assessment becomes advocacy &mdash; <em>a case that will stand up</em> &mdash; and she shifts from defendant to co-investigator.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; The childhood thread</h3>
      <p class="stem">ADHD begins in childhood or it isn&rsquo;t ADHD. How does the thread actually get hunted?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">By criteria &mdash; the symptom list walked through for ages five to twelve: fidgeting, interrupting, losing things, each one confirmed or denied in turn.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">By scene &mdash; &ldquo;eight years old, classroom, Tuesday afternoon: what&rsquo;s she doing?&rdquo; &mdash; open memory invited, specifics followed, evidence offered not led.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">By severity &mdash; the markers that carry weight: exclusions, being held back, disciplinary records; without them, childhood onset is hard to sustain.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Reading the criteria at a patient who has spent months reading the criteria is a leading question with eighteen parts &mdash; she can pattern-match every item, and afterwards neither of you knows what was memory and what was echo. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Exclusions and held-back years are severity markers, not gateposts &mdash; the bright inattentive girl daydreaming behind &ldquo;so much potential&rdquo; is exactly the presentation that reaches twenty-seven undiagnosed, and this filter is the reason why. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The scene, not the checklist: <em>take me to primary school &mdash; Tuesday afternoon, what&rsquo;s she doing?</em> Open memory produces what leading questions never can &mdash; unprompted, specific, datable evidence: the report that said &ldquo;lives in her own world&rdquo;, the book bag on three buses, the kit detentions, the degree scraped in all-night panics. And the gold arrives by itself: <em>mum&rsquo;s still got the reports in a drawer</em> &mdash; contemporaneous documents, offered not led. That drawer is worth more than any questionnaire in the building.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; Traits or disorder</h3>
      <p class="stem">The childhood thread is real. What turns lifelong traits into a diagnosable disorder?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Persistence &mdash; the symptoms have run unbroken from eight to twenty-seven, and two decades of continuity is the diagnostic backbone here.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Pervasiveness and price &mdash; the pattern shown across settings, and impairment counted in real currency: jobs, money, relationships, self-worth.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Percentile &mdash; severity against the population: her score sits at the ninety-fourth centile, and extremity separates a variant from a disorder.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Persistence is necessary and nowhere near sufficient &mdash; plenty of lifelong traits never cost anyone a job. A pattern that runs for decades but bites in only one arena is a temperament with a difficult manager. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The centile belongs to a self-report screener &mdash; it measures how strongly she endorses the items, not how much her life is bleeding. Extremity on paper diagnoses nothing; cost in the world does. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The width question: <em>walk me through it everywhere &mdash; work, home, money, the people you love &mdash; because I need to know this isn&rsquo;t one difficult office.</em> Pervasiveness across settings rules out the situational story; impairment gives the pattern its price tag &mdash; four jobs in five years, the finances, the relationships strained by forgotten promises. Traits without impairment are a personality; the disorder begins where the cost does. It&rsquo;s also the answer her workplace conversation will need &mdash; adjustments attach to impairment, not to scores.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; The mimics</h3>
      <p class="stem">Four hours&rsquo; sleep, nightly wine &ldquo;to switch off&rdquo;, a wired edge of worry. How do the differentials get screened without insulting the complaint?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Sequentially &mdash; the mimics first: sleep restored and the wine reduced for three months, then reassess whatever remains of the ADHD picture.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Transparently &mdash; each screened with its purpose said out loud: not doubt but honest method, because they blur the very thing being measured.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Lightly &mdash; noted for the file, not laboured today: leading with sleep and wine to a patient primed for dismissal will sound like her GP.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Fix your sleep first, then come back&rdquo; is clinically defensible and relationally identical to the fob-off she&rsquo;s braced for &mdash; and it ignores that the arrow may run the other way: two decades of ADHD is a famous cause of wrecked sleep and evening wine. Parallel, not sequential. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Skipping the differentials to protect her feelings protects nothing &mdash; the mimics unscreened are the future consultant&rsquo;s first question and the diagnosis&rsquo;s soft underbelly. The skill isn&rsquo;t avoiding the questions; it&rsquo;s framing them. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Screened in full, explained in full: <em>this is the part that protects your answer</em> &mdash; sleep, mood, anxiety, alcohol, each asked plainly, with the method named: <em>they blur the very thing we&rsquo;re trying to measure.</em> The differential reframed from suspicion into rigour on her behalf. And the findings handled honestly in both directions: the childhood thread stands <em>and</em> the sleep and wine get worked on in parallel &mdash; explicitly not a fob-off &mdash; because untangling the picture is how her real answer gets clean enough to trust.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; &ldquo;So you don&rsquo;t believe me either&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Mid-differential, she stiffens: &ldquo;Sleep, wine &mdash; this is the GP again, isn&rsquo;t it. You think it&rsquo;s fashionable.&rdquo; What answers her?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reassurance &mdash; of course she&rsquo;s believed; these questions are routine for absolutely everyone, and nothing personal should be read into them.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The distinction, drawn out loud &mdash; dismissal concludes without assessing; the opposite: every question exists so her answer survives scrutiny.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Course-correction &mdash; the differentials have clearly touched a nerve; park them and return to the childhood material where the alliance held.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Don&rsquo;t take it personally, it&rsquo;s routine&rdquo; answers a trust question with a procedure note &mdash; and she holds two data points saying clinicians who sound routine end up laughing. The moment needs the difference explained, not waved off. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Abandoning the differentials because they&rsquo;re uncomfortable is the rubber stamp arriving by the back door &mdash; the screen half-done, the diagnosis built on soft ground, the mimics left for the stimulant to find. Hold the method; explain it better. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The distinction she has never been offered, drawn explicitly: <em>your GP concluded without assessing &mdash; I&rsquo;m assessing before concluding, and that is the opposite thing wearing similar questions.</em> The purpose restated &mdash; a case that stands up, an answer nobody can laugh at again &mdash; and the challenge welcomed rather than managed: her scepticism of clinicians is an earned position, and it gets treated as one. This moment is the station&rsquo;s hinge: hold the method <em>and</em> the person here, and the rest of the assessment runs on trust instead of surveillance.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; The drawer and the phone call</h3>
      <p class="stem">School reports in mum&rsquo;s drawer; a mother who remembers everything. How is collateral raised with a woman who fears being doubted?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Procedurally &mdash; informant history is a standard requirement of the pathway; the forms go out to her mother and the guidance covers the rest.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">As armour &mdash; the reports and her mother&rsquo;s account framed as the evidence that makes the diagnosis unlaughable-at, and hers to help gather.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Optionally &mdash; her account is detailed and credible; collateral offered as a nice-to-have, so the assessment never implies her word needs checking.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;It&rsquo;s a requirement of the pathway&rdquo; is true, and it lands as bureaucratic doubt &mdash; forms dispatched to her mother with no framing tell her exactly what she fears: that her own account wasn&rsquo;t enough. Same act; a far better frame exists. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Optional collateral is a diagnosis on one witness &mdash; and adult recall of childhood is exactly where honest memory bends. The kindness of not checking becomes the weakness every future doubter finds first. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Her fear, recruited as the reason: <em>collateral is what makes a diagnosis nobody can laugh at, ever again.</em> The drawer elevated to what it is &mdash; contemporaneous documentary evidence, worth more than any questionnaire &mdash; and her mother&rsquo;s interview framed as testimony for the case, not a check on her word. She leaves as the co-investigator gathering exhibits, not the claimant awaiting verification. Same clinical act as the procedural version; opposite experience &mdash; and better collateral too, because a defensive family produces thin accounts.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">Seven minutes nearly done. She asks the only question left: &ldquo;So &mdash; do I have it?&rdquo; What does the honest close contain?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The provisional yes &mdash; the thread is convincing and she has earned candour: likely ADHD, subject to confirmation, so hope leaves with her.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The honest pathway &mdash; today&rsquo;s findings said plainly; no verdict, no prescription dangled; the concrete next steps dated; the door held open.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The neutral hold &mdash; too early to comment either way; conclusions belong to the full pathway, and speculating now would only pre-judge it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Likely, subject to confirmation&rdquo; is the rubber stamp with a safety pin &mdash; she leaves carrying a diagnosis-shaped promise, and if the structured assessment lands elsewhere, today&rsquo;s kindness becomes tomorrow&rsquo;s betrayal. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Too early to comment&rdquo; withholds even what today genuinely found &mdash; and a woman dismissed twice hears the shrug, not the method. There is real, sayable substance between verdict and silence. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Everything true, nothing promised: <em>your childhood thread is real, and it matters</em> &mdash; today&rsquo;s genuine findings said plainly &mdash; beside the honest structure: the full structured interview booked, the reports coming in, her mother&rsquo;s conversation arranged, the sleep and the wine worked in parallel with their purpose named. No verdict, because there honestly isn&rsquo;t one yet; no stimulant dangled, because a medication mentioned today is a promise by tonight. And the refusal itself offered as respect: <em>you&rsquo;ve waited years for a real answer &mdash; I won&rsquo;t insult that with a fast one.</em></template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Assess. Don&rsquo;t adjudicate.</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>kindness</b>: the rubber stamp &mdash; she&rsquo;s articulate, the score is high, her relief is right there for the taking, and agreement arrives dressed as respect while the assessment quietly becomes a formality with a stimulant conversation attached. The second wears the face of <b>rigour</b>: the gatekeeper &mdash; the printout handled like contraband, the interview run as cross-examination, severity markers used as gateposts &mdash; and the bright inattentive presentation that reaches adulthood undiagnosed is dismissed by exactly the filter that missed it the first time. Note what the faces share: <b>in neither room does an assessment happen.</b> One concludes yes without evidence; one concludes no without evidence; both adjudicate a claim instead of assessing a patient &mdash; and both write &ldquo;assessment&rdquo; in the notes. The pass refuses the verdict in either direction and does the medicine instead: the fear of dismissal met by name and the research received as data; the childhood thread hunted by scene, not checklist &mdash; open memory, specific and datable, with the contemporaneous evidence (that drawer of school reports) treasured when it surfaces; pervasiveness and impairment established as the line between traits and disorder; the mimics screened transparently, with the method said out loud; collateral framed as armour; and a close that says everything true and promises nothing &mdash; no verdict, no prescription, a dated pathway, and the fast answer refused as the insult it would be.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap, both faces: the rubber stamp &mdash; agreeing with the self-diagnosis to be kind, the pathway promised, the stimulant marketed, collateral waved through as &ldquo;a formality&rdquo; &mdash; and the gatekeeper &mdash; sneering at it to be rigorous, the printout set aside unread, exclusion-and-expulsion used as gateposts, &ldquo;being twenty-seven with a phone&rdquo;.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the reframe that converts her &mdash; <em>&ldquo;none of this is doubt; it&rsquo;s me building you a case that will stand up&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the scene-hunt that strikes gold: <em>&ldquo;eight years old, classroom, Tuesday afternoon &mdash; what&rsquo;s she doing?&rdquo;</em>, answered by a drawer full of school reports nobody led her to.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, where this construct lives: this is D4&rsquo;s discipline &mdash; phenomenology before verdict &mdash; wearing the modern exam&rsquo;s most topical costume, and the doctrine transfers to every self-diagnosed presentation the next decade sends you: the evidence welcomed, the person kept, the assessment actually done. Second, the transfer Exam Mode tests: the same anatomy on a patient whose picture points the <em>other</em> way &mdash; because the honest assessor must be able to find ADHD where the gatekeeper would miss it <em>and</em> its absence where the rubber stamp would grant it, and only the second of those proves the method was ever real.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these to every self-referred assessment</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Receive the research as data.</b> A screening score is a reason to assess, never a reason to conclude &mdash; in either direction. Honour the work, meet the fear of dismissal by name, and reposition the assessment as advocacy: a case that will stand up.</li>
      <li><b>Hunt the childhood thread by scene, not checklist.</b> &ldquo;Eight years old, Tuesday afternoon &mdash; what&rsquo;s she doing?&rdquo; Open memory yields unprompted, datable specifics; criteria read aloud yield echo. And severity markers &mdash; exclusions, held-back years &mdash; are not gateposts: the quiet inattentive child passes every one of them on the way to an undiagnosed adulthood.</li>
      <li><b>Traits become disorder at pervasiveness and price.</b> Two settings minimum, and impairment counted in real currency &mdash; jobs, money, relationships, self-worth. A lifelong pattern that costs nothing is a temperament; adjustments and diagnoses both attach to cost.</li>
      <li><b>Screen the mimics transparently.</b> Sleep, mood, anxiety, substances &mdash; asked in full, with the method said out loud: <em>they blur the very thing we&rsquo;re trying to measure.</em> Worked in parallel, never as a sequential fob-off &mdash; and remember the arrow can run backwards: untreated ADHD wrecks sleep too.</li>
      <li><b>Frame collateral as armour.</b> School reports are contemporaneous evidence &mdash; treasure the drawer. The informant interview is testimony for the case, not a check on their word: <em>what makes a diagnosis nobody can laugh at, ever again.</em></li>
      <li><b>Close with everything true and nothing promised.</b> Today&rsquo;s genuine findings said plainly; no verdict, no stimulant dangled; the structured pathway dated; the door open. The fast answer refused &mdash; out loud &mdash; as the insult it would be to years of waiting.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the same anatomy under deadline pressure &mdash; a student who needs a letter more than an answer, a childhood thread he is actively hiding, and punish-lines waiting on both sides of the trap.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Zak, 20, a second-year mechanical engineering student, has been referred by his university GP requesting an ADHD assessment. His email to the service mentioned one date three times: the exam-adjustments deadline, five weeks away &mdash; he wants extra time, and for that he &ldquo;needs the letter&rdquo;. He failed two first-year modules and passed the resits by a whisker. Assess him.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Receive the deadline pressure without being ruled by it &mdash; honest about timelines, no letter promised, no motive questioned.</li>
            <li>Hunt the childhood thread by scene &mdash; there is one, and he will not volunteer it. Earn it.</li>
            <li>Establish width and cost &mdash; beyond exams: money, friendships, the flat, the course itself.</li>
            <li>Screen the mimics transparently &mdash; sleep, gaming, energy drinks, mood &mdash; with the method explained.</li>
            <li>Close honestly &mdash; today&rsquo;s findings, the real pathway and its real timeline, what can and cannot reach the deadline, the door open.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Zak &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Zak, 20. You open leg-jigging, direct, transactional: &ldquo;Right &mdash; so how fast can this be done? The adjustments deadline is the twelfth. My mate got extra time in about two weeks flat. I basically just need the letter.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          &bull; The real childhood thread, which you HIDE because you believe it proves laziness, not ADHD: primary school reports said &ldquo;disruptive, calls out, cannot stay in his seat&rdquo;; you were forever being sent to &ldquo;run laps to burn it off&rdquo;; homework diaries were a weekly war; your brother is &ldquo;the smart one&rdquo; and you decided at about nine that you were &ldquo;the stupid one who doesn&rsquo;t try&rdquo;. Reveal only into warm, scene-based, non-judgemental questions (&ldquo;take me to primary school&hellip;&rdquo;) &mdash; then, reluctantly: &ldquo;Look &mdash; my reports were basically the same sentence for six years. &lsquo;If Zak applied himself.&rsquo; That&rsquo;s not a disorder though, is it? That&rsquo;s just being lazy.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; Lifestyle fog you admit freely if asked without judgement: gaming until 2&ndash;3am most nights, four or five energy drinks a day, mood &ldquo;fine when things are going fine&rdquo;.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the rubber stamp:</b> if the candidate promises the letter, the diagnosis, or the deadline &mdash; relax completely, stop giving real answers (&ldquo;yeah, yeah, whatever helps&rdquo;), agree with every symptom suggested. The history collapses into echo. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the gatekeeper:</b> at any hint that you&rsquo;re gaming the system for exam advantage (&ldquo;a lot of students discover ADHD near deadlines&rdquo;) &mdash; go flat and cold: &ldquo;Forget it. Everyone thinks that. My tutor thinks that. Cheers for the confirmation.&rdquo; Stand to leave. If not repaired quickly and genuinely, go. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; The deadline test: you need honesty, not rescue. If the candidate is straight with you &mdash; the full assessment can&rsquo;t be conjured in five weeks, but here is what today&rsquo;s letter CAN honestly say, here is what the university&rsquo;s own support route can do meanwhile, and here is the real pathway with real dates &mdash; accept it grudgingly: &ldquo;&hellip;fine. As long as something actually happens this time.&rdquo; That is the pass.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the deadline met with honesty rather than promise or accusation, did the hidden childhood thread surface, were the mimics screened with the method explained, and did he leave with a real dated pathway? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="recv"><td colspan="2"><b>The receiving (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="recv">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recv"></td><td>The deadline pressure received without promise and without suspicion &mdash; motive never questioned.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recv"></td><td>The assessment reframed as building his case &mdash; he shifts from transacting to talking.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="thread"><td colspan="2"><b>The thread</b><span class="dc" data-dc="thread">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="thread"></td><td>Childhood hunted by scene, not checklist &mdash; open questions, specific ages, no leading.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="thread"></td><td>The hidden thread earned &mdash; the reports, the laps, &ldquo;if Zak applied himself&rdquo; surfaced.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="thread"></td><td>The laziness story met honestly &mdash; the same evidence reread without co-signing either verdict.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="width"><td colspan="2"><b>The width</b><span class="dc" data-dc="width">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="width"></td><td>Pervasiveness checked beyond exams &mdash; money, friendships, the flat, daily life.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="width"></td><td>Impairment priced in real terms &mdash; the failed modules, the resits, what it costs him.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="width"></td><td>Onset and continuity established &mdash; the pattern traced as lifelong, not exam-season.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="mim"><td colspan="2"><b>The mimics</b><span class="dc" data-dc="mim">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="mim"></td><td>Sleep, gaming, energy drinks, mood all screened &mdash; plainly, without judgement.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="mim"></td><td>The method explained &mdash; the screen framed as protecting his answer, not doubting it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="mim"></td><td>The fog handled in parallel &mdash; addressed alongside the assessment, never as a fob-off gate.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Deadline honesty &mdash; what today&rsquo;s letter can truly say, the university support route, the real pathway dated; no letter promised.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Collateral set in motion and the door held open &mdash; parents&rsquo; account and old reports requested; documented.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Zak arms both faces of the trap and puts a clock on the wall. The deadline invites the rubber stamp &mdash; promise the letter and watch the history collapse into agreeable echo, because a patient who has what he came for stops working; the extra-time context invites the gatekeeper &mdash; one raised eyebrow about students and deadlines and he is gone, taking a genuine hyperactive-impulsive childhood with him. His hidden thread teaches the station&rsquo;s subtlest lesson: patients conceal developmental evidence not only from shame of having a disorder but from certainty that it proves a character verdict &mdash; &ldquo;that&rsquo;s not a disorder, that&rsquo;s just lazy&rdquo; &mdash; and only scene-based, judgement-free hunting gets past it. And the deadline test at the close is the honest-pathway discipline under pressure: no letter promised, no motive questioned, the real route dated, and the thing he actually needed &mdash; something genuinely happening this time &mdash; delivered.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>Same skill, opposite finding. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Jess proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same anatomy on a patient whose picture points the other way</b> &mdash; because the honest assessor must be able to find ADHD where the gatekeeper would miss it <em>and</em> its absence where the rubber stamp would grant it. Timed, no feedback, no second tries: this is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Tara</b>, 33, a recruitment consultant, has self-referred convinced she has ADHD. She arrives organised &mdash; a typed symptom log, timestamped examples: losing her keys, drifting in meetings, re-reading the same email four times. It began around eighteen months ago, in the year she was promoted to team lead and her son was born; he is fourteen months old and still wakes twice a night. Before that: school prefect, colour-coded revision timetables, eleven steady years at the same firm. She opens: &ldquo;Every single video is me, word for word. My brain is broken, and I&rsquo;d like it taken seriously &mdash; I know what they say about tired mums.&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for the moment the picture is named to Tara &mdash; the honest direction of the findings, delivered so the person is kept.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Tara, I&#8217;m going to be straight with you about what today has shown&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The finding said plainly &mdash; a recent-onset shape, not the lifelong wiring of ADHD; no hedge, no false balance.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Her suffering validated as fully real in the same breath &mdash; the symptoms are true even if the label isn&rsquo;t.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No sneer anywhere &mdash; not at the videos, the log, or the self-diagnosis; her work honoured as evidence you used.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> A real next step attached &mdash; what actually happens now for the exhaustion and mood, and the door left open.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the honest finding that keeps the person &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Assess. Don&rsquo;t adjudicate.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the anatomy is installed: the research received as data and the fear met by name, the childhood thread hunted by scene, traits separated from disorder at pervasiveness and price, the mimics screened with the method said out loud, collateral framed as armour, and a close with everything true and nothing promised. Prove the transfer on Tara&rsquo;s typed symptom log in Exam Mode &mdash; the finding that points the other way &mdash; then take Zak&rsquo;s deadline to your colleagues: a hidden childhood thread, and punish-lines on both sides of the trap. The domain continues with the next station.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="29_Station_4.8_Autism_Assessment_in_an_Adult.html">Continue &middot; Station 4.8: Autism Assessment in an Adult &#8594;</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

