<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 4.8 &middot; Domain 4</span>
      <span class="tag">MSE &amp; Phenomenology</span><span class="tag">Adult Autism</span>
      <span class="tag">Gen Adult</span><span class="tag">Interview Craft</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Autism Assessment in an Adult</h1>
    <p class="sub">Bryony, 31, a supermarket checkout supervisor, sits down, smiles, shakes your hand and makes steady eye contact. Her son Rafi was diagnosed autistic three months ago; filling in his questionnaire, she found she was describing her own childhood. She is warm, fluent and funny for the whole seven minutes &mdash; and everything you have just watched is a performance she has rehearsed since she was nine. The trap in this room does not feel like a trap. It feels like clinical observation: <b>she is sociable, employed, married, and looking straight at you &mdash; so she cannot be autistic. Coping is being read as absence.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it becomes the adaptation test, and it runs in two halves. Does this doctor <em>change the interview</em> &mdash; processing time, concrete questions, permission not to hold eye contact, a word about the room &mdash; and understand that the adaptation is data-gathering, not merely courtesy? And does the doctor then read what it shows correctly: asking what things <em>cost</em> rather than whether she <em>can</em>; treating a learned script as evidence of effort rather than proof of ease; recognising the scaffolding already built around her; hunting the developmental thread through the mechanics of childhood rather than its headlines; screening the differentials as things that may sit <em>alongside</em> autism rather than instead of it; and closing honestly, with no verdict today and no promise about her son?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">A generation of autistic adults &mdash; women especially &mdash; reached adulthood undiagnosed because they were assessed against a picture drawn from eight-year-old boys, and because the very skills they built to survive were counted as evidence against them. This station tests four things. <b>The adaptation:</b> the interview changed to fit the person &mdash; processing time given and not filled, questions made concrete, eye contact released, the room and its noise acknowledged &mdash; and understood as method, because an unadapted interview does not merely feel unkind, it manufactures the wrong data. <b>The cost question:</b> not &ldquo;can you do it?&rdquo; but &ldquo;what does it take, and what happens afterwards?&rdquo; &mdash; the difference between capacity and cost being the difference between a life that looks fine and one that is being paid for in private. <b>The developmental thread:</b> hunted through the mechanics of childhood &mdash; who started the friendships, what happened in unstructured time, the <em>quality</em> of the interests rather than their subject matter &mdash; because &ldquo;I had friends&rdquo; and &ldquo;she was no trouble&rdquo; conceal far more than they reveal. And <b>the honest reading:</b> masking and accommodations recognised as evidence of need; differentials screened as possible companions rather than rivals; a close with no verdict, a real pathway, and nothing promised about her son.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> the failures here are made of ordinary clinical habits. The first is the surface read &mdash; she is warm, employed, married and looking right at you, so the observation of the room is quietly promoted into a finding, and thirty years of rehearsal are scored as sociability. The second is subtler and rarely noticed: the interview itself is never adapted, so the patient is assessed under conditions that suppress exactly what is being looked for &mdash; abstract questions, rapid turn-taking, silence filled before she can use it &mdash; and then the resulting thin, flat, unrevealing answers are treated as the findings. One misreads the evidence. The other destroys it. Notice both in the decisions below.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure reads a rehearsed performance as the person. The other never lets the person appear at all. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 4.8</b> &middot; The outpatient clinic &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The surface read</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The unadapted interview</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE SURFACE READ.</b> Friendly, observant, confident &mdash; and every observation points the wrong way. Watch thirty years of rehearsal get scored as sociability.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Bryony</span><span class="txt"><em>(handshake, warm smile, steady eye contact)</em> Thanks so much for seeing me &mdash; I know how long these lists are. So: my little boy Rafi was diagnosed in June, and when I was filling in his forms I kept thinking, well, that&rsquo;s me. That was me at six. My husband Owen said I should stop reading over his shoulder and actually ask someone. So here I am, asking.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(smiling back, relaxed)</em> Well, can I say straight away &mdash; you present very differently from Rafi, I&rsquo;d imagine. You came in, you shook my hand, you&rsquo;ve got lovely eye contact, you&rsquo;re making jokes. You supervise a team, you&rsquo;re married, you&rsquo;ve got a child. That&rsquo;s a lot of social skill on display in two minutes. I&rsquo;m not saying it&rsquo;s impossible, but I&rsquo;d be surprised. Parents often see themselves in their children &mdash; it&rsquo;s very common after a diagnosis.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Four observations, all accurate, all pointed backwards. The handshake, the eye contact, the jokes, the job &mdash; each one is a <em>skill</em>, and skills are learned, which is the entire question. Nobody has yet asked what any of it costs or where it came from. And the explanation has arrived before the assessment: her recognition of herself in her son&rsquo;s forms has been reclassified as a known parental artefact, when in reality it is the single commonest route by which autistic women are finally identified. The room has been read instead of the history.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Bryony</span><span class="txt"><em>(smile holding, voice slightly flatter)</em> Right. No, I get that. I&rsquo;m all right at the people bit, I suppose. It&rsquo;s just&hellip; <em>(pause)</em> it&rsquo;s not that I can&rsquo;t do it. It&rsquo;s what it&rsquo;s like after. But no, you&rsquo;re probably right.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">And you&rsquo;ve managed all of it &mdash; work, marriage, motherhood &mdash; which really is the point, isn&rsquo;t it? Autism is a diagnosis we make when someone&rsquo;s life is significantly impaired, and yours plainly isn&rsquo;t. What I&rsquo;d wonder about is whether you&rsquo;re a bit anxious and a bit run down &mdash; which would be entirely understandable with a newly diagnosed six-year-old at home. Shall we think about that instead? I can put something in to your GP about the stress side of things.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>She just handed over the whole case &mdash; <em>it&rsquo;s not that I can&rsquo;t do it, it&rsquo;s what it&rsquo;s like after</em> &mdash; and it went straight past, because the candidate was already assembling a reassurance. That sentence is the station: capacity is not cost. And &ldquo;impairment&rdquo; has been measured by the visible checklist of a life &mdash; job, marriage, child &mdash; without one question about what holds it up: the recovery days, the crashes, the phone calls she cannot make, the nine years of avoided nights out. An anxious, run-down mother is now the working diagnosis, and it was reached without a history.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 4:15pm)</em> Self-referred following son&rsquo;s ASD diagnosis. Presentation: warm, sociable, good eye contact and reciprocity throughout; employed in a supervisory role; married with one child. No evidence of significant social communication impairment on interview. Impression: parental over-identification; likely stress and low-level anxiety. Not for autism pathway. <em>(Bryony thanks him, walks to the car park, and sits in her car for forty minutes before she can drive. She does not ask anyone again for six years.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;No evidence of significant social communication impairment on interview&rdquo; &mdash; a seven-minute observation of a woman doing the thing she has practised since she was nine, written up as though it were a lifetime. This is the map&rsquo;s named trap, executed with perfect confidence: masking read as absence. Note the cost of the error. It is not simply a missed label; it is her explanation of herself withdrawn, her son&rsquo;s inheritance left unexamined, and forty minutes in a car park that nobody will ever hear about.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE UNADAPTED INTERVIEW.</b> No sneer, no shortcut &mdash; just the standard interview, run at standard speed. Watch it suppress the very thing it is looking for.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Bryony</span><span class="txt"><em>(handshake, warm smile, steady eye contact)</em> Thanks so much for seeing me &mdash; I know how long these lists are. My son Rafi was diagnosed in June, and filling in his forms I kept thinking: that&rsquo;s me. That was me at six.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(brisk, pleasant, notes open, strip light humming overhead)</em> Good, thank you. So tell me about yourself &mdash; how would you describe your personality? <em>(before she answers)</em> And how do you find social situations generally? Would you say you&rsquo;re more of an introvert or an extrovert? Take relationships &mdash; how easy do you find it to put yourself in someone else&rsquo;s shoes?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Four questions in one breath, every one abstract and self-summarising: <em>describe your personality</em>, <em>generally</em>, <em>would you say</em>. These are the hardest possible questions for the person in the chair &mdash; they require her to generalise across a lifetime and produce an instant social self-portrait &mdash; and they are the easiest to answer with a rehearsed line, which is exactly what she will now do. No processing time, no concrete anchors, and nobody has mentioned the light or the noise. The method is already generating the wrong data.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Bryony</span><span class="txt"><em>(a beat too long, then the smooth version)</em> Um &mdash; I&rsquo;m a people person, I think. Yeah. Sociable. I like a laugh at work. <em>(She has answered the only one of the four she could hold on to, with a sentence she has used a hundred times.)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(filling the pause immediately, moving on)</em> Lovely. And childhood &mdash; happy? Any problems making friends at school? <em>(&ldquo;No, I had friends.&rdquo;)</em> Good. Any obsessions, lining things up, spinning, that kind of thing? <em>(&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t think so.&rdquo;)</em> And you can obviously read people all right &mdash; you&rsquo;ve read me perfectly well this morning. Right. I think we can be fairly clear here.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every silence closed before she can use it &mdash; and processing time is not politeness here, it is the instrument. &ldquo;Happy?&rdquo; and &ldquo;any problems making friends?&rdquo; are yes/no doors that a lifetime of masking will always close politely; the mechanics behind &ldquo;I had friends&rdquo; &mdash; who chose, who followed, what happened at breaktime &mdash; are never opened. &ldquo;Obsessions, lining things up, spinning&rdquo; is the eight-year-old-boy checklist, and she truthfully says no, because her interest was horses: the breed registries, the bloodlines, the exact colour terminology, catalogued for eleven years. Everyone said girls love horses. The question as asked could not find it.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 4:12pm)</em> Reciprocal, fluent interview. Denies childhood peer difficulties; denies repetitive behaviours or restricted interests. Describes self as sociable. No developmental history to support ASD. Discharged. <em>(Everything in that note is accurate. Every finding in it was produced by the way the questions were asked. Bryony goes home and tells Owen she must have got it wrong.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>This is the failure candidates never see coming, because nothing rude happened: no eye-rolling, no dismissal, a courteous doctor asking recognised questions. And yet the record is false from top to bottom &mdash; not because he misread the evidence, but because the interview he ran could not produce it. Held at speed, in the abstract, against a childhood-boy template, in a bright humming room, this assessment was over before it began. An unadapted interview does not test the patient. It tests the mask, and the mask always passes.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The interview adapted and named as method &mdash; then cost, mechanics, scaffolding, honest differentials, and a close that promises nothing.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Bryony</span><span class="txt"><em>(handshake, warm smile, steady eye contact)</em> Thanks so much for seeing me &mdash; I know how long these lists are. My son Rafi was diagnosed in June, and filling in his forms I kept thinking: that&rsquo;s me. That was me at six.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">That is one of the commonest ways adults get here, and it&rsquo;s a good reason, not a lesser one. Before we start &mdash; three things, and they&rsquo;re yours to take or leave. You don&rsquo;t have to look at me; plenty of people think better looking somewhere else, and I won&rsquo;t read anything into it. I&rsquo;m going to ask slowly and leave gaps, and if a gap goes on it&rsquo;s me waiting, not me expecting. And this room: the light hums and there&rsquo;s a fan behind you &mdash; would you like either off, or the chair turned? <em>(She stares at him. Then: &ldquo;&hellip;the light. Please. Nobody has ever asked me that.&rdquo;)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Sixty seconds, and the whole assessment has changed footing. Note that each adaptation is <em>offered</em>, not imposed &mdash; no assumptions made about a woman who has not yet been assessed &mdash; and note what the third one does: asking about the light is simultaneously a courtesy, a piece of history-taking, and a message that this room does not require performance. &ldquo;Nobody has ever asked me that&rdquo; is the first unrehearsed sentence she has said. The mask has been given permission to come down &mdash; which is the only condition under which the next six minutes can produce true data.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Now &mdash; you&rsquo;re clearly good with people at work, and I want to ask about that differently. Not whether you can do it. What it takes, and what happens afterwards. <em>(It comes out slowly, then quickly: the checkout script &mdash; the same forty seconds, three hundred times a day, which is why she is &ldquo;the friendly one&rdquo;; the staffroom, which is unbearable, because there is no script and everyone talks across each other; the eye contact she taught herself at nine from watching television, three seconds then away, still counted; Friday nights face-down in the dark with the door shut; no plans until Sunday; nine years of declined work nights out; Owen making every phone call; the earplugs she wears &ldquo;because of the tannoy&rdquo;.)</em> Thank you. That&rsquo;s the part nobody sees.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The cost question, asked precisely &mdash; <em>not whether you can, but what it takes and what happens afterwards</em> &mdash; and everything the surface read scored as sociability is now correctly identified: a script, not a skill; eye contact executed by a rule she wrote at nine; and a life held up by scaffolding &mdash; the recovery days, the declined invitations, the outsourced phone calls, the earplugs with a cover story. Note the last of those. Accommodations that a person builds and hides are among the strongest evidence in this assessment, and they are invisible to anyone who only asks what she is capable of.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Back to being six, then &mdash; not whether you had friends, but how it worked. Who decided what you played? <em>(&ldquo;Them. Always. I copied a girl called Sam for about four years.&rdquo;)</em> Breaktimes? <em>(&ldquo;The library. I said it was because I liked reading.&rdquo;)</em> And what were you into &mdash; properly into? <em>(The horses arrive: not riding them, never riding them &mdash; the breed registries, the bloodlines, the colour terms, catalogued in notebooks for eleven years. &ldquo;Everyone just said girls love horses.&rdquo;)</em> That&rsquo;s the kind of detail I needed. Two more things: I want to ask your mum what school said about the first term when you didn&rsquo;t speak, and I&rsquo;d like to see Rafi&rsquo;s paperwork if you&rsquo;re willing &mdash; not to diagnose you by proxy, but because his assessment asked good questions about the family. And last, the honest part. Some of what you&rsquo;ve described &mdash; the dread before the staffroom, the crashes &mdash; could also be anxiety, or exhaustion, or low mood, and I&rsquo;ll be screening for all three. That isn&rsquo;t me looking for a different answer instead of this one; those things sit <em>alongside</em> autism far more often than they replace it, and if they&rsquo;re there they&rsquo;re treatable now, whatever the assessment concludes. So: the full structured assessment, booked, with your mum&rsquo;s account and any school reports; today gives you no verdict, because a real one takes longer than seven minutes. And I&rsquo;m not going to tell you what this means for Rafi, or that it changes anything about his diagnosis &mdash; what I can tell you is that whatever we find, the way you&rsquo;ve been getting through Fridays isn&rsquo;t something you have to keep doing silently.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Three disciplines in one turn. The thread hunted through <em>mechanics</em>, the only way it survives a lifetime of camouflage &mdash; not &ldquo;did you have friends&rdquo; but who chose, who copied whom, where breaktime actually went &mdash; and the interest interrogated for its <em>quality</em> rather than its subject, which is exactly how eleven years of systematised cataloguing hid inside &ldquo;girls love horses&rdquo;. Then collateral named specifically: her mother, the school&rsquo;s account of the silent first term, and her son&rsquo;s file &mdash; requested with the caveat that shows he knows the difference between family evidence and diagnosis by proxy. And then the differentials, screened without displacing the referral &mdash; <em>alongside, not instead of</em> &mdash; which is the honest position and also, quietly, the clinically correct one: anxiety and depression are more common in this group, not less, and treating them does not require the assessment to finish first. Then the close: no verdict, a dated pathway with named collateral, and two refusals that show real discipline &mdash; nothing promised about Rafi, and no false comfort offered about what a label would do. What she is given instead is true and small and hers: the Fridays do not have to stay a secret.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skills on a very different patient &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; The first minute</h3>
      <p class="stem">She shakes your hand, smiles, holds your gaze, and explains that she recognised herself in her son&rsquo;s forms. What does the opening do?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Notes the presentation &mdash; the handshake, the eye contact, the fluency are real clinical observations, and they belong in the picture from the start.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Offers adaptations &mdash; eye contact released, processing gaps promised, the room&rsquo;s light and noise raised as her choice, none of it assumed or imposed.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Explores the trigger &mdash; recognising yourself in a newly diagnosed child is common, so the meaning of that recognition is examined before anything else.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Those observations are accurate and they point backwards: a handshake and steady gaze are <em>skills</em>, and where a skill came from and what it costs is the entire question. Promoting the first two minutes of a rehearsed performance into evidence is the named trap. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Treating her recognition as a phenomenon to be explained puts the explanation before the assessment &mdash; and this route, a mother seeing herself in her child&rsquo;s paperwork, is the commonest way autistic women are finally identified, not a known artefact to be discounted. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The adaptation, offered and not imposed: no assumptions made about a woman who has not been assessed yet, and every option hers to decline. Notice what the third one does &mdash; asking about the humming light and the fan is a courtesy, a piece of history-taking, and a signal that this room does not require the performance, all at once. <em>&ldquo;Nobody has ever asked me that&rdquo;</em> is the first unrehearsed sentence of the interview, and everything true that follows depends on it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; Capacity or cost</h3>
      <p class="stem">She is, by every visible measure, good with people: a supervisor, praised as the friendly one on the tills. How is that explored?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">As reassurance to be tested &mdash; if she manages a customer-facing role all day, that capability has to weigh against the referral question.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">As a question about price &mdash; not whether she can do it at all, but what doing it takes out of her, and what happens in the hours afterwards.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">As a strength to build on &mdash; her workplace competence is the foundation any support plan would be built on, so it is mapped out carefully first.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>This is the surface read wearing a clinical face. Capability weighs nothing until you know what produces it &mdash; and here it is produced by the same forty-second script three hundred times a day, which is why the staffroom, where there is no script, is unbearable. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Mapping strengths is a fine thing to do later; right now it skips the diagnostic question entirely. A support plan built on &ldquo;she&rsquo;s great with customers&rdquo; without knowing what Friday night costs her would recommend exactly the wrong things. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The single most useful question in the station: <em>not whether you can, but what it takes and what happens afterwards.</em> It converts the whole visible life into evidence &mdash; the script that makes her &ldquo;the friendly one&rdquo;, the scriptless staffroom she cannot survive, the Friday nights face-down in the dark, the Sundays kept empty. Capacity and cost are different measurements, and only one of them is diagnostic.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; The eye contact she taught herself</h3>
      <p class="stem">She mentions, almost in passing, that she learned eye contact at nine from watching television &mdash; three seconds, then away &mdash; and still counts. What is that?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reassuring &mdash; whatever its origin, the skill is now fluent and automatic, and social learning of this kind is something most people do to some degree.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Evidence, and strong evidence &mdash; a rule consciously written and still consciously run, in place of something that is normally never learned at all.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A curiosity to note &mdash; interesting for the formulation, but self-reported childhood strategy is soft data compared with what is observable in the room.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Everyone adjusts socially; almost nobody writes an explicit rule for eye contact at nine and is still counting seconds at thirty-one. Calling that ordinary is precisely how masking gets scored as absence &mdash; the fluency is the achievement, not the disproof. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Ranking what you observe above what she reports inverts the evidence in this assessment: the observable is the mask, and it is thirty years rehearsed. The account of building it is the more reliable of the two. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Precisely inverted from the failing takes, and correctly: the eye contact that Fail 1 scored as sociability is, once its history is known, one of the clearest pieces of evidence in the room. A consciously authored rule, still consciously executed two decades later, in place of something typically acquired without instruction &mdash; that is compensation, and compensation implies the thing being compensated for. Effort, not ease.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; &ldquo;I had friends&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Asked about school, she says she had friends and was never in trouble. How does the developmental thread get hunted?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">By the standard screen &mdash; the recognised childhood markers worked through in turn: lining things up, repetitive movements, obsessions, meltdowns, delayed speech.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">By mechanics &mdash; who decided what was played, who copied whom, where breaktimes were actually spent, and what the interests were like underneath their subject.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">By record &mdash; her own recall at thirty-one is unreliable either way, so the question is parked until the school reports and her mother&rsquo;s full account arrive.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>That checklist was drawn from eight-year-old boys, and she will truthfully answer no to all of it &mdash; which is exactly how a generation of women were assessed out of a diagnosis they had. Her interest was horses; her difficulty was invisible; the questions as framed cannot reach either. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Collateral matters enormously here and it is not a reason to stop asking &mdash; her own account, opened up properly, is what tells you which questions to put to her mother and what to look for in the reports. Deferring the whole thread abandons the interview. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>&ldquo;I had friends&rdquo; is a headline; the mechanics are the history. <em>Who decided what you played?</em> &mdash; them, always, and four years spent copying one girl. <em>Breaktimes?</em> &mdash; the library, with a cover story about reading. And the interest interrogated for quality rather than subject: not riding horses but cataloguing breed registries, bloodlines and colour terms in notebooks for eleven years, hidden in plain sight because everyone said girls love horses. The same childhood, asked about properly, tells the opposite story.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; The scaffolding</h3>
      <p class="stem">Owen makes every phone call. Fridays are spent in the dark. Nine years of work nights out declined. Earplugs, explained as being for the tannoy. What weight does this carry?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">It shows the system works &mdash; she has found sustainable strategies and a supportive marriage, which is why her life has held together so well.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">It is evidence of need &mdash; supports built, hidden and paid for privately, without which the functioning everyone admires would not exist.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">It raises a risk &mdash; avoidance maintained over years is how anxiety disorders entrench, so the pattern is best addressed as avoidance behaviour.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;It works&rdquo; describes the outcome and erases the mechanism. A life that holds together only because a husband absorbs every phone call and one night a week is spent recovering in the dark is not evidence of no difficulty &mdash; it is the difficulty, with a workaround bolted on. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Reclassifying the scaffolding as avoidance to be reversed is how autistic adults get pushed into treatment that removes their supports and calls the collapse progress. Some of this may well be anxiety too &mdash; but it is not automatically that, and stripping it is not automatically the answer. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Accommodations that a person invents, funds and conceals are among the strongest evidence available &mdash; and they are structurally invisible to any assessment that only asks what she is capable of. Note the earplugs especially: a support hidden behind a cover story is a support she has learned to be ashamed of. The functioning everyone points to is real, and it is being purchased. This is the answer to &ldquo;but her life isn&rsquo;t impaired&rdquo;.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; Anxiety, exhaustion, low mood</h3>
      <p class="stem">Much of what she describes could also be anxiety or depression. How are the differentials handled?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">In sequence &mdash; the mood and anxiety picture treated first, with the neurodevelopmental question revisited once the situation is more stable.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">As companions, not rivals &mdash; screened in full and named as things that sit alongside autism, treatable now whatever the assessment concludes.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">By parsimony &mdash; a single explanation covering the anxiety, the exhaustion and the social difficulty is preferable to stacking several diagnoses.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Come back when you&rsquo;re stable&rdquo; is how autistic adults spend a decade in services without ever being assessed &mdash; and it has the causation backwards: much of the anxiety and exhaustion is generated by masking, so waiting for it to clear is waiting for something the assessment itself would explain. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Parsimony is a fine instinct in the wrong place. Anxiety and depression are commoner in autistic adults, not rarer, and forcing a lifelong developmental pattern and a treatable mood disorder to compete for one slot loses whichever one you discard. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Screened honestly, without displacing the referral: <em>alongside, not instead of.</em> That framing is clinically correct &mdash; co-occurrence is the norm here &mdash; and it is what stops the differential being heard as a fob-off by a woman who has waited months to be asked. And the practical half matters just as much: if the low mood and the exhaustion are there, they are treatable now, on their own dates, without waiting for a structured assessment to finish.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; The close, and Rafi</h3>
      <p class="stem">Time is nearly gone. She asks: &ldquo;Do you think I am? And what does it mean for Rafi if I am?&rdquo; What does the close contain?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">A provisional answer &mdash; she has waited long enough and the picture is striking: likely autistic, pending confirmation, the kindest thing to send her with.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Today&rsquo;s truth and no more &mdash; no verdict today, the pathway and collateral dated, nothing promised about Rafi, and one honest thing that is hers to keep.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A careful deferral &mdash; nothing can responsibly be said before the structured assessment, so both questions are held over to avoid pre-judging the outcome.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A provisional yes is a verdict with a caveat attached, and she will hear the verdict. If the structured assessment lands elsewhere, today&rsquo;s kindness becomes a second wound &mdash; and the promise will already have travelled to her mother, her husband and Rafi&rsquo;s school. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Holding both questions withholds even what today genuinely established &mdash; and a woman who has spent thirty years being told she seems fine will hear a familiar nothing. There is real, sayable substance between a verdict and a shrug. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Everything true, nothing promised: what today actually found, the structured assessment booked, her mother&rsquo;s account and the school reports named as the next evidence &mdash; and two disciplined refusals. Nothing is claimed about what this means for Rafi, whose diagnosis stands on its own and is not up for revision here; and no false comfort is offered about what a label would fix. What she gets instead is small, true and hers: the way she has been surviving Fridays does not have to stay a secret.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Coping is not absence.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Both failures in this station are made of ordinary clinical habits, which is exactly why they are dangerous. The first is <b>the surface read</b>: she shakes your hand, holds your gaze, supervises a team and makes you laugh &mdash; so the room gets promoted into a finding and thirty years of rehearsal are scored as sociability. Every observation is accurate; every one points backwards, because a handshake and steady eye contact are <em>skills</em>, and where a skill came from and what it costs is the whole question. The second is <b>the unadapted interview</b>, and candidates rarely see it coming because nothing rude happens: a courteous doctor asks recognised questions at standard speed &mdash; abstract, self-summarising, gaps closed before they can be used, childhood screened against a template drawn from eight-year-old boys &mdash; and the thin, fluent, unrevealing answers this produces are then written up as the findings. One misreads the evidence. The other destroys it before it exists. The pass does neither: the interview is changed first and named as method &mdash; processing time, concrete questions, eye contact released, the room&rsquo;s light and noise offered as her choice, all offered and none imposed &mdash; and then what it shows is read correctly. Cost, not capacity. A rule authored at nine and still running at thirty-one, recognised as compensation rather than proof of ease. Scaffolding &mdash; the outsourced phone calls, the empty Sundays, the earplugs with a cover story &mdash; counted as evidence of need. Childhood hunted through mechanics rather than headlines, and interests interrogated for their quality rather than their subject. Differentials screened as companions, not rivals. And a close with no verdict, dated collateral, nothing promised about her son, and one true thing that is hers to keep.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap in either form: masking and accommodations read as absence of difficulty &mdash; &ldquo;you&rsquo;re making eye contact, you&rsquo;re married, you have a job, so you can&rsquo;t be autistic&rdquo; &mdash; or an interview run entirely on neurotypical defaults: abstract questions at speed, no processing time, silence filled, the boy-child checklist, and the resulting emptiness recorded as a negative developmental history.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments lift this station: the adaptation offered in the first minute &mdash; <em>&ldquo;you don&rsquo;t have to look at me&hellip; and would you like that light off?&rdquo;</em>, answered by <em>&ldquo;nobody has ever asked me that&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the question that reframes an entire life: <em>&ldquo;not whether you can do it, but what it takes, and what happens afterwards.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, where this construct lives: this is D4&rsquo;s phenomenology married to D1&rsquo;s interview craft, and the union is the point &mdash; in neurodevelopmental assessment the method <em>is</em> the instrument, and an interview held on default settings measures the mask rather than the person. Second, the transfer Exam Mode tests: the same two disciplines on a patient who could not look more different &mdash; a young man whose autism is not in doubt for a second, where the adaptation must serve a different purpose entirely and the thing being missed is not the diagnosis but the person&rsquo;s distress underneath it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every neurodevelopmental assessment</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Adapt the interview before you assess with it.</b> Processing time given and not filled; concrete questions instead of abstract self-summary; eye contact explicitly released; the room&rsquo;s light and noise raised as the patient&rsquo;s choice. Offer, never impose &mdash; and understand that this is method, not manners: an unadapted interview measures the mask, and the mask always passes.</li>
      <li><b>Ask what it costs, not whether they can.</b> Capacity and cost are different measurements and only one is diagnostic. The script that makes her &ldquo;the friendly one&rdquo;, the scriptless staffroom, the Friday spent face-down in the dark, the Sunday kept empty &mdash; that is the finding.</li>
      <li><b>Read masking as effort, not ease.</b> A rule for eye contact authored at nine and still running at thirty-one is compensation, and compensation implies something being compensated for. Fluency is the achievement, not the disproof.</li>
      <li><b>Count the scaffolding.</b> Supports a person builds, funds and hides &mdash; the outsourced phone calls, the declined invitations, the earplugs with a cover story &mdash; are evidence of need, and invisible to anyone who only asks about capability. A hidden support is one they have learned to be ashamed of.</li>
      <li><b>Hunt childhood by mechanics, and interests by quality.</b> Not &ldquo;did you have friends&rdquo; but who chose, who copied whom, where breaktime was really spent. Not the subject of the interest but its texture &mdash; eleven years of cataloguing bloodlines hid inside &ldquo;girls love horses&rdquo;. The boy-child checklist will return a truthful, useless no.</li>
      <li><b>Differentials are companions, not rivals.</b> Anxiety and depression are commoner in autistic adults, not rarer. Screen them in full, name them as things that sit alongside rather than instead of, and treat what is treatable now &mdash; without making the assessment wait, and without making it compete.</li>
      <li><b>Close with today&rsquo;s truth and nothing more.</b> No verdict at seven minutes; the pathway and collateral dated; no promises made about anyone else&rsquo;s diagnosis; no false comfort about what a label fixes &mdash; and one honest thing they can take home.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the same disciplines with a hostile clock and a bruised history &mdash; a woman already refused once, facing a disciplinary at work, with punish-lines waiting on both sides: the surface read, and its clumsy opposite.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Verity, 38, a bank cashier, has been referred for autism assessment by her GP. Her employer has begun a formal process about her &ldquo;attitude&rdquo; &mdash; three complaints that she is blunt, rude and cold with colleagues &mdash; and there is a meeting in four weeks. She has been assessed once before, at 22, and was told she could not be autistic because she was married. Assess her.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Adapt the interview and offer, don&rsquo;t impose &mdash; she will tell you what she needs if asked as an equal.</li>
            <li>Ask about cost, not capacity &mdash; twenty years of a counter-facing job is not the answer to the question.</li>
            <li>Hunt the developmental thread by mechanics &mdash; and expect the boy-child checklist to return nothing.</li>
            <li>Handle the workplace situation honestly &mdash; what an assessment can and cannot do in four weeks, and what does not require a diagnosis at all.</li>
            <li>Close with today&rsquo;s truth, the pathway dated, collateral named, and the 22-year-old verdict addressed rather than ignored.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Verity &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Verity, 38. You sit down without small talk and go straight in, flat and precise: &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll tell you now, I did this at twenty-two. He said I couldn&rsquo;t be autistic because I had a husband. So you&rsquo;ll understand if I don&rsquo;t have much faith in the process. There&rsquo;s a meeting on the fourteenth about my attitude and I need to know what I am before then.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          &bull; The cost, revealed only to cost-not-capacity questions: twenty years at the counter is survivable because the transactions are identical; what destroys her is the team meeting, the leaving cards, the &ldquo;quick catch-up&rdquo; with no agenda. You take annual leave the day after any work social. You eat lunch in your car every day, and have done for eleven years.<br>
          &bull; The &ldquo;rudeness&rdquo; from the inside, if asked what actually happened: you told a colleague her presentation had four errors, in front of the team, because it did and she would have wanted to know; you don&rsquo;t do the greeting ritual in the mornings because you are already counting the float. You are bewildered, not defiant: &ldquo;I was being <em>useful</em>. I don&rsquo;t understand what I&rsquo;m supposed to have done.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; Childhood, revealed only to mechanics questions: no friends until a girl adopted you at fourteen and told you what to say; breaktimes spent walking the perimeter fence, exactly the same route; your interest was the bus network &mdash; timetables, route numbers, the depot allocations &mdash; but you learned by twelve not to talk about it. School said &ldquo;pleasant, very quiet, works alone.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the surface read:</b> if the candidate cites your marriage, your job, your articulacy or your eye contact as evidence against &mdash; go completely flat: &ldquo;Right. That&rsquo;s what he said.&rdquo; Answer in single words from then on, and start looking at the door. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the clumsy opposite:</b> if the candidate over-adapts &mdash; talks slowly and simply as if to a child, praises you for coping, uses a soft sing-song voice, or announces what you need without asking &mdash; go cold and cutting: &ldquo;I have a mortgage and I count forty thousand pounds a day. You can speak to me normally.&rdquo; Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; The deadline test: you want honesty, not rescue. If the candidate is straight &mdash; a full assessment cannot be completed in four weeks, but here is what can be written now, here is what your employer must consider regardless of any diagnosis, and here is the pathway with dates &mdash; you accept it: &ldquo;All right. That&rsquo;s the first useful thing anyone&rsquo;s said to me about this.&rdquo; That is the pass.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Were adaptations offered as choices rather than imposed or omitted, did the cost behind twenty years at a counter surface, was childhood reached through mechanics, and did she leave with honest deadline information? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="adapt"><td colspan="2"><b>The adaptation (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="adapt">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="adapt"></td><td>Adaptations offered as her choice &mdash; processing time, question style, the room &mdash; never imposed or announced for her.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="adapt"></td><td>Register kept adult and equal &mdash; no slowed, simplified or sing-song delivery; no praise for coping.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="cost"><td colspan="2"><b>The cost</b><span class="dc" data-dc="cost">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cost"></td><td>Asked what things take and what follows them &mdash; not whether she can manage the counter.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cost"></td><td>The scaffolding surfaced &mdash; eleven years of lunch in the car, leave booked after socials.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="cost"></td><td>The &ldquo;rudeness&rdquo; explored from inside her account, without either endorsing or scolding.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="thread"><td colspan="2"><b>The thread</b><span class="dc" data-dc="thread">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="thread"></td><td>Childhood hunted by mechanics &mdash; who chose, who adopted whom, where breaktimes went.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="thread"></td><td>The interest reached by quality, not subject &mdash; and the fact she learned to hide it by twelve.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="thread"></td><td>Collateral named specifically &mdash; school records, family account &mdash; and framed as evidence, not verification.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="diff"><td colspan="2"><b>The differentials</b><span class="dc" data-dc="diff">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="diff"></td><td>Mood, anxiety and burnout screened &mdash; plainly, and without displacing the referral question.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="diff"></td><td>Framed as companions, not rivals &mdash; alongside autism rather than instead of it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="diff"></td><td>The 22-year-old verdict addressed honestly &mdash; named as wrong reasoning, without theatrical apology.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Deadline honesty &mdash; what can be written in four weeks, what the employer must consider without any diagnosis, the pathway dated.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>No verdict today, no promises about the meeting, and the door held open &mdash; documented.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Verity arms both sides of the adaptation and puts a disciplinary meeting on the calendar. She punishes the surface read with the exact sentence she has heard before &mdash; <em>that&rsquo;s what he said</em> &mdash; and she punishes its clumsy opposite just as hard, which is the lesson candidates almost never get taught: over-adaptation is not a safe error. Slowed speech, simplified words and praise for coping are experienced as condescension by an articulate adult, and &ldquo;announcing what she needs&rdquo; is the same failure to ask as never adapting at all. Her &ldquo;rudeness&rdquo; teaches the station&rsquo;s clinical heart &mdash; four errors named in front of a team, delivered as helpfulness and received as cruelty &mdash; and it must be explored from inside her account without the candidate either endorsing it or scolding her for it. And the deadline forces the honest-pathway discipline into its most useful real-world form: workplace adjustments do not require a completed diagnosis, which is the single most practical thing she can be told today.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>Same disciplines. Opposite patient. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Bryony proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: <b>the same two disciplines on a patient who could not look more different</b> &mdash; a young man whose autism nobody doubts for a second, where the adaptation serves an entirely different purpose and the thing being missed is not the diagnosis but the person underneath it. Timed, no feedback, no second tries: this is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Ewan</b>, 24, was diagnosed autistic at nine. He lost his job at a garden centre four months ago when the rota changed. His mother rang the GP to say he &ldquo;isn&rsquo;t himself&rdquo;, and he has come, on time to the minute, because she asked him to. He sits sideways to you, answers in short exact sentences, corrects you when you round a number, and does not look up. Asked how he is, he says: &ldquo;Fine.&rdquo; His mother&rsquo;s view, given at the door, was: &ldquo;He&rsquo;s always been like this &mdash; it&rsquo;s just how he is.&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for the moment you say back to Ewan what you have heard &mdash; the low mood and the thoughts he described &mdash; in language he can use.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Ewan, I want to say back what I heard, in plain words&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Literal and unambiguous &mdash; no euphemism, no metaphor, nothing he has to interpret; the words say exactly what they mean.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> His account taken at face value &mdash; the flat delivery not read as small distress, and nothing discounted because it was said calmly.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The low mood named as its own condition &mdash; treatable in its own right, not folded into &ldquo;that&rsquo;s his autism&rdquo;.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> A concrete next step attached &mdash; a named person, an actual time, and what he does in the meantime.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the distress heard, the words kept literal &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Coping is not absence.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the anatomy is installed: the interview adapted first and understood as method, cost asked instead of capacity, masking read as effort, scaffolding counted as evidence, childhood hunted by mechanics and interests by quality, differentials named as companions rather than rivals, and a close that promises nothing about anybody else. Prove the transfer on Ewan in Exam Mode &mdash; where the diagnosis is obvious and the person underneath it is the thing being missed &mdash; then take Verity&rsquo;s disciplinary meeting to your colleagues: a bruised history, a hostile clock, and punish-lines on both sides of the adaptation. The domain continues with the next station.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="30_Station_4.9_PTSD_Assessment.html">Continue &middot; Station 4.9: PTSD Assessment &#8594;</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

