  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '30_Station_4.9_PTSD_Assessment'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 4.9 &middot; Domain 4</span>
      <span class="tag">MSE &amp; Phenomenology</span><span class="tag">PTSD</span>
      <span class="tag">Gen Adult</span><span class="tag">Risk &amp; Safety</span>
    </div>
    <h1>PTSD Assessment</h1>
    <p class="sub">Erin, 38, a train driver, has been off work for eight months. A man stepped in front of her train at Marsden Cutting and she has not been in a cab since. She sits down, puts both hands flat on her knees and says: &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll answer whatever you need. Just&hellip; please don&rsquo;t make me go through it again. I did that with the investigators for four hours and I couldn&rsquo;t drive home afterwards.&rdquo; Everything you need for the diagnosis is available in this room. <b>Almost none of it is in the story of that morning, and the way you ask is the whole station: around the event, not through it.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it becomes the handling test. Does this doctor set a contract in the first minute &mdash; what today is for, what will not be asked, and the fact that she can stop &mdash; and then get a complete diagnosis by asking how the thing lives in her <em>now</em>: the intrusions, the avoidance, the arousal, what she has concluded about herself? When distress rises in the room, is she steadied and reoriented, rather than pushed through it on the theory that reaching the memory is progress? Is her guilt asked for and heard before anyone rushes to argue with it? Are the alcohol and the risk screened plainly, without hiding behind the trauma? And does she leave the room steadier than she entered it, with an honest account of what treatment involves?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Almost every other assessment in psychiatry rewards going closer to the material. This one does not, and that is precisely why it is examined. The station tests four things. <b>The contract:</b> the first minute used to say what today is for, what will not be asked, and that she may stop or decline any question &mdash; because control is not a courtesy in this condition, it is the difference between an interview and a re-exposure. <b>The screening:</b> a complete diagnosis assembled from how the thing lives in her now &mdash; intrusive images and dreams that arrive uninvited, what she has stopped doing and where she will no longer go, the startle and the broken sleep and the checking, and the conclusions she has drawn about herself &mdash; with duration and functional cost established. Every criterion can be reached this way; none of it requires the narrative of the morning itself. <b>The steadying:</b> distress in the room managed rather than mined &mdash; slowed, grounded, reoriented, resumed only by permission &mdash; because a patient dissociating in front of you is not the assessment succeeding. And <b>the honest ground:</b> the guilt asked for and received before it is argued with, the alcohol and the risk screened plainly, and a close that is truthful about what treatment is: trauma-focused therapy in due course, not a single cathartic retelling today.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> the two failures sit on opposite sides of the same anxiety. The first is the detail hunt &mdash; the instinct that a proper history means the whole story, so the candidate walks her back into the cutting minute by minute, mistakes her flooding for productive contact with the memory, and ends the station with a woman who cannot drive home and a form with half the criteria unfilled. The second is the wide berth &mdash; the candidate so frightened of causing harm that nothing is asked at all: euphemisms, sympathy, careful circling, no intrusions established, no avoidance mapped, no guilt reached, no alcohol, no risk. It feels kind and it is empty, and the thing she most needed to say goes unsaid because it looked too raw to touch. Notice both in the decisions below.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure goes through the event. The other never goes anywhere near it. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 4.9</b> &middot; The outpatient clinic &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The detail hunt</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The wide berth</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE DETAIL HUNT.</b> Thorough, unhurried, apparently rigorous &mdash; and it does harm while filling in half a form.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Erin</span><span class="txt">I&rsquo;ll answer whatever you need. Just &mdash; please don&rsquo;t make me go through it again. I did four hours with the investigators and I couldn&rsquo;t drive myself home afterwards.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(kindly, pen ready)</em> I understand, and I&rsquo;ll be as gentle as I can &mdash; but I do need to understand what actually happened, because that&rsquo;s the event we&rsquo;re building everything else on. So take your time. It&rsquo;s the fourteenth of March, you&rsquo;re coming into the cutting &mdash; what&rsquo;s the first thing you see?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>She asked for one thing and it was declined in the politest possible terms. Note the reasoning, which is the trap in a sentence: <em>I need to understand what happened, because that&rsquo;s what everything is built on.</em> It is not. The diagnosis is built on how the event lives in her now &mdash; intrusions, avoidance, arousal, altered cognition, duration, function &mdash; and every one of those is reachable without a single detail of that morning. The narrative is the one part of the history that is both unnecessary today and capable of causing harm.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Erin</span><span class="txt"><em>(voice thinning, eyes fixed somewhere past his shoulder)</em> He was&hellip; there was a bag. He put a bag down first, on the &mdash; <em>(she stops. Her breathing changes. Her hands come off her knees.)</em> Sorry. Sorry. I can see it. It&rsquo;s like it&rsquo;s &mdash;</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(leaning in, softly, encouraging)</em> That&rsquo;s all right &mdash; that&rsquo;s exactly what we want. Stay with it if you can. I know it&rsquo;s hard, but talking about it is how it starts to lose its power, and this is important material. Take a breath, and tell me what you saw next.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Here is the misconception that makes this failure dangerous rather than merely unhelpful: <em>that&rsquo;s exactly what we want</em>. She is not disclosing; she is re-experiencing, in the room, without preparation, without a therapeutic frame, and with a stranger who has seven minutes and no plan for what to do with her afterwards. Catharsis is not a treatment for this condition &mdash; the evidence on single-session emotional debriefing runs the other way &mdash; and encouraging someone into a flashback in a first assessment is an intervention with a known capacity for harm. &ldquo;Stay with it&rdquo; is the wrong instruction from the wrong person at the wrong time.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 3:40pm)</em> Detailed account of index event obtained; patient tearful and distressed throughout, briefly unresponsive to questioning. Clear PTSD. Insufficient time to complete symptom review or risk assessment. Referred to psychology. <em>(Erin sits in the car park for fifty minutes before she can drive. She cancels the psychology appointment. Nobody has asked her about the wine, or about the nights she sleeps downstairs, or what she believes she should have seen.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The arithmetic of this failure is brutal. The one thing that could not be obtained safely was pursued for six minutes; the things that were both safe and essential &mdash; the symptom clusters, the drinking, the risk, what she has concluded about herself &mdash; were never reached. And the cost is not confined to a form: she has learned that telling a doctor about it makes her worse, which is the belief that will keep her out of treatment for the next year. This is the map&rsquo;s named trap, executed with kindness and a pen.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE WIDE BERTH.</b> Careful, warm, respectful of her request &mdash; and it assesses nothing at all.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Erin</span><span class="txt">I&rsquo;ll answer whatever you need. Just &mdash; please don&rsquo;t make me go through it again. I did four hours with the investigators and I couldn&rsquo;t drive myself home afterwards.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(gently, almost tiptoeing)</em> Of course. Absolutely not &mdash; we won&rsquo;t go anywhere near any of that, I promise. I can&rsquo;t imagine what you&rsquo;ve been through. Let&rsquo;s talk about something else entirely. How have things been at home? Is your husband supportive? Are you managing to get out at all?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The promise is the problem. &ldquo;We won&rsquo;t go anywhere near any of that&rdquo; sounds like respect and functions as avoidance &mdash; and it is worth noticing who it protects. She asked not to be walked through the event; she did not ask to have the entire condition placed off-limits. There is a wide, safe territory between the narrative and small talk, and this candidate has just conceded all of it. &ldquo;How are things at home&rdquo; will fill the time pleasantly and establish not one diagnostic criterion.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Erin</span><span class="txt"><em>(a flicker of something &mdash; disappointment, or relief, hard to tell)</em> Fine. It&rsquo;s all&hellip; fine. Mark&rsquo;s been good. <em>(pause)</em> I don&rsquo;t sleep much. <em>(She has just opened a door. She will not open it twice.)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">That&rsquo;s very understandable after everything. Sleep is often the last thing to come back &mdash; be kind to yourself, these things take time. There&rsquo;s no rush at all, and you mustn&rsquo;t feel you have to be over it by any particular point. Shall I write to your GP and arrange to see you again in a month, when things have settled a bit more?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>She said <em>I don&rsquo;t sleep much</em> &mdash; the smallest possible opening, offered by a woman who has been signalled all consultation that difficult things are unwelcome &mdash; and it was met with a platitude. Two safe questions were available and unasked: what happens when you try to sleep, and what gets you to sleep. The first reaches the nightmares; the second reaches the wine. Neither goes near the cutting. And &ldquo;when things have settled&rdquo; is a month of untreated illness dressed as patience.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 3:32pm)</em> Traumatic incident at work; understandably distressed. Did not wish to discuss the event and this was respected. Reassured that recovery takes time. Supportive husband. For review in one month. <em>(No intrusions recorded. No avoidance recorded &mdash; though she drives twenty miles around to avoid the bridge. No arousal, no guilt, no alcohol, no risk. The word &ldquo;PTSD&rdquo; does not appear, because nothing was asked that could have found it.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;This was respected&rdquo; is doing enormous work in that sentence, and what it is covering is a consultation in which no assessment took place. Note the symmetry with the detail hunt: both candidates left the room without the symptom clusters, the alcohol or the risk. One got there by going too close, the other by never leaving the doorway &mdash; and this version is the harder of the two to spot, because everyone in the room felt it went well.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> A contract in the first minute, the whole diagnosis reached around the event, distress steadied not mined &mdash; and she leaves calmer than she arrived.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Erin</span><span class="txt">I&rsquo;ll answer whatever you need. Just &mdash; please don&rsquo;t make me go through it again. I did four hours with the investigators and I couldn&rsquo;t drive myself home afterwards.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then we won&rsquo;t, and let me tell you why that isn&rsquo;t me being nice to you. I don&rsquo;t need the story of that morning to work out what&rsquo;s wrong or what will help &mdash; I need to know how it&rsquo;s been living in you since. So I&rsquo;m going to ask about now: your nights, what you&rsquo;ve stopped doing, what your body does when a door bangs. Three rules. You can say pass to anything. You can stop me at any point. And if anything starts to pull you back there, say so and we stop and come back to the room &mdash; that isn&rsquo;t you failing the appointment, it&rsquo;s information I need. All right?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The contract, and note that it does the opposite of the wide berth: it declines the narrative while explicitly claiming the rest of the territory, so nothing diagnostic is conceded. The three rules hand her control of an interview about an event in which she had none &mdash; which is not merely humane but clinically load-bearing in this condition. And the last clause is quietly expert: distress, if it comes, is pre-framed as data rather than as her failure, which makes it far likelier she will tell him when it starts instead of hiding it until she is gone.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Does it come back when you don&rsquo;t want it to &mdash; pictures, dreams? <em>(&ldquo;Every night. Three, four times a week I wake up with the noise. And in the day it just&hellip; arrives.&rdquo;)</em> When it arrives in the day, is it remembering, or is it more like it&rsquo;s happening? <em>(&ldquo;Happening. For a few seconds I&rsquo;m there.&rdquo;)</em> What have you stopped doing? <em>(&ldquo;Everything. Cab, obviously. I go twenty miles round to miss the bridge. I don&rsquo;t watch the news.&rdquo;)</em> And a door goes in the house &mdash; <em>(&ldquo;I&rsquo;m off the sofa. I sleep downstairs now, I don&rsquo;t know why. I check the doors twice.&rdquo;)</em> Eight months of that. What&rsquo;s it cost you? <em>(&ldquo;My job. And I don&rsquo;t &mdash; I&rsquo;m not really there with the kids.&rdquo;)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Watch what has just happened: the full diagnosis, and not one question about the fourteenth of March. Intrusions established and &mdash; crucially &mdash; phenomenologically distinguished, because <em>is it remembering, or is it more like it&rsquo;s happening?</em> is what separates re-experiencing from ordinary distressing recall. Avoidance mapped in behaviour rather than adjectives, hyperarousal caught in the specifics of a slammed door and a sofa she no longer sleeps above, duration and functional cost both stated in her own words. Every criterion, reached around the event.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">One more, and it might be the important one: in all this, what have you decided about yourself? <em>(A long silence. Then, flat:)</em> <em>&ldquo;That I should have seen him sooner. I&rsquo;ve gone over it &mdash; I could have had another second on the brake.&rdquo;</em> <em>(He doesn&rsquo;t rush in.)</em> Thank you for telling me that. I&rsquo;m not going to argue you out of it in the next four minutes, because you&rsquo;ve been arguing with it for eight months and it hasn&rsquo;t shifted &mdash; and that particular belief is exactly what the treatment works on. What&rsquo;s getting you to sleep? <em>(&ldquo;&hellip;Wine. Most nights. More than I&rsquo;d like.&rdquo;)</em> Thank you &mdash; that&rsquo;s honest and it matters. And have there been times you&rsquo;ve thought you&rsquo;d rather not be here? <em>(&ldquo;Not that. Just&hellip; I want it to stop.&rdquo;)</em> Right. Before you go: feet on the floor, tell me two things you can hear in this corridor. <em>(She does. Her shoulders drop.)</em> Good. That&rsquo;s how we finish &mdash; you leave here steadier than you came in.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Three difficult things, all landed. The guilt asked for directly and then <em>not</em> argued with &mdash; because premature reassurance here is the panic station&rsquo;s lesson transposed: it relieves nobody and it tells her the belief was too silly to examine. Instead it is named as the target of treatment, which dignifies it and gives it a future. Then alcohol and risk screened plainly, neither of them lost behind the trauma, and both received without a flinch. And the ending is the mark of someone who has done this before: the interview is closed deliberately with grounding, because in this condition the state a patient leaves in is part of the clinical outcome.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skills on a patient whose symptom is harder to classify &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; &ldquo;Please don&rsquo;t make me go through it again&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Her first sentence is a request. What does the opening minute do with it?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Negotiates it &mdash; agreeing to be gentle and to go at her pace, while explaining that some account of the event will still be needed today.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Grants it and claims the rest &mdash; the narrative declined outright, the territory that remains named, and three rules of control handed to her.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Honours it completely &mdash; a promise that nothing to do with the incident will be raised at all, so that she can feel entirely safe in the room.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Gently, at your pace, but I do need the account&rdquo; is the detail hunt with better manners &mdash; and it is a refusal of the only thing she asked for. The narrative is the one part of this history that is unnecessary today and capable of harm. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>That promise concedes far more than she requested. She asked not to be walked through the morning; she did not ask for the whole condition to be placed out of bounds &mdash; and a doctor who agrees to avoid everything has agreed to assess nothing. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The contract, and it does two jobs at once: the story is declined <em>and</em> the diagnostic territory is explicitly claimed &mdash; nights, avoidance, what her body does when a door goes &mdash; so nothing is surrendered. Then the three rules: say pass, stop me, tell me if it pulls you back. In a condition defined by an event during which she had no control, handing her control of the interview is not politeness; it is part of the method.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; Reaching the diagnosis</h3>
      <p class="stem">The criteria still have to be established. Without the narrative, where does the evidence come from?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">From the record &mdash; the investigation report and occupational health file give the event in detail, so the criteria can be checked against documents.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">From the present tense &mdash; what returns uninvited, what she has stopped doing, what her body does at a bang, what she has concluded about herself.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">From a rating scale &mdash; a validated PTSD questionnaire completed in the room, which covers every criterion without her having to speak about any of it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The file will tell you what happened on the railway and nothing about what is happening in her: no intrusions, no avoidance, no arousal, no cognitions. The diagnosis does not live in the event. It lives in the eight months since. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A scale is a useful adjunct and a poor interview. It cannot ask <em>is it remembering, or is it more like it&rsquo;s happening?</em>, it will not reach the wine or the guilt, and handing a distressed woman a form is a way of not being in the room with her. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The whole diagnosis is reachable in the present tense, and this is the technical heart of the station. Intrusions: does it come back when you don&rsquo;t want it to &mdash; and is it remembering, or more like it&rsquo;s happening? Avoidance, asked as behaviour: what have you stopped doing, where won&rsquo;t you go? Arousal, caught in specifics: the slammed door, the sofa, the double-checked locks. Cognition: what have you decided about yourself? Then duration and cost. Six criteria, no cutting.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; She starts to go</h3>
      <p class="stem">Mid-answer her breathing changes, her eyes fix past your shoulder, and she says &ldquo;sorry &mdash; I can see it.&rdquo; What now?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Support her through it &mdash; staying with the memory while she is in contact with it, since this is the material and avoidance maintains it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Stop and bring her back &mdash; name it, slow it down, orient her to the room and the present, and only resume the questions with her permission.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Give her space &mdash; sitting quietly and letting the wave pass without speaking, so that she is neither interrupted nor pressured while it happens.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>This is the reasoning that makes the detail hunt dangerous. Exposure is a treatment delivered inside a prepared therapeutic frame by someone who will still be there afterwards &mdash; not something improvised on a stranger in a seven-minute assessment. Encouraged reliving here has a known capacity to harm. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Silence is the right instinct in many rooms and the wrong one here: someone who is dissociating is already unmoored, and quiet company does not bring her back. She needs an anchor &mdash; a voice, an orientation, something to put her feet on. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Steadied, not mined. Name what is happening plainly, slow everything down, bring her attention to the present &mdash; the chair, the floor, two sounds in the corridor &mdash; and resume only when she says so. Note the reframe that makes this work: it is not the appointment going wrong, it is the appointment doing what it should. A doctor who can settle someone is also demonstrating, in miniature, that the distress is survivable and containable &mdash; which is a better advertisement for treatment than any leaflet.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; What she has decided about herself</h3>
      <p class="stem">Asked what she has concluded, she says: &ldquo;That I should have seen him sooner. I could have had another second on the brake.&rdquo; What follows?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Correction &mdash; the physics explained clearly and kindly: at that speed no driver could have stopped, and she needs to hear that plainly from a professional.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Receipt, then framing &mdash; thanked for saying it, not argued with in four minutes, and named as the very belief the treatment is designed to work on.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Exploration &mdash; the reasoning examined in detail: what she thinks she saw, when, and what she believes she could realistically have done differently.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>She has heard the physics from the investigators, her manager and her union, and the belief has not moved &mdash; because it is not a factual error, it is a survivor&rsquo;s conclusion. Correcting it in four minutes achieves nothing except teaching her the thought was too foolish to be taken seriously. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Examining what she saw and when is the detail hunt returning through a side door &mdash; it walks her straight back into the seconds before impact, which is precisely the territory the contract excluded. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Heard before it is handled. Thanking her for it acknowledges what it cost to say; declining to argue in the time available is honest rather than dismissive &mdash; <em>you&rsquo;ve been arguing with it for eight months and it hasn&rsquo;t shifted</em> &mdash; and naming it as the target of treatment does something no reassurance can: it gives the belief a future and a purpose. Survivor guilt is a core feature of this condition, not an incidental sadness, and it belongs in the formulation.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; The things that get missed</h3>
      <p class="stem">Time is going. What must not be left out of a PTSD assessment?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The trauma history &mdash; earlier adverse experiences, since prior trauma shapes both the presentation and the likely response to treatment.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Alcohol and risk &mdash; what gets her to sleep, and whether she has wanted not to be here; asked plainly, and not hidden behind the trauma.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The support network &mdash; her husband, family and colleagues, since social support is the strongest protective factor in the outcome literature.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Relevant, and not the priority in seven minutes &mdash; and asking a woman who has just declined one narrative to supply several earlier ones is a poor use of the trust she has extended. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Worth knowing, and it is the comfortable question. Support networks are safe ground for both parties; the wine and the wish for it all to stop are not, which is exactly why they are the ones that get skipped. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The two questions that both failing takes missed. Alcohol, because self-medicated sleep is the commonest complication of this condition and it will wreck the treatment if it goes unnamed &mdash; and note the route in, <em>what&rsquo;s getting you to sleep</em>, which is safe, specific and hard to dodge. And risk, asked directly. Neither belongs in the shadow of the trauma; both are ordinary psychiatric duties that a difficult topic does not suspend.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; &ldquo;So do I just have to talk about it?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">She asks what the treatment is, with visible dread. What is the honest answer?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Yes, in essence &mdash; the treatments work by processing the memory, so she should be prepared for the fact that talking about it is unavoidable.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Not today, and not like this &mdash; a structured therapy, done with preparation and at her pace, which is a different thing from retelling it cold.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Not necessarily &mdash; there are effective options that require very little discussion of the event itself, and medication can do much of the work.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Blunt, broadly true, and it will lose her &mdash; because what she is picturing is this morning repeated, and that is not what trauma-focused therapy is. An accurate sentence that produces a cancelled appointment has not informed anybody. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Overselling the escape route buys comfort now and a betrayal later. The trauma-focused therapies do engage with the memory, in a prepared and structured way; medication has a place and is not the main event. She will discover the truth in session two. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Honest consent again, in this station&rsquo;s form: the distinction between what she fears &mdash; retelling it cold to whoever is in the room &mdash; and what is actually offered: a structured, prepared, paced therapy delivered by someone who will still be there afterwards. It also draws the line under today: this assessment was deliberately not that, which is why the contract at the start was kept. Naming both protects the referral she is deciding, right now, whether to attend.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; The last ninety seconds</h3>
      <p class="stem">The history is complete and she is upset but holding. How does the station end?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Efficiently &mdash; the remaining time used for the plan: referral, sick note, GP letter and follow-up, so she leaves with the practical arranged.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">With her steadied &mdash; deliberately grounded and reoriented before she stands up, because the state she leaves in is part of the clinical outcome.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">With warmth &mdash; time taken to acknowledge how hard the appointment was and and to praise her courage in coming and speaking so openly today.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The plan matters and it is not the last thing that should happen. A woman discharged into a car park mid-surge will remember the surge, not the referral &mdash; and she has to drive home. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Kind, and it is a comment rather than an intervention. Praise does not reorient anybody; it fills the closing minute with warmth while leaving her exactly as activated as she was. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The detail that separates a doctor who has done this from one who has read about it. Feet on the floor, two sounds in the corridor, a slower breath &mdash; thirty seconds of deliberate grounding, then the plan. In this condition the state a patient walks out in is a clinical outcome, not an afterthought: it determines whether she can drive, and it is the evidence she will use to decide whether talking to professionals makes things better or worse.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Around the event, not through it.</h2>
    <p class="intro">The two failures sit on opposite sides of one anxiety, and both leave the room with the same empty form. <b>The detail hunt</b> comes from a decent instinct &mdash; a proper history means the whole story &mdash; and it is wrong here in a way it is wrong almost nowhere else in psychiatry. The candidate walks her back into the cutting, mistakes flooding for productive contact with the memory, says <em>stay with it</em> to a woman who is no longer entirely in the room, and finishes with the symptom clusters unasked, the alcohol unasked, the risk unasked and a patient who cannot drive home. Encouraged reliving in an unprepared seven-minute assessment is not a strong history; it is an intervention with a known capacity for harm, and it teaches her that telling doctors makes it worse. <b>The wide berth</b> is the mirror image and is harder to spot, because everybody leaves feeling it went well: the promise that <em>we won&rsquo;t go anywhere near any of that</em>, the euphemisms, the sympathetic circling, the small opening &mdash; <em>I don&rsquo;t sleep much</em> &mdash; met with a platitude. She asked not to be walked through the morning; she did not ask for her illness to be placed out of bounds, and a doctor who concedes the whole territory has assessed nothing. The pass takes the third road. It declines the narrative <em>and</em> claims everything else, in the first minute, out loud, with three rules that hand her control of an interview about an event in which she had none. Then it builds the entire diagnosis in the present tense &mdash; what returns uninvited and whether it is remembering or happening, what she has stopped doing, what her body does when a door bangs, what she has concluded about herself, for how long and at what cost. It steadies her rather than mining her when the distress rises. It asks for the guilt and then declines to argue with it. It asks about the wine and the wish for it to stop. And it ends deliberately, with her grounded, because in this condition the state she leaves in is part of the clinical outcome.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap in either direction: pushing for the trauma narrative &mdash; &ldquo;I do need to know what actually happened&rdquo;, &ldquo;stay with it&rdquo; &mdash; and retraumatising a patient who explicitly asked not to be taken there; or the opposite, promising to avoid the whole subject and producing a warm, empty consultation with no intrusions, no avoidance, no arousal, no guilt, no alcohol and no risk assessment in it.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make this station: the contract that declines the story while claiming the diagnosis &mdash; <em>&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t need that morning; I need to know how it&rsquo;s been living in you since&rdquo;</em>, with the three rules attached &mdash; and the question that nails the phenomenology without going near the cutting: <em>&ldquo;when it arrives in the day, is it remembering, or is it more like it&rsquo;s happening?&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, where this construct lives: D4&rsquo;s phenomenology and D1&rsquo;s interview craft, joined by a principle that generalises well beyond trauma &mdash; the information you need and the information the patient dreads giving are usually not the same information, and a doctor who can tell them apart gets a fuller history at a fraction of the cost. Second, the transfer Exam Mode tests: the same disciplines on a symptom that is genuinely hard to classify, where a young man says he can see the man who attacked him standing in the room &mdash; and the whole case turns on what that experience actually is.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every trauma assessment</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Contract in the first minute.</b> Say what today is for, decline the narrative explicitly, name the territory you <em>will</em> cover, and hand over three rules: say pass, stop me, tell me if it pulls you back. Control is not a courtesy in this condition; it is part of the method.</li>
      <li><b>Get the whole diagnosis in the present tense.</b> What returns uninvited; what she has stopped doing and where she will not go; what her body does at a bang; what she has concluded about herself; for how long, at what cost. Every criterion is reachable without the story.</li>
      <li><b>Ask the phenomenological question.</b> <em>Is it remembering, or is it more like it&rsquo;s happening?</em> That single question separates re-experiencing from ordinary distressing recall &mdash; and it costs the patient nothing.</li>
      <li><b>Steady, don&rsquo;t mine.</b> If distress rises: name it, slow it, orient to the room, resume only by permission. Catharsis is not treatment, and encouraged reliving without a prepared frame can do harm. Settling someone also demonstrates that the distress is survivable.</li>
      <li><b>Ask for the guilt, then don&rsquo;t argue with it.</b> Survivor cognitions are core features, not incidental sadness. Receive it, decline to demolish it in four minutes, and name it as what the treatment works on &mdash; that gives the belief a future instead of a rebuttal.</li>
      <li><b>Never let the trauma hide the ordinary duties.</b> What gets you to sleep, and have you wanted not to be here. Alcohol and risk are the two things both failure modes miss, and a difficult topic does not suspend basic psychiatry.</li>
      <li><b>End it deliberately.</b> Ground before she stands: feet, sounds, a slower breath &mdash; then the plan. The state a patient leaves in is a clinical outcome, and it decides whether she believes that talking to professionals helps or harms.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the same disciplines thirty-odd years late, in a man who has never told anyone anything &mdash; where the detail hunt ends the interview and the wide berth wastes the only chance he will give you.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Iwan, 58, a warehouse supervisor, served in the army in his early twenties. His wife made this appointment and drove him here. The GP letter says: &ldquo;Sleep disturbance, irritability, drinking. Wife reports nightmares and shouting in his sleep, worse since their son joined up in March.&rdquo; He has never spoken to anyone about his service. Assess him.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Set a contract and hand him control &mdash; he is expecting to be asked what he saw, and he will not answer it.</li>
            <li>Establish the diagnosis in the present tense &mdash; intrusions, avoidance, arousal, cognition, duration, function.</li>
            <li>Ask the phenomenological question about the daytime experiences.</li>
            <li>Screen the alcohol and the risk plainly &mdash; neither is optional, and neither hides behind the history.</li>
            <li>Be honest about what treatment is, and that thirty-five years is not too late.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Iwan &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Iwan, 58. You sit with your arms folded and your coat on, and you open flatly: &ldquo;Right &mdash; before we start, I&rsquo;m here because Bethan made me. I&rsquo;m not talking about what happened out there. That&rsquo;s the deal. So if that&rsquo;s what this is, we can both save ourselves half an hour.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          &bull; Present-tense symptoms, given freely to present-tense questions: nightmares four or five nights a week, always the same one; you wake shouting and have slept in the back bedroom for six years. Daytime episodes since March where you are &ldquo;back there for a few seconds &mdash; not remembering it, in it&rdquo;. You cannot watch the news, you left your son&rsquo;s passing-out parade after ten minutes, you sit facing the door in the pub, and you check the house twice a night.<br>
          &bull; Cognition, given only to a direct question about what you have decided about yourself: &ldquo;That I&rsquo;m the one who came back.&rdquo; You will not elaborate, and you should not be pushed &mdash; but if it is received without argument you add, quietly: &ldquo;Two didn&rsquo;t. I don&rsquo;t know why it was them.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; Alcohol, admitted if asked plainly and without a lecture: six or seven cans most nights, starting at about nine, &ldquo;or I don&rsquo;t go off at all&rdquo;. You have not been drunk in years and you do not consider it a problem; you consider it the only thing that works.<br>
          &bull; Risk, if asked directly: no plans and no intent &mdash; but &ldquo;there&rsquo;ve been mornings I&rsquo;ve thought it&rsquo;d be easier if I didn&rsquo;t wake up&rdquo;. You say this matter-of-factly and move on quickly. If the candidate skips risk entirely, mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the detail hunt:</b> any request for the account &mdash; what you saw, what happened, &ldquo;just broadly&rdquo;, &ldquo;in your own time&rdquo; &mdash; and you stand up, put your hand on the door and say: &ldquo;I told you. That was the one thing.&rdquo; Leave unless the candidate immediately and genuinely repairs it by dropping the request altogether. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the wide berth:</b> if the candidate is so careful that nothing real gets asked &mdash; sympathy, generalities, &ldquo;how are things at home&rdquo; &mdash; go monosyllabic, then at the end: &ldquo;So what was the point of that, then?&rdquo; Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; The pass: a candidate who tells you plainly that they do not need the story, names what they <em>do</em> need, and gives you the right to stop &mdash; gets all of it. At the end you say: &ldquo;Thirty-five years and nobody&rsquo;s ever asked me it like that.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the narrative declined explicitly while the diagnosis was still obtained, was the phenomenological question asked, were the drinking and the risk both screened, and did he leave steadier than he arrived? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="control"><td colspan="2"><b>The control (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="control">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="control"></td><td>The narrative declined explicitly &mdash; and the reason given, so it reads as method rather than squeamishness.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="control"></td><td>Rules of control handed over &mdash; say pass, stop me, tell me if it pulls you back.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="around"><td colspan="2"><b>The screening</b><span class="dc" data-dc="around">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="around"></td><td>Intrusions and avoidance established in the present tense, as behaviour and not adjectives.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="around"></td><td>The phenomenological question asked &mdash; remembering, or more like it is happening.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="around"></td><td>Arousal, duration and functional cost covered &mdash; the back bedroom, the door seat, the parade.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="steady"><td colspan="2"><b>The steadying</b><span class="dc" data-dc="steady">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="steady"></td><td>No request for the account at any point &mdash; not even softened, hedged or &ldquo;broadly&rdquo;.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="steady"></td><td>The survivor cognition asked for, received, and not argued with in the time available.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="steady"></td><td>Pace managed and the ending deliberate &mdash; he leaves steadier than he came in.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="risk"><td colspan="2"><b>The risk</b><span class="dc" data-dc="risk">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Alcohol asked plainly, by route rather than lecture &mdash; what gets you to sleep.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Risk asked directly, and the &ldquo;easier if I didn&rsquo;t wake up&rdquo; answer received without alarm or dismissal.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Neither hidden behind the trauma nor postponed to a future appointment.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Treatment described honestly &mdash; structured, prepared, paced; not a retelling; thirty-five years is not too late.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>A real next step and the door left open &mdash; documented, with his wife&rsquo;s account offered a route in.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Iwan enforces the station&rsquo;s discipline with a door. He announces the one boundary in his first sentence, which makes the detail hunt not merely unwise but terminal &mdash; and candidates discover that &ldquo;just broadly&rdquo; and &ldquo;in your own time&rdquo; are the same request wearing softer clothes. Equally, he punishes the wide berth with the sentence that should haunt anyone who mistakes gentleness for care: <em>so what was the point of that, then?</em> Between those two walls lies everything &mdash; nightmares, the back bedroom, the parade he walked out of, the seat facing the door &mdash; all of it freely available to present-tense questions. His cognition, <em>that I&rsquo;m the one who came back</em>, teaches receipt without argument in its hardest form, because the urge to say something consoling is almost irresistible and would close the subject for another thirty-five years. And the drinking he does not consider a problem, plus the mornings he would rather not wake, are the two duties that a difficult history does not suspend.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>Same disciplines. A symptom that could be two things. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Erin proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: <b>the same disciplines on an experience that has to be classified before anything can be done about it</b> &mdash; a young man who says he can see the man who attacked him, standing in the room. Get the phenomenology wrong and he goes home on an antipsychotic with a diagnosis that will follow him for thirty years. Get it right without ever asking him to describe the attack, and the case solves itself. Timed, no feedback, no second tries: this is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Idris</b>, 26, works in a phone shop. Five months ago he was threatened with a knife during a robbery at the shop; he was not physically hurt. He was referred urgently by his GP after telling her: <em>&ldquo;I see him. He&rsquo;s standing by the counter and I can see him as clearly as I can see you.&rdquo;</em> The referral letter asks whether he needs an antipsychotic. He arrives frightened and embarrassed, and his first sentence is: &ldquo;Just tell me straight &mdash; am I going mad?&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for the moment you tell Idris what this experience actually is &mdash; without asking him for one detail of that night.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Idris, I can tell you what this is, and it isn&#8217;t what you&#8217;re afraid of&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Named plainly &mdash; a flashback, memory intruding rather than perception inventing; ordinary words he could repeat to his mother.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> His actual fear answered directly &mdash; what this is not, and why, rather than a bare &ldquo;you&rsquo;re not going mad&rdquo;.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No account of that night asked for or implied anywhere in the explanation.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> A next step attached &mdash; what treatment is, what happens meanwhile, and who he contacts.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the experience named without the event &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Around the event, not through it.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the anatomy is installed: a contract in the first minute that declines the story and claims the diagnosis, the whole picture assembled in the present tense, the phenomenological question that costs the patient nothing, distress steadied rather than mined, the guilt received before it is argued with, the alcohol and the risk asked plainly, and an ending that leaves her steadier than she arrived. Prove the transfer on Idris in Exam Mode &mdash; where an experience has to be classified before anyone reaches for a prescription &mdash; then take Iwan to your colleagues: thirty-five years of silence, a door he will walk out of, and a question that makes the wide berth indefensible. The domain continues with the next station.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="31_Station_4.10_Alcohol_Dependence_Assessment.html">Continue &middot; Station 4.10: Alcohol Dependence Assessment &#8594;</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

