  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '31_Station_4.10_Alcohol_Dependence_Assessment'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 4.10 &middot; Domain 4</span>
      <span class="tag">MSE &amp; Phenomenology</span><span class="tag">Alcohol Dependence</span>
      <span class="tag">Addictions</span><span class="tag">Risk &amp; Safety</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Alcohol Dependence Assessment</h1>
    <p class="sub">Duncan, 47, a self-employed courier, has come because his partner Kelly told him to. He is pleasant, apologetic and slightly damp, and he opens with what sounds like the best news you will hear all day: &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve made my mind up. Last drink was this morning and that&rsquo;s me done &mdash; I&rsquo;m stopping tonight. I just need something to help me sleep through it.&rdquo; Everything in his manner invites you to work on his motivation. <b>Two years ago he had a fit on a hotel bathroom floor two days after stopping, and nobody in this room has asked. The units are not the assessment, and tonight is not a plan &mdash; it is a medical emergency waiting to be booked in.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it becomes the safety test underneath a friendly conversation. Does this doctor elicit the <em>dependence syndrome</em> &mdash; craving, impaired control, relief drinking, tolerance, narrowed repertoire, primacy over everything else &mdash; rather than totting up units and pronouncing on limits? Are the two questions asked that decide what happens tonight: when was the last drink, and what happens to his body when he does not have one? Is the withdrawal history taken properly &mdash; fits, hallucinations, previous detoxes &mdash; because that single answer changes the setting of treatment? Is thiamine thought about before the plan is written? And when he announces he is stopping tonight, is he told the counterintuitive truth: not yet, not on your own, and keep drinking until we can do this safely?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Alcohol is the commonest serious drug problem a psychiatrist meets and the one most often assessed badly, because two easy consultations are available and both feel productive. The station tests four things. <b>The syndrome:</b> dependence established as a clinical pattern &mdash; the compulsion to drink, the loss of control once started, drinking to relieve rather than to enjoy, rising tolerance, the repertoire narrowing to one drink bought in one shop at one time, and the steady promotion of alcohol above work, money and the people he loves. Units describe consumption; the syndrome is the diagnosis. <b>The two questions:</b> when was the last drink, and what happens when there isn&rsquo;t one &mdash; the shakes, the sweats, the morning retching, the drink at nine that settles it. Those two answers decide what has to happen in the next twenty-four hours. <b>The danger:</b> a withdrawal history taken deliberately &mdash; ever had a fit, ever seen or heard things that were not there, how did previous detoxes go &mdash; because a single prior withdrawal seizure moves this from a community plan to a supervised one, and because Wernicke&rsquo;s encephalopathy is prevented by thiamine given early rather than diagnosed late. And <b>the counterintuitive instruction:</b> a dependent drinker who announces he is stopping tonight must be told, kindly and clearly, to keep drinking until assisted withdrawal can be arranged.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> both failures are competent-looking consultations that leave the same hole. The first is the units audit &mdash; a meticulous quantity-and-frequency history, the arithmetic done, the safe limits explained, a drink diary issued and a gradual reduction advised. It is thorough, it is numerate, and it never once asks what happens to him at six in the morning. The second is warmer and more dangerous: the motivational conversation, where a skilled candidate explores ambivalence beautifully, hears him commit to abstinence from tonight, praises the decision and books follow-up in a fortnight &mdash; sending a man with a previous withdrawal seizure home to stop unaided. Notice that the friendlier consultation is the one that could kill him, and that neither candidate asked a single question about his body.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure counts the drinks. The other builds the motivation. Neither examines the patient. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 4.10</b> &middot; The outpatient clinic &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The units audit</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The motivation conversation</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE UNITS AUDIT.</b> Numerate, systematic, entirely about consumption &mdash; and it never touches the patient&rsquo;s body.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Duncan</span><span class="txt">I&rsquo;ve made my mind up, doctor. Last drink was this morning and that&rsquo;s me finished &mdash; I&rsquo;m stopping tonight. Kelly&rsquo;s had enough and she&rsquo;s right. I just need something to get me through the first few nights, that&rsquo;s all I&rsquo;m after.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(pen out, working through it)</em> Good, well let&rsquo;s get an accurate picture first. What are you drinking &mdash; spirits, beer? <em>(&ldquo;Vodka.&rdquo;)</em> And how much in a typical day? <em>(&ldquo;Bottle and a half, near enough.&rdquo;)</em> Right &mdash; so that&rsquo;s about sixty units a day, which is over four hundred a week. The recommended limit is fourteen. So we&rsquo;re looking at something like thirty times the guideline. Does that surprise you? How many days a week would you say that&rsquo;s the pattern?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Accurate arithmetic, correct guideline, and not one clinically useful thing established. &ldquo;Thirty times the guideline&rdquo; tells a man drinking a bottle and a half of vodka something he could have worked out himself, and it tells the doctor nothing about whether he is dependent, what happens when he stops, or what is going to happen to him tonight. Note also what the number does in the room: it invites shame, and shame in an alcohol history reliably produces smaller numbers from here on.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Duncan</span><span class="txt"><em>(shifting)</em> Every day, more or less. Look &mdash; I know it&rsquo;s bad. That&rsquo;s why I&rsquo;m stopping. <em>(He rubs his hands on his knees. They are not steady, and the movement is to hide it.)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">And that&rsquo;s a really positive step. My advice would be not to go from sixty units to zero overnight &mdash; that&rsquo;s a big drop &mdash; so what I&rsquo;d suggest is a gradual reduction: cut it by about a quarter each week, and keep a drink diary so we can see the trend. Here&rsquo;s a form for that. Any thoughts on what you&rsquo;ll replace it with in the evenings? Exercise can be very helpful at this stage.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>His hands are shaking in front of the candidate and it has not been remarked on &mdash; the physical sign that answers the question nobody asked. And notice the shape of the advice: a self-directed taper, a diary and a chat about exercise, offered to a man who is physically dependent and who will not be able to execute a quarter reduction because his body will make the decision for him at four in the morning. This is a lifestyle intervention delivered to a medical problem.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 2:20pm)</em> Alcohol history: vodka, approx 60 units/day, daily pattern. Advised re: recommended limits. Motivated to change. Advised gradual reduction rather than abrupt cessation; drink diary issued. Lifestyle advice given. Review 4 weeks. <em>(Nobody asked when he last drank, or what happens when he doesn&rsquo;t. Nobody asked about the hotel bathroom two years ago. He goes home, follows the advice as best he can, and by Thursday he is drinking more than when he came in.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>This is the map&rsquo;s named trap in its purest form: the units audited, the syndrome never elicited, and the medically dangerous thing &mdash; what his body does without alcohol tonight &mdash; not examined at all. The note reads competently and contains no diagnosis: dependence is nowhere stated, because nothing was asked that could have established it. And the taper advice, which sounds cautious, is the one plan that a dependent drinker cannot deliver alone.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE MOTIVATION CONVERSATION.</b> Warm, skilful, genuinely good technique &mdash; deployed at the wrong moment, and far more dangerous than the audit.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Duncan</span><span class="txt">I&rsquo;ve made my mind up. Last drink was this morning and that&rsquo;s me finished &mdash; I&rsquo;m stopping tonight. Kelly&rsquo;s had enough and she&rsquo;s right.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(warmly, leaning in)</em> That&rsquo;s a big decision, and I can hear how much thought has gone into it. Can I ask &mdash; what&rsquo;s made this the moment? <em>(&ldquo;Kelly. And I missed my nephew&rsquo;s christening. I was in the van outside and I couldn&rsquo;t go in.&rdquo;)</em> That sounds like it really mattered to you. And if you imagine six months from now, sober &mdash; what does that look like? What would Kelly notice first?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>This is good technique. Open questions, change talk elicited, values connected to behaviour &mdash; in an outpatient follow-up this would be excellent work. The problem is sequence, not skill. A man has said he is going to stop drinking tonight, and before any of this can safely happen somebody has to find out whether his body will tolerate it. The candidate is building a plan on a foundation nobody has checked, and every warm minute spent here is a minute not spent on the two questions that decide whether he is safe to go home.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Duncan</span><span class="txt"><em>(emotional now)</em> She&rsquo;d notice I was there. Properly there. <em>(pause)</em> I do feel rough in the mornings, mind. Till I&rsquo;ve had one.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">That&rsquo;s completely normal, and it does pass &mdash; the first few days are the hardest and then it gets easier. Honestly, the fact you&rsquo;ve got this level of determination is the single best predictor of success. Let&rsquo;s make tonight the start of it. I&rsquo;ll refer you to the community service, they&rsquo;ll usually be in touch within a fortnight, and I&rsquo;ll see you myself in two weeks to see how you&rsquo;ve got on. Well done, genuinely.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>He just said it: <em>I feel rough in the mornings till I&rsquo;ve had one.</em> That is relief drinking &mdash; the cardinal feature of physical dependence &mdash; volunteered without being asked, and it was received as an ordinary hangover and reassured away. Two follow-up questions were sitting there: rough how, and what happens if you don&rsquo;t have one. Instead the candidate has endorsed an unsupervised cessation tonight, praised him for it, and arranged to find out how it went in a fortnight.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 2:14pm)</em> Strong motivation to achieve abstinence; clear change talk, good insight, supportive partner. Plans to cease alcohol from today. Referred to community alcohol service. Review in 2 weeks. <em>(Duncan does exactly what he said he would. On Wednesday evening, thirty-six hours after his last drink, Kelly finds him on the kitchen floor. It is the second fit of his life, and the first one is written in a set of hospital notes nobody in that clinic asked about.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The most instructive fact about this take is that everybody left the room feeling good. Rapport excellent, technique textbook, patient grateful, note glowing &mdash; and the outcome is a withdrawal seizure that was entirely predictable from a question that takes four seconds to ask. Warmth is not safety. In this station the friendly consultation is the lethal one, and the examiner is watching for whether a candidate can hold a good conversation and a medical assessment in mind at the same time.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The syndrome elicited, the two questions asked, the seizure found &mdash; and the counterintuitive instruction delivered so that he accepts it.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Duncan</span><span class="txt">I&rsquo;ve made my mind up. Last drink was this morning and that&rsquo;s me finished &mdash; I&rsquo;m stopping tonight. I just need something to get me through the first few nights.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I&rsquo;m glad you&rsquo;ve decided, and I&rsquo;m going to take it seriously enough to ask some dull medical questions first, because how we do this matters as much as that you do it. This morning &mdash; what time, and how much? <em>(&ldquo;Seven-ish. Couple of large ones before I got in the van.&rdquo;)</em> And if you don&rsquo;t have that first one &mdash; what happens? <em>(A pause. Then, quietly:)</em> <em>&ldquo;Shakes. Sweat through the sheets. I&rsquo;m sick until I&rsquo;ve had it. It goes about half nine.&rdquo;</em> Thank you. That&rsquo;s the most useful thing you&rsquo;ve told me, and it changes what I&rsquo;m going to advise.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The two questions, asked inside the first minute, and framed so that they read as taking him seriously rather than as delay. Time and quantity of the last drink puts him on the withdrawal clock; <em>what happens if you don&rsquo;t have it</em> establishes relief drinking &mdash; morning tremor, sweats, vomiting, settling within hours of the first drink &mdash; which is physical dependence demonstrated rather than asserted. Note the last line: telling him it changes the advice prepares him to hear something he did not come for.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Now the ones that decide where this happens. Have you ever had a fit &mdash; ever come round on the floor &mdash; when you&rsquo;ve stopped or cut down? <em>(He looks at his hands.)</em> <em>&ldquo;Once. Two years back, in a hotel. I&rsquo;d gone two days without. Ambulance and everything. I&rsquo;ve not told Kelly that.&rdquo;</em> Thank you for telling me &mdash; that is exactly what I needed to know. Have you ever seen or heard things that weren&rsquo;t there when you&rsquo;ve stopped? <em>(&ldquo;No. Bugs on the wall once, coming down. Just the once.&rdquo;)</em> And the drinking itself: is it still doing anything for you, or is it just to feel normal? <em>(&ldquo;Just normal. I don&rsquo;t even like it.&rdquo;)</em> Eating? <em>(&ldquo;Not really. Toast, maybe.&rdquo;)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Everything in that exchange changes management. A previous withdrawal seizure and a probable brief perceptual disturbance move this out of the category of a community taper and into supervised, medically assisted withdrawal &mdash; and both facts existed in both failing takes, undiscovered, because nobody asked. Note the syndrome being completed alongside: drinking that has stopped producing pleasure and now only restores normality is the narrowed, relief-driven pattern of established dependence. And the question about eating is not small talk; it is the first move of the thiamine decision.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">So here is the part you won&rsquo;t like, and I want to explain it rather than just say it. Do not stop tonight. With a fit already behind you, coming off suddenly on your own is genuinely dangerous &mdash; the risk is another seizure, and worse. So: keep drinking at your usual level, no more, until we do this properly &mdash; and I know how that sounds, coming from me. What I&rsquo;m arranging is a supervised withdrawal with medication to cover it, and given that seizure I&rsquo;ll be pushing for it to be done as an inpatient. Thiamine starts today, not on the day you stop, because months of toast is its own risk. I&rsquo;ll ring the service this afternoon and phone you with a date. Two more things: no driving that van if you&rsquo;re shaking or you&rsquo;ve drunk &mdash; and if you get confused, or see things, or have any kind of fit before we start, that is 999, not a wait-and-see. Does that sound like being fobbed off, or like a plan?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The counterintuitive instruction, delivered with its reasoning &mdash; which is what stops it sounding like permission to carry on drinking. Note the components an examiner is ticking: abrupt unaided cessation refused and explained; the interim instruction explicit (usual level, no more); assisted withdrawal offered with the setting justified by the seizure history; thiamine started now rather than at detox; the occupational risk named without a lecture; and a safety-net in plain words with a threshold he can actually apply. Then the last question, which converts an instruction into an agreement &mdash; because a man told to keep drinking needs to leave understanding why, or he will simply do what he planned.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same knowledge on a ward, at speed, where the diagnosis has already started &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; &ldquo;I&rsquo;m stopping tonight&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">He announces abstinence from this evening and asks for something to help him sleep. Where does the assessment start?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">With the quantity &mdash; an accurate consumption history first, since what he is drinking determines everything that follows about how to stop it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">With the clock and the body &mdash; when the last drink was and how large it was, and what happens to him in the morning when there isn&rsquo;t one.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">With the decision &mdash; what has brought him to this point, since the motivation is fragile and deserves to be strengthened before anything else.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Quantity is worth knowing and it is not what is urgent. Sixty units a day and six units a day can both be dependence or neither; what tells you is what his body does without it. The number describes the drinking, not the danger. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Motivation is real work and this is the wrong moment for it &mdash; he has announced a plan for tonight, and building on it before checking whether tonight is safe is constructing on unexamined ground. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The two questions that decide the next twenty-four hours. The time and size of the last drink puts him on the withdrawal clock; <em>what happens if you don&rsquo;t have it</em> reaches the morning tremor, the sweats, the vomiting and the drink that settles it by half nine &mdash; relief drinking, which is physical dependence demonstrated rather than assumed. Framing them as dull medical questions asked because the decision deserves to be taken seriously is what stops them feeling like an obstacle.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; Units, or the syndrome</h3>
      <p class="stem">The drinking history has to be taken properly. What is actually being established?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Consumption in detail &mdash; type, volume, pattern across the week and the arithmetic done, so the severity can be quantified and fed back to him.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The pattern of dependence &mdash; compulsion, impaired control, relief drinking, tolerance, narrowed repertoire, and alcohol above everything else.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The consequences &mdash; the harm done to work, money, health and relationships, which is what establishes that the drinking has become a disorder.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>This is the named trap. The arithmetic is easy, it feels rigorous, and it produces a number rather than a diagnosis &mdash; plus a dose of shame that makes every subsequent answer smaller. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Harm matters and it does not distinguish dependence from heavy harmful drinking &mdash; plenty of non-dependent drinkers wreck their finances, and the distinction decides whether he can safely stop tonight. Not the same question. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The syndrome is the diagnosis and units are just the volume. Craving; being unable to stop once started; drinking to relieve rather than to enjoy &mdash; <em>&ldquo;just to feel normal, I don&rsquo;t even like it&rdquo;</em>; tolerance; the repertoire narrowed to one drink, one shop, one time of day; and primacy, with the van, the money and the christening all giving way to it. That pattern tells you what he has, what will happen when it stops, and what treatment he needs.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; The question that changes the setting</h3>
      <p class="stem">You now know he is dependent. What single piece of history most changes the management plan?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">How much he drinks &mdash; the daily amount, since the severity of withdrawal scales with the quantity his body has become accustomed to.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Whether he has ever had a fit, or seen things that were not there, when stopping &mdash; and how any previous detoxes went for him.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Whether he has support at home &mdash; a partner who is present and willing is what makes a community withdrawal feasible or impossible.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Quantity correlates loosely and predicts poorly. The man drinking less with two previous withdrawal seizures is in far more danger than the man drinking more with none, and only one of those facts is on the table. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Home support genuinely shapes feasibility and it is a second-order question. It determines whether a community detox is practical; the seizure history determines whether one is safe at all. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>One prior withdrawal seizure changes everything &mdash; it predicts recurrence, and it moves this out of self-directed tapering and community management into supervised, medically assisted withdrawal, with a strong case for inpatient care. Ask it directly and concretely: <em>ever had a fit, ever come round on the floor, when you stopped or cut down?</em> Add hallucinations and previous detox experience. In both failing takes that hotel bathroom existed and stayed invisible, because the question was never put.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; &ldquo;So I&rsquo;ll stop tonight then&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">He is dependent, has had a withdrawal seizure, and intends to stop unaided this evening. What is he told?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">To reduce gradually &mdash; a structured taper over the coming weeks rather than abrupt cessation, which avoids the dangers of stopping suddenly.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Not tonight, and not alone &mdash; keep drinking at his usual level until a supervised, medicated withdrawal is arranged, with the reason explained.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">To go to A&amp;E &mdash; if he is determined to stop today, the safest place for an unplanned withdrawal with a seizure history is an emergency department.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A self-directed taper is the plan a physically dependent drinker cannot execute &mdash; his body sets the pace, not his intentions &mdash; and prescribing willpower to a man with a seizure history is not a safety plan. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>An emergency department is where you send someone already withdrawing dangerously, not a man who is currently drinking and can be booked properly. Sending him there now converts a plannable admission into an unplanned crisis. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The instruction that feels wrong and is right: <em>keep drinking at your usual level, no more, until we can do this safely.</em> Said flatly it sounds like permission or a fob-off, so it comes with its reason &mdash; a previous fit means unaided cessation carries a real risk of another one, and worse &mdash; and with the alternative attached immediately: supervised withdrawal, medication to cover it, inpatient given that history, and a date. The mark is for the sequence: refuse, explain, replace.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; Toast, mostly</h3>
      <p class="stem">He has been eating almost nothing for months. What follows from that?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Dietary advice and monitoring &mdash; nutrition addressed as part of the recovery plan, with weight and bloods checked at the next review.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Thiamine, starting today &mdash; and parenteral with any suggestion of Wernicke&rsquo;s, because this is prevented early rather than diagnosed late.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Blood tests first &mdash; liver function, vitamin levels and a full screen, so that any deficiency is confirmed before treatment is committed to.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Nutrition advice for a man living on toast and vodka is not wrong, it is just far too slow for the actual risk &mdash; and the harm here is neurological and can arrive within days, not a matter for the next review. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Waiting for confirmation is exactly the error this condition punishes. The tests are unreliable, the treatment is safe, and the damage is irreversible &mdash; which is why thiamine is given on suspicion and not on proof. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Thiamine now, not on the day the detox begins &mdash; the deficiency exists already, and glucose without it can precipitate the very thing you are trying to prevent. Any hint of confusion, eye signs or unsteadiness moves it to parenteral treatment urgently. This is the small physical item that most reliably distinguishes a psychiatrist who has managed dependent drinkers from one who has only talked to them.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; The van</h3>
      <p class="stem">He is a self-employed courier and drives all day. How is this handled?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Left for now &mdash; raising his livelihood in the first appointment risks the engagement, and it can be picked up once the withdrawal is under way.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Named plainly and briefly &mdash; not driving while drinking or shaking, the licensing duty stated once, without a lecture and without a threat.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Made the priority &mdash; the immediate risk to the public is the overriding concern, so the obligations and consequences are set out in full.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Deferring it is the comfortable choice and leaves a dependent drinker driving a van all week with your knowledge and no advice. Engagement is not preserved by omitting the thing you would be criticised for omitting. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Making it the centrepiece converts a medical consultation into an enforcement interview, and a man who feels his livelihood is under threat will minimise everything from that point on &mdash; including the drinking. Proportion matters. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Said once, clearly, in proportion: do not drive having drunk or while withdrawing, and the duty to inform the licensing authority explained as a fact rather than brandished as a penalty. It belongs in this consultation because it is both a safety issue and something he needs to plan around financially &mdash; and the way it is said determines whether he tells you the truth about anything else.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; The last minute</h3>
      <p class="stem">The plan is made. What does he leave the room with?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Encouragement and a referral &mdash; his decision affirmed, the service referral made, and a follow-up appointment so momentum is not lost here.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The interim instruction, a date, thiamine and a threshold &mdash; what to do tonight, and what counts as an emergency before the date arrives.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Written information &mdash; leaflets on dependence and local services, so that the detail survives a consultation he may not remember clearly.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Affirmation plus a referral is the second failing take with better timing. He came in intending to stop tonight; unless he leaves knowing not to, and why, encouragement is the last thing he hears before doing the dangerous thing. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Leaflets are a supplement, not a plan &mdash; and the one instruction that matters tonight is the one he will not believe unless a doctor says it to his face and explains it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Four concrete things: keep drinking at your usual level until we do this properly; a date being arranged today with a phone call to follow; thiamine started now; and a threshold in plain words &mdash; confusion, seeing things, or any fit means 999 rather than waiting. Then the check that converts instruction into agreement: <em>does that sound like being fobbed off, or like a plan?</em> A man told to keep drinking must understand why, or he will simply do what he came in intending to do.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Count the syndrome, not the units.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Both failures produce a respectable-looking consultation with the same hole in the middle of it. <b>The units audit</b> is the named trap: the quantity established, the arithmetic performed, the guideline quoted, a drink diary issued and a gradual reduction advised. It is thorough, numerate and diagnostically empty &mdash; sixty units a day tells you about the drinking and nothing about the drinker, the number invites shame that shrinks every subsequent answer, and a self-directed taper is precisely the plan a physically dependent man cannot execute, because his body will set the pace at four in the morning. <b>The motivational conversation</b> is warmer, technically better, and more dangerous. The technique is genuinely good; the sequence is fatal. A man announces he will stop tonight, mentions in passing that he feels rough in the mornings <em>until he&rsquo;s had one</em> &mdash; relief drinking, volunteered unasked &mdash; and it is heard as a hangover and reassured away, while his determination is praised and follow-up is booked for a fortnight. Everyone leaves feeling the appointment went well, and thirty-six hours later he has his second withdrawal seizure. The pass holds the conversation and the medicine in mind at once: the two questions inside the first minute &mdash; when was the last drink, and what happens when there isn&rsquo;t one; the dependence syndrome elicited as a pattern rather than a volume; the withdrawal history asked directly, because one prior fit moves this from a community taper to supervised withdrawal; thiamine started today rather than on detox day; the driving named once, plainly and in proportion; and then the counterintuitive instruction delivered with its reasoning attached &mdash; <em>do not stop tonight, keep drinking at your usual level, until we can do this safely</em> &mdash; followed by a date, a threshold and a question that turns an instruction into an agreement.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap in either form: the units audited and the syndrome never elicited &mdash; limits quoted, diary issued, dependence nowhere established &mdash; or the medically dangerous thing missed entirely, with an unsupervised cessation tonight endorsed or encouraged in a man who has already had a withdrawal seizure, and no thiamine, no threshold, no safety-net attached.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make this station: the question that establishes dependence in four seconds &mdash; <em>&ldquo;and if you don&rsquo;t have that first one, what happens?&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the instruction almost nobody says well: <em>&ldquo;do not stop tonight. Keep drinking at your usual level, no more, until we do this properly &mdash; and I know exactly how that sounds coming from me.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, where this construct lives: D4&rsquo;s phenomenology fused to D6&rsquo;s emergency thinking, and the fusion is the lesson &mdash; in addictions the psychological consultation and the physical assessment are not alternatives, and a candidate who can only do one of them is unsafe whichever one they choose. Second, the transfer Exam Mode tests: the same knowledge with the clock already running, on a general ward, in a woman whose alcohol history nobody took on admission and who is now, forty-eight hours in, becoming something the ward is about to misname.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every drinking history</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Ask the two questions early.</b> When was the last drink, and what happens when there isn&rsquo;t one. Time on the withdrawal clock and the presence of relief drinking decide what must happen in the next twenty-four hours &mdash; before motivation, before goals, before arithmetic.</li>
      <li><b>Diagnose the syndrome, not the volume.</b> Craving, impaired control, relief drinking, tolerance, a narrowing repertoire, primacy over work and people. <em>&ldquo;Just to feel normal &mdash; I don&rsquo;t even like it&rdquo;</em> is worth more than any unit calculation.</li>
      <li><b>Always ask about fits and hallucinations.</b> One previous withdrawal seizure predicts another and moves treatment from a self-directed taper to supervised, medicated withdrawal, with a strong case for inpatient care. It is four seconds of history that changes the whole plan.</li>
      <li><b>Tell a dependent drinker not to stop unaided &mdash; and say why.</b> <em>Keep drinking at your usual level until we can do this safely.</em> Without the reasoning it sounds like permission or a brush-off; with it, and with a date attached, it becomes the most protective sentence in the consultation.</li>
      <li><b>Thiamine early, on suspicion.</b> Poor intake plus dependence is enough. Treat before confirmation, parenterally if there is any hint of Wernicke&rsquo;s, and never give glucose to a dependent drinker without thinking of it first.</li>
      <li><b>Name the occupational and driving risk once, in proportion.</b> Plainly, without a lecture and without a threat &mdash; enough to be safe and honest, not so much that he stops telling you the truth about anything.</li>
      <li><b>Close with a threshold, not encouragement.</b> A date, thiamine, the interim instruction, and what counts as an emergency before then &mdash; confusion, seeing things, any fit. Then check he has understood it as a plan rather than a refusal.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the same assessment in a patient nobody screens &mdash; where the units audit closes her down completely, and the word &ldquo;alcoholic&rdquo; ends the interview.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Rhiannon, 68, a retired school secretary, has been referred by her GP after two falls in six weeks and abnormal liver blood tests. She lives alone; her husband Gwilym moved into a care home eighteen months ago. The referral says: &ldquo;Query low mood, query early cognitive decline. Denies significant alcohol intake &mdash; reports a couple of sherries.&rdquo; Assess her.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Take the history without the audit &mdash; she will match her answer to your manner.</li>
            <li>Elicit the dependence syndrome if it is there, and ask the two questions that decide tonight.</li>
            <li>Ask the withdrawal history directly &mdash; fits, seeing things, previous attempts to stop.</li>
            <li>Think about the falls, the head, and the thiamine.</li>
            <li>Close with a real plan, and without ever needing the word she is dreading.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Rhiannon &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Rhiannon, 68. You are polite, well-presented and quietly formidable, and you open by managing the appointment: &ldquo;I think Dr Whelan has rather over-egged this. I&rsquo;ve been a bit unsteady, that&rsquo;s all &mdash; and at my age everybody&rsquo;s a bit unsteady. I have a sherry in the evening. That&rsquo;s the extent of it.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          &bull; The true pattern, disclosed only to unhurried, non-quantifying, non-judgemental questions (&ldquo;what does a normal day look like&hellip;&rdquo; &mdash; not &ldquo;how many units&rdquo;): a bottle of white wine most days, starting at about half eleven in the morning, and brandy in the evening. It began after Gwilym went into the home. You buy from three different shops so nobody sees, and the bottles go out in a neighbour&rsquo;s bin.<br>
          &bull; Dependence features, given if asked in ordinary language: you cannot get going in the morning until you have had one &mdash; your hands are unreliable and you feel sick; it settles within the hour. You have tried to stop twice and could not manage past the second day. You do not enjoy it any more: &ldquo;It isn&rsquo;t a pleasure. It&rsquo;s just how the day works now.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; The falls: both were in the afternoon; the second was down three stairs and you hit the back of your head and did not tell anyone. You are eating almost nothing &mdash; &ldquo;toast, and I don&rsquo;t always bother.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; Withdrawal history, if asked directly: no fits. On the second attempt to stop you saw &ldquo;shapes, and Gwilym&rsquo;s mother, standing by the wardrobe&rdquo; on the second night. You have never told anyone this and you are frightened it means dementia.<br>
          &bull; The real fear, revealed late and only into warmth: not the drinking &mdash; that the family will decide you cannot manage alone and you will lose the house, and the visits to Gwilym.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the units audit:</b> any run of &ldquo;how many glasses, how many nights, that&rsquo;s X units, the limit is Y&rdquo; &mdash; become charming, vague and utterly unyielding. &ldquo;Oh, I couldn&rsquo;t say. Not many.&rdquo; Disclose nothing further for the rest of the station. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the label:</b> if the candidate says &ldquo;alcoholic&rdquo;, &ldquo;alcohol abuse&rdquo;, or delivers any version of &ldquo;you need to admit you have a problem&rdquo; &mdash; go cold and formal: &ldquo;I think that&rsquo;s quite enough. I&rsquo;d like to go now.&rdquo; Gather your coat. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; The pass: a candidate who asks about your day rather than your units, treats the morning shakes as a medical fact rather than a confession, asks about the wardrobe without alarm, and tells you plainly not to stop on your own &mdash; gets everything. You say: &ldquo;Nobody&rsquo;s asked me any of that. I thought I was losing my mind.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Did the true pattern come out, were the morning shakes and the wardrobe both reached, was she told not to stop unaided, and did the head injury and thiamine get thought about? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="ask"><td colspan="2"><b>The two questions (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="ask">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="ask"></td><td>When she last drank, and how the day is actually structured around it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="ask"></td><td>What happens when she doesn&rsquo;t have one &mdash; the morning shakes reached and treated as a medical fact.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="syndrome"><td colspan="2"><b>The syndrome</b><span class="dc" data-dc="syndrome">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="syndrome"></td><td>Asked about her day rather than her units &mdash; no arithmetic, no limits quoted, no shame introduced.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="syndrome"></td><td>Relief drinking, impaired control and the failed attempts to stop all established.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="syndrome"></td><td>Primacy and concealment reached &mdash; three shops, the neighbour&rsquo;s bin &mdash; without the word &ldquo;alcoholic&rdquo;.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="danger"><td colspan="2"><b>The danger</b><span class="dc" data-dc="danger">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="danger"></td><td>Withdrawal history asked directly &mdash; fits, and seeing things; the wardrobe surfaced and received calmly.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="danger"></td><td>The falls taken seriously as falls &mdash; the unreported head injury identified.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="danger"></td><td>Thiamine and nutrition addressed &mdash; not deferred to a future appointment.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="plan"><td colspan="2"><b>The plan</b><span class="dc" data-dc="plan">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Told plainly not to stop on her own, with the reason given.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Supervised withdrawal offered with a route and a timescale, not a leaflet.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>The wardrobe explained honestly &mdash; withdrawal, not dementia &mdash; and cognition still screened properly.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Her real fear reached &mdash; the house, and the visits to Gwilym &mdash; and answered without false promises.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>A threshold and a contact given; documented, with no labels used at any point.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Rhiannon is the patient this station exists for, because nobody screens her. She is sixty-eight, well-spoken and referred as low mood or early dementia, and the referral itself has already accepted her cover story. She punishes the units audit in the way real patients do &mdash; not with anger but with charm and vagueness, which feels like a consultation going perfectly well while yielding nothing &mdash; and she punishes the label instantly, because &ldquo;alcoholic&rdquo; is the word she has spent eighteen months not saying to herself. Everything the station teaches is reachable by asking about her <em>day</em> instead of her units: the half-eleven start, the three shops, the neighbour&rsquo;s bin, the mornings that do not work until she has had one. And her hidden material carries two clinical stings the candidate must catch &mdash; a head injury nobody knows about, and hallucinations on the second night of a failed attempt to stop, which she has silently interpreted as dementia. Telling her plainly what that actually was, and that she must not try again alone, is the whole station.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>Same knowledge. The clock already running. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Duncan proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: <b>the same knowledge on a general ward, where nobody took the history on admission and the withdrawal has already started</b> &mdash; and where the ward has a diagnosis of its own ready for you. Timed, no feedback, no second tries: this is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Farah</b>, 44, was admitted forty-eight hours ago after a fall at home with a fractured wrist. You are the liaison psychiatry registrar. The ward has bleeped you for &ldquo;an urgent review &mdash; acute confusion, query psychosis&rdquo;. The nurse tells you at the desk: &ldquo;She was fine yesterday. Since this morning she&rsquo;s been picking at the sheets, she&rsquo;s sweating through her gown, she says there are insects on the curtain, and she tried to get out of bed twice. Her heart rate&rsquo;s 118. The FY1 thinks it&rsquo;s a UTI and wants something to calm her down.&rdquo; The admission clerking records no alcohol history at all.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would on the ward &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words to Farah&rsquo;s daughter, who has arrived on the ward frightened and is asking what is happening to her mother.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="I can tell you what we think is happening, and what we&#8217;re doing about it right now&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Named plainly &mdash; alcohol withdrawal, said in ordinary words; not &ldquo;a bit confused&rdquo;, not &ldquo;a funny turn&rdquo;, not hidden in jargon.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Serious and treatable, both said &mdash; the danger stated honestly without theatre, and the fact that it responds to treatment.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No blame &mdash; not of her mother, and not of the daughter for not knowing; the missed admission history owned rather than glossed.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Concrete next steps &mdash; what is being given now, what the next hours look like, and what you need from her.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the danger named plainly to the family &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Count the syndrome, not the units.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the anatomy is installed: the two questions asked before anything else, the dependence syndrome elicited as a pattern rather than a volume, the withdrawal history taken deliberately because one previous fit changes the setting of treatment, thiamine started today, the driving named once and in proportion, and the counterintuitive instruction delivered with its reasoning and a date attached. Prove the transfer on Farah in Exam Mode &mdash; forty-eight hours in, on a ward that has already decided what this is &mdash; then take Rhiannon to your colleagues: a woman nobody screens, who will meet a units audit with charm and disclose nothing at all. The domain continues with the next station.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="32_Station_4.11_Behavioural_Change_in_Learning_Disability.html">Continue &middot; Station 4.11: Behavioural Change in Learning Disability &#8594;</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

