<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 4.11 &middot; Domain 4</span>
      <span class="tag">MSE &amp; Phenomenology</span><span class="tag">Learning Disability</span>
      <span class="tag">Risk &amp; Safety</span><span class="tag">Interview Craft</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Behavioural Change in Learning Disability</h1>
    <p class="sub">Alec, 34, has a moderate learning disability and has lived in the same supported house for nine years. For six weeks he has been hitting the side of his own head with the flat of his hand, refusing the day centre, waking at three, leaving his food, and last Tuesday he pushed a support worker into a doorframe. The referral asks you to &ldquo;review his medication for challenging behaviour&rdquo;. Alec is in the room, and he has not been asked anything by anyone. <b>He has an abscess under a lower molar. Behaviour is the message, and the referral has already mistaken it for the diagnosis.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it becomes the overshadowing test. Does this doctor talk to <em>Alec</em> &mdash; adapting the questions rather than routing around him &mdash; and handle the acquiescence that makes every leading question worthless? Is the framing right: not <em>how do we stop this behaviour</em> but <em>what is he telling us</em>? Is the change anchored against his own baseline, with the timeline hunted for what is new? Is the body examined first &mdash; pain, illness, constipation, ears, teeth, medication, seizures, sensory loss &mdash; before any behavioural formulation is built on top of it? And when the house asks for something to calm him down, is that declined with a reason and replaced with a plan that finds the cause?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
