</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">People with learning disabilities die younger than they should, and a large share of that gap is made of illnesses that presented as behaviour and were treated as personality. This station tests four things. <b>The voice:</b> the patient addressed directly, with the interview adapted &mdash; short sentences, one idea at a time, concrete anchors, time given, and a working awareness of acquiescence, because a man who says yes to every question has told you nothing at all. <b>The framing:</b> the question changed from <em>how do we stop it</em> to <em>what is he telling us</em>. Behaviour that is new is a symptom, and calling it &ldquo;challenging behaviour&rdquo; is a description of the effect on staff, not a diagnosis. <b>The change:</b> the picture anchored against his own baseline &mdash; what he was like three months ago, what is different now, when it started, and what else changed around that time, since the answer is frequently a new medication, a new member of staff, a new room or a loss nobody explained to him. And <b>the body first:</b> pain, infection, constipation, teeth, ears, reflux, seizures, side effects and sensory loss actively hunted and examined for, before a single behavioural formulation is written &mdash; because the commonest cause of new behaviour in this population is something hurting.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> the two failures come from opposite ends of the profession and land in the same place. The first is diagnostic overshadowing in its plainest form: the behaviour attributed to the learning disability itself, an antipsychotic reached for, the man never spoken to, nothing examined &mdash; the referral&rsquo;s framing accepted whole. The second is more sophisticated and, to a modern trainee, more tempting: the behavioural formulation done beautifully &mdash; antecedents, behaviour, consequences, charts issued, reinforcement discussed, staff training recommended &mdash; while the abscess under his molar goes unexamined for another three weeks. Both consultations are confident, both produce a plan, and in neither does anybody look in his mouth. Notice both in the decisions below.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure blames the disability. The other analyses the behaviour. Neither examines the man. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 4.11</b> &middot; The community LD clinic &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The overshadowing</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The behaviour plan</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE OVERSHADOWING.</b> The referral&rsquo;s framing accepted whole, the man discussed in the third person while he sits there.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Support worker</span><span class="txt">He&rsquo;s just not himself. Six weeks of it now &mdash; banging his head, won&rsquo;t go to the centre, up half the night. And Tuesday he pushed Danielle. He&rsquo;s never done that in nine years. We&rsquo;re all a bit at the end of it, honestly.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(to the support worker, notes open; Alec is sitting two feet away)</em> Right. And is this the sort of thing he&rsquo;s done before, historically? <em>(&ldquo;Not like this.&rdquo;)</em> Mm. Well, with his level of disability this kind of behavioural presentation isn&rsquo;t unusual &mdash; frustration, difficulty communicating, and it can escalate. Does he understand much of what&rsquo;s said to him?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Two failures in one exchange. <em>&ldquo;Does he understand much?&rdquo;</em> is asked about a man who is in the room and has not been greeted &mdash; and the way to find out what Alec understands is to speak to Alec. And the reasoning is the trap itself: the behaviour explained by the disability, which explains nothing, because the disability has been constant for thirty-four years and the behaviour is six weeks old. Whatever changed, it was not his learning disability.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Support worker</span><span class="txt">He does understand. He tells us things &mdash; in his way. <em>(Alec hits the side of his head twice, hard, and makes a low sound.)</em> See, that&rsquo;s it. That&rsquo;s what he does.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(glancing up, then writing)</em> Yes, I can see. Well &mdash; given the escalation and the incident with staff, I think we do need to consider something to take the edge off. I&rsquo;ll start him on a low dose of an antipsychotic and we&rsquo;ll review in six weeks; if it&rsquo;s not settling we can always increase. Keep a record of any further incidents for me.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>He has just demonstrated the symptom in front of the candidate &mdash; repeated blows to <em>one side</em> of his head, which is a localising sign if anybody chooses to read it as one &mdash; and it was recorded as evidence for a prescription rather than as a clue. An antipsychotic is now going into a man whose mouth nobody has looked in, with a built-in escalation clause. This is precisely the pattern that national programmes exist to stop, and it is being set up in ninety seconds.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 10:35am)</em> Challenging behaviour in the context of moderate LD. Escalation including assault on staff. Commenced antipsychotic, low dose, review 6/52 with view to titration. Incident recording advised. <em>(Alec stops sleeping properly on the medication and gains four kilos. The head-banging continues. At the six-week review the dose is increased. The abscess is found eleven weeks later by a dentist doing a routine check, after the tooth has gone.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Challenging behaviour in the context of moderate LD&rdquo; is not a diagnosis; it is a description of how the house feels, with a medical framing bolted on. Note the trajectory this sets: the drug does not work, because it was never aimed at the problem, and the response to it not working is more of it. Eleven weeks of untreated dental pain, a lost tooth, a sedated man four kilos heavier, and a note that will follow him for years describing him as assaultive.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE BEHAVIOUR PLAN.</b> Modern, thoughtful, non-medicating &mdash; and it analyses the behaviour of a man in pain.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Support worker</span><span class="txt">Six weeks now &mdash; head-banging, won&rsquo;t go to the centre, up at night. And he pushed Danielle on Tuesday. He&rsquo;s never done that.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(warmly, to the support worker)</em> Thank you &mdash; and I want to say first that I&rsquo;m not going to reach for medication here. What I&rsquo;d like to do is understand the function of the behaviour. So: what tends to happen immediately before an episode? And what do you all do when it happens &mdash; who goes to him, what do you say?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The refusal to medicate reflexively is genuinely good and the candidate deserves credit for it. But watch the assumption underneath the next sentence: that the behaviour has a <em>function</em> in the environment &mdash; that it is doing something interpersonally &mdash; which presupposes the very thing that has not been established. A functional analysis is the right instrument for behaviour that is communicative or learned. It is the wrong instrument for toothache, and nothing yet distinguishes the two.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Support worker</span><span class="txt">Well &mdash; it&rsquo;s often round mealtimes, actually. And when it kicks off someone always sits with him, calms him down, sometimes takes him out of the room for a bit. <em>(Alec pushes his water glass away.)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">That&rsquo;s really helpful &mdash; so there may be a reinforcement pattern here: the behaviour is reliably followed by one-to-one attention and escape from the situation. What I&rsquo;d suggest is ABC charts for two weeks so we can map antecedents properly, a consistent low-arousal response across the team, and some staff training on planned ignoring with positive attention at other times. I&rsquo;ll ask psychology to pick it up.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Two enormous clinical gifts have just been handed over and processed as behavioural data. <em>It&rsquo;s often round mealtimes</em> &mdash; and the man pushed his water away as she said it. In a patient who is off his food, hitting one side of his head, and worse when he eats or drinks something cold, mealtimes are not an antecedent. They are a symptom. The plan that follows is coherent, evidence-flavoured and directs the whole team to respond to signals of pain with planned ignoring.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 10:28am)</em> Behaviours of concern; hypothesised functions: attention and escape. No medication indicated. ABC charts x2 weeks, low-arousal approach agreed, staff training re: differential reinforcement. Psychology referral. Review 4/52. <em>(For a fortnight the house diligently records antecedents while Alec stops eating on the right side of his mouth. He loses six kilos before anyone connects it to his face.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>This failure is more dangerous than it looks precisely because it reads so well &mdash; no medication, psychological formulation, multidisciplinary plan. And it commits the same original error as the overshadowing take: it explains a six-week change without ever asking what changed six weeks ago, and it builds an entire intervention on top of an unexamined body. The order matters. Exclude the physical, <em>then</em> formulate the behaviour &mdash; not the other way round, and never only one of them.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Alec spoken to first, the change anchored to a baseline, the body hunted &mdash; and the pattern read as a message.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Support worker</span><span class="txt">Six weeks now &mdash; head-banging, won&rsquo;t go to the centre, up at night. He pushed Danielle on Tuesday. He&rsquo;s never done that in nine years.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(turning the chair to face Alec, slowing right down)</em> Hello Alec. I&rsquo;m Sam. I&rsquo;m a doctor. <em>(pause)</em> I want to help you. <em>(pause)</em> Alec &mdash; are you sore? <em>(He looks up. Nothing.)</em> Can you show me? Show me where it hurts. <em>(A long wait. Then Alec raises his hand and holds it flat against his left jaw &mdash; not the temple, the jaw &mdash; and leaves it there.)</em> Thank you. That&rsquo;s really helpful. Is it sore <em>here</em>? <em>(touching his own jaw)</em> <em>(Alec makes the low sound again and does not move his hand.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Ninety seconds, and the station is effectively solved &mdash; by the one person nobody else in this scenario thought to ask. Note the technique: name and role, one idea per sentence, generous pauses, and crucially <em>show me</em> rather than a yes/no question, which sidesteps acquiescence entirely. The gesture is the finding: he has moved his hand from the side of his head to his jaw, which reframes six weeks of &ldquo;head-banging&rdquo; as a man hitting the thing that hurts.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(to the support worker, Alec still included)</em> Now &mdash; take me back to the Alec of three months ago. What was he like on a good day? <em>(&ldquo;Chatty. Loved the centre. Ate like a horse.&rdquo;)</em> And what is different now, in his day, apart from this? <em>(&ldquo;He won&rsquo;t have his cold drinks. He used to have squash with ice constantly &mdash; he pushes it away now. And he&rsquo;s chewing on one side, now you mention it.&rdquo;)</em> When did the night waking start relative to the head-hitting? <em>(&ldquo;Same week, near enough.&rdquo;)</em> Anything else change six weeks ago &mdash; new staff, new medication, a bereavement, a room move? <em>(&ldquo;No, nothing. Same as ever.&rdquo;)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The baseline question &mdash; <em>what was he like three months ago</em> &mdash; is the one that converts &ldquo;challenging behaviour&rdquo; back into a change in a person, and it is the question both failing takes skipped. Then the timeline is worked properly: cold drinks refused, unilateral chewing, night pain, all clustering in the same week. And note that the social and iatrogenic causes are actively excluded rather than ignored &mdash; new staff, new medication, a loss, a move &mdash; because if the answer had been yes, this would be a different station with a different ending.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then here is what I think, and what I&rsquo;m not going to do. I don&rsquo;t think this is behaviour. I think Alec has toothache, badly, and has been telling us for six weeks in the only way available to him &mdash; and I&rsquo;m not putting him on an antipsychotic for a dental problem. So: I&rsquo;d like to look in his mouth now with his agreement, we need an urgent dental appointment today with someone who can see him properly, and regular pain relief starting this afternoon rather than only when he&rsquo;s distressed. I&rsquo;ll check his bowels, his ears and his temperature while he&rsquo;s here, because he can&rsquo;t easily tell us about those either. If the pain settles and the behaviour settles with it, we have our answer. If it doesn&rsquo;t, I&rsquo;ll see him again and we look further &mdash; and <em>then</em> we think about whether something else is going on. <em>(to Alec)</em> Alec &mdash; we&rsquo;re going to fix your sore tooth. Sarah is taking you today.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Everything an examiner wants, in order. The reframe stated out loud &mdash; <em>I don&rsquo;t think this is behaviour</em> &mdash; with the prescription refused and a reason given rather than a simple no. Then examination now, urgent dental review today, and regular analgesia rather than as-required, which matters because a man who cannot ask for painkillers will otherwise only get them after he has hurt himself. The wider physical screen for the things he equally cannot report. A falsifiable plan with a review built in. And the last line, which costs four seconds and is the whole ethic of the station: Alec is told what is going to happen to Alec.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same discipline where the cause is not in the body at all &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; Who gets spoken to</h3>
      <p class="stem">Alec is in the room with his support worker, who has brought a written account of six weeks of incidents. Where does the assessment begin?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">With the support worker &mdash; she has the observations and the dates, and a reliable account has to come before anything else can be interpreted.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">With Alec &mdash; greeted, addressed directly and asked in adapted language, with the support worker used as collateral rather than as a substitute.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">With the records &mdash; the incident log and medication chart reviewed first, so the pattern is clear before either of them describes it to you.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Her account is essential and it is not the starting point. Beginning with the carer, while the patient sits unaddressed two feet away, is how the whole consultation ends up being conducted about him rather than with him &mdash; and it is exactly what has already happened to him for six weeks. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The records matter and they contain nobody&rsquo;s experience of the last six weeks. Alec is present, he has information, and the notes will still be there in five minutes. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>He is the patient, and in this station he is also the person holding the answer. Name and role, one idea per sentence, real pauses, concrete language &mdash; and an assumption of capability until it is tested rather than the reverse. The support worker is then invaluable as collateral, which is a different job from speaking for him. Note what the failing takes lose by skipping this: ninety seconds with Alec produces a localising gesture that no incident log contains.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; &ldquo;Yes&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Asked if he is in pain, Alec says yes. Asked if he is happy, he also says yes. Asked if he slept well, yes. How is this handled?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">By repetition &mdash; the important questions asked again later in the interview, so that consistent answers can be distinguished from inconsistent ones.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">By changing the form &mdash; showing and pointing instead of yes-or-no, offering real choices, and asking him to demonstrate rather than confirm.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">By discounting it &mdash; his answers are unreliable, so the history is taken from the support worker and his responses treated as non-contributory.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Repeating a leading question produces a repeated yes. Acquiescence is not inconsistency to be caught out; it is a stable response to a question format that offers agreement as the easiest available answer. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>This is the conclusion that empties the consultation of the patient, and it is wrong: the problem is the question, not the man. Discounting him also discards the single most direct source of information about where it hurts. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Acquiescence is a property of the format, so change the format. <em>Show me where it hurts</em> cannot be answered with a yes. Offer genuine either-or choices rather than confirmations, use gesture, pointing and objects, and watch what he does as well as what he says &mdash; his hand went to his jaw and stayed there, which is worth more than any number of yeses. A candidate who knows this is a candidate who has actually worked with people who have learning disabilities.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; The question that reframes it</h3>
      <p class="stem">The referral asks for help with &ldquo;challenging behaviour&rdquo;. What question should the assessment actually be answering?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">How the behaviour can be reduced &mdash; the escalation is the reason for referral, staff are being hurt, and reducing it is what the house needs.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">What he is telling us &mdash; new behaviour treated as a symptom with a cause to be found, rather than as a problem that has to be managed.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">What function it serves &mdash; what the behaviour achieves for him in his environment, since that determines the right behavioural intervention.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Reduction is the right goal and the wrong question &mdash; it takes the referral&rsquo;s framing whole, and every intervention aimed at suppressing a signal makes the underlying cause harder to find. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Functional analysis is a good instrument used in the wrong order here. It presupposes that the behaviour is doing something interpersonally &mdash; which is exactly what has not yet been established, and is false if the man has an abscess. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The reframe the whole station turns on. &ldquo;Challenging behaviour&rdquo; describes the effect on other people; it is not a diagnosis and it has no cause built into it. New behaviour in a person who cannot easily report symptoms is a presenting complaint, and the job is the same as with any presenting complaint: find out what it is caused by. Everything else &mdash; the analysis, the plan, the medication question &mdash; follows from getting this order right.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; Six weeks</h3>
      <p class="stem">The behaviour is six weeks old. What does that fact demand?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Urgency &mdash; six weeks of escalating behaviour with an assault on staff means the situation is deteriorating and needs an intervention now.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">A baseline and a timeline &mdash; what he was like before, and what else changed in that same week: medication, staff, room, routine, a loss.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Longitudinal history &mdash; how his behaviour has run across his adult life, so that this episode can be placed in the context of his usual pattern.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Urgency without direction is what produces the antipsychotic. Something has been wrong for six weeks; acting fast on the wrong formulation is not better than acting fast on the right one. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Lifelong pattern is useful background and it answers a different question. What matters is not how he has behaved across thirty-four years but what is different about the last six weeks &mdash; and against what. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>A change needs something to be a change <em>from</em>: <em>take me back to the Alec of three months ago &mdash; what was he like on a good day?</em> Then the timeline is worked for coincidences, because in this population the answer is so often sitting in that same week &mdash; a new drug, a new night worker, a room move, a day centre reshuffle, a death nobody explained. Here the cluster is cold drinks refused, chewing on one side and night waking, all arriving together. That is not a behavioural pattern; it is a clinical one.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; Before any formulation</h3>
      <p class="stem">What has to be excluded before a behavioural plan is written?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Mental illness &mdash; depression and psychosis present atypically here, so a psychiatric diagnosis must be considered before behaviour is formulated.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The body &mdash; pain, infection, constipation, teeth, ears, reflux, seizures, side effects and sensory loss, all actively hunted and examined for.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Environmental causes &mdash; changes in staffing, routine, placement and relationships, the commonest triggers for deterioration in supported settings.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Mental illness genuinely does present as behaviour change here and belongs in the differential &mdash; after the physical, not before it. Diagnosing depression in a man with an abscess treats the wrong organ. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Environmental causes matter enormously and were rightly asked about &mdash; but they were asked about and excluded here, and they cannot be the <em>first</em> screen when the commonest cause of new behaviour in this group is something hurting. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The body first, every time, and actively &mdash; not &ldquo;no physical symptoms reported&rdquo; in a man who cannot report them. Teeth, ears, throat, chest, abdomen and bowels, urinary symptoms, reflux, headache, seizures, constipation from the medication he is already on, and vision and hearing. Then examine. In a population that dies young of treatable illness presenting as behaviour, this screen is not a formality; it is the intervention that most often changes the outcome.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; &ldquo;Can he have something to calm him down?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The house is exhausted and a staff member has been pushed. They ask directly for medication. What answers them?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">A short course &mdash; something low-dose and time-limited to hold the situation safely while the underlying cause is investigated properly.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Declined with its reason, and replaced &mdash; an explanation of what the behaviour is, plus today&rsquo;s dental appointment and regular pain relief.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A clear refusal &mdash; antipsychotics are not indicated for behaviour in learning disability, and the request should be declined on that principle.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Short and low-dose while we investigate&rdquo; is how almost every long-term antipsychotic in this population began. It sedates the signal you are still trying to read, and the review that was supposed to stop it becomes the review that increases it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The principle is right and a bare refusal abandons a team who are frightened and out of ideas &mdash; and staff left with nothing will manage the situation some other way, possibly worse. The refusal needs a replacement attached. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Refuse, explain, replace. The explanation does the real work: this is not behaviour, it is a man in pain telling you the only way he can, and sedating him will not fix his tooth. Then hand them something better than a prescription &mdash; the dental appointment today, regular analgesia rather than as-required, and a review with a date. A team who understand what is happening will manage tonight far better than a team holding a sedative and a theory.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; The plan</h3>
      <p class="stem">You believe this is dental pain. How is the plan constructed?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Definitively &mdash; the dental cause identified and treated, with the behavioural referral withdrawn now the explanation has been established.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Falsifiably &mdash; treat the pain, screen the rest of the body, and review: if behaviour settles with the pain there is the answer; if not, look on.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">In parallel &mdash; the dental treatment arranged and the full behavioural programme started simultaneously, so that no time is lost either way.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Confidence ahead of evidence is how the first two takes went wrong, just in a nicer direction. The abscess is a strong hypothesis, not a proven cause, and closing the other doors before it is treated risks repeating the same error with a different label. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Running everything at once sounds thorough and destroys the experiment: if the behaviour settles you will not know what settled it, and a whole behavioural programme will have been built around a man whose problem was a tooth. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>A plan that can be proved wrong. Treat the pain properly, screen the rest of the body that he cannot report on, tell the team what to watch, and set a review: if the head-hitting and the night waking resolve as the tooth is treated, the formulation is confirmed by the response. If they do not, the door to a psychiatric or behavioural explanation is still open and you have lost nothing but a fortnight of pain he was having anyway. And Alec is told, in his own language, what is going to happen to him.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>It&rsquo;s never just the learning disability.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Both failures accept the referral&rsquo;s framing and neither looks in his mouth. <b>The overshadowing</b> is the map&rsquo;s named trap in its plainest dress: the behaviour explained by the disability &mdash; which explains nothing, because the disability has been constant for thirty-four years and the behaviour is six weeks old &mdash; the man discussed in the third person while he sits two feet away, <em>&ldquo;does he understand much?&rdquo;</em> asked about someone who could have answered it, and an antipsychotic started with an escalation clause built in. <b>The behaviour plan</b> is the failure a good modern trainee is far likelier to commit, and it is more dangerous than it looks because it reads so well: no medication, functional analysis, ABC charts, low-arousal responses, psychology referral. But a functional analysis presupposes that behaviour is doing something interpersonally, and that is precisely what has not been established &mdash; so when the support worker says the episodes cluster around mealtimes and Alec pushes his water away, two clinical findings are logged as antecedents and a whole team is instructed to respond to signals of pain with planned ignoring. The pass changes the question before answering it. Alec is greeted and asked, in adapted language, with acquiescence sidestepped by <em>show me</em> rather than yes-or-no &mdash; and ninety seconds later his hand is flat against his jaw. The change is anchored to a baseline, <em>what was he like three months ago</em>, and the timeline worked for what else arrived that week: new drug, new staff, new room, a loss. The body is hunted actively rather than recorded as unremarkable in a man who cannot report it. The prescription is refused with a reason and replaced with something better &mdash; dental review today, regular analgesia rather than as-required, because a man who cannot ask for painkillers otherwise only gets them after he has hurt himself. And the plan is built to be proved wrong, with a review that tests it.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap either way: &ldquo;it&rsquo;s the learning disability&rdquo; &mdash; behaviour attributed to the diagnosis, medication started, nothing examined, the patient never addressed &mdash; or a behavioural formulation built on an unexamined body, with pain, infection, constipation, teeth, ears and medication side effects never actively hunted, and the communication never adapted.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make this station: the question that defeats acquiescence and localises the problem in one gesture &mdash; <em>&ldquo;Alec, can you show me? Show me where it hurts&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the sentence that refuses the referral&rsquo;s premise out loud: <em>&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t think this is behaviour. I think he has toothache, and he&rsquo;s been telling us for six weeks.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, where this construct lives: D4&rsquo;s phenomenology and D6&rsquo;s physical vigilance, joined by a principle that outlives this station &mdash; whenever a patient cannot easily report symptoms, their behaviour becomes the presenting complaint, and every rule you know about taking a presenting complaint still applies. That covers dementia, delirium, aphasia after stroke, and the sedated patient on any ward. Second, the transfer Exam Mode tests: the same discipline where the cause is not in the body at all &mdash; a woman whose deterioration has been referred as possible psychosis, where the answer is sitting in the timeline and nobody has thought to ask what happened in her family five months ago.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every behaviour referral</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Talk to the patient first.</b> Greet them, name yourself, one idea per sentence, real pauses, concrete language. The carer is collateral, not a substitute &mdash; and deciding what someone understands without asking them is the first move of overshadowing.</li>
      <li><b>Defeat acquiescence by changing the format.</b> <em>Show me</em> cannot be answered with a yes. Use gesture, pointing, objects and genuine either-or choices, and read behaviour as data alongside speech.</li>
      <li><b>New behaviour is a symptom, not a diagnosis.</b> &ldquo;Challenging behaviour&rdquo; describes the effect on staff. Ask what he is telling us, not how to stop it &mdash; and remember a lifelong disability cannot explain a six-week change.</li>
      <li><b>Anchor to a baseline, then work the timeline.</b> What was he like three months ago, and what else changed that week &mdash; a new medication, a new staff member, a room move, a routine change, a death nobody explained to him?</li>
      <li><b>Hunt the body actively.</b> Teeth, ears, throat, chest, abdomen, bowels, urinary, reflux, seizures, sensory loss and side effects &mdash; and examine. &ldquo;No physical symptoms reported&rdquo; means nothing in someone who cannot report them.</li>
      <li><b>Refuse, explain, replace.</b> Decline the sedative with its reason, then hand the team something better: the cause, the treatment, regular rather than as-required analgesia, and a date. A frightened team given nothing will manage it some other way.</li>
      <li><b>Build a plan that can be proved wrong.</b> Treat the suspected cause, screen the rest, tell people what to watch, and review. If the behaviour settles with the pain, that is your evidence; if it does not, every other door is still open.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the same discipline through a carer rather than the patient &mdash; where the overshadowing has been going on for a year, and the mother has been told twice that this is just what happens.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Cormac, 49, has Down syndrome and a moderate learning disability. He lives with his mother Maeve, 76. Over about a year he has become withdrawn, stopped his beloved Saturday bowling, has begun getting lost in the house at night, and has had two episodes of incontinence. The GP letter says: &ldquo;Mother very anxious. Behaviour change, likely part of underlying condition. Query needs day placement.&rdquo; Speak to Maeve.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Take her observations as expert evidence &mdash; she has watched him for forty-nine years.</li>
            <li>Anchor to a baseline and work the timeline &mdash; what has gone, and in what order.</li>
            <li>Hunt the treatable: thyroid, hearing, vision, sleep, constipation, depression, medication.</li>
            <li>Keep Cormac present &mdash; how he will be seen and involved, not decided about in his absence.</li>
            <li>Be honest about what this may be, without either dismissing it or flattening her with a diagnosis today.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Maeve &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Maeve, 76. You are polite, tired and braced for being managed. You open: &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve been told twice now that this is just part of his condition and that I&rsquo;m worrying. I&rsquo;ve had him forty-nine years, doctor. This isn&rsquo;t him.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          &bull; The timeline, given in detail to baseline questions (&ldquo;what was he like two years ago&hellip;&rdquo;): he used to lay the table every evening and now cannot; he stopped bowling in March because he could not remember where to stand; he has begun asking for his father, who died in 2011; twice this month he has been found in the hallway at 3am, unable to say what he was looking for.<br>
          &bull; Things that are new and physical, admitted if asked specifically: he has become very slow and cold, sleeps in the afternoons, has put on weight, and his skin is dry &mdash; &ldquo;I thought that was just getting older.&rdquo; He snores terribly and stops breathing in his sleep, and he strains in the bathroom for ages. He has not had his hearing or eyes tested in over six years.<br>
          &bull; The order of loss, if the candidate asks what went first: the memory changes came before the withdrawal, not after &mdash; &ldquo;he went quiet <em>because</em> he kept getting things wrong, not the other way round.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; Her real fear, revealed only into warmth: not the diagnosis &mdash; &ldquo;I&rsquo;m seventy-six. If I go first, who explains him to people?&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the overshadowing:</b> any version of &ldquo;this is part of Down syndrome&rdquo;, &ldquo;expected with his condition&rdquo;, or moving straight to placement &mdash; go quiet and formal: &ldquo;Yes. That&rsquo;s what the last one said.&rdquo; Answer in short sentences, volunteer nothing further. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the absent patient:</b> if the candidate makes a plan entirely about Cormac without ever asking how he might be seen, involved or told &mdash; say flatly: &ldquo;Does anybody want to meet him, or is it all going to be about him?&rdquo; Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; The pass: a candidate who treats your observations as evidence, asks what went first, hunts the physical, and says plainly that this needs proper assessment rather than a placement form &mdash; gets everything, including the fear. You say: &ldquo;Thank you. That&rsquo;s the first time anyone&rsquo;s written any of it down.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Were her observations treated as evidence, was the order of loss established, were the treatable physical causes hunted, and was Cormac kept present in the plan? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="voice"><td colspan="2"><b>The voice (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="voice">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="voice"></td><td>Maeve&rsquo;s account received as expert evidence &mdash; &ldquo;this isn&rsquo;t him&rdquo; taken as a finding, not as anxiety.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="voice"></td><td>Cormac kept present &mdash; how he will be seen, involved and told, rather than decided about.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="change"><td colspan="2"><b>The change</b><span class="dc" data-dc="change">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="change"></td><td>A real baseline established &mdash; what he could do a year and two years ago, concretely.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="change"></td><td>The order of loss asked for &mdash; memory before withdrawal, not withdrawal before memory.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="change"></td><td>Function mapped in daily specifics &mdash; the table, the bowling, the nights, the continence.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="body"><td colspan="2"><b>The body</b><span class="dc" data-dc="body">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="body"></td><td>Thyroid symptoms actively sought &mdash; cold, slow, weight, skin, sleepiness.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="body"></td><td>Sleep and bowels covered &mdash; the snoring and apnoea, and the straining.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="body"></td><td>Hearing and vision raised &mdash; six years untested, and both reversible causes of &ldquo;withdrawal&rdquo;.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="plan"><td colspan="2"><b>The plan</b><span class="dc" data-dc="plan">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Investigations named concretely &mdash; bloods including thyroid, hearing and vision, sleep review.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Depression considered alongside dementia, not instead of it &mdash; and the placement form declined for now.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="plan"></td><td>Honest about the dementia possibility without delivering a diagnosis today &mdash; and a baseline assessment arranged.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Her real fear reached &mdash; what happens to Cormac if she goes first &mdash; and answered without false comfort.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Dated next steps and a named contact; documented, with her observations recorded as history.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Cormac moves the station&rsquo;s discipline into the two places it is hardest &mdash; a collateral interview, and a year-long decline where the overshadowing has already happened twice and been written into a GP letter. Maeve is the examiner&rsquo;s instrument: she has watched this man for forty-nine years, her judgement that <em>this isn&rsquo;t him</em> is the single most reliable piece of evidence available, and she has been told twice that it is anxiety. Candidates who repeat that lose her in one sentence. The case also carries the specific knowledge this population demands: adults with Down syndrome have a materially higher risk of early Alzheimer&rsquo;s disease, and they also have a materially higher risk of hypothyroidism, sleep apnoea, hearing loss, visual impairment and constipation &mdash; every one of which produces exactly the picture in that GP letter and every one of which is treatable. The order-of-loss question separates the strong candidates: memory failing first and withdrawal following is a very different story from withdrawal first, and only one of them is chiefly a depression. And her real fear, which is not the diagnosis at all, is what the last minute is for.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>Same discipline. The cause isn&rsquo;t in the body. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Alec proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: <b>the same discipline where the answer is not physical at all</b> &mdash; a woman referred as a possible psychosis, whose deterioration has a date on it that nobody in the house has connected to anything. Timed, no feedback, no second tries: this is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Nia</b>, 27, has a moderate learning disability and lives in a group home. Over four months she has stopped going out, shouts at night, has torn up photographs from her bedroom wall, and refuses to do her Thursday activity, which she used to look forward to all week. The referral reads: &ldquo;Marked deterioration. Staff report she talks to someone who is not there, particularly at night. Query emerging psychosis &mdash; ? antipsychotic.&rdquo; The home manager mentions in passing, on the way in, that Nia&rsquo;s father died in the spring.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words to Nia &mdash; telling her about her father, and what happens next, in language she can hold on to.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Nia, I want to tell you something true about your dad&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Plain and concrete &mdash; short sentences, one idea at a time; the word &ldquo;died&rdquo; used, with no euphemism she has to decode.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Nothing she is doing is treated as wrong &mdash; missing him, shouting at night and talking to him are all allowed to be normal.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No false comfort and no promise you cannot keep &mdash; you do not say he is coming back or that she will stop feeling sad soon.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Something concrete she can hold &mdash; a named person, a thing that will happen, and when.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the truth said in words she can hold &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>It&rsquo;s never just the learning disability.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the anatomy is installed: the patient addressed first and the interview adapted, acquiescence defeated by asking him to show rather than confirm, the referral&rsquo;s framing refused and the question changed to what he is telling us, the change anchored against a baseline and the timeline worked, the body hunted actively before any behavioural formulation, and the sedative declined with a reason and replaced with a plan that can be proved wrong. Prove the transfer on Nia in Exam Mode &mdash; where the cause is not physical and the date is sitting in the referral letter &mdash; then take Cormac and Maeve to your colleagues: a year of decline, a mother told twice that she is worrying, and every treatable cause still unexamined. That completes Domain 4 &mdash; return to the hub to review the domain, or move on when you are ready.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="33_Domain_5_Hub_Capacity_Consent_and_the_Law.html">Domain 4 complete &middot; Continue &mdash; Domain 5: Capacity, Consent &amp; the Law</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

<footer><div class="wrap">
  <span>The CASC Academy &middot; by MedLex Foundations</span>
  <span>Station 4.11 &middot; all patients are fictional composites</span>
</div></footer>


