  insert into public.content_blocks (unit_id, source_key, block_type, sort_order, content)
  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '33_Domain_5_Hub_Capacity_Consent_and_the_Law'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Domain 5 of 8</span>
      <span class="tag">Capacity, Consent &amp; the Law</span>
      <span class="tag">6 constructs · the statutory domain</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Capacity, Consent &amp; the Law</h1>
    <p class="sub">This is the domain where psychiatry meets the law on the law&rsquo;s terms — and where a specific failure hides in plain sight: candidates who deliver <b style="color:var(--lgold)">a verdict without a test</b>. &ldquo;Lacks capacity&rdquo; goes in the notes as if it were a property of the person — read off a diagnosis, or off a decision the doctor dislikes — when the law demands something narrower and harder: a specific decision, tested against explicit statutory criteria, at a specific moment. This domain trains the three verbs the marks are built on — <b>presuming</b> capacity until it is properly displaced, <b>testing</b> the decision in front of you, and <b>proving</b> it: an assessment written so its legal reasoning shows. A capacity assessment is not an opinion. It is evidence.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE LENS, APPLIED TO THIS DOMAIN</b>
      &ldquo;Would I trust this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; — In this domain the question sharpens into the cross-examination test: if a barrister read this doctor&rsquo;s assessment aloud in a tribunal, would the statutory steps be visibly there — the presumption honoured, the criteria tested one by one, the reasoning shown? Every assessment in this domain is a document a court may one day read. That is what every station here is really asking.</div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ============ 1 WHY FIRST ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this domain matters</span>
    <h2>The founding failure: the verdict without the test</h2>
    <p class="intro">Every jurisdiction&rsquo;s capacity law rests on the same architecture, and the exam tests whether you carry it reflexively. Capacity is <b>presumed</b> — the burden of displacing it sits with the doctor, never the patient. It is <b>decision-specific and time-specific</b> — a person may lack capacity for one choice this morning and hold it for another this afternoon, and may regain what illness took. It is <b>functional</b> — understand, retain, use and weigh, communicate — tested explicitly, one element at a time, and linked to an impairment of the mind or brain. And it is <b>indifferent to wisdom</b> — an unwise decision is not an incapacitous one, which is the single doctrine candidates find hardest to hold when the unwise decision is fatal. Around that core sit the conversations this domain trains: consent that actually counts, the informal patient at the door, a family&rsquo;s questions inside confidentiality&rsquo;s limits, and detention explained to the person detained. The failure that unites every failed station here is the same: <b>a conclusion reached before, or instead of, the test.</b></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THE CONSTRUCTS ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The constructs</span>
    <h2>Six things this domain will build into reflex</h2>
    <div class="constructs">
      <div class="con"><span class="n">1</span><span><b>The statutory test, made explicit</b> — understand, retain, use and weigh, communicate: assessed one element at a time, out loud, and tied to an impairment of mind or brain. Not an impression of the patient — a test of the decision, with its working shown.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">2</span><span><b>Decision-specific, time-specific</b> — capacity is not a property of the person. It attaches to this decision, now: it can be absent for surgery and present for tea, lost in delirium and regained by Friday — and every assessment carries a date for that reason.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">3</span><span><b>Consent, refusal, and the right to be unwise</b> — consent that counts is informed, voluntary, and capacitous; a capacitous refusal binds, however much it costs. Unwise is not incapacitous: easy to recite, brutal to apply — which is why the exam applies it brutally.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">4</span><span><b>Capacity under pressure</b> — the patient with a coat on and a hand on the door: capacity and risk assessed in minutes, without arguing, without bargaining, and without the assessment becoming the obstacle the patient is escaping.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">5</span><span><b>Confidentiality with a family in the room</b> — what a relative may be told, what they may always be told, and what they can give you regardless: listening is never a breach. Neither the open book nor the locked door — the lawful middle, held warmly.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">6</span><span><b>The frameworks, explained to their subject</b> — detention, rights, tribunals, appeal, translated for the person they apply to. The law recited is smallprint; the law explained is care. And underneath: which framework, for which purpose, for this patient.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 STATIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The stations in this domain</span>
    <h2>Walk them in order</h2>
    <div class="stn">
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">5.1</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Refusing Treatment</b><span>A man on a surgical ward is refusing an operation his surgeons call necessary. The station this whole domain stands on: the statutory test, run explicitly, one element at a time, at a bedside.</span></span>
        <span class="chip go">Begin here</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">5.2</span>
        <span class="t"><b>&ldquo;I&rsquo;m Leaving&rdquo;</b><span>An informal patient has his coat on. No section, no seclusion — a decision at a door, made of capacity, risk, and minutes. The same test under pressure, where arguing is the trap.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 2</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">5.3</span>
        <span class="t"><b>The Relative Who Wants Everything</b><span>A father wants his son&rsquo;s diagnosis, medication, and prognosis — and the son has said no. Between telling everything and hiding behind the rules sits the lawful middle. Find it warmly.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 3</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">5.4</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Sectioned</b><span>A detained patient asks what has happened to her, and what she can do about it. Rights, tribunals, appeal — the law translated for its subject, without smallprint and without spin.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 4</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 ENTRY DECISION ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Before you enter</span>
    <h2>One decision at the door</h2>
    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>Where does a capacity assessment begin?</h3>
      <p class="stem">A man with schizophrenia is refusing antibiotics for a serious leg infection. The surgical team ring: &ldquo;he lacks capacity — he&rsquo;s psychotic — can you confirm so we can treat?&rdquo; What is your first move?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Confirm the logic — active psychosis undermines decision-making, and the infection cannot wait for formalities.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Decline the shortcut — capacity is presumed, attaches to this decision, and gets tested at the bedside before anyone concludes anything.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Protect his autonomy — a refusal is a refusal, and the team&rsquo;s discomfort with it is not a clinical question.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Diagnosis is not incapacity — a man can be psychotic about his neighbour and clear-eyed about his leg. The verdict arrived before the test. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>An untested refusal is as lawless as an untested override — autonomy invoked without assessment is the same shortcut, walked the other way. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The domain in one move: the presumption honoured, the shortcut declined in both directions, and the statutory test carried to the bedside — this decision, these criteria, today. Whether he can understand, retain, weigh and communicate a choice about THIS operation is a finding you do not yet have. Now go and get it — Station 5.1 is exactly this room.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ DONE<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Domain open</span>
    <h2>Presume it. Test it. Prove it.</h2>
    <p>Six constructs, four rooms, one statute book — and one lens over all of it: would your assessment survive cross-examination? Begin with the station this whole domain stands on: a man on a surgical ward, saying no.</p>
    <a class="btn mark" href="34_Station_5.1_Refusing_Treatment.html">Begin Station 5.1 · Refusing Treatment</a>
  </div>
</section>

