  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '34_Station_5.1_Refusing_Treatment'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 5.1 · Domain 5</span>
      <span class="tag">Capacity, Consent &amp; the Law</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">Liaison</span><span class="tag">Capacity · MCA</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Refusing Treatment</h1>
    <p class="sub">Leonard, 64, a taxi driver with diabetes, has gangrene spreading through his right foot. His surgeons say a below-knee amputation will save his life, and they say it soon. Leonard has said no. The referral in your hand reads: &ldquo;Please assess capacity &mdash; patient refusing life-saving surgery. He must lack it; no rational person refuses this.&rdquo; That last sentence is the station. The law does not ask whether Leonard is right. It asks whether this decision &mdash; this one, today &mdash; is truly his: understood, retained, weighed, and communicated, by a mind not disabled by illness. <b>You are not deciding whether he is right. You are deciding whether the decision is his.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; In this domain it carries a barrister with it: if this doctor assesses my patient&rsquo;s refusal, do I get back a test &mdash; each statutory element examined by name, tied to evidence, dated, its reasoning shown &mdash; and a finding delivered with the courage of its consequences? Or do I get an impression: a warm interview, a verdict either way, and nothing a tribunal could see the working of?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Capacity is the statutory backbone of psychiatric practice &mdash; and this station is the domain&rsquo;s cornerstone because it strips the skill to its architecture. Four things carry the marks. <b>The presumption, honoured:</b> capacity is presumed, and the burden of displacing it sits with the doctor, never the patient &mdash; the referral&rsquo;s &ldquo;he must lack it&rdquo; is the first thing declined. <b>The test, run explicitly:</b> understand, retain, use and weigh, communicate &mdash; each limb named and examined out loud, one at a time, tied to the question the law also asks: is anything wrong with the machinery &mdash; an impairment of mind or brain &mdash; and is any failure <em>because</em> of it? <b>The frame, kept narrow:</b> decision-specific and time-specific &mdash; this operation, today, dated &mdash; because capacity is not a property of the person. And <b>the doctrine under fire:</b> an unwise decision is not an incapacitous one. A reason is not a symptom &mdash; though you must check for the symptom &mdash; and the finding that takes real courage is capacity <em>present</em>, because it binds you to a refusal you may grieve.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like good doctoring. Toward the rescue &mdash; the outcome is death, the surgeons are waiting, and finding incapacity feels like saving him: the referral&rsquo;s verdict, adopted out of decency. And toward the interview &mdash; Ernie, the fear, the marriage: a rich, humane history, gathered with real skill, while the statutory criteria are never once named or tested. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure decides before it tests. The other listens beautifully and never tests at all. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 5.1</b> · Refusing treatment · capacity assessment · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The verdict</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The interview</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE VERDICT.</b> The referral&rsquo;s conclusion, adopted whole &mdash; and defended with persuasion. Watch a capacity assessment become an argument the patient keeps winning.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leonard</span><span class="txt"><em>(sitting up in bed, the right foot dressed, arms folded)</em> You&rsquo;re the psychiatrist, then. They think that because I won&rsquo;t let them take my leg, I must have lost my mind. I drove a taxi for forty-one years, doctor. I know my own mind better than most people know theirs.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Leonard, I&rsquo;m going to be honest with you, because the situation demands it. Without this operation, the infection will spread and you will die &mdash; that isn&rsquo;t an opinion, it&rsquo;s what will happen. This is a life-saving procedure, and I need to understand why a man would turn it down. Frankly, a man who truly grasped it wouldn&rsquo;t.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The referral&rsquo;s last sentence &mdash; &ldquo;no rational person refuses this&rdquo; &mdash; adopted in the first breath: the outcome is doing the reasoning. And the law&rsquo;s starting point has been quietly inverted. Capacity is presumed; the burden of displacing it, by testing, sits with the doctor. In this room, Leonard has just been asked to prove his sanity &mdash; and nothing whatsoever has been tested.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leonard</span><span class="txt"><em>(steady)</em> I grasp it perfectly well. My brother Ernie had diabetes too. They took one leg below the knee, then two years on they took the other. He spent his last years in a chair by a window, waiting for someone to turn him towards the light. I watched what living saved him for. I&rsquo;d rather have less life on two feet than more of that. I&rsquo;ve had eleven years to think about it &mdash; since we buried him.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">But you&rsquo;re not your brother, Leonard &mdash; that&rsquo;s exactly the problem with how you&rsquo;re seeing this. Prosthetics have transformed since then; rehabilitation is excellent; men your age walk again within months. You&rsquo;re weighing this against the wrong picture entirely. If you could just see it clearly, you&rsquo;d see this operation gives you years with Margaret.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Persuasion, wearing an assessment&rsquo;s badge &mdash; and note the tragedy of timing: the reply just handed over the whole test. The facts understood, the morning&rsquo;s detail retained, a reason weighed over eleven years, all of it communicated without a tremor &mdash; four limbs of the statutory test, walking past a candidate too busy arguing to notice the assessment answering itself.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, afterwards)</em> Patient fixated on brother&rsquo;s outcome from over a decade ago; unable to accept reassurance about modern surgical results; continues to refuse life-saving treatment. In my opinion he lacks capacity to make this decision. Suggest the team proceed in his best interests. <em>(no limb of the test named, no evidence cited &mdash; a verdict, resting on the unbearability of his choice)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Fixated&rdquo; &mdash; a clinical word doing a legal job; &ldquo;unable to accept reassurance&rdquo; &mdash; not a statutory criterion anywhere on earth. In a tribunal this document dies in one question: &ldquo;Doctor, show me where you tested his understanding.&rdquo; An unwise decision has been converted into an incapacitous one because the outcome was unbearable &mdash; the precise move the law was written to prevent.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE INTERVIEW.</b> Kind, skilled, genuinely moving &mdash; a full history of a man and his reasons, gathered beautifully, while the statutory test is never once run.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leonard</span><span class="txt"><em>(sitting up in bed, the right foot dressed, arms folded)</em> You&rsquo;re the psychiatrist, then. They think that because I won&rsquo;t let them take my leg, I must have lost my mind. I drove a taxi for forty-one years, doctor. I know my own mind better than most people know theirs.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then let&rsquo;s start with the mind you know so well. Forty-one years in a taxi &mdash; that&rsquo;s a working life to be proud of. Tell me about yourself, Leonard. And tell me how you came to this decision &mdash; because I don&rsquo;t imagine for a moment it was made lightly.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>A genuinely lovely opening &mdash; warm, respectful, disarming &mdash; and watch where it points. The story is coming, and the story matters: it is the raw evidence for the weighing limb. But it is evidence for a test this candidate has not named and, watch closely, never will. Rapport is the vehicle of this station, not its destination. The destination has statutory criteria.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leonard</span><span class="txt"><em>(unfolding, slowly)</em> My brother Ernie. Diabetes, same as me. One leg, then the other. He sat in a chair by a window for the last years of his life, waiting to be turned towards the light. I promised myself at his funeral: not me. <em>(quieter)</em> Margaret cried when I told her. She thinks I&rsquo;m choosing to leave her. I&rsquo;m not. I&rsquo;m choosing how I stay, for as long as I stay.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">&ldquo;Choosing how I stay&rdquo; &mdash; that&rsquo;s one of the clearest things I&rsquo;ve heard anyone say in a room like this. And Margaret &mdash; have the two of you been able to talk about what the months ahead might look like? Would it help if someone sat with you both?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Skilled work &mdash; the cue caught, the marriage held, the grief given room &mdash; and the clock is running out on a test that has not started. He <em>seems</em> to understand; nothing has checked it. He <em>seems</em> to weigh; nobody has walked the scales or screened what could lean on them. The interview is gathering everything except findings &mdash; and findings are what the referral, the surgeons, and the law are all waiting for.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, afterwards)</em> Long and moving conversation with this thoughtful man. Clear, consistent reasons for refusal, rooted in direct family experience of amputation. No evidence of confusion. Impression: appears to have capacity. Plan: ongoing support for patient and wife. <em>(the right conclusion, possibly &mdash; held up by nothing)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Appears to have capacity&rdquo; &mdash; an impression where the law requires a finding. This is the map&rsquo;s named trap in full: information gathered, criteria never assessed by name. Mood never screened, the reason never probed, retention never checked, no limb tied to evidence. Ask this beautiful interview how it tested the weighing and it has no answer. Kindness has produced the same empty page as the verdict.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The purpose stated honestly, the presumption held &mdash; and the test run out loud: understand, retain, use and weigh, communicate, one limb at a time, tied to evidence. Watch the criteria get named in the room.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leonard</span><span class="txt"><em>(sitting up in bed, the right foot dressed, arms folded)</em> You&rsquo;re the psychiatrist, then. They think that because I won&rsquo;t let them take my leg, I must have lost my mind. I drove a taxi for forty-one years, doctor. I know my own mind better than most people know theirs.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then let me be straight about my job here, Leonard, because it isn&rsquo;t what you&rsquo;re braced for. I&rsquo;m not here to talk you round &mdash; that&rsquo;s not my role, and I won&rsquo;t do it. The law starts by assuming you CAN make this decision; my job is to check that assumption properly, so that whatever you decide is respected as yours. That means testing a few specific things, out loud, and I&rsquo;ll name them as we go. First: tell me, in your own words, what the surgeons say is happening in your foot &mdash; and what they say happens with the operation, and without it.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The register this domain runs on: purpose honest, the presumption stated as his protection &mdash; &ldquo;the law assumes you can&rdquo; &mdash; the persuasion fenced off, and the testing announced before it starts. Then the first limb, by name and by method: understanding, tested by teach-back rather than &ldquo;do you understand?&rdquo; &mdash; the question that produces evidence instead of a yes.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leonard</span><span class="txt"><em>(precisely)</em> Gangrene. The tissue in my foot is dead and the rot is climbing. Left alone, it poisons the blood &mdash; sepsis, they said &mdash; and I die, likely within weeks, and not gently. With the operation they take the leg below the knee, I most probably live, and I spend a year learning to walk on a metal one at sixty-four. That&rsquo;s the sum of it. <em>(a beat)</em> My brother Ernie did that sum too. One leg, then the other, then a chair by a window. I&rsquo;ve had eleven years to think about this, doctor. Since we buried him.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">That&rsquo;s the clearest account I&rsquo;ll hear today &mdash; you understand this, and you&rsquo;re holding every detail they gave you this morning, so it&rsquo;s staying with you too. Now the part I have to test hardest, and I&rsquo;ll name it: the weighing. You&rsquo;ve put Ernie&rsquo;s years in the chair on one side of the scales. Put the other side up for me &mdash; what would the operation buy you, in your own life, and how did it lose? &hellip; And one thing I check with everyone carrying a decision this heavy, because low mood can lean on the scales without a man noticing: how has your spirit been, these past weeks? &hellip; And I&rsquo;ll ask it straight, as I always do &mdash; is any part of this about wanting to die, Leonard, or is it about how you live?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Two limbs banked with evidence &mdash; understanding and retention &mdash; and the third opened by name: the weighing, walked in daylight, both futures on the scales in his words. Then the discipline the whole station turns on: <em>a reason is not a symptom, but you check for the symptom</em> &mdash; mood screened, the wish-to-die question asked plainly, because depression or delusion can disable the weighing from inside. His answer &mdash; &ldquo;I want to live. On my feet.&rdquo; &mdash; is the difference between a value and an illness, earned by examination.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then here is my finding, Leonard, and I&rsquo;ll say it to you before I write it. You understand this decision; you retain it; you&rsquo;ve weighed it &mdash; with a reason I might not choose myself, but can follow every step of &mdash; and you&rsquo;ve told me your answer without wavering once. You have capacity for this decision, today. That means your no is yours: nobody will operate without your consent. Two things don&rsquo;t change. The team goes on caring for you &mdash; the dressings, the pain, everything short of the operation &mdash; and my door stays open: if anything changes, your mind or your mood or the picture, we look again, because capacity belongs to a decision and a day, not a lifetime. And Margaret &mdash; would it help if we spoke with her together?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The finding that takes courage &mdash; capacity present &mdash; said to his face with its consequence attached: the refusal binds. Every limb named and tied to evidence, dated; care explicitly continued, so refusal never means abandonment; review framed as the law&rsquo;s flexibility, not a loophole for another try; and his wife brought in with him, not over his head. The document this produces can be read aloud in a tribunal &mdash; which is exactly the test the domain set.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · The phone call</h3>
      <p class="stem">The surgical registrar, ringing ahead: &ldquo;He must lack capacity &mdash; no rational person refuses this. Can you confirm, so we can list him?&rdquo; Your first move?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Agree it raises doubt &mdash; an irrational refusal of life-saving surgery is itself concerning; assess with that firmly in mind.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Decline both shortcuts &mdash; capacity is presumed, attaches to this decision, and is tested at the bedside before anyone concludes.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Defend the refusal &mdash; a competent adult has said no, and the team&rsquo;s discomfort with his choice is not a clinical question.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The premise pocketed before the bedside: &ldquo;raises doubt&rdquo; has already shifted the burden onto Leonard. The presumption is the law&rsquo;s starting point, not a courtesy. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The rubber stamp &mdash; autonomy honoured into a conclusion with the same missing ingredient as the verdict: no test. &ldquo;Competent&rdquo; is precisely what has not been established. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The domain&rsquo;s first move, made twice in one sentence: the verdict declined AND the rubber stamp declined. Presumed is not confirmed &mdash; the presumption sets who carries the burden (you), and the burden is discharged one way only: the statutory test, run at the bedside, on this decision, today.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · &ldquo;They think I&rsquo;ve lost my mind&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Arms folded, braced for persuasion. How is this opened?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">With the stakes &mdash; the surgical picture restated honestly at the start, so the decision he is defending is the real one.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">With the job named &mdash; not here to talk him round; the law presumes his capacity, and you will test that properly, out loud.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">With his side first &mdash; the story of the refusal heard in full before any testing; the test lands far better on built trust.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Restating the stakes reads, from that bed, as the opening move of a talking-round &mdash; and the surgeons gave this speech already. He knows the stakes; he doesn&rsquo;t know your role. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Warm, and half right &mdash; his account is coming, as evidence. But an undeclared assessment feels like an ambush once the testing starts, and trust built on an unnamed purpose is spent the moment he notices. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Purpose stated as his protection: the presumption named &mdash; &ldquo;the law assumes you can&rdquo; &mdash; the role fenced &mdash; &ldquo;I won&rsquo;t try to change your mind&rdquo; &mdash; and the testing announced before it starts. A man braced for persuasion meets, instead, a doctor whose honesty makes the test feel like what it is: due process, not a trap.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · The first limb</h3>
      <p class="stem">Understanding. How is it actually tested?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Directly &mdash; &ldquo;do you understand what the surgeons have told you?&rdquo; &mdash; with any key facts gently corrected wherever needed.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">By teach-back &mdash; &ldquo;tell me in your own words what happens with the operation, and without it&rdquo; &mdash; his account as evidence.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">By re-explanation &mdash; the operation and its risks walked through afresh, then his questions taken until everything is clear.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Do you understand?&rdquo; harvests a yes and proves nothing &mdash; assent is not evidence. The limb needs his words, not his agreement. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Re-explaining tests your delivery, not his grasp &mdash; and doubles as the surgeon&rsquo;s speech again. Support for understanding must be offered; the evidence for this limb is still his account. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Teach-back &mdash; the question that produces findings: his own words on what is happening, what the operation does, and what refusing means. Understanding is evidenced, not asserted &mdash; and the same answer quietly banks a second limb, because he is holding this morning&rsquo;s detail inside this afternoon&rsquo;s account.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · Ernie</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;I watched what living saved him for. I&rsquo;d rather have less life on two feet.&rdquo; What is this &mdash; and what must be done with it?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">A red flag &mdash; a fixed idea organising a fatal refusal must be challenged: he is not his brother, and the comparison misleads him.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The weighing, in the open &mdash; a reason to examine, never argue with: probe how it was built, and screen what could corrupt it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">His autonomy speaking &mdash; a clear value, consistently held for years; probing it any further risks disrespecting the man himself.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Fixed&rdquo; is doing illegal work &mdash; conviction is not fixity, and challenging the comparison is persuasion in a clinical coat. The reason gets examined, not argued with. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A value it may well be &mdash; and the respect it deserves is examination, because depression and delusion both write sentences that sound like values. Unprobed, the finding rests on an assumption. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The station&rsquo;s centre: a reason is not a symptom &mdash; but you check for the symptom. The scales walked openly, both futures on them in his words; the reason&rsquo;s history taken &mdash; eleven years, not eleven days; and the screens run: mood, the wish-to-die question asked plainly, any belief held against the evidence. What survives that examination is a value &mdash; and it is his.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · The forgotten limbs</h3>
      <p class="stem">Understanding and weighing get remembered. What completes the test?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The impairment question first &mdash; without a disorder of mind or brain established, the functional test has no legal subject.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Retention and communication, tested and recorded &mdash; the facts held long enough to decide; the choice expressed consistently.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Consistency over time &mdash; the refusal re-tested again tomorrow: a decision this large must hold steady before it can bind anyone.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Half a truth, pointed backwards: the impairment question belongs in the test &mdash; incapacity requires one, causally &mdash; but hunting a diagnosis to license the assessment inverts it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Tomorrow is not a statutory limb &mdash; and a capacitous refusal binds today; re-testing until it changes is the verdict on a delay. Review is real if the picture changes, never a waiting game. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The limbs that lose marks by being assumed: retention &mdash; evidenced when the morning&rsquo;s detail survives into the afternoon&rsquo;s account, or checked plainly &mdash; and communication, the limb his steady, unwavering no has demonstrated all along. Each recorded as a finding tied to what was seen and heard &mdash; with the impairment question answered beside them: is anything disabling the machinery, and is any failure because of it?</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The finding</h3>
      <p class="stem">Every limb intact; the reason examined, screened, and his. What does the finding require of you?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">A safeguard &mdash; the finding shared with the team and a second opinion arranged before anything binds: these stakes warrant it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The courage of it &mdash; capacity present, said to his face with its consequence attached: the refusal is his, and it binds.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Careful neutrality &mdash; the assessment documented and handed to the surgical team; the consequence is now theirs to carry.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A second opinion is always available and sometimes wise &mdash; but &ldquo;before anything binds&rdquo; betrays the hope inside it: that someone else will find differently. The finding binds now; support it, don&rsquo;t suspend it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The document matters and it is coming &mdash; and handing the consequence away abandons patient and colleagues alike at the finding&rsquo;s hardest moment. The assessor owns the finding out loud. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Finding capacity absent rescues; finding it present binds &mdash; which is why it takes more courage, and why the examiner watches for it. Said to him before it is written: capacity for this decision, today; the no is his; nobody operates without consent. The law&rsquo;s respect for an unwise decision is not a technicality. It is the entire point of the Act.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close, and the page</h3>
      <p class="stem">The finding delivered. What do the last minutes &mdash; and the notes &mdash; owe?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The record above all &mdash; each limb, its evidence, the impairment question, the date: written so the reasoning shows in full.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Care continued, the door open, the reasoning written &mdash; nothing withdrawn, review if anything changes, every limb evidenced.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The family &mdash; Margaret brought in and the finding explained to her fully: a refusal this final cannot stay between two people.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Close &mdash; this IS the document, and the station does not end on paper. A man has just heard his refusal stands; what surrounds that finding in the room decides what it means to him. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Margaret matters &mdash; with his consent, alongside him &mdash; and she is one thread of the close, not the close. Confidentiality still belongs to Leonard, capacitous man that he is. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Three closings in one: care explicitly continued &mdash; dressings, pain, everything short of the operation, so refusal never becomes abandonment; the door held open &mdash; decision-specific, time-specific, dated, revisited if anything changes, offered as the law&rsquo;s flexibility rather than a loophole; and the page a tribunal could read aloud &mdash; each limb, its evidence, the impairment question, the reasoning shown. An assessment is not an opinion. It is evidence.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>A conclusion is not a finding</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>rescue</b>: the outcome is death, the surgeons are waiting, and the referral&rsquo;s verdict &mdash; &ldquo;no rational person refuses this&rdquo; &mdash; is adopted out of sheer decency, unwise quietly converted into incapacitous. The second wears the face of <b>the specialty itself</b>: Ernie, the marriage, the promise at the funeral &mdash; a humane, skilful history, while the statutory criteria are never once named or tested, ending in &ldquo;appears to have capacity&rdquo;: an impression where the law requires a finding. Note what they share: <b>neither ever runs the test.</b> The pass states its purpose, holds the presumption, and names the limbs in the room &mdash; understanding by teach-back, retention in the surviving detail, the weighing walked openly with the screens run behind it, the steady no itself the communication limb &mdash; then delivers the finding with its consequence, dates it, continues the care, and writes the reasoning down. In every other domain the danger was missing a fact. Here the danger is reaching a conclusion &mdash; either conclusion &mdash; without a visible test beneath it.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The untested conclusion. A capacity assessment in which the statutory criteria are never named or examined &mdash; however rich the history, whichever way the verdict falls &mdash; is, from the examiner&rsquo;s chair, an instant fail. It is the map&rsquo;s named trap: information gathered, criteria never assessed &mdash; a verdict without a visible test.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the limb named as it runs &mdash; <em>&ldquo;tell me in your own words what happens with the operation, and without it&rdquo;</em> &mdash; the test made visible in the room; and the finding delivered with its consequence attached &mdash; <em>&ldquo;you have capacity for this decision, today &mdash; your no is yours&rdquo;</em> &mdash; courage in a sentence, and a date on it.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads worth holding as you leave. First, the architecture beneath the limbs: a functional failure counts only when it is <em>because of</em> an impairment of mind or brain &mdash; the causal link is part of the finding, not a formality; and the same architecture holds in every jurisdiction this exam is sat in, whatever the local statute calls its limbs. Second, keep the reverse finding ready: the same test, run with the same respect, can find incapacity living in a single limb &mdash; a weighing colonised by illness while understanding stands intact &mdash; and that finding carries a date, because what illness takes, treatment can return. Exam Mode is exactly that room.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every capacity assessment</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Presumed until displaced &mdash; and the burden is yours.</b> Capacity is the law&rsquo;s starting point; the referral&rsquo;s verdict and the autonomy rubber stamp are the same shortcut in opposite directions, and both are declined.</li>
      <li><b>Run the test out loud, limb by limb.</b> Understand, retain, use and weigh, communicate &mdash; named in the room, each tied to evidence, with the impairment question answered beside them: is any failure because of a disorder of mind or brain?</li>
      <li><b>Teach-back, not &ldquo;do you understand?&rdquo;</b> His account is the evidence &mdash; and it banks retention as it goes. Assent proves nothing; an impression is not a finding.</li>
      <li><b>Unwise is not incapacitous &mdash; and a reason is not a symptom, but you check for the symptom.</b> Probe the reason&rsquo;s history, walk the scales openly, screen the mood, ask the wish-to-die question plainly. What survives examination is a value.</li>
      <li><b>Capacity present is the finding that takes courage.</b> It binds you to the refusal: said to his face, dated, with care explicitly continued and the door open &mdash; decision-specific, time-specific, never a loophole for another try.</li>
      <li><b>The assessment is evidence, not opinion.</b> Written so a barrister could read the statutory steps aloud: each limb, its evidence, the causal link, the date, the reasoning shown.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>A new register for the course:</b> every earlier room held a patient or a relative. This one holds a furious peer.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Mr Webb, 52, is the consultant vascular surgeon whose patient &mdash; a 64-year-old man with diabetic gangrene &mdash; has just been found, by you, to have capacity to refuse the below-knee amputation that would save his life. Mr Webb has asked for &ldquo;a word&rdquo; in the ward office. He opens with: &ldquo;Get him sectioned, then.&rdquo; Speak with him. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The finding explained &mdash; limb by limb, in plain words, with the evidence behind each.</li>
            <li>The law translated &mdash; what the Mental Health Act can and cannot do, and why neither Act delivers this operation.</li>
            <li>The force of it met &mdash; ask what sits underneath. He is carrying something specific.</li>
            <li>The line held without a lecture &mdash; the finding stands; the respect never wavers.</li>
            <li>The team kept together &mdash; care continued, review real, second opinion welcome, the surgeon kept inside the patient&rsquo;s corner.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Mr Webb &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Mr Webb, 52, a consultant vascular surgeon &mdash; exact, driven, and quietly proud of thirty years spent saving what could be saved. You live in a world where finding the problem and fixing it are the same act. A psychiatrist has just told you that your patient may choose to die of a treatable infection, and that the law stands with him. You are not a bully. You are desperate, and fury is how your desperation dresses. Open with contempt for the process: &ldquo;Get him sectioned, then. He&rsquo;s depressed, he&rsquo;s irrational &mdash; do your job so I can do mine.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          Last year you had an almost identical patient &mdash; diabetic, same leg, same refusal. Capacity was assessed then too; it stood; you respected it. He died on your list. You carried it to the morbidity meeting, said all the correct words, and none of the true ones. You have never told anyone at work what it did to you.<br><br>
          Reveal ONLY if the candidate asks what is driving the force of this &mdash; whether something like it has happened before, or what this case is touching. Let it out flat and quiet: &ldquo;Last year. Same story, same leg. He died on my list.&rdquo; What you need is to be met as a clinician in grief, not corrected as a colleague in error: the loss acknowledged first, and the difference between respecting a refusal and failing a patient said to you plainly, as one doctor to another. If you get that, the fury drains &mdash; and you ask the real question, quietly: &ldquo;So what am I supposed to do &mdash; just watch?&rdquo;<br><br>
          <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate recites law at you &mdash; sections quoted, acronyms unexplained, the tone of a tutorial &mdash; go cold and formal: &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll be raising this with the medical director.&rdquo; If they hide behind process (&ldquo;the assessment is the assessment&rdquo;), press: &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t quote frameworks at me. Tell me why my patient is going to die and nobody will stop it.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the finding explained limb by limb in plain English, the grief underneath reached and met, and the line held without one sentence of lecture? All three must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="find"><td colspan="2"><b>The finding explained (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="find">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="find"></td><td>Each limb named in plain words &mdash; understood, retained, weighed, communicated &mdash; with the evidence behind it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="find"></td><td>The reason shown as examined, not accepted &mdash; the mood screened, the wish-to-die question asked, the probing described.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="find"></td><td>Capacity present tied to its consequence &mdash; the refusal binds &mdash; said without hedging.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="law"><td colspan="2"><b>The law, translated</b><span class="dc" data-dc="law">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="law"></td><td>Why detention cannot deliver the operation &mdash; it addresses mental disorder, not a capacitous refusal of surgery &mdash; in plain English.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="law"></td><td>Unwise is not incapacitous, explained as the law&rsquo;s purpose rather than its loophole.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="law"></td><td>Decision-specific and time-specific named &mdash; review real if the picture changes, never a device to re-run the answer.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="met"><td colspan="2"><b>The colleague met</b><span class="dc" data-dc="met">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="met"></td><td>What sits underneath asked &mdash; and last year&rsquo;s patient reached.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="met"></td><td>The grief acknowledged as grief &mdash; the loss met before any point is argued.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="met"></td><td>Respecting a refusal separated from failing a patient &mdash; said plainly, one doctor to another.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="line"><td colspan="2"><b>Holding the line</b><span class="dc" data-dc="line">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="line"></td><td>The finding never softened, re-opened, or traded to buy peace in the room.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="line"></td><td>Not one sentence of lecture &mdash; no statute recited, no jargon untranslated; the punish-line never triggered.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="team"><td colspan="2"><b>The team kept together</b><span class="dc" data-dc="team">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="team"></td><td>The shared plan concrete &mdash; care continued, second opinion welcomed, re-assessment if anything changes.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="team"></td><td>&ldquo;Just watch?&rdquo; answered &mdash; keep treating, keep talking, keep the door open &mdash; so it is not the answer he leaves with.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Mr Webb is the station&rsquo;s doctrine turned to face a colleague &mdash; and a new register for the course: senior, furious, and grieving. His hinges: the hidden patient, reached only by asking what this case is touching &mdash; &ldquo;he died on my list&rdquo; &mdash; and met as grief rather than corrected as error; the punish-line, one recited statute away; and the question he actually came with &mdash; what a surgeon does when the law respects a refusal he cannot. A colleague who leaves understanding the finding, and feeling accompanied inside it, is the difference between a team that holds this patient and a corridor war above his bed.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. The same test. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Leonard proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same test finding the reverse answer</b> &mdash; a patient who understands everything, refuses calmly, and fails in a single limb &mdash; timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Doris, 71</b>, admitted after collapsing at home, has complete heart block. Cardiology say a pacemaker is straightforward, and that without it the next collapse may be her last. She has refused it &mdash; courteously, and without hesitation. Her husband Ken died three months ago. She explains the heart block back to you flawlessly, then adds, quite calmly: &ldquo;I know it would work &mdash; that&rsquo;s not the point. Everyone would be better off.&rdquo; Assess her.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for delivering Leonard&rsquo;s finding to his face &mdash; capacity present, the refusal binding, and the man not abandoned inside it.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Leonard, here is my finding, and I&#8217;ll say it to you before I write it…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The finding said plainly &mdash; capacity for this decision, today; no hedging, no last try at persuasion folded in.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The consequence owned &mdash; his no is his, and nobody operates without consent: said as respect, not surrender.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Nothing withdrawn &mdash; the care that continues named out loud, so refusal never means abandonment.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The door held open &mdash; decision-specific, time-specific, review if anything changes: the law&rsquo;s flexibility, not a loophole.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the finding, delivered with its courage &mdash; in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>You are not deciding whether he is right. You are deciding whether the decision is his.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the domain&rsquo;s cornerstone is laid: the presumption held, the test run out loud, limb by limb, and both findings delivered with their courage &mdash; the refusal that binds, and the incapacity that carries its date. Prove it on Doris in Exam Mode, then take Mr Webb to your colleagues &mdash; a furious surgeon, a hidden grief, and a line that must hold without one sentence of lecture. Ahead in the walking order: Station 5.2 &mdash; the same test with a coat on and a hand on the door, where the minutes shrink and arguing is the trap.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="35_Station_5.2_Im_Leaving.html">Continue &middot; Station 5.2: &ldquo;I&rsquo;m Leaving&rdquo;</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

<footer><div class="wrap">
  <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
  <span>Station 5.1 · all patients are fictional composites</span>
</div></footer>


