<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 5.3 · Domain 5</span>
      <span class="tag">Capacity, Consent &amp; the Law</span><span class="tag">Difficult Conversations</span>
      <span class="tag">Any specialty</span><span class="tag">Confidentiality</span>
    </div>
    <h1>The Relative Who Wants Everything</h1>
    <p class="sub">Alan, 61, a joiner, has driven two hours and is waiting in the family room with a handwritten list. His son Jake, 24, was admitted twelve days ago &mdash; his second admission with psychosis &mdash; and Jake has told the team, clearly and with capacity: &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t tell my dad anything.&rdquo; Alan wants the diagnosis, the medication, the prognosis, and a date. Two doors stand open, and both lead to a failed station: tell him everything, and a capacitous man&rsquo;s confidence is broken out of kindness; tell him nothing, and a frightened father is handed a policy where a doctor should have been. <b>Between the open book and the locked door sits the lawful middle &mdash; and it is found warmly, or it is not found at all.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; In this room it asks: if my registrar meets this father, do I get back the line held AND the family served &mdash; the refusal honoured as the patient&rsquo;s right, everything hearable heard, everything general given generously, the consent question carried back to the son, the carer seen as a person? Or do I get one of the two cheap endings: a breach committed out of sympathy, or a policy recited at a frightened man while the listening, the giving, and the carer all go unused?</div>
  </div>
</header>

