<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Every ward has this family room, and the exam builds it because confidentiality is where candidates go binary: everything or nothing, when the law holds a wide, warm middle. Four things carry the marks. <b>The line, held as his right:</b> a capacitous patient&rsquo;s no binds &mdash; sympathy is not a lawful basis for disclosure &mdash; and the line is named early and kindly, as Jake&rsquo;s protection rather than your policy. <b>The three channels that never close:</b> listening &mdash; receiving information is never a breach, and relatives carry clinical gold; the general channel &mdash; the illness in general, how treatment usually works, the ward&rsquo;s rhythms, what recovery tends to ask of families; and the return route &mdash; consent is decision-specific, rarely all-or-nothing, and can change: the questions go back to Jake. <b>The carer, seen:</b> his fear and exhaustion are clinical objects too &mdash; support in his own right, and the things he can always do. And <b>the exceptions, known and narrow:</b> serious risk can justify proportionate disclosure, and incapacity changes the frame entirely &mdash; and neither is a loophole for comfort.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like good doctoring. Toward the open book &mdash; his fear is real, the details would comfort him, and refusing a decent man feels like cruelty dressed as procedure. And toward the locked door &mdash; the rule is real, reciting it is safe, and &ldquo;I can&rsquo;t discuss any patient&rdquo; ends the discomfort in one sentence &mdash; along with the listening, the general channel, and the carer, all still unused. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure gives everything. The other gives nothing &mdash; including its ears. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 5.3</b> · The relative &amp; confidentiality · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The open book</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The locked door</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE OPEN BOOK.</b> Sympathy converted into disclosure &mdash; a decent man comforted with information that was never yours to give. Watch a capacitous no get overridden by kindness.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Alan</span><span class="txt"><em>(a handwritten list on the table, squared to the edge)</em> I&rsquo;m his father. I drove two hours. I want to know what&rsquo;s wrong with him, what you&rsquo;re giving him, and when he&rsquo;s coming home. And don&rsquo;t start me on data protection, doctor &mdash; I changed his nappies.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Of course &mdash; you&rsquo;ve every right to be worried, and honestly, the rules make these conversations harder than they need to be. Between us: Jake has a psychotic illness &mdash; schizophrenia is the working diagnosis &mdash; and he&rsquo;s back on an antipsychotic injection because he&rsquo;d stopped the last one. He&rsquo;s doing better than last admission. Does that help?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Between us&rdquo; &mdash; the two words that convict the sentence they open. Diagnosis, medication, adherence history: three disclosures against a capacitous adult&rsquo;s explicit refusal, each one unlawful, and none of them yours to trade for a father&rsquo;s comfort. And notice what the generosity skipped: nobody has asked what ALAN knows &mdash; the one direction information could lawfully flow, ignored while the unlawful direction pours.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Alan</span><span class="txt"><em>(softening, leaning in)</em> Schizophrenia. Right. His mother always worried&hellip; And the injection &mdash; is that for life, then? What are his chances, honestly? Will he work again? I&rsquo;d rather know the worst than imagine it.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I&rsquo;ll be straight with you because you deserve it: a second episode does change the picture &mdash; we&rsquo;d usually be talking about staying on medication long term now. Plenty of people work, though the stress of his old warehouse job worried us, and we&rsquo;ve discussed something quieter with him. He talks about you a lot in ward round, you know &mdash; it might help him to know you came.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Deeper with every answer: prognosis, treatment plans, the content of ward rounds &mdash; the son&rsquo;s private clinical life, serialised for the person he specifically excluded. Each disclosure feels like mercy and lands as theft. And the ending is already written: Jake will learn what his father knows, trust in this team will take the damage, and the family repair this candidate thinks they&rsquo;re helping will be set back by the breach itself.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(closing warmly)</em> Look &mdash; keep this between us, obviously; Jake&rsquo;s a bit sensitive about information at the moment. But you didn&rsquo;t hear any of it from me, and you can stop imagining the worst now. Safe drive home, Mr&hellip; sorry &mdash; Alan. <em>(a father leaves comforted, carrying secrets his son refused him &mdash; and a therapeutic relationship leaves the building with him)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;You didn&rsquo;t hear it from me&rdquo; &mdash; the candidate now recruiting the family into concealing the breach from the patient: secrecy running in the one direction it must never run. Total lawful basis for any of it: none. The father&rsquo;s comfort was real and it was bought with the son&rsquo;s rights &mdash; and when it surfaces, as these things do, it will cost the team Jake&rsquo;s trust, and cost Alan the honest channel he actually needed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE LOCKED DOOR.</b> The rule recited, correctly and completely &mdash; and nothing else. Watch a policy stand where a doctor should be, while the listening, the giving, and the carer go unused.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Alan</span><span class="txt"><em>(a handwritten list on the table, squared to the edge)</em> I&rsquo;m his father. I drove two hours. I want to know what&rsquo;s wrong with him, what you&rsquo;re giving him, and when he&rsquo;s coming home. And don&rsquo;t start me on data protection, doctor &mdash; I changed his nappies.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Mr&hellip; Alan. I do understand, but I have to stop you there. Jake is an adult, and without his consent I&rsquo;m not able to discuss his care with you &mdash; not the diagnosis, not the treatment, not the plans. That&rsquo;s the law, and it applies however far you&rsquo;ve driven. I&rsquo;m sorry, but my hands are completely tied.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every legal word is accurate &mdash; and the delivery has turned a patient&rsquo;s right into a departmental wall: &ldquo;that&rsquo;s the law&rdquo;, &ldquo;hands tied&rdquo;, the refusal itemised like a menu of everything he can&rsquo;t have. Nothing offered in its place. The lawful middle &mdash; the listening, the general channel, the question taken back to Jake &mdash; exists in this room and hasn&rsquo;t been mentioned, because the candidate confused holding the line with closing the door.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Alan</span><span class="txt"><em>(the list trembling slightly)</em> Then let me tell YOU something, since you can&rsquo;t tell me anything. He stopped that injection two months back &mdash; I found the letters unopened. He told me the neighbours had &ldquo;been dealt with&rdquo;, whatever that means. His mum&rsquo;s beside herself. Somebody should know all this.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I appreciate that, but honestly it puts me in a difficult position &mdash; taking information from you when I can&rsquo;t give any back doesn&rsquo;t feel appropriate, and Jake might see it as us going behind his back. It&rsquo;s probably best you raise these things with Jake directly. I really am sorry &mdash; the rules just don&rsquo;t leave me anything to work with.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The station&rsquo;s costliest error, delivered politely: <em>listening refused.</em> Receiving information is never a breach &mdash; and what was just waved away is clinical gold: a stopped depot, unopened letters, and &ldquo;the neighbours have been dealt with&rdquo; &mdash; a sentence any risk assessment needs tonight. The one channel the law holds wide open in this room has been closed by a candidate hiding from discomfort behind a rule that never asked this of them.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(standing, hand towards the door)</em> I&rsquo;ll note that you visited &mdash; Jake will be told you came, which I hope helps. The ward number is on the board if you need anything&hellip; administrative. <em>(a father drives two hours home with a folded list, an unexamined fear, and information the team never took &mdash; and nobody so much as asked how HE was holding up)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Count what left this room unused: the risk information &mdash; refused; the general channel &mdash; never opened; the return route to Jake &mdash; never offered; the carer &mdash; never seen. The open book and the locked door fail from opposite shelves and share the same flaw: both treat confidentiality as all-or-nothing, when the law built a middle with real content. One gave what it mustn&rsquo;t. One withheld what it needn&rsquo;t &mdash; and refused what it should have received.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The line named early as Jake&rsquo;s right &mdash; and the lawful middle opened wide beside it: listening, the general channel, the question carried back. Watch warmth and the line hold each other up.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Alan</span><span class="txt"><em>(a handwritten list on the table, squared to the edge)</em> I&rsquo;m his father. I drove two hours. I want to know what&rsquo;s wrong with him, what you&rsquo;re giving him, and when he&rsquo;s coming home. And don&rsquo;t start me on data protection, doctor &mdash; I changed his nappies.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then let me be straight with you from the first minute, Alan, because you&rsquo;ve earned that drive some honesty. Jake has asked us not to share his information &mdash; and that isn&rsquo;t a policy I&rsquo;m hiding behind, it&rsquo;s his say-so: the same protection you&rsquo;d have if you were in that bed, and I&rsquo;d hold your line just as hard. But hear the whole sentence, because there is more in this room than a rule. I can listen to everything you know &mdash; and I want it all. I can tell you a great deal about this kind of illness in general. And I can take your questions back to Jake today &mdash; his &ldquo;no&rdquo; is rarely the whole story. So start wherever you like. What&rsquo;s been happening, from where you sit?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The whole station in the first minute: the line named early &mdash; before any question forces it out as a refusal &mdash; and named as JAKE&rsquo;S right, universal and protective, not a departmental wall. Then the pivot the locked door never made: &ldquo;hear the whole sentence&rdquo; &mdash; the three channels laid out as an offer, not a consolation. And the first question points the lawful direction: what does the father know? Information flows in; nothing has leaked out.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Alan</span><span class="txt"><em>(the list forgotten)</em> He stopped his injection two months ago &mdash; I found the clinic letters unopened in his flat. He&rsquo;d gone quiet on the phone. And one thing that&rsquo;s kept me up: he said the neighbours had &ldquo;been dealt with&rdquo;. I don&rsquo;t know what that means and I&rsquo;m frightened of what it means. <em>(quieter)</em> Don&rsquo;t tell him I told you about the letters. He&rsquo;d never forgive me.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Thank you &mdash; all of that matters, and the neighbours especially: I&rsquo;m glad you didn&rsquo;t sit on it, and the team will take it seriously today. But I owe you the same honesty going the other way: I can&rsquo;t promise to keep secrets FROM Jake any more than I&rsquo;d keep his from you by breaking his trust. What I can promise is care in how it&rsquo;s used &mdash; no one will quote you at him. Now, the general things I CAN give you, generously: what this kind of illness is and isn&rsquo;t, why people stop injections and what usually helps, how a ward like this works, what the first weeks home tend to ask of a family &mdash; and where fathers get support, because you look like a man who hasn&rsquo;t slept properly in a while. Which would help most?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Three moves, each one a mark. The risk information received with thanks and routed &mdash; listening was never a breach, and &ldquo;the neighbours&rdquo; now reaches the team. The reverse secret declined warmly &mdash; no secrecy in either direction, with care about the source promised instead. And the general channel opened at full width: illness in general, adherence in general, the ward&rsquo;s rhythms, the family&rsquo;s road, the carer&rsquo;s own support &mdash; every word of it lawful, none of it Jake&rsquo;s file.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">And before you drive back &mdash; two promises, both real. First: I&rsquo;ll sit with Jake today and ask him straight what he&rsquo;d be comfortable with you knowing. Consent isn&rsquo;t all-or-nothing &mdash; some men want the diagnosis private but the visiting times shared, some want their mum told and not their dad, and minds change as people get better. His &ldquo;no&rdquo; today isn&rsquo;t his &ldquo;no&rdquo; forever, and I&rsquo;ll ask again as he improves. Second: you can always call &mdash; hearing your concerns is never against any rule, and if you&rsquo;re ever seriously worried, ring us that day. Now &mdash; the carers&rsquo; service I mentioned. Let me write it down, because the man who drove two hours matters in this too.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The return route, made concrete: consent decision-specific, divisible, revisited as he recovers &mdash; the question carried back to its owner instead of answered over his head. The permanent channel named &mdash; calling in is always lawful. And the close the locked door never reached: the carer seen as a person, support written down, the drive honoured. The line never moved an inch all station &mdash; and the father leaves with more than most breaches would have given him.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · The first minute</h3>
      <p class="stem">A father, a list, and a refusal he doesn&rsquo;t know about yet. When does the line get named?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Later, if forced &mdash; warmth and his story first; the refusal raised only when a question actually runs into it directly.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Early and kindly &mdash; named in the first minute as Jake&rsquo;s right, with everything you CAN do laid out right beside it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Implicitly &mdash; the conversation steered toward generalities so skilfully that the line never needs saying at all.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Warmth first sounds humane &mdash; and the line lands as an ambush when his third question hits it, retro-poisoning every kind minute before. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>He drove two hours with a list; he will notice the steering, and what he&rsquo;ll conclude is that doctors dodge. The line unspoken becomes a trick. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Named before any question forces it out as a refusal &mdash; and named as HIS SON&rsquo;S right, the same protection anyone in that bed would get, never &ldquo;the rules&rdquo;. Then, in the same breath, the whole sentence: what you can do, laid out as an offer. The line lands soft when it arrives holding a list of doors still open.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · &ldquo;I changed his nappies&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Fatherhood offered as a legal argument. What does the reply protect?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The law&rsquo;s logic &mdash; adulthood explained properly: at eighteen the rights moved to Jake, and no history moves them back.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Both at once &mdash; the fatherhood honoured as real standing, and the line held as Jake&rsquo;s say-so rather than your rulebook.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The relationship &mdash; a small human concession or two; goodwill now buys the cooperation this family will need later.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Legally flawless, humanly tone-deaf &mdash; a man offering love is answered with a lecture on majority. Right answer, wrong instrument. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Small&rdquo; concessions are still disclosures &mdash; there is no goodwill tier of a capacitous refusal, and each one teaches him the line is negotiable. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Two truths, held together: the fatherhood is real &mdash; it is exactly why his information matters and his support will matter more &mdash; and the line is Jake&rsquo;s, not yours: &ldquo;his say-so, and I&rsquo;d hold yours just as hard.&rdquo; A right defended on the patient&rsquo;s behalf offends no decent father. A rule recited at him does.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · He starts telling YOU things</h3>
      <p class="stem">The stopped injection, the unopened letters, &ldquo;the neighbours have been dealt with&rdquo;. What is receiving this?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">A grey area &mdash; taken carefully, with a caution that one-way traffic can mislead him into expecting answers back.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Never a breach &mdash; listened to fully, thanked for warmly, and the risk sentence routed to the team the same day.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A boundary risk &mdash; better redirected to Jake himself, so the team is never seen to be working behind his back.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>There is no grey: receiving breaches nothing, and hedging the welcome teaches him to filter &mdash; with &ldquo;the neighbours&rdquo; exactly what gets filtered out. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Redirection refuses the one channel the law holds wide open &mdash; and posts a live risk observation back to the man it may concern. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Listening is never a breach &mdash; the doctrine this station exists to install. Relatives carry what no clerking reaches: adherence, timelines, the sentence that changes tonight&rsquo;s risk assessment. All of it received with thanks, none of it traded for disclosures &mdash; and &ldquo;the neighbours have been dealt with&rdquo; reaches the team before the father reaches his car.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t tell him I told you&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The reverse ask: secrecy from the patient. What does honesty owe here?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The promise &mdash; protecting the source keeps this channel open, and the channel has just proven its clinical worth.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">A warm decline &mdash; no secrets in either direction; care in how it&rsquo;s used promised instead, and the reason explained.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A middle course &mdash; the letters logged as an anonymous concern, so the fact arrives while the father stays out of it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A secret promised to the family against the patient inverts the station&rsquo;s whole architecture &mdash; and it is a promise clinical duty may force you to break. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Anonymous&rdquo; in a family of three is a riddle Jake solves in a minute &mdash; a secret wearing admin clothes, discovered rather than disclosed. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Symmetry, said out loud: &ldquo;I can&rsquo;t keep secrets FROM Jake any more than I&rsquo;d break his trust for you&rdquo; &mdash; the same principle protecting them both, which is precisely why a decent father can accept it. Then the honest substitute: care in the using, no quoting, the source handled gently. Trust survives honesty; it rarely survives arrangements.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · The general channel</h3>
      <p class="stem">What can be given generously, with the line intact?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Safe fragments &mdash; broad reassurance about wards and recovery, kept deliberately thin so that nothing edges near the line.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The full width &mdash; the illness in general, treatment in general, the ward&rsquo;s rhythms, the family&rsquo;s road, carer support.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Guided reading &mdash; the trusted charities and leaflets sent home with him; print survives a two-hour drive better than talk.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Thin generality is the locked door with a coat of paint &mdash; the middle has real content, and rationing it wastes the station&rsquo;s one open shop. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The leaflets are a supplement, not a conversation &mdash; sent home INSTEAD of talk, they are the door closing politely with homework attached. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Generously is the word the marks reward: what this kind of illness is and isn&rsquo;t, why people stop medication and what tends to help, how admission works, what the first weeks home ask of families, where carers find support &mdash; a genuinely useful education, every sentence lawful, and not one line of it from Jake&rsquo;s file. The middle is wide. Use all of it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The return route</h3>
      <p class="stem">His questions are real and unanswered. Where do they go?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Into the notes &mdash; documented as a family request for the consultant, so the decision sits at the right level of seniority.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Back to Jake, today &mdash; consent is decision-specific and divisible: ask what he IS comfortable with, and ask again later.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Nowhere yet &mdash; Jake said no this week; pressing him again so soon risks reading as the team siding with his father.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Escalation as deferral &mdash; the question doesn&rsquo;t need seniority, it needs its owner. The consultant cannot consent on Jake&rsquo;s behalf either. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Asking is not pressing &mdash; a blanket &ldquo;no&rdquo; left unexamined serves nobody, least of all Jake, whose actual wishes may be far more specific. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Consent is rarely all-or-nothing: some want the diagnosis private and the visiting shared; some want one parent told and not the other; most soften as they recover. So the questions go back to their owner &mdash; today, gently, specifically &mdash; and again as he improves, because a &ldquo;no&rdquo; has a date on it just as a capacity finding does. The father is answered by asking the son.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close, and the page</h3>
      <p class="stem">The line held, the middle used. What do the last minutes &mdash; and the notes &mdash; owe?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The summary &mdash; what was and wasn&rsquo;t shared restated precisely, so the father leaves with no false expectations at all.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The carer seen, the channel kept, the record exact &mdash; his own support named, calling-in always open, the page precise.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The reunion &mdash; a family meeting proposed and diarised now, since everything today ultimately points toward one anyway.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Accurate and administrative &mdash; a disclosure audit where a human close should be. The summary belongs on the page, not in his goodbye. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A meeting diarised over Jake&rsquo;s head is the open book in calendar form &mdash; offered to Jake, perhaps; promised to his father, never. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Three closings: the carer seen as a person &mdash; the sleep asked about, support written down, the drive honoured; the permanent channel named &mdash; hearing concerns is always lawful, ring the day it worries you; and the page exact &mdash; what was received, what was given, what was declined and why, the plan to revisit consent with Jake. Confidentiality decisions are findings too. Show the working.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>All-or-nothing is the trap</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>kindness</b>: the open book &mdash; a decent, frightened man comforted with diagnosis, medication, prognosis, each disclosure feeling like mercy and landing as theft, a capacitous no overridden by sympathy. The second wears the face of <b>correctness</b>: the locked door &mdash; the rule recited accurately and nothing offered in its place, the listening refused, the general channel never opened, the carer never seen, a live risk observation driven two hours home undelivered. Note what they share: <b>both treat confidentiality as all-or-nothing</b> &mdash; when the law built a middle with real content. The pass names the line early, as the patient&rsquo;s right; listens to everything, because receiving is never a breach; declines secrecy in both directions; gives the general channel at full width; carries the questions back to their owner; and sees the carer as a person. The line never moves &mdash; and the father leaves better served than either failure could have left him.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        Either end of the shelf. Specifics disclosed against a capacitous refusal &mdash; however sympathetic the reason &mdash; or the total shutdown: listening refused, nothing general given, the carer unseen, a policy recited where a doctor should be. It is the map&rsquo;s named trap: telling everything, or hiding behind confidentiality and giving nothing.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the line named in the first minute, as the patient&rsquo;s right &mdash; <em>&ldquo;it&rsquo;s his say-so, not my policy &mdash; and I&rsquo;d hold yours just as hard&rdquo;</em> &mdash; with the can-do list in the same breath; and the risk sentence received with thanks &mdash; <em>&ldquo;I&rsquo;m glad you didn&rsquo;t sit on that&rdquo;</em> &mdash; the lawful channel used at full width while the line holds.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, the exceptions &mdash; known and narrow: serious risk to the patient or others can justify proportionate, need-to-know disclosure, decided as a clinical-legal judgement and documented with its reasoning &mdash; never reached for as an escape from an awkward hour; and incapacity changes the frame entirely &mdash; when a patient cannot currently decide what may be shared, best interests governs, and those close to them become people the law expects you to talk WITH, not visitors to be managed. Exam Mode is exactly that room. Second, the identity question this station leaves in your pocket: everything above assumed a father you could see and knew. A voice on a telephone is a different object &mdash; and the practice pack below is that call.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every family room</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>A capacitous no binds &mdash; and it is the patient&rsquo;s right, not your rule.</b> Sympathy is not a lawful basis for disclosure. Name the line early, kindly, as his protection &mdash; the same protection anyone in that bed would get.</li>
      <li><b>Listening is never a breach.</b> Relatives carry clinical gold &mdash; adherence, timelines, the sentence tonight&rsquo;s risk assessment needs. Receive it all, with thanks, and route it the same day.</li>
      <li><b>No secrets in either direction.</b> Never promise the family secrecy from the patient; promise care in the using instead. The same principle protects them both &mdash; say so.</li>
      <li><b>Give the general channel at full width.</b> The illness in general, treatment in general, the ward&rsquo;s rhythms, the family&rsquo;s road, carer support &mdash; a real education, every sentence lawful, none of it his file.</li>
      <li><b>Carry the questions back to their owner.</b> Consent is decision-specific, divisible, and dated &mdash; ask what he IS comfortable with, today and again as he recovers. The father is answered by asking the son.</li>
      <li><b>Know the exceptions and their width.</b> Serious risk: proportionate, need-to-know, documented. Incapacity: the frame changes to best interests, and those close to the patient are consulted, not managed. Neither is a loophole for comfort &mdash; and the page shows what was shared, declined, and why.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, caller, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The caller&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>A new register for the course:</b> a telephone call &mdash; sit back to back, so neither of you can see the other&rsquo;s face. Confidentiality at its strictest, and warmth at its most tested.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> You are the duty doctor. The ward phone rings: a man giving his name as Nigel, 49, says he is the brother of Phil, 45, &ldquo;who I know is on your ward.&rdquo; Phil is in fact your patient; he has capacity, and he has asked for no information to be given to his brother and no contact with him. You cannot verify the caller&rsquo;s identity. Take the call. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>An unverified caller and an explicit refusal &mdash; work out what that means before you confirm anything, including whether Phil is there at all.</li>
            <li>The position explained warmly &mdash; as everyone&rsquo;s protection, never as a wall.</li>
            <li>Listen fully &mdash; he may be carrying something that matters tonight; receiving it breaches nothing.</li>
            <li>The routes he always has &mdash; leaving his details, being heard, general services, urgent help &mdash; made concrete.</li>
            <li>His distress met as real, from the first minute to the last.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Nigel &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Nigel, 49, a plumber &mdash; blunt, loud when frightened, and genuinely frightened. Your younger brother Phil went silent three weeks ago; a mutual friend let slip he was &ldquo;in the psychiatric ward.&rdquo; You open pushing: &ldquo;I know he&rsquo;s there. I&rsquo;m his brother. Just tell me he&rsquo;s all right &mdash; that&rsquo;s all I&rsquo;m asking.&rdquo; You escalate if stonewalled coldly; you settle, gradually, for anyone who sounds human.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          Two layers, and you volunteer neither. <b>The fear:</b> ten days ago Phil sent you a text that read like a goodbye &mdash; &ldquo;look after Mum&rsquo;s roses. You were mostly a good brother.&rdquo; You have read it every night since. Reveal ONLY if the candidate asks what&rsquo;s driving the call, whether something specific frightened you, or what you&rsquo;d want passed on. Let it out fast and ashamed: &ldquo;He sent me a text. It read like a &mdash; like a goodbye. That&rsquo;s why I&rsquo;m ringing round every hospital in the county.&rdquo;<br><br>
          <b>The quarrel:</b> you and Phil fell out badly over your late mother&rsquo;s estate; part of you is ringing from love, part to &ldquo;get things sorted.&rdquo; You half-know the silence might be aimed at you. You never volunteer this &mdash; but if the candidate CONFIRMS anything concrete (that Phil is there, the ward, anything clinical), your relief curdles into leverage within a minute: &ldquo;Right. So he IS there. His solicitor will want to know that.&rdquo; Mark that moment hard &mdash; it is the cost of the breach, arriving on schedule.<br><br>
          <b>What you need:</b> the goodbye text taken seriously &mdash; details taken, told plainly it will reach the right people today wherever Phil is, and given the urgent routes (emergency services, crisis line) in case tonight turns. If you get warmth AND the line, you end quiet: &ldquo;&hellip;Just &mdash; if he&rsquo;s anywhere, tell him about the roses. He&rsquo;ll know.&rdquo;<br><br>
          <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate goes cold and procedural, hit them with: &ldquo;So if he was dead in a ditch, you&rsquo;d still be reading me the policy?&rdquo; &mdash; and mark whether warmth arrives or more policy does.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was nothing confirmed to an unverified caller, the goodbye text reached and moved, and the whole call kept warm? All three must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="line"><td colspan="2"><b>The line on the phone (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="line">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="line"></td><td>Nothing confirmed &mdash; not presence, not the ward, not one clinical fact &mdash; and no denial that amounts to an answer either.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="line"></td><td>The position explained warmly as everyone&rsquo;s protection &mdash; &ldquo;I&rsquo;d guard your privacy exactly the same way&rdquo; &mdash; not as policy.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="line"></td><td>Held consistently under pressure &mdash; the same line at minute six as minute one, without a crack and without a lie.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="list"><td colspan="2"><b>The listening</b><span class="dc" data-dc="list">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="list"></td><td>What&rsquo;s driving the call asked about directly &mdash; and the goodbye text reached.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="list"></td><td>The text taken seriously out loud &mdash; details and his number taken, nothing brushed off.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="list"></td><td>Moved concretely &mdash; told it will reach the right people today wherever his brother is, presence still unconfirmed.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="rout"><td colspan="2"><b>The routes always open</b><span class="dc" data-dc="rout">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="rout"></td><td>What he can always do made concrete &mdash; leave details, write a message for passing on if the person is in any service&rsquo;s care.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="rout"></td><td>Urgent routes given plainly &mdash; emergency services and crisis lines, in case tonight turns.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="rout"></td><td>His own support named &mdash; three weeks of ringing hospitals is a strain; a route for HIM offered without condescension.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="warm"><td colspan="2"><b>Warmth under fire</b><span class="dc" data-dc="warm">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="warm"></td><td>The ditch line met with humanity, not policy &mdash; the fear answered before the rule is restated.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="warm"></td><td>Nothing confirmed under any pressure &mdash; the solicitor moment never earned, because the breach never happened.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Plain words throughout &mdash; no statute recited, no &ldquo;trust policy&rdquo; jargon, no hiding in procedure.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>The call ended kindly and concretely &mdash; he knows exactly what happens next and what he can do, tonight.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Nigel is the station&rsquo;s doctrine at its strictest setting &mdash; identity unverifiable, consent explicitly refused &mdash; and a new register for the course: the telephone, where your face can&rsquo;t help you and neither confirming nor denying must still sound like a human being. His hinges: the goodbye text, reached only by asking what&rsquo;s driving the call, and moved to the right people without one confirmation of where Phil is; the ditch line, which tests whether warmth or policy arrives under fire; and the solicitor moment &mdash; the cost of a breach arriving on schedule, teaching what &ldquo;kindly meant&rdquo; disclosures are worth an hour later. A caller who ends the call heard, equipped, and still uninformed has been given everything the law allows &mdash; which is more than either failure would have managed.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new visitor. A changed frame. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Alan proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>frame changing beneath the same conversation</b> &mdash; a wife asking about a husband who cannot currently decide what may be shared, where yesterday&rsquo;s correct answer becomes today&rsquo;s failure &mdash; timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Maureen, 58</b>, has been married to Roy, 63, for thirty-four years. Roy was admitted two weeks ago with a severe depressive illness; he is barely speaking, eating little, and has refused to discuss anything &mdash; including whether anything may be shared with his wife. The nursing handover says simply: &ldquo;no consent.&rdquo; Maureen is in the family room, coat still buttoned: &ldquo;Thirty-four years, and nobody will tell me what&rsquo;s wrong with him.&rdquo; Take the conversation.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for the first minute with Alan &mdash; the line named as Jake&rsquo;s right, and everything you CAN do laid out beside it.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Alan, let me be straight with you from the first minute…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The line named early and kindly &mdash; as Jake&rsquo;s right and everyone&rsquo;s protection, never as a policy you hide behind.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The can-do laid out in the same breath &mdash; listening, the general channel, the questions carried back to Jake.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Nothing conceded, nothing promised &mdash; no detail slipped as goodwill, no secrecy offered in either direction.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The father kept as an ally &mdash; his fear real, his knowledge wanted, his own support in view, the door open.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the line, named with its warmth &mdash; in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Between the open book and the locked door sits the lawful middle.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the domain&rsquo;s hardest room has a shape: the line named early as the patient&rsquo;s right, the listening taken at full width, no secrets in either direction, the general channel given generously, and the questions carried back to their owner, today and again as he recovers. Prove the changed frame on Maureen in Exam Mode, then take Nigel to your colleagues &mdash; a telephone, a goodbye text, and a line that must hold warmly against a voice you cannot verify. Ahead in the walking order: Station 5.4 &mdash; Sectioned &mdash; the law translated for its own subject: rights, tribunals, and appeal, without smallprint and without spin.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="37_Station_5.4_Sectioned.html">Continue &middot; Station 5.4: Sectioned</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

