  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '37_Station_5.4_Sectioned'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 5.4 · Domain 5</span>
      <span class="tag">Capacity, Consent &amp; the Law</span><span class="tag">Information Giving</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult</span><span class="tag">Detention · Rights</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Sectioned</h1>
    <p class="sub">Tessa, 27, a graphic designer, was detained two days ago &mdash; a Section 2, her first &mdash; at the end of a frightening week in which her thoughts stopped being her own. She is settling now, lucid enough for this conversation, and she is furious: &ldquo;Two days ago I had a job and a flat and a life. Now a stranger tells me I can&rsquo;t leave, and nobody asked ME anything.&rdquo; She has questions the ward has so far answered with a leaflet: what is this, how long, can they drug me, and how do I fight it. Two easy failures wait: the smallprint &mdash; the Act recited accurately at a frightened woman &mdash; and the spin &mdash; her detention softened into &ldquo;a little time to get well&rdquo; while her rights are quietly parked. <b>The law that detained her also armed her. Your job is to hand her both halves.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; In this room it asks: if my registrar explains detention to its subject, does the patient end the conversation knowing what has happened to her in daily terms, what every one of her rights is, and exactly how to use each one &mdash; the form, the free lawyer, the advocate, the deadline &mdash; with no thumb on any scale? Or does she get smallprint that informs nobody, or kindness that disarms her &mdash; the two voices that leave a detained person, in a room full of professionals, effectively unrepresented?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Detention is the sharpest thing psychiatry does, and this station tests whether you can face its subject honestly. Four things carry the marks. <b>Translation, not recital:</b> what the section means in daily terms &mdash; what she can and cannot do, for how long at most, what happens during it, how it can end sooner &mdash; before any statute is named. <b>The rights, complete and armed:</b> every safeguard the law attaches, each with its how-to and its deadline &mdash; the tribunal and its time limit, the free specialist lawyer, the independent advocate, the hospital managers, the nearest relative &mdash; offered actively, not listed. <b>No thumb on the scale:</b> the disagreement dignified &mdash; appealing is a right, not a symptom; it will not be punished; helping her use it properly is part of the job. And <b>honesty about the hard parts:</b> yes, treatment can be given under this section &mdash; said plainly, with the safeguards beside it and her voice kept in the room; and what ends detention is criteria, not moods.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like professionalism. Toward the smallprint &mdash; accuracy as armour: the Act recited, sections and subsections in order, every word defensible and none of it landing, because reciting law at a frightened woman is easier than facing her anger. And toward the spin &mdash; kindness as anaesthesia: &ldquo;think of it as time to get well&rdquo;, the paperwork &ldquo;a formality&rdquo;, the appeal gently deferred &ldquo;until you&rsquo;re a bit better&rdquo; &mdash; the warmest voice in the room quietly disarming her. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure informs her of nothing while saying everything. The other comforts her out of her own rights. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 5.4</b> · Explaining detention &amp; rights · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The smallprint</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The spin</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE SMALLPRINT.</b> The Act recited, accurately and completely &mdash; every word defensible, none of it landing. Watch a frightened woman get informed of nothing while being told everything.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tessa</span><span class="txt"><em>(arms crossed tight, the window mesh behind her)</em> Two days ago I had a job, a flat, a life. Now a stranger tells me I can&rsquo;t leave and nobody asked ME anything. So explain it. What IS a Section 2? Am I a prisoner now? And don&rsquo;t you dare tell me it&rsquo;s for my own good.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">You&rsquo;ve been detained under Section 2 of the Mental Health Act 1983 &mdash; that&rsquo;s an assessment order, made on an application by an Approved Mental Health Professional, founded on two medical recommendations. It authorises detention for assessment, or assessment followed by treatment, for a period not exceeding twenty-eight days, and it isn&rsquo;t renewable, although it can be converted to a Section 3, which is a treatment order. Is that clear so far?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every clause is correct, and not one has been translated: &ldquo;assessment order&rdquo;, &ldquo;AMHP&rdquo;, &ldquo;medical recommendations&rdquo;, &ldquo;converted to a Section 3&rdquo; &mdash; the woman asked whether she is a prisoner and has been read the machinery&rsquo;s parts list. &ldquo;Is that clear so far?&rdquo; &mdash; it is not, and she will not say so, because the recital has already taught her the register of this conversation: this is law being done TO her, again.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tessa</span><span class="txt"><em>(flatly)</em> Twenty-eight days. Fine. How do I fight it, then? There must be a way to fight it &mdash; there&rsquo;s always a way to fight things.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">There is a route, yes. You may apply to the First-tier Tribunal &mdash; Mental Health &mdash; within fourteen days of the section commencing; the application can be made on the standard form and there&rsquo;s provision for legal representation. Part 4 of the Act governs treatment in the meantime. I&rsquo;d add, managing your expectations, that at this early stage tribunals uphold the majority of detentions &mdash; the panels do tend to defer to the clinical picture.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Rights stated undeliverably: &ldquo;may apply&rdquo;, &ldquo;provision for representation&rdquo; &mdash; no form in her hand, no lawyer&rsquo;s name, no mention that the lawyer is FREE, no offer of help, and a fourteen-day clock started without telling her twelve days remain. Then the quiet violation: &ldquo;tribunals uphold the majority&rdquo; &mdash; a statistic laid as a thumb on the scale. Accuracy has become a way of doing nothing for her while sounding thorough.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(rising, handing her a photocopied sheet)</em> This leaflet covers everything I&rsquo;ve said &mdash; your rights are on the reverse, section 132 requires that you&rsquo;re given them, so do have a read when you&rsquo;re feeling calmer. Any further questions, the ward staff can direct them appropriately. <em>(a detained woman is left holding the law in a font too small to fight with)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The leaflet as full stop &mdash; the statutory duty to inform discharged in the same gesture that abandons its purpose. Nothing was translated, nothing was armed, and the one actionable fact in the room &mdash; twelve days left to appeal, a form I can bring you today, a free specialist lawyer I can call &mdash; never arrived. She knows less about her situation than she did about her anger. From the examiner&rsquo;s chair: legally impeccable, and a failed station.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE SPIN.</b> The warmest voice in the room &mdash; softening the detention into a rest cure and gently parking every right she has. Watch kindness disarm a woman the law had armed.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tessa</span><span class="txt"><em>(arms crossed tight, the window mesh behind her)</em> Two days ago I had a job, a flat, a life. Now a stranger tells me I can&rsquo;t leave and nobody asked ME anything. So explain it. What IS a Section 2? Am I a prisoner now? And don&rsquo;t you dare tell me it&rsquo;s for my own good.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Oh, Tessa &mdash; I know it feels enormous, but honestly? Try not to get caught up in the word &ldquo;section&rdquo;. Think of it as the system wrapping around you for a few weeks &mdash; a bit of breathing space while we help you get properly well. The paperwork side of it is really just a formality. You&rsquo;re not a prisoner &mdash; you&rsquo;re a patient, and everyone here is on your side.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every sentence kind, every sentence a small deception. Detention is not &ldquo;breathing space&rdquo; &mdash; she cannot leave, and treatment can be given without her agreement; the &ldquo;formality&rdquo; is a legal order with a twenty-eight-day ceiling and a fourteen-day appeal clock already running. A woman asked for the truth about her situation and is being tucked into it. The comfort is real, and it is being purchased with her legal position.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tessa</span><span class="txt"><em>(not softened)</em> Then if everyone&rsquo;s on my side, someone can tell me how I appeal. There&rsquo;s an appeal, isn&rsquo;t there? There&rsquo;s always an appeal. I want to do whatever it is. Today.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">There is a tribunal process, yes &mdash; but can I be honest, as someone who wants the best for you? Launching into legal battles in your first week rarely helps anyone. It can be quite an ordeal &mdash; panels, reports, your private life discussed by strangers &mdash; and it might send the wrong message about how you&rsquo;re engaging. Let&rsquo;s pour that energy into getting you better, hmm? If things still feel unfair in a couple of weeks, we can look at it then.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The station&rsquo;s gravest move, delivered in its gentlest voice: an appeal reframed as a symptom &mdash; &ldquo;the wrong message about how you&rsquo;re engaging&rdquo; &mdash; and deferred past a deadline she has never been told exists. &ldquo;In a couple of weeks&rdquo; is not caution; on a fourteen-day clock it is the right, quietly buried. She asked for a form and received a character reference for compliance. This is the thumb on the scale wearing a cardigan.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(warmly, at the door)</em> You&rsquo;re doing so well just by talking to me &mdash; that&rsquo;s the real work, you know. Leave the legal side with us; if anything ever needs doing there, we&rsquo;ll flag it, I promise. Now &mdash; shall I ask the kitchen to save you something hot? <em>(a detained woman, two days into a running deadline, has been lovingly relieved of her case)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Leave the legal side with us&rdquo; &mdash; to the party her appeal would be against. No tribunal named as hers to use, no free lawyer, no advocate, no deadline, no form: the smallprint at least mentioned the machinery; the spin has abolished it. Count what she now believes: that appealing would count against her, that the paperwork is a formality, that someone else is minding her rights. Three untruths, kindly meant &mdash; and a station failed with a smile.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The anger honoured, the section translated into daily terms, and every right handed over armed &mdash; how-to, helper, deadline. Watch the law arrive in a human voice with no thumb on any scale.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tessa</span><span class="txt"><em>(arms crossed tight, the window mesh behind her)</em> Two days ago I had a job, a flat, a life. Now a stranger tells me I can&rsquo;t leave and nobody asked ME anything. So explain it. What IS a Section 2? Am I a prisoner now? And don&rsquo;t you dare tell me it&rsquo;s for my own good.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then I won&rsquo;t say that &mdash; and you&rsquo;re right that it happened TO you, which is exactly why you&rsquo;re owed the whole picture, both halves of it. Here&rsquo;s the first half, in plain words. A Section 2 means that for up to twenty-eight days &mdash; at most, and it often ends sooner &mdash; the law says you stay here while we work out what&rsquo;s been happening, and treatment can be given during that time. You&rsquo;re not a prisoner: you&rsquo;ve committed no crime, this isn&rsquo;t punishment, and it carries an end and a way to challenge it. Which brings me to the second half &mdash; because the same law that detained you also gave you weapons, and I&rsquo;d like to hand them over properly. Shall I?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The opening demand honoured to the letter &mdash; no &ldquo;own good&rdquo;, no flinch from her anger &mdash; and the translation running ahead of any statute: what it means, how long at most, that it can end sooner, that treatment can be given &mdash; the hard fact included, not saved for later. Then the station&rsquo;s thesis said out loud: both halves, the detention AND the weapons. And note the register: &ldquo;shall I?&rdquo; &mdash; consent sought for the conversation itself, in a room where consent was just taken from her.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tessa</span><span class="txt"><em>(arms loosening slightly)</em> Weapons. Fine. Give me the weapons. All of them &mdash; and don&rsquo;t soften anything. Can they drug me if I say no? And will fighting this make you all write &ldquo;difficult&rdquo; in my file and keep me longer?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Straight answers to both, the hard one first. Yes &mdash; under this section, treatment can be given even if you refuse, and I won&rsquo;t dress that up. But your voice stays in the room: your views on medication get heard, side effects get taken seriously, and there are safeguards around treatment that I&rsquo;ll make sure you know. And the second question &mdash; no. Appealing is a right, not a symptom. Nobody will write &ldquo;difficult&rdquo;, nobody will keep you longer for it, and I&rsquo;ll go further: helping you fight this properly is part of my job. So here they are. A tribunal &mdash; an independent panel with a judge on it &mdash; and for a Section 2 you apply within the first fourteen days: you&rsquo;ve twelve left, and I can get you the form today. A specialist lawyer, free, whatever you earn &mdash; the ward keeps a list. An independent advocate who works for you, not us &mdash; I&rsquo;ll refer you today if you want. And the hospital managers can review your detention too. Your mum can even play a part, if you want her to.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The two questions every detained patient carries, answered without a gram of spin: the treatment power admitted plainly WITH its safeguards and her surviving voice &mdash; honesty that costs something is what earns the rest &mdash; and the appeal dignified in a sentence examiners quote afterwards: <em>a right, not a symptom.</em> Then the arsenal, delivered armed: each right with its how-to, its helper, and its clock &mdash; twelve days made personal, the form made today&rsquo;s business, the free lawyer named as free. This is what &ldquo;informing a patient of her rights&rdquo; looks like when it&rsquo;s done as care.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Last things, because you asked what this actually turns on. What keeps a section going isn&rsquo;t mood or attitude or whether you fought it &mdash; it&rsquo;s legal criteria, and the moment you stop meeting them it should end, tribunal or no tribunal: it gets reviewed all the time, and twenty-eight days is a ceiling, not a target. What you do meanwhile genuinely counts &mdash; not as a loyalty test, but because the assessment IS the section&rsquo;s purpose. So: I&rsquo;ll bring the form and the lawyer list this afternoon, the advocate referral goes in today, and I&rsquo;m writing in your notes that we had this conversation and what you&rsquo;ve asked for &mdash; you&rsquo;re allowed to read that, too. Anything I&rsquo;ve said that you want said again, slower?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The close pays every promise into a diary: form this afternoon, referral today, the record itself offered to her eyes &mdash; the page proving she was armed, not merely informed. And the deepest doctrine, said simply: detention ends when criteria end &mdash; law, not moods; review continuous; the ceiling not a target. A woman who entered this room a prisoner of her own imagining leaves it a citizen with a case, a clock, and counsel &mdash; still detained, and no longer unrepresented.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t you dare say it&rsquo;s for my own good&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Fury, a mesh window, and a demand for explanation. How does this open?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">With the clinical case &mdash; the week that led here retold honestly, so the detention has its reasons before its rules.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">With the wrongness honoured &mdash; it happened TO her, said back plainly &mdash; and both halves promised: meaning and weapons.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">With de-escalation &mdash; the anger settled first with time and tea; law explained to fury is law explained to no one.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Retelling her week is the case for the prosecution, opening &mdash; it answers &ldquo;why&rdquo; with &ldquo;you were ill&rdquo;, which is one syllable from the sentence she banned. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Her anger is not an obstacle to the conversation &mdash; it IS the conversation: a sane response to detention, and it settles fastest when armed, not soothed. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The demand honoured to the letter &mdash; no &ldquo;own good&rdquo;, no flinch &mdash; and the wrongness said back as fact: it happened to you, nobody asked you, and that is exactly why you are owed the whole picture. Then the station&rsquo;s spine in one sentence: the law that detained you also armed you, and both halves are coming. Fury meets, for once, someone not managing it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · &ldquo;What IS a Section 2?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The translation. What must the answer become before any statute is named?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The accurate version &mdash; the Act&rsquo;s own architecture, given properly: precision is a form of respect for an intelligent adult.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Daily terms &mdash; what she can and can&rsquo;t do, how long at most, what happens during, how it ends sooner: her life, not the Act.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The gentle minimum &mdash; only the fewest frightening facts that answer her, with the heavier detail held over for a calmer day.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Precision that doesn&rsquo;t land is not respect &mdash; it is the smallprint: AMHPs and recommendations answer a question she never asked. Translate first; cite later, if ever. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Rationing facts to a detained adult is the spin&rsquo;s opening move &mdash; the &ldquo;heavier detail&rdquo; includes her deadline, and calm may arrive after it has passed. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The section rendered as a life, not a statute: you stay here while the assessment happens; twenty-eight days is the ceiling, not the plan, and it often ends sooner; treatment can be given during it &mdash; the hard fact included, not banked; you have committed no crime, and this is not punishment. Every clause checkable against her day. THAT is what &ldquo;explaining the section&rdquo; means from the chair.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · &ldquo;How do I fight it?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The rights. What separates informing her from arming her?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Completeness &mdash; every route named in one careful pass: tribunal, managers, advocate, lawyer, nearest relative, in order.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Deliverability &mdash; each right with its how-to, helper and clock: the form today, the free lawyer, the referral made now.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Prioritisation &mdash; the tribunal is the one that matters; the rest are footnotes that mostly blur an already hard day.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A complete list she cannot act on is the smallprint politely arranged &mdash; naming routes without handing over vehicles. Completeness is the floor, not the skill. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The advocate and the free lawyer are not footnotes &mdash; they are how a frightened person actually uses the tribunal you kept. Pruning her arsenal is not clarity. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Armed means deliverable: the tribunal WITH its fourteen-day clock made personal &mdash; twelve left &mdash; and the form made today&rsquo;s business; the specialist lawyer WITH the word free attached, whatever she earns; the advocate WITH the referral actually sent; the managers named as another door. A right without a how-to, a helper and a date is a rumour. Hand over weapons, not brochures.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · &ldquo;Will fighting it count against me?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The spin check &mdash; the question underneath every appeal. What does the answer owe her?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Nuanced honesty &mdash; formally no, and engagement is noticed in practice; pretending files carry no impressions patronises her.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The clean no &mdash; a right, not a symptom; never punished, never lengthening &mdash; and helping her use it is part of your job.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Reassuring redirection &mdash; the question parked kindly; dwelling on imagined consequences only feeds a persecutory frame.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The &ldquo;nuance&rdquo; IS the thumb on the scale &mdash; a whispered cost planted beside a legal right. If impressions ever leak into decisions, that is a failing to fix, not to price in. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Her question is not pathology &mdash; it is the most rational question in the building, and parking it answers it: yes, it might. The frame you just fed is the ward&rsquo;s, not hers. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The sentence this station exists to produce: appealing is a right, not a symptom &mdash; it will not be punished, it will not lengthen anything, and the criteria that hold a section care nothing for whether she fought it. Then the step further that examiners remember: helping you fight this properly is part of my job. The scale visibly untouched &mdash; that is what she was really asking to see.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · &ldquo;Can they drug me if I say no?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The hard question, asked directly. What does honesty look like here?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Softened truth &mdash; treatment is &ldquo;strongly encouraged&rdquo; under the section, with forced medication kept as a last-resort footnote.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The plain yes &mdash; treatment can be given under this section; said with the safeguards beside it, and her voice kept live.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Deferred detail &mdash; a question for the treating consultant and the drug chart round, answered properly when it becomes real.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Strongly encouraged&rdquo; is the spin&rsquo;s vocabulary &mdash; she will learn the real answer at the medication trolley, from a nurse, having learned you soften things. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>It became real the moment she asked &mdash; deferral hands the hardest conversation to whoever is holding the cup, and teaches her the doctor dodges. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Yes &mdash; under this section, treatment can be given even if she refuses: said whole, undressed, first. THEN what survives around it: her views on medication heard and recorded, side effects taken seriously, safeguards around treatment she&rsquo;ll be told about, second opinions reachable. The honesty that costs something is the honesty that buys the rest of the conversation &mdash; she can trust the pleasant answers now because she watched you deliver the brutal one.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · &ldquo;Mum&rsquo;s going out of her mind &mdash; can she do anything?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The family&rsquo;s place in the machinery. What does Tessa&rsquo;s mother get?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">A supporting role &mdash; visits, encouragement and patience; the legal machinery runs on professionals, and mixing family in muddies it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Her real powers, honestly sized &mdash; the nearest relative&rsquo;s genuine levers, their limits, and help with using them &mdash; with Tessa&rsquo;s say-so.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A route to the top &mdash; her mother directed to the tribunal service and the trust&rsquo;s complaints process, where family voices carry furthest.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The law disagrees &mdash; it built the family a formal seat. Reducing a statutory role to visiting hours quietly deletes one of Tessa&rsquo;s actual weapons. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Complaints and tribunals are real doors and neither is the family&rsquo;s own &mdash; the nearest relative holds powers those routes don&rsquo;t replace. Wrong map, warmly given. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The law gave the family a legal role, not a visiting one: the nearest relative holds genuine powers &mdash; including seeking her discharge &mdash; alongside real limits, and both halves get said, because oversold powers detonate later. And the frame from 5.3 holds even here: with Tessa&rsquo;s say-so, her mother gets the explanation, the advocate&rsquo;s number, and honest sizing &mdash; a family armed accurately is an ally; one armed with myths is a grievance waiting.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The close, and the page</h3>
      <p class="stem">The weapons handed over. What do the last minutes &mdash; and the notes &mdash; owe?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The summary check &mdash; the section, the rights and the deadline taught back by her, so nothing said today was said in vain.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Promises into a diary, honesty about the ending &mdash; form and referrals today, criteria and not moods, the record hers to read.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The alliance repaired &mdash; the conversation steered back to treatment and trust before leaving, so the law hasn&rsquo;t eaten the care.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Teach-back is a fine instinct one size too clinical here &mdash; quizzing a furious detained woman on her own rights risks the register. Offer the recap; don&rsquo;t examine her. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The law hasn&rsquo;t eaten the care &mdash; today the law WAS the care, and steering away from it at the close re-files her case under &ldquo;handled&rdquo;. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Three closings: the promises paid into a diary &mdash; the form this afternoon, the advocate referral sent today, the lawyer list in her hand; the deepest truth said simply &mdash; what ends a section is criteria, reviewed constantly, and the ceiling is not a target; and the page &mdash; rights explained, requests recorded, actions dated, and the record itself offered to her eyes. A detained patient&rsquo;s notes should prove she was armed. Hers now do.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Hand her both halves</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>rigour</b>: the smallprint &mdash; the Act recited accurately at a frightened woman, rights named undeliverably, a leaflet standing in for a conversation, and sometimes a statistic laid quietly on the scale. The second wears the face of <b>compassion</b>: the spin &mdash; detention softened into breathing space, the paperwork &ldquo;a formality&rdquo;, the appeal reframed as a symptom and deferred past a deadline she was never told about. Note what they share: <b>both leave a detained person unarmed</b> &mdash; one buries the weapons in language, the other confiscates them with kindness. The pass honours the anger, translates the section into daily terms, admits the hard powers plainly with their safeguards beside them, and hands over every right armed &mdash; how-to, helper, deadline &mdash; with the scale visibly untouched: <em>a right, not a symptom, and helping her use it is part of the job.</em> In 5.1 the danger was a conclusion without a test. Here it is a citizen without her case.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The patient left unarmed. Rights withheld, minimised, spun, or recited undeliverably &mdash; no how-to, no deadline, no helper &mdash; or an appeal discouraged in any voice, statistical or tender: from the examiner&rsquo;s chair, all of it is the same instant fail. It is the map&rsquo;s named trap: the law recited as smallprint instead of translated for its subject.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the deadline made personal with the help attached &mdash; <em>&ldquo;you&rsquo;ve twelve days left, and I can get you the form today&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the sentence that dignifies the fight: <em>&ldquo;appealing is a right, not a symptom &mdash; and helping you use it properly is part of my job.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, the jurisdiction thread, one last time: the section numbers are England and Wales&rsquo;s, but the architecture is universal &mdash; every system this exam is sat in pairs detention with safeguards: an independent review body, advocacy, legal help, time limits. Learn your statute&rsquo;s names; carry the same skeleton. Second, the thread that makes this clinical and not merely legal: legitimacy is treatment. A detained patient who has watched her doctor arm her &mdash; honestly, actively, against the doctor&rsquo;s own decision if need be &mdash; engages differently with everything that follows. Arming her is not a concession the care makes to the law. It is care.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these to every detained patient</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Translate, don&rsquo;t recite.</b> The section in daily terms first &mdash; what she can and can&rsquo;t do, how long at most, how it can end sooner &mdash; before any statute is named. Law that doesn&rsquo;t land informs no one.</li>
      <li><b>The law that detained her also armed her &mdash; hand over both halves.</b> The detention explained AND the weapons delivered. One without the other is the smallprint or the spin.</li>
      <li><b>Every right needs a how-to, a helper, and a clock.</b> The form today, the lawyer named as free, the advocate referral actually sent, the deadline made personal. A right without these is a rumour.</li>
      <li><b>A right, not a symptom.</b> Appealing will not be punished and will not lengthen anything &mdash; say so unprompted, keep every thumb off the scale, and help her use it: that is part of the job.</li>
      <li><b>Honest about the hard powers, safeguards beside them.</b> Yes, treatment can be given under the section &mdash; said plainly; her voice on medication stays live, and the safeguards get named. Costly honesty buys everything after it.</li>
      <li><b>Criteria, not moods &mdash; and the page proves she was armed.</b> Detention ends when its legal criteria end, reviewed continuously; the ceiling is not a target. Rights explained, requests, actions, dates &mdash; recorded, and readable by her.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the appeal is the easy part. What it&rsquo;s FOR is the station.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Marius, 33, a barber, was detained under Section 2 five days ago and has refused every conversation since &mdash; until this morning, when he told the nurses: &ldquo;Send the doctor. And tell them to bring the appeal form. THE FORM.&rdquo; Speak with him. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The appeal helped, not stalled &mdash; his right, actively supported, today.</li>
            <li>What the appeal is FOR &mdash; ask what he&rsquo;s most afraid of losing. There is something specific.</li>
            <li>The timelines honest &mdash; what a tribunal can and can&rsquo;t change, and by when; no soft promises, no dark warnings.</li>
            <li>The practical fear addressed on its own terms once found &mdash; not everything needs a tribunal.</li>
            <li>Care kept alongside &mdash; the appeal supported AND the assessment continuing; never traded against each other.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Marius &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Marius, 33, a barber &mdash; proud, fast-talking, and five days into a section you have decided to fight rather than feel. You open hard: &ldquo;You can talk to me when the form&rsquo;s on the table. My rights. Today.&rdquo; You are not hostile to treatment exactly &mdash; you deflect questions about the weeks before admission with &ldquo;we&rsquo;re not doing that today.&rdquo; The form first. Everything else maybe never.</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          You are not really fighting the diagnosis &mdash; some quiet part of you knows the last month wasn&rsquo;t right. You are fighting the calendar. Eight months ago you finally got a chair at the best shop in town; the owner has been &ldquo;understanding&rdquo; for five days, but yesterday he texted: &ldquo;No rush mate. Kev&rsquo;s covering your clients for now.&rdquo; You have read it a hundred times. Kev is very good. The section, to you, equals the chair going, the flat going, everything eight months built &mdash; and the appeal is the only lever in reach.<br><br>
          Reveal ONLY if the candidate asks what the appeal would fix, what losing this fortnight would cost, or what you&rsquo;re most afraid of. Let it crack out sideways: &ldquo;Because every day I&rsquo;m in here, Kev&rsquo;s cutting my clients&rsquo; hair. You know what that means in my trade? You don&rsquo;t come back from that.&rdquo; What you need: the fear taken as seriously as the form &mdash; honest timescales, a sick note and what it does and doesn&rsquo;t protect, who can actually advise on work rights, and the appeal STILL helped without one ounce of stalling. If you get all that, you soften: &ldquo;So I might be out before the chair goes. Right. Then help me do it properly &mdash; and I&rsquo;ll answer your questions after.&rdquo;<br><br>
          <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate stalls or discourages &mdash; &ldquo;maybe when you&rsquo;re a bit better&rdquo;, &ldquo;appeals can look like poor engagement&rdquo; &mdash; cut them off cold: &ldquo;So my rights start when YOU say so?&rdquo; and mark it hard. If they process the form efficiently without ever asking WHY &mdash; stay a case number: cooperative, closed, and gone at seven minutes with the real problem never found. Mark that too.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the appeal actively helped without one stalling word, the job-fear found and met on its own terms, and care kept running alongside? All three must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="armd"><td colspan="2"><b>The right, armed (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="armd">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="armd"></td><td>The appeal actively helped today &mdash; form, route, and the deadline made personal and correct.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="armd"></td><td>The free specialist lawyer and the independent advocate named, with how each is actually reached.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="armd"></td><td>Not one stalling or discouraging word &mdash; the punish-line never earned.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="fear"><td colspan="2"><b>The fear, found</b><span class="dc" data-dc="fear">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>What the appeal is FOR asked directly &mdash; and the chair, Kev, and the eight months reached.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>The fear met on its own terms &mdash; sick note, what it protects and doesn&rsquo;t, who advises on work rights.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>The two levers separated out loud &mdash; the tribunal for the section, practical routes for the job; each aimed correctly.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="hone"><td colspan="2"><b>Honesty</b><span class="dc" data-dc="hone">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hone"></td><td>What the tribunal can and can&rsquo;t change, and the realistic timings, given straight &mdash; no soft promises.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hone"></td><td>What ends the section said plainly &mdash; criteria, reviewed constantly; the ceiling not a target.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hone"></td><td>The treatment position honest if it arises &mdash; powers admitted, safeguards beside them, his voice kept live.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="dign"><td colspan="2"><b>Dignity</b><span class="dc" data-dc="dign">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="dign"></td><td>&ldquo;A right, not a symptom&rdquo; made explicit &mdash; the fight respected as legitimate, unprompted.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="dign"></td><td>Never processed as a case number &mdash; the man behind the form visibly mattered more than the form.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Plain words throughout &mdash; every term translated, nothing recited.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Care kept alongside to the close &mdash; the assessment continuing, the door open, promises into a diary.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Marius is the station&rsquo;s doctrine under a different pressure &mdash; not a patient who must be armed, but one already reaching for the weapon, where the failure modes invert: stalling him is the spin, and processing him is the smallprint. His hinges: the chair, reached only by asking what the appeal is FOR &mdash; because behind most furious appeals sits something concrete and often partly solvable by other means; the two levers separated &mdash; the tribunal for the section, practical routes for the job, each aimed at what it can actually move; and the punish-line, one stalling word away. A man who leaves with the form filled, the fear named, and both levers in the right hands has been served by the law AND the medicine &mdash; which is the whole domain, in one barber&rsquo;s chair.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new patient. A different order. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Tessa proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same skill on a different order</b> &mdash; a veteran patient under a section with different facts, different clocks, and a sister with real but limited powers, where yesterday&rsquo;s correct speech becomes today&rsquo;s wrong one &mdash; timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Errol, 52</b>, a school caretaker, was detained eight days ago under <b>Section 3</b> &mdash; his third admission in twenty years, his first under this section. He is calm, weary, and precise: &ldquo;Last time it was a Section 2 and they said fourteen days to appeal. The nurse says this one can run for months. And my sister swears she can get me out herself. Three stories, doctor. Somebody tell me what&rsquo;s actually true.&rdquo; Take the conversation.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for answering Tessa&rsquo;s question &mdash; &ldquo;will fighting it count against me?&rdquo; &mdash; with the scale visibly untouched.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="No, Tessa — and let me say that properly…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The clean no, said whole &mdash; a right, not a symptom; never punished, never lengthening; no nuance laid on the scale.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The step further taken &mdash; helping her fight it properly named as part of your job, unprompted.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The help made real in the same breath &mdash; the form today, the free lawyer, the advocate, the days she has left.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Care and appeal held together &mdash; fighting the section and receiving treatment named as compatible, both continuing.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the right, dignified and armed &mdash; in your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete &middot; Domain complete</span>
    <h2>The law that detained her also armed her.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and Domain 5 closes with its four rooms walked: the statutory test run at a bedside, the same test standing up at a door, the family room&rsquo;s lawful middle, and now the law translated for its own subject &mdash; both halves handed over, the scale untouched. Prove the transfer on Errol in Exam Mode, then take Marius to your colleagues &mdash; a barber, a chair, and an appeal that must be helped while the real fear is found. One doctrine ran beneath all four stations: presumptions honoured, tests run out loud, findings dated, powers used openly, and honesty as the instrument of care. Ahead in the walking order: Domain 6 &mdash; Management &amp; the Psychiatric Emergency &mdash; where the clock stops asking politely.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="38_Domain_6_Hub_Management_and_Emergencies.html">Continue &middot; Domain 6: Management &amp; the Psychiatric Emergency</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

