  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '38_Domain_6_Hub_Management_and_Emergencies'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Domain 6 of 8</span>
      <span class="tag">Management &amp; the Psychiatric Emergency</span>
      <span class="tag">7 constructs · the urgent domain</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Management &amp; the Psychiatric Emergency</h1>
    <p class="sub">This is the domain where the clock joins the consultation &mdash; and where a specific failure hides in plain sight: candidates who conduct <b style="color:var(--lgold)">a clinic consultation in a burning room</b>. The history is thorough, the empathy lands, the structure is beautiful &mdash; while the potassium climbs, the lithium level waits unchecked, the baby sleeps two doors from a psychotic mother, or a frightened man&rsquo;s agitation is answered with a syringe before a single sentence has been tried. This domain trains the four verbs the marks are built on &mdash; <b>prioritising</b> what is dangerous tonight, <b>de-escalating</b> with words before drugs, <b>recognising</b> the medical emergency wearing psychiatric clothes, and <b>handing over</b> so the person on the other end of the phone can act. An emergency is not a faster clinic appointment. It is a different consultation.</p></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE LENS, APPLIED TO THIS DOMAIN</b>
      &ldquo;Would I trust this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; &mdash; In this domain the question sharpens into the 3am test: if this doctor takes the emergency call alone, do they find what is dangerous tonight and deal with that first? Do words come before medication, and the cause before the label? Would they spot the toxic, the delirious, the medically collapsing patient inside the &ldquo;psychiatric&rdquo; referral? And when they ring upward, does the consultant hear risk and the ask in the first breath &mdash; or a history in chronological order while the clock runs? Every station here is really asking that.</div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ============ 1 WHY FIRST ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this domain matters</span>
    <h2>The founding failure: the clinic consultation in a burning room</h2>
    <p class="intro">Emergencies arrive at every psychiatrist&rsquo;s door wearing ordinary referrals, and the exam tests whether you change gear when they do. The discipline is <b>prioritisation</b> &mdash; not &ldquo;what is wrong&rdquo; but &ldquo;what is dangerous tonight&rdquo;, answered first and acted on first. It is <b>words before drugs</b> &mdash; agitation is a symptom with a cause, and de-escalation is a clinical skill with technique, not a corridor courtesy before the syringe; when medication is genuinely needed, it is offered with choice, given stepwise, and followed by monitoring, never abandonment. It is <b>the hunt for the cause</b> &mdash; delirium, toxicity, hypoglycaemia, akathisia, a silent overdose: the medical emergency in psychiatric clothing kills more patients than violence ever will, and it is found by looking, not labelling. And it is <b>the handover</b> &mdash; the emergency isn&rsquo;t managed until the story has moved: risk first, the ask explicit, the details in service of the decision. The failure that unites every failed station here is the same: <b>the pace of the clinic brought to the scene of the fire &mdash; or the syringe brought before the sentence.</b></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THE CONSTRUCTS ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The constructs</span>
    <h2>Seven things this domain will build into reflex</h2>
    <div class="constructs">
      <div class="con"><span class="n">1</span><span><b>Prioritisation: what is dangerous tonight</b> &mdash; every emergency consultation opens with triage, not history: what can kill or maim before morning, named first, acted on first. The elegant assessment of the wrong problem is this domain&rsquo;s signature fail.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">2</span><span><b>De-escalation before medication</b> &mdash; space, calm, names, listening, the reason asked for: a technique with steps, not a mood. Most agitation settles when someone stops managing the room and starts talking to the person in it.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">3</span><span><b>The rapid tranquillisation conversation, done safely</b> &mdash; when words are genuinely not enough: oral offered before injection, choice preserved to the last possible moment, the stepwise ladder respected &mdash; and after any medication, monitoring: the patient watched, never abandoned.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">4</span><span><b>Delirium: recognise, investigate, manage</b> &mdash; the fluctuating, inattentive patient is a medical emergency until proven otherwise. The cognitive test is not the assessment; the hunt for the cause is.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">5</span><span><b>The toxidromes: recognise and act</b> &mdash; serotonin syndrome, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, lithium toxicity: rare, deadly, and disguised as psychiatry. The reflex is a threshold: the moment the pattern is possible, the pace changes and the medics are in the room.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">6</span><span><b>The emergency with two patients</b> &mdash; a mother and a baby, a patient and the person beside them: risk assessed for both, safety arranged for both, and neither forgotten while the other is examined. Divided attention, held deliberately.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">7</span><span><b>The handover: risk first, then the ask</b> &mdash; what the on-call consultant needs to hear, in the order they need it: who, how sick, what you&rsquo;re worried about, what you want. A perfect assessment that cannot be handed over dies in the corridor.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 STATIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The stations in this domain</span>
    <h2>Walk them in order</h2>
    <div class="stn">
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">6.1</span>
        <span class="t"><b>The Agitated Patient in A&amp;E</b><span>A big man pacing a small cubicle, security hovering, and a consultant asking you to &ldquo;give him something.&rdquo; The domain&rsquo;s grammar: words before drugs, the cause before the label, and choice held to the last moment.</span></span>
        <span class="chip go">Begin here</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">6.2</span>
        <span class="t"><b>The Lithium Toxicity Call</b><span>A routine-sounding phone call that isn&rsquo;t: tremor, vomiting, confusion &mdash; and a level nobody has checked. The medical emergency in psychiatric clothing, recognised in minutes and acted on <em>now</em>.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 2</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">6.3</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Postpartum Psychosis</b><span>A new mother, days from delivery, and thoughts that are not her own. The emergency with two patients: her illness assessed, the baby&rsquo;s immediate safety never out of frame. The domain&rsquo;s flagship.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 3</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">6.4</span>
        <span class="t"><b>The Overdose Handover</b><span>Everything you&rsquo;ve assessed, moved upward in ninety seconds: risk first, the ask explicit, details in service of the decision. The station where good assessments go to die &mdash; unless you&rsquo;ve learned to tell them.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 4</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 ENTRY DECISION ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Before you enter</span>
    <h2>One decision at the door</h2>
    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>Where does an emergency begin?</h3>
      <p class="stem">The A&amp;E consultant, on the phone: &ldquo;Agitated man in cubicle nine, shouting, won&rsquo;t engage &mdash; can you prescribe something quick before someone gets hurt?&rdquo; What is your first move?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Prescribe on the description &mdash; a standard oral dose charted now; safety cannot wait for a psychiatrist to cross the hospital.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Go now &mdash; agitation is a symptom, not an order form: the room, the words, and the cause come before any drug.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Decline for now &mdash; no meaningful assessment is possible while he&rsquo;s like this; ask them to call back when he can engage.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Prescribing for a patient you haven&rsquo;t seen, on a description shouted down a phone &mdash; the syringe before the sentence, and before the glucose, the pain, the fear, or the psychosis that might be underneath. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Call back when he can engage&rdquo; declines the emergency itself &mdash; the agitated patient IS the referral, and de-escalation is the assessment&rsquo;s first act, not its prerequisite. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The domain in one move: you go, because the emergency is where you are needed; you treat agitation as a symptom with a cause &mdash; fear, pain, psychosis, intoxication, a sugar of 2.1 &mdash; and you reach for the room and the words before the chart. Medication may yet be needed; it will be offered, not fired. Station 6.1 is exactly this cubicle.</template>
      </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Domain open</span>
    <h2>First the room. Then the cause. Then the call.</h2>
    <p>Seven constructs, four rooms, one clock &mdash; and one lens over all of it: could you be sent to this emergency alone? Begin with the station the domain&rsquo;s grammar lives in: a big man pacing a small cubicle, and a hospital that wants him quiet.</p>
    <a class="btn mark" href="39_Station_6.1_The_Agitated_Patient.html">Begin Station 6.1 &middot; The Agitated Patient in A&amp;E</a>
  </div>
</section>

