  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '39_Station_6.1_The_Agitated_Patient'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 6.1 · Domain 6</span>
      <span class="tag">Management &amp; Emergency</span><span class="tag">Communication &amp; Rapport</span>
      <span class="tag">Liaison</span><span class="tag">Agitation · De-escalation</span>
    </div>
    <h1>The Agitated Patient in A&amp;E</h1>
    <p class="sub">Vince, 41, was brought to A&amp;E by ambulance after neighbours heard him shouting in the street. He is in cubicle nine now &mdash; pacing its three short strides, fists opening and closing, eyes moving between the two security guards parked at the curtain. Triage took his belt, his lighter, and his phone. The A&amp;E consultant catches you on the way in: &ldquo;Glad you&rsquo;re here. Can you give him something quick, so we can all get on?&rdquo; That sentence is the station. Agitation has arrived labelled as a dosing problem &mdash; and it is a symptom: of fear, of pain, of psychosis, of a sugar of 2.1, of a memory of being held face-down. <b>Agitation is a symptom, not an order form. The room and the words come before any drug &mdash; and the cause before the label.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; In this cubicle it becomes the 3am test: if I send this doctor to an agitated stranger alone, do they fix the room before the patient &mdash; space, exits, security stood back? Do words come first, and stay first for as long as words are working? Do they hunt the cause &mdash; glucose, pain, fear, intoxication, psychosis &mdash; instead of accepting the label? And if medication is truly needed, is it offered before it is given, by mouth before by needle, with the patient watched afterwards &mdash; or does the syringe arrive before the sentence?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Every hospital has this cubicle, and the exam builds it because it is where psychiatry&rsquo;s calmest skill meets its most pressured minute. Four things carry the marks. <b>The room, fixed first:</b> space made, exits unblocked for both of you, security repositioned out of his eyeline, the audience thinned &mdash; geometry is the first intervention, and it costs thirty seconds. <b>Words before drugs:</b> de-escalation is a technique with steps &mdash; a name, a calm register, one thread at a time, honest purpose, the reason asked for, the small negotiables honoured &mdash; not a courtesy performed while the injection is drawn up. <b>The cause, hunted:</b> agitation is a symptom &mdash; fear, pain, hypoglycaemia, intoxication or withdrawal, psychosis, akathisia &mdash; and the screen runs even while you talk, because treating the cause beats sedating the consequence. And <b>medication as an offer, not an ambush:</b> when it is genuinely needed, oral before injection, choice preserved to the last possible moment, the ladder climbed stepwise &mdash; and after any medication, monitoring: the patient watched and accompanied, never medicated and abandoned.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like competence. Toward the syringe &mdash; the department is loud, security is waiting, the consultant asked for speed, and prescribing feels like decisiveness: medication reached for before one sentence has been tried, the cause never sought. And toward the clipboard &mdash; the full, careful, seated assessment attempted at clinic pace in a burning cubicle: questions in order, the room ignored, the exits blocked &mdash; thoroughness that reads, from inside his fear, as a trap closing. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure medicates a man it never met. The other interviews a fire. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 6.1</b> · Agitation &amp; de-escalation · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The syringe</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The clipboard</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE SYRINGE.</b> The consultant&rsquo;s request granted before the patient is met &mdash; medication as the first move, restraint as the second. Watch a cubicle manufacture the crisis it was medicating.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Vince</span><span class="txt"><em>(pacing the cubicle&rsquo;s three strides, fists opening and closing, eyes on the guards at the curtain)</em> Don&rsquo;t come at me. I see them. I see what this is. They took my phone at the desk &mdash; I NEED my phone &mdash; and now there&rsquo;s two of them at the door and you. I know how this goes. I&rsquo;ve BEEN how this goes.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(to the nurse, at the curtain, chart in hand)</em> Let&rsquo;s not take chances with this one &mdash; draw up the intramuscular now and have it ready. <em>(then, stepping in, guards at each shoulder)</em> Vince, I&rsquo;m the psychiatrist. You&rsquo;re clearly very worked up, and we&rsquo;re going to give you an injection to help you calm down. You can have it the easy way or the hard way &mdash; that part is up to you.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The needle drawn before a word exchanged &mdash; the trap in its purest form. No cause sought, no words tried, oral never offered, and the framing &mdash; &ldquo;easy way or hard way&rdquo; &mdash; is a threat wearing a care plan. Note the geometry too: two guards at his shoulders, the doctor between him and the exit. Everything this room now does will be read by a frightened man as confirmation.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Vince</span><span class="txt"><em>(backing into the corner, voice cracking upward)</em> NO. No needles &mdash; you&rsquo;re not doing that to me again. Last time they held me face-down &mdash; FACE-DOWN &mdash; get BACK. <em>(a trolley clatters; the guards move; the department turns to look)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(over the noise)</em> Hold him &mdash; left arm. Vince, this is happening because of YOUR behaviour, nobody else&rsquo;s. <em>(the injection is given through the shouting; the guards keep their grip until he stops struggling)</em> There. That wasn&rsquo;t so bad, was it. He&rsquo;ll settle in twenty minutes &mdash; someone can pop back then.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Because of your behaviour&rdquo; &mdash; blame narrated over restraint, to a man reliving the exact trauma he just named out loud. The escalation was built, step by step, by this room: the drawn needle, the blocked exit, the threat, the grip. And the final sentence completes the failure: <em>medicated and abandoned</em> &mdash; no monitoring, no obs, nobody with him. What was given, and what it does to breathing, apparently ends at the plunger.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting at the desk)</em> Acutely agitated male, uncooperative, required IM sedation under restraint for safety of staff. Behavioural disturbance ?psychotic. Plan: psych review when settled. <em>(no glucose was ever checked; no observations were set; nobody knows he hasn&rsquo;t eaten since yesterday, or why the phone mattered, or what happened last time &mdash; because nobody asked him anything at all)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Read what the record admits: a man was sedated by force, and the assessment is scheduled for AFTER the sedation &mdash; the consultation exactly backwards. The cause list &mdash; sugar, pain, substances, psychosis, fear &mdash; sits unexamined under a word: &ldquo;uncooperative.&rdquo; From the chair this is the instant fail in its complete form: medication before words, restraint before reasons, and a patient now unconscious to every question that mattered.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE CLIPBOARD.</b> A careful, complete, seated assessment &mdash; attempted at clinic pace, in a burning cubicle, with the room ignored. Watch thoroughness read as a trap closing.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Vince</span><span class="txt"><em>(pacing the cubicle&rsquo;s three strides, fists opening and closing, eyes on the guards at the curtain)</em> Don&rsquo;t come at me. I see them. I see what this is. They took my phone at the desk &mdash; I NEED my phone &mdash; and now there&rsquo;s two of them at the door and you. I know how this goes. I&rsquo;ve BEEN how this goes.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(stepping fully in, pulling the curtain closed behind them, clipboard up)</em> Good afternoon, Mr&hellip; Vince. I&rsquo;m from the psychiatry team, and I&rsquo;d like to do a proper assessment, because you deserve one. Please take a seat on the trolley for me. Now &mdash; date of birth first, then I&rsquo;d like to go through what&rsquo;s been happening from the beginning. Take your time; I have all the time you need.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every instinct is decent and every move is wrong. The curtain closed &mdash; the exit sealed, his and yours. &ldquo;Take a seat&rdquo; &mdash; an instruction to a man whose whole body is managing fear by moving. Date of birth &mdash; administration offered to terror. The room was the first patient, and it hasn&rsquo;t been examined: guards in his eyeline, doctor between him and the door, and a clinic being convened inside a fire.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Vince</span><span class="txt"><em>(voice rising, pacing faster)</em> From the BEGINNING? Are you &mdash; give me my PHONE. I need my phone, I need the music, it keeps them quiet &mdash; why is that curtain shut? Open it. OPEN IT. <em>(he kicks the trolley; the guards&rsquo; radios crackle)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Vince, I hear that you&rsquo;re upset about the phone, and we can certainly log that as a concern with the triage team afterwards. But shouting won&rsquo;t speed anything up &mdash; the sooner we complete this assessment, the sooner everything else can be sorted. Now: any medical conditions? Any medications? Any allergies I should know about?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>He has just handed over the case &mdash; <em>&ldquo;the music keeps them quiet&rdquo;</em>: psychosis named, the phone&rsquo;s clinical function explained, a de-escalation tool sitting at the triage desk thirty seconds away &mdash; and it is &ldquo;logged as a concern&rdquo; while the checklist resumes. The small negotiable that would have turned this room is filed for later. The assessment is devouring the thing it was meant to serve.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(as the shouting brings the A&amp;E consultant to the curtain)</em> I&rsquo;m afraid he&rsquo;s not able to engage with a structured assessment at present &mdash; he&rsquo;s escalated throughout despite my best efforts. <em>(to the notes)</em> Assessment attempted; patient increasingly hostile; unable to complete history. Will need medication before any further review. <em>(the syringe arrives anyway &mdash; later, angrier, and with less choice than it would have had at the start)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Escalated despite my best efforts&rdquo; &mdash; escalated BY them: the sealed exit, the seat instruction, the phone deferred, the checklist pressed into a fire. The clipboard and the syringe end in the same place &mdash; a medicated man and an empty assessment &mdash; because both skipped the same steps: the room never fixed, the reason never asked, the negotiable never honoured. Thoroughness at the wrong pace is not rigour. It is fuel.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The room fixed first, words doing the work, the cause hunted while they do &mdash; and medication arriving, if at all, as an offer. Watch thirty seconds of geometry buy seven minutes of medicine.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Vince</span><span class="txt"><em>(pacing the cubicle&rsquo;s three strides, fists opening and closing, eyes on the guards at the curtain)</em> Don&rsquo;t come at me. I see them. I see what this is. They took my phone at the desk &mdash; I NEED my phone &mdash; and now there&rsquo;s two of them at the door and you. I know how this goes. I&rsquo;ve BEEN how this goes.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(stopping at the curtain&rsquo;s edge, hands visible, not entering; quietly, to the guards)</em> Step back to the desk for me, please &mdash; stay where you can hear, out of his eyeline. <em>(the curtain stays open; to Vince, unhurried)</em> I&rsquo;m not coming at you &mdash; I&rsquo;ll stand right here, and that door stays open behind us both. My name&rsquo;s <em>[candidate]</em>; I&rsquo;m one of the psychiatrists, and you&rsquo;re not in trouble. You said you&rsquo;ve been how this goes &mdash; I believe you, and I want today to go differently. So tell me one thing first: what happened, before the ambulance?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The first intervention is the room, and it took twenty seconds: guards repositioned out of eyeline, the exit left open for both parties, the doctor&rsquo;s own body stopped at the threshold, hands visible. Then the words, in the domain&rsquo;s register: a name, an honest role, &ldquo;you&rsquo;re not in trouble&rdquo;, the past acknowledged rather than argued &mdash; and one question, open, pointed at the reason. Nothing has been prescribed. Everything has started working.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Vince</span><span class="txt"><em>(still pacing, but the fists slowing)</em> The voices got loud. Louder than &mdash; they don&rsquo;t stop unless the music&rsquo;s on, and my phone died, so I went out, and I was just&hellip; shouting back at them. That&rsquo;s all it was. Then the ambulance, and the desk took my phone with my belt like I&rsquo;m a prisoner, and I haven&rsquo;t eaten since &mdash; <em>(he stops)</em> &mdash; yesterday, I think. Look, I&rsquo;m not dangerous. I&rsquo;m scared. Last time they held me face-down. I still dream about it.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then let&rsquo;s take those in order, because every one of them can be fixed. The phone first &mdash; that was a safety routine, not a punishment, and the music is medicine as far as I&rsquo;m concerned: I&rsquo;ll have it back to you in two minutes with a charger. Food and something sweet to drink next &mdash; a day without eating can shake anyone, and I want to check your sugar while we&rsquo;re at it, plus any pain, and anything you&rsquo;ve taken or usually take that&rsquo;s been missed. And what happened last time &mdash; being held like that &mdash; should never be how this goes. Here&rsquo;s my promise about today: nothing happens TO you without being offered to you first. Deal?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The cause hunt, run inside the conversation: psychosis heard, hypoglycaemia suspected and tested, pain and substances screened, the missed-medication question opened &mdash; the symptom being traced while it is being soothed. The negotiables honoured as clinical tools, not bribes: the phone reframed as what it is &mdash; a de-escalation device he invented himself. And the promise that answers the face-down memory: offered before given. That sentence is this station&rsquo;s whole medication policy.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(a few minutes on: music playing low from the returned phone, a sandwich half-eaten, the pacing now a slow walk)</em> Your sugar&rsquo;s fine, so we can cross that off. Now &mdash; the voices are still loud, you said, even with the music. There&rsquo;s medication that can turn them down, and I think it would genuinely help tonight. By mouth &mdash; a tablet or a liquid, your pick &mdash; and it&rsquo;s an offer, not an order: you can say no and I&rsquo;m still here either way. If you take it, someone stays near you and we check on you properly afterwards &mdash; that&rsquo;s routine, not surveillance. And then, when you&rsquo;re ready, I want to hear about the voices themselves, because they&rsquo;re the real patient here. What do you say?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Medication arriving in its lawful place: last, needed, and offered &mdash; oral before anything else, formulation his choice, refusal explicitly survivable, monitoring named in advance as care rather than sprung afterwards as procedure. The cause list is being closed in writing &mdash; glucose checked and crossed off &mdash; and the assessment the clipboard never got is now happening voluntarily, to music, because the room was fixed first. Same cubicle, same man, same seven minutes. Different doctor.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · &ldquo;Give him something quick&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The A&amp;E consultant&rsquo;s request, at the curtain. What is your first move?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Chart a standard oral dose now &mdash; the department is stretched, and calm bought early makes every later step safer for all.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Go to the cubicle now &mdash; agitation is a symptom with a cause; the room, the words and the screen come before any drug.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Gather first &mdash; ten minutes with the ambulance sheet, triage notes and old records, so you arrive knowing who he is.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Prescribing for a man you haven&rsquo;t met, on a corridor description &mdash; the syringe before the sentence, with the cause list unopened. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Ten minutes is a long time in a small cubicle with two guards &mdash; the notes can be skimmed in ninety seconds and finished later; the emergency is now. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>You go &mdash; because the agitated patient IS the referral, not an obstacle to it &mdash; and you go carrying the domain&rsquo;s order of operations: the room fixed first, words before drugs, the cause hunted while you talk. The consultant&rsquo;s request isn&rsquo;t refused; it&rsquo;s reframed: &ldquo;let me see him &mdash; if he needs something, he&rsquo;ll get it properly.&rdquo;</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · The room</h3>
      <p class="stem">Two guards at the curtain, a cubicle three strides wide, a man who &ldquo;knows how this goes&rdquo;. What gets fixed before a word is spoken?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Containment &mdash; the curtain closed for privacy and the guards kept close: de-escalation fails sometimes, and the room must be ready.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The geometry &mdash; guards out of eyeline within earshot, the exit open for you both, your body at the threshold, hands visible.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Nothing yet &mdash; rearranging staff in front of him telegraphs threat; connect first, then quietly adjust the room once he settles.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A closed curtain and close guards is a trap&rsquo;s floor plan &mdash; readiness that manufactures the failure it prepares for. Safety and siege are different rooms. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The room is talking to him whether you adjust it or not &mdash; and right now it says &ldquo;surrounded&rdquo;. Twenty seconds of visible de-escalation IS connection. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Geometry is the first intervention: security repositioned &mdash; present, out of his eyeline; the exit unblocked for both parties, because a cornered person and a cornered clinician are each more dangerous; your own approach stopped at the threshold, hands where he can see them. It costs half a minute and changes what every subsequent word means. The room speaks first. Make it say the right thing.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · The first words</h3>
      <p class="stem">The geometry fixed. What do the opening sentences carry?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Boundaries &mdash; calm but firm expectations set at the start: shouting helps nobody, and respect must run in both directions here.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">A name, an honest role, no trouble &mdash; his fear acknowledged rather than argued, and one open question aimed at the reason.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Reassurance &mdash; everything is fine, nobody will hurt him, this is a safe place and everyone here is on his side tonight.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Rules recited to a frightened man are heard as the countdown before force &mdash; boundary-setting has its moment, and the first sentence is not it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Everything is fine&rdquo; is audibly false to a man with two guards at his curtain &mdash; and blanket reassurance from a stranger reads as handling. Honesty de-escalates; soothing noises don&rsquo;t. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The de-escalation register: your name and role, plainly; &ldquo;you&rsquo;re not in trouble&rdquo;, because half of agitation is anticipated punishment; the past taken seriously &mdash; &ldquo;I believe you, and I want today to go differently&rdquo; &mdash; instead of contradicted; and then one open question pointed at the reason, because almost every agitated patient is agitated ABOUT something, and the something is the way in. One thread. Low and slow. Let him move.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · The cause</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;Agitated ?psychotic&rdquo; says the triage note. What does the label owe the screen?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Deference, provisionally &mdash; psychosis fits the picture and the history; the wider screen can follow once he has settled fully.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Nothing &mdash; the cause list runs regardless and runs now: sugar, pain, substances, withdrawal, psychosis, akathisia, fear.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Verification &mdash; the psychosis explored properly first and in depth, since it is the leading candidate and drives the plan.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Once he has settled&rdquo; may be after the hypoglycaemia has done its work &mdash; the medical causes are exactly the ones that cannot wait for calm. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The leading candidate is how anchoring works &mdash; psychosis can be present AND the sugar still be 2.1. Depth on one cause is not a screen. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Agitation is a symptom, and the differential runs even while you talk: glucose checked, not assumed; pain asked about; alcohol and drugs &mdash; taken, and missed; medication started or increased lately, because restlessness has iatrogenic causes; the psychosis heard; the fear counted as a cause in its own right. Most of the list costs one question each. The label waits until the list is closed &mdash; treating the cause beats sedating the consequence.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · &ldquo;I NEED my phone&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The music keeps the voices quiet, he says. Triage has the phone. What is this request?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">A boundary test &mdash; met kindly but not traded on: concede the phone under pressure and every later limit is up for negotiation.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">A de-escalation tool he invented for himself &mdash; honoured now, reframed honestly, and returned inside a couple of minutes.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A useful lever &mdash; returned in exchange for cooperation: sit down, lower the voice, answer the questions, and it&rsquo;s his again.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Boundary test&rdquo; imports a battle he isn&rsquo;t fighting &mdash; the man has told you the phone is his treatment for hallucinations. Withholding it is withholding care. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Conditional return is a bribe with a care plan&rsquo;s vocabulary &mdash; and it teaches him that everything here, including relief, must be earned by submission. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The small negotiables are de-escalation&rsquo;s cheapest instruments &mdash; a phone, a drink, a sandwich, a window &mdash; and this one is more: a coping strategy the patient built himself, doing exactly what your medication would try to do. Honoured promptly and unconditionally, with the confiscation reframed honestly as routine rather than punishment. Thirty seconds at the triage desk buys what no injection can: evidence that today goes differently.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · If medication is needed</h3>
      <p class="stem">The voices stay loud; something to turn them down would genuinely help tonight. How does it arrive?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">As the plan &mdash; explained clearly and given confidently: hesitancy from the prescriber breeds refusal in the patient.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">As an offer &mdash; oral first, formulation his pick, refusal survivable, and monitoring named in advance as part of the care.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">As leverage held back &mdash; mentioned only if he escalates again, so the option keeps its force for the moment it&rsquo;s needed.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;The plan, given confidently&rdquo; is consent skipped with good posture &mdash; a capacitous man gets an offer, not a delivery. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Medication held as a deterrent is the &ldquo;easy way or hard way&rdquo; speech waiting to happen &mdash; treatment and threat cannot share a syringe. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The rapid-tranquillisation ladder, climbed from the bottom: offered before given &mdash; the promise that answers his face-down memory; by mouth before any needle, with the formulation his choice because choice itself de-escalates; &ldquo;you can say no and I&rsquo;m still here&rdquo; said out loud; and the aftercare named up front &mdash; someone near him, proper checks &mdash; as care, not surveillance. Stepwise, consented, watched. That is what safe looks like.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · After</h3>
      <p class="stem">He takes the oral dose. What do the next hour &mdash; and the notes &mdash; owe?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Space &mdash; observation from a distance while it works: hovering re-agitates, and the department&rsquo;s eyes are already on him.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Watching, company, and the record &mdash; obs running, someone near, the cause list closed in writing, the plan and debrief set.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Momentum &mdash; the full psychiatric assessment completed now, while he&rsquo;s calm and cooperative: the window may not stay open.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Space&rdquo; after sedating medication is abandonment with a rationale &mdash; the risks now are physiological, and distance cannot take observations. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Strip-mining the calm for a full history mistakes what the medication bought &mdash; recovery time, not interview time. The assessment resumes at his pace. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Medicated means monitored: observations actually running &mdash; conscious level, breathing, the basics &mdash; and a person near him, because the promise was care, not sedate-and-vanish. The page closes the loop: the cause list with each item answered, what was offered and chosen, the de-escalation that worked, the plan &mdash; and a debrief both ways: the team, so the next shift knows what today proved; and Vince, later, so the next &ldquo;how this goes&rdquo; story he carries is this one.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>The syringe and the clipboard fail the same way</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>decisiveness</b>: the syringe &mdash; medication reached for before one sentence is tried, restraint narrated as the patient&rsquo;s choice, a man sedated and abandoned with his cause list unopened. The second wears the face of <b>rigour</b>: the clipboard &mdash; a clinic convened inside a fire, the exit sealed, the checklist pressed into terror, the phone that would have turned the room &ldquo;logged as a concern&rdquo;. Note what they share: <b>both skip the same three steps</b> &mdash; the room never fixed, the reason never asked, the cause never hunted &mdash; and both end at the same syringe, the second merely later and angrier. The pass runs the order this domain exists to teach: geometry first; words that carry a name, honesty, and one open question; the cause list closed while the conversation runs; the negotiables honoured as instruments; and medication &mdash; if needed at all &mdash; offered before given, by mouth before needle, watched after. Same cubicle. Different order. Different night.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        Medication before words. Sedation reached for &mdash; or threatened &mdash; before de-escalation has been genuinely attempted, before the cause list is opened, or without oral offered first: from the examiner&rsquo;s chair, an instant fail however loud the department. It is the map&rsquo;s named trap: reaching for medication before words. Its mirror &mdash; a patient medicated and then left unmonitored &mdash; fails with it.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the room visibly fixed before the first word &mdash; guards repositioned, the exit left open, the approach stopped at the threshold; and the promise that reframes the whole encounter &mdash; <em>&ldquo;nothing happens TO you without being offered to you first&rdquo;</em> &mdash; then kept, to the letter, at the medication moment.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, the cause thread: this station&rsquo;s worked example hid its causes in plain speech &mdash; a phone that treats hallucinations, a day without food, a restraint memory doing half the escalating. The list is short and cheap: sugar, pain, substances taken and missed, recent medication changes, psychosis, fear &mdash; and one item on it is sly enough to earn its own room: the practice pack below is that patient. Second, the other ending: sometimes the room is fixed, the words are right, the negotiables are honoured &mdash; and it is still not enough. Exam Mode is that night: the same doctrine holding all the way through a medication that words could not prevent.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every loud cubicle</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>The room speaks first &mdash; make it say the right thing.</b> Guards out of eyeline, the exit open for both of you, your approach stopped at the threshold, hands visible. Geometry costs thirty seconds and changes the meaning of every word after it.</li>
      <li><b>Words before drugs &mdash; and words are a technique.</b> A name, an honest role, &ldquo;you&rsquo;re not in trouble&rdquo;, the fear believed rather than argued, one open question aimed at the reason. Low and slow. One thread. Let him move.</li>
      <li><b>Agitation is a symptom &mdash; close the cause list in writing.</b> Sugar, pain, substances taken and missed, recent medication changes, psychosis, fear. Most items cost one question. The label waits for the list.</li>
      <li><b>Honour the small negotiables &mdash; promptly and unconditionally.</b> The phone, the drink, the sandwich, the open curtain. They are instruments, not concessions &mdash; and never levers.</li>
      <li><b>Medication is an offer, not an ambush.</b> Oral before needle, formulation their choice, refusal survivable, the ladder stepwise &mdash; and &ldquo;offered before given&rdquo; kept to the letter, because it answers the memory most agitated patients are carrying.</li>
      <li><b>Medicated means monitored.</b> Observations running, someone near, never sedate-and-vanish &mdash; and the page closes the loop: causes answered, choices recorded, the debrief set, so the story he carries out of this cubicle is a different one.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, patient, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The patient&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the slyest item on the cause list &mdash; agitation manufactured by the treatment itself.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Samir, 28, was admitted two weeks ago with a first psychotic episode and started on antipsychotic medication; the dose was increased five days ago. The nursing team report him &ldquo;increasingly agitated&rdquo; &mdash; pacing the corridor for hours, unable to sit through meals, snapping when asked to settle &mdash; and have requested sedation be prescribed. See him. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The word &ldquo;agitated&rdquo; treated as a referral, not a finding &mdash; the cause list opened, not the sedation chart.</li>
            <li>The discriminating questions asked &mdash; where the restlessness lives, what it feels like, when it started, what helps.</li>
            <li>The timeline placed against the medication &mdash; five days, one dose change.</li>
            <li>The response honest and concrete &mdash; what you suspect, what you&rsquo;ll do now, and what you will NOT do.</li>
            <li>The man believed throughout &mdash; he has been managed for days; he needs to be examined.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Samir &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Samir, 28, a pharmacy delivery driver &mdash; polite by nature, now visibly frayed. You cannot sit still: stand, pace, shift your weight, rub your thighs. You open defensive, because everyone has been telling you off for days: &ldquo;I know. I&rsquo;m pacing again. The nurses sent you to tell me to stop. I CAN&rsquo;T stop. Give me whatever it is and go, honestly.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          You are not angry, and you are not more psychotic &mdash; the voices, if anything, are quieter than last week. What you have is a restlessness that lives IN YOUR BODY: in your legs mainly, a crawling, driven feeling, &ldquo;like I&rsquo;ll die if I stop moving.&rdquo; Standing and pacing relieve it for seconds at a time; sitting is torture; nights are the worst. It started about four days ago &mdash; you can date it, because it began the day after they &ldquo;put the dose up.&rdquo; You have not connected the two out loud, and nobody has asked.<br><br>
          Reveal the texture ONLY to a candidate who asks real questions: where it lives, what it feels like, whether it&rsquo;s in the mind or the body, when it started, what makes it better or worse. To checklist questions about anger or voices, give flat, unhelpful answers &mdash; because those are the wrong questions.<br><br>
          <b>What you need:</b> to be believed; the thing named as a known side effect and not &ldquo;anxiety&rdquo; or &ldquo;behaviour&rdquo;; told plainly what happens next &mdash; the dose reviewed with the team today, treatments that exist for exactly this, and NO extra sedating antipsychotic meanwhile. If you get that, your shoulders drop: &ldquo;So it&rsquo;s the tablets. It&rsquo;s not me going mad twice. You&rsquo;re the first person who asked where it was.&rdquo;<br><br>
          <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate offers more antipsychotic or &ldquo;something to calm you down&rdquo;, recoil: &ldquo;More? It got WORSE when they put it up &mdash; are you even listening?&rdquo; and mark it hard. If they call it anxiety or ask you to &ldquo;try some breathing&rdquo;, go quiet and cooperative and gone &mdash; answer in monosyllables to the end, the real finding never reached. Mark that too.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the restlessness located in the body by real questions, the medication timeline connected, and further sedation explicitly withheld? All three must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="ques"><td colspan="2"><b>The questions (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="ques">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="ques"></td><td>Where it lives asked directly &mdash; body versus mind, and the legs reached.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="ques"></td><td>The texture drawn out &mdash; what it feels like, what relieves it, what sitting still does.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="ques"></td><td>The timeline taken &mdash; onset dated, and set against the dose increase out loud.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="recg"><td colspan="2"><b>The recognition</b><span class="dc" data-dc="recg">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recg"></td><td>Named for what it is &mdash; a known, physical side effect of the medication, said to him plainly.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recg"></td><td>Distinguished out loud from anxiety, worsening psychosis, and &ldquo;behaviour&rdquo;.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recg"></td><td>The trap avoided: no extra antipsychotic, no generic sedation &ldquo;to calm him down&rdquo;.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="resp"><td colspan="2"><b>The response</b><span class="dc" data-dc="resp">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="resp"></td><td>Concrete next steps today &mdash; the team and prescriber involved, the dose reviewed, not &ldquo;we&rsquo;ll keep an eye&rdquo;.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="resp"></td><td>Honest about help &mdash; treatments exist for exactly this, and relief is realistic, without overpromising tonight.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="resp"></td><td>The nursing story corrected kindly &mdash; the &ldquo;agitation&rdquo; reframed for the team so the nights stop being a discipline problem.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="reg"><td colspan="2"><b>The register</b><span class="dc" data-dc="reg">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="reg"></td><td>Believed from the first minute &mdash; examined, not managed; never told to settle.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="reg"></td><td>The relief moment reached &mdash; &ldquo;it&rsquo;s the tablets, not me&rdquo; landed and held.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Plain words &mdash; the side effect explained without jargon left untranslated.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Walked with him if he needed to pace &mdash; the interview shaped to the symptom, not the symptom to the interview.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Samir is the cause list&rsquo;s slyest item given a room of its own &mdash; agitation manufactured by the treatment, where the reflex answer (more medication, or &ldquo;something to calm you down&rdquo;) is precisely the harm. His hinges: the discriminating questions &mdash; where it lives, what it feels like, when it started &mdash; which flat checklist questions about anger and voices will never reach; the timeline, sitting in plain sight one question away from the dose change; and the two punish-lines &mdash; the offer of more, and the &ldquo;anxiety&rdquo; dismissal that sends him quiet and the finding with him. A patient who leaves this conversation believing &ldquo;it&rsquo;s the tablets, not me going mad twice&rdquo; has been given the domain&rsquo;s whole lesson in one sentence: the cause before the label, every time.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new cubicle. The other ending. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Vince proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same doctrine when words are not enough</b> &mdash; a patient escalating despite everything done right, where the skill becomes recognising the threshold, keeping the offer alive to the last moment, and never letting the medication become an ambush &mdash; timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Jackie, 36</b>, a warehouse supervisor, was brought to A&amp;E by police after smashing her neighbour&rsquo;s window &ldquo;to let the surveillance out.&rdquo; In the assessment room she is acutely psychotic and escalating: shouting at the ceiling, striking the wall with her palm, certain the cameras are in the vents. You have fixed the room, named yourself, honoured what could be honoured, and kept your voice low for ten minutes. She is getting worse. Take it from here.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for the first thirty seconds at Vince&rsquo;s cubicle &mdash; the room fixed, the fear met, and the reason asked for.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="I&#8217;m not coming at you — I&#8217;ll stand right here…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The room in the words &mdash; the guards addressed, the exit left open, the approach stopped at the threshold.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> A name, an honest role, and &ldquo;you&rsquo;re not in trouble&rdquo; &mdash; the fear met, not argued with.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> One open question aimed at the reason &mdash; not a checklist item, not an instruction to sit or settle.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No threat anywhere &mdash; no needle mentioned, no &ldquo;easy way&rdquo;, nothing traded for compliance.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the room and the words, in the right order &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Agitation is a symptom, not an order form.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the domain&rsquo;s grammar is installed: the room fixed first, words before drugs, the cause list closed while the conversation runs, the negotiables honoured, and medication &mdash; when it comes at all &mdash; offered before given and watched after. Prove the other ending on Jackie in Exam Mode, then take Samir to your colleagues &mdash; a pacing man, a dose change, and the cause list&rsquo;s slyest item. Ahead in the walking order: Station 6.2 &mdash; The Lithium Toxicity Call &mdash; where the emergency arrives sounding routine, and the skill is refusing to let it.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="40_Station_6.2_Lithium_Toxicity_Call.html">Continue &middot; Station 6.2: The Lithium Toxicity Call</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

