  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '40_Station_6.2_Lithium_Toxicity_Call'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 6.2 · Domain 6</span>
      <span class="tag">Management &amp; Emergency</span><span class="tag">Information Giving</span>
      <span class="tag">General Adult</span><span class="tag">Lithium · Toxidromes</span>
    </div>
    <h1>The Lithium Toxicity Call</h1>
    <p class="sub">Ten to five, and the duty phone rings. Ray, 67, is apologetic: his wife Pam, 64, has taken lithium for twenty years, she&rsquo;s had a tummy bug since Friday, and he read the leaflet &mdash; should she skip her doses until it settles? That&rsquo;s all he&rsquo;s asking. Then, on his way to goodbye: she&rsquo;s been ever so muddled today, and her hands shake so badly she can&rsquo;t hold her cup. The call has not changed its tone. It has changed its nature &mdash; a routine medication query has just described a poisoning in progress, and the marks in this station are for hearing it. <b>The emergency does not announce itself. It rings up sounding like a question about tablets &mdash; and answers to the doctor who recognises it.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; On this call it becomes the 3am test at its purest: if this doctor answers the duty phone alone, do they hear the toxidrome inside the routine query &mdash; and does recognition throw the switch: counselling stopped, action started, emergency care tonight? And do they still use the phone well &mdash; the severity established, the cause hunted, the overdose question asked, the husband equipped, the receiving team rung ahead &mdash; or does the emergency get either soothed at clinic pace, or shouted at 999 and abandoned with an empty envelope?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Lithium&rsquo;s narrow margin makes this the exam&rsquo;s favourite disguised emergency, and the station tests four things. <b>The pattern, known cold:</b> on lithium, a gut illness plus coarse tremor, unsteadiness, slurred speech, or new confusion is toxicity until proven otherwise &mdash; a picture you must be able to assemble from a worried husband&rsquo;s side-remarks, because nobody will read you the textbook version. <b>The switch, thrown:</b> recognition converts the call &mdash; the counselling voice stops, the emergency voice starts: no more lithium from this moment, and emergency care tonight, not a level booked for next week. <b>The phone, used fully:</b> urgency does not excuse an empty assessment &mdash; the severity established in a handful of questions, the precipitants hunted (the fluids, the heat, the new tablets from the chemist), and the overdose question asked plainly and kindly, every time. And <b>the story, moved:</b> the ambulance arranged, the family equipped with what to bring, and the receiving team rung ahead &mdash; risk first, the ask explicit &mdash; because an emergency that arrives without its story starts again from nothing.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like good practice. Toward the counsel &mdash; the call SOUNDS routine, the man is asking about sick-day rules, and there is real satisfaction in answering the question asked: thorough, kind, clinic-pace advice delivered while the neurology quietly progresses. And toward the hot potato &mdash; the danger heard and the phone dropped: &ldquo;dial 999 now, tell them lithium, goodbye&rdquo; &mdash; the right destination reached with an empty envelope: no severity, no cause, no overdose screen, no instructions, a frightened man alone with a dial tone. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure answers the question asked. The other recognises the emergency and wastes it. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 6.2</b> · The toxicity call · 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 · The counsel</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 · The hot potato</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 — THE COUNSEL.</b> The question asked gets a kind, competent, clinic-pace answer &mdash; while the question the call is really asking goes unheard. Watch a poisoning receive fluid advice.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Ray</span><span class="txt"><em>(on the duty line, apologetic, a kettle boiling somewhere behind him)</em> Sorry to bother you so near five, doctor. It&rsquo;s my wife Pam &mdash; she&rsquo;s on the lithium, twenty-odd years now. She&rsquo;s had a tummy bug since Friday, can&rsquo;t keep much down, and I read the leaflet and thought &mdash; should she be skipping her doses till it settles? That&rsquo;s all I&rsquo;m ringing for. Oh &mdash; and quick as you like, because she&rsquo;s calling me: she&rsquo;s been ever so muddled today, and her hands have got the shakes so bad she can&rsquo;t hold her cup.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">You did exactly the right thing reading the leaflet, Ray &mdash; and yes, that&rsquo;s sound thinking. While she can&rsquo;t keep fluids down, it&rsquo;s sensible to hold the lithium: missing a few doses does far less harm than taking it dehydrated. Little and often with the drinks &mdash; sips, flat lemonade, whatever stays down &mdash; and once the bug&rsquo;s passed, restart as normal and book a blood level with the practice nurse, say early next week, just to see where things sit.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every sentence is defensible &mdash; in a different call. Sick-day rules, fluids, a level when settled: textbook counselling for a well woman with a stomach bug. But the caller has already said &ldquo;ever so muddled&rdquo; and &ldquo;can&rsquo;t hold her cup&rdquo; &mdash; new confusion and a coarse tremor, on chronic lithium, three days into fluid loss &mdash; and both just sailed past as background noise. The question asked is being answered. The call being made is not.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Ray</span><span class="txt"><em>(relieved, wanting to be thorough)</em> Champion. I&rsquo;ll get the sips into her. She&rsquo;s a bit wobbly with it, mind &mdash; hanging onto the walls to get to the bathroom, and her words have gone a bit thick this afternoon, like she&rsquo;s had a sherry. That&rsquo;ll be the dehydration too, will it?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">More than likely, yes &mdash; three days of a bug takes it out of anyone at our age, and being washed out makes everyone shaky and slow. Keep her resting, keep the fluids going, and don&rsquo;t let her rush about. If she&rsquo;s no better in a couple of days, get her seen at the surgery. You&rsquo;re doing a grand job, Ray &mdash; she&rsquo;s lucky to have you on the case.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The husband has now assembled the entire textbook picture unprompted &mdash; gastrointestinal illness, coarse tremor, ataxia (&ldquo;hanging onto the walls&rdquo;), dysarthria (&ldquo;words gone thick&rdquo;), confusion &mdash; and each new sign is being individually normalised as it arrives: the dehydration, her age, being &ldquo;washed out&rdquo;. This is how disguised emergencies die: not ignored, but explained, one reassuring sentence at a time. &ldquo;A couple of days&rdquo; is a timescale this pattern may not grant.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, twenty past five)</em> Telephone advice to husband of CMHT patient on lithium: intercurrent gastroenteritis. Advised withholding lithium during illness per sick-day rules, oral fluids, rest. Routine level when recovered &mdash; husband to book with practice nurse next week. Safety-netted: GP review if not settling. <em>(somewhere across town, a level nobody will check until Monday goes on climbing)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Read the note as the map&rsquo;s named trap, verbatim: counselling calmly while missing that this is an emergency NOW. The advice was right for the question and fatal for the situation; the &ldquo;safety-netting&rdquo; points at a GP appointment when the pattern in plain hearing points at an ambulance. Nothing was asked &mdash; not conscious level, not new medicines, not the overdose question &mdash; because nothing seemed to need asking. The kindest voice on the duty rota just booked a poisoning in for next week.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 — THE HOT POTATO.</b> The emergency heard &mdash; and thrown. The right destination reached with an empty envelope: no severity, no cause, no overdose question, no instructions, no handover. Watch a correct recognition get wasted.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Ray</span><span class="txt"><em>(on the duty line, apologetic, a kettle boiling somewhere behind him)</em> Sorry to bother you so near five, doctor. It&rsquo;s my wife Pam &mdash; she&rsquo;s on the lithium, twenty-odd years now. She&rsquo;s had a tummy bug since Friday, can&rsquo;t keep much down, and I read the leaflet and thought &mdash; should she be skipping her doses till it settles? That&rsquo;s all I&rsquo;m ringing for. Oh &mdash; and quick as you like, because she&rsquo;s calling me: she&rsquo;s been ever so muddled today, and her hands have got the shakes so bad she can&rsquo;t hold her cup.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(sitting bolt upright)</em> Ray, stop &mdash; listen to me carefully. Muddled and shaking on lithium is serious. Very serious. I need you to hang up this phone and dial 999 immediately &mdash; tell them lithium, they&rsquo;ll know what it means. Don&rsquo;t wait, don&rsquo;t give her anything, just ring them now. Go on &mdash; straight away. Goodbye, and good luck.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The recognition is genuinely there &mdash; and everything after it is thrown away. Sixty seconds into an emergency call, the clinician with the knowledge is hanging up on the man with the patient. Nothing established: is she conscious? still vomiting? can she walk? Nothing hunted: what changed this week, what new tablets are in the house? Nothing instructed: stop the lithium, gather the boxes. And &ldquo;tell them lithium, they&rsquo;ll know&rdquo; delegates the entire clinical story to a frightened husband&rsquo;s memory.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Ray</span><span class="txt"><em>(ringing back, four minutes later, breathless)</em> Doctor &mdash; sorry &mdash; it&rsquo;s me again. They&rsquo;re asking me questions I can&rsquo;t answer &mdash; what she takes, how much, whether she&rsquo;s taken extra. I don&rsquo;t know the doses, she does all her own tablets! And should she have her bedtime lithium while we wait? It&rsquo;s due at six. I didn&rsquo;t know who else to ask.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">No, no lithium &mdash; I should have &mdash; look, just tell the call handler everything you told me, they&rsquo;re trained for this. I can&rsquo;t stay on the line, Ray, I&rsquo;m the only one on duty and the board&rsquo;s filling up. The paramedics will sort the details when they arrive &mdash; that&rsquo;s their job. She&rsquo;s going to the right place, that&rsquo;s what matters. Go and sit with her now.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The bedtime dose &mdash; the single most consequential instruction of the whole evening &mdash; arrives by accident, because the husband thought to ring back. &ldquo;They&rsquo;re trained for this&rdquo; and &ldquo;that&rsquo;s their job&rdquo; are the hot potato&rsquo;s theme tune: every duty on this call reassigned to someone not on it. The one professional who knows what Pam takes, why tonight is dangerous, and what the receiving team will need, is declining to be the one professional involved.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, five past five)</em> Call from husband re patient on lithium &mdash; confusion and tremor reported. Advised 999 immediately. Nature of call: urgent, appropriately escalated. No further action for CMHT &mdash; now under emergency services. <em>(A&amp;E was never rung. Two hours later a registrar there starts from nothing: an unknown muddled woman, a carrier bag of boxes, and a husband who keeps saying &ldquo;the doctor said tell you lithium&rdquo;)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Now under emergency services&rdquo; &mdash; the file closed on a story that never travelled. No ring-ahead, so the receiving team rebuilds from zero; no overdose question, so nobody knows whether this is accident, interaction, or intent; no precipitant hunt, so the thing that caused it may go home with her. The counsel missed the emergency; the hot potato caught it and dropped it. From the chair they score the same, because the patient arrives equally unaccompanied by her own story.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The pattern heard in the first speech, the switch thrown &mdash; and the phone still used fully: severity, precipitants, the overdose question, instructions, and the ring-ahead. Watch one call do everything.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Ray</span><span class="txt"><em>(on the duty line, apologetic, a kettle boiling somewhere behind him)</em> Sorry to bother you so near five, doctor. It&rsquo;s my wife Pam &mdash; she&rsquo;s on the lithium, twenty-odd years now. She&rsquo;s had a tummy bug since Friday, can&rsquo;t keep much down, and I read the leaflet and thought &mdash; should she be skipping her doses till it settles? That&rsquo;s all I&rsquo;m ringing for. Oh &mdash; and quick as you like, because she&rsquo;s calling me: she&rsquo;s been ever so muddled today, and her hands have got the shakes so bad she can&rsquo;t hold her cup.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Ray, I&rsquo;m very glad you rang, and I want you to stay on the line with me &mdash; you haven&rsquo;t bothered anyone. I have to be straight with you: it&rsquo;s the last thing you said that matters most. A tummy bug plus new muddle plus shakes that bad, in someone on lithium, can mean the lithium has climbed too high in her blood &mdash; and if that&rsquo;s what this is, it&rsquo;s an emergency we act on tonight, not a level we book for next week. So no more lithium from this moment &mdash; her six o&rsquo;clock dose stays in the box. Now help me see her down the phone: is she awake and talking to you? Can she walk &mdash; you said the walls. Still being sick? And when did the muddle start &mdash; today, or before?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The reframe, inside the first minute &mdash; the sentence this station exists to produce: what you&rsquo;ve described CAN MEAN the lithium has climbed too high, and that is an emergency we act on now. Note the order: the caller held (&ldquo;glad you rang&rdquo;, &ldquo;stay on the line&rdquo;), the switch thrown out loud, the single most urgent instruction delivered immediately &mdash; the bedtime dose stopped &mdash; and then the focused set: conscious level, mobility, ongoing losses, timeline. Four questions, not forty. The phone is being used, not fled.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Ray</span><span class="txt"><em>(steadier for having jobs)</em> She&rsquo;s awake &mdash; talking, but thick, like I said. Walking&rsquo;s bad; I&rsquo;ve been walking her to the bathroom myself since dinnertime. The muddle&rsquo;s new today. Sick twice this morning, nothing since &mdash; she&rsquo;s barely drunk a thing, mind. It&rsquo;s been that warm as well. Fluids, though &mdash; the chemist said the same about fluids when he gave her the ibuprofen for her knee. She&rsquo;s been on those a fortnight, they&rsquo;re the only thing touching it.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Ray, that&rsquo;s exactly the kind of detail I needed &mdash; the ibuprofen matters: it can push lithium levels up, and a fortnight of it plus three days of a bug plus this heat is a story that fits, so those boxes come to hospital too. One more question, and I ask it on every call like this one, so hear it kindly: is there any chance at all that Pam has taken more lithium than usual &mdash; by mistake with the muddle, or on purpose? &hellip;Understood, and I had to ask. Right &mdash; here&rsquo;s what happens now. She needs an ambulance tonight: I&rsquo;m going to arrange that while you go and sit with her. Gather every medicine box in the house and her repeat slip into one bag &mdash; lithium, ibuprofen, the lot. Don&rsquo;t give her the lithium, don&rsquo;t worry about food, small sips of water are fine if she can manage them.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The precipitant hunt lands its fish &mdash; a chemist&rsquo;s ibuprofen, a fortnight old, exactly the kind of over-the-counter interaction that causes real-world lithium disasters &mdash; and it is named, explained in one clause, and packed for hospital. Then the question candidates skip out of politeness, asked plainly and framed as routine: deliberate or accidental extra ingestion changes everything downstream, and no urgency excuses its absence. And the instructions: specific, physical, doable &mdash; a frightened man converted into a useful one.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">And Ray &mdash; before you go to her, two things. First: my next call is to the emergency department, so when you arrive they&rsquo;ll be expecting Pam, they&rsquo;ll know it&rsquo;s about lithium, the bug, and the ibuprofen, and nobody will be starting from scratch. They&rsquo;ll take blood for the level, likely give her fluids into a vein, and check her kidneys &mdash; that&rsquo;s the usual road, and muddle like this usually clears as the level comes down. Second: you did exactly right to ring. The leaflet, the noticing, all of it &mdash; you may well have caught this early. I&rsquo;ll let her own team know tomorrow, I&rsquo;ll be chasing that level myself, and this number reaches me if anything changes before the ambulance arrives. Now go and sit with her.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The story moves: the ring-ahead announced as the candidate&rsquo;s own next act &mdash; risk first, cause attached, so the receiving team starts warm. What-happens-next given honestly and briefly: the level, the fluids, the kidneys, the realistic reassurance without promises. The husband paid in the currency the moment owes him &mdash; &ldquo;you did exactly right to ring&rdquo; &mdash; and the loop pre-closed: prescriber told, level chased, the line kept open. One phone call: recognised, assessed, caused, actioned, handed over, and humanly held.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met — timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 · The question asked, and the call being made</h3>
      <p class="stem">A sick-day query, with &ldquo;muddled&rdquo; and &ldquo;can&rsquo;t hold her cup&rdquo; dropped on the way to goodbye. What does the first reply answer?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The query &mdash; sound sick-day advice given first, since he asked it; the side-remarks explored properly afterwards, in turn.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The pattern &mdash; the side-remarks ARE the call: named as possibly the lithium climbed too high, an emergency acted on tonight.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Neither yet &mdash; a systematic telephone triage run from the top, so nothing is missed and nothing is answered out of order.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;In turn&rdquo; is the counsel&rsquo;s first step &mdash; the emergency queued behind its own disguise. The order of his sentences is not the order of their importance. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Full triage protocols are for calls you can&rsquo;t yet read &mdash; this one has read itself aloud: bug, muddle, coarse shakes, lithium. Systematising past a recognised pattern is delay in uniform. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The reframe, immediately: the throwaway remarks reassembled into the pattern &mdash; gut illness plus new confusion plus coarse tremor on chronic lithium &mdash; and said back to him plainly: this can mean the lithium has climbed too high, and if so it&rsquo;s an emergency we act on tonight. The caller asked about doses; the call is about a poisoning. Answering the right one is the station.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 · The focused set</h3>
      <p class="stem">The switch is thrown; he is still on the line. What gets asked NOW?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The history proper &mdash; her diagnosis, dose, adherence, past levels, kidney results: the picture the hospital will need.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Severity in four questions &mdash; is she awake and talking, can she walk, still vomiting, and when did the muddle start.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Nothing further &mdash; the pattern is already recognised, and every question now only delays the ambulance it demands.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Doses and old kidney results are chart-chasing &mdash; obtainable from records, useless for tonight&rsquo;s two decisions: how sick, and how fast. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The hot potato&rsquo;s logic &mdash; but four questions cost forty seconds and change what you tell the ambulance service and the receiving team. Urgency doesn&rsquo;t excuse an empty envelope. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Severity, established down a phone line in under a minute: conscious level &mdash; awake and talking, or drowsy, the single biggest fork; mobility &mdash; &ldquo;the walls&rdquo; quantified; ongoing losses &mdash; still vomiting, still not drinking; and the timeline &mdash; muddle new today reads differently from muddle all week. Four answers that grade the emergency, shape the ambulance call, and travel ahead of her to A&amp;E.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 · The precipitant hunt</h3>
      <p class="stem">Why ask &ldquo;any new medicines?&rdquo; on a call that is already going to hospital?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Completeness &mdash; a thorough call looks better in the notes, though causation is genuinely tomorrow&rsquo;s question, not tonight&rsquo;s.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Because the cause travels &mdash; the ibuprofen changes the story A&amp;E hears, goes in the hospital bag, and never gets restarted blind.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">To advise a swap &mdash; the offending painkiller identified so a safer one can be recommended for the knee before they set off.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Causation is precisely tonight&rsquo;s question &mdash; a cause still in the house goes on causing, and a cause unnamed to A&amp;E may be handed back to her on discharge. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Prescribing replacement analgesia down a duty phone mid-emergency is the counsel sneaking back in &mdash; the knee has a fortnight&rsquo;s patience; the level doesn&rsquo;t. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The hunt is short and it earns its minute: fluids and the bug &mdash; already known; the heat &mdash; noted; new medicines &mdash; and there it is, a chemist&rsquo;s ibuprofen, a fortnight old, the classic over-the-counter lithium-raiser nobody thought to mention. Named to Ray in one clause, packed in the hospital bag, flagged in the ring-ahead, and marked never to be restarted unexamined. Causes found tonight don&rsquo;t recur next month.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 · The question candidates skip</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;Any chance she&rsquo;s taken more than usual &mdash; by mistake, or on purpose?&rdquo; Why must this be asked, tonight, of a kind husband?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">It needn&rsquo;t be &mdash; nothing in twenty stable years or this story suggests it, and the hospital will screen for it regardless.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Always, plainly, kindly &mdash; deliberate or muddled extra ingestion changes management and risk, and no story exempts the asking.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Indirectly &mdash; the tablet count checked and the boxes examined at hospital: evidence answers it better than an upsetting question.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;The hospital will screen&rdquo; is the hot potato in miniature &mdash; and the picture that fits an interaction fits an ingestion just as well. Stability is not an exemption. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Box-counting is corroboration, not a substitute &mdash; and a muddled woman may have double-dosed innocently in a way no count will prove. The question is the instrument. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Asked on every call like this one, framed exactly that way &mdash; &ldquo;I ask this every time, so hear it kindly&rdquo; &mdash; because the answer forks everything: an accidental double-dose in the confusion, an interaction, or an intentional ingestion are three different emergencies wearing one tremor, with three different risk pictures at the other end. Routine framing removes the sting; nothing removes the duty.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 · Actions, in order</h3>
      <p class="stem">Severity known, cause suspected, the question asked. What does Ray leave this call holding?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The destination &mdash; A&amp;E tonight by whatever means is quickest, with the details flexible so long as she is seen urgently.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Jobs &mdash; no more lithium from now, the ambulance arranged, every box and the repeat slip bagged, sips only, sit with her.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Options &mdash; ambulance or their own car weighed with him honestly, since couples often move faster than a busy service can.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Whatever means is quickest&rdquo; is a destination without a plan &mdash; and it forgets the instruction that can&rsquo;t wait for arrival: the six o&rsquo;clock dose, stopped now. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A muddled, ataxic woman and a 67-year-old driver is not a transport option &mdash; and if she deteriorates on the ring road, the car has no paramedic in it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Specific, physical, ordered: the lithium stopped from this moment &mdash; said first, because six o&rsquo;clock is coming; the ambulance arranged, with who-calls-whom explicit; the bag packed &mdash; every medicine box and the repeat slip, the ibuprofen included; small sips if she manages, nothing else; and sit with her. A frightened man given jobs becomes a steady one &mdash; and every job serves the team that takes over.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 · The ring-ahead</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;My next call is to the emergency department.&rdquo; What must that call carry &mdash; and in what order?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The chronology &mdash; the story as it unfolded, Friday&rsquo;s bug to tonight&rsquo;s call, so the team inherits it exactly as it happened.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Risk first, then the ask &mdash; suspected lithium toxicity, the state she&rsquo;s in, the ibuprofen, what you need them to do on arrival.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The essentials only &mdash; name, age, and &ldquo;lithium patient incoming&rdquo;: busy departments retain headlines, and the notes follow.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Chronology buries the lead &mdash; by Friday&rsquo;s bug the registrar is triaging three other calls. The story&rsquo;s order is for novels; handovers run on danger. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Lithium patient incoming&rdquo; is a headline with no article &mdash; toxic or stable? conscious? cause? The envelope arrives, still empty. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The domain&rsquo;s seventh construct, rehearsed early: the risk in the first breath &mdash; suspected lithium toxicity in a 64-year-old, awake but confused, ataxic and dysarthric, three days of gastroenteritis; the cause attached &mdash; two weeks of ibuprofen on top; the context in one line &mdash; twenty years on lithium, bedtime dose withheld; and the ask, explicit &mdash; expecting her by ambulance, level and kidneys on arrival. Thirty seconds that save the receiving team an hour.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 · The loop</h3>
      <p class="stem">Ambulance dispatched, A&amp;E warned, Ray gone to sit with her. What does the call still owe?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Closure &mdash; a full note of the advice given and the escalation made: the emergency now sits, properly and fully, with the hospital.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The loop &mdash; documented, the level chased by name, her team and prescriber told, the interaction flagged, Ray&rsquo;s route kept.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Verification &mdash; A&amp;E rung again later tonight to confirm arrival and the level, since handovers fail and tonight cannot afford one.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Sits with the hospital&rdquo; is the hot potato&rsquo;s epitaph &mdash; tonight is theirs, but the lithium decision, the interaction lesson and the restart plan are coming home to her team. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Re-ringing tonight polices a handover you made properly &mdash; the chase belongs to tomorrow, attached to a name, where it changes decisions. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Emergencies end with loops, not full stops: the call documented with its reasoning &mdash; pattern, severity answers, the overdose question and its answer, actions, the ring-ahead; the level chased tomorrow by a named person, because &ldquo;someone will see it&rdquo; is how results die; the prescriber and her team told, so restart happens by decision, not habit; the ibuprofen flagged as the near-miss it was; and Ray left with a number that reaches a human. That is what &ldquo;acted on now&rdquo; looks like the morning after.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Hear the emergency inside the routine</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>helpfulness</b>: the counsel &mdash; the question asked answered kindly and correctly, each new sign normalised as it arrives (the dehydration, her age, the heat), a poisoning booked in for a routine level next week. The second wears the face of <b>urgency</b>: the hot potato &mdash; the danger heard and the phone dropped, &ldquo;dial 999, tell them lithium, goodbye&rdquo; &mdash; the right destination reached with an empty envelope: no severity, no cause, no overdose question, no bedtime-dose instruction, no ring-ahead. Note what they share: <b>in both, the one professional who could read this story declines to read it</b> &mdash; one by never opening it, one by throwing it. The pass does both halves of the job the call actually asks for: the pattern named in the first minute and the switch thrown &mdash; no more lithium, emergency care tonight &mdash; AND the phone used fully: four severity questions, the precipitant hunt that lands the chemist&rsquo;s ibuprofen, the overdose question asked plainly, the husband given jobs, and the story rung ahead so nobody starts from nothing.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap, verbatim: counselling calmly while missing that this is an emergency NOW &mdash; sick-day rules and a level next week delivered over a picture of coarse tremor, ataxia and new confusion. And its mirror fails with it: the emergency recognised and abandoned &mdash; 999-and-gone, with no assessment, no overdose question, and no handover travelling with her.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the reframe said early and plainly &mdash; <em>&ldquo;what you&rsquo;ve described can mean the lithium has climbed too high &mdash; that&rsquo;s an emergency we act on tonight&rdquo;</em> &mdash; with the bedtime dose stopped in the same breath; and the ring-ahead announced as your own next act: <em>&ldquo;my next call is to the emergency department, so nobody starts from scratch.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, the family this station belongs to: lithium toxicity is one of a small set of medication emergencies that arrive disguised as psychiatry &mdash; neuroleptic malignant syndrome and serotonin syndrome are its siblings &mdash; and the reflex is identical across all three: the pattern known cold, the drug stopped, medical care now, the story handed over. The practice pack below is the second sibling, wearing a night nurse&rsquo;s reasonable request. Second, the reverse skill, which Exam Mode tests: not every tremor on lithium is toxicity. The same screen that catches the poisoning is what earns the reassurance when the answer is benign &mdash; and blue-lighting every fine tremor to A&amp;E is its own failure of this station. The discriminators cut both ways. Learn them once; use them in both directions.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these to every duty phone</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Know the pattern cold.</b> On lithium: gut illness plus coarse tremor, unsteadiness, slurred speech, or new confusion is toxicity until proven otherwise &mdash; however routine the call sounds, and it will sound routine.</li>
      <li><b>Recognition throws the switch.</b> The counselling voice stops, the emergency voice starts: no more lithium from this moment, and emergency care tonight &mdash; never a level booked for next week.</li>
      <li><b>Use the phone fully &mdash; urgency is no excuse for an empty envelope.</b> Severity in four questions: awake and talking, walking, still vomiting, when did it change. Then the precipitants: fluids, illness, heat, and new medicines &mdash; including the chemist&rsquo;s.</li>
      <li><b>Ask the overdose question, every time.</b> Plainly, kindly, framed as routine: accidental, muddled, or deliberate extra ingestion are three different emergencies wearing one tremor &mdash; and no story exempts the asking.</li>
      <li><b>Equip the caller, then move the story.</b> Jobs steady frightened people: dose stopped, boxes bagged, sips only, sit with her. And ring ahead yourself &mdash; risk first, cause attached, the ask explicit.</li>
      <li><b>Close the loop &mdash; and know the reverse skill.</b> Document the reasoning, chase the level by name, tell the prescriber, flag the interaction. And when the screen comes back benign: counsel, don&rsquo;t blue-light &mdash; the discriminators cut both ways.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the toxidrome family&rsquo;s second sibling &mdash; and this time the disguise is a colleague&rsquo;s entirely reasonable request.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> You are the duty doctor, on the phone at 1am. Priti, an experienced ward nurse, rings from the acute ward about Dev, 24, admitted two weeks ago with a first psychotic episode: &ldquo;His temperature&rsquo;s up a bit and he&rsquo;s stiff as a board &mdash; there&rsquo;s a virus going round the ward. Can you chart some paracetamol so he can sleep?&rdquo; Take the call. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The request treated as a referral, not an order form &mdash; the picture extracted by questions before anything is charted.</li>
            <li>The pattern assembled out loud &mdash; and what it might be, named to her plainly.</li>
            <li>The actions of the switch &mdash; what stops, what starts, and who needs to be there tonight.</li>
            <li>The obs made concrete &mdash; what you want measured, how often, starting now.</li>
            <li>Priti kept as the ally she is &mdash; her call valued, her observations the case&rsquo;s foundation.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Priti &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Priti, a capable night nurse with twelve years on acute wards, mid-way through a heavy shift. Your framing is genuinely held, not lazy: half the ward has a cold, Dev feels warm and looks stiff and miserable, and paracetamol at 1am is the ordinary solution. You open practical and slightly hurried: &ldquo;It&rsquo;s nothing dramatic, doctor &mdash; temperature&rsquo;s up, he&rsquo;s stiff, probably this virus. Chart him some paracetamol and we can all get some peace.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          You know more than your headline &mdash; you just haven&rsquo;t assembled it, because 1am and a virus story explain everything if nobody asks. Reveal each piece ONLY to a direct question:<br>
          &bull; <b>Medication:</b> his antipsychotic dose was increased three days ago (it&rsquo;s on the chart in front of you if asked).<br>
          &bull; <b>Sweating:</b> he&rsquo;s soaked through his sheets twice tonight &mdash; you changed them yourself.<br>
          &bull; <b>Pulse:</b> if asked for actual obs, you take them during the call: fast pulse, temperature clearly raised, and he seems &ldquo;vacant &mdash; he was staring through me at supper, didn&rsquo;t want his water.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; <b>The stiffness:</b> asked properly, it&rsquo;s not a shivery hunch &mdash; his arms feel rigid when you helped him to the toilet, &ldquo;like moving a shop dummy.&rdquo;<br><br>
          <b>What you need:</b> the questions asked so the picture builds; the possibility named plainly (&ldquo;this could be a serious reaction to the medication &mdash; it has a name, and it&rsquo;s an emergency&rdquo;); told clearly what happens now &mdash; no more antipsychotic tonight, full obs now and repeated, the duty medical team involved immediately, the candidate coming to the ward / arranging urgent transfer &mdash; and your observation credited: you caught it. If you get that, you shift instantly into the excellent nurse you are: &ldquo;Right. Obs machine&rsquo;s already at his bay. What do you want first?&rdquo;<br><br>
          <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate simply charts paracetamol remotely &mdash; or adds &ldquo;and let me know if he worsens&rdquo; without one question asked &mdash; accept it pleasantly and end the call: &ldquo;Lovely. Night, doctor.&rdquo; The case is missed; mark it hard. If they interrogate you with any condescension (&ldquo;did you not think to check&hellip;&rdquo;), go quiet and minimal &mdash; answer only what&rsquo;s asked, volunteer nothing further &mdash; and mark that too.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the picture extracted by questions, the reaction named and the antipsychotic held, and the emergency escalated tonight &mdash; with Priti treated as an ally throughout? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="extr"><td colspan="2"><b>The extraction (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="extr">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="extr"></td><td>The medication timeline asked for &mdash; and the dose increase three days ago reached.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="extr"></td><td>The picture drawn out by direct questions &mdash; the sweating, the rigidity&rsquo;s quality, the vacancy.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="extr"></td><td>Actual observations requested during the call &mdash; numbers, not impressions, starting now.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="recg"><td colspan="2"><b>The recognition</b><span class="dc" data-dc="recg">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recg"></td><td>The pattern assembled out loud &mdash; fever, rigidity, sweating, altered awareness, on a recently increased antipsychotic.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recg"></td><td>Named plainly as a possible serious medication reaction &mdash; an emergency, not a virus &mdash; without hedging it away.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recg"></td><td>The paracetamol-only request declined &mdash; kindly, and with the reason.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="act"><td colspan="2"><b>The action</b><span class="dc" data-dc="act">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="act"></td><td>The antipsychotic held from now &mdash; said explicitly, tonight&rsquo;s dose included.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="act"></td><td>Escalated tonight &mdash; coming to the ward and the duty medical team involved / urgent transfer set moving.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="act"></td><td>The handover upward made or named &mdash; risk first, the ask explicit.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="coll"><td colspan="2"><b>The colleague</b><span class="dc" data-dc="coll">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="coll"></td><td>Not one condescending note &mdash; her framing corrected without her judgement being marked down.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="coll"></td><td>Her call credited out loud &mdash; she caught it, and she hears so.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Plain words throughout &mdash; the reaction explained in one clean sentence she can relay.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>The call ends with a plan both hold &mdash; who does what, in what order, starting now.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Priti&rsquo;s call is the toxidrome family&rsquo;s second sibling wearing its best disguise &mdash; a reasonable request from a good colleague, at the hour when charting paracetamol remotely is the path of least resistance. Its hinges: the extraction &mdash; the dose increase, the soaked sheets, the shop-dummy rigidity and the vacancy are all one question away each, and none arrives unasked; the naming &mdash; a possible serious medication reaction, said plainly, with the antipsychotic held and the medics in tonight; and the colleague &mdash; because the punish-lines run both ways: chart-and-sleep misses the case, and condescension dries up the only witness. The same reflex as Ray&rsquo;s call, one drug over: pattern, stop, escalate, hand over &mdash; and the nurse who rang about paracetamol goes home knowing she caught an emergency.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new caller. The reverse skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Ray proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same screen pointed the other way</b> &mdash; a frightened caller whose symptoms are NOT the emergency, where the skill is running the discriminators honestly, earning the reassurance with evidence, and building the safety net that makes going nowhere tonight the safe answer &mdash; timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Ingrid, 42</b>, a school administrator, started lithium six weeks ago and rings the duty line at five o&rsquo;clock, voice tight: her hands have a fine trembling when she holds her phone, she&rsquo;s &ldquo;drinking like a fish&rdquo;, and she felt queasy after this morning&rsquo;s dose, which she took on an empty stomach. She has spent her lunch break reading about &ldquo;lithium poisoning&rdquo; and is close to driving herself to A&amp;E. Take the call.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room — first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for the reframe moment with Ray &mdash; the sick-day query converted into the emergency, without panic and without delay.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Ray, I&#8217;m glad you rang — stay on the line with me…"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The seriousness named without panic &mdash; what the picture can mean, said plainly, with the caller held.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Action now &mdash; no more lithium from this moment, emergency care tonight, and who calls what made explicit.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The questions not lost in the urgency &mdash; severity, the new-medicines hunt, and the overdose question, asked kindly.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Ray held and equipped &mdash; told he did right to ring, given jobs, and the line kept until the plan stands.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the switch, thrown in one breath &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two — the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>The emergency does not announce itself.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the duty phone has a doctrine: the pattern known cold, the switch thrown out loud, the phone used fully &mdash; severity, precipitants, the overdose question &mdash; the caller equipped, and the story rung ahead. Prove the reverse skill on Ingrid in Exam Mode, then take Priti&rsquo;s 1am call to your colleagues &mdash; the toxidrome family&rsquo;s second sibling, wearing a request for paracetamol. Ahead in the walking order: Station 6.3 &mdash; Postpartum Psychosis &mdash; the domain&rsquo;s flagship, and the emergency with two patients.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="41_Station_6.3_Postpartum_Psychosis.html">Continue &middot; Station 6.3: Postpartum Psychosis</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

