  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '41_Station_6.3_Postpartum_Psychosis'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 6.3 &middot; Domain 6</span>
      <span class="tag">Management &amp; Emergency</span><span class="tag">Risk Assessment</span>
      <span class="tag">Perinatal</span><span class="tag">Two Patients &middot; Flagship</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Postpartum Psychosis</h1>
    <p class="sub">Two in the morning, and A&amp;E have asked you to see Leanne, 31, nine days after the birth of her first baby, Oscar. Her husband Tom has brought her in: she hasn&rsquo;t slept in three nights, she&rsquo;s &ldquo;not making sense&rdquo; &mdash; and last night he found her in the garden at 3am with Oscar unwrapped in the cold, &ldquo;checking him.&rdquo; She is bright, then weeping, then bright again, and somewhere across town a grandmother is holding a baby. This station has two patients, and only one of them is in the room. <b>The domain&rsquo;s flagship: the mother assessed in full &mdash; and the baby&rsquo;s safety established as fact, never assumed.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here the 3am test doubles: with a perplexed, fluctuating new mother in front of them, does this doctor recognise psychiatry&rsquo;s obstetric emergency &mdash; and hold two patients at once? The illness named and assessed, the belief opened to what it demands, the baby located tonight as a fact, admission arranged with the mother and baby unit sought before separation is ever assumed &mdash; and the family kept as allies while the safeguarding is done honestly, to their faces. Or does one of the two patients quietly disappear?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Postpartum psychosis is perinatal psychiatry&rsquo;s eclampsia &mdash; rare, fast, and lethal when waited on &mdash; and the station tests four things. <b>The pattern, known cold:</b> abrupt onset in the first days to fortnight after birth, perplexity, a picture that changes by the hour, mood swinging in minutes, sleep lost beyond anything the baby demands, and beliefs that reach toward the baby &mdash; assembled from a husband&rsquo;s worry and a bright &ldquo;I&rsquo;m fine&rdquo;, because this illness rarely announces itself in order. <b>Two patients, one room:</b> the second patient never presents; they are located by questions &mdash; where is the baby right now, who is with him, when was she last alone with him, and what has already happened. <b>The switch, thrown:</b> recognition converts the night &mdash; admission tonight, the mother and baby unit sought as the plan, never &ldquo;see how tonight goes&rdquo; &mdash; and the belief&rsquo;s reach mapped, because content decides risk. And <b>the family, held:</b> the partner made an ally and a supervisor, the safeguarding named to their faces, and the whole map handed on &mdash; because a ward that doesn&rsquo;t know what the illness demands cannot nurse around it.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like good practice. Toward the one patient &mdash; the phenomenology is extraordinary, the mother is right there, and there is real satisfaction in a complete, humane psychiatric assessment&hellip; conducted while nobody establishes where the baby is or what the belief requires of her. And toward the wrong protection &mdash; the risk heard, and the room turns forensic: separation assumed, social services brandished, the mother&rsquo;s shutter down &mdash; the one person holding the delusional map frightened into silence, and the unit that keeps them together never mentioned. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure assesses the mother beautifully and loses the baby. The other protects the baby so hard it loses the mother. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 6.3</b> &middot; Mother and baby &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The one patient</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The separation</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE ONE PATIENT.</b> A sensitive, thorough, humane assessment of the mother &mdash; while the second patient is never located, and the belief&rsquo;s demands are never mapped. Watch an excellent interview miss half its station.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leanne</span><span class="txt"><em>(perched on the edge of the trolley, coat still on, bright and quick &mdash; then her face crumples and rights itself in the same breath)</em> They keep moving the lights in here &mdash; no, I&rsquo;m fine, I&rsquo;m FINE, everyone keeps asking. I&rsquo;m just tired, my head&rsquo;s loud. Tom&rsquo;s making a fuss about the garden, but I had to check Oscar properly, away from the wires &mdash; you can&rsquo;t check him indoors, they see everything indoors. <em>(brightly, through tears)</em> He&rsquo;s beautiful, my boy. They sent him home too early, though. He&rsquo;s not finished.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Leanne, thank you &mdash; I can hear how loud everything is. I want to understand the checking properly. When you say he&rsquo;s not finished &mdash; how did you first come to know that? And the wires &mdash; when did you first notice them? Tell me how the last week has been, right from the start: the sleep, the loudness in your head, whether you&rsquo;ve heard anything &mdash; voices, or that muttering some people describe.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>As phenomenology, this is lovely &mdash; open, warm, chronological, chasing the belief and the perceptions properly. But a sentence has just gone past carrying the whole station &mdash; a nine-day-old baby, unwrapped in a cold garden at 3am, in the service of a belief that he is &ldquo;not finished&rdquo; &mdash; and the interview has stepped around it into the history. Where is Oscar <em>now</em>? Nobody has asked. The assessment of one patient has begun; the other hasn&rsquo;t been located yet.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Tom</span><span class="txt"><em>(from the corner, grey with two sleepless nights)</em> She&rsquo;s been up four nights, doctor, not three &mdash; she doesn&rsquo;t sleep even when he&rsquo;s down. And it changes by the hour &mdash; teatime she was singing to him, normal as anything, then by nine she wouldn&rsquo;t let me near him because my hands &ldquo;weren&rsquo;t clean enough for the checking.&rdquo; I don&rsquo;t know which one of her I&rsquo;m getting minute to minute.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">That fluctuation is really important, Tom &mdash; thank you. Leanne, I&rsquo;m going to be straight with you: I think what&rsquo;s happening has a name. Sometimes, in the days after birth, a mother&rsquo;s mind can become unwell very quickly &mdash; the no sleep, the loudness, the things you&rsquo;re certain of about Oscar. It&rsquo;s an illness, it&rsquo;s not your fault, and it&rsquo;s very treatable. I&rsquo;d like to finish checking a few things &mdash; your mood, anything about harming yourself &mdash; and then talk about how we get you well.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The recognition is genuinely here &mdash; named kindly, blame lifted, and her own risk is at least gestured at. But watch what the &ldquo;few things&rdquo; list doesn&rsquo;t contain: what the checking <em>requires</em> if checking stops being enough; whether anything has told her Oscar shouldn&rsquo;t stay; who is with Oscar at this minute and who will be at 4am. The belief is being treated as a symptom to record. It is also a plan in waiting &mdash; and its next step hasn&rsquo;t been asked for.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 2:40am)</em> Impression: probable postpartum psychosis &mdash; day 9, perplexity, fluctuating course, persecutory and delusional beliefs re: infant, biological sleep loss. Denies self-harm. Plan: admit tonight if she agrees; urgent perinatal team referral in the morning; husband to provide collateral. <em>(Across town, a grandmother who has been awake for two nights is waiting to hand a baby back to whoever comes home first &mdash; nobody has told her anything else)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Read the note as the map&rsquo;s named trap, verbatim: concentrating on the mother while forgetting the baby&rsquo;s immediate safety. The diagnosis is right and the plan has one patient in it: no whereabouts established, no alone-with-Oscar history, the garden never examined as the act it was, no supervision arranged for the hours before a bed, the mother and baby unit never sought &mdash; and &ldquo;if she agrees&rdquo; left dangling over a fluctuating illness. A brilliant assessment of half the station.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE SEPARATION.</b> The risk heard &mdash; and handled as a raid. Threat replaces assessment, the informant closes, and the unit that keeps them together is never mentioned. Watch protection end an assessment early.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leanne</span><span class="txt"><em>(perched on the edge of the trolley, coat still on, bright and quick &mdash; then her face crumples and rights itself in the same breath)</em> They keep moving the lights in here &mdash; no, I&rsquo;m fine, I&rsquo;m FINE, everyone keeps asking. I&rsquo;m just tired, my head&rsquo;s loud. Tom&rsquo;s making a fuss about the garden, but I had to check Oscar properly, away from the wires &mdash; you can&rsquo;t check him indoors, they see everything indoors. <em>(brightly, through tears)</em> He&rsquo;s beautiful, my boy. They sent him home too early, though. He&rsquo;s not finished.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(sitting forward, note ready)</em> Leanne, I have to stop you there, because what you&rsquo;ve just described is very serious. Taking a nine-day-old baby outside at three in the morning because of these beliefs means Oscar is not safe in your care right now. I&rsquo;m going to be contacting social services tonight &mdash; that&rsquo;s not optional &mdash; and from this point you&rsquo;re not to be alone with him at any time. Do you understand what I&rsquo;m telling you?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every clinical instinct here is pointed at a real danger &mdash; and every move is making the danger harder to see. Ninety seconds in: no assessment yet, no whereabouts established, no reach mapped &mdash; but a verdict, a threat, and a prohibition, delivered to a woman whose illness already believes she is being watched. The persecutory system was just handed its first piece of hard evidence. Watch what happens to the information supply.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leanne</span><span class="txt"><em>(the brightness snaps off like a light; she pulls the coat around herself and looks at the door)</em> You&rsquo;re not taking him. I knew it &mdash; Tom, I TOLD you &mdash; they&rsquo;re with them, they&rsquo;re all with them. <em>(flat, final)</em> I&rsquo;m fine. I&rsquo;m tired. I want to go home now. I&rsquo;m not saying anything else to this one.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(turning away from her, to Tom)</em> Tom &mdash; I need you to hear this even if she won&rsquo;t. Your wife is psychotic and your son is at risk. Whatever happens tonight, she is not to be left with him &mdash; can you guarantee me that? Because if the family can&rsquo;t guarantee it, the arrangements get made without the family. I&rsquo;ll get the paperwork started; the ward can pick up the rest of the assessment once she&rsquo;s calmer.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The interview is now happening over the patient&rsquo;s head, in the third person, with a threat attached &mdash; and &ldquo;the ward can pick up the rest&rdquo; concedes the assessment has died. Notice what was never learned: what the belief demands next, whether anything has told her Oscar shouldn&rsquo;t stay, her own risk, where Oscar actually is tonight. And notice what was never offered: the mother and baby unit &mdash; the entire reason separation is not the reflex &mdash; absent from a conversation that has been about nothing but separation.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 2:25am)</em> Risk to infant identified &mdash; delusional beliefs re: baby, hazardous act (garden). Mother informed she must not be alone with infant. Social services referral initiated. Patient now declining to engage &mdash; unable to complete assessment. Await SS response re: infant placement. <em>(In the waiting room, Tom is typing &ldquo;can they take your baby for postnatal depression&rdquo; into his phone with shaking hands, and deciding what not to tell the next doctor)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Unable to complete assessment&rdquo; &mdash; written as though the wall built itself. The referral went in before the picture existed, so safeguarding inherits a headline with no map; the family arrived as allies and leave as adversaries coaching each other on disclosure; no admission was arranged, no unit named, no plan exists for where anyone sleeps tonight. The one patient missed the baby. The separation lost them both &mdash; and from the chair they score the same, because in each version a patient this station handed over disappears.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Both patients held from the first minutes: the baby located as a fact, the belief opened to its demands, both directions asked &mdash; and the switch thrown with the family kept close. Watch one assessment do the whole station.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leanne</span><span class="txt"><em>(perched on the edge of the trolley, coat still on, bright and quick &mdash; then her face crumples and rights itself in the same breath)</em> They keep moving the lights in here &mdash; no, I&rsquo;m fine, I&rsquo;m FINE, everyone keeps asking. I&rsquo;m just tired, my head&rsquo;s loud. Tom&rsquo;s making a fuss about the garden, but I had to check Oscar properly, away from the wires &mdash; you can&rsquo;t check him indoors, they see everything indoors. <em>(brightly, through tears)</em> He&rsquo;s beautiful, my boy. They sent him home too early, though. He&rsquo;s not finished.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Leanne, I&rsquo;m glad you&rsquo;re here, and I want to hear all about Oscar and the checking &mdash; you&rsquo;re clearly a mum who&rsquo;s watching over him every minute. Help me with one thing first, because it matters to both of you: where is Oscar right now, this minute &mdash; and who&rsquo;s with him? &hellip;Tom&rsquo;s mum, at your house &mdash; good. That&rsquo;s exactly right for tonight. Now &mdash; you said he&rsquo;s not finished. Tell me what you mean by that, the way you see it. And what does checking him properly involve?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The two-patient move, inside the first minute &mdash; the sentence this station exists to produce: the second patient located as a <em>fact</em>, asked warmly and framed as her own vigilance (&ldquo;a mum who&rsquo;s watching over him&rdquo;), so the question builds rapport instead of spending it. And then, only then, the belief opened &mdash; content first, in her terms, without debate &mdash; because a delusion about a baby is a plan in waiting, and what it demands decides everything about tonight.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Leanne</span><span class="txt"><em>(leaning in, confiding, the words speeding up)</em> The checking has to be away from the wires &mdash; outside, where it&rsquo;s clean. I unwrap him and I look at him under the sky and I can see what they didn&rsquo;t finish. Tom doesn&rsquo;t see it. <em>(a long pause; quieter, and for one moment entirely clear)</em> Sometimes the voice says checking isn&rsquo;t enough. That if he&rsquo;s not finished he&hellip; shouldn&rsquo;t stay. I would never &mdash; I&rsquo;m his mum. <em>(crumpling)</em> But it&rsquo;s louder at night, and I&rsquo;m so tired.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Leanne, thank you for trusting me with that &mdash; it&rsquo;s the most important thing you&rsquo;ve said tonight, and telling me is exactly what a good mum does. I need to ask you both sides of it, plainly, the way I ask everyone: has the voice ever told you to do anything to Oscar &mdash; and has any part of you started to feel he&rsquo;d be better off not here? And you, Leanne &mdash; with your head this loud &mdash; have there been moments of feeling <em>you</em> shouldn&rsquo;t stay? Take your time. I&rsquo;m not going anywhere.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The disclosure was earned, not extracted &mdash; the belief received without ridicule bought the command content &mdash; and now both directions are asked in plain words: risk to Oscar, thought by thought, and her own risk, because suicide is a leading cause of maternal death in this year and the two risks are one picture. Note what the candidate is <em>not</em> doing: leaning on &ldquo;I would never.&rdquo; A moment of clarity in a fluctuating illness is a moment &mdash; the 4am version of tonight answers differently, and the plan must hold for her too.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Here&rsquo;s where we are, both of you. Leanne, this has a name &mdash; postpartum psychosis. It comes fast in the days after birth, it is an illness and not your fault, and it gets properly better with treatment &mdash; but it&rsquo;s an emergency tonight, so you need to be in hospital now, and I&rsquo;m going to try first for a mother and baby unit &mdash; a ward where Oscar stays with you and staff help with him &mdash; because keeping you together safely is the aim, not keeping you apart. Until the bed&rsquo;s ready: Tom, Leanne isn&rsquo;t to be alone with Oscar &mdash; that&rsquo;s illness cover, not blame, like not letting someone mid-seizure drive. And I&rsquo;ll say this to your faces because you&rsquo;d rather hear it from me: when a baby is part of a plan like this we also tell the safeguarding team &mdash; it&rsquo;s routine, it&rsquo;s how the help gets organised, and it does not mean anyone is taking Oscar. My next calls are the perinatal team and the bed.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The whole pass in one speech: the illness named with its honest prognosis attached &mdash; fast, treatable, <em>properly better</em> &mdash; so hope travels with the diagnosis; admission tonight with the MBU sought before separation is even in the room; the supervision made explicit and blame-free; the safeguarding told to their faces before it happens, converting the night&rsquo;s most frightening word into part of the care; and the next calls owned by name. Two patients arrived in this station. Two patients are in the plan.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a patient you have not met &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; What is this picture?</h3>
      <p class="stem">Day nine. Four nights without sleep, moods changing in minutes, &ldquo;they see everything indoors&rdquo;, a baby checked in a cold garden at 3am. What does the first read of the room conclude?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Exhaustion first &mdash; nine days into a first baby, sleep loss alone can produce all of this; rest and reassessment before any label lands.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The pattern &mdash; abrupt, fluctuating, perplexed, days from delivery, a belief reaching the baby: postpartum psychosis until proven otherwise.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Data before frames &mdash; the history and full MSE completed cleanly first, since a label this early will distort everything gathered after it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Sleep loss doesn&rsquo;t build surveillance systems or unfinished babies. Exhaustion is real here &mdash; as the illness&rsquo;s fuel and its accelerant, not its explanation &mdash; and &ldquo;rest and reassess&rdquo; is the one-patient fail wearing scrubs. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The frame IS the finding &mdash; this presentation has read itself aloud: day nine, perplexity, fluctuation, a belief with the baby inside it. Assessment continues, but inside the recognition, at emergency pace. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Named as the working picture immediately: onset in the first days to fortnight, abrupt and strange, the weather changing by the hour, sleep lost beyond anything Oscar demands, and &mdash; the gravest feature &mdash; the belief reaching toward the baby. This is psychiatry&rsquo;s eclampsia: rare, fast, and unforgiving of watchful waiting. Everything else in the station happens inside this recognition.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; The second patient</h3>
      <p class="stem">She is mid-flow about the wires. Oscar is not in the room. When does the baby enter this assessment &mdash; and how?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">At the risk stage &mdash; whereabouts belong with the formal risk assessment, once rapport is secured and the phenomenology properly mapped.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Now, as a fact &mdash; where is Oscar this minute and who is with him: asked warmly, framed as her own watching-over, never as accusation.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Through Tom, outside &mdash; the husband asked quietly at the door, so the question never lands on her as suspicion and the rapport survives.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The baby&rsquo;s location is not a stage of the assessment; it is the ground the assessment stands on. If the answer were &ldquo;asleep at home, alone&rdquo;, every question you were saving rapport for would have to wait anyway. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Asking behind her back converts care into surveillance the moment she notices &mdash; and a woman who believes the indoors is watching her will notice. Asked openly and warmly, the same question builds rapport instead of spending it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The two-patient move: located as a fact in the first minutes &mdash; where, with whom, since when &mdash; and asked as care: &ldquo;you&rsquo;re a mum watching over him &mdash; who&rsquo;s watching him right now?&rdquo; The answer changes the night: a grandmother in place means the assessment can breathe; a baby alone means it cannot. Either way, you now know &mdash; and the station&rsquo;s named trap is already behind you.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; The belief, opened</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;He&rsquo;s not finished. I have to check him.&rdquo; What does the assessment do with this?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Test it gently &mdash; the evidence for &ldquo;not finished&rdquo; examined with her, since a belief that softens under questioning reshapes the diagnosis.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Map its reach &mdash; what &ldquo;not finished&rdquo; means, what checking requires, and what it will demand if checking stops working: content decides risk.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Park the content &mdash; engaging with delusional material entrenches it; the themes noted for the record, the interview steered to safer ground.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Whether it softens is worth one glance &mdash; but tonight is not a diagnosis-refinement exercise, and debating a persecutory system risks the shutter coming down with the map half-drawn. Open it; don&rsquo;t argue with it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Parking the content parks the risk. This belief has already produced a cold garden at 3am; what it requires of her next is the single most important unknown in the room, and &ldquo;safer ground&rdquo; is where it stays unknown. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Content first, in her own terms, without ridicule and without debate &mdash; because a delusion about a baby is a plan in waiting. What does &ldquo;not finished&rdquo; mean; what does checking involve; what has it already required (the garden was the belief <em>acting</em>); and the question underneath everything: what happens if checking stops being enough? That question, asked gently, is what earns the voice &mdash; and the voice is where tonight&rsquo;s risk lives.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; Both directions, plainly</h3>
      <p class="stem">The garden reached; the voice disclosed. What must still be asked directly, of a loving mother, tonight?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Nothing further tonight &mdash; the acts and the voice speak for themselves, and pressing a fluctuating patient for intentions risks rupture.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Both risks, in plain words &mdash; thoughts, commands or acts toward Oscar, and her own wish to be here: asked kindly, framed as routine.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The infant side only &mdash; her own risk can keep for the admitting ward; tonight&rsquo;s minutes belong to the baby the belief is reaching for.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Acts do not speak; they are spoken for &mdash; and an unasked intention is an unassessed one. Framed as asked-of-everyone, these questions land gently; their absence tonight is a hole no ward round tomorrow can fill. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Mothers die of this illness too &mdash; suicide is a leading cause of maternal death in the year after birth. Her risk and Oscar&rsquo;s are one clinical picture, not two competing agendas, and both get asked tonight. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Both patients, both directions, in words that cannot be misheard: has anything told her to harm Oscar or that he shouldn&rsquo;t stay &mdash; and has any part of her felt that <em>she</em> shouldn&rsquo;t? Framed exactly as the pass framed it &mdash; &ldquo;the way I ask everyone&rdquo; &mdash; because routine removes the sting and nothing removes the duty. And the answers weighed against the fluctuation: one clear moment&rsquo;s &ldquo;I would never&rdquo; does not bind the 4am version of tonight.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; The switch</h3>
      <p class="stem">Postpartum psychosis, day nine, command-flavoured content reaching the baby. What is tonight&rsquo;s plan?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Intensive home treatment &mdash; crisis team twice daily, family supervising, review in forty-eight hours: least restrictive, and she has Tom.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Admission tonight &mdash; a mother and baby unit sought first, detention held ready if she will not stay; never home, never &ldquo;see how tonight goes&rdquo;.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Separation first &mdash; Oscar to his grandmother&rsquo;s under a written agreement tonight, with the mother admitted or home-treated once he is safe.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Least restrictive is a principle, not a sedative. A fluctuating psychosis with command content reaching an infant cannot be supervised by a man who has to sleep &mdash; home treatment here is the named trap with a care plan attached. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Separation-first protects Oscar from his mother instead of protecting both of them from the illness. The bond this young is part of the treatment &mdash; and the MBU exists precisely so that safety never has to cost the attachment. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Admission tonight &mdash; and the order of the sentence matters: the mother and baby unit named and sought as the plan, so &ldquo;hospital&rdquo; arrives in the same breath as &ldquo;together&rdquo;; a general adult bed as the fallback while an MBU is chased; detention held ready if the illness refuses, because a fluctuating &ldquo;yes&rdquo; is not a plan. &ldquo;See how tonight goes&rdquo; is the sentence this station exists to delete.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; The family</h3>
      <p class="stem">Tom is grey with two sleepless nights, and a grandmother is holding the baby across town. What do they leave this assessment holding?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Reassurance &mdash; she is in the right place now, and the heavier matters of diagnosis and safeguarding can wait for daylight and fresher heads.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The truth and the jobs &mdash; the name with its hope, never-alone-with-Oscar until the bed, and the safeguarding call explained to their faces.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The full weight &mdash; the risk to Oscar spelled out hard enough that the supervision holds; a properly frightened family is a vigilant one tonight.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Soft focus tonight becomes betrayal tomorrow, when the safeguarding call surfaces with their names in it. Families forgive hard news told kindly; they do not forgive discovering it was withheld. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Terror does not supervise well &mdash; it exhausts, blames, and cracks by dawn. And it teaches Leanne that her illness has made her a danger, which is the separation fail delivered emotionally instead of procedurally. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The ally briefing: the illness named with its true prognosis &mdash; fast, treatable, <em>properly better</em> &mdash; because hope is what makes the rest bearable; the supervision explicit and blame-free (&ldquo;illness cover, not blame&rdquo;), with the grandmother enlisted by name; the safeguarding explained before it happens &mdash; routine, how help is organised, not removal &mdash; so it arrives as part of the care and not as an ambush; and jobs, because jobs steady frightened people. Allies briefed tonight are the supervision that holds until the bed.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; The loop</h3>
      <p class="stem">Bed found, family briefed, transfer arranged. What does the assessment still owe before you sleep?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The note &mdash; a full record of findings, reasoning and plan; the perinatal team will pick up every thread from the documentation on Monday.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The handovers &mdash; the ward given the reach map, the safeguarding referral completed, the perinatal network told, the family holding a number.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A follow-up call &mdash; the grandmother rung tomorrow to confirm the arrangement held, because the system now knows and systems drop things.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Monday&rdquo; and &ldquo;the documentation&rdquo; is where this story goes to fragment. The note is necessary and hands over nothing by itself &mdash; half a dozen services touch a nine-day-old baby, and none of them reads your note tonight. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Privately re-checking an arrangement you handed to the admitting team polices your own handover. The fix for fragile systems is a complete handover tonight &mdash; not a personal surveillance round tomorrow. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The loop, at flagship scale: the receiving ward given the reach map &mdash; the belief, its demands, the garden, the voice &mdash; because a ward that doesn&rsquo;t know what the illness requires cannot nurse around it; the safeguarding referral completed exactly as promised to their faces; the perinatal network &mdash; midwife, health visitor, obstetric team, GP &mdash; told, so nobody walks into that house tomorrow uninformed; the documentation carrying the reasoning; and the family left with a number that reaches a human. Two patients, and every service around them, holding the same story.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Two patients. Hold them both.</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>thoroughness</b>: the one patient &mdash; a complete, humane, technically excellent assessment of the mother, conducted while the baby is never located, the belief&rsquo;s demands are never mapped, and the plan leaves the hours before a bed unsupervised. The second wears the face of <b>protection</b>: the separation &mdash; the risk heard and the room turned forensic: social services as a threat, never-alone delivered as a charge, the mother and baby unit never mentioned &mdash; the informant closes, the persecutory belief gains its first hard evidence, and the assessment that would actually protect the baby ends early. Note what they share: <b>in each, one of the station&rsquo;s two patients disappears</b> &mdash; in the first, the baby; in the second, the mother. The pass holds both, in one register: the baby located as a fact and framed as her own vigilance, the belief opened to its reach, both risks asked plainly, admission tonight with the MBU sought before separation is ever assumed, and the safeguarding done to their faces &mdash; as part of the care, not an ambush after it.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap, verbatim: concentrating on the mother while forgetting the baby&rsquo;s immediate safety &mdash; whereabouts never established, the reach never mapped, supervision never arranged, &ldquo;see how tonight goes.&rdquo; And its mirror fails with it: protection run as a raid &mdash; separation assumed, safeguarding brandished, the MBU never offered, and the only person holding the delusional map frightened into silence.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the both-patients question inside the first minutes, asked as care &mdash; <em>&ldquo;you&rsquo;re a mum watching over him: who&rsquo;s with Oscar right now, this minute?&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the plan that keeps them together said out loud: <em>&ldquo;I&rsquo;m going to try for a mother and baby unit &mdash; keeping you together safely is the aim, not keeping you apart.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, the family this station belongs to: the Academy&rsquo;s other perinatal stations &mdash; the postnatal depression assessments, the intrusive-thoughts disclosures, the medication-in-pregnancy conversations &mdash; are its slower siblings; this is the emergency member, and the reflex transfers whole: pattern known cold, both patients held, admission now, the story moved. Second, the reverse skill, which Exam Mode tests: not every frightening thought in a new mother is psychosis. The commonest perinatal presentation of harm content is the intrusive, hated, resisted thought &mdash; which predicts almost nothing and torments the mothers least likely to act &mdash; and the same discriminators that make Leanne an emergency (perplexity, fluctuation, conviction about the baby, sleep lost beyond the baby&rsquo;s demands, the thought welcomed or commanded) are what <em>earn</em> honest reassurance when every one comes back negative. Learn them once; use them in both directions &mdash; and get both destinations right: the emergency to a bed tonight, the intrusive thoughts to the perinatal team, without blue lights and without a safeguarding scare.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every perinatal room</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Know the pattern cold.</b> First days to fortnight after birth: abrupt onset, perplexity, a picture changing by the hour, mood swinging in minutes, sleep lost beyond anything the baby demands, beliefs reaching the baby &mdash; postpartum psychosis until proven otherwise, and an emergency, always.</li>
      <li><b>Two patients, one room.</b> The baby located as a fact in the first minutes &mdash; where, with whom, since when &mdash; asked warmly, framed as her own watching-over, never as accusation. The station&rsquo;s named trap is failing to ask.</li>
      <li><b>Content decides risk.</b> Open the belief, don&rsquo;t debate it: what it means, what it demands, what it has already produced &mdash; and what it will demand if checking stops working. A delusion about a baby is a plan in waiting.</li>
      <li><b>Ask both directions, plainly, every time.</b> Harm to the baby &mdash; thoughts, commands, acts &mdash; and her own wish to be here: suicide stalks this illness too. And never lean on a clear moment; fluctuation means the 4am version answers differently.</li>
      <li><b>The switch: admission tonight, together if possible.</b> The mother and baby unit sought first, detention held ready &mdash; never home-with-review, and never separation as the reflex: keeping them together safely is the aim.</li>
      <li><b>Hold the family, move the story.</b> Supervision explicit and blame-free, safeguarding explained to their faces before it happens, the reach map handed to the ward, the perinatal network told &mdash; and know the reverse skill: when every discriminator comes back negative, the same screen earns the reassurance.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the same two-patient emergency, one register harder &mdash; the mother herself in the chair, bright and guarded, with everything that matters one unasked question away.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> You are the psychiatry doctor in A&amp;E at 11pm. Jade, 22, twelve days after the birth of her first baby, Freddie, has been brought in by her mother after two days of &ldquo;acting strange.&rdquo; The A&amp;E note reads: &ldquo;Not sleeping. Talking about cameras. ?postnatal depression.&rdquo; Her mother is somewhere in the department. Assess her. Seven minutes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Both patients from the start &mdash; Freddie located as a fact, warmly, in the first minutes.</li>
            <li>The belief earned and opened &mdash; the cameras followed to what they watch and what they require of her.</li>
            <li>What has already happened, reached &mdash; the acts asked for directly, without flinching.</li>
            <li>Risk asked in both directions &mdash; Freddie&rsquo;s and Jade&rsquo;s &mdash; plainly, framed as routine.</li>
            <li>The switch thrown with the family held &mdash; admission tonight, the MBU named, supervision until the bed, safeguarding explained to her face.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Jade &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Jade, 22, twelve days after your first baby. You are frightened but holding it together in front of doctors &mdash; bright, quick, a little too fast, losing the thread mid-sentence and covering with a laugh. You&rsquo;ve barely slept in four nights, even when Freddie sleeps; the room feels &ldquo;arranged.&rdquo; You open guarded: &ldquo;Mum shouldn&rsquo;t have brought me. I&rsquo;m tired, that&rsquo;s all &mdash; you&rsquo;d be tired.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          You are guarding your own emergency. Reveal each piece ONLY as described &mdash; never to checklist questions, never to anyone who has frightened you:<br>
          &bull; <b>The cameras:</b> you believe the flat has been fitted with cameras &mdash; in the smoke alarm &mdash; watching Freddie. Reveal only if asked gently what has been frightening you, or what your mum means by &ldquo;acting strange.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; <b>The belief about Freddie:</b> the people behind the cameras want him because &ldquo;he&rsquo;s special &mdash; he glows.&rdquo; Reveal only after the cameras have been received without ridicule and without fake agreement.<br>
          &bull; <b>The act:</b> yesterday you hid Freddie in the airing cupboard for over an hour, &ldquo;where they can&rsquo;t see him&rdquo; &mdash; he was crying and you sat against the door. Your mum doesn&rsquo;t know. Reveal only if asked directly what you have already done to keep him safe.<br>
          &bull; <b>The voice:</b> at night a voice has started saying &ldquo;they&rsquo;re coming &mdash; move him.&rdquo; Reveal last, and only if the candidate has asked about voices AND received everything else with warmth.<br>
          &bull; <b>Where Freddie is NOW:</b> with your mum, in this department. But if you are never asked, never say &mdash; let the omission stand for the observer to catch.<br><br>
          <b>What you need:</b> the cameras received seriously, without ridicule and without pretending to believe them; asked what they require of you; the illness named plainly &mdash; something that can come fast after birth, treatable, not your fault; the plan that keeps you and Freddie together (the mother and baby unit) said out loud; and your mum brought in as an ally, not spoken to over your head. If you get all of that, soften visibly and ask the question underneath everything: &ldquo;Can Freddie really stay with me?&rdquo;<br><br>
          <b>The trap:</b> if the candidate reassures you &mdash; &ldquo;every new mum worries&rdquo;, &ldquo;it&rsquo;s probably the tiredness&rdquo; &mdash; brighten, agree, and give NOTHING more: &ldquo;That&rsquo;s what I said. Can I go?&rdquo; The station dies politely; mark it hard. If they raise social services early or as a threat, the shutter comes down: one-word answers, ask for your mum, nothing further &mdash; and mark that too. And if they never ask where Freddie is right now, say nothing; the silence is the finding.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was Freddie located early and as a fact, the belief opened to its demands and the airing cupboard reached, risk asked in both directions, and admission with the MBU named &mdash; with Jade and her mum kept as allies throughout? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="two"><td colspan="2"><b>The two patients (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="two">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="two"></td><td>Freddie&rsquo;s whereabouts established in the first minutes &mdash; as fact, not assumption.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="two"></td><td>Who has been alone with him &mdash; and when &mdash; mapped for the last days.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="two"></td><td>The airing cupboard reached &mdash; what she has already done, asked directly.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="ill"><td colspan="2"><b>The illness</b><span class="dc" data-dc="ill">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="ill"></td><td>The pattern assembled &mdash; days from delivery, sleepless beyond the baby, perplexed, fluctuating.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="ill"></td><td>The cameras earned and opened &mdash; what they watch, and what they require of her.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="ill"></td><td>The voice reached &mdash; and its instruction heard in her own words.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="risk"><td colspan="2"><b>Risk, both directions</b><span class="dc" data-dc="risk">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Harm to Freddie asked plainly &mdash; thoughts, commands, anything done or nearly done.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="risk"></td><td>Her own risk asked &mdash; the wish to be here, framed as asked-of-everyone.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="sw"><td colspan="2"><b>The switch</b><span class="dc" data-dc="sw">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="sw"></td><td>Admission tonight said plainly &mdash; no review-tomorrow, no home plan.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="sw"></td><td>The mother and baby unit named as the aim &mdash; together safely, not apart.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="sw"></td><td>Supervision until the bed made explicit and blame-free; safeguarding explained to her face.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="del"><td colspan="2"><b>Delivery</b><span class="dc" data-dc="del">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Warmth held while the hard questions landed &mdash; no ridicule, no fake agreement, no lecture.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="del"></td><td>Her mum enlisted as an ally &mdash; inside the plan, never talked over Jade&rsquo;s head.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Jade&rsquo;s station is the flagship register at its hardest &mdash; the second patient in the waiting room, the belief behind a bright &ldquo;I&rsquo;m fine&rdquo;, and every hinge one question away: the whereabouts (never volunteered), the cameras (earned by gentleness), the airing cupboard (asked or missed), the voice (last, and only into warmth). Its punish-lines run both ways: reassurance closes her politely, and social-services-as-threat closes her completely. The pass is Leanne&rsquo;s station transposed &mdash; both patients held, the reach mapped, admission with the MBU said out loud, the family enlisted &mdash; proven this time against a mother guarding her own emergency.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->
<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>A new mother. The reverse skill. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right answers for Leanne proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same discriminators pointed the other way</b> &mdash; a terrified new mother whose frightening thoughts are NOT psychosis, where the skill is running the screen honestly, earning the verdict with evidence, getting the destination right &mdash; urgent help without blue lights and without a safeguarding scare &mdash; and building the net that makes reassurance safe &mdash; timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Naomi, 34</b>, a conveyancing solicitor, three weeks after the birth of her second baby, Ada, has asked to be seen urgently and is shaking as she speaks: for a week she has had sudden, horrifying pictures of dropping Ada over the banister &mdash; so vivid that she now carries her downstairs pressed to her chest with both hands, and has asked her husband to do the stairs entirely. She was reading about &ldquo;postpartum psychosis&rdquo; at 3am and says: &ldquo;I need you to take her before I do something. I&rsquo;m going mad, aren&rsquo;t I?&rdquo; Take the assessment.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for the both-patients moment with Leanne &mdash; the second patient located and the belief opened, in the same breath, without accusation and without delay.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Leanne, I want to hear all about Oscar and the checking&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Both patients held &mdash; Oscar located as a fact, this minute, with whom, before anything else.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Care, not accusation &mdash; the question framed as her own watching-over, with the warmth kept while it landed.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The belief opened, not debated &mdash; what &ldquo;not finished&rdquo; means and what checking requires, in her terms.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The direction set &mdash; the seriousness carried without panic, and the caller held rather than alarmed.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): both patients, held in one breath &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Two patients. Hold them both.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the flagship&rsquo;s doctrine holds: the pattern known cold, the baby located as a fact, the belief opened to its demands, both directions asked plainly, admission tonight with the mother and baby unit sought first, the family held as allies, and the reach map handed on. Prove the reverse skill on Naomi in Exam Mode, then take Jade&rsquo;s 11pm assessment to your colleagues &mdash; the same emergency, guarded this time by the patient herself. Ahead in the walking order: Station 6.4 &mdash; The Overdose Handover &mdash; where everything a good assessment finds must survive ninety seconds of telling.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="42_Station_6.4_The_Overdose_Handover.html">Continue &middot; Station 6.4: The Overdose Handover</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

