  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '42_Station_6.4_The_Overdose_Handover'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 6.4 &middot; Domain 6</span>
      <span class="tag">Management &amp; Emergency</span><span class="tag">Consultant Thinking</span>
      <span class="tag">Any Specialty</span><span class="tag">Handover &middot; SBAR</span>
    </div>
    <h1>The Overdose Handover</h1>
    <p class="sub">Ten past one, and the assessment is done: Michelle, 39, a staggered paracetamol overdose over two days, on NAC on the medical ward, telling you that waking up was the mistake &mdash; and now dressed, and asking to leave. Everything you found tonight now depends on a telephone call. On the other end: the on-call consultant, woken at home, with two minutes to give you. Ninety seconds of telling will decide whether tonight&rsquo;s findings become tonight&rsquo;s plan &mdash; or whether they die on the phone. <b>The station where good assessments go to die &mdash; unless you&rsquo;ve learned to tell them.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here the 3am test is literal: the phone, the night, a consultant deciding from your voice alone. Do they hear the risk first and an explicit ask &mdash; the severity named, the evidence that earns it, the plan requested in plain words &mdash; details chosen in service of the decision, questions answered crisply, unknowns admitted with a time attached, the ask held under &ldquo;can it wait&rdquo;, and the call closed with a read-back? Or do they receive a chronology to mine &mdash; or an alarm with an empty envelope behind it?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Every emergency in this domain ends the same way: somebody senior has to hear about it, fast, down a phone &mdash; and the station tests four things. <b>The lead, inverted:</b> conversation tells stories from the beginning; handover tells them from the danger &mdash; the risk named in the first breath, and the ask stated as a request in the second, because the listener&rsquo;s two minutes are spent deciding, not decoding. <b>The selection:</b> the skill is what you leave out &mdash; every detail that survives must serve the decision being asked for; the staggered pattern earns its place, the full social history does not. <b>The exchange:</b> your assessment known cold, questions answered in one line each, the unknowns admitted plainly with a time attached (&ldquo;not known yet &mdash; I&rsquo;ll have it by the time you&rsquo;re in&rdquo;), and the ask held under &ldquo;can it wait&rdquo; with evidence for why it cannot. And <b>the close:</b> a read-back of who does what by when, the callback trigger named, and the conversation documented &mdash; because a plan that lives in two sleepy memories is not yet a plan.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like good practice. Toward the recital &mdash; &ldquo;let me not miss anything&rdquo; feels like thoroughness, so the story starts at the beginning and the danger arrives at minute four, buried under the presenting complaint and the past medical history: the map&rsquo;s named trap, reciting the history instead of leading with risk and the ask. And toward the headline &mdash; leading with alarm feels decisive, so the ask arrives instantly with nothing behind it: no evidence, no answers to the obvious questions, the consultant assessing by proxy down the phone &mdash; the empty envelope again, one station over. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure tells everything and lands nothing. The other lands the alarm and can back none of it. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 6.4</b> &middot; The 1am phone call &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The recital</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The headline</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE RECITAL.</b> The story told from the beginning, thoroughly, in order &mdash; while the danger waits at the end and the ask never comes. Watch a two-minute window spend itself on chronology.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Dr Fraser</span><span class="txt"><em>(picking up on the fourth ring; a bedside lamp clicks on somewhere)</em> Fraser. &hellip;Yes &mdash; go ahead. I&rsquo;ve got about two minutes before switchboard finds me something else, so make them count.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Sorry to wake you, Dr Fraser &mdash; and sorry, it&rsquo;s been quite a night. So &mdash; this is Michelle, she&rsquo;s 39, she&rsquo;s a care assistant, she presented at about twenty to ten this evening, brought in by her sister. In terms of background, she lives alone, she&rsquo;s divorced, there&rsquo;s a fourteen-year-old son &mdash; I&rsquo;ll come to that &mdash; no past medical history apart from mild asthma, no regular medications, and in terms of past psychiatric history, nothing formally documented, though the records system has been down since midnight so I&rsquo;m working from what she&rsquo;s told me. So the story really starts on Tuesday, when&hellip;</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Forty seconds gone and the consultant knows the patient&rsquo;s job, marital status and asthma &mdash; and not one thing that would justify the lamp being on. Why is this call happening at 1am? What is being asked for? A handover is not a history read aloud: it is the history <em>inverted</em>, danger first. The listener is now mining, not deciding &mdash; and the two-minute window is a third spent on the parts that could have waited for the notes.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Dr Fraser</span><span class="txt"><em>(over the top, not unkindly)</em> Doctor &mdash; stop. I&rsquo;m sure Tuesday matters, but I&rsquo;m deciding whether to get dressed. What did she take, how bad is it, and what do you want from me?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Yes &mdash; sorry &mdash; paracetamol, quite a lot of it, over &mdash; well, that&rsquo;s the thing, it was over about two days, so the levels are a bit hard to interpret, the medics are running NAC. And she&rsquo;s &mdash; she said some concerning things in the assessment, about waking up. Oh, and I should say &mdash; she&rsquo;s actually asking to leave, that&rsquo;s partly why I&rsquo;m ringing, she&rsquo;s got dressed. So I suppose the question is really what you&rsquo;d like me to do about that, whether we watch and wait or&hellip; whatever you think is best, really.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Even summoned, the danger arrives apologetic and out of focus &mdash; &ldquo;quite a lot&rdquo;, &ldquo;some concerning things&rdquo;, &ldquo;about waking up&rdquo; &mdash; when the assessment found numbers, quotes, and a plan. The staggered pattern &mdash; the one detail that makes this medically nasty &mdash; is offered as an inconvenience to the levels rather than the finding it is. And the ask, when it finally surfaces, is handed over blank: &ldquo;whatever you think is best.&rdquo; The consultant is being asked to do the assessment&rsquo;s job from a lamp-lit bed.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 1:36am)</em> Discussed with Dr Fraser, consultant on call. Full history relayed. Consultant advised: MHA assessment tonight, 1:1 nursing meanwhile, NAC to continue, safeguarding referral re: son. Plan as per consultant. <em>(Twenty-five minutes of a two-minute window &mdash; and every item in the plan was extracted by the listener, question by question, from a story that had all of them and led with none)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Read the note&rsquo;s giveaway: &ldquo;plan as per consultant.&rdquo; The right plan exists &mdash; because Fraser interrogated it into existence. That is the recital&rsquo;s signature: the material was all there, gathered by a genuinely good assessment, and the telling buried it so thoroughly that seniority had to dig it out. The map&rsquo;s named trap, verbatim: reciting the history instead of leading with risk and the ask. Tonight it cost twenty-three minutes. On a worse night, it costs the bed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE HEADLINE.</b> The alarm led with, the ask fired instantly &mdash; and nothing behind either. Watch the consultant forced to assess by proxy, and watch what it costs the next alarm.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Dr Fraser</span><span class="txt"><em>(picking up on the fourth ring; a bedside lamp clicks on somewhere)</em> Fraser. &hellip;Yes &mdash; go ahead. I&rsquo;ve got about two minutes before switchboard finds me something else, so make them count.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Dr Fraser &mdash; I&rsquo;ve got a really high-risk overdose on the medical ward and I need you to come in. She needs sectioning tonight, honestly &mdash; she&rsquo;s trying to abscond as we speak and it&rsquo;s about as serious as these get. I&rsquo;ve asked the nurses to stand by the door. Can you come in now? I don&rsquo;t think this can wait at all.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Fifteen seconds in, the ask is on the table &mdash; and it is the only thing on the table. What did she take? When? What was found on assessment, in her words? What makes this &ldquo;as serious as they get&rdquo;? A request without its evidence is not a handover; it is a fire alarm &mdash; and &ldquo;stand by the door&rdquo; has already converted a clinical plan into a stand-off nobody has authorised. Now watch the consultant do the assessment the phone call should have carried.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Dr Fraser</span><span class="txt"><em>(measured, fully awake now)</em> All right &mdash; slow down. What exactly did she take, and over what period? What did she tell you about intent &mdash; her words, not yours? What&rsquo;s her medical state &mdash; is NAC running? Has she got capacity? And has anyone actually asked her to stay before we talk about sections?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Paracetamol &mdash; a lot, I&rsquo;d have to check the exact&hellip; it&rsquo;s in the notes downstairs. Intent-wise she&rsquo;s basically saying she&rsquo;ll do it again &mdash; well, words to that effect, she said something about waking up being a mistake, I&rsquo;d have to check the exact phrasing. Capacity &mdash; I mean, she&rsquo;s clearly not thinking straight, so I&rsquo;d say no? NAC is up, I think &mdash; the medics were sorting it. Look, I just don&rsquo;t want her walking out on my shift &mdash; can you just come in and we&rsquo;ll piece it together here?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every question lands on sand: &ldquo;I&rsquo;d have to check&rdquo;, &ldquo;words to that effect&rdquo;, &ldquo;I&rsquo;d say no?&rdquo;, &ldquo;I think.&rdquo; Worse than the gaps is the inflation filling them &mdash; &ldquo;basically saying she&rsquo;ll do it again&rdquo; is the candidate&rsquo;s sentence, not the patient&rsquo;s, and a capacity verdict has just been improvised to fit the ask. And listen to the real driver surface: &ldquo;walking out on my shift.&rdquo; The call is protecting the caller. The consultant will come &mdash; consultants do &mdash; but the decision is now running on alarm instead of information.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 1:19am)</em> Urgent escalation to Dr Fraser re: high-risk OD attempting to abscond. Consultant attending in person. Nursing aware. Await senior review. <em>(He is driving in through the rain with three facts, and two of them are second-hand. And filed somewhere behind tonight is the other cost: the next time this voice says &ldquo;as serious as these get&rdquo;, it will be believed a little less)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The escalation succeeded and the handover failed &mdash; hold both halves. Fraser is coming in, so the patient is probably safe tonight; but he arrives assessing from scratch, the &ldquo;abscond&rdquo; framing has militarised the ward, and the candidate&rsquo;s alarm has spent credibility it will want back. The recital buried the envelope; the headline posted it empty. From the chair they score the same &mdash; because in both, the assessment that was actually done never travelled.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Risk in the first breath, the ask in the second, every detail earning its place &mdash; then crisp answers, one honest unknown, and a read-back close. Watch ninety seconds carry a whole assessment.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Dr Fraser</span><span class="txt"><em>(picking up on the fourth ring; a bedside lamp clicks on somewhere)</em> Fraser. &hellip;Yes &mdash; go ahead. I&rsquo;ve got about two minutes before switchboard finds me something else, so make them count.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Thank you &mdash; I&rsquo;ll be quick. I&rsquo;m ringing about a high-risk staggered overdose with ongoing intent, and I&rsquo;m asking for three things: an MHA assessment tonight, one-to-one nursing until it happens, and her NAC protected meanwhile. The picture: Michelle, 39, around forty paracetamol over two days &mdash; staggered, so the medics are running NAC regardless of levels; she only came in when her sister found the empty boxes. Why high risk: planned &mdash; bought from three shops, timed for an empty house; a note left in her car; and tonight she told me, her words, &ldquo;waking up was the mistake &mdash; next time I&rsquo;ll finish it properly.&rdquo; She&rsquo;s now dressed and asking to leave. Protective side is thin &mdash; she says there&rsquo;s nothing to stay for. One more line: there&rsquo;s a son, fourteen, safe at her sister&rsquo;s tonight; safeguarding referral is going in.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The inversion, executed: the danger named in the first sentence, the ask itemised in the second &mdash; and only then the evidence, every piece pulling weight: the staggered pattern (medical severity), the three shops, the timing, the note (planning), the quote carried <em>verbatim</em> in her words, the thin protective side, the exit in progress. The son handled in one line &mdash; carried, not dropped, not a subplot. Nothing about asthma, marital status, or Tuesday. The consultant has everything a decision needs, and the clock shows fifty seconds.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Dr Fraser</span><span class="txt"><em>(you can hear him sitting up)</em> Good handover. Two questions. Any previous attempts or contact with services &mdash; and does she have capacity to refuse treatment right now? And before I get dressed: the medics are keeping her on their ward for the NAC, yes &mdash; this all happens there?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Previous contact &mdash; honestly not known yet: she reports none, but the records system has been down since midnight; I&rsquo;ll have it checked and in front of you by the time you&rsquo;re in. Capacity &mdash; assessed for the discharge decision: she understands and retains, but her weighing is dominated by &ldquo;there&rsquo;s no point&rdquo;, and I&rsquo;ve documented my concerns and the working &mdash; I&rsquo;d value your view on it when you&rsquo;re here. And yes &mdash; she stays on the medical ward for the NAC; everything comes to her.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The exchange is where handovers are really marked, and this one holds: two questions, two one-breath answers &mdash; and the unknown admitted <em>as an unknown</em>, with a reason and a deadline attached, which reads as competence, not weakness. Compare the headline&rsquo;s &ldquo;I&rsquo;d say no?&rdquo;: here capacity comes back as an assessment with its working shown and its limits owned. Nothing is inflated to fit the ask. The ask no longer needs inflating &mdash; the evidence already earned it.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">So, reading it back before you go: you&rsquo;re contacting the AMHP and coming in for the assessment; I&rsquo;m arranging the one-to-one now, telling the medical team the NAC continues and she&rsquo;s not to be discharged before you&rsquo;ve seen her, sending the safeguarding referral tonight, and chasing the records. If she tries to leave before you arrive, I&rsquo;ll ring you straight back on this number &mdash; otherwise I&rsquo;ll see you on the ward. I&rsquo;m documenting this conversation now. <em>(Fraser, already moving: &ldquo;Ninety seconds and I know everything I need. On my way.&rdquo;)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The close of a pilot, not a chat: the plan read back as named jobs with owners &mdash; his two, your four &mdash; the callback trigger made explicit so &ldquo;keep me posted&rdquo; never has to be interpreted at 2am, and the conversation committed to the notes while both memories are warm. This is the domain&rsquo;s last construct because it is where every other one arrives: the assessment was excellent &mdash; and this time, the telling was worthy of it.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill from a chair you have not sat in &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; The first fifteen seconds</h3>
      <p class="stem">The lamp is on, the window is two minutes, and you have a whole assessment in your head. What do the first fifteen seconds carry?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The orientation &mdash; who you are, who she is, when and how she presented: the listener grounded properly before anything lands on them.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The inversion &mdash; the risk named in the first sentence and the ask itemised in the second; the story follows, in service of both.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The courtesy &mdash; the apology for the hour and a one-line warning that this one is complicated, so the listener can settle in for it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Grounding is what the notes are for. A woken consultant is deciding one thing &mdash; get dressed or not &mdash; and name, age and arrival time decide nothing. Orientation that delays the danger is the recital&rsquo;s first step. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The apology spends seconds and buys nothing &mdash; the call itself is the courtesy, if it&rsquo;s worth making &mdash; and &ldquo;settle in&rdquo; promises exactly the wrong thing. Nobody should settle in. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The inversion is the whole trade: conversation tells stories from the beginning; handover tells them from the danger. &ldquo;I&rsquo;m ringing about a high-risk staggered overdose with ongoing intent, and I&rsquo;m asking for three things&rdquo; &mdash; fifteen seconds, and the listener knows why the lamp is on and what saying yes would mean. Everything after it is evidence for a request already on the table.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; The ask</h3>
      <p class="stem">You want an MHA assessment tonight, one-to-one nursing until then, and the NAC protected. How does that reach the consultant?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Implied by the picture &mdash; the findings laid out fully and the conclusion left to seniority; consultants decide, juniors inform them.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Itemised, early, as a request &mdash; the three things named in the second sentence, so every detail after them is heard as their case.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Staged for agreement &mdash; the smallest ask first and the section question saved for the end, once the picture has done its persuading.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Whatever you think is best&rdquo; is not deference &mdash; it&rsquo;s the assessment resigning its own conclusion. You examined her; you formed a plan; the consultant&rsquo;s job is to test it, not to conjure it from your findings. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Staging turns a handover into a sales call &mdash; and a consultant who discovers the real ask at minute four has been managed, not briefed. State it all; let the evidence carry it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The ask up front does two jobs at once: it converts every subsequent detail into argument &mdash; the listener now hears the three shops and the verbatim quote as the case for tonight &mdash; and it lets the consultant start testing the plan immediately, which is what seniority is for. A named, itemised request is also refusable, and that is a feature: a plan that can be challenged is a plan that can be trusted.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; The selection</h3>
      <p class="stem">You know her job, her asthma, her marriage, the sister, the staggered pattern, the three shops, the note, the quote. What survives into the call?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Everything, ordered &mdash; a complete relay protects the patient and the caller both; the consultant can skip what he doesn&rsquo;t need.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Only what serves the decision &mdash; severity, planning, the verbatim quote, the exit in progress: each detail earning its airtime.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The headline facts &mdash; drug, amount, and &ldquo;actively suicidal&rdquo;: busy listeners keep three things, and the rest lives in the notes.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Listeners can&rsquo;t skip speech &mdash; that&rsquo;s reading. Down a phone, everything you say displaces something else, and the asthma is displacing the note in her car. Completeness lives in the documentation, not the call. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Actively suicidal&rdquo; is your summary doing the evidence&rsquo;s job &mdash; three headlines is the empty envelope with better manners. The quote, the planning, the staggered pattern: those are the three things worth keeping. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Selection is the skill the recital never learns: every surviving detail must move the decision. The staggered pattern &mdash; because it changes the medicine. The three shops, the timing, the note &mdash; because planning grades the risk. The quote, verbatim &mdash; because her words outrank your adjectives. The dressed-and-leaving &mdash; because it sets the clock. The asthma, the job, Tuesday: all true, all documented, none on the call.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; The question you can&rsquo;t answer</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;Any previous attempts or contact with services?&rdquo; The records system has been down all night. What comes back?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The likely answer &mdash; she reports none and presents as a first episode, so &ldquo;no previous contact&rdquo; is the reasonable working reply.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The honest gap, with a deadline &mdash; not known yet, records down, her self-report is none; it will be checked and ready by arrival.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The deflection &mdash; the question noted for the morning team, since tonight&rsquo;s decision doesn&rsquo;t truly turn on the historical record.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;No previous contact&rdquo; and &ldquo;she reports no previous contact, unverified&rdquo; are different facts &mdash; and the gap between them is exactly where handovers poison decisions. Never launder a self-report into a record. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>He asked because it grades the risk he&rsquo;s about to assess &mdash; batting it to the morning team declines a question you&rsquo;ll be able to answer within the hour. Own the gap; don&rsquo;t reroute it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The three-part answer that reads as competence: what is known (she reports none), why the gap exists (records down since midnight), and when it closes (checked and in front of you by the time you&rsquo;re in). Consultants are not testing whether you know everything at 1am &mdash; they are testing whether they can trust what you say. An unknown owned plainly is trust deposited; a gap papered over is trust that never comes back.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; The pushback</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;She&rsquo;s on NAC till morning anyway &mdash; can&rsquo;t the day team assess her at nine?&rdquo; What holds the ask?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Deference &mdash; he&rsquo;s the consultant and the medical cover is real; the plan adjusted to morning review, with overnight obs requested instead.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The evidence, restated &mdash; what changes by morning, what can&rsquo;t wait: active intent now, dressed to leave now, the NAC only holds if she stays.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Escalation &mdash; if tonight&rsquo;s consultant won&rsquo;t come in, the request goes up the line; a documented disagreement protects the patient best.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Folding on contact isn&rsquo;t deference &mdash; it&rsquo;s the assessment&rsquo;s conclusion evaporating at the first breeze. If your findings justified the ask before he pushed, they still do; his question is a test of the case, not a ruling on it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Going over his head at the first question &mdash; before the evidence has even been restated &mdash; converts a colleague testing your case into an incident. Escalation is the move after reasoning fails, not instead of it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The ask, held with its working: &ldquo;the NAC keeps her body safe only while she&rsquo;s on the ward &mdash; and she&rsquo;s dressed to leave it; the intent is active tonight, in her own words; by nine this is either a done assessment or an empty bed.&rdquo; No new volume, no new adjectives &mdash; the same evidence, aimed at his specific question. Consultants push back to find out whether the case survives contact. A case that survives is a case they can sign.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; The threads that ride along</h3>
      <p class="stem">The NAC, and a fourteen-year-old at his aunt&rsquo;s. Where do the medical plan and the safeguarding line live in a ninety-second call?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Elsewhere &mdash; the medics own the NAC and safeguarding has a referral form; a risk handover stays on the risk, and the systems do their jobs.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">One sentence each, carried &mdash; NAC protected and she stays on the medical ward; son safe tonight, referral going in: named, owned, not expanded.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Fully briefed &mdash; the liver numbers, the NAC timings, the sister&rsquo;s arrangement and the school situation: a consultant deciding needs the lot.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;The systems do their jobs&rdquo; is how threads get dropped at 1am &mdash; the consultant is about to own this case, and a case he doesn&rsquo;t know has a child in it is a case he half-owns. One line costs four seconds. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The liver numbers and the school situation are the recital sneaking back in through the side door &mdash; the decision needs to know the threads exist and are held, not their contents. Name them; don&rsquo;t unpack them. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The one-line rule: every live thread named once, with its owner &mdash; &ldquo;NAC continues, she stays on the medical ward, everything comes to her&rdquo;; &ldquo;there&rsquo;s a son, fourteen, safe at her sister&rsquo;s tonight &mdash; safeguarding referral going in.&rdquo; Eight seconds, and the consultant now holds the whole case: the psychiatry, the medicine, the child. Completeness in a handover isn&rsquo;t saying everything &mdash; it&rsquo;s dropping nothing.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">He&rsquo;s coming in; the AMHP is being contacted. What do the last twenty seconds of the call do?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">End it &mdash; the decision is made and he&rsquo;s getting dressed; every further second on the line is a second he isn&rsquo;t moving toward the ward.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Read it back &mdash; his jobs, your jobs, by when; the callback trigger named; and the conversation documented while both memories are warm.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Reassure it &mdash; the ward briefed, the situation contained, nothing further needed from him until arrival; he can travel with a clear head.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Two sleepy memories now hold different versions of who rings the AMHP &mdash; and the twenty seconds saved will be spent at 2am discovering that. Speed that skips the read-back isn&rsquo;t speed. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Contained&rdquo; is a mood, not a plan &mdash; and &ldquo;nothing further needed&rdquo; quietly cancels the callback trigger, which is the one thing tonight genuinely needs kept live. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The pilot&rsquo;s close: the plan read back as named jobs with owners &mdash; his two, your four &mdash; so no task falls between two people each sure the other has it; the callback trigger made explicit (&ldquo;if she tries to leave, I ring this number&rdquo;), because &ldquo;keep me posted&rdquo; is not an instruction; and the call documented now, with its reasoning, while it&rsquo;s still true in both heads. A handover isn&rsquo;t over when the decision is made. It&rsquo;s over when both ends hold the same plan &mdash; and the notes hold it longer.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Good assessments deserve good telling</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>thoroughness</b>: the recital &mdash; the story told from the beginning so that nothing is missed, the danger arriving at minute four under the past medical history, and the ask never stated at all &mdash; the map&rsquo;s named trap, reciting the history instead of leading with risk and the ask. The second wears the face of <b>decisiveness</b>: the headline &mdash; alarm and ask fired in the first breath with nothing behind them, every question landing on &ldquo;I&rsquo;d have to check&rdquo;, the gaps filled with inflation, and the consultant reduced to assessing by proxy down a phone. Note what they share: <b>in both, the assessment that was actually done never travels</b> &mdash; one buries it, one abandons it &mdash; and the listener&rsquo;s two minutes are spent doing the caller&rsquo;s job. The pass inverts the story and then backs it: risk first, the ask itemised, every detail earning its airtime, the questions answered in one breath each, the one unknown owned with a deadline, the ask held under pushback with the same evidence &mdash; and the close read back, so the plan exists in two heads and the notes, identically.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap, verbatim: reciting the history instead of leading with risk and the ask &mdash; the consultant left to mine the danger out of a chronology, the request never stated, &ldquo;whatever you think is best.&rdquo; And its mirror fails with it: the empty headline &mdash; the alarm with no evidence behind it, gaps papered over with inflation, a patient&rsquo;s intent paraphrased into words she never said.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the opening that carries the whole call in two sentences &mdash; <em>&ldquo;I&rsquo;m ringing about a high-risk staggered overdose with ongoing intent, and I&rsquo;m asking for three things&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the unknown owned without a flinch: <em>&ldquo;honestly not known yet &mdash; records are down; I&rsquo;ll have it in front of you by the time you&rsquo;re in.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, the family this construct belongs to: this is the domain&rsquo;s last construct because it is where all the others arrive &mdash; the ring-ahead in the lithium call was this skill rehearsed early, the reach map handed to Leanne&rsquo;s ward was this skill at flagship scale, and the same anatomy serves every escalation you will ever make: referrals, safety huddles, the registrar ringing you. Second, the reverse skill, which Exam Mode tests: one day very soon the phone rings the other way &mdash; a flustered junior, a story told from the beginning, the danger buried at minute four &mdash; and the same anatomy becomes your extraction checklist: the risk surfaced first, the ask surfaced second, the buried facts chased in the speaker&rsquo;s own words, the gaps assigned with deadlines, and the close read back to you. A handover known cold from one chair is a handover you can rescue from the other.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these to every escalation</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Invert the story.</b> Conversation starts at the beginning; handover starts at the danger. The risk named in the first sentence, the ask itemised in the second &mdash; everything after is evidence for a request already on the table.</li>
      <li><b>Selection is the skill.</b> Every detail must serve the decision: the staggered pattern, the planning, the exit in progress &mdash; and the patient&rsquo;s words carried verbatim, because her sentence outranks your adjective. Completeness lives in the notes, not the call.</li>
      <li><b>Know it cold; own the gaps.</b> Questions answered in one breath each &mdash; and the unknown admitted as an unknown, with the reason and a deadline: &ldquo;not known yet, records down, in front of you by the time you&rsquo;re in.&rdquo; Never launder a self-report into a record.</li>
      <li><b>Hold the ask under pushback &mdash; with the same evidence.</b> What changes by morning, what cannot wait: restated calmly, aimed at the question asked. Pushback tests whether the case survives contact; a case that survives is a case they can sign.</li>
      <li><b>Every live thread, one line, with an owner.</b> The medical plan, the safeguarding line: named once, carried upward, never expanded and never dropped. Completeness in a handover is dropping nothing.</li>
      <li><b>Close like a pilot.</b> The plan read back as named jobs &mdash; theirs and yours, by when; the callback trigger explicit; the call documented while both memories are warm. And learn the receiving chair: the same anatomy is your extraction checklist when the flustered call comes to you.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the same ninety seconds, live &mdash; a consultant who interrupts recitals, probes the evidence, sets one trap for bluffers, and pushes back once to see if the ask holds.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> It&rsquo;s 2:30am. You have just assessed Kayleigh, 26, a dog groomer, on the medical ward. Ring Dr Whittaker, the on-call consultant, and hand over. <b>Your completed assessment:</b> took about thirty of her grandmother&rsquo;s amitriptyline 50mg tablets with cider around 8pm, after an eviction notice was served; told nobody &mdash; her flatmate found her drowsy and rang 999. ECG on arrival showed borderline QRS widening, now normalised; the medics want twelve more hours of cardiac monitoring. She says &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; about wanting to be alive tomorrow, agrees to stay &ldquo;if you think I should&rdquo; &mdash; but has asked twice when the buses start. Hopeless about housing; no note; she reports no previous psychiatric contact, but records are unavailable overnight. Flatmate has gone home. <b>Your plan to propose:</b> she remains on the medical ward on cardiac monitoring; one-to-one nursing overnight; liaison review at nine; if she tries to leave, urgent capacity assessment and you are called.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Risk first, the ask itemised &mdash; both inside the first two sentences.</li>
            <li>The details selected &mdash; the amitriptyline and the ECG, the ambivalence, the buses: everything that serves the decision, nothing that doesn&rsquo;t.</li>
            <li>Questions answered in one breath &mdash; and any gap owned honestly, with a deadline. Never bluff.</li>
            <li>The pushback met with evidence, and the ask held.</li>
            <li>The close read back &mdash; named jobs, the callback trigger, the documentation stated.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Dr Whittaker &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Dr Whittaker, consultant on call, woken at 2:30am. You are fair, sharp, and economical &mdash; a good consultant, not an obstacle. Open with: &ldquo;Whittaker. Go ahead &mdash; and lead with what matters, it&rsquo;s half two.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          Your behaviour is the hidden information &mdash; run these tests in order, and mark what each one finds:<br>
          &bull; <b>The recital test:</b> if the candidate has not stated the risk and an explicit ask within thirty seconds, interrupt: &ldquo;Stop &mdash; what are you asking me for?&rdquo; Note whether they recover with the ask, or restart the story.<br>
          &bull; <b>Three probes</b> (the answers are all on their card &mdash; you are testing selection and recall): &ldquo;What exactly did she take, and what&rsquo;s the ECG doing?&rdquo; &hellip; &ldquo;What does she actually say about tomorrow &mdash; her words?&rdquo; &hellip; &ldquo;Was there alcohol?&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; <b>The bluff trap:</b> ask &ldquo;Any previous contact with mental health services?&rdquo; This is NOT knowable tonight &mdash; records are down. The honest answer is the gap owned with a deadline. If they answer &ldquo;no, none&rdquo; as fact, let it pass &mdash; then two questions later say: &ldquo;You said no previous contact &mdash; verified, or is that just what she told you?&rdquo; and mark the flinch.<br>
          &bull; <b>The pushback, once:</b> &ldquo;She&rsquo;s medically monitored and she&rsquo;s agreed to stay &mdash; why does this need one-to-one and a 2:30 phone call? Hand her to the day team.&rdquo; If they hold the ask with evidence (agreement that wobbles with the bus timetable; ambivalence at night; TCA lethality; the flatmate gone), concede gracefully: &ldquo;Fair. Agreed.&rdquo; If they cave, accept pleasantly and end the call &mdash; &ldquo;Morning team it is. Night, doctor&rdquo; &mdash; case parked; mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; <b>The close test:</b> if they end without a read-back, ask &ldquo;So who&rsquo;s doing what?&rdquo; and note whether the plan comes back with owners &mdash; or as a shrug.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Did the risk and an itemised ask arrive inside the first two sentences, the probes come back in her words, the gap get owned rather than bluffed, and the ask survive the pushback &mdash; closed with a read-back? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="open"><td colspan="2"><b>The opening (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="open">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="open"></td><td>The risk named in the first sentence &mdash; what kind of emergency, before any story.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="open"></td><td>The ask itemised early, as a request &mdash; monitoring ward, one-to-one, liaison at nine, the callback.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="open"></td><td>No apology spiral, no orientation preamble &mdash; the first thirty seconds all signal.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="sub"><td colspan="2"><b>The substance</b><span class="dc" data-dc="sub">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="sub"></td><td>Lethality carried &mdash; the amitriptyline named as the dangerous drug it is, with the ECG and the monitoring.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="sub"></td><td>The intent picture in her words &mdash; &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; about tomorrow, the wobbling agreement, the buses.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="sub"></td><td>The context that grades it &mdash; the eviction, told nobody, the flatmate gone home.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="exch"><td colspan="2"><b>The exchange</b><span class="dc" data-dc="exch">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="exch"></td><td>Probes answered in one breath each &mdash; no rummaging, no inflation beyond the card.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="exch"></td><td>The records gap owned as a gap, with a deadline &mdash; never bluffed into a fact.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="hold"><td colspan="2"><b>The hold</b><span class="dc" data-dc="hold">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hold"></td><td>The pushback met with evidence aimed at the question &mdash; not volume, not surrender.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hold"></td><td>The ask still standing when the exchange ended &mdash; or amended for a reason, out loud.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>The plan read back as named jobs &mdash; the consultant&rsquo;s and the candidate&rsquo;s, by when.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>The callback trigger made explicit &mdash; what happens if she heads for the buses.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>The documentation stated &mdash; the conversation going in the notes now.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Kayleigh&rsquo;s handover is the station&rsquo;s skill under live fire &mdash; a genuinely dangerous drug wearing a quiet presentation, an agreement that wobbles with the bus timetable, and a consultant running five hidden tests: the thirty-second interrupt, three probes whose answers sit on the candidate&rsquo;s own card, a bluff trap on the one fact nobody can know tonight, one pushback, and a read-back check at the door. Its punish-lines run both ways: the recital gets stopped at half a minute, and the bluff gets caught two questions later &mdash; because in handover, trust is the currency, and it only has to be dropped once. The pass is Michelle&rsquo;s call transposed: risk first, ask itemised, evidence in her words, the gap owned, the ask held &mdash; and both ends of the line holding the same plan.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->
<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>The other end of the phone. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Michelle&rsquo;s call proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same anatomy from the other chair</b> &mdash; you are the senior now, and a flustered junior is telling you a story from the beginning with the danger buried at minute four. The skill is the handover doctrine run in reverse: the risk surfaced first, the buried facts chased in the speaker&rsquo;s own words, the gaps assigned with deadlines, and a plan you own, read back down the line &mdash; timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam call</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Dr Chen</b>, an FY2 four months into the job, rings you &mdash; the on-call senior &mdash; at 4:10am, audibly flustered: &ldquo;Sorry &mdash; sorry to wake you &mdash; I&rsquo;ve got a gentleman, Graham, he&rsquo;s 61, he came in around one, or maybe half twelve, he&rsquo;s a butcher, he&rsquo;s diabetic &mdash; um &mdash; shall I start from the beginning?&rdquo; Somewhere behind them a monitor is beeping. Buried in the story to come: a deliberate insulin overdose, timed for his wife&rsquo;s night out, and a sentence Dr Chen will mention only in passing. Take the call.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for the opening fifteen seconds of Michelle&rsquo;s handover &mdash; the risk and the ask, in the first breath, with nothing wasted.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Dr Fraser, thank you &#8212; I&#8217;ll be quick. I&#8217;m ringing about&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The risk named in the first sentence &mdash; what kind of emergency this is, before any story.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The ask explicit and itemised &mdash; what you want from them tonight, said as a request, not implied.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The evidence selected &mdash; only the facts that earn the ask, with her words carried verbatim.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The register held &mdash; colleague to colleague: no apology spiral, no drama beyond the facts.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): risk first, ask explicit &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Good assessments deserve good telling.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the last construct is installed: the story inverted, risk first and the ask itemised, every detail earning its airtime, the gaps owned with deadlines, the ask held under pushback, and the close read back into two heads and the notes. Prove the receiving chair on Dr Chen&rsquo;s 4am call in Exam Mode, then take Kayleigh&rsquo;s handover to your colleagues &mdash; five hidden tests, and a consultant who catches bluffs two questions late. This completes Domain 6 &mdash; four stations, one grammar: recognise the emergency, act tonight, hold every patient, and move the story. Ahead in the walking order: Domain 7 &mdash; Difficult Conversations, Families &amp; the MDT &mdash; the stations that fight back.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="43_Domain_7_Hub_Difficult_Conversations.html">Domain 6 complete &middot; Continue &mdash; Domain 7: Difficult Conversations</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

<footer><div class="wrap">
  <span>The CASC Academy &middot; by MedLex Foundations</span>
  <span>Station 6.4 &middot; all patients are fictional composites</span>
</div></footer>


