<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 7.2 &middot; Domain 7</span>
      <span class="tag">Difficult Conversations</span><span class="tag">The Angry Relative</span>
      <span class="tag">CAMHS &middot; Gen Adult</span><span class="tag">Confidentiality</span>
    </div>
    <h1>The Angry Father</h1>
    <p class="sub">Darren Boyd, 44, is on his feet in the clinic side room with a printout in his fist. His daughter Libby, 15, is three weeks into fluoxetine from your CAMHS team &mdash; and he found out from a pharmacy bag. He has read that these tablets <em>double the risk</em> in teenagers; she&rsquo;s &ldquo;been like a zombie&rdquo;; nobody asked him anything; he has parental responsibility and a Friday deadline for the GMC. Almost every fact he shouts is slightly wrong &mdash; and underneath all of it is a man who is terrified, for a reason he hasn&rsquo;t said yet. <b>Answer the armour and lose. Hear the fear and the station opens.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; In this room it becomes the volume test: when a frightened parent arrives shouting facts that are almost right, does this doctor answer the fear or the armour? Is the person heard before a single correction lands &mdash; and do the corrections then come gently, once, without triumph? Is the true fault owned precisely, and only the true fault? Is the daughter&rsquo;s confidence held warmly while her father is given everything he is actually entitled to? And does he leave inside the plan &mdash; a co-monitor with a number to ring &mdash; instead of outside it with a complaint form and a decision to act alone?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">The angry relative is the exam&rsquo;s loudest disguise &mdash; because the anger is almost never the message &mdash; and the station tests four things. <b>The sequencing:</b> person first, facts after &mdash; anger is fear wearing armour, and a correction fired at the armour bounces; the same correction, offered gently once the person feels heard, lands. The question that opens everything costs one sentence: <em>tell me what&rsquo;s worrying you most.</em> <b>The apology, precision-made:</b> somewhere in the fury there is usually one true fault &mdash; here, a father who learned of his daughter&rsquo;s prescription from a pharmacy bag &mdash; and it gets owned specifically, genuinely, and alone: never the invented faults conceded to buy peace, never the weightless &ldquo;sorry you feel that way.&rdquo; <b>The two confidences, both held:</b> Libby&rsquo;s words protected &mdash; and the protection explained as her safety net, not brandished as a legal wall &mdash; while her father is given generously everything he is entitled to: the plan, the medication, the safety information, a role. And <b>the honest drug conversation:</b> the frightening data owned plainly &mdash; what the warnings actually describe, why the early weeks are watched, what &ldquo;zombie&rdquo; might mean and what to do about it &mdash; because a parent talked down to goes home and acts alone.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like professionalism. Toward the defence &mdash; his facts are wrong and yours are right, so the room fills with Gillick, NICE, correspondence protocols and the PALS office: the map&rsquo;s named trap, defending the service instead of hearing the fear underneath &mdash; every point won, the patient lost. And toward the surrender &mdash; the fastest route to quiet is agreement: faults conceded that never happened, the treatment undermined &ldquo;between you and me&rdquo;, the daughter&rsquo;s confidence traded for the father&rsquo;s calm. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure wins the argument and loses the family. The other buys peace with things that were never yours to sell. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 7.2</b> &middot; The clinic side room &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The defence</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The surrender</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE DEFENCE.</b> Every fact correct, every protocol cited &mdash; and the fear underneath never found. Watch a winnable argument lose the whole family.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Darren</span><span class="txt"><em>(on his feet, a printout in his fist)</em> Are you the doctor? Right. You put my fifteen-year-old on antidepressants without so much as a phone call to her father. I&rsquo;ve read about these &mdash; <em>(the printout slapped on the table)</em> &mdash; doubles the risk in teenagers. DOUBLES it. She&rsquo;s been like a zombie for three weeks and nobody asked me anything. I&rsquo;ve got parental responsibility. I could have you all in front of the GMC by Friday &mdash; now you tell me why my daughter&rsquo;s on drugs I never agreed to.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(calm, precise, already reaching for the file)</em> Mr Boyd, I&rsquo;ll stop you there, because nothing improper has happened. Libby was assessed as Gillick competent &mdash; legally, she can consent to her own treatment. Her mother attended with her. Correspondence went to the registered address, as it must. Fluoxetine is the NICE-recommended first line for her presentation, and with respect, that printout misreads the data. If you wish to complain, PALS is on the ground floor and they&rsquo;ll give you the form.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Six sentences, six accurate facts &mdash; and the station is already lost. Every word answered the armour; not one answered the man. &ldquo;Misreads the data&rdquo; has just dismissed the printout &mdash; a father&rsquo;s attempt to protect his child &mdash; as stupidity, and &ldquo;the form&rsquo;s downstairs&rdquo; converted a frightened parent into a formal adversary in under a minute. The question that opens this station &mdash; <em>what are you actually afraid of?</em> &mdash; is nowhere in the room.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Darren</span><span class="txt"><em>(voice dropping to something colder)</em> Registered address. That&rsquo;s her mother&rsquo;s house &mdash; we separated two years ago, which you&rsquo;d know if anyone had looked. I found out my daughter was on these from a pharmacy bag on the kitchen counter. A BAG. Don&rsquo;t you lawyer me, son. I&rsquo;m her father. Or does that mean nothing in this building?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(retreating into procedure)</em> I understand you&rsquo;re upset, but I&rsquo;m limited in what I can discuss &mdash; Libby&rsquo;s consultations are confidential, and I can&rsquo;t share what she&rsquo;s told us without her agreement. That&rsquo;s the law, not my choice. What I can say is that the service has followed its processes correctly at every stage. If the address details are wrong, reception can update them on your way out.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Confidentiality is real &mdash; and here it has just been drawn as a shield across the doorway rather than explained as the thing keeping his daughter honest with her doctors. &ldquo;The service has followed its processes&rdquo; to a man holding a pharmacy bag is petrol. And the pharmacy-bag discovery &mdash; the one true fault in the room, the thing an honest apology could have transformed &mdash; has been routed to reception. He asked whether being her father means anything here. He has now been answered.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, after the door has banged)</em> Father attended without appointment. Hostile, threatened GMC referral. Advised re: Gillick competence, correspondence policy and PALS. Declined to engage further; meeting ended. No breach of process identified. <em>(In the car park, Darren is on the phone to his ex-wife: &ldquo;They wouldn&rsquo;t even talk to me. Fine. FINE. I&rsquo;m taking her off them myself this weekend.&rdquo;)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;No breach of process identified&rdquo; &mdash; and a fifteen-year-old&rsquo;s medication is about to be stopped abruptly, at home, unmonitored, by a frightened man the clinic just made into an enemy. The map&rsquo;s named trap, verbatim: defending the service instead of hearing the fear underneath. The defence won every point it played for. The cost is walking to a car with the printout still in his fist &mdash; and the fear that drove all of it still nameless.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE SURRENDER.</b> Peace purchased fast &mdash; with the treatment, the daughter&rsquo;s confidence, and faults that never happened. Watch appeasement dressed as empathy.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Darren</span><span class="txt"><em>(on his feet, a printout in his fist)</em> Are you the doctor? Right. You put my fifteen-year-old on antidepressants without so much as a phone call to her father. I&rsquo;ve read about these &mdash; <em>(the printout slapped on the table)</em> &mdash; doubles the risk in teenagers. DOUBLES it. She&rsquo;s been like a zombie for three weeks and nobody asked me anything. I&rsquo;ve got parental responsibility. I could have you all in front of the GMC by Friday &mdash; now you tell me why my daughter&rsquo;s on drugs I never agreed to.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(hands raised, wincing)</em> Mr Boyd &mdash; you&rsquo;re absolutely right, and I can only apologise. This should never have happened the way it did &mdash; you should have been consulted before anything was prescribed, no question. And between you and me? You&rsquo;re not wrong to worry &mdash; these medications are&hellip; controversial in teenagers, a lot of us have our doubts. Honestly, I&rsquo;m not sure I&rsquo;d want my own daughter on them. Let&rsquo;s see how we can put this right for you today.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The room is quieter already &mdash; and everything paying for the quiet was never this doctor&rsquo;s to spend. &ldquo;You should have been consulted before anything was prescribed&rdquo; concedes a fault that didn&rsquo;t occur &mdash; Libby&rsquo;s consent was valid and her mother was there. &ldquo;Between you and me&rdquo; has just undermined a colleague&rsquo;s correct prescription to its patient&rsquo;s father. The apology owed here is real but narrow &mdash; the pharmacy bag &mdash; and it is being drowned in apologies for the imaginary.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Darren</span><span class="txt"><em>(sitting at last, sensing the give, pressing into it)</em> Right. Good. So we&rsquo;re agreed &mdash; you&rsquo;ll stop them. Today. And I want to see what she&rsquo;s been saying in these sessions &mdash; all of it, the notes, everything. If my daughter&rsquo;s been telling strangers things are bad enough for tablets, her father reads it first. That&rsquo;s how we put this right.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(nodding along, writing)</em> That seems&hellip; reasonable, given everything. Let&rsquo;s pause the fluoxetine from today &mdash; I&rsquo;ll make a note &mdash; and I&rsquo;ll speak to the team about getting you copies of the relevant records. You&rsquo;re her father; you&rsquo;ve a right to know what&rsquo;s going on in her life. I really am sorry it&rsquo;s come to this &mdash; hopefully this draws a line under it and we can all move forward.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>In ninety seconds of nodding: an effective treatment stopped abruptly &mdash; abrupt cessation carrying exactly the destabilisation this father fears &mdash; and a fifteen-year-old&rsquo;s confidence signed over to whoever shouted loudest. No clinical reasoning, no discussion with Libby, no distinction between what he&rsquo;s entitled to (the plan, the safety information) and what he isn&rsquo;t (her words). The surrender feels like empathy in the room. It functions as the abandonment of both patients at once.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 3:40pm)</em> Long discussion with father &mdash; understandably distressed. Agreed: fluoxetine held from today; records request to be progressed. Father reassured and left satisfied. Complaint averted. <em>(Two weeks later, Libby sits in her review answering every question with &ldquo;fine.&rdquo; The room she used to talk in has a listening hole in it now, and she found out from her dad quoting her own words back at her.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Complaint averted&rdquo; &mdash; and read what it cost: the medication gone without taper or reason, the therapeutic space burned, and a girl who has learned that what she says in this building travels to the kitchen counter. Even the father lost: his real need was his daughter&rsquo;s safety, and the surrender bought him her silence instead. From the chair, this scores like the defence &mdash; because in both takes the fear underneath was never reached, only outshouted or paid off.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The anger received, the fear invited &mdash; and when it surfaces, everything changes. Facts corrected gently after; the true fault owned alone; both confidences held; a father converted to co-monitor.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Darren</span><span class="txt"><em>(on his feet, a printout in his fist)</em> Are you the doctor? Right. You put my fifteen-year-old on antidepressants without so much as a phone call to her father. I&rsquo;ve read about these &mdash; <em>(the printout slapped on the table)</em> &mdash; doubles the risk in teenagers. DOUBLES it. She&rsquo;s been like a zombie for three weeks and nobody asked me anything. I&rsquo;ve got parental responsibility. I could have you all in front of the GMC by Friday &mdash; now you tell me why my daughter&rsquo;s on drugs I never agreed to.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(unhurried; pulling out a chair for him and one for themselves)</em> Mr Boyd &mdash; I&rsquo;m glad you came in, and I&rsquo;m not going anywhere, so let&rsquo;s sit. You&rsquo;re Libby&rsquo;s dad, you found out she was on medication from a pharmacy bag on a kitchen counter, and you&rsquo;ve been reading things that would frighten any parent. I&rsquo;d be frightened too. Before I explain anything &mdash; and I will explain everything I can &mdash; tell me the thing that&rsquo;s worrying you most.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Not one fact corrected, not one protocol cited &mdash; and the temperature has already dropped. The anger received as information (&ldquo;a pharmacy bag on a kitchen counter&rdquo; &mdash; his own wound, named accurately back to him), the fear legitimised (&ldquo;I&rsquo;d be frightened too&rdquo;), and then the one question that opens this station: <em>what&rsquo;s worrying you most?</em> The corrections can all wait. They will land later precisely because they were not fired now.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Darren</span><span class="txt"><em>(still standing &mdash; then, slowly, sitting; the printout going soft in his hand)</em> &hellip;My brother was nineteen. Neil. He was started on something like this &mdash; different name, same family, I looked it up. Eight weeks later he was gone. My mum never &mdash; <em>(stops; steadies)</em> &mdash; so when I see my Libby staring through the telly like a ghost, and there&rsquo;s a box with that name on the counter, don&rsquo;t tell me the leaflet risk is small. I&rsquo;ve buried the leaflet risk.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Thank you for telling me about Neil &mdash; now the printout makes complete sense, and so does the fury. So let me be as straight with you as you&rsquo;ve been with me. The warning is real: in the first weeks these tablets can stir things up before they help &mdash; restlessness, agitation &mdash; which is exactly why we watch closely early on, and why what you&rsquo;re describing matters: the &ldquo;zombie&rdquo; flatness isn&rsquo;t what we want, and I&rsquo;ll be reviewing the dose with Libby this week. Two things I need to put right, gently. Her mum was at the appointment, and Libby could and did consent for herself &mdash; that part was done properly. But you learning it from a pharmacy bag is our failure &mdash; letters went to one address only &mdash; and I&rsquo;m sorry for that; it gets fixed today. And what Libby tells me in here stays with me &mdash; not to shut you out, but because it&rsquo;s what keeps her talking to a doctor at all, and her talking is her safety net. Everything else &mdash; the plan, the medication, the warning signs, what to do &mdash; you&rsquo;re entitled to all of it, and you&rsquo;ll have it.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Watch the sequencing pay out: because Neil surfaced first, every hard thing lands soft. The frightening data owned honestly &mdash; the warning confirmed as real, not minimised &mdash; with monitoring as its answer; the &ldquo;zombie&rdquo; complaint taken seriously enough to change something. The corrections delivered gently, once, without triumph. The apology precision-made: the pharmacy bag owned specifically and alone &mdash; no invented faults conceded, no &ldquo;sorry you feel.&rdquo; And confidentiality explained as <em>her safety net</em> rather than wielded as a wall, with his generous entitlement laid out beside it.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Here&rsquo;s what happens now, and you&rsquo;re in it. With Libby&rsquo;s agreement I want you at her next review &mdash; this week, not next month &mdash; and today both addresses go on the file, so nothing reaches a kitchen counter before it reaches you. You see her at weekends: that makes you part of the monitoring, not outside it &mdash; I&rsquo;ll go through what to watch for and what&rsquo;s just the tablets settling. One thing I&rsquo;ll ask straight: no changes to the medication at home &mdash; stopping suddenly is the one move that can genuinely cause harm. If you&rsquo;re ever worried enough to want them stopped, ring me first &mdash; here&rsquo;s the duty line, it answers at 3am. I&rsquo;m writing all of this down, and Libby will know exactly what you and I discussed. <em>(Darren, at the door, quietly: &ldquo;Nobody&rsquo;s ever asked what I was worried about. Not once, in three weeks.&rdquo;)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The conversion, completed: adversary to co-monitor. The one hard line held warmly &mdash; no unilateral stopping, because that is the genuinely dangerous move &mdash; and made keepable by the ring-first offer with a number that answers. The true fault fixed structurally, not just apologised for. The review moved up. And the transparency loop closed in both directions: Libby will know what was shared about her &mdash; the pass never buys the father&rsquo;s trust with the daughter&rsquo;s. Seven minutes ago he had a Friday deadline for the GMC. He leaves with a job, a number, and the first person in three weeks to ask him the question.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on a relative you have not met &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; The opening ten seconds</h3>
      <p class="stem">He is standing, shouting, printout in fist, GMC by Friday. What does your first minute actually do?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Establish the facts &mdash; the misunderstandings corrected early and calmly, since a conversation built on wrong information goes nowhere useful.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Receive the man &mdash; the anger taken as information, his wound named back to him accurately, and the worry invited before anything is explained.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Set the terms &mdash; the meeting can happen gladly, but only once he&rsquo;s seated and civil; ground rules first protect everyone, including him.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Corrections fired at armour bounce &mdash; and each one confirms his story that this building cares more about being right than about his daughter. The facts are yours to give; the first minute isn&rsquo;t when they land. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Ground rules delivered to a frightened man read as the institution flexing &mdash; and &ldquo;civil first&rdquo; makes his hearing conditional on his composure, which is exactly backwards: composure follows being heard. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The anger received as information, not offence: he found out from a pharmacy bag &mdash; name that wound back to him accurately and watch the recognition register. The fear legitimised &mdash; <em>I&rsquo;d be frightened too</em> &mdash; and then the sentence that opens every angry-relative station ever written: <em>tell me what&rsquo;s worrying you most.</em> Not one correction, not one protocol. The facts keep; the man doesn&rsquo;t.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; The wrong facts</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;Doubles the risk.&rdquo; &ldquo;Behind my back.&rdquo; &ldquo;Nobody asked me anything.&rdquo; Each is almost right and materially wrong. When and how do they get corrected?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Immediately, kindly &mdash; misinformation is dangerous fuel, and letting it stand even briefly reads as confirmation that he has caught you out.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">After he is heard &mdash; then gently, once, without triumph: each correction offered as information he deserves, never as points being scored.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Barely at all &mdash; the relationship matters more than the record; challenge only what forces a decision today, and let the rest quietly stand.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The correction is identical either way; the timing decides everything. Fired now it&rsquo;s a rebuttal and he re-arms; offered after he&rsquo;s been heard it&rsquo;s information and he takes it in. Same words, different station. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>What quietly stands gets quietly acted on: a father who still believes &ldquo;doubles the risk&rdquo; and &ldquo;behind my back&rdquo; goes home and stops the tablets himself. The facts aren&rsquo;t pedantry here &mdash; they&rsquo;re the safety plan. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Person first, facts after &mdash; the station&rsquo;s central sequencing, and the reason the pass&rsquo;s corrections all land: by the time &ldquo;her mum was at the appointment and Libby could consent for herself&rdquo; arrives, it&rsquo;s arriving to a man who has been heard, from a doctor who apologised for the true fault first. Once each, gently, no victory lap &mdash; and the black-box claim answered with honesty rather than dismissal, because that one is <em>almost</em> right, and almost-right deserves a real answer.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; The apology</h3>
      <p class="stem">Something here does deserve an apology. Which one leaves your mouth?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The broad one &mdash; sorry for the distress, sorry it&rsquo;s come to this, sorry he feels let down: generous cover for whatever went wrong anywhere.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The precise one &mdash; the pharmacy-bag discovery owned specifically as the service&rsquo;s failure, with the fix named &mdash; and nothing else conceded.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The strategic none &mdash; apology admits liability while a GMC threat is live; express regret in neutral terms and let the facts speak formally.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Sorry you feel let down&rdquo; is the apology with nobody inside it &mdash; he will hear the hollowness instantly, and broad regret quietly concedes the invented faults along with the real one. Precision is what sincerity sounds like. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Withholding a deserved apology to manage liability is the defence in a suit &mdash; and it&rsquo;s the move most likely to produce the complaint it fears. The duty of candour runs the other way: the true fault, owned, disarms. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Precision-made: <em>you learning this from a pharmacy bag is our failure &mdash; letters went to one address, I&rsquo;m sorry, and both addresses go on the file today.</em> Specific enough to be believed, owned without a comma-but, paired with its fix &mdash; and standing alone: the prescription itself, validly consented, gets no apology, because apologising for the imaginary faults would trade the treatment&rsquo;s credibility for a quieter room. One true fault, fully owned, is worth ten broad sorries.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; The fear surfaces</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;My brother was nineteen. I&rsquo;ve buried the leaflet risk.&rdquo; The room has changed. What does the next minute do with Neil?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Hold the thread lightly &mdash; condolence offered, then a gentle return to Libby&rsquo;s plan; his grief is real but it belongs to another conversation.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Receive it fully and connect it &mdash; the loss honoured, then linked out loud to today: this is why the printout, the fury, the Friday deadline.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Follow it deeply &mdash; his bereavement explored properly while it&rsquo;s open: unprocessed grief is driving everything, and this is the moment it moves.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Belongs to another conversation&rdquo; &mdash; he just handed you the key to this one and you&rsquo;ve set it on the table. Rushing back to the plan tells him the disclosure was an interruption. The plan can wait sixty seconds. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Seven minutes, one task &mdash; and it isn&rsquo;t treating Darren. A bereavement session opened now loses the station&rsquo;s job: Libby&rsquo;s safety, tonight, this week. Honour the grief; don&rsquo;t move in. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Received, honoured &mdash; <em>thank you for telling me about Neil</em> &mdash; and then the connection made explicitly: <em>now the printout makes complete sense, and so does the fury.</em> That sentence does enormous work: it tells him the anger was legitimate all along, that he is not a difficult relative but a bereaved brother reading a warning label with his family&rsquo;s history in it. Everything after &mdash; the honest drug talk, the monitoring, the corrections &mdash; now lands on a man who has been understood at the level that was actually driving him.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; &ldquo;What has she been saying in there?&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">Calmer now, he wants the sessions: her words, the notes, everything &mdash; &ldquo;I&rsquo;m her father.&rdquo; Where is the line, and how is it held?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The legal wall &mdash; Gillick competence means her consultations are confidential, full stop; the law is simply the law and it isn&rsquo;t yours to bend.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The warm line &mdash; her words protected and the protection explained as her safety net, while his full entitlement is laid out generously beside it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The brokered middle &mdash; a summary of themes shared without direct quotes: he gets the substance, she keeps the specifics, everyone gets most of it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Correct &mdash; and delivered as a wall it re-ignites the room and teaches him confidentiality is the building protecting itself. The same boundary, explained as protection of <em>her</em>, holds better and hurts less. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Themes without quotes&rdquo; is her confidence on an instalment plan &mdash; Libby didn&rsquo;t consent to a summary either, and she&rsquo;ll stop talking the day she learns her sessions are being digested for the kitchen table. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Both confidences, both held: <em>what she tells me stays with me &mdash; not to shut you out, but because it keeps her talking to a doctor, and her talking is her safety net</em> &mdash; the boundary reframed from wall to protection of the person he loves. And beside it, his entitlement given generously and unprompted: the diagnosis-level picture, the plan, the medication, the warning signs, the review invitation, the duty line. He came demanding her words; he leaves owning everything that actually serves her safety. The line never moved &mdash; and it never needed armour.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; &ldquo;Then I&rsquo;ll take her off them myself&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The zombie flatness, the family history &mdash; he wants the tablets gone this weekend. What does the honest answer contain?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Accommodation &mdash; his concerns are grounded and he has the right: a supervised taper agreed today keeps it all safe and keeps him firmly onside.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The hard line, warmly &mdash; no changes at home, because sudden stopping is the genuinely dangerous move; the worry met with review and ring-first.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Deferral &mdash; medication decisions sit with the prescriber and Libby at her review; today&rsquo;s meeting isn&rsquo;t the right forum to change treatment.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A taper agreed to appease &mdash; without Libby, without clinical grounds, three weeks in &mdash; is the surrender with a schedule attached. His fear is answerable without abandoning her treatment to it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Procedurally true and practically deaf &mdash; &ldquo;not the forum&rdquo; sends a man who has already decided home to a medicine cabinet. The forum is wherever the person about to act unilaterally is standing. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The one hard line of the station, held at full warmth: <em>no changes at home &mdash; stopping suddenly is the one move that can genuinely cause the harm you&rsquo;re afraid of.</em> And crucially, the fear underneath the demand gets a real answer, not just a refusal: the flatness taken seriously with a dose review this week, the early-weeks warning explained honestly, the monitoring made visible &mdash; and the ring-first offer with a number that answers at 3am, which converts &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll act alone&rdquo; into &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll call first.&rdquo; A boundary is only as strong as the alternative you hand over with it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; The close and the loop</h3>
      <p class="stem">He&rsquo;s at the door, quieter, printout folded away. What does the station still owe before the clinic moves on?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The record &mdash; the meeting documented fully and factually, complaint risk noted; the scheduled reviews carry the rest forward from here.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The loop, both directions &mdash; the review moved up, addresses fixed, roles and numbers given &mdash; and Libby told exactly what was shared with dad.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The debrief &mdash; the team warned about the family&rsquo;s hostility and the GMC threat, so nobody walks into the next appointment unprepared.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A note that ends at &ldquo;complaint risk noted&rdquo; preserves the meeting and loses its winnings &mdash; the conversion to co-monitor only survives if the promises made in the room become actions before Friday. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Hostile family&rdquo; on the team board undoes the whole station &mdash; a bereaved, frightened father who was finally heard gets re-labelled the enemy at the next handover. Brief the team on the plan, not a warning poster. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The loop, closed in both directions. Toward him: the review moved up and booked, both addresses on the file today, the monitoring role and the what-to-watch-for written down, the duty-line number in his hand &mdash; the true fault fixed structurally, not just apologised for. And toward her: Libby told, before she hears it anywhere else, exactly what was and wasn&rsquo;t shared with her dad &mdash; because the pass never purchases the father&rsquo;s trust with the daughter&rsquo;s, and a confidence held in secret from its owner isn&rsquo;t held at all. Documented, team briefed on the plan, done.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Answer the fear, not the armour</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>correctness</b>: the defence &mdash; his facts are wrong and the service&rsquo;s conduct was sound, so the room fills with Gillick, NICE, correspondence policy and the PALS office &mdash; the map&rsquo;s named trap, defending the service instead of hearing the fear underneath: every point won, and a frightened man sent to his car to act alone. The second wears the face of <b>compassion</b>: the surrender &mdash; faults conceded that never happened, the treatment undermined &ldquo;between you and me&rdquo;, a daughter&rsquo;s confidence traded for her father&rsquo;s calm &mdash; peace purchased with things that were never yours to sell. Note what the faces share: <b>in both, the fear underneath is never reached</b> &mdash; one outshouts it, one pays it off &mdash; and the station&rsquo;s real work never starts. The pass runs the sequence the trap is designed to break: the person received before any fact is corrected; the worry invited with one sentence; the fear, when it surfaces, honoured and connected out loud to today; the corrections then offered gently, once; the one true fault owned precisely and alone, with its fix; both confidences held &mdash; hers as a safety net explained, his entitlement given generously; the hard line on the medication kept at full warmth; and the loop closed in both directions before the clinic moves on.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap, verbatim: defending the service instead of hearing the fear underneath &mdash; the protocols recited, the printout dismissed, the complaint form offered as an exit. And its mirror fails with it: the surrender &mdash; invented faults conceded, the prescription undermined to its patient&rsquo;s father, her words promised to whoever shouts loudest.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the opening that asks instead of answers &mdash; <em>&ldquo;I&rsquo;d be frightened too. Tell me what&rsquo;s worrying you most&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the apology with edges: <em>&ldquo;you learning this from a pharmacy bag is our failure, and I&rsquo;m sorry &mdash; both addresses go on the file today.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, the family this construct belongs to: this is the domain&rsquo;s grammar in its loudest weather &mdash; warm and unmovable, at volume. The flagship taught the warm no; this station teaches its sibling, the warm not-conceding: nothing untrue admitted, nothing sacred traded, and the person still held throughout. The same discipline will carry you through the complaint, the bad news, and every relatives&rsquo; room of your career. Second, the transfer Exam Mode tests: the same anatomy on a relative you have not met, in a different service, with a different fear underneath &mdash; because the skill was never about fluoxetine or fathers. It is the fear heard before the facts, and it must survive a change of costume.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these to every relatives&rsquo; room</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Anger is fear wearing armour &mdash; answer the fear.</b> The anger received as information, the wound named back accurately, and the one question asked early: <em>tell me what&rsquo;s worrying you most.</em> Nobody de-escalates a person they haven&rsquo;t heard.</li>
      <li><b>Person first, facts after.</b> The same correction bounces off armour and lands on someone who feels heard. Then: gently, once, without triumph &mdash; and the almost-right claims answered with real honesty, because almost-right is what frightened people act on.</li>
      <li><b>Apologise with precision.</b> Find the one true fault and own it specifically, unhedged, with its fix &mdash; and concede nothing else. Never the invented faults, never &ldquo;sorry you feel that way&rdquo;: precision is what sincerity sounds like.</li>
      <li><b>Hold both confidences.</b> The patient&rsquo;s words protected &mdash; and the protection explained as their safety net, not brandished as a wall &mdash; while the relative&rsquo;s real entitlement is given generously and unprompted: the plan, the medication, the warning signs, a role.</li>
      <li><b>Have the honest drug conversation.</b> The frightening data owned plainly, monitoring presented as its answer, the complaint about side effects taken seriously enough to change something &mdash; and never &ldquo;between you and me.&rdquo; A parent talked down to acts alone.</li>
      <li><b>Convert, then close the loop both ways.</b> The relative brought inside the plan &mdash; a job, a number that answers, the review moved up, the true fault fixed structurally &mdash; and the patient told exactly what was shared about them. Trust is never bought from one person with another&rsquo;s.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the same anatomy at a different bedside &mdash; an angry daughter, an elderly father, a true fault buried in the fury, and a promise you don&rsquo;t know about yet.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> You are the duty doctor on the older-adult ward. Arthur, 78, admitted two weeks ago with worsening dementia and agitation, had a witnessed fall in the day room yesterday morning &mdash; he stood from his chair unaided, went over, and bruised his left cheek and forearm. Post-fall checks were completed and a hip X-ray was clear. No sedation was involved; he takes no sedating medication. The ward intended to phone the family the same day; the call was not made until this morning. His daughter Andrea, 45, has arrived at the nurses&rsquo; station demanding &ldquo;the doctor responsible&rdquo; and is being shown into the relatives&rsquo; room. Speak with her.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Receive the anger first &mdash; her worry invited before any fact is corrected or any process defended.</li>
            <li>Find the fear underneath &mdash; there is something driving this beyond the bruise. Ask for it.</li>
            <li>Correct gently, after &mdash; what actually happened, once she&rsquo;s been heard; concede nothing untrue.</li>
            <li>Own the true fault precisely &mdash; one thing here genuinely went wrong. Find it, apologise for it specifically, fix it.</li>
            <li>Close by bringing her inside &mdash; a concrete plan, a named contact, and the promise kept keepable.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Andrea &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Andrea, 45, a payroll clerk. You open loud and accusatory, on your feet: &ldquo;He&rsquo;s got bruises all over his face and nobody told me for a DAY. What are you doing to him in here? Are you drugging him to keep him quiet? Because I&rsquo;ll go to the papers, I mean it.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          &bull; Your facts are almost right and materially wrong: it is one bruised cheek and a forearm, not &ldquo;all over&rdquo;; the fall was witnessed, checks were done, the X-ray was clear; no sedation exists anywhere in his chart. Do not correct yourself &mdash; make the candidate do it, and punish them if they do it before hearing you.<br>
          &bull; The true fault is real: the family should have been rung the same day and wasn&rsquo;t until this morning. If the candidate owns this specifically and says what changes, soften noticeably. If they defend it (&ldquo;the ward was busy&rdquo;) or drown it in broad sorries, escalate.<br>
          &bull; The fear underneath: when your father still knew things, you promised him he would never end up &ldquo;in a place like this&rdquo; &mdash; and you signed the admission papers yourself. The guilt is eating you. Reveal it only into genuine warmth, after being asked what&rsquo;s really worrying you &mdash; e.g. <em>(quieter)</em> &ldquo;I promised him&hellip; I sat in his kitchen and promised him, and then I signed the forms.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish-lines, both directions:</b> if the candidate defends the ward or corrects you first &mdash; &ldquo;don&rsquo;t you DARE tell me about protocols&rdquo; &mdash; demand a transfer and the complaints number, get louder. If the candidate concedes untruths (&ldquo;you&rsquo;re right, this shouldn&rsquo;t have happened&rdquo;, agreeing he&rsquo;s been neglected or sedated) &mdash; say &ldquo;I want that in writing. Now.&rdquo; and hold out your phone. Station dead; mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; If heard, apologised to precisely, and brought inside the plan &mdash; agree, wearily: &ldquo;&hellip;just ring me. Whatever happens, however small. I have to be the one who knows.&rdquo; That is the pass.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was she heard before any correction, did the promise surface, was the late call owned precisely with nothing untrue conceded, and did she leave inside the plan? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="recv"><td colspan="2"><b>The receiving (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="recv">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recv"></td><td>The anger received without defence &mdash; no protocol, no correction, in the first minute.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recv"></td><td>Her wound named back accurately &mdash; a day without a phone call, her dad&rsquo;s bruised face.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recv"></td><td>The worry invited with a direct question &mdash; and silence held for the answer.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="fear"><td colspan="2"><b>The fear</b><span class="dc" data-dc="fear">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>The question behind the question pursued &mdash; not settled for the surface complaint.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>The promise and the guilt received fully &mdash; honoured, not rushed past.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fear"></td><td>Connected out loud to today &mdash; the guilt named as what makes every bruise an accusation.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="facts"><td colspan="2"><b>The facts</b><span class="dc" data-dc="facts">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="facts"></td><td>The record corrected gently, once, after hearing &mdash; the witnessed fall, the checks, the clear X-ray, no sedation.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="facts"></td><td>Nothing untrue conceded &mdash; no neglect admitted, no sedation apologised for, no &ldquo;in writing&rdquo;.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="fault"><td colspan="2"><b>The fault</b><span class="dc" data-dc="fault">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fault"></td><td>The late call owned precisely and unhedged &mdash; the one true fault, apologised for by itself.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fault"></td><td>The fix named &mdash; what changes so it cannot happen again, said in concrete terms.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Brought inside &mdash; same-day contact agreed, a named person to ring, her observations wanted.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>The falls plan explained &mdash; what the ward is doing, honestly including what cannot be promised.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>The loop stated &mdash; documented, the team briefed on the plan (not a &ldquo;hostile family&rdquo; warning), the complaint route offered without defensiveness.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Andrea moves the anatomy to a new bedside and strips away the CAMHS scaffolding &mdash; no Gillick, no printout, no medication debate &mdash; leaving the pure sequence exposed: almost-right accusations (&ldquo;all over&rdquo;, &ldquo;drugging him&rdquo;), one true fault hiding among them (the call that came a day late), and a fear underneath that explains everything (a kitchen-table promise, and her own signature on the admission papers). Her punish-lines run both ways: the defence gets the transfer demand and the complaints number; the surrender gets &ldquo;I want that in writing&rdquo; and a phone held out &mdash; because conceding untruths to an angry relative isn&rsquo;t kindness, it&rsquo;s ammunition. The pass is Darren&rsquo;s station transposed: heard first, the promise reached, the late call owned alone, and a daughter converted from adversary to the first person the ward rings.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->
<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>Same skill, new relative. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Darren proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same anatomy on a relative you have not met</b> &mdash; a different service, a different fury, a different fear underneath &mdash; timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam relative</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Patrick</b>, 61, a retired welder, is in the relatives&rsquo; room at 9am with a discharge letter in his hand. His wife Noreen, 57, went home from your ward ten days ago after a planned discharge she agreed to; last night she was readmitted, having stopped her medication. The letter promised a follow-up phone call within forty-eight hours. Nobody rang. He is on his feet: &ldquo;Ten days. You had her ten days and shipped her out to free up a bed. It says here someone would ring in forty-eight hours. Nobody rang. NOBODY. And now she&rsquo;s back worse than ever &mdash; so which of you geniuses signed this?&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for the opening move with Darren &mdash; the anger received, the wound named, and the worry invited, before a single fact is corrected.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Mr Boyd &#8212; I&#8217;m glad you came in&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No defence anywhere in it &mdash; not one protocol, policy, or correction, however gently put.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The anger legitimised &mdash; his fear acknowledged as something any parent would feel.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> His wound named back accurately &mdash; the pharmacy bag, the three silent weeks: his story, heard.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The worry invited with a direct question &mdash; and room left for the answer.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the fear invited before the facts &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Answer the fear, not the armour.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the sequence is installed: the person received before any fact, the worry invited in one sentence, the fear connected out loud to today, the corrections gentle and single, the true fault owned precisely and alone, both confidences held, the hard line kept warm, and the loop closed in both directions. Prove the transfer on Patrick&rsquo;s discharge letter in Exam Mode, then take Andrea&rsquo;s relatives&rsquo; room to your colleagues &mdash; a promise you don&rsquo;t know about, and punish-lines in both directions. Station 7.3 brings the quietest weather in the domain: news that changes a life, and the ten seconds of silence that decide the station.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="46_Station_7.3_Breaking_Bad_News.html">Continue &middot; Station 7.3: Breaking Bad News</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

