  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '46_Station_7.3_Breaking_Bad_News'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 7.3 &middot; Domain 7</span>
      <span class="tag">Difficult Conversations</span><span class="tag">Breaking Bad News</span>
      <span class="tag">Any Specialty</span><span class="tag">The Silence That Stands</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Breaking Bad News</h1>
    <p class="sub">Aaron, 24, has come to his early intervention appointment with his jacket on, like a man not planning to stay. Six weeks ago he was admitted with a first psychotic episode; he&rsquo;s better, he&rsquo;s back at the gym &mdash; and he&rsquo;s done with the word &ldquo;episode&rdquo;. His uncle Vic went into hospital at twenty-five and never properly came out, and Aaron has been on the websites. Today he asks the question directly: <em>is it schizophrenia?</em> The structure of breaking bad news is learnable in an afternoon. <b>The station is failed in the ten seconds after the news lands &mdash; when the silence should stand, and the frightened candidate fills it.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; In this room it becomes the stillness test: when a direct question deserves a direct answer, does this doctor say the word &mdash; plainly, kindly, without fog and without an instant &ldquo;but&rdquo;? And then comes the real examination: the ten seconds after. Does the silence stand &mdash; the news left in the patient&rsquo;s hands, presence held, nothing rescued &mdash; or does the candidate&rsquo;s own discomfort flood the room with recovery statistics? Is the impossible question answered at its true level? Is the hope kept honest &mdash; real differences, no promises? And does he leave with three things he can hold, or thirteen he can&rsquo;t?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Every candidate arrives knowing a framework for bad news &mdash; warn, tell, pause, chunk, check, close &mdash; and the station tests whether the framework survives contact with a real face. Four things are being watched. <b>The word, said plainly:</b> the diagnosis delivered in its own name &mdash; not &ldquo;a psychosis-spectrum picture&rdquo;, not &ldquo;an evolving process&rdquo; &mdash; because fog feels like kindness and functions as abandonment: the patient gets the real answer that night, alone, from the worst corner of the internet. <b>The silence, allowed to stand:</b> the news is the patient&rsquo;s to react to, not yours to manage away &mdash; and the ten seconds after the word are where he takes ownership of it; fill them and you take the news back. <b>The impossible question, answered honestly:</b> every disclosure produces one &mdash; <em>is this the whole story of me?</em> &mdash; and it is never really asking for statistics; it is asked at the level of identity and fear, and it must be answered there, without false precision and without a dodge. And <b>the hope, kept honest:</b> what can truly be offered &mdash; treatment that works, real differences from the past, a plan, a person &mdash; given fully, and never inflated into promises that the illness may not keep.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like compassion. Toward the fog &mdash; the word withheld &ldquo;to avoid labels&rdquo;, the diagnosis dissolved into mist, the warning shot fired forever while the bullet never comes: warned endlessly, told never &mdash; and an unnamed illness cannot justify a named treatment, so the medication logic dies with the word. And toward the smother &mdash; the map&rsquo;s named trap: delivering the news, then filling the silence that should stand &mdash; the word said at second four and buried by second six under recovery rates, trials and brochures, the patient&rsquo;s ten seconds spent on the doctor&rsquo;s comfort. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure never says the word. The other says it and instantly buries it. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 7.3</b> &middot; The early intervention clinic &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The fog</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The smother</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE FOG.</b> The question asked directly &mdash; and answered with mist. Watch a diagnosis dissolve, and the treatment&rsquo;s logic dissolve with it.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Aaron</span><span class="txt"><em>(sitting forward, jacket still on, like a man not planning to stay)</em> They keep saying &ldquo;an episode&rdquo;. Episode&rsquo;s a word for telly, doctor. Six weeks I&rsquo;ve been taking the tablets, doing the groups, and nobody will say the actual word. I&rsquo;ve been on the websites. My uncle Vic went into hospital at twenty-five and never properly came out &mdash; Christmases in the day room, the lot. So no leaflets, no &ldquo;spectrum&rdquo;. Look at me straight and tell me: is it schizophrenia?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(a small, careful smile)</em> I hear you, Aaron &mdash; but we really do try not to rush to labels. Diagnosis at this stage is honestly more art than science. What we can say is that you experienced a psychotic episode, and plenty of people have one episode and never have another. Let&rsquo;s focus on how well you&rsquo;re doing &mdash; and the label question can wait until the longer picture declares itself, hmm?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>He asked a direct question and received a policy. &ldquo;More art than science&rdquo; may soothe the speaker; it tells the man in the jacket nothing &mdash; and &ldquo;plenty never have another&rdquo; floats without its honest other half. The fog feels like kindness. It functions as abandonment: he came here to hear the word in a room with somebody in it, and he will now hear it tonight, alone, from the worst corner of the internet.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Aaron</span><span class="txt"><em>(leaning further forward)</em> So it&rsquo;s NOT schizophrenia? Because if it&rsquo;s a one-off &mdash; if it&rsquo;s stress, whatever &mdash; then why am I on tablets that make me feel like porridge? Go on. Give me one reason to keep taking them.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(shuffling the papers)</em> Let&rsquo;s not get ahead of ourselves &mdash; I didn&rsquo;t say that either. The medication is&hellip; think of it as a precaution while things settle. A safety net. We&rsquo;ll review everything in due course, and honestly, Aaron, you&rsquo;re doing so well that I don&rsquo;t want today to get bogged down in terminology. Same time next month?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Watch the fog reach the medication: an unnamed illness cannot justify a named treatment, and &ldquo;a precaution&rdquo; has just told a 24-year-old that the tablets are optional. The adherence conversation of the next five years was lost in that sentence. And note the tell &mdash; &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t want today to get bogged down&rdquo;: the fog was never for his comfort. It was for the speaker&rsquo;s.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 2:25pm)</em> Diagnosis discussed in broad terms. Patient keen on labels &mdash; deferred pending longitudinal picture. Doing well; continue aripiprazole. Review 4/52. <em>(That night, 1:40am: Aaron, ten pages deep on his phone, his uncle&rsquo;s face in every thread &mdash; reading the word nobody would say to him, in the dark, with nobody in the room)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Deferred pending longitudinal picture&rdquo; &mdash; the picture was in the chair, asking. Six weeks of treatment, a classic presentation, a direct question from a competent adult: the deferral protected nobody but the person holding the pen. The station&rsquo;s question was never diagnostic. It was whether this doctor would stand inside the truth with him &mdash; and he is standing in it alone, at 1:40am, with the porridge tablets on the nightstand looking more optional by the hour.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE SMOTHER.</b> The word said &mdash; and buried within two seconds. Watch a man&rsquo;s ten seconds get spent on the doctor&rsquo;s comfort.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Aaron</span><span class="txt"><em>(sitting forward, jacket still on, like a man not planning to stay)</em> They keep saying &ldquo;an episode&rdquo;. Episode&rsquo;s a word for telly, doctor. Six weeks I&rsquo;ve been taking the tablets, doing the groups, and nobody will say the actual word. I&rsquo;ve been on the websites. My uncle Vic went into hospital at twenty-five and never properly came out &mdash; Christmases in the day room, the lot. So no leaflets, no &ldquo;spectrum&rdquo;. Look at me straight and tell me: is it schizophrenia?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(bright, brisk, already reaching for the printer)</em> Yes &mdash; it&rsquo;s schizophrenia &mdash; BUT, and this is the important bit, there has genuinely never been a better time to get this diagnosis. Recovery rates have been transformed. Early intervention changes everything &mdash; that&rsquo;s us! There are new treatments, apps, peer groups &mdash; there&rsquo;s a footballer, actually, I forget the name &mdash; honestly, some of our patients say the diagnosis was the best thing that happened to them. Let me print you our recovery pack, it&rsquo;s brilliant&mdash;</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The word said at second four and buried by second six &mdash; and the &ldquo;BUT&rdquo; is doing the burying: everything after it exists to stop the news from landing. For whose sake? He has waited six weeks for this sentence; the ten seconds that should now be his &mdash; to stare at the window, to feel the floor move &mdash; are being spent on a printer and a footballer whose name the speaker can&rsquo;t recall. The news was delivered. It was not allowed to arrive.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Aaron</span><span class="txt"><em>(quietly, into the stream of it)</em> My uncle Vic&mdash;</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(over the top, wagging a kind finger)</em> &mdash;and you must NOT compare yourself to your uncle! Different era, different treatments, completely different outcomes &mdash; comparing is honestly the worst thing you can do to yourself. Now &mdash; the recovery college runs a brilliant six-week course, Tuesdays, and I&rsquo;m going to pop you on the list, and there&rsquo;s a carers&rsquo; evening your mum would love&hellip;</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>His one attempt to react &mdash; two words &mdash; talked over and ruled inadmissible. The uncle IS the reaction: Vic is the door to every fear in this room, and it has just been shut with a course timetable. Reassurance this fast isn&rsquo;t landing on him; it&rsquo;s bouncing off a man who has stopped listening, fired by a doctor who never started. The examiner&rsquo;s pen moves: the silence never stood, and neither did the patient&rsquo;s chance to own his own news.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 2:20pm)</em> Dx disclosed. Took news well &mdash; engaged with psychoeducation. Extensive recovery-focused discussion; signposted to recovery college and peer support. Upbeat at close. <em>(At the bus stop, the recovery pack sits unopened on his knees. He could not repeat a single sentence said after the word. The first person to sit with him inside the news will be nobody)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Took news well&rdquo; &mdash; the map&rsquo;s named trap in four words: delivering the news, then filling the silence that should stand. He took nothing; there was no room to take it. The silence after bad news isn&rsquo;t empty &mdash; it is where the patient&rsquo;s reaction was going to live, and it was bricked up with brochures. From the chair, this scores like the fog &mdash; because in both takes, the man who asked for the truth never got to hold it.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The word, plainly &mdash; then the hardest thing in the station: nothing. Ten seconds. Then the impossible question, answered at its true level, and a close he can carry.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Aaron</span><span class="txt"><em>(sitting forward, jacket still on, like a man not planning to stay)</em> They keep saying &ldquo;an episode&rdquo;. Episode&rsquo;s a word for telly, doctor. Six weeks I&rsquo;ve been taking the tablets, doing the groups, and nobody will say the actual word. I&rsquo;ve been on the websites. My uncle Vic went into hospital at twenty-five and never properly came out &mdash; Christmases in the day room, the lot. So no leaflets, no &ldquo;spectrum&rdquo;. Look at me straight and tell me: is it schizophrenia?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">You&rsquo;ve asked me straight, Aaron, and you&rsquo;ve earned straight &mdash; so I&rsquo;ll tell you now. Yes. Putting everything together &mdash; the episode, the pattern of it, these six weeks &mdash; the diagnosis is schizophrenia. <em>(And then the candidate does the hardest thing in the station: nothing. The pen goes down. Ten seconds. The radiator ticks. Aaron looks at the window, at the floor, at his hands. The candidate stays exactly where he can find them &mdash; present, unhurried, not reaching for a single leaflet &mdash; and lets the news be his.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Both halves, in order. The word said plainly &mdash; in its own name, prefaced by one breath of warning (&ldquo;so I&rsquo;ll tell you now&rdquo;), with no fog and no instant &ldquo;but&rdquo;. And then the silence, allowed to stand: this is the station&rsquo;s decisive act, and it is a non-act. The news belongs to him; the ten seconds are where he takes ownership of it. Fill them and you take it back. The presence &mdash; still, warm, findable &mdash; is doing more than any sentence could.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Aaron</span><span class="txt"><em>(finally, flat, still looking at the window)</em> So that&rsquo;s me, then. That&rsquo;s the whole story of me. Vic mark two &mdash; Christmases in the day room, the works. <em>(turning)</em> Go on. Tell me I&rsquo;m wrong.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I&rsquo;m not going to pretend the word isn&rsquo;t heavy &mdash; you&rsquo;ve watched it be heavy your whole life. But you asked for exact, so here&rsquo;s exact. Schizophrenia is something you have. It was never going to be the story of you &mdash; you&rsquo;re six weeks in and already back at the gym, already asking better questions than half my colleagues. And I won&rsquo;t wave Vic away &mdash; he&rsquo;s the reason this terrifies you, and it means you know things about this illness most people never see. But here is what&rsquo;s honestly different: your uncle got 1980s treatment, a decade too late. You got this decade&rsquo;s, six weeks in. That&rsquo;s not a promise &mdash; nobody honest will promise you the future. It&rsquo;s a real difference, and it&rsquo;s yours. Now &mdash; what do you want to know today, and what would you rather keep for next week?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The impossible question answered at its true level &mdash; identity, not statistics: <em>something you have, never the story of you</em>. The uncle received in full, not ruled inadmissible &mdash; and even honoured: &ldquo;you know things most people never see.&rdquo; The era difference offered as fact, with the promise explicitly withheld &mdash; hope kept honest. And then the agenda handed back to him &mdash; chunked at his pace &mdash; because a man in shock owns the syllabus, and the check-question is how you find out what he can carry today.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Three things today, then &mdash; that&rsquo;s all. One: this sheet &mdash; one page, in my writing; no websites tonight, and if you must look, only these two places. Two: next Thursday, you and me, a full hour &mdash; bring every question you think of, and bring your mum if you&rsquo;d like her there. Three: the tablets finally have a reason with a name on it &mdash; they treat schizophrenia, and they&rsquo;re a large part of why these six weeks went the way they did, so nothing changes there unless we decide it together. Two things I&rsquo;m naming today so they never ambush you &mdash; driving, and what you tell work. Real conversations, next week, done properly. Tonight &mdash; who&rsquo;s home? <em>(His mum.)</em> Good. I&rsquo;m writing all of this down, and I&rsquo;m here Thursday.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The holdable close: three things, not thirteen &mdash; shock has a carrying capacity, and this candidate is packing for it. The websites pre-empted honestly instead of wished away; the medication finally tethered to a named illness &mdash; the adherence conversation the fog lost forever is won here in one sentence; the hard topics named, not sprung and not hidden; the tonight-question asked plainly. He came in his jacket for a word. He leaves with the word, three things he can carry, and somebody in it with him.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on news you have not delivered &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; The warning shot &mdash; when he&rsquo;s already fired first</h3>
      <p class="stem">The frameworks say: align, warn, invite. He has just demanded it straight, no leaflets. What happens to the warning shot?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">It runs in full &mdash; perception checked, invitation formalised, information needs mapped: the framework exists precisely for moments this big.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">It compresses to one breath &mdash; his request honoured as the invitation it is, with a single warning beat before the word: &ldquo;so I&rsquo;ll tell you now.&rdquo;</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">It vanishes &mdash; he asked for straight and any preamble now reads as another dodge; the word comes first and all the framing can follow after it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Running the full ritual at a man who has just begged you to skip it is the framework serving itself &mdash; every checklist question now sounds like the mist he came to end. The structure bends to the person, never the reverse. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Cold-opening the word skips the one beat that lets a body brace &mdash; even a man demanding straight needs the half-second of &ldquo;here it comes.&rdquo; Unwarned news doesn&rsquo;t land harder; it lands scattered. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The framework, compressed to its function: he has already given the invitation &mdash; honour it by name (&ldquo;you&rsquo;ve asked me straight, and you&rsquo;ve earned straight&rdquo;), and keep exactly one warning beat &mdash; <em>so I&rsquo;ll tell you now</em> &mdash; the breath that lets him brace. Alignment isn&rsquo;t a checklist; it&rsquo;s the proof you heard the terms he set. The whole apparatus of warning-and-inviting, delivered in nine words, because that&rsquo;s the size this patient needs it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; The word</h3>
      <p class="stem">The moment of telling. What does the sentence actually contain?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The careful version &mdash; &ldquo;a psychosis-spectrum presentation&rdquo;, &ldquo;an evolving picture&rdquo;: technically sound, and gentler than the loaded label.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The word itself &mdash; &ldquo;the diagnosis is schizophrenia&rdquo;: plainly, kindly, in its own name, with nothing appended to soften its landing.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The word cushioned &mdash; said, then immediately paired with the good news, so the diagnosis and the hope arrive together as one package.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Spectrum presentation&rdquo; is the fog wearing a lanyard &mdash; he will nod, leave, and google his way to the word at 1am without you. If the diagnosis is clear enough to treat, it is clear enough to say. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The instant pairing is the smother&rsquo;s opening move &mdash; the &ldquo;but&rdquo; arrives before the news has touched the ground, and everything after it exists to stop the landing. The hope is real; its moment is later. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The word, in its own name, unaccompanied: <em>the diagnosis is schizophrenia.</em> Plainly &mdash; because fog is abandonment with better manners; kindly &mdash; because plain and brutal are different registers; and alone &mdash; because the sentence that carries the news must not also carry its rebuttal. Everything else this station offers &mdash; the honest hope, the plan, the era difference &mdash; is real and coming. It just cannot live in this sentence, or the news never lands at all.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; The ten seconds</h3>
      <p class="stem">The word is out. He&rsquo;s looking at the window. The silence is one second old and already unbearable. What do the next ten seconds contain?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">A gentle check &mdash; &ldquo;are you okay? I know it&rsquo;s a lot&rdquo; &mdash; so he knows the silence is allowed and doesn&rsquo;t feel abandoned inside it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Nothing &mdash; the pen down, the body still, the presence held: the news left in his hands for as long as he needs to turn it over.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The bridge &mdash; a quiet start on what happens next, softly enough to interrupt nothing: shock absorbs facts better than it seems to.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Are you okay?&rdquo; two seconds in is the doctor&rsquo;s discomfort asking for reassurance &mdash; and it hands him a social task (answering you) at the exact moment he owes himself the private one. The silence was already allowed; saying so revokes it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Nothing said in the ten seconds after bad news is heard &mdash; shock absorbs almost none of it &mdash; and the soft bridge steals the only thing happening in the room: his reaction, forming. Whatever you say now, you will only say again later. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The station&rsquo;s decisive act is a non-act. The silence after the word isn&rsquo;t empty &mdash; it is where his reaction lives, where the news transfers from your mouth to his hands &mdash; and the discomfort you feel as it stretches is precisely the thing being examined: whose comfort gets rescued? The pen down, the body still, the face warm and findable &mdash; presence without rescue. He will end the silence himself, with the thing he actually needs to say &mdash; and that sentence is the station&rsquo;s real beginning.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; &ldquo;That&rsquo;s the whole story of me&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;Vic mark two &mdash; Christmases in the day room. Go on. Tell me I&rsquo;m wrong.&rdquo; What is he actually asking &mdash; and what answers it?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">A prognosis question &mdash; answered with the real figures: remission rates, functional outcomes, what the early-intervention evidence shows.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">An identity question &mdash; answered there: something he has, never the story of him &mdash; with Vic received and honoured, not ruled inadmissible.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A distortion to interrupt &mdash; catastrophising this early sets like concrete; the comparison gently but firmly closed before it takes root.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>He didn&rsquo;t ask what percentage of him is doomed &mdash; and figures fired at an identity question sail straight past it. The numbers have a place, later, if he asks for numbers. This wasn&rsquo;t that ask. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Don&rsquo;t compare yourself to your uncle&rdquo; slams the one door he opened &mdash; Vic isn&rsquo;t a distortion, he&rsquo;s the whole architecture of Aaron&rsquo;s fear, and he has to be walked through, not bricked up. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The impossible question, answered at its true level: <em>schizophrenia is something you have &mdash; it was never going to be the story of you</em> &mdash; with the evidence drawn from his own last six weeks, not a journal. And Vic received in full: the fear honoured as earned knowledge (&ldquo;you know things most people never see&rdquo;), then the era difference laid beside it as fact &mdash; 1980s treatment a decade late versus this decade&rsquo;s at six weeks. Identity first, honesty throughout, and not one statistic he didn&rsquo;t ask for.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; How much, today</h3>
      <p class="stem">The word has landed; the first question is answered. Between here and the door lies everything else &mdash; prognosis, medication, driving, work, relapse signatures. How much of it happens today?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">All of it, carefully &mdash; he asked for straight, the shock will only grow overnight, and a complete picture today beats a week of dread.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">What he chooses &mdash; the agenda handed to him in one question, chunks offered at his pace, and the rest named and parked for next week.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Almost none &mdash; the word was today&rsquo;s work; everything else lands better on a settled mind, so protect him from detail until the review.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A complete picture delivered into shock is a picture nobody keeps &mdash; he&rsquo;ll retain three sentences of your forty, chosen at random. Straight was about honesty, not volume. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Protective withholding is the fog&rsquo;s younger sibling &mdash; and a man with a new diagnosis and an empty evening will not wait for your review; he&rsquo;ll fill the vacuum with the internet&rsquo;s version. Ask what he wants; give what he asks. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The syllabus is his: <em>what do you want to know today &mdash; and what would you rather keep for next week?</em> One question converts the lecture into a conversation, sizes the information to what shock can actually carry, and treats him as the adult who asked for straight. The chunks come at his pace with a check after each; the unchosen topics get named and parked &mdash; visible, not hidden &mdash; so nothing ambushes him later and nothing is being withheld. That balance is the skill.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; The hope</h3>
      <p class="stem">He needs something to leave with. What does honest hope actually consist of?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Momentum &mdash; recovery rates, the transformed landscape, the footballer who went public: belief is protective, and today it needs building.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Real differences, no promises &mdash; treatment that demonstrably works, his own six weeks as evidence, the future explicitly unpromised.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Realism &mdash; a serious illness deserves a serious tone: manage expectations now so that no relapse ever arrives as a broken promise.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Inflated hope is a loan the illness may call in &mdash; the first bad week after &ldquo;recovery rates transformed!&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t just disappoint, it discredits every true thing you said alongside it. Hope has to be load-bearing. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Expectation-management as a philosophy leaves him with a diagnosis and a shrug &mdash; bleakness isn&rsquo;t honesty, it&rsquo;s just pessimism wearing honesty&rsquo;s coat. There are true good things; withholding them is its own distortion. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Hope that can bear weight: built only from things that are true &mdash; treatment that works and is already working (his six weeks are the exhibit), an era genuinely unlike his uncle&rsquo;s, a team and a plan and a person &mdash; with the promise explicitly withheld: <em>that&rsquo;s not a promise; nobody honest will promise you the future. It&rsquo;s a real difference, and it&rsquo;s yours.</em> Honest hope survives bad weeks because it never claimed there wouldn&rsquo;t be any. That durability is the entire point of it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; The close</h3>
      <p class="stem">Seven minutes are nearly gone. What does he actually walk out holding?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The full scaffold &mdash; care plan drafted, relapse signature listed, DVLA process explained, work disclosure rehearsed: certainty is the kindness now.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Three holdable things &mdash; one page, one appointment, one reason for the tablets &mdash; hard topics named for next week, and the tonight question asked.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Open space &mdash; no homework after news like this: the follow-up stands booked, and everything practical waits for a settled mind that can meet it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A scaffold built on shock collapses in the car park &mdash; five processes explained to a man who can currently hold three sentences is administration, not care. The certainty you&rsquo;re offering is for your checklist. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Open space is where the internet moves in &mdash; a man with a new diagnosis, an empty evening, and no page in his pocket will write his own plan by midnight, sourced from the worst places. Give him something to hold. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Packed for shock&rsquo;s carrying capacity: <em>three things today, that&rsquo;s all</em> &mdash; the one-page sheet (with the websites pre-empted, not wished away), the near-term hour with the door open to his mum, and the tablets finally tethered to a named illness &mdash; the adherence sentence that pays out for years. The hard topics &mdash; driving, work &mdash; named so they never ambush him, parked so they never crush him. And the tonight question &mdash; <em>who&rsquo;s home?</em> &mdash; asked plainly, because the first night with a new diagnosis is part of the station&rsquo;s duty. Documented, and the door held open: <em>I&rsquo;m here Thursday.</em></template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Say the word. Then let it land.</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>gentleness</b>: the fog &mdash; the word withheld &ldquo;to avoid rushing to labels&rdquo;, the diagnosis dissolved into spectrum-mist, the patient warned forever and told never &mdash; and the costs cascade quietly: he learns the word alone at 1am, the medication loses its reason the moment the illness loses its name, and the person who asked for the truth concludes that this building doesn&rsquo;t deal in it. The second wears the face of <b>positivity</b>: the smother &mdash; the map&rsquo;s named trap, delivering the news, then filling the silence that should stand &mdash; the word said and instantly buried under recovery rates and brochures, the patient&rsquo;s ten seconds spent on the doctor&rsquo;s discomfort, his one attempt to react talked over. Note what the faces share: <b>in both, the patient never gets to hold his own news</b> &mdash; one never hands it over, the other snatches it back &mdash; and &ldquo;took news well&rdquo; goes in the notes either way. The pass does both halves in order: the word in its own name, plainly and kindly, after one warning breath &mdash; and then nothing: the pen down, the presence held, the silence allowed to stand until he ends it himself with the thing he actually needs to say. Then the impossible question answered at the level it was asked, the agenda handed back, the hope built only from true things, and a close packed for what shock can carry.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap, verbatim: delivering the news, then filling the silence that should stand &mdash; the &ldquo;but&rdquo; at second six, the brochure reflex, the reaction talked over. And its mirror fails with it: the fog &mdash; the direct question answered with mist, the word withheld for the speaker&rsquo;s comfort, the treatment left orphaned by an illness nobody would name.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the word said plainly and then the pen going down &mdash; ten full seconds of held, warm, unhurried silence &mdash; and the impossible question answered where it was asked: <em>&ldquo;schizophrenia is something you have &mdash; it was never going to be the story of you.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, the family this construct belongs to: this is the domain&rsquo;s quietest weather, and the same two escapes are still the enemy &mdash; the fog is the flagship&rsquo;s soft yes wearing scrubs (comfort now, betrayal later), and the smother is the surrender&rsquo;s cousin (the room&rsquo;s temperature bought with things that weren&rsquo;t yours to spend &mdash; here, his ten seconds). Warm and unmovable translates here as <em>kind and plain</em>: the truth said fully, the person held fully, neither traded for the other. Second, the transfer Exam Mode tests: the same anatomy on news you have never delivered, to a man you have never met &mdash; because the skill was never about schizophrenia. It is the word, the silence, the impossible question, and the honest hope &mdash; and the news changes costume more than any station in this course.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these to every disclosure</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Warn in one breath, then say the word.</b> The diagnosis in its own name &mdash; plainly, kindly, unaccompanied. Fog is abandonment with better manners, and the sentence that carries the news must not also carry its rebuttal.</li>
      <li><b>Let the silence stand.</b> The ten seconds after the word are where the news transfers into the patient&rsquo;s hands. Pen down, body still, presence held &mdash; no rescue, no bridge, no &ldquo;are you okay?&rdquo; He ends the silence himself, and what he ends it with is the station&rsquo;s real beginning.</li>
      <li><b>Answer the impossible question where it was asked.</b> &ldquo;Is this the whole story of me?&rdquo; is an identity question &mdash; answer it with identity, not statistics. And receive the family ghost in full: the uncle, the mother &mdash; the fear&rsquo;s architecture must be walked through, never ruled inadmissible.</li>
      <li><b>Hand over the agenda.</b> <em>What do you want to know today &mdash; and what would you rather keep for next week?</em> Chunk at their pace, check after each piece, and name-and-park the rest in plain sight. Shock owns the syllabus.</li>
      <li><b>Build hope only from true things.</b> Treatment that works, differences that are real, their own recent evidence &mdash; with the promise explicitly withheld. Honest hope survives bad weeks because it never claimed there wouldn&rsquo;t be any.</li>
      <li><b>Close for shock&rsquo;s carrying capacity.</b> Three holdable things &mdash; a page, an appointment, a reason for the treatment; the hard topics named for next time; the websites pre-empted; and the tonight question asked: <em>who&rsquo;s home?</em> Then document, and hold the door open.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the same anatomy with different news &mdash; a diagnosis she half-expects and wholly dreads, a mother she hasn&rsquo;t mentioned yet, and an escape hatch she will offer you twice.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> First outpatient follow-up. Grace, 29, a wedding photographer, was discharged two weeks ago after a three-week admission with a first manic episode &mdash; grandiose plans, four days without sleep, spending she&rsquo;s still untangling. She has recovered well on medication. The team&rsquo;s conclusion is clear: bipolar disorder, type one. Nobody has yet said this to her. Today&rsquo;s job is to say it &mdash; and everything that must follow it.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Warn in one breath, then the word &mdash; in its own name, plainly, with nothing appended.</li>
            <li>Then nothing &mdash; the silence held for as long as she needs; her stillness is not agreement, and not fine.</li>
            <li>Receive whatever surfaces &mdash; there is something underneath her dread. Make room for it; do not manage it away.</li>
            <li>Answer her questions honestly at the level they&rsquo;re asked &mdash; including the ones with no comfortable answer.</li>
            <li>Close holdable &mdash; a few things she can carry, the treatment tethered to the named illness, the tonight question, the follow-up soon.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Grace &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Grace, 29. You open composed, almost businesslike &mdash; coat folded on your lap, answers brisk: &ldquo;I&rsquo;m much better, sleeping fine, back to editing. So &mdash; what&rsquo;s the verdict? I assume there is one.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          &bull; The dread: your mum has bipolar disorder. Your childhood was hospital corridors, missed birthdays, the &ldquo;good spells&rdquo; that never lasted. You have spent your whole adult life watching yourself for signs. Reveal this only if the word is said plainly AND the silence afterwards is genuinely held &mdash; then, quietly: &ldquo;My mum has this. <em>Has.</em> You&rsquo;d think I&rsquo;d have seen it coming in myself.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; The escape hatch, offered twice: &ldquo;But it could just have been the stress, couldn&rsquo;t it? Wedding season, no sleep &mdash; anyone would tip over.&rdquo; If the candidate co-signs the stress story or fogs the diagnosis (&ldquo;let&rsquo;s not label it yet&rdquo;), accept it with visible relief, mention you&rsquo;ll &ldquo;probably wind down the tablets before the spring bookings&rdquo;, and leave content. Station dead; mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; If the news is smothered &mdash; the word instantly buried under reassurance, your silence filled, your &ldquo;My mum&mdash;&rdquo; talked over &mdash; go polite and flat: nod, agree with everything, take the leaflets, disengage. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; The records question: &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t put the word bipolar in my notes &mdash; brides google everything, doctor.&rdquo; The honest answer you need: no false promise; what the record is, who can and cannot see it, and why an accurate record protects her. A candidate who promises to keep it out fails the moment.<br>
          &bull; The mother question, if the room has earned it: &ldquo;Will I end up like her?&rdquo; You need honesty with real differences &mdash; her untreated years versus your treated position, her era versus yours &mdash; and no promises. If honest and warm: soften, engage, ask one real question about the medication. That is the pass.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the word said plainly, did the silence stand, did the mother surface, was the stress escape declined honestly, and did she leave holding a real plan? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="tell"><td colspan="2"><b>The telling (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="tell">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="tell"></td><td>One warning breath before the word &mdash; not a cold open, not a ritual.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="tell"></td><td>The word said in its own name &mdash; &ldquo;bipolar disorder&rdquo; &mdash; plainly and kindly, no fog.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="tell"></td><td>Nothing appended &mdash; no instant &ldquo;but&rdquo;, no reassurance riding in the same sentence.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="sil"><td colspan="2"><b>The silence</b><span class="dc" data-dc="sil">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="sil"></td><td>The silence after the word genuinely held &mdash; no filling, no bridge, no &ldquo;are you okay?&rdquo;</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="sil"></td><td>Her stillness read correctly &mdash; not mistaken for taking it well; presence kept until she ended it.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="recv"><td colspan="2"><b>The receiving</b><span class="dc" data-dc="recv">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recv"></td><td>Her first reaction received in full &mdash; whatever it was, not managed away.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recv"></td><td>The mother heard completely &mdash; the childhood honoured, never ruled inadmissible.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recv"></td><td>The stress escape declined honestly &mdash; kindly, without co-signing and without a lecture.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="hon"><td colspan="2"><b>The honesty</b><span class="dc" data-dc="hon">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>The records question answered truly &mdash; no false promise; what the record is and why it protects her.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>&ldquo;Will I end up like her?&rdquo; answered with real differences and no promises.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hon"></td><td>The medication tethered to the named illness &mdash; and the spring wind-down met head-on, together-decisions only.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>A holdable close &mdash; a few carryable things, hard topics named for next time, follow-up soon.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>The tonight question asked &mdash; who&rsquo;s home &mdash; and the conversation documented.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Grace runs the anatomy against its two hardest enemies at once. Her composure invites the smother &mdash; a woman this brisk seems to need brightness, and the role-player punishes it with polite, flat disengagement. Her escape hatch invites the fog &mdash; &ldquo;it could just have been the stress&rdquo; is offered twice, sounds almost reasonable, and co-signing it ends the station with a relieved patient planning to stop her medication before wedding season. The mother is the buried heart &mdash; a childhood of hospital corridors, released only if the word is said and the silence truly stands &mdash; and she brings the two questions with no comfortable answers: the record that brides can google, and &ldquo;will I end up like her?&rdquo; The pass is Aaron&rsquo;s station transposed: the word, the silence, the ghost received, the hope built from true things &mdash; and a woman who arrived composed leaving with something real to hold instead.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->
<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>Same skill, different news. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Aaron proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the <b>same anatomy carrying news you have never delivered</b>, to a man you have never met &mdash; timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Martin</b>, 38, a driving instructor, watched his father die of Huntington&rsquo;s disease two years ago and requested predictive testing. Today he receives the result, and the result is gene-positive: he carries the expansion, and the disease will develop. He has asked to hear it alone &mdash; his wife Anna is in the corridor; their children are seven and four, and they do not know the word Huntington&rsquo;s exists. He sits very still, hands flat on his knees: &ldquo;Just say it, doctor. Whatever it is &mdash; just say it.&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for the moment of telling Aaron &mdash; the warning breath and the word, plainly &mdash; and then, in brackets, the stage direction you will obey for the ten seconds after.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="You&#8217;ve asked me straight, and you&#8217;ve earned straight&#8230; [then:&#8230;]"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> One warning breath present &mdash; a beat that lets him brace, not a ritual and not a cold open.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The word itself, in its own name &mdash; no fog, no spectrum, no &ldquo;evolving picture&rdquo;.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Nothing appended &mdash; no &ldquo;but&rdquo;, no reassurance, no recovery talk in the same breath.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The silence planned deliberately &mdash; your stage direction schedules nothing for ten seconds.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the word said plainly, the silence planned &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Say the word. Then let it land.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the anatomy is installed: one warning breath, the word in its own name, the ten seconds that belong to the patient, the impossible question answered where it was asked, the agenda handed back, hope built only from true things, and a close packed for what shock can carry. Prove the transfer on Martin&rsquo;s result in Exam Mode &mdash; the heaviest news in the course &mdash; then take Grace&rsquo;s follow-up to your colleagues: an escape hatch offered twice, and a mother in the walls. One station remains. Station 7.4 closes the domain: something genuinely went wrong, the person opposite knows it &mdash; and between the non-apology and the grovel sits the professional apology.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="47_Station_7.4_The_Complaint_and_the_Apology.html">Continue &middot; Station 7.4: The Complaint and the Apology</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

