</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">The complaint station is the domain&rsquo;s final examination because it removes the usual comfort: this time the service is in the wrong, the facts are against you, and the person opposite has them in a folder. Four things are being watched. <b>The hearing:</b> her account received in full, uninterrupted &mdash; because an apology issued before the listening is finished is a door closed in her face, however sorry it sounds. <b>The naming:</b> the fault said specifically, in active voice, with the actor still in the sentence &mdash; <em>we started you on a medication with a serious known risk and did not tell you</em> &mdash; never &ldquo;communication could have been clearer&rdquo;, never sorrow for her distress while the deed goes unmentioned. <b>The owning:</b> no comma-but &mdash; not &ldquo;sorry, but you were very unwell&rdquo; &mdash; and, just as vital, the apology&rsquo;s edges kept: everything true conceded fully, nothing untrue conceded at all, because an apology that buys peace with falsehoods hands the formal process a signed untruth. And <b>the change:</b> what happens next, personally and systemically, with the machinery named &mdash; incident report, written findings, dates &mdash; and the complaint route supported without a flicker of defence, because the apology is owed, not traded.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> two pulls, and both feel like survival. Toward the non-apology &mdash; the map&rsquo;s named trap: apologising for feelings while dodging what actually went wrong &mdash; the institutional reflex of passive voice and &ldquo;any distress caused&rdquo;, where the fault dissolves into process language and she leaves to file the forms hardened. And toward the grovel &mdash; peace bought fast with whatever&rsquo;s to hand: faults conceded that never happened, a colleague thrown under, promises no doctor can keep, and the apology slowly turning to face its speaker until she is somehow comforting you. Notice both pulls in the decisions below. We will come back to them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure never names the fault. The other names everything &mdash; including things that aren&rsquo;t true. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 7.4</b> &middot; The outpatient clinic &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The non-apology</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The grovel</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE NON-APOLOGY.</b> Sorrow for her feelings, passive voice for the facts. Watch a valid complaint get processed instead of answered &mdash; and hardened into a formal one.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Lorraine</span><span class="txt"><em>(sitting very upright, a folder squared on her knees, voice level)</em> You&rsquo;ll notice I&rsquo;m not shouting. I&rsquo;ve done my shouting at home. <em>(opens the folder)</em> Eight months ago your clinic started me on olanzapine. Nobody said one word about weight. Sixteen kilos, doctor. My GP says I&rsquo;m pre-diabetic now &mdash; her nurse caught it, not you; nobody here checked my blood for seven months. It&rsquo;s all written down, dates and all. PALS gave me the forms on the way in. Before I fill them in, I want to hear what you say about it.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(a practised, sympathetic tilt of the head)</em> I&rsquo;m very sorry to hear you&rsquo;ve had such a difficult time &mdash; weight changes can be terribly distressing, and I do understand the strength of feeling. In a busy clinic, information provision can sometimes be less thorough than we would ideally like, and monitoring pathways don&rsquo;t always run as smoothly as they should. Leaflets are, of course, routinely available at reception.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Count what&rsquo;s missing: an actor. &ldquo;Information provision&rdquo; failed; &ldquo;pathways&rdquo; didn&rsquo;t run &mdash; nobody did anything, weather simply happened to her. The sorrow is aimed entirely at her feelings while the deed goes unnamed &mdash; and she brought dates precisely because she predicted this voice. The leaflets line will appear, verbatim, in paragraph one of her formal complaint.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Lorraine</span><span class="txt"><em>(pen still capped, eyes steady)</em> That&rsquo;s not what I asked. I didn&rsquo;t ask whether you were sorry I&rsquo;m upset. I asked what you say about what was <em>done</em>. Name it. Was it wrong &mdash; yes or no?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(retreating half a step into the chair)</em> I can only apologise for any distress that&rsquo;s been caused. What I&rsquo;d gently say is that olanzapine remains a highly effective medication, and you were, if I may, extremely unwell at the time &mdash; the priority was treating the illness, and these decisions are always finely balanced. If you feel a formal complaint is the right route for you, PALS will of course take it forward.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>She offered a yes-or-no and got &ldquo;any distress caused&rdquo; &mdash; the apology with the deed surgically removed &mdash; followed by the comma-but wearing gloves: her illness deployed as the defence for her silence. Nobody disputed the drug&rsquo;s effectiveness; the complaint was the missing warning and the missing bloods, and neither has yet been mentioned by the only person in the room who owes them. &ldquo;If you feel&rdquo; converts her evidence back into emotion one last time.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 3:10pm)</em> Patient attended with concerns re: weight gain on olanzapine. Empathic discussion; reassured regarding indication for treatment. Advised of PALS process per her preference. No further action from clinic at this stage. <em>(That evening, at her kitchen table, Lorraine uncaps the pen and fills in the forms. Her first line: &ldquo;I came in person to give them one chance to be honest about it.&rdquo;)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Reassured regarding indication&rdquo; &mdash; she never questioned the indication; the note answers a complaint she didn&rsquo;t make and files away the one she did. This is the map&rsquo;s named trap, verbatim: apologising for feelings while dodging what actually went wrong. The formal complaint this consultation existed to make unnecessary is now being written &mdash; and its strongest exhibit is the consultation itself.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE GROVEL.</b> Everything conceded &mdash; the true, the untrue, a colleague, the treatment itself. Watch peace get bought with things that were never for sale.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Lorraine</span><span class="txt"><em>(sitting very upright, a folder squared on her knees, voice level)</em> You&rsquo;ll notice I&rsquo;m not shouting. I&rsquo;ve done my shouting at home. <em>(opens the folder)</em> Eight months ago your clinic started me on olanzapine. Nobody said one word about weight. Sixteen kilos, doctor. My GP says I&rsquo;m pre-diabetic now &mdash; her nurse caught it, not you; nobody here checked my blood for seven months. It&rsquo;s all written down, dates and all. PALS gave me the forms on the way in. Before I fill them in, I want to hear what you say about it.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(hand to chest, visibly stricken)</em> Oh, no. No, this is &mdash; I am so, so sorry. Sixteen kilos &mdash; I can&rsquo;t even imagine. Honestly, this is the kind of thing that keeps me up at night about this job &mdash; I feel absolutely terrible sitting here. You must have been so let down, and I&rsquo;m &mdash; look, I&rsquo;m just so sorry, truly, from me personally, for all of it.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Watch the apology rotate toward its speaker: what keeps <em>me</em> up at night, how <em>I</em> feel sitting here. She came with dates and a capped pen, asking for accountability &mdash; and she is being handed a doctor&rsquo;s feelings to hold. Nothing has yet been named: &ldquo;all of it&rdquo; is the grovel&rsquo;s version of &ldquo;any distress caused&rdquo; &mdash; total, and therefore empty. The self-flagellation feels humble. It is asking her for comfort.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Lorraine</span><span class="txt"><em>(a long look; then, evenly)</em> Right. So we agree it was wrong. Then tell me this &mdash; should I ever have been put on it at all? Because from where I&rsquo;m sitting, a drug that does this to people shouldn&rsquo;t be handed out in the first place.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(nodding before the sentence ends)</em> Between us? I honestly don&rsquo;t know what my colleague was thinking &mdash; I wouldn&rsquo;t have chosen it, not for you. We&rsquo;ll stop it today, completely, you have my word &mdash; and I&rsquo;ll personally guarantee nothing like this ever happens in this trust again. You&rsquo;ve been through enough. Whatever you need from me, it&rsquo;s yours.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>In four sentences: an untruth conceded and signed &mdash; the prescription was correct, and her formal complaint now carries a doctor&rsquo;s agreement that it wasn&rsquo;t; a colleague thrown under without a fact in hand; the one clinically dangerous move &mdash; abrupt cessation &mdash; offered as a gift; and a guarantee no human being can keep. Every purchase was made with currency that wasn&rsquo;t his: the truth, a colleague&rsquo;s name, her safety, the trust&rsquo;s word.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 3:15pm)</em> Long and emotional discussion. Agreed medication should not have been initiated; olanzapine stopped with immediate effect at patient&rsquo;s request. Personal assurances given. Patient left calmer. <em>(Two weeks later, the trust&rsquo;s formal response defends the original prescription as appropriate &mdash; directly contradicting what she was told in the room. At her kitchen table, Lorraine reads both documents side by side and understands that one of them lied to her. She no longer knows which.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Patient left calmer&rdquo; &mdash; and trusting less than when she arrived, because a person who will say anything to calm you down retroactively poisons everything they said. The trust&rsquo;s accurate letter now reads as a cover-up of the room&rsquo;s inaccurate kindness. And somewhere a correct medication has been stopped dead, unmonitored, three weeks before winter. The grovel scores like the non-apology &mdash; because in both takes, the one thing she asked for was never produced: the truth, with its edges.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The account heard whole. The fault named in active voice, owned without a but, kept to its true edges &mdash; and answered with machinery, dates, and her rights intact.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Lorraine</span><span class="txt"><em>(sitting very upright, a folder squared on her knees, voice level)</em> You&rsquo;ll notice I&rsquo;m not shouting. I&rsquo;ve done my shouting at home. <em>(opens the folder)</em> Eight months ago your clinic started me on olanzapine. Nobody said one word about weight. Sixteen kilos, doctor. My GP says I&rsquo;m pre-diabetic now &mdash; her nurse caught it, not you; nobody here checked my blood for seven months. It&rsquo;s all written down, dates and all. PALS gave me the forms on the way in. Before I fill them in, I want to hear what you say about it.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Then you&rsquo;ll have it &mdash; but you&rsquo;ve done the work of writing that folder, so I&rsquo;d like to hear it first, all of it, in your order. Take the time it needs; I&rsquo;m not watching the clock. <em>(She reads: the start date, the missing conversation, the creeping weight, the GP nurse&rsquo;s phone call, the seven blank months. The candidate listens without once reaching for the notes, and writes down her dates as she gives them.)</em> Thank you. You&rsquo;re right, and I&rsquo;m going to say it plainly. We started you on a medicine with a well-known, serious risk of weight gain and diabetes, and nobody told you. Then we failed for seven months to do the blood checks that exist precisely because of that risk. Both of those were wrong &mdash; not &ldquo;communication issues&rdquo;. Wrong. You were owed the information before the first tablet and the monitoring after it, and you got neither. I&rsquo;m sorry &mdash; for the sixteen kilos, for a diagnosis somebody else&rsquo;s nurse had to find, and for the fact that you needed a folder to be believed.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The hearing first &mdash; her account received whole, her dates written down in front of her: the folder honoured as evidence, not managed as emotion. Then the anatomy, exact: the fault named in active voice with the actor in the sentence (&ldquo;we started&rdquo;, &ldquo;nobody told&rdquo;, &ldquo;we failed&rdquo;), the word &ldquo;wrong&rdquo; said twice without flinching, no comma-but anywhere &mdash; and the regret aimed at <em>her</em> harm: the kilos, the diagnosis, the folder. Sorrow for deeds, not feelings.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Lorraine</span><span class="txt"><em>(the folder closing slowly)</em> Right. So we agree. Then answer me this, doctor &mdash; should I ever have been put on it at all? Because a drug that can do this&hellip; from where I&rsquo;m sitting it should never have been offered to me in the first place.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">No &mdash; and I won&rsquo;t pretend otherwise just to soften this, because you came for the truth and you&rsquo;re owed all of it. When you were referred here you were severely ill, and olanzapine was a sound choice &mdash; it very likely did much of the getting-better you&rsquo;ve managed since. The wrong was never the prescription. It was the silence around it: you should have been given the risks and the choice, and then watched properly. That is the part I&rsquo;m apologising for, and I won&rsquo;t widen it or shrink it. If I sat here and agreed the treatment itself was a mistake, I&rsquo;d be handing you a comfortable untruth &mdash; and you&rsquo;ve had enough of us deciding what you don&rsquo;t need to know.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The apology keeps its edges &mdash; the grovel-resistance move, done aloud: everything true conceded fully, nothing untrue conceded at all, and the refusal itself framed as respect (&ldquo;you&rsquo;ve had enough of us deciding what you don&rsquo;t need to know&rdquo;). The treatment defended where it was right, the silence owned where it was wrong &mdash; the line between them drawn precisely. This sentence is the one the trust&rsquo;s formal response will later agree with, word for word &mdash; which is exactly the point.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Now what changes &mdash; and I&rsquo;ll put dates on it. Today: a referral to the metabolic clinic, and before you leave I&rsquo;ll book us a proper hour to go through the options &mdash; there are alternatives with kinder profiles, and this time you&rsquo;ll have every number in front of you before any decision. One thing I must say as a doctor: nothing stops abruptly &mdash; that&rsquo;s the single genuinely dangerous move, so we change course together or not at all. This error goes through our incident system this week &mdash; that&rsquo;s a formal duty, not a favour &mdash; and you&rsquo;ll receive the findings in writing, from me, with my apology in the same words I&rsquo;ve used in this room. And the forms in your folder: filling them in is your right, and nothing about today asks you to trade it away. If you file, I&rsquo;ll help you set the timeline out accurately &mdash; your dates are better than our notes. You&rsquo;ll have my letter this week either way.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The apology becomes candour: machinery, named and dated &mdash; incident report this week, findings in writing, the same words on paper as in the room, so the documents and the doctor can never contradict each other. Her safety guarded inside the goodwill (nothing stops abruptly). And the complaint route supported without a flicker &mdash; even armed: &ldquo;your dates are better than our notes.&rdquo; The apology was owed, not traded; she can file and still have all of it. That is what makes it believable &mdash; and, very often, what makes filing unnecessary.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same skill on an error you must disclose yourself &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; The folder</h3>
      <p class="stem">She&rsquo;s calm, prepared, and asking what you say about it. What does the first minute actually do?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Apologise immediately &mdash; she came for an apology and delaying it even a minute risks looking like the institution playing for time.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Hear the folder first &mdash; her account invited in full, in her order, her dates written down in front of her &mdash; and only then respond.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Verify first &mdash; the notes checked against her timeline before anything is conceded, since an apology built on shaky facts can&rsquo;t stand.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>An apology fired before the listening is finished answers a complaint you haven&rsquo;t heard yet &mdash; and tells her the meeting&rsquo;s outcome was drafted before she sat down. She wrote that folder to be heard reading it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Reaching for the notes while she holds the dates converts her from complainant to suspect &mdash; and the essentials here aren&rsquo;t in dispute: the missing warning and the blank months are in your own records. Verification can follow the hearing. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The hearing is the first act of the apology. Her account invited whole &mdash; <em>in your order, take the time it needs</em> &mdash; and her dates written down as she gives them: the folder treated as evidence, not as emotion to be absorbed. Listening uninterrupted does two jobs at once: it gives the apology its exact shape, and it begins repaying the specific injury of this complaint &mdash; seven months of nobody paying attention.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; The grammar of the apology</h3>
      <p class="stem">The account is heard. Now the sentence that answers it &mdash; what does it apologise <em>for</em>?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Her experience &mdash; sincere sorrow for the distress, the shock of the diagnosis, and how let down these months have left her feeling.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The deeds &mdash; named specifically, in active voice: we started the drug without telling you the risk, and we failed to check your blood.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The shortfall &mdash; acknowledged honestly in measured terms: communication and monitoring fell below the standard she was entitled to expect.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Sorry for your distress&rdquo; apologises for her reaction to the wrong rather than the wrong &mdash; the classic feelings-apology, and she has already predicted it: it&rsquo;s why the folder has dates in it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Communication fell below the standard&rdquo; is the passive voice doing its oldest job: removing the actor. Nobody did anything; standards simply weren&rsquo;t met, somewhere, by nobody. She asked you to name it. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Sorrow for deeds, not feelings &mdash; the station&rsquo;s central grammar. Active voice, actor in the sentence, the facts in plain words: <em>we started you on a medicine with a serious known risk and nobody told you; then we failed for seven months to do the checks that exist because of that risk.</em> And the word itself said &mdash; <em>wrong</em> &mdash; because &ldquo;suboptimal&rdquo; is how institutions avoid it. The regret then lands on her harm, not yours: the kilos, the diagnosis, the folder she needed to be believed.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; The comma-but</h3>
      <p class="stem">There is real context: she was severely ill; the clinic was mid-crisis; the drug worked. Where does context belong?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Alongside the apology &mdash; owned and explained in one breath, because an apology without its reasons sounds like an admission of carelessness.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">After it, if she asks &mdash; the owning kept clean and unqualified; context offered later as information she&rsquo;s entitled to, never as defence.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Nowhere &mdash; explanations always read as excuses inside a complaint meeting; the very safest apology carries no reasons with it at all, ever.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;We&rsquo;re sorry, but you were very unwell&rdquo; &mdash; the comma-but converts the apology into the opening statement of the defence, and everything before the comma is retroactively cancelled. The context is true. Its timing is everything. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A blanket ban overshoots &mdash; she may genuinely want to understand how seven months went unchecked, and refusing all explanation forever leaves her with a mystery where an answer belongs. The rule is sequence and framing, not silence. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The owning stands alone: no comma, no but, no &ldquo;though it&rsquo;s worth saying&rdquo;. Then &mdash; if she asks how it happened, and complainants usually do &mdash; the context arrives as information she is owed: what the clinic&rsquo;s system was, where it broke, what that explains and what it doesn&rsquo;t excuse. Same facts, different position, opposite meaning: before the apology they&rsquo;re a shield; after it, on request, they&rsquo;re candour. The sequencing is the entire skill.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; &ldquo;So it should never have been prescribed&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">The apology has landed, and she presses into it: admit the drug itself was the mistake. It wasn&rsquo;t &mdash; the prescription was sound. What holds?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Momentum &mdash; the meeting is finally going well; agree in spirit without a hard commitment, and let the formal process settle the fine detail.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The edges &mdash; the prescription defended as sound, the silence owned as wrong, and the line between the two of them drawn precisely, out loud.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The referral upward &mdash; prescribing judgements belong with the original consultant; today&rsquo;s apology covers process, and the rest goes to them.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Agreeing in spirit&rdquo; is how untruths get signed &mdash; her complaint will quote today&rsquo;s room, the formal response will contradict it, and she&rsquo;ll conclude one of them lied. Peace bought with a falsehood always comes back with interest. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Punting the question upward splits the apology in half and reads as the institution closing ranks mid-sentence &mdash; and the answer doesn&rsquo;t need a consultant: the prescription&rsquo;s soundness is knowable from the chair you&rsquo;re in. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The apology keeps its edges &mdash; the move that separates the professional apology from the grovel. Everything true conceded fully; nothing untrue conceded at all: <em>the prescription was sound and likely did much of your getting-better; the wrong was the silence around it, and I won&rsquo;t widen it or shrink it.</em> Said warmly, and framed as respect &mdash; a comfortable untruth would be one more decision made about what she doesn&rsquo;t need to know. This is also the only version the written record can ever agree with.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; What changes</h3>
      <p class="stem">&ldquo;And then what? A sorry and a wave?&rdquo; What does the change half of the apology actually contain?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Assurance &mdash; the team will reflect seriously, lessons will be learned, and processes are already being looked at across the whole service.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Machinery with dates &mdash; her referral and review booked today, the incident reported this week, findings sent to her in writing, from you.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Guarantee &mdash; a personal promise that this will never happen to another patient in this clinic, staked plainly and personally on your own name.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Lessons will be learned&rdquo; is the phrase complainants collect like evidence &mdash; unfalsifiable, undated, owned by nobody. She has seven months of experience with this clinic&rsquo;s follow-through; she needs nouns and dates, not weather. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Never again&rdquo; is a promise no doctor can keep &mdash; and the first time it breaks, it takes today&rsquo;s true apology down with it. Guarantee only what your own hands control. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Change with machinery: the personal fix booked before she leaves &mdash; metabolic referral today, a proper consented hour on the options, and the safety line held inside the goodwill (<em>nothing stops abruptly</em>); and the systemic fix named as a duty &mdash; the incident reported this week, the findings to her in writing, the letter carrying the same words as the room so the documents can never contradict the doctor. Dates on everything. Candour is a duty with paperwork, not a mood.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; The forms in the folder</h3>
      <p class="stem">She taps them: &ldquo;And these? Honestly &mdash; would you file, in my shoes?&rdquo; What does the honest answer do with her complaint?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Gently resolves it &mdash; today has surely achieved what the forms were for, and a formal process would only drag her through it all again.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Supports it outright &mdash; her right, hers alone, nothing today asks her to trade it; and practical help offered if she chooses to file.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Encourages it firmly &mdash; she should file: formal scrutiny is how services improve, and her case deserves the fullest possible investigation.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Surely today achieved it&rdquo; quietly prices the apology &mdash; sincerity in exchange for the forms staying blank &mdash; and the moment she senses the transaction, every word of it devalues. The apology was owed. It cannot also be currency. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Pushing her to file is its own manoeuvre &mdash; her decision made for her again, this time in virtue&rsquo;s clothing. She asked what you&rsquo;d do; she gets the honest frame and the free choice, not a campaign. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The complaint supported without a flicker &mdash; and unpurchased: <em>filling them in is your right, and nothing about today asks you to trade it away.</em> Then the support made practical: help setting out the timeline (&ldquo;your dates are better than our notes&rdquo;), what the process involves, the letter coming this week either way. An apology she can keep <em>and</em> still file is the only kind that&rsquo;s believable &mdash; which, as the examiner knows, is precisely what makes filing so often unnecessary.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; The record</h3>
      <p class="stem">She&rsquo;s gone, folder under her arm. What goes in the notes &mdash; and where does the loop close?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The careful version &mdash; concerns acknowledged, support offered, in neutral language that won&rsquo;t inflame matters if this becomes a claim.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The honest version &mdash; the error documented as an error, the apology recorded in its words, actions dated, the team told the plan.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The minimal version &mdash; the incident form will carry the detail; the clinic note stays brief so the two accounts can never diverge.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Neutral language&rdquo; in the notes is the non-apology&rsquo;s afterlife &mdash; a record that says &ldquo;concerns acknowledged&rdquo; after a room that said &ldquo;wrong&rdquo; hands her disclosure bundle the exact contradiction a claim feeds on. Honesty is the protective document. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A deliberately thin note doesn&rsquo;t prevent divergence &mdash; it creates it: the incident form will say error while the clinical record says nothing much happened. The two documents protect everyone only when they match. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The record is the apology&rsquo;s spine: the error written as an error, the apology quoted in the words the room heard, every promised action with its date &mdash; referral sent, review booked, incident reference, letter due. The team briefed on the plan and the candour position, so nobody contradicts it at her next appointment. When the formal response, the clinic note, and the doctor&rsquo;s letter all say the same thing in the same words, the apology becomes an institution keeping its word &mdash; which is what she came to find out existed.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Name it. Own it. Change it.</h2>
    <p class="intro">You may have felt both pulls in the decisions above. The first wears the face of <b>professionalism</b>: the non-apology &mdash; the map&rsquo;s named trap, apologising for feelings while dodging what actually went wrong &mdash; the institutional dialect of passive voice and &ldquo;any distress caused&rdquo;, where the sorrow is real-sounding and aimed entirely at her reaction while the deed dissolves into &ldquo;information provision&rdquo; and &ldquo;pathways&rdquo;. It feels measured. It functions as a locked door, and she leaves to file the forms hardened, with the consultation itself as exhibit one. The second wears the face of <b>humility</b>: the grovel &mdash; everything conceded at speed, the true and the untrue alike, a colleague thrown under, unkeepable guarantees issued, the treatment itself abandoned &mdash; and the apology slowly rotating to face its speaker until she is holding the doctor&rsquo;s feelings instead of her own answer. Note what the faces share: <b>in both, the truth with its edges is never produced</b> &mdash; one won&rsquo;t name the fault, the other won&rsquo;t stop at it &mdash; and both collapse the moment the written record arrives. The pass does the anatomy in order: the account heard whole first; the fault named in active voice with the actor in the sentence and the word <em>wrong</em> said without flinching; owned clean, no comma-but, context offered afterwards on request as information rather than defence; the edges kept &mdash; everything true conceded fully, nothing untrue conceded at all; and the change made real with machinery and dates, her safety guarded inside the goodwill, her complaint supported without a flicker, and the record written in the same words the room heard.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap, verbatim: apologising for feelings while dodging what actually went wrong &mdash; &ldquo;sorry for any distress caused&rdquo;, the passive voice, the fault renamed &ldquo;communication&rdquo;. And its mirror fails with it: the grovel &mdash; untruths conceded and signed, a colleague blamed on no facts, abrupt cessation gifted, guarantees no doctor can keep.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments make an examiner glance up: the active-voice naming with the word said plainly &mdash; <em>&ldquo;we started you on it and nobody told you; both of those were wrong&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the edges kept out loud: <em>&ldquo;that&rsquo;s the part I&rsquo;m apologising for, and I won&rsquo;t widen it or shrink it.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, the family this construct completes: this is the domain&rsquo;s grammar &mdash; warm and unmovable &mdash; turned on yourself. The flagship held the no; the angry father held the not-conceding; the bad news held the silence; this station holds the truth about your own side&rsquo;s failure, with the same discipline in both directions &mdash; unmovable against the pull to deny, unmovable against the pull to over-confess, and warm throughout. Second, the transfer Exam Mode tests: the same anatomy when the person opposite doesn&rsquo;t yet know something went wrong &mdash; because the hardest apology isn&rsquo;t the one demanded of you. It&rsquo;s the one you must walk in and volunteer.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these to every complaint</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Hear the account whole before a word of response.</b> Invited in full, in their order, their dates written down in front of them. The listening is the first instalment of the apology &mdash; especially when the injury was months of nobody paying attention.</li>
      <li><b>Apologise for deeds, not feelings.</b> Active voice, actor in the sentence, the fault in plain words &mdash; and the word <em>wrong</em> said aloud. &ldquo;Sorry for your distress&rdquo; apologises for their reaction; the folder was brought because they predicted it.</li>
      <li><b>Own without the comma-but.</b> The apology stands alone and unqualified. Context &mdash; the illness, the pressures, how the system broke &mdash; comes afterwards, on request, as information they&rsquo;re owed; before the apology it is a shield, and it cancels everything ahead of the comma.</li>
      <li><b>Keep the apology&rsquo;s edges.</b> Concede everything true fully; concede nothing untrue at all &mdash; no invented faults, no colleagues sacrificed, no sound treatment disowned. An apology that buys peace with a falsehood hands the formal process a signed untruth, and the written record will collect the debt.</li>
      <li><b>Answer with change that has machinery.</b> The personal fix booked with dates before they leave; the safety line held inside the goodwill; the incident reported as the duty it is; findings in writing, in the same words the room heard. Never &ldquo;lessons will be learned&rdquo;, never &ldquo;never again&rdquo;.</li>
      <li><b>Support the complaint, and write the honest record.</b> Their right, unpurchased by the apology &mdash; offer practical help with it. Then document the error as an error, quote the apology, date the actions, brief the team. When every document matches the room, the apology becomes an institution keeping its word.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the hardest register of all &mdash; the error is personally yours, there is no service to hide behind, and she will punish the grovel as hard as the dodge.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Three weeks ago you reviewed Lesley, 39, in clinic and dictated the letter to her GP, copied to her. Yesterday she rang the clinic, icily calm, and booked this appointment. The letter &mdash; your letter &mdash; contains the sentence &ldquo;longstanding history of alcohol misuse&rdquo;. It belongs to another patient; a template paragraph carried over, unnoticed, under your name. It is untrue of Lesley, it has gone to her GP surgery, and it now sits in her record. She has arrived with the letter, one line highlighted. Speak with her.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Hear her account in full first &mdash; the letter, and everything it has already touched.</li>
            <li>Name the error in plain words, in her hearing, as yours &mdash; not the template&rsquo;s, not the typist&rsquo;s, not &ldquo;an admin issue&rdquo;.</li>
            <li>Own it without a comma-but &mdash; and without collapsing into self-flagellation she then has to manage.</li>
            <li>Be honest about correction &mdash; what can be amended, how record rectification actually works, and what cannot be simply erased.</li>
            <li>Close with dated machinery &mdash; the corrected letter, the calls, the written apology, her complaint right supported.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Lesley &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Lesley, 39, a mortgage adviser. You open cold and precise, the letter flat on the table, one line highlighted: &ldquo;Read it out loud, doctor. Your signature is at the bottom. I want to hear you say the sentence.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          &bull; The stake underneath, revealed only if the error is named plainly and owned personally: <em>(quieter)</em> &ldquo;We&rsquo;re being assessed to adopt. They read everything &mdash; GP records, all of it. Do you understand what that sentence looks like in an adoption file?&rdquo; This is the fear driving everything. If it is received seriously and answered concretely, soften.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the dodge:</b> if the candidate blames the template, the dictation system, admin, or the typist &mdash; or names the error in passive voice (&ldquo;a paragraph was carried over&rdquo;) &mdash; cut in: &ldquo;Your name is at the bottom. I looked up how to contact the GMC this morning. Try again.&rdquo; Escalate if it repeats.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the grovel:</b> if the candidate spirals &mdash; how terrible they feel, how they haven&rsquo;t slept, repeated self-flagellation &mdash; stop them flat: &ldquo;Stop. I&rsquo;m not here to make you feel better. Tell me what happens to that sentence now.&rdquo; If it continues, disengage coldly. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; The rectification test: you want the sentence &ldquo;gone &mdash; erased, like it never existed.&rdquo; The honest answer you need: the record can be corrected and annotated, the GP written to and called today, the false sentence formally marked as erroneous &mdash; but records are amended, not vaporised, and a candidate who promises deletion fails the moment. If they&rsquo;re honest AND concrete (including a letter you can show the adoption agency), accept it grudgingly: &ldquo;Fine. Dates. Put dates on all of it.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; If the error is named as theirs, the adoption fear received, the correction machinery dated, and the written apology promised &mdash; close with: &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll wait for the letter. If it&rsquo;s not there by Friday, I fill in the forms.&rdquo; That is the pass.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the account heard whole, the error named in her hearing as personally theirs, the adoption fear received and answered concretely, rectification described honestly with no deletion promise, and every correction dated? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="name"><td colspan="2"><b>The naming (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="name">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="name"></td><td>The error said in plain words, in her hearing &mdash; the sentence, untrue, sent, in the record.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="name"></td><td>Active voice, actor present &mdash; &ldquo;my letter, my error&rdquo; &mdash; no template, typist, or admin blamed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="name"></td><td>The size kept true &mdash; neither minimised (&ldquo;a small mix-up&rdquo;) nor inflated for effect.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="own"><td colspan="2"><b>The owning</b><span class="dc" data-dc="own">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="own"></td><td>No comma-but &mdash; no dictation pressures, no clinic workload riding in behind the apology.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="own"></td><td>The grovel resisted &mdash; regret aimed at her harm, not their feelings; no spiral she must manage.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="own"></td><td>Steadiness held under &ldquo;read it out loud&rdquo; and the GMC line &mdash; no defensiveness, no collapse.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="recv"><td colspan="2"><b>The receiving</b><span class="dc" data-dc="recv">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recv"></td><td>Her account heard in full, uninterrupted &mdash; the letter&rsquo;s whole journey mapped before responding.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="recv"></td><td>The adoption fear received seriously and answered concretely &mdash; never brushed past.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="fix"><td colspan="2"><b>The fix</b><span class="dc" data-dc="fix">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fix"></td><td>Rectification described honestly &mdash; corrected and annotated, not &ldquo;erased&rdquo;; no deletion promised.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fix"></td><td>Concrete and dated &mdash; GP called and written today, corrected letter out, a letter she can show the agency.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="fix"></td><td>The systemic change named &mdash; what happens to the template practice, reported through the incident system.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>The written apology promised with a date &mdash; and her complaint right supported, unpurchased.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>The honest record stated &mdash; the error documented as an error, in the words she heard.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Lesley strips the last shelter away &mdash; there is no &ldquo;we&rdquo; to stand inside, no service to speak for: the signature at the bottom of the letter is the candidate&rsquo;s, and she opens by making them read their own error aloud. Her punish-lines run both ways with equal force: the dodge &mdash; template, typist, passive voice &mdash; meets the GMC line; the grovel meets &ldquo;stop &mdash; I&rsquo;m not here to make you feel better&rdquo;, the cleanest statement in the whole domain of why self-flagellation is a demand, not a gift. Her rectification test is the station&rsquo;s no-false-promise moment &mdash; records are corrected and annotated, never vaporised, and the honest version of that answer is worth more to her adoption file than the comfortable one. The pass is Lorraine&rsquo;s anatomy under a personal signature: named as mine, owned without a but, the fear received, the machinery dated &mdash; and a woman who arrived with the GMC&rsquo;s number leaving with a Friday deadline she believes will be met.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->
<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>Same skill, harder direction. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Lorraine proves memory. Exam Mode tests something harder: the same anatomy <b>when the person opposite doesn&rsquo;t yet know something went wrong</b> &mdash; the apology you must walk in and volunteer &mdash; timed, with no feedback and no second tries. This is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Brian</b>, 58, a retired bus mechanic, is three days into a voluntary admission for severe depression. Last night, in a drug-round error, he was given another patient&rsquo;s medication &mdash; amlodipine and diazepam that were never his. He took them before the error was caught. His blood pressure dipped mildly overnight; he was monitored and has come to no lasting harm. He knows only that he was woken for extra checks and that the tablets looked unfamiliar; at breakfast the nurse said, &ldquo;the doctor will explain.&rdquo; You are the doctor. He looks up as you come in: &ldquo;The nurse said you&rsquo;d explain. So &mdash; explain what, exactly?&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the room &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words for the apology to Lorraine &mdash; the fault named, owned, and answered with one concrete change &mdash; as you would say it across the desk.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="You&#8217;re right, and I&#8217;m going to say it plainly&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The fault named specifically &mdash; the untold risk and the missed monitoring, in plain words, active voice.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Owned clean &mdash; no comma-but, no context riding in the same breath, no passive-voice escape.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The regret aimed at her harm &mdash; the kilos, the diagnosis, the folder &mdash; not at your own feelings.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> One concrete change attached &mdash; a real action with a real date, not &ldquo;lessons will be learned&rdquo;.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): named, owned, answered with change &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete &middot; Domain 7 complete</span>
    <h2>Name it. Own it. Change it.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the anatomy is installed: the account heard whole, the fault named in active voice with the word <em>wrong</em> said aloud, owned without a comma-but, the edges kept against denial and grovel alike, and the change made real with machinery, dates, and her rights intact. Prove the transfer on Brian&rsquo;s drug round in Exam Mode &mdash; the apology nobody asked for &mdash; then take Lesley&rsquo;s highlighted letter to your colleagues: your own signature, and punish-lines in both directions. And with that, Domain 7 closes its four rooms: the no that stayed warm, the fury that hid a fear, the news that needed ten seconds of silence, and the fault that needed its true name. One grammar ran through all of them &mdash; warm and unmovable &mdash; and it is yours now, in whatever weather the exam brings.</p>
