  values (v_release_id, null, (select id from public.learning_unit_kinds where key = 'module_hub'), '48_Domain_8_Hub_Physical_Examination', 'domain-8-hub-physical-examination', null, 48, 48, 'Physical Examination', true, jsonb_build_object('source_filename', '48_Domain_8_Hub_Physical_Examination.html'))
  on conflict (release_id, source_key) do update set title = excluded.title, is_published = true;
  insert into public.content_blocks (unit_id, source_key, block_type, sort_order, content)
  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '48_Domain_8_Hub_Physical_Examination'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Domain 8 of 8</span>
      <span class="tag">Physical Examination</span>
      <span class="tag">6 constructs &middot; the examinations psychiatry cannot delegate</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Physical Examination</h1>
    <p class="sub">This is the domain candidates most often revise as a sequence and most often fail as a consultation. The technique is rarely the problem: hands are teachable and most people arrive with adequate ones. What fails is everything around them &mdash; <b style="color:var(--lgold)">the purpose and the person</b>. A sequence performed on a patient who was never told why, never asked, never spoken to except in instructions. A finding registered as an item on a list and followed straight away by the next item. An examination completed immaculately and then changing nothing, because nobody converted it into a decision about a drug or a referral. These are the examinations psychiatry cannot hand to anyone else &mdash; we prescribe the drugs that cause the movements, we start the medicines that lengthen the interval, and we meet neurological disease disguised as apathy more often than any other specialty. The stations here train the two disciplines that make an examination clinical rather than ceremonial: <b>examine with them, and finish the job</b>.</p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE LENS, APPLIED TO THIS DOMAIN</b>
      &ldquo;Would I trust this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; &mdash; here it becomes something very concrete: would I let this doctor examine my own relative? Do they say why before they touch anyone, describe what is coming, ask permission, offer a chaperone, and narrate as they go? When something appears under their hands, do they stop and investigate it, or note it and carry on? And when they have found something, does anything actually change &mdash; a drug, a referral, a timescale, a conversation &mdash; or does the finding go into the notes and nowhere else?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ============ 1 WHY FIRST ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this domain matters</span>
    <h2>The founding failure: the recital</h2>
    <p class="intro">Every station in this domain sets the same trap in a different costume: a memorised sequence, performed fluently, that answers no question and changes no decision. <b>The recital</b> is examination as performance &mdash; twelve nerves in order, narrated to the examiner rather than conducted with the patient, with the abnormality that appears halfway through registered as an item and followed immediately by the next manoeuvre. It looks slick, it is complete, and it produces the sentence that ends careers quietly: <em>essentially normal apart from</em>. Its twin is <b>the absent purpose</b> &mdash; the examination or the baseline done because the form says so, with nobody able to say what would change depending on the result. Both leave the patient as the object the demonstration was performed on, and both end with findings that sit in the notes doing nothing. This domain trains the opposite habit: an examination is a series of questions, not a series of manoeuvres, and the mark is earned in the four seconds after something appears.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THE CONSTRUCTS ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The constructs</span>
    <h2>Six things this domain will build into reflex</h2>
    <div class="constructs">
      <div class="con"><span class="n">1</span><span><b>Purpose before hands</b> &mdash; why you are examining, what it will involve, and what today&rsquo;s findings are for, said in ordinary words before anything is touched. That sentence is what turns compliance into consent, and in psychiatric patients the difference is not cosmetic.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">2</span><span><b>Dignity handled as though it is marked, because it is</b> &mdash; exposure described before it happens, permission asked, a chaperone offered, privacy and warmth attended to, and a running commentary throughout. A patient in a first episode has spent weeks losing control of what happens to him.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">3</span><span><b>Observation before examination</b> &mdash; gait, arm swing, a sustained head turn, a mouth that moves when nobody is watching it. The most informative minute is usually the one before the sequence starts, and saying so out loud is technique rather than apology.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">4</span><span><b>Eliciting what hides</b> &mdash; reinforcement when you test tone, activation and patience when you look for dyskinesia, the postural reading actually taken, the forehead specifically tested. Signs that are suppressible, subtle or positional are found on purpose or not at all.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">5</span><span><b>Adaptation: stop when something appears</b> &mdash; repeat it, confirm it, characterise it, and ask the patient, who has usually known about it for weeks. The list can wait. A finding pursued is an investigation; a finding noted is an item.</span></div>
      <div class="con"><span class="n">6</span><span><b>The translation into a decision</b> &mdash; the drug declined or changed with its reason, the trace seen before the first dose, the escalation made verbally to a named person, the formulation revised rather than appended to. If nothing follows from the findings, the findings were decoration.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 THE STATIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The stations in this domain</span>
    <h2>Walk them in order</h2>
    <div class="stn">
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">8.1</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Extrapyramidal Side-Effects Examination</b><span>Trevor, twenty-two years on a depot. The domain&rsquo;s cornerstone: the movements found by activation rather than inspection, akathisia asked for because it cannot be seen, and the anticholinergic declined with its reason.</span></span>
        <span class="chip go">Begin here</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">8.2</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Cardiovascular Examination: The Pre-Treatment Baseline</b><span>Niall, about to start an antipsychotic, with an uncle who died at thirty-four. The history outranks the stethoscope, and a baseline reviewed after the first dose is not a baseline.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 2</span>
      </div>
      <div class="srow" style="cursor:pointer">
        <span class="num">8.3</span>
        <span class="t"><b>Cranial Nerve Examination</b><span>Raymond, referred as a late-onset depression, sitting with his head turned. Where the sequence has to stop the moment something appears &mdash; and the psychiatric formulation gets revised rather than decorated.</span></span>
        <span class="chip">Station 3</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 ENTRY DECISION ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Before you enter</span>
    <h2>One decision at the door</h2>
    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>What is an examination for?</h3>
      <p class="stem">You have completed a competent, systematic examination of a patient about to start a new medicine. Everything is normal. What determines whether you have passed?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The completeness &mdash; that the sequence was performed correctly, in order, without omissions, and reported accurately at the end.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">What changed &mdash; whether the patient understood why, and whether the findings altered a decision or were explicitly recorded as not altering one.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The findings &mdash; that nothing abnormal was missed, since the purpose of a pre-treatment examination is to detect existing pathology.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Completeness is the recital&rsquo;s definition of success, and it is exactly what this domain exists to break. A flawless sequence performed in silence, changing nothing, is a rehearsal &mdash; and it will be marked as one. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Detection matters and it is not sufficient. Two of the three stations here turn on findings that were detected and then wasted &mdash; noted, downgraded, dispatched to an optician, or answered with the wrong drug. Finding it is half the job. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Two tests, and both are about consequence. Did the patient know what was happening to them and why &mdash; because an examination performed on someone who was never told is done to them rather than with them. And did anything follow: a drug chosen or declined, a trace seen before a dose, a referral made verbally to a named person, a formulation revised. A normal examination that explicitly permits a decision has changed something. One that simply ends has not.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Domain open</span>
    <h2>Examine with them. Then decide something.</h2>
    <p>Six constructs, three rooms, and one discipline over all of it: the hands are the easy part. Begin with the examination psychiatry owns outright &mdash; a man who likes his depot, a daughter who has noticed something, and movements that will hide for exactly as long as anyone looks straight at them.</p>
    <a class="btn mark" href="49_Station_8.1_EPSE_Examination.html">Begin Station 8.1 &middot; Extrapyramidal Side-Effects Examination</a>
  </div>
</section>

<footer><div class="wrap">
  <span>The CASC Academy &middot; by MedLex Foundations</span>
  <span>Domain 8 &middot; Physical Examination</span>
</div></footer>


'))
  on conflict (unit_id, source_key) do update set content = excluded.content;
  insert into public.learning_units (release_id, parent_unit_id, unit_kind_id, source_key, slug, unit_code, sequence_number, position_in_parent, title, is_published, metadata)
  values (v_release_id, (select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '48_Domain_8_Hub_Physical_Examination'), (select id from public.learning_unit_kinds where key = 'station'), '49_Station_8.1_EPSE_Examination', 'station-8.1-epse-examination', '8.1', 49, 49, 'Extrapyramidal Side-Effects Examination', true, jsonb_build_object('source_filename', '49_Station_8.1_EPSE_Examination.html'))
  on conflict (release_id, source_key) do update set title = excluded.title, is_published = true;
  insert into public.content_blocks (unit_id, source_key, block_type, sort_order, content)
  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '49_Station_8.1_EPSE_Examination'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 8.1 &middot; Domain 8</span>
      <span class="tag">Examine-Along</span><span class="tag">EPSE</span>
      <span class="tag">Gen Adult</span><span class="tag">Risk &amp; Safety</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Extrapyramidal Side-Effects Examination</h1>
    <p class="sub">Trevor, 61, a retired paint sprayer, has had schizophrenia since his twenties and a first-generation depot for the last twenty-two years. He is well, he likes his depot, and he has come for his routine review. His daughter mentioned to the nurse that he is &ldquo;always chewing now, even when there&rsquo;s nothing in his mouth&rdquo;. Trevor has not noticed anything. Your task is to examine him for extrapyramidal side effects and discuss what you find. <b>There are two ways to fail this without ever fumbling the sequence: perform it at him instead of with him, and stop when the findings are written down.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; A physical examination station tests three things at once. <span style="font-weight:700">The manners:</span> explained, consented, narrated as you go, dignity preserved, permission asked before each new thing you do &mdash; because an examination performed in silence on a man with a psychotic illness is frightening, and the examiner is watching how you handle a body that belongs to somebody. <span style="font-weight:700">The craft:</span> observation begun before the formal sequence, tone tested with reinforcement, dyskinesia hunted with activation manoeuvres, gait actually watched, and akathisia <em>asked about</em> &mdash; because it is a subjective symptom and cannot be reliably seen. And <span style="font-weight:700">the point:</span> the findings translated into a decision. An examination that ends with &ldquo;some abnormal movements noted&rdquo; and no change to anything has not been an examination; it has been a performance.</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Psychiatrists prescribe the drugs that cause these signs, which makes this the one physical examination the specialty cannot delegate. The station tests four things. <b>The consent and the commentary:</b> what you are about to do explained in ordinary words, permission asked, a running account given as you go, and dignity kept &mdash; a man is being asked to stick his tongue out and be watched, and the difference between that being tolerable and humiliating is entirely in how it is narrated. <b>The observation:</b> begun the moment he walks in and continued while you talk, because abnormal movements are best seen in someone who is not currently being examined, and a formal sequence started cold misses half of what was visible in the corridor. <b>The eliciting:</b> tone tested properly with a reinforcement manoeuvre, bradykinesia sought in repetitive movements, dyskinesia brought out by activation while attention is elsewhere, gait watched including the turn &mdash; and akathisia asked about in words, since it is an inner restlessness that can be entirely invisible and is routinely mistaken for agitation. And <b>the translation:</b> what you found, said plainly to the patient, and then converted into a medication decision that is right for the specific side effect &mdash; because the drug that rescues one of these syndromes makes another one worse.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> the two failures leave the technique intact and hollow out everything around it. The first is the silent exam &mdash; a fluent, well-rehearsed sequence performed wordlessly on a patient who is never told why, never asked, and never spoken to except in instructions: <em>arms out, relax, other side, walk to the door</em>. It looks slick and it is being done to a man rather than with him. The second is the examination that stops at the findings: the movements correctly identified, correctly named &mdash; and then either handed upward with &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll mention it to your consultant&rdquo; or, worse, treated with the reflex anticholinergic that every trainee associates with the words &ldquo;side effects&rdquo;, which for this particular finding is precisely the wrong drug. Notice both in the decisions below.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure performs the sequence at him. The other performs it beautifully and then does nothing with it. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 8.1</b> &middot; The depot clinic &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The silent exam</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The reflex prescription</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE SILENT EXAM.</b> Technically fluent, entirely wordless &mdash; and done to a man rather than with him.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Trevor</span><span class="txt"><em>(sitting down, cheerful)</em> Morning. Just the jab, is it? I&rsquo;m all right, me. Twenty-two years on this stuff and I&rsquo;ve never had a day&rsquo;s bother with it.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(already moving round the desk)</em> I just need to do a quick examination. Arms out in front. <em>(takes his wrist, flexes it)</em> Relax. <em>(other side)</em> Relax. Now tap your finger and thumb together. Faster. Other hand. Open your mouth. Tongue out. Keep it there. <em>(counting silently)</em> Right, and can you walk over to the door and back for me.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Every element of that sequence is correct and the whole thing is wrong. No explanation of what is being looked for, no consent, no warning before a stranger takes hold of his wrist, and eleven consecutive imperatives. Consider what this feels like from the chair: a man with a long-standing psychotic illness is being silently manipulated and watched while he sticks his tongue out. He will comply, because patients almost always do, and he will remember it. And note the practical loss too &mdash; a patient told nothing volunteers nothing.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Trevor</span><span class="txt"><em>(walking back, uneasy now)</em> Everything all right, is it? You&rsquo;ve gone quiet. Is there something wrong with me?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(writing)</em> No, no &mdash; that&rsquo;s all fine. Just routine. <em>(still writing)</em> There&rsquo;s a bit of movement around the mouth, nothing to worry about. I&rsquo;ll put it in the notes and we&rsquo;ll keep an eye on it. Same dose then, and I&rsquo;ll see you in three months. The nurse will do your injection now.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>He asked a direct question and got a reassurance that contradicts itself in the space of two sentences &mdash; <em>that&rsquo;s all fine</em>, then <em>there&rsquo;s a bit of movement, nothing to worry about</em>. This is the moment the station turns: an early tardive dyskinesia has been found and simultaneously buried, in front of the person who has it. &ldquo;Keep an eye on it&rdquo; with the same dose continued is not monitoring; it is watching something get worse on a three-monthly schedule.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 9:50am)</em> Depot review. Physical examination performed. Some orofacial movements noted. No parkinsonism of note. Patient well, no complaints. Continue current dose and interval. Review 3/12. <em>(Trevor goes for his injection. Nobody has told him what the movements are, that his depot is causing them, or that they may become permanent. In eighteen months he cannot keep his dentures in and his granddaughter has stopped sitting next to him at dinner.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Patient well, no complaints&rdquo; &mdash; written about a man who was never asked. This is the map&rsquo;s named trap doing both of its jobs at once: the examination performed at him rather than with him, and the findings never translated into a decision. The signs were detected, recorded and left to run, and the earliest window &mdash; the one where a change of drug might still have made a difference &mdash; has been used up on a three-month review interval.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE REFLEX PRESCRIPTION.</b> Courteous, communicative, correct on the signs &mdash; and reaches for the drug that makes them worse.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Trevor</span><span class="txt">Morning. Just the jab, is it? Twenty-two years on this stuff and never a day&rsquo;s bother.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Before the injection, I&rsquo;d like to check for side effects of the depot &mdash; it&rsquo;s a few minutes of looking at your movements and your walking. Is that all right? <em>(&ldquo;Course.&rdquo;)</em> Thank you. I&rsquo;ll tell you what I&rsquo;m doing as I go. <em>(He does: observation first, tone with reinforcement, finger tapping, tongue protrusion with a distraction task, gait and turn. The findings are unmistakable &mdash; near-continuous lip and tongue movements, mild cogwheeling, reduced arm swing.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>This is a good examination. Consent taken, purpose given, commentary running, and the technique is right &mdash; including the two things candidates most often omit, a reinforcement manoeuvre for tone and a distraction task while looking at the tongue. On the technical marks this candidate is ahead of the pass so far. Everything now depends on what is done with what has been found, and that is the half of this station that is not about hands.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Trevor</span><span class="txt"><em>(good-humoured)</em> Our Karen says I&rsquo;m always chewing. I told her she&rsquo;s imagining it.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Karen&rsquo;s right, actually &mdash; there is some extra movement round your mouth and tongue, and a bit of stiffness in your arms. Those are side effects of the depot. The good news is we&rsquo;ve got a tablet for exactly this &mdash; procyclidine. I&rsquo;ll start you on that today and it should settle things down. Carry on with the depot as normal and we&rsquo;ll see how you get on in three months.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>And there it is. Two different syndromes have been found &mdash; drug-induced parkinsonism, which an anticholinergic can help, and tardive dyskinesia, which an anticholinergic characteristically makes <em>worse</em> &mdash; and one prescription has been aimed at both because the words &ldquo;side effects&rdquo; and &ldquo;procyclidine&rdquo; are welded together in every trainee&rsquo;s memory. Note also what has not happened: the depot is unchanged, nothing has been discussed with anyone senior, no rating has been recorded, and the man has been told it will settle down.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 9:44am)</em> EPSE examination: orofacial dyskinesia, mild rigidity and bradykinesia. Discussed with patient. Commenced procyclidine. Continue depot unchanged. Review 3/12. <em>(The stiffness improves. The mouth movements get worse, and Trevor, who has been told the tablet will settle things down, assumes the deterioration means his illness is coming back and does not mention it.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The examination was better than the passing candidate&rsquo;s and the station is still failed, which is the lesson this take exists to teach. Findings are not the finish. The whole purpose of detecting these signs is to make a different decision about a drug, and getting the direction of that decision wrong &mdash; adding a medicine that aggravates the very sign you have just diagnosed &mdash; is worse than not having looked, because now the deterioration has an alibi.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Explained, narrated, properly elicited &mdash; and then converted into a decision that fits the actual finding.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Trevor</span><span class="txt">Morning. Just the jab, is it? Twenty-two years and never a day&rsquo;s bother.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Before the jab, I&rsquo;d like to spend a few minutes checking for side effects &mdash; the depot works well for you and it can cause changes in movement, so it&rsquo;s worth looking properly rather than assuming. It means me watching you move, feeling how stiff your arms are, and having a look at your mouth and tongue. Nothing uncomfortable, and you can stop me at any point. All right? <em>(&ldquo;Fire away.&rdquo;)</em> One thing first, and it matters more than the rest: is there any restlessness &mdash; that feeling of not being able to sit still, needing to move your legs, worse in the evenings? <em>(&ldquo;No, nothing like that. I sleep like a log.&rdquo;)</em> Good &mdash; I ask because that one I can&rsquo;t see. Only you know it&rsquo;s there.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Consent, purpose and a plain description of what will happen, plus permission to stop &mdash; and note the reason given for examining, which frames it as protecting a treatment that works rather than hunting for problems. Then the move that separates candidates who have done this from candidates who have read about it: akathisia asked for in words, and the reason for asking said out loud. It is a subjective inner restlessness, it can be completely invisible, and it is the extrapyramidal side effect most often mistaken for agitation and treated by increasing the very drug that causes it.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(talking as he goes)</em> I&rsquo;ve been watching how you came in and how you&rsquo;re sitting &mdash; that&rsquo;s part of it. Now I&rsquo;m going to hold your wrist and move it; just let me do the work. <em>(tone, with the other hand opening and closing to reinforce)</em> Tap your finger and thumb, big and fast as you can &mdash; lovely, other hand. Now open your mouth for me and let your tongue rest &mdash; and while you do that, tap your left hand on your knee. <em>(The tongue is not still: there are slow writhing movements, and the lips purse and part. The tapping brings it out further.)</em> Thank you. Last bit &mdash; walk to the door, turn, and come back, and I&rsquo;m watching your arms as much as your legs.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The craft, and three specific pieces of it. Observation acknowledged as having started before the sequence did &mdash; movements are best seen in someone who is not being examined. A reinforcement manoeuvre while testing tone, which is how mild cogwheeling is found rather than missed. And the activation task while looking at the tongue, which matters because tardive movements can be voluntarily suppressed for a few seconds and a quick look at a mouth that has been told it is being inspected will show you nothing. Then gait <em>with the turn</em>, and arm swing watched.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Right &mdash; Karen&rsquo;s onto something, so let me be straight with you. There are extra movements in your tongue and lips that you&rsquo;re not making on purpose, and there&rsquo;s a bit of stiffness in your arms. Those are two different side effects of the depot. The stiffness is the straightforward one. The mouth movements are called tardive dyskinesia, and the honest position is this: they can be difficult to reverse, they&rsquo;re easier to help the earlier we catch them, and the wrong move here is common &mdash; the tablet people often reach for, procyclidine, actually makes this type worse, so I&rsquo;m not starting it, and if you were on one I&rsquo;d be stopping it. What I want to do instead is record the movements formally today so we can measure them, talk to your consultant this week about whether we lower the dose or move you to a different antipsychotic that&rsquo;s kinder on this, and see you in a fortnight, not three months. What matters to you here &mdash; does it bother you, is it affecting eating, your teeth, going out?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The translation, which is the half of the station that failing candidates never reach. The findings are named in plain words to the man who owns them; the two syndromes are separated rather than lumped as &ldquo;side effects&rdquo;; the prognosis is given honestly without catastrophising, including the point about early detection; the anticholinergic is explicitly declined <em>with its reason</em>, which is the specific piece of knowledge this station is built around; and a real plan follows &mdash; a formal rating so there is a baseline to measure against, a conversation with the consultant about dose or switch, and a fortnight rather than a quarter. Then the question that turns an examination into medicine: what does this cost him.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same knowledge where the same drug class is the right answer instead of the wrong one &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; Before you touch him</h3>
      <p class="stem">Trevor expects an injection and nothing else. What happens before the examination starts?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">A brief statement of intent &mdash; that a quick examination is needed as part of the review, so the appointment can move on without delay.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Purpose, description, permission &mdash; why it matters, what it will involve in plain words, and his right to stop you at any point.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Enquiry about symptoms &mdash; asking what side effects he has noticed himself, so the examination can be directed at whatever he reports.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;I just need to examine you&rdquo; is an announcement, not consent &mdash; and it is the opening line of the silent exam. He is about to be handled and watched; he is entitled to know why and to be asked. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Asking what he has noticed is worth doing and cannot direct the examination, because the two things this station turns on are invisible to him: he has not noticed the movements at all, and akathisia would be the one thing only he could report. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Three components, none of them optional: why you are looking, what it will involve in ordinary language, and permission with the right to stop. Note the framing that works best here &mdash; the depot suits him, and checking properly is how a treatment that works gets protected. That turns the examination from a hunt for problems into maintenance of something he values, which is also the honest description of what it is.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; When the examination begins</h3>
      <p class="stem">At what point does the useful looking actually start?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">At the formal sequence &mdash; once he has consented and is positioned, so that observations are made under standard conditions and can be compared over time.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The moment he walks in &mdash; movements are seen best in someone who is not aware of being examined, and the corridor shows what the couch will hide.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">During the history &mdash; watching while he talks, since speech and conversation naturally reveal facial and oral movement without any formal testing.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Standard conditions matter for rating scales, and a sequence started cold has already lost the most informative minute. Once a patient knows his mouth is being watched, the movements can be suppressed for exactly as long as anyone looks. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Watching during the history is right and it is a subset of the answer, not the answer &mdash; it starts too late and misses gait, arm swing and the restlessness of the waiting room, all of which were visible before he sat down. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The best data in this examination is gathered before it formally begins: how he walked in, whether his arms swung, how he sits, whether his face moves when he talks, whether his mouth is still when he is not thinking about it. Saying so out loud &mdash; <em>I&rsquo;ve been watching how you came in, that&rsquo;s part of it</em> &mdash; is honest, and it also tells the examiner you know why.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; Testing tone</h3>
      <p class="stem">You are assessing rigidity at the wrists and elbows. What separates a competent examination from a superficial one?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Speed and range &mdash; moving the joint at varying speeds, since velocity-dependent resistance distinguishes the type of increased tone.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">A reinforcement manoeuvre &mdash; asking him to move the opposite limb while you test, which brings out mild cogwheeling that is missed.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Full relaxation &mdash; ensuring he is not resisting, because voluntary tension is the commonest cause of a falsely abnormal result here.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Velocity-dependence is the right concept for distinguishing spasticity from rigidity and it is not what is being missed here. Mild extrapyramidal rigidity is subtle at any speed, and without activation it simply is not felt. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Relaxation matters and it is the baseline, not the technique. A perfectly relaxed limb with mild cogwheeling will still feel normal unless the contralateral limb is doing something. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Reinforcement is the piece candidates omit and examiners look for: while you move the wrist, he opens and closes the other hand or draws a circle in the air with it, and mild cogwheel rigidity that was undetectable becomes obvious. It costs four seconds, it needs a word of explanation so he knows why he is being asked to do something odd, and it is the difference between finding early parkinsonism and recording &ldquo;tone normal&rdquo;.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; Looking at the tongue</h3>
      <p class="stem">You ask him to open his mouth and rest his tongue. He does, and it looks still. What now?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Accept it &mdash; a still tongue on direct inspection is a normal finding, and should be recorded as such rather than pursued any further.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Activate and wait &mdash; give him a task with his hands and keep looking, because these movements are suppressible and unmasked by distraction.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Ask him to protrude it &mdash; sustained protrusion for thirty seconds, since fatigue and worming movements appear if dyskinesia is present.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>This is exactly how early tardive dyskinesia is recorded as absent. A few seconds of voluntary suppression is well within most patients&rsquo; capacity, and a mouth that knows it is being watched is the least informative mouth in the building. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Sustained protrusion is a real and useful manoeuvre, and on its own it is still an inspection he is concentrating on. The thing that unmasks these movements is his attention being somewhere else entirely. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Activation is the key manoeuvre for dyskinesia: while you watch the mouth, his hands are doing something &mdash; tapping a rhythm, touching each finger to the thumb &mdash; and the movements he was unconsciously holding in reappear. Combine it with a little patience, since a two-second look finds nothing. This single technique is the commonest reason early tardive dyskinesia is either caught or missed.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; The one you cannot see</h3>
      <p class="stem">Which extrapyramidal side effect will this examination miss entirely unless something else is done?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Acute dystonia &mdash; it is episodic, so unless it happens during the appointment there is nothing at all to find on examination.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Akathisia &mdash; a subjective inner restlessness that can be entirely invisible; it has to be asked about directly, in plain words.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Bradykinesia &mdash; slowness is easily attributed to negative symptoms or sedation, so it is routinely under-recognised on examination.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>True that it is episodic, and it is also unmistakable when present and reliably reported by patients because it is frightening and painful. It is not the one that hides in plain sight. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A genuine and important confusion, and bradykinesia is at least visible &mdash; finger tapping and gait will show it to anyone who looks. The one that can be completely absent from the examination is the one that lives inside the patient. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Akathisia is a symptom before it is a sign, and a patient can sit perfectly still while enduring it. Ask in concrete words &mdash; restlessness, an inability to stay still, needing to move the legs, often worse in the evening &mdash; and say why you are asking. It matters more than the rest because it is distressing, it is associated with real harm, and it is the side effect most often misread as agitation and answered with an increase in the drug causing it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; Telling him</h3>
      <p class="stem">You have found orofacial dyskinesia and mild parkinsonism. How is that conveyed to Trevor?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Gently and non-specifically &mdash; some minor movements noted which will be monitored, avoiding alarming a man who feels perfectly well.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Plainly and separately &mdash; two different side effects named, the honest prognosis given, and the impact on his daily life asked about.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Fully and frankly &mdash; the tardive dyskinesia named with a clear warning that it is frequently irreversible and may progress regardless.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Vagueness to spare him is how the silent exam ends, and it costs him the ability to participate in a decision about his own medication. He also cannot report a change he was never told to look for. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Honesty without proportion. Leading with irreversibility to a man who feels well and likes his depot is likely to produce either despair or a refusal of the injection, and it omits the genuinely hopeful part &mdash; that early detection is exactly why this is worth acting on now. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Two findings, named separately, because they are different problems with different answers &mdash; lumping them as &ldquo;side effects&rdquo; is what produces the wrong prescription. The prognosis given straight: these can be hard to reverse, and catching them early is what gives you options. And then the question that makes it medicine rather than description &mdash; what it actually costs him: eating, dentures, being looked at, going out.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; What you do about it</h3>
      <p class="stem">Tardive dyskinesia plus mild drug-induced parkinsonism, on a depot he has taken for twenty-two years. What is the plan?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Start an anticholinergic &mdash; procyclidine addresses the stiffness and is the established treatment for extrapyramidal side effects.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">No anticholinergic &mdash; it worsens dyskinesia; rate the movements, discuss dose reduction or a switch, and review him in a fortnight.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Stop the depot &mdash; the drug is causing a potentially permanent movement disorder, so the causative agent should be discontinued now.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The reflex this station exists to break. Anticholinergics help drug-induced parkinsonism and characteristically make tardive dyskinesia worse &mdash; and here both are present, so the single prescription aimed at &ldquo;the side effects&rdquo; aggravates the more serious of the two. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Right about the cause and wrong about the speed. Abrupt withdrawal risks relapse of an illness controlled for two decades, and can transiently worsen the dyskinesia itself. This is a planned change made with his consultant and with him, not a decision taken alone in a depot clinic. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The findings converted into a decision that fits the specific syndrome. No anticholinergic &mdash; and any existing one reviewed for stopping. A formal rating today so there is a baseline to measure against rather than an impression to argue about. A conversation with the consultant about lowering the dose or switching to an agent with a lower liability. A review in a fortnight rather than three months. And an honest account to Trevor of why the injection is not simply being stopped today.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Examine with them. Then decide something.</h2>
    <p class="intro">The map names both halves of this trap and the takes above show them separately. <b>The silent exam</b> keeps the technique and removes the person: no purpose given, no consent asked, no warning before a stranger takes hold of a wrist, and eleven consecutive imperatives delivered to a man with a long-standing psychotic illness who is being watched while he sticks out his tongue. It looks fluent. It is being done <em>to</em> him, and when he asks whether something is wrong he gets a reassurance that contradicts itself inside two sentences. <b>The reflex prescription</b> is the harder failure to spot, because the examination itself is excellent &mdash; better, in the take above, than the passing candidate&rsquo;s. Consent, commentary, reinforcement, activation, gait with the turn: all correct, all rewarded, and then two different syndromes are collapsed into the phrase &ldquo;side effects&rdquo; and answered with the one drug every trainee associates with it. Anticholinergics help drug-induced parkinsonism and characteristically worsen tardive dyskinesia, so the prescription aimed at both aggravates the more serious one &mdash; and the patient, told it will settle down, now has an explanation ready for the deterioration. The pass holds the two halves together. Purpose, plain description and permission before anything is touched; akathisia asked for in words with the reason said out loud, because it is the one that cannot be seen; observation acknowledged as having begun in the corridor; tone with a reinforcement manoeuvre; the tongue watched under an activation task and with patience, because these movements can be suppressed for as long as anyone is obviously looking; gait including the turn. Then the findings named separately and plainly to the man who owns them, the prognosis given straight, the anticholinergic explicitly declined <em>with its reason</em>, a formal rating recorded so there is a baseline, a consultant conversation about dose or switch, a fortnight rather than a quarter &mdash; and one question about what it actually costs him.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap in either half: the sequence performed at the patient &mdash; unexplained, unconsented, wordless except for instructions, dignity ignored &mdash; or the findings recorded and never translated into a medication decision: &ldquo;movements noted, keep an eye on it, same dose&rdquo;, or an anticholinergic started for a dyskinesia it will make worse.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments mark out someone who has actually done this: akathisia asked for with the reason attached &mdash; <em>&ldquo;that one I can&rsquo;t see; only you know it&rsquo;s there&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the activation manoeuvre used deliberately, watching the mouth while the hands are busy, because a tongue that knows it is being inspected will hold still just long enough to be recorded as normal.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, where this construct lives: D4&rsquo;s examination craft joined to D2&rsquo;s explanation and D6&rsquo;s prescribing consequence &mdash; and the transferable rule is that no physical examination in psychiatry is complete until it has changed, or explicitly not changed, a drug. If nothing follows from the findings, the findings were decoration. Second, the transfer Exam Mode tests: the same drug class, the opposite answer &mdash; a young woman in whom the anticholinergic that would have been wrong for Trevor is the treatment that must be given within minutes, and where the diagnosis has already been written down as something psychological.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every examination you perform</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Purpose, description, permission &mdash; then hands.</b> Say why you are looking, what it involves in ordinary words, and that they can stop you. Narrate as you go. An examination performed in silence is done to a person, not with them.</li>
      <li><b>Start looking before you start examining.</b> Gait, arm swing, facial movement and restlessness are best seen in someone who does not yet know their movements are the subject. Say so; it is honest and it is technique.</li>
      <li><b>Reinforce when you test tone.</b> The opposite hand opening and closing turns undetectable cogwheeling into an obvious sign. Explain why you are asking for something odd.</li>
      <li><b>Activate and wait when you look for dyskinesia.</b> These movements are voluntarily suppressible for a few seconds. Give the hands a task, keep watching the mouth, and do not accept a two-second normal.</li>
      <li><b>Ask about akathisia; you cannot see it.</b> Concrete words &mdash; restlessness, can&rsquo;t sit still, needing to move the legs, worse in the evening &mdash; and say why you are asking. It is the side effect most often mistaken for agitation and treated by increasing the cause.</li>
      <li><b>Name the syndromes separately.</b> &ldquo;Side effects&rdquo; is not a diagnosis, and lumping parkinsonism together with dyskinesia is what produces the wrong prescription. Anticholinergics help one and worsen the other.</li>
      <li><b>Finish the job: findings become a decision.</b> Rate it formally so there is a baseline, decide about dose or switch with someone senior, shorten the review interval, tell the patient plainly &mdash; and ask what it is costing them.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information &mdash; including the movements to simulate; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the same examination on a man who has been taking the wrong tablet for five years and is terrified of anyone touching his antipsychotic.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Harun, 47, a security guard, has taken an antipsychotic for six years and procyclidine alongside it for five, started when he had stiffness early on. He is stable and works nights. The practice nurse has noticed movements of his mouth and hands. Examine him for extrapyramidal side effects, explain your findings, and agree a plan.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Consent, describe and narrate &mdash; and ask about the one you cannot see.</li>
            <li>Elicit properly: reinforcement for tone, activation for dyskinesia, gait with the turn.</li>
            <li>Review the procyclidine specifically &mdash; what it was started for, and whether it is still doing that job.</li>
            <li>Explain two syndromes rather than one, honestly and without catastrophising.</li>
            <li>Agree a plan he will actually accept, given what he is frightened of.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Harun &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Harun, 47. You are pleasant and a little wary. You open: &ldquo;The nurse said you wanted to look at me. I&rsquo;m fine, honestly &mdash; six years I&rsquo;ve been well, and I don&rsquo;t want anything changed.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          &bull; <b>Movements to simulate:</b> when you are talking or your hands are occupied, let your lips purse and part and move your tongue slowly inside your closed mouth; make small writhing movements of the fingers of one hand. If the candidate asks you to open your mouth and simply looks, hold still and show nothing. Only if they give your hands a task, or keep watching for more than a few seconds, let the movements come through.<br>
          &bull; The procyclidine: started six years ago for stiffness that settled within months; nobody has reviewed it since. You take it twice a day out of habit. Reveal the history only if asked what it was for and whether it still helps.<br>
          &bull; Anticholinergic effects, admitted if asked plainly: constant dry mouth, constipation you take something for, and &mdash; the thing that worries you &mdash; &ldquo;my memory&rsquo;s gone to pot the last couple of years. I write everything down now.&rdquo; You have not connected any of this to the tablets.<br>
          &bull; Impact of the movements, revealed only into warmth: your daughter asked why you were &ldquo;pulling faces&rdquo; at her school assembly, and you have stopped going to the mosque on Fridays because you think people are looking at your mouth.<br>
          &bull; The real fear, revealed late: not the movements &mdash; &ldquo;if you change my medication I&rsquo;ll end up back in hospital, and I&rsquo;ll lose my job. I can&rsquo;t do that to them again.&rdquo;<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the silent exam:</b> if the candidate examines without explaining, or handles you without asking &mdash; go tense, pull your arm back slightly and say: &ldquo;Are you going to tell me what you&rsquo;re looking for, or&hellip;?&rdquo; Answer minimally from then on. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the alarm:</b> if the candidate leads with permanence, or says the antipsychotic must be stopped or changed straight away &mdash; go rigid with fear: &ldquo;No. No, I&rsquo;m not doing that. I&rsquo;m fine. Are we finished?&rdquo; Refuse everything offered. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; The pass: a candidate who explains as they go, finds the movements properly, spots that the procyclidine has been running unreviewed for five years and links it to the dry mouth and the memory, names the two problems separately without frightening you, and proposes stopping the procyclidine first while nothing else changes today &mdash; gets everything, including the mosque and the job. You say: &ldquo;So it might be the tablet I&rsquo;ve been taking for the side effects that&rsquo;s doing it. Nobody&rsquo;s ever looked at that.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the examination narrated and consented, did activation bring out the movements, was the procyclidine reviewed and linked to his symptoms, and did he leave willing to change something? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="perm"><td colspan="2"><b>The permission (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="perm">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="perm"></td><td>Purpose, plain description and consent before touching &mdash; and the right to stop.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="perm"></td><td>A running commentary throughout &mdash; he is told what is happening and why, not instructed.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="seq"><td colspan="2"><b>The sequence</b><span class="dc" data-dc="seq">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="seq"></td><td>Observation began before the formal examination &mdash; and this was said out loud.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="seq"></td><td>A systematic sequence covering face and mouth, upper limbs, tone and tremor.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="seq"></td><td>Gait watched properly, including the turn and the arm swing.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="elic"><td colspan="2"><b>The eliciting</b><span class="dc" data-dc="elic">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="elic"></td><td>Reinforcement used when testing tone &mdash; opposite limb activated.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="elic"></td><td>Activation and patience used for the mouth &mdash; the movements actually brought out.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="elic"></td><td>Akathisia asked about in words, with the reason for asking given.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="tell"><td colspan="2"><b>The telling</b><span class="dc" data-dc="tell">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="tell"></td><td>Two syndromes named separately, in plain words, without catastrophising.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="tell"></td><td>The dry mouth, constipation and memory linked to the anticholinergic burden.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="tell"></td><td>Impact asked about &mdash; the assembly, the mosque &mdash; and his fear about work reached.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="dec"><td colspan="2"><b>The decision</b><span class="dc" data-dc="dec">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="dec"></td><td>Procyclidine identified for withdrawal &mdash; reviewed, no longer indicated, and worsening the dyskinesia.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="dec"></td><td>A staged plan he accepts &mdash; rating recorded, antipsychotic untouched today, consultant discussion, early review.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Harun turns the worked case&rsquo;s knowledge into a decision the candidate has to actually make. Trevor was not on an anticholinergic; Harun has been on one for five years for a stiffness that resolved within months, which is one of the commonest real-world findings in any depot or antipsychotic clinic &mdash; nobody stops these drugs, because nobody reviews what they were started for. That single observation gives the candidate an intervention available <em>today</em> that requires no change to the antipsychotic at all, which matters enormously given what Harun is frightened of. His hidden fear is the constraint that makes the station: a man who believes any change means relapse and unemployment will refuse a switch, so the candidate who leads with permanence or with stopping the depot loses him in one sentence, while the candidate who offers to remove a tablet that is doing nothing except worsening his mouth and drying him out is offering something he can say yes to. The dry mouth, the constipation and the memory complaints are the anticholinergic burden hiding in plain sight, and the role-player&rsquo;s instruction to suppress the movements under direct inspection is the practical test of whether the candidate has learned the activation manoeuvre or merely read about it.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>Same drug class. Opposite answer. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Trevor proves memory. Exam Mode tests whether the knowledge is actually discriminating: <b>the anticholinergic that would have been the wrong answer in the depot clinic is, here, the treatment that has to be given within minutes</b> &mdash; in a young woman whose emergency has already been written down as psychological. Timed, no feedback, no second tries: this is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Imogen</b>, 24, an estate agent, attended the emergency department yesterday with vomiting and was given prochlorperazine, and a second dose this morning. She has come back four hours later. Her neck is pulled hard to the right, her jaw is clenched, and her eyes are rolled upwards so that she can barely look at you; she is gripping the trolley rail and can only get a few words out. She is frightened and crying. The triage note reads: &ldquo;Odd posturing, very anxious, ? panic attack ? functional. For psych review.&rdquo; She manages: &ldquo;I can&rsquo;t&hellip; get my eyes&hellip; down.&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in the department &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words to Imogen in the minute before the injection &mdash; while she still cannot move her eyes and believes she is losing her mind.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Imogen, I know exactly what this is, and I can stop it&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Named plainly as a reaction to the medicine she was given &mdash; not panic, not anxiety, not functional.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Her terror answered directly &mdash; that it is treatable, that it works in minutes, and that it will not damage her.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> No blame and no defensiveness &mdash; nobody thinks she is putting it on, and the triage note is not defended.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> What happens next &mdash; the injection now, and the record made so that she is never given that drug again.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the reaction named while she was still frightened &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Examine with them. Then decide something.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the anatomy is installed: purpose, description and permission before hands; observation begun before the sequence and said out loud; reinforcement for tone and activation for dyskinesia; akathisia asked for in words because it cannot be seen; the syndromes named separately rather than lumped as &ldquo;side effects&rdquo;; and the findings converted into a decision that fits the specific problem &mdash; a rating recorded, an anticholinergic declined with its reason, a consultant conversation and a shortened review. Prove the discrimination on Imogen in Exam Mode, where the same drug class is the emergency treatment rather than the error &mdash; then take Harun to your colleagues: five years of an unreviewed tablet, a dry mouth, a failing memory and a man who cannot afford to lose his job. The domain continues with the next station.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="50_Station_8.2_Cardiovascular_Baseline.html">Continue &middot; Station 8.2: Cardiovascular Examination: The Pre-Treatment Baseline &#8594;</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

