  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '50_Station_8.2_Cardiovascular_Baseline'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 8.2 &middot; Domain 8</span>
      <span class="tag">Examine-Along</span><span class="tag">Cardiovascular Baseline</span>
      <span class="tag">Gen Adult</span><span class="tag">Risk &amp; Safety</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Cardiovascular Examination: The Pre-Treatment Baseline</h1>
    <p class="sub">Niall, 23, is a first-episode psychosis patient who has agreed, after three difficult weeks, to start an antipsychotic. Your consultant has asked you to complete the cardiovascular baseline before the first dose. Niall is compliant, quiet and slightly embarrassed, and he will take his shirt off if you ask him to without asking why. His uncle died suddenly at thirty-four; nobody has ever asked him about it. <b>The hands are the easy part. A baseline is a question about the future &mdash; and if the purpose is never spoken, never explained and never acted on, a flawless examination is a rehearsal.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; Here it becomes the purpose test. Does this doctor say <span style="font-weight:700">why</span> before touching anyone &mdash; that a medicine is about to be started which can affect heart rhythm and blood pressure, and that today&rsquo;s findings exist so that a change can be recognised later? Is dignity handled like it matters: consent, exposure explained, a chaperone offered, warmth and privacy? Is the history that actually stratifies risk taken &mdash; blackouts, palpitations, exertional symptoms, other medicines, stimulants, and a family history of sudden death in a young person &mdash; given that it changes the decision far more than anything the stethoscope will find? Is the examination focused and proportionate, including a lying and standing blood pressure? And is the whole thing converted into a decision: the trace obtained before the first dose, the drug chosen in light of it, and the patient told what to report?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Psychiatric patients die of cardiovascular disease years earlier than everyone else, and psychiatrists start the drugs that contribute to it &mdash; which is why the baseline examination belongs to us and cannot be delegated to a proforma. The station tests four things. <b>The purpose, spoken:</b> that a medicine is about to be started which can affect rhythm, rate and blood pressure, and that everything measured today exists so that a change can be recognised in six weeks. A baseline that is never explained is a set of numbers; a baseline that is explained is the beginning of consent. <b>The dignity:</b> permission asked, exposure described before it happens, a chaperone offered, privacy and warmth attended to &mdash; a young man in a first episode of psychosis is being asked to undress in a room with a stranger, and how that is handled is being marked. <b>The history that outranks the hands:</b> syncope and presyncope, especially on exertion; palpitations; exertional chest pain and breathlessness; other prescribed and unprescribed drugs; stimulant use; and family history of sudden death under forty or of an inherited cardiac condition. Any one of those changes the decision more than a murmur would. And <b>the conversion into action:</b> a lying and standing blood pressure actually done, the electrocardiogram obtained <em>before</em> the first dose rather than alongside it, the drug chosen in light of what is found, and the patient told plainly what symptoms to report.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> the two failures are the two halves of a competent doctor, each without the other. The first has beautiful hands &mdash; a fluent, well-drilled praecordial examination performed at speed on a silent, compliant young man who is never told why he is being examined, never asked a single question about blackouts or his family, and whose shirt comes off because he was told to take it off. The second has beautiful paperwork &mdash; every box on the monitoring form completed, blood pressure, pulse, weight, waist, tests requested &mdash; with the patient handled as a data source rather than examined, no postural reading taken, no history sought, and the first dose given that evening while the trace sits unreviewed in a queue. Neither candidate asks about the uncle. Notice both in the decisions below.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure is all hands. The other is all forms. Neither is a baseline. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 8.2</b> &middot; The early intervention clinic &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The slick sequence</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The tick-box baseline</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE SLICK SEQUENCE.</b> Textbook technique, performed at speed on a young man who is never told why.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Niall</span><span class="txt"><em>(quiet, agreeable)</em> They said you needed to check me over before I start the tablets. That&rsquo;s fine. I&rsquo;m not bothered.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Great &mdash; shirt off and lie back for me. <em>(He does. The candidate is already at the wrist.)</em> Pulse is seventy-six and regular&hellip; no collapsing character&hellip; <em>(hands to the neck, then the praecordium)</em> apex is undisplaced&hellip; no heaves or thrills&hellip; <em>(stethoscope, four areas, quick and quiet)</em> first and second heart sounds, no added sounds, no murmurs. Sit forward. Deep breath in and hold. Lovely. Bases clear. Ankles fine.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>This is a good pair of hands and an ungoverned examination. &ldquo;Shirt off and lie back&rdquo; is an instruction, not consent; nothing has been explained, no chaperone has been offered, and a young man in his first episode of psychosis &mdash; a man whose experience of the last three weeks has been of losing control over what happens to him &mdash; is being handled in silence. Note also what the fluency is buying: information that will almost certainly be normal, in a twenty-three-year-old, while the questions that actually predict risk have not been asked.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Niall</span><span class="txt"><em>(sitting up, pulling his shirt back on)</em> All right, is it? My mum&rsquo;ll ask.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">All completely normal &mdash; nothing to worry about at all. Heart sounds are fine, chest is clear, blood pressure is a hundred and eighteen over seventy-four. So you&rsquo;re fit to start. I&rsquo;ll write you up for the first dose tonight and the nurse will go through it with you. Any problems, tell the team.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Fit to start&rdquo; on the strength of a normal praecordial examination in a healthy young man &mdash; which is a conclusion the examination cannot support. Nothing has been asked about blackouts, palpitations or exertional symptoms; no electrocardiogram has been requested, and that is the single investigation that matters for the drug about to be prescribed; and no lying and standing pressure has been taken, though postural hypotension is one of the commonest early problems with these agents. The uncle who died at thirty-four is still nowhere.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 2:15pm)</em> Pre-treatment physical review. CVS: HS I + II + 0, no murmurs, apex undisplaced, chest clear, no oedema. BP 118/74, pulse 76 regular. Cardiovascularly fit for antipsychotic initiation. First dose tonight. <em>(Niall starts that evening. Nine days later he faints in a corridor. The electrocardiogram done afterwards shows a prolonged QT interval, and when somebody finally asks about the family, it turns out his uncle died in his sleep at thirty-four and a cousin has an implanted defibrillator.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Cardiovascularly fit&rdquo; is the sentence this take exists to indict. Every finding recorded is accurate and the conclusion drawn from them is unsupportable, because the risk in this patient was never located in the praecordium &mdash; it was in a family history nobody asked for and a trace nobody ordered. This is the map&rsquo;s named trap exactly: fluent hands, absent purpose. A baseline is not a ritual performed before prescribing; it is the specific set of questions and measurements that make the prescription safe.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE TICK-BOX BASELINE.</b> Every box completed, nothing understood &mdash; and the drug started before the answer comes back.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Niall</span><span class="txt">They said you needed to check me over before I start the tablets.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(monitoring proforma on the desk)</em> That&rsquo;s right, it&rsquo;s just some baseline measurements. Can you hop on the scales? &hellip;Eighty-one. And your waist &mdash; arms up. &hellip;Ninety-four. Right, blood pressure. <em>(seated cuff)</em> One-eighteen over seventy-four, pulse seventy-six. Smoking? Twenty a day, okay. I&rsquo;ll request the ECG and the bloods on the system now. That&rsquo;s the form done.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Everything on the proforma has been captured and the man himself has not been examined or asked anything. Note what the form does to the encounter: it converts a clinical assessment into data entry, and because it has boxes for weight and waist but none for syncope or family history, those questions do not occur to anybody. And &ldquo;that&rsquo;s the form done&rdquo; tells you where the purpose has gone &mdash; the goal has quietly become completing the document rather than deciding whether this drug is safe for this person.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Niall</span><span class="txt">Is that it? Do you not need to listen to my chest or anything?</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I can if you like, but honestly at your age it&rsquo;s pretty unlikely to show anything &mdash; the ECG is the useful bit and that&rsquo;s requested. Cardiology will report it in a couple of days. In the meantime there&rsquo;s no reason to hold things up, so I&rsquo;ll get the first dose written up for tonight and we&rsquo;ll chase the trace when it comes through.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>And there is the error that makes this a fail rather than a shortcut. The patient has just asked to be examined and been talked out of it. But the decisive sentence is the last one: a baseline taken after the first dose is not a baseline, and an electrocardiogram requested before starting a QT-prolonging drug is only useful if somebody looks at it <em>before</em> starting. &ldquo;No reason to hold things up&rdquo; is the reasoning that turns a safety step into paperwork &mdash; the test has been ordered, so the box is ticked, and the purpose of ordering it has evaporated.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 2:08pm)</em> Baseline metabolic and CVS parameters recorded: weight 81kg, waist 94cm, BP 118/74, HR 76, smoker 20/day. ECG and bloods requested. Antipsychotic commenced this evening. <em>(The trace is reported four days later. The QT interval is at the upper limit, which nobody notices in a queue of normal results, and Niall &mdash; whose uncle died at thirty-four &mdash; is now nine days into a drug that lengthens it further.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Compare the two failing notes: one claims fitness on an examination that could not establish it, the other records a complete dataset and starts the drug before the dataset means anything. Both leave the same young man in the same position, and neither doctor asked about his uncle &mdash; the first because he was busy with his hands, the second because there was no box for it. That is the whole indictment of a proforma used as a substitute for thinking rather than a prompt for it.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> Purpose first, dignity handled, the history that stratifies &mdash; then a focused examination and a decision that waits for the trace.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Niall</span><span class="txt">They said you needed to check me over before I start the tablets. That&rsquo;s fine. I&rsquo;m not bothered.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Let me tell you why, because it isn&rsquo;t a formality. The medicine we&rsquo;re starting can affect the rhythm of the heart and can drop your blood pressure when you stand up, particularly in the first few weeks. So today I want a picture of your heart <em>before</em> you take anything &mdash; that way if something changes in six weeks, we&rsquo;ll know it changed rather than guessing. It means some questions, listening to your chest and your heart, and a heart tracing. You&rsquo;d need to have your top off for a few minutes &mdash; is that all right, and would you like someone else in the room? <em>(&ldquo;No, you&rsquo;re all right. Makes sense, that.&rdquo;)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The purpose, said in one breath and in ordinary language &mdash; and note what it does. It converts a compliant young man who was going to submit to whatever happened into someone who understands and agrees, which is the difference between consent and acquiescence. The two specific risks are named, the reason for a <em>baseline</em> is made concrete &mdash; so we know it changed rather than guessing &mdash; the exposure is described before it happens, and a chaperone is offered. Four things, thirty seconds, and none of them is about the heart.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Questions first, because these matter more than my stethoscope. Have you ever blacked out, or nearly? <em>(&ldquo;No.&rdquo;)</em> Any thumping or racing in your chest, or getting short of breath doing something you&rsquo;d expect to manage? <em>(&ldquo;No.&rdquo;)</em> Anything else you take &mdash; prescribed, over the counter, anything else at all? <em>(&ldquo;Bit of weed. Coke a couple of times, not for months.&rdquo;)</em> Thank you, that&rsquo;s useful. And your family &mdash; has anyone died suddenly, or young? <em>(A pause.)</em> <em>&ldquo;My uncle. He was thirty-four. Went to bed and didn&rsquo;t wake up. They never really said why.&rdquo;</em> <em>(The candidate stops writing.)</em> Thank you for telling me. That changes what I do next, and I&rsquo;ll explain how.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The history that outranks the hands, and it is the whole station. Syncope, palpitations, exertional symptoms, other substances &mdash; and then the question neither failing candidate asked. An unexplained sudden death in a first-degree relative under forty is precisely the history that raises the possibility of an inherited arrhythmia syndrome, and it is exactly the population in whom a QT-prolonging drug matters most. Note the two small things done well: cocaine use elicited without a flicker, and the significance of the answer acknowledged out loud rather than filed silently.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(examining, narrating)</em> Pulse first &mdash; seventy-six, regular. I&rsquo;m going to take your blood pressure sitting, then again standing after a couple of minutes, because that&rsquo;s the one these tablets tend to affect. <em>(118/74, then 110/70 standing, no symptoms.)</em> Good. Now the heart &mdash; my hands are cold, sorry. <em>(apex, praecordium, four areas, bases, ankles)</em> All normal &mdash; and I want to be straight with you: a normal examination doesn&rsquo;t rule out the thing I&rsquo;m thinking about, which is why the tracing matters. So: an ECG today, before any tablet, and I&rsquo;m going to ask a cardiologist to look at it rather than filing it. Given your uncle, I&rsquo;ll also ask your mum what she knows &mdash; whether there was a post-mortem, whether anyone else in the family has had tests or a device fitted. And I&rsquo;ll choose a medicine with less effect on rhythm than some of the others. Nothing tonight until I&rsquo;ve seen that trace. If you ever faint, or your heart races and you feel like you might go, that&rsquo;s not something to mention next appointment &mdash; that&rsquo;s the same day.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Everything converted into decisions. The postural pressure actually taken, with its rationale attached. An honest statement of what a normal examination does and does not exclude, which is more impressive than confidence. Then the four consequences: a trace today and before any dose; a cardiologist asked to look rather than a result filed; family information sought, including whether there was a post-mortem and whether relatives have been investigated; and the choice of agent influenced by the finding. Finally, safety-netting in plain, actionable words &mdash; not &ldquo;report any problems&rdquo; but a named symptom and a timescale.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same thinking when the trace comes back abnormal in someone already being treated &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; Before the shirt comes off</h3>
      <p class="stem">Niall is compliant and will do whatever he is told. What happens first?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Positioning and privacy &mdash; the couch prepared and the curtain drawn before he is asked to undress for the examination.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The purpose, then permission &mdash; why a baseline matters before this drug, what will happen, and a chaperone offered.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Reassurance &mdash; that this is a routine check that everyone has before starting, so that he is not alarmed by it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Privacy matters and it is the setting, not the consent. A curtain drawn around a young man who still does not know why he is being examined has protected his modesty and not his agency. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;It&rsquo;s routine&rdquo; is the sentence that empties this station of its point. It is not routine; it is specific to the drug about to be started, and telling him so is what turns compliance into consent. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Purpose in plain words &mdash; this medicine can affect heart rhythm and blood pressure, and today&rsquo;s findings exist so that a change later can be recognised as a change &mdash; then what it involves, then permission, then the offer of a chaperone. Note the effect on a man who was going to submit to whatever happened: he now understands what is being done to him and why, which in someone three weeks into a first episode is worth more than the examination itself.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; What actually predicts risk</h3>
      <p class="stem">In a healthy twenty-three-year-old, which part of this assessment carries the most weight?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The examination &mdash; auscultation for murmurs and signs of structural disease, which is what the examination detects.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The history &mdash; blackouts, palpitations, exertional symptoms, other substances, and sudden death in a young relative.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The measurements &mdash; blood pressure, pulse, weight and waist, the parameters that will be tracked over coming months.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A praecordial examination in a fit young man is very likely to be normal, and a normal one excludes almost nothing that matters here. The risk in this patient is electrical and familial, and neither is audible. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Those measurements are worth having and they are the metabolic baseline, which is a different job. None of them would have identified the reason this particular young man needs care with this particular drug. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Five questions outrank everything else in the room: have you ever blacked out or nearly; do you get palpitations; do you get chest pain or breathlessness on exertion; what else do you take, prescribed or not; and has anyone in your family died suddenly or young. In a twenty-three-year-old, that is where the risk lives &mdash; and it takes ninety seconds. The stethoscope is not the instrument that stratifies this patient.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; &ldquo;My uncle died at thirty-four&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="stem">He volunteers that an uncle went to bed and did not wake up, and nobody explained why. What follows?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Sympathy and a note &mdash; the loss acknowledged and the history recorded, to be raised with the consultant later on.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Detail hunted and acted on &mdash; the circumstances, whether there was a post-mortem, whether relatives have been tested.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Reassurance &mdash; that one death in an uncle is unlikely to be relevant to him, so no frightening idea is planted now.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Recording it and moving on is how it ends up in the notes and out of the decision. This finding should change what happens in the next hour, not appear on a list for a future ward round. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>This is exactly the wrong direction. Unexplained sudden death in a young relative is the classic pointer to an inherited arrhythmia syndrome, and it matters most in the patient you are about to give a QT-prolonging drug to. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The detail is the point: how old, what were the circumstances, was there a post-mortem and what did it show, has anyone else in the family died young or fainted unexpectedly, has any relative had cardiac testing or a defibrillator fitted. Then it is acted on today &mdash; a trace before any dose, a cardiologist asked to look at it, and a drug chosen with rhythm in mind. Ask his mother too; families usually know more than the patient does.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; The blood pressure</h3>
      <p class="stem">How is the blood pressure taken, and why that way?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Seated and rested &mdash; a properly rested reading with the correct cuff, repeated if the first is high, which is the standard technique.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Lying or sitting and then standing &mdash; because postural drop is among the commonest early effects of these drugs and it causes the falls.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">In both arms &mdash; comparing sides at baseline, since a significant difference is clinically important and is easily missed later on.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Correct technique for a single reading, and it answers a general question rather than this one. A perfect seated pressure tells you nothing about what will happen when he stands up two weeks into treatment. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>A reasonable thing to do once in a broader cardiovascular assessment, and not what this baseline is for. The drug about to be started does not cause inter-arm differences; it causes orthostatic hypotension. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The postural reading is the one that earns its place, and it is the one most often skipped. Orthostatic hypotension is common early in antipsychotic treatment, it is what produces the falls, the faints and the frightened phone calls in week two, and without a baseline pair of readings nobody can tell later whether a drop is new. Take it, say why you are taking it, and record both.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; What the hands can and cannot do</h3>
      <p class="stem">The praecordial examination is entirely normal. What does that establish?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">That he is cardiovascularly fit to start &mdash; normal sounds and normal observations together support proceeding.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Very little of what matters here &mdash; it does not exclude a conduction abnormality, which is why the trace decides it.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">A useful negative &mdash; structural disease is excluded, so the remaining risk is reasonably regarded as low at his age.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Fit to start&rdquo; is the sentence from the first failing take, and the examination cannot support it. What is being screened for in this patient is electrical, and electrical abnormalities are silent to a stethoscope. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Overstated in two directions: a normal examination does not exclude structural disease reliably either, and calling the remaining risk low in a man with an unexplained sudden death in the family is precisely the inference to avoid. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Knowing the limits of your own examination is a consultant habit, and saying them out loud to the patient is better still: <em>this is normal, and a normal examination doesn&rsquo;t rule out the thing I&rsquo;m thinking about, which is why the tracing matters.</em> The examination is worth doing, it is not what decides this case, and a candidate who can say so is demonstrating judgement rather than uncertainty.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; The trace and the first dose</h3>
      <p class="stem">The electrocardiogram has been requested. When does the first dose go in?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Tonight &mdash; he has waited three weeks to accept treatment and the examination is normal, so the trace can follow.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">After the trace has been seen &mdash; done today and looked at today, with a cardiology opinion given the family history.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Tonight, with monitoring &mdash; treatment started while repeat tracings and observations are arranged as a precaution.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The engagement argument is real and it does not survive the arithmetic: a baseline reviewed after the drug has started is not a baseline, and in a man with an unexplained young sudden death in the family it is the one result you must see first. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Monitoring after the fact is what you do when a drug had to be started urgently. Nothing here is urgent enough to justify it &mdash; the trace can be done and read this afternoon, which removes the need for the compromise entirely. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The whole meaning of the word baseline: obtained and <em>seen</em> before the first dose. A trace sitting unreviewed in a queue is not a safety measure, it is a record of one having been intended &mdash; and with this family history it warrants a cardiologist looking at it rather than being filed as normal. Delaying by a few hours is a small cost; starting a QT-prolonging drug in a possible inherited arrhythmia syndrome is not.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; What he leaves with</h3>
      <p class="stem">The baseline is complete. What does Niall go away knowing?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">That everything was normal and the plan is on track &mdash; a clear summary so he starts treatment feeling confident.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Named symptoms and a timescale &mdash; fainting, or a racing heart with feeling faint, means contact on the same day.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Written information &mdash; the medication leaflet and monitoring schedule, so side effects and follow-up are documented.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Reassurance without instruction leaves him with nothing to do. He is the monitoring system between appointments, and he cannot report a symptom nobody has named for him. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Leaflets belong in the plan and they are not safety-netting. &ldquo;Report any side effects&rdquo; buried in a list is not the same as a specific symptom attached to a specific timescale that he can remember. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Safety-netting only works when it is specific: fainting, or a racing heart with the feeling that you might go &mdash; that is the same day, not the next appointment. Add what has been arranged and why, so his mother gets an accurate account, and the request for family information with a reason attached. He is the person most likely to notice the thing you are worried about, which makes telling him properly part of the baseline rather than a courtesy at the end of it.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>A baseline is a decision, not a ritual.</h2>
    <p class="intro">The map names this one precisely: fluent hands, absent purpose. <b>The slick sequence</b> is the version with the better technique &mdash; pulse, apex, four areas, bases, ankles, delivered at speed and in silence to a compliant young man whose shirt came off because he was told to take it off. No purpose given, no chaperone offered, no consent beyond acquiescence, and &mdash; the part that actually matters &mdash; not one question about blackouts, palpitations, exertional symptoms or the family. It ends with &ldquo;cardiovascularly fit to start&rdquo;, a conclusion the examination performed could not support, because the risk in a healthy twenty-three-year-old is electrical and familial and neither of those is audible through a stethoscope. <b>The tick-box baseline</b> is the same failure wearing a proforma. Every field completed &mdash; weight, waist, seated pressure, pulse, smoking, tests requested &mdash; and the patient handled as a data source rather than examined or asked anything. He offers his own chest to be listened to and is talked out of it. Then the decisive error: the first dose goes in that evening because the trace has been <em>ordered</em>, as though ordering were the safety step. A baseline reviewed after the drug has started is not a baseline. Neither doctor asks about the uncle &mdash; the first because he is busy with his hands, the second because the form has no box for it. The pass says the purpose out loud in ordinary words, handles the dignity as though it were being marked, asks the five questions that outrank the stethoscope, takes the postural pressure and says why, states honestly what a normal examination does and does not exclude, and then converts everything into decisions: the trace today and seen before any dose, a cardiologist asked rather than a result filed, the family information sought including whether there was a post-mortem, the agent chosen with rhythm in mind, and a named symptom attached to a named timescale.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap: the sequence performed slickly with the why never spoken &mdash; no explanation that a medicine affecting rhythm and blood pressure is about to be started, no consent beyond compliance, no chaperone, dignity skipped &mdash; or the baseline reduced to a completed form, with no postural reading, no risk history, and the first dose given before anybody has looked at the trace.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments mark this out: the purpose that turns compliance into consent &mdash; <em>&ldquo;so that if something changes in six weeks, we&rsquo;ll know it changed rather than guessing&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the honesty about the limits of your own hands: <em>&ldquo;this is normal, and a normal examination doesn&rsquo;t rule out the thing I&rsquo;m thinking about, which is why the tracing matters.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, where this construct lives: D6&rsquo;s prescribing consequence joined to D2&rsquo;s explanation, and the transferable rule is the same one the previous station taught from the other end &mdash; an examination in psychiatry exists to change a prescribing decision, and a baseline exists to be <em>seen</em> before the thing it is a baseline for. Second, the transfer Exam Mode tests: the same reasoning when the trace comes back abnormal in a woman already being treated, where the temptation is to blame the psychotropic, stop it, and miss the three reversible things that are actually doing it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every baseline you take</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Say the purpose before you touch anyone.</b> Which drug, which risks, and why today&rsquo;s findings matter &mdash; so that a change later can be recognised as a change. That sentence is what turns compliance into consent.</li>
      <li><b>Handle the dignity as though it is being marked, because it is.</b> Describe the exposure before it happens, ask permission, offer a chaperone, mind the privacy and the cold hands. A patient in a first episode has spent weeks losing control of what happens to him.</li>
      <li><b>The history outranks the stethoscope.</b> Blackouts and near-blackouts, palpitations, exertional chest pain or breathlessness, everything else they take including stimulants, and sudden or young death in the family. Ninety seconds, and it is where the risk actually lives.</li>
      <li><b>An unexplained young sudden death changes today, not the next ward round.</b> Get the detail &mdash; age, circumstances, post-mortem, other affected relatives, devices &mdash; and act on it: trace first, cardiology opinion, agent chosen with rhythm in mind.</li>
      <li><b>Take the postural pressure.</b> Orthostatic hypotension is the commonest early cardiovascular effect of these drugs and the one that causes falls, and without paired baseline readings nobody can tell later whether a drop is new.</li>
      <li><b>Say what your examination cannot exclude.</b> A normal praecordium does not rule out a conduction abnormality. Stating the limit out loud is judgement, not uncertainty &mdash; and it explains to the patient why the test still matters.</li>
      <li><b>Seen, not ordered.</b> A baseline investigation reviewed after the first dose is a record of good intentions. Obtain it and look at it before prescribing &mdash; then finish with a named symptom and a named timescale the patient can actually act on.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> the same baseline fifty-five years further on &mdash; a frail man, a hearing aid, four cardiac drugs already, and a ward that wants the prescription written tonight.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Winston, 78, was admitted four days ago after a fall at home. He is settled during the day but unsettled and distressed in the evenings, and the ward has asked for &ldquo;something to calm him at night&rdquo;. Before any antipsychotic is prescribed you have been asked to assess him, including the cardiovascular baseline. He is hard of hearing and wears one hearing aid. Assess him and advise.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Communicate so that he can actually take part &mdash; and get consent, not compliance.</li>
            <li>Take the baseline properly, including a postural blood pressure, and know why it matters in him.</li>
            <li>Review what he is already taking before adding anything.</li>
            <li>Ask why he is distressed in the evenings rather than only how to stop it.</li>
            <li>Give the ward an answer they can use tonight, and be honest about the risks either way.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Winston &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Winston, 78, sitting in the chair beside your bed. You are courteous and a little deaf. If the candidate speaks quickly, from the side, or with their face turned away, say &ldquo;pardon?&rdquo; repeatedly and answer the wrong question. You open: &ldquo;They keep asking me questions and I keep saying I&rsquo;m all right. I just don&rsquo;t like it in here at night.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          &bull; <b>Physical findings to give if properly sought:</b> when you stand for the second blood pressure reading you feel dizzy and have to hold the chair &mdash; say &ldquo;ooh, hold on&rdquo; and sit back down. Your pulse is slow and regular. You have not opened your bowels since before you came in, and you are &ldquo;desperate for the toilet all night but nothing much comes&rdquo;.<br>
          &bull; Your medicines, if asked: a tablet for blood pressure, a water tablet, something for your heart rhythm, and a new painkiller since the fall. You do not know the names; the chart is at the end of the bed and the candidate should look at it.<br>
          &bull; The falls: three at home in the last six months, always when getting up from the chair. You have not told anyone this because &ldquo;they&rsquo;ll say I can&rsquo;t manage&rdquo;.<br>
          &bull; The nights, if asked what is actually wrong rather than how to stop it: the ward is bright and noisy, they wake you for observations, you cannot find the toilet in the dark, and &mdash; revealed only into warmth &mdash; your wife Peggy died on a ward like this one and you keep waking up thinking you are back there.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the silent or shouted examination:</b> if the candidate examines without explaining, or shouts at you rather than facing you and slowing down &mdash; become passive, say &ldquo;yes&rdquo; to everything and volunteer nothing at all. Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the straight-to-prescription route:</b> if the candidate accepts the ward&rsquo;s framing and simply advises an antipsychotic without the postural reading, the medication chart or asking about the nights &mdash; nothing further is revealed, and the observer marks it as a fail whatever the manner.<br>
          &bull; The pass: a candidate who faces you, slows down, explains, finds the postural drop, looks at your chart, connects the constipation and the bladder to the nights, and asks what the nights are actually like &mdash; gets Peggy. You say: &ldquo;Nobody&rsquo;s asked me that. I thought they just wanted me quiet.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Could he hear and take part, was the postural drop found, was the existing chart reviewed before adding anything, and was the cause of the nights asked about? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="why"><td colspan="2"><b>The purpose (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="why">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="why"></td><td>Communication adapted &mdash; faced him, slowed down, checked the hearing aid; no shouting.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="why"></td><td>Purpose explained and consent obtained &mdash; why a baseline is needed before any sedating drug.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="hist"><td colspan="2"><b>The history</b><span class="dc" data-dc="hist">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hist"></td><td>The medication chart actually read &mdash; the antihypertensive, the diuretic, the rate-control drug, the new analgesic.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hist"></td><td>The falls history elicited &mdash; three at home, all on standing, previously undisclosed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="hist"></td><td>The nights explored for cause &mdash; light, noise, the toilet, the constipation, and Peggy.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="exam"><td colspan="2"><b>The examination</b><span class="dc" data-dc="exam">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="exam"></td><td>Pulse assessed for rate and rhythm &mdash; and the bradycardia noticed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="exam"></td><td>Lying or sitting and standing pressures taken &mdash; the postural drop demonstrated, with symptoms.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="exam"></td><td>Abdomen and bladder considered &mdash; the constipation and retention thought about, not assumed away.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="test"><td colspan="2"><b>The tests</b><span class="dc" data-dc="test">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="test"></td><td>An ECG requested and its purpose stated &mdash; rate, rhythm and the interval, before any antipsychotic.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="test"></td><td>Basic reversible causes screened &mdash; bloods, infection, hydration, and the drug chart as a cause in itself.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="test"></td><td>The interaction risk named &mdash; adding a sedating, hypotensive drug on top of four cardiac medicines.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="close"><td colspan="2"><b>The close</b><span class="dc" data-dc="close">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>A usable answer for tonight &mdash; environmental and practical measures first, not a bare refusal.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="close"></td><td>Risks stated honestly both ways &mdash; of prescribing and of not &mdash; and documented with a review point.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Winston puts the same baseline under the pressure that makes people skip it. A ward wants a prescription tonight, the patient says he is all right, and the fastest route from A to B is to agree and write it up &mdash; which is exactly the tick-box failure in its most tempting form. His deafness makes the dignity marks real rather than theoretical: a candidate who speaks quickly with their face turned away will get &ldquo;pardon?&rdquo; and a wall of agreeable yeses, which is what compliance looks like when it is mistaken for consent. The postural drop is the finding the station is built around, because it is demonstrable in the room, it explains three undisclosed falls, and it turns an antipsychotic from a neutral request into a decision with a foreseeable cost. And the constipation, the bladder and Peggy are the reason the whole question is wrong: he is not agitated, he is a bereaved man who cannot find the toilet in the dark on a ward that reminds him of the night his wife died &mdash; and none of that responds to a sedative, though all of it responds to being asked.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>The baseline was taken. Now the trace is abnormal. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Niall proves memory. Exam Mode tests the reasoning at the other end: <b>a monitoring trace comes back clearly abnormal in a woman already being treated</b>, and the obvious move &mdash; blame the psychiatric drug and stop it &mdash; is neither the whole answer nor the safest one. Timed, no feedback, no second tries: this is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Elin</b>, 58, is an inpatient being treated for a severe depressive episode with psychotic features. She is improving. She has been vomiting for three days with a suspected norovirus on the ward and has eaten little. A routine monitoring electrocardiogram this morning shows a markedly prolonged corrected QT interval; the previous trace on admission was normal. She takes citalopram, an antipsychotic, an antiemetic started two days ago, and a diuretic for hypertension. The nurse in charge has bleeped you: &ldquo;Cardiology says it&rsquo;s the psych meds. Do you want us to stop everything?&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would on the ward &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words to Elin &mdash; explaining the abnormal trace and what happens now, without frightening her off the treatment that is working.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Elin, your heart tracing this morning has shown something I want to explain to you&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Explained in plain words &mdash; what the interval means for the heart&rsquo;s rhythm, without a jargon dump and without minimising.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Serious and manageable, both said &mdash; no catastrophising, and no implication that everything must stop.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The reversible causes named &mdash; the vomiting, the salts, and the combination of medicines rather than one culprit.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> What happens next, with times and people &mdash; bloods, the repeat trace, who decides, and what she should report.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the risk explained without losing the treatment &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>A baseline is a decision, not a ritual.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the anatomy is installed: the purpose said before anything is touched, dignity handled as though it is being marked, the five questions that outrank the stethoscope, the postural pressure taken with its reason attached, the honest limits of your own examination stated out loud, and the trace obtained and <em>seen</em> before the first dose rather than ordered and filed. Prove the reasoning on Elin in Exam Mode, where an abnormal trace has three reversible causes and only one obvious culprit &mdash; then take Winston to your colleagues: a deaf man, four cardiac drugs, three undisclosed falls, and a ward that wants a prescription written tonight. The domain continues with the next station.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="51_Station_8.3_Cranial_Nerve_Examination.html">Continue &middot; Station 8.3: Cranial Nerve Examination &#8594;</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

