  values ((select id from public.learning_units where release_id = v_release_id and source_key = '51_Station_8.3_Cranial_Nerve_Examination'), 'static-html', 'legacy_static_html', 0, jsonb_build_object('html', '

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topbar">
      
      <span>The CASC Academy · by MedLex Foundations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tagrow">
      <span class="tag prime">Station 8.3 &middot; Domain 8</span>
      <span class="tag">Examine-Along</span><span class="tag">Cranial Nerves</span>
      <span class="tag">Liaison</span><span class="tag">Old Age</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Cranial Nerve Examination</h1>
    <p class="sub">Raymond, 62, has been referred as a late-onset depression: three months of apathy, withdrawal and &ldquo;not being himself&rdquo;, no response to an antidepressant his GP started eight weeks ago. He sits with his head turned slightly to the right and has done throughout the waiting room. Your task is to assess him, including a cranial nerve examination. <b>His left eye will not abduct, he has been sick twice this week before breakfast, and the referral letter contains the word that will explain all of it away if you let it.</b></p>
    <div class="lens"><b>THE QUESTION BEHIND EVERY MARK</b>
      &ldquo;Would I be confident to have this candidate as my registrar?&rdquo; This station is not testing whether you can recite twelve nerves. It is testing whether you examine <span style="font-weight:700">to a question</span> rather than through a list. Does the examination stop when something appears, and turn into a proper characterisation of that finding rather than continuing to the next item? Is the patient asked what <em>he</em> notices &mdash; because a man with a new palsy usually knows about it long before you do? Once there is a finding, is the examination widened and the red-flag history taken? Is the finding then spoken to the patient in the room, in plain words, rather than announced over his head in the vocabulary of an exam? And does the psychiatric formulation get revised, rather than the neurology being appended to it?</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="modebar"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="label">MODE</span>
  <div class="mtoggle">
    <button id="mLearn" class="active">Learn Mode</button>
    <button id="mExam">Exam Mode</button>
  </div>
  <span class="timer" id="timer">07:00</span>
  <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div><span class="pct" id="pct">0%</span>
</div></div>

<div id="learnPanel">

<!-- ============ 1 WHY THIS STATION EXISTS ============ -->
<section id="s1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">01</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Why this station exists</span>
    <h2>What the examiners built this to find out</h2>
    <p class="intro">Psychiatrists meet neurological disease disguised as psychiatric illness more often than any other specialty, and the disguise is usually apathy. The station tests four things. <b>The open question:</b> a referral saying &ldquo;depression&rdquo; is a hypothesis, not a finding, and a candidate who examines in order to confirm it will examine badly &mdash; three months of apathy and withdrawal in a man of sixty-two with no history is a presentation that earns a physical examination, not a second antidepressant. <b>The adaptive sequence:</b> a competent, ordered examination that <em>stops</em> when something appears. The mark is not for completing all twelve nerves; it is for what happens in the four seconds after an abnormality shows itself &mdash; whether the examination becomes an investigation of that finding or continues to the next item on a memorised list. <b>The patient as a source:</b> asking what he has noticed, because a man with a new sixth nerve palsy has usually been seeing double for weeks, has worked out that turning his head fixes it, and has never been asked. And <b>the translation:</b> the finding widened into a proper neurological and red-flag history, spoken plainly to the patient rather than over him, acted on the same day, and &mdash; the part candidates forget &mdash; used to revise the psychiatric formulation rather than sitting alongside it.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>What makes it hard:</b> the two failures both find the abnormality and both waste it. The first is the recital &mdash; a fluent, well-drilled progression through the nerves, narrated in the third person to the examiner, in which the failure of left abduction is registered as an item, noted, and followed immediately by the corneal reflex. The sequence is completed, the presentation is delivered, and the conclusion is late-onset depression with an incidental eye sign. The second is more startling and just as damaging: the abnormality is spotted and announced &mdash; <em>&ldquo;there&rsquo;s a left sixth nerve palsy&rdquo;</em> &mdash; out loud, in front of a frightened man who has just heard the word palsy applied to himself, and who is then left holding it while the candidate proceeds to the ninth and tenth nerves. Notice that the technique in both is perfectly adequate. Notice both in the decisions below.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 THREE TAKES ============ -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">02</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The station, three ways</span>
    <h2>Two ways to fail. One way to pass.</h2>
    <p class="intro">One failure files the finding. The other broadcasts it. Neither examines the man. Tap the gold circles for the examiner&rsquo;s thinking at each moment.</p>

    <div class="player">
      <div class="player-head">
        <span class="who"><b>Station 8.3</b> &middot; The liaison clinic &middot; 7 minutes</span>
        <div class="toggle">
          <button class="active" data-t="a">Fail 1 &middot; The recital</button>
          <button data-t="b">Fail 2 &middot; The announcement</button>
          <button data-t="c">The pass</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-a">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 1 &mdash; THE RECITAL.</b> Twelve nerves, in order, out loud, to the wrong person &mdash; and the finding becomes an item on a list.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Raymond</span><span class="txt"><em>(head turned slightly right, flat voice)</em> They said you&rsquo;d want to examine me. I don&rsquo;t know what for. The tablets haven&rsquo;t done anything, that&rsquo;s all it is. I&rsquo;m just tired of it all.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(to the examiner, not to Raymond)</em> I&rsquo;ll perform a cranial nerve examination. Cranial nerve one &mdash; I&rsquo;d normally ask about smell. Cranial nerve two &mdash; acuity, fields to confrontation, pupillary reflexes: all intact. Cranial nerves three, four and six &mdash; follow my finger, please. <em>(He does. The left eye fails to abduct.)</em> I note some limitation of left lateral gaze. Cranial nerve five &mdash; clench your teeth for me&hellip;</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The technique is fine and the address is wrong &mdash; the whole examination is being narrated to me rather than conducted with him, and he has become the object the demonstration is performed on. But the decisive moment is the one that just went past: <em>I note some limitation of left lateral gaze</em>, followed immediately by the fifth nerve. That finding is the reason this man is in the building, and it has been filed as a line item. The examination is now a list being completed rather than a question being answered.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Raymond</span><span class="txt"><em>(as the sequence continues)</em> Is my eye all right? Only I&rsquo;ve been&mdash; <em>(The candidate is checking the corneal reflex and does not look up.)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Just a moment. Cranial nerve seven &mdash; raise your eyebrows, screw your eyes shut, show me your teeth. Symmetrical. Cranial nerve eight &mdash; can you hear that? Nine and ten &mdash; open wide and say ah. Palate elevates centrally. Eleven &mdash; shrug against me. Twelve &mdash; tongue out, no deviation. <em>(standing back)</em> So: cranial nerve examination essentially normal apart from some limitation of left lateral gaze.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>He tried to tell you. <em>&ldquo;Is my eye all right? Only I&rsquo;ve been&mdash;&rdquo;</em> is the patient volunteering the history that would have finished this station, and he was told to wait so that the corneal reflex could be completed. And then the summary sentence, which is the whole failure in eleven words: <em>essentially normal apart from</em>. Nothing about a new failure of abduction in a sixty-two-year-old is essentially normal, and the phrase exists purely to let the list be reported as completed.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 11:20am)</em> Late-onset depressive episode; poor response to first-line antidepressant. Cranial nerves: mild limitation left lateral gaze, otherwise NAD. Impression: depression, treatment-resistant. Plan: switch antidepressant, review 4/52. Refer optician re: eye movement. <em>(Raymond goes home with a new tablet and an optician&rsquo;s appointment. Three weeks later he is admitted through the emergency department, vomiting, with a headache he has had every morning since June.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Otherwise NAD&rdquo; sitting one line above &ldquo;impression: depression&rdquo; is the map&rsquo;s named trap in its final form: a memorised sequence that never adapted when a finding appeared. Note the referral to an optician &mdash; the finding has been acknowledged, downgraded and dispatched, which is worse than missing it, because it creates a record of having considered it. Nobody asked about headache. Nobody asked about vomiting. The apathy that got him referred was the presenting symptom of the thing behind the eye sign.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-b" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>FAIL 2 &mdash; THE ANNOUNCEMENT.</b> The finding recognised, correctly named &mdash; and detonated over the head of the man it belongs to.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Raymond</span><span class="txt">They said you&rsquo;d want to examine me. The tablets haven&rsquo;t done anything. I&rsquo;m just tired of it all.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Follow my finger with your eyes, keeping your head still. <em>(Raymond follows; the left eye stops short going left.)</em> <em>(to the examiner)</em> There&rsquo;s a failure of abduction on the left &mdash; that&rsquo;s a sixth nerve palsy. Given the subacute history I&rsquo;d be concerned about a space-occupying lesion, so this needs urgent imaging. <em>(back to Raymond)</em> Sorry &mdash; look up for me now.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Clinically, that is entirely correct, and it is the best reasoning in any of the three takes. It has also just been delivered in the third person, eighteen inches from a man who heard <em>palsy</em>, <em>space-occupying lesion</em> and <em>urgent</em>, in that order, about his own head. He is not a teaching aid. The examiner is not the audience. And the instruction that follows &mdash; <em>look up for me now</em> &mdash; tells you the candidate has not registered that anything has changed in the room.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Raymond</span><span class="txt"><em>(very still)</em> A lesion. You mean a tumour. Is that what you&rsquo;re saying? <em>(His hands have started to shake.)</em></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">I don&rsquo;t want to speculate at this stage &mdash; we&rsquo;d need the scan first. Let me just finish the examination. Open wide and say ah&hellip; and shrug your shoulders against my hands&hellip; good. I&rsquo;ll get onto the medical team and they&rsquo;ll arrange the imaging. Try not to worry until we know more.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>He asked the only question he has, and he was told not to speculate by the person who had just speculated aloud in front of him. Then the examination resumed. &ldquo;Let me just finish&rdquo; is the recital reasserting itself under pressure &mdash; the list matters more than the man now sitting in front of you with shaking hands. And &ldquo;try not to worry until we know more&rdquo; is issued to someone who has been given a frightening word and no framework whatever for holding it.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(documenting, 11:14am)</em> Left VI nerve palsy on examination. Subacute personality change and apathy. Query space-occupying lesion. Discussed with medical registrar; urgent CT requested. Patient informed. <em>(The clinical management is correct and prompt. Raymond sits in the car park for an hour before he can drive, and when his wife asks what they said, he tells her the doctor thinks he has a tumour and then would not talk about it.)</em></span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>&ldquo;Patient informed&rdquo; is doing a great deal of work there. This take will pass the medicine and fail the station, and candidates find that hard to accept &mdash; the diagnosis was right, the escalation was right, the imaging was arranged. But the second half of the map&rsquo;s named trap is exactly this: talking to the examiner instead of the patient. A finding of this weight has to be delivered <em>to</em> the person it concerns, in words he can carry home, with the uncertainty held honestly rather than dropped on him and withdrawn.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="script" id="tk-c" style="display:none">
        <p class="takenote"><b>THE PASS.</b> The examination stops when the finding appears, turns into an investigation, and is then spoken to the man it belongs to.</p>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Raymond</span><span class="txt">They said you&rsquo;d want to examine me. I don&rsquo;t know what for. The tablets haven&rsquo;t done anything. I&rsquo;m just tired of it all.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">The reason is this: three months of feeling like that, at your age, with nothing that led up to it &mdash; I don&rsquo;t want to assume it&rsquo;s only low mood without checking you over properly. So I&rsquo;m going to test how your eyes, face and swallowing are working. Nothing uncomfortable, and tell me if anything I ask you to do is difficult or looks odd. <em>(He begins, talking to Raymond throughout &mdash; acuity, fields, pupils, then eye movements.)</em> Follow my finger, head still&hellip; and out to the left. <em>(The left eye stops short.)</em> <em>(He stops.)</em> Right &mdash; hold on. Do that again for me. When you look over that way, do you see one of my finger or two?</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Two things before a hand is raised: the referral&rsquo;s label is held open rather than confirmed, and the reason for examining is given to the patient in plain words. Then the moment the station is built around. The finding appears and the sequence <em>stops</em> &mdash; <em>hold on, do that again</em> &mdash; and the very next thing is a question to the patient rather than a note to himself. That is the difference between examining to a question and completing a list, and it takes six seconds.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line"><span class="spk">Raymond</span><span class="txt"><em>(the flatness lifting slightly &mdash; someone has noticed)</em> Two. Side by side. It&rsquo;s been like that since about&hellip; May? I keep my head round like this and it stops. I thought I needed new glasses and never got round to it. Nobody asked.</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt">Thank you &mdash; that&rsquo;s important, and you&rsquo;ve been managing it cleverly. Now some things I need to ask because of it. Headaches? <em>(&ldquo;Most mornings. I put it down to the tablets.&rdquo;)</em> Worse when you first wake, or when you cough or bend down? <em>(&ldquo;&hellip;Yes. Both.&rdquo;)</em> Have you been sick? <em>(&ldquo;Twice this week. Before breakfast, out of nowhere.&rdquo;)</em> Thank you. I&rsquo;m going to finish looking at the rest of the nerves, then look at the backs of your eyes, check your arms and legs and watch you walk &mdash; because when one thing turns up, I need to know whether there&rsquo;s anything else.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>The finding has converted the interview. Morning headache, worse on coughing and bending, with early-morning vomiting and a sixth nerve palsy &mdash; that is a picture of raised intracranial pressure, and the sixth nerve is the classic false localising sign within it. None of that history was in the referral, and none of it appeared in either failing take, because in both the finding was an endpoint. Here it became a question, and the question widened the examination properly: fundi, limbs, gait.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="line cand"><span class="spk">Candidate</span>
          <span class="txt"><em>(sitting down, at eye level)</em> Raymond, I want to tell you what I&rsquo;ve found and what happens now, and I&rsquo;m going to be straight with you. One of the nerves that moves your left eye isn&rsquo;t working properly &mdash; that&rsquo;s the double vision. With the morning headaches and the sickness, that combination can mean there&rsquo;s pressure building inside the head, and I need a scan today to find out. I don&rsquo;t know yet what&rsquo;s causing it and I&rsquo;m not going to guess in front of you &mdash; what I do know is that this is why you&rsquo;ve felt like you have. Three months of flatness that came from nowhere, that no tablet touched: I don&rsquo;t think that was ever a depression. Is your wife with you today? I&rsquo;d like her to hear this from me, not from you in the car park. I&rsquo;m ringing the medical team now, and I&rsquo;ll come back and tell you what they say.</span>
          <button class="flagbtn">!</button>
          <div class="read"><b>WHAT THE EXAMINER IS THINKING</b>Everything the second take got right, delivered to the right person. Sitting down and at eye level; the finding in ordinary words; the concern named honestly with the uncertainty held rather than filled in; and the refusal to speculate stated as a principle rather than used to shut him up. Then the sentence that reframes three months of his life &mdash; <em>I don&rsquo;t think that was ever a depression</em> &mdash; which is the formulation being revised rather than the neurology being bolted onto it. His wife brought in. And the promise to come back, which is what stops this being an abandonment dressed as an urgent referral.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 DECISIONS ============ -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">03</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Decision points</span>
    <h2>Now you&rsquo;re in the chair</h2>
    <p class="intro">Seven moments from this station. Choose, read the examiner&rsquo;s response, and try again until you find the move that works. <span style="color:var(--grey)">(Exam Mode will then test the same discipline where the examination is entirely normal and the danger is in the history &mdash; timed, with no help.)</span></p>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>1 &middot; The referral says depression</h3>
      <p class="stem">Three months of apathy in a man of sixty-two, no response to treatment. How is the referral&rsquo;s label treated?</p>
      <p class="hint">Choose an answer. If it&rsquo;s not the best one, you can try again.</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">As the working diagnosis &mdash; the task is to establish why it has not responded and what ought to be tried instead.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">As a hypothesis to be tested &mdash; new apathy at his age with no precipitant earns an examination, not a second tablet.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">As probably right but incomplete &mdash; a depression with an organic contribution needing investigation alongside it.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="0-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Accepting the label sets the whole encounter to confirm it, and a candidate examining to confirm will file an abnormality as incidental. Treatment resistance is a question about the diagnosis before it is a question about the drug. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="0-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Closer, and it still keeps depression as the frame with the neurology as a subplot. Here the apathy is the presenting symptom of the physical problem, not a comorbidity beside it &mdash; and that distinction changes what you do next. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>A referral letter is somebody&rsquo;s hypothesis. Late-onset apathy and personality change with no precipitant and no response to treatment is one of the most reliable ways neurological disease presents to psychiatry, and the correct response is to examine rather than to prescribe again. Saying that reason out loud to the patient also earns his cooperation: he has been told he is depressed for three months and has never been told why anyone would examine him.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>2 &middot; Before you start</h3>
      <p class="stem">He has kept his head turned slightly to the right throughout. What is that?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Posture worth noting &mdash; recorded in the general observation and revisited if the examination produces something.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">A finding already &mdash; a compensatory head position is how people manage double vision, and it can be asked about now.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">Probably psychomotor &mdash; withdrawn posture and reduced engagement are common in depression and fit the referral.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="1-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Noting it and moving on is the recital beginning early. A sustained head turn in a man about to be examined for eye movements is not background detail; it is the answer arriving before the question. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="1-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>This is the referral doing your thinking. Depression produces reduced eye contact and stooping, not a consistent rotation of the head to one side maintained for weeks &mdash; and reading it that way is precisely how the label swallows the finding. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>People with diplopia find the head position that fuses the images and hold it, often without knowing they are doing it. Observation before examination applies to cranial nerves as much as to movement disorders, and a single question &mdash; <em>do you ever see double?</em> &mdash; would have unlocked this station before the finger was raised. Raymond has been doing it since May and nobody has asked.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>3 &middot; The left eye stops short</h3>
      <p class="stem">On testing eye movements, the left eye fails to abduct. What happens in the next four seconds?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The finding is noted and the sequence continues &mdash; completing the examination first so the whole picture is available.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The sequence stops &mdash; the movement repeated, and the patient asked whether he sees one finger or two looking that way.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The other nerves supplying the eye are tested at once &mdash; the third and fourth checked to localise the lesion precisely.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="2-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>&ldquo;Complete first, interpret later&rdquo; sounds systematic and is the named trap. A list completed around an abnormality produces &ldquo;essentially normal apart from&rdquo;, which is how a new palsy in a man of sixty-two ends up referred to an optician. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="2-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Good instinct, wrong order by one step. Localising is worth doing and the fastest, richest source in the room is the man himself &mdash; he can tell you the direction, the duration and the compensation in one sentence, which no amount of finger-following will give you. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The examination becomes an investigation of the finding. Repeat the movement to confirm it is real and reproducible, then ask the patient &mdash; one image or two, in which direction, are they side by side, how long has it been happening, does anything make it better. Raymond answers all of it, including the head turn and <em>since about May</em>. Six seconds of adaptation, and the station is effectively solved.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>4 &middot; The questions the finding demands</h3>
      <p class="stem">There is a new sixth nerve palsy of some weeks&rsquo; standing. What history now becomes urgent?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Vascular risk &mdash; diabetes, hypertension, smoking and cholesterol, since microvascular palsy is the commonest cause in this age group.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Raised pressure &mdash; headache and its pattern on waking, on coughing and on bending, plus vomiting and any visual obscurations.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The cognitive picture &mdash; memory, executive function and behaviour, to characterise the three months of change more precisely.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="3-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Entirely relevant and not the priority. A microvascular palsy is common and is a diagnosis of exclusion in someone who also has three months of personality change &mdash; and it would not explain the apathy that brought him here. Ask it second. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="3-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Worth doing and not now. Characterising the cognitive change in detail is a task for after the scan; asking about morning headache and vomiting is a task for the next thirty seconds, because it determines whether this man leaves the building today. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The sixth nerve has the longest intracranial course and is the classic false localising sign of raised intracranial pressure &mdash; so the questions are headache worse on waking, worse on coughing, straining or bending, early-morning vomiting, and transient visual blurring. Raymond has been sick twice this week before breakfast and has had a headache most mornings that he attributed to his antidepressant. That history was available in both failing takes and was never sought.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>5 &middot; What else gets examined</h3>
      <p class="stem">The remaining cranial nerves are normal. Is the examination finished?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Yes &mdash; the cranial nerve examination is what was asked for, and it has been completed thoroughly and in order.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">No &mdash; fundi, limbs and gait, because once one thing has appeared you need to know whether anything else has too.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">No &mdash; cognitive testing, since the presenting problem is three months of personality change that needs quantifying.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="4-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>The task set the boundary and the finding moved it. Stopping at the twelfth nerve because that is what the instruction said is the list governing the doctor, which is the thing this station exists to break. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="4-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>It belongs in the assessment and not in the next two minutes. Cognitive testing quantifies the deficit; fundoscopy and a quick look at the limbs and gait tell you whether there is raised pressure and whether anything else is going on. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>An abnormality obliges you to widen, not to stop. Look at the fundi, or say plainly that you would and arrange for someone who can; check tone, power and reflexes in the limbs; and watch him walk, which takes twenty seconds and is one of the highest-yield tests available. The purpose is no longer completing an examination &mdash; it is establishing how much neurology is here before you pick up the telephone.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>6 &middot; What you say, and who to</h3>
      <p class="stem">You are now seriously concerned. Raymond is sitting in front of you. What happens?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">The concern is articulated precisely &mdash; the sign named and the differential set out, so the seriousness is conveyed.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">Sit down and tell him &mdash; the finding in ordinary words, the concern named, the uncertainty held, and his wife brought in.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The examination is finished and the medical team called first &mdash; so the plan is in place before he is told anything.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="5-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Precision aimed at the wrong audience. &ldquo;Sixth nerve palsy&rdquo; and &ldquo;space-occupying lesion&rdquo; spoken across a man rather than to him is the second half of the named trap, and it leaves him holding the two worst words with no framework at all. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="5-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Well meant, and he watched your face change ten minutes ago. Arranging everything before speaking to him means he sits through the rest of the examination and a telephone call knowing something is wrong and being told nothing. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>Sit down, get to eye level, and use words he can repeat at home: a nerve that moves the eye is not working, and with the headaches and the sickness that can mean pressure building inside the head, so a scan is needed today. Name the uncertainty rather than filling it, and say why you will not guess. Ask whether his wife is with him, because the alternative is that she hears it in a car park from a frightened man. Then the sentence that reframes his year &mdash; <em>I don&rsquo;t think this was ever a depression</em>.</template>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="q" data-q>
      <h3>7 &middot; The formulation</h3>
      <p class="stem">What goes in the letter, and what happens to the psychiatric diagnosis?</p>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="0">Both recorded &mdash; a depressive episode plus a new neurological sign needing urgent investigation alongside treatment.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-ok data-oi="1">The formulation revised &mdash; the apathy reframed as a symptom, the medication question deferred, escalation documented.</button>
      <button class="opt" data-oi="2">The diagnosis suspended &mdash; no psychiatric conclusion recorded until imaging is available, so nothing wrong is entered.</button>
      <div class="fbs">
        <template data-fb="6-0"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>This is the neurology bolted onto the psychiatry, and it leaves &ldquo;depressive episode&rdquo; in the record as a parallel truth. If the apathy is the presenting symptom of raised pressure then it is not a comorbidity, and the letter should say so. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="6-2"><b>THE EXAMINER&rsquo;S VIEW</b>Too blank. Recording nothing leaves the next reader without your reasoning, and your reasoning is the most valuable thing in this encounter &mdash; a candidate who suspects the apathy is neurological should write that down, as a suspicion, with the grounds. Try again.</template>
        <template data-fb="ok"><b>THE EXAMINER AGREES</b>The three months of apathy stops being the diagnosis and becomes a symptom to be explained; the medication question waits; and the escalation is written with times, names and what was asked for, because this letter is the record of when the concern was raised. Say plainly that in your view this was not a primary depressive illness and give the grounds. That single reframing is what stops him being sent back to psychiatry with a different antidepressant if the first scan is equivocal.</template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 THE TRAP ============ -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">04</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The hidden trap &mdash; now that you&rsquo;ve felt it</span>
    <h2>Examine to a question, not to a list.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Both failures have adequate hands and both waste the finding. <b>The recital</b> is the map&rsquo;s named trap in its purest form: twelve nerves in order, narrated in the third person to the examiner, with the failure of left abduction registered as an item and followed straight away by the corneal reflex. The patient tries to interrupt &mdash; <em>&ldquo;is my eye all right? Only I&rsquo;ve been&mdash;&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and is told to wait so the sequence can be completed. It ends in the phrase that exists solely to allow a list to be reported as finished: <em>essentially normal apart from</em>. Nothing about new failure of abduction in a man of sixty-two is essentially normal, and referring it onward to an optician is worse than missing it, because it creates a record of having considered it. <b>The announcement</b> reasons correctly and detonates the finding over the head of the man it belongs to &mdash; <em>palsy</em>, <em>space-occupying lesion</em>, <em>urgent</em>, in the third person, eighteen inches from him &mdash; and then resumes the ninth and tenth nerves while his hands shake. That take gets the medicine right and fails the station, which candidates find hard to accept until they notice that <em>talking to the examiner instead of the patient</em> is half of what the trap is named for. The pass holds the referral&rsquo;s label open, gives the reason for examining in plain words, and then does the thing the whole station is built around: when the finding appears, the sequence <em>stops</em>. The movement is repeated, the patient is asked whether he sees one finger or two, and six seconds later the case is solved &mdash; double vision since May, a compensatory head turn nobody has asked about, morning headaches worse on coughing, and vomiting before breakfast twice this week. Then the examination widens to fundi, limbs and gait; the finding is spoken to Raymond at eye level in words he can carry home, with the uncertainty held rather than filled; his wife is brought in; and the formulation is revised rather than having neurology bolted onto it.</p>
    <div class="duo">
      <div class="flagcard red"><b>&#9873; Red flag &mdash; the instant fail</b>
        The map&rsquo;s named trap either way: a memorised sequence that never adapts when a finding appears &mdash; noted, filed, list completed, &ldquo;essentially normal apart from&rdquo; &mdash; or an examination conducted and reported to the examiner rather than to the patient, with a serious finding announced in clinical vocabulary over the head of the person it concerns and no explanation offered to him.</div>
      <div class="flagcard smile"><b>&#9786; The examiner&rsquo;s smile</b>
        Two moments separate the candidates: the six seconds when the sequence stops &mdash; <em>&ldquo;hold on. Do that again. When you look that way, do you see one finger or two?&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and the sentence that gives a man back three months of his life: <em>&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t think this was ever a depression.&rdquo;</em></div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro" style="margin-top:18px">Two threads to carry out. First, where this construct lives: D4&rsquo;s examination craft joined to D6&rsquo;s escalation, and the transferable rule closes the physical set &mdash; an examination is a series of questions, not a series of manoeuvres, and the mark is earned in the four seconds after an abnormality appears. That applies to every examination you will ever perform, and to a great deal that is not examination at all. Second, the transfer Exam Mode tests: the same discipline where there is nothing to find &mdash; a woman whose cranial nerve examination is completely normal, whose history is an emergency, and where &ldquo;cranial nerves intact&rdquo; is the sentence that will kill her.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 TAKE-HOMES ============ -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">05</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">Take-home points</span>
    <h2>Carry these into every neurological examination</h2>
    <ol class="takes">
      <li><b>Treat the referral&rsquo;s label as a hypothesis.</b> New apathy or personality change in later life, with no precipitant and no response to treatment, is one of the commonest ways neurological disease reaches psychiatry. Examine rather than prescribe again &mdash; and tell the patient why you are examining.</li>
      <li><b>Observe before you touch.</b> A sustained head turn, a ptosis, a facial asymmetry at rest, spectacles never worn. In this station the diagnosis was visible in the waiting room.</li>
      <li><b>When something appears, stop.</b> Repeat it, confirm it is real, and characterise it. The mark is earned in the four seconds after the abnormality shows itself, and &ldquo;complete first, interpret later&rdquo; is how a finding becomes a line item.</li>
      <li><b>Ask the patient &mdash; they usually know.</b> One image or two, which direction, how long, what makes it better. A man with a new palsy has been managing it for weeks and has never been asked.</li>
      <li><b>Let the finding rewrite your questions.</b> A sixth nerve palsy demands the raised-pressure history: headache on waking, worse on coughing or bending, early-morning vomiting, transient visual blurring. Then widen the examination &mdash; fundi, limbs, gait.</li>
      <li><b>Speak to the patient, not the examiner.</b> Sit down, get to eye level, use words they can repeat at home, name the concern honestly and hold the uncertainty rather than filling it. Bring the relative in rather than leaving them to hear it in a car park.</li>
      <li><b>Revise the formulation; don&rsquo;t append to it.</b> If the apathy is the presenting symptom of the physical problem, say so and give the grounds. Then escalate with times and names, because your letter is the record of when the concern was raised.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 PRACTICE PACK ============ -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="stepnum">06</span><span class="eyebrow" style="margin-left:8px">The practice system</span>
    <h2>Practise the skill on a fresh case</h2>
    <p class="intro">Three people: candidate, colleague, observer. Seven minutes for the station, eight for feedback read aloud. The colleague&rsquo;s card contains hidden information &mdash; including the signs to simulate; candidates, don&rsquo;t read it. <b>The angle here:</b> a finding that arrives in the middle of a routine appointment, where the whole diagnosis turns on one muscle group and the wrong reassurance is as dangerous as the wrong alarm.</p>

    <div id="packs">
    <div class="pack">
      <div class="pcard c-cand">
        <div class="ph"><b>Candidate card</b><span>you</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p><b>Task:</b> Bryn, 55, attends the community team for a routine review. He has bipolar affective disorder, stable for years. He arrives distressed: he woke this morning and the right side of his face &ldquo;had dropped&rdquo;. He is convinced he has had a stroke. Assess him, examine, explain and act.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Examine the face properly &mdash; the distinction you need is in one specific muscle group.</li>
            <li>Take the history that goes with it: onset, pain, hearing, taste, rash, limbs and speech.</li>
            <li>Neither reassure before examining nor alarm before thinking.</li>
            <li>Address the eye &mdash; there is something he must be given today.</li>
            <li>Explain, and act within a timescale that matters.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-role">
        <div class="ph"><b>Role-player card</b><span>plays Bryn &mdash; keep hidden</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>You are Bryn, 55, frightened and talking quickly. You open: &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve had a stroke, haven&rsquo;t I. I woke up and my face was like this. My dad had a stroke at fifty-eight and he never spoke again.&rdquo;</p>
          <div class="secret"><b>HIDDEN INFORMATION &mdash; ONLY REVEAL IF EARNED</b><br>
          &bull; <b>Signs to simulate:</b> the right side of your face is weak. Crucially, when asked to raise your eyebrows or wrinkle your forehead, the right side does <em>not</em> move &mdash; the forehead is involved. You cannot fully close your right eye; when you try, it stays slightly open. Your smile is asymmetrical. Everything else is normal: your arms and legs are strong, your speech is clear, your vision is fine.<br>
          &bull; If the candidate does not specifically ask you to raise your eyebrows or wrinkle your forehead, do not volunteer it. This is the whole examination and it must be sought.<br>
          &bull; Associated history, given if asked: two days of aching behind and below your right ear; sounds seem uncomfortably loud on the right; food has tasted odd since yesterday. No rash that you have seen, no recent tick bites, no fever. No weakness anywhere else, no trouble speaking or swallowing.<br>
          &bull; The eye: it has been watering all morning and feels gritty. You have been rubbing it. Nobody has mentioned it.<br>
          &bull; The fear beneath the fear, revealed only into warmth: not the face &mdash; &ldquo;I can&rsquo;t be like my dad. He sat in a chair for eleven years and my mother did everything. I&rsquo;d rather not be here than be that.&rdquo; If this is reached, the candidate must respond to it properly.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the recital:</b> if the candidate examines in silence and narrates to the room, or announces a diagnosis in clinical vocabulary without addressing you &mdash; interrupt: &ldquo;Will you please tell me what you&rsquo;re finding, because I&rsquo;m sat right here.&rdquo; Mark it hard.<br>
          &bull; <b>Punish the premature reassurance:</b> if the candidate says any version of &ldquo;this looks like Bell&rsquo;s palsy, nothing to worry about&rdquo; before testing your forehead &mdash; ask flatly: &ldquo;How do you know? You haven&rsquo;t looked at anything.&rdquo; Mark it hard, whatever they say next.<br>
          &bull; The pass: a candidate who tests the forehead, explains what that distinction means in plain words, takes the rest of the history, notices the eye and arranges protection for it, acts within the day, and answers your fear about your father &mdash; gets everything. You say: &ldquo;So it&rsquo;s not the same as him. You&rsquo;re sure about the forehead thing?&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pcard c-obs">
        <div class="ph"><b>Observer card</b><span>marked by domain &mdash; the examiner&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="pb">
          <p>Tick only what you actually saw. In feedback, go domain by domain; the weakest domain is the next thing to practise.</p>
          <div style="margin:4px 0 14px">
            <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Before any ticking &mdash; your overall judgement:</p>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Pass</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Borderline</button>
              <button class="opt" style="flex:0 0 auto; width:auto; padding:9px 16px">Fail</button>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:6px">Was the forehead specifically tested, was the distinction explained to him rather than about him, was the eye protected, and was his father answered? All must be yes. One sentence of evidence out loud &mdash; then tick the domains.</p>
          </div>
          <table class="rub" data-rub>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="setup"><td colspan="2"><b>The setup (core)</b><span class="dc" data-dc="setup">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="setup"></td><td>His fear received first, and the examination explained before it began.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="setup"></td><td>No verdict offered before examining &mdash; neither reassurance nor alarm ahead of the findings.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="seq"><td colspan="2"><b>The sequence</b><span class="dc" data-dc="seq">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="seq"></td><td>The forehead specifically tested &mdash; eyebrows raised, brow wrinkled &mdash; and the involvement identified.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="seq"></td><td>Eye closure tested, and the incomplete closure noticed rather than assumed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="seq"></td><td>Limbs, speech and swallow checked &mdash; the rest of the neurology not skipped because the face is dramatic.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="adapt"><td colspan="2"><b>The adaptation</b><span class="dc" data-dc="adapt">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="adapt"></td><td>The associated history sought &mdash; ear pain, sound sensitivity, taste, rash, fever.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="adapt"></td><td>The eye noticed as a problem in itself &mdash; watering, gritty, being rubbed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="adapt"></td><td>Stroke considered seriously rather than dismissed, and the reasoning made explicit.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="tell"><td colspan="2"><b>The telling</b><span class="dc" data-dc="tell">0/3</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="tell"></td><td>The forehead distinction explained to him in plain words he can repeat.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="tell"></td><td>Honest about certainty &mdash; what the examination shows and what still needs same-day assessment.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="tell"></td><td>His father answered &mdash; the comparison addressed directly, including what he said about not wanting to be here.</td></tr>
            <tr class="dom" data-d="act"><td colspan="2"><b>The action</b><span class="dc" data-dc="act">0/2</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="act"></td><td>Eye protection arranged today &mdash; lubrication and taping at night explained, not left to chance.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><input type="checkbox" data-d="act"></td><td>Same-day medical assessment arranged and the timescale explained; documented with times.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p class="scoreline">Total: <b data-sc>0 / 13</b> &mdash; <span data-verdict>go domain by domain, not by the total.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="printrow">
      <button class="btn ghost">Print all cards</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Candidate only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Role-player only</button>
      <button class="btn ghost">Observer only</button>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why this case:</b> Bryn reduces the station&rsquo;s discipline to a single muscle group and then punishes both directions of laziness. The role-player is instructed not to volunteer the forehead finding, so a candidate who does not specifically ask him to raise his eyebrows simply will not have the information on which the entire distinction rests &mdash; which is what &ldquo;examine to a question&rdquo; means in practice. He punishes the recital by refusing to be discussed in the third person, and he punishes premature reassurance with the only question that matters: <em>how do you know? You haven&rsquo;t looked at anything.</em> The case also carries two things candidates routinely omit even when the diagnosis is right. The first is the eye: an eye that will not close is at real risk, and lubrication with taping at night is the intervention available today, before anyone has decided anything else. The second is that a bedside distinction, however confident, does not by itself substitute for same-day assessment of a new facial weakness. And beneath all of it is his father in a chair for eleven years, and a sentence about not wanting to be here that a candidate focused on the seventh nerve will walk straight past.</div>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /learnPanel -->

<!-- ============ EXAM MODE ============ -->
<div id="examPanel">
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Exam Mode</span>
    <h2>Nothing to find. Everything to do. Seven minutes.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Knowing the right moves for Raymond proves memory. Exam Mode tests the discipline where it is hardest to hold: <b>an examination that is completely normal, in a woman whose history is an emergency</b> &mdash; and where &ldquo;cranial nerves intact&rdquo; is the sentence that sends her home. Timed, no feedback, no second tries: this is how a skill moves from <b>explored</b> to <b>recognised</b> &mdash; and the writing task after your results takes it a step further, toward <b>demonstrated</b>.</p>
    <div class="exambox" id="examStart">
      <h3>Your exam patient</h3>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch"><b>Dilys</b>, 71, has been under the older adults team for eighteen months with generalised anxiety. She is seen at home today. Over the last ten days she has had three episodes, each lasting a few minutes, of &ldquo;a grey curtain coming down&rdquo; over her right eye, which then lifted completely. Once, four days ago, her left hand &ldquo;wouldn&rsquo;t do what I told it&rdquo; for about twenty minutes while she was making tea. The team&rsquo;s notes from last week read: &ldquo;Reports odd visual sensations and hand symptoms &mdash; likely anxiety-related; reassured; continue sertraline.&rdquo; Your cranial nerve examination today, and the rest of your neurological examination, are entirely normal.</p>
      <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:64ch; margin-top:10px">Answer as you would in her front room &mdash; first instinct, under time. You need <b>at least 5 of 7</b>, including all three <b>critical decisions</b>, for the constructs to count as recognised. The full examiner analysis unlocks when you finish.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn">Start Exam Mode &#9654;</button></p>
    </div>
    <div id="examQs" style="display:none"></div>
    <div id="examSubmitRow" style="display:none; margin-top:18px"><button class="btn">Submit answers</button></div>

    <div class="results" id="results">
      <div class="scorebig" id="scoreLine"></div>
      <p id="verdictLine" style="margin-top:6px; max-width:66ch"></p>
      <div class="constructs" id="constructBox"></div>
      <div id="examReview" style="margin-top:22px"></div>
      <div class="exambox" id="produce" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h3>Now produce it &mdash; in your own words</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px; max-width:66ch">Recognising the right move is one skill; saying it is another. Type your exact words to Dilys &mdash; telling a woman with an anxiety diagnosis that her &ldquo;odd sensations&rdquo; are an emergency, without frightening her into refusing to go.</p>
        <textarea id="ftxt" rows="3" style="width:100%; margin-top:10px; font-family:var(--sans); font-size:15px; padding:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--hair); border-radius:9px" placeholder="Dilys, I need to tell you what I think those episodes were&#8230;"></textarea>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn" id="ftbtn">Check my wording</button></p>
        <div id="ftchecks" style="display:none; margin-top:12px">
          <p style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px">Now check your sentence honestly against the four principles:</p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Named plainly &mdash; warning strokes, in ordinary words; no &ldquo;funny turns&rdquo;, no burying it in caution.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The urgency given a timescale &mdash; today, and what &ldquo;today&rdquo; actually means in practice.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> The anxiety framing corrected without blaming her, or her team, for the delay.</label></p>
          <p><label><input type="checkbox"> Concrete arrangements &mdash; who is coming or where she is going, who you have spoken to, and what to do if it happens again on the way.</label></p>
          <p id="ftdone" style="display:none; margin-top:8px; color:var(--goldd); font-weight:700">Demonstrated (self-checked): the episodes named as an emergency, kindly &mdash; your own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn ghost">Return to Learn Mode</button>
      <button class="btn" id="retakeBtn" style="margin-left:10px">Retake Exam Mode</button></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); margin-top:8px">Best retaken after a day or two &mdash; the spacing is part of the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<!-- ============ DONE ============ -->
<section class="done on-navy" id="s7">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow gold">Station complete</span>
    <h2>Examine to a question, not to a list.</h2>
    <p>Two failures felt, seven decisions made &mdash; and the anatomy is installed: the referral&rsquo;s label held open, the reason for examining given to the patient, observation before touching, and the sequence stopped the moment something appeared so that it could be characterised rather than filed. Then the finding rewriting the questions, the examination widened, the news delivered at eye level in words he can carry home, and the formulation revised rather than appended to. Prove the discipline on Dilys in Exam Mode, where there is nothing to find and everything to do &mdash; then take Bryn to your colleagues: one muscle group, a frightened man, and his father in a chair for eleven years. That completes Domain 8 &mdash; return to the hub to review the domain, or move on when you are ready.</p>
    <a class="btn" style="margin-top:18px" href="48_Domain_8_Hub_Physical_Examination.html">Return to the Domain 8 hub &middot; Domain 8 is complete</a>
    <div class="tag2">Where Medicine Meets Justice</div>
  </div>
</section>

